THE LATEST VERSION oF AMi-EXPRESS |\n `:::::::::::::::|            | TONS OF ONLiNE MESSAGES AND MULTi-CHATS |\n  `::::::::::::::`------------+  NUP IS REQUIRED SO ASK WHOEVER POSTED  |\n   `::::::::::::::::::::::::::|    THIS ADVERT FOR THE NUMBER AND NUP   |\n    `:::::::::::::::::::::::::`-----------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',15748,4,0,NULL,NULL,'aops_tu1','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('aqannl10','AQANNL10.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/AQANNL10.LHA','AnnounceLogon','XIM','AQANNL10.LHA AnnounceLogon',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/                        \ /  AnnounceLogon v1.0   /\/ /       _____  _____     \',replace('   /\__________/\________/\\n  /                        \\n /  AnnounceLogon v1.0   /\/\n/       _____  _____     \\n\/\  /\/     \/     \/\  /\n   \/                  \/\n   a door for AmiExpress \n Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n33.6 DigITal-X Prd/NirVana Dist Site! 33.6\n','\n',char(10)),'AnnounceLogon.doc',replace('1993-08-04\n\n             //\  // \\  //  //  //\   || ))  ||  //  //  / /\\n            //\ \ \\ //  \\_//  //\ \  || \\  ||  \\_//   \ \   / Independent\n           //  \ \    ~                                  \/ /\n                                presents:\n\n                           /\__________/\________/\\n                          /                        \\n                         /  AnnounceLogon v1.0   /\/\n                        /       _____  _____     \\n                        \/\  /\/     \/     \/\  /\n                           \/                  \/\n                                              - a door for AmiExpress\n\n\n                        Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n\n               Permission is granted to freely distribute\n             this product for non-commercial purposes only,\n                as long as all files remain unmodified.\n\n\nArchive (AQANNL10.LHA) contents\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nAnnounceLogon            11128 bytes     The door, the executable.\nAnnounceLogon.doc         5520 bytes     This document file!\nannlogon.info             1270 bytes     Command icon for AmiExpress 3.x.\n\n\nDescription\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nThis little door is for multi-node boards. When a user logs on, the door\nnotifies the users already online of the logon. It can look something like\nthis:\n\nAquarius just logged in on node 2.\n\nThe door also tells the one who logged on whether any users were notified of\nthat.\n\nBut the users on the board can''t rely on this door to inform them of EVERY\nlogon, because it only notifies them when they''re "idle", doing nothing.\nOtherwise the "messages" would probably disturb them too much, and the board\ncould even crash.\n\n\nRequirements\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nSo far, it has only been tested on AmiExpress v2.39 and v3.6. I don''t think it\nwill work on earlier versions than 2.04, but I''m not sure. It shouldn''t be\ndangerous to test it on ANY version of AmiExpress though. Please contact me if\nyou encounter any problems.\n\nIt requires AmigaDOS 2.0 or higher to run.\n\nIMPORTANT! The "RX" command must be present in your "SYS:REXXC" directory!\n\n\nInstallation\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nIf you have AmiExpress 2.xx, you''ll have to add a line to your SYS.CMD (or\nCustomCommands). For example:\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n*ANNLOGON XM001Doors:AnnounceLogon\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^- File name of the door with full path.\n ^^^\n The command that will invoke the door.\n\nFor AmiExpress 3.x, just copy "annlogon.info" to your "Commands/SysCmd" drawer.\n\nThe door has to be run just after the logon, so it is most suitable to run it\nfrom your Logon.txt with the help of MCI. The smallest possible Logon.txt is:\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n~CC_ANNLOGON\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n\nNote that you must have a return, a space, or a ''|'' after the last MCI command\non a line. Else it will be ignored by AmiExpress. That''s why there is an empty\nrow in the example above.\n\nIf you want some text in the Logon.txt as well, a board logo perhaps, you can\nadd that right after. But MCI slows the text down, so if you want to avoid\nthat, use my door "Displayer". That will make it possible to have as many texts\nas you want at the logon. Displayer can also insert pauses ("Space to Resume")\nbefore and/or after the text it displays. Let''s say you install a Displayer\ncommand called "DISPLOGO" that is supposed to display a logo for your board.\nThen the Logon.txt could look like this:\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n~CC_ANNLOGON ~CC_DISPLOGO\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n\nI recommend that you run a WHO door right before AnnounceLogon. For that, my\nAquaWho v1.1 (or higher) is perfect. The Logon.txt would then be:\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n~CC_WHO ~CC_ANNLOGON ~CC_DISPLOGO\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n\n\nIf you want logons of NEW USERS to be announced, then run the door from\n"GuestLogon.txt" as well.\n\n\nContact me!\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nIf you discover any bugs or you think the door can be improved in some way,\nplease contact me. But also if you''re happy with it and you''re using it at your\nboard I''d like to hear from you.\n\nThe safest way to reach me is to call my friend Prof Do Right''s very nice board\nNightfall (+46-90-120369 or +46-90-178169), and leave me, Aquarius, a message.\nYou can also mail me through ALiENET, and through AmiXnet (@SWE001).\n\n\nSome greetings\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nProf Do Right/2ooo AD/PC <<< This door was your idea! :) Thanx!\nAXL/iNDEPENDENT          <<< I think I just found a way to improve this door\n                             drastically...\nRaJ-Ban/Independent      <<< Hope you''ll be connected world-wide real soon...\nDANSKEN/Insane           <<< Lookin'' forward to calling your board again.\n\n\nDisclaimer\n~~~~~~~~~~\nNo responsibility is taken by the author for what effect this program might\nhave on your system.\n\n\nHistory\n~~~~~~~\n0.1  Private version used at Nightfall only.\n\n1.0  (1993-08-04) First release.\n\n     Replaced the string "No users notified of your logon." with "No users have\n     been notified of your logon."\n\n     Added check for V36 of dos.library.\n\n     Removed some unnecessary parts of the code.\n\n     It now requires "rx" to be in the "SYS:rexxc" directory, because\n     AmiExpress 3.6 (at least) doesn''t supply doors with the system ''path''.\n\n\n\nThat''s all! Keep your eyes open for new stunning creations by me!\n\n                  >>  Aquarius / No group  <<\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'XIM-BBSCmd',16847,8,0,NULL,NULL,'aqannl10_announcelogon','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('aqdsbl11','AQDSBL11.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/AQDSBL11.LHA',NULL,'XIM','Disable.030',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/                        \ /      Disable v1.1     /\/ /       _____  _____     \',replace('   /\__________/\________/\\n  /                        \\n /      Disable v1.1     /\/\n/       _____  _____     \\n\/\  /\/     \/     \/\  /\n   \/                  \/\n   a door for AmiExpress \n Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n33.6 DigITal-X Prd/NirVana Dist Site! 33.6\n','\n',char(10)),'Disable.doc',replace('1993-08-04\n\n             //\  // \\  //  //  //\   || ))  ||  //  //  / /\\n            //\ \ \\ //  \\_//  //\ \  || \\  ||  \\_//   \ \   / Independent\n           //  \ \    ~                                  \/ /\n                                presents:\n                            /\____________/\_\n                           /                 \\n                         //   Disable v1.1    \\\n                         \\    ____      ___  //\n                           \/\/    \/\  /   \/\n                                      \/       - for AmiExpress\n\n\n                        Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n\n               Permission is granted to freely distribute\n             this product for non-commercial purposes only,\n                as long as all files remain unmodified.\n\n\nArchive (AQDSBL11.LHA) contents\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nDisable.000              26128 bytes   All-processors version of the door.\nDisable.030              26128 bytes   030 version of Disable - the door.\nEnable.000                8624 bytes   All-processors version of Enable.\nEnable.030                8624 bytes   030 version of Enable - CLI command.\nDisable.doc               9765 bytes   This document file!\nDisable.config             264 bytes   Sample configuration file.\ndis.info                  1274 bytes   Command icon for AmiExpress 3.x.\n\n\nDescription\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nDisable is the perfect system for control of your users paying for free\ndownload! It lets you specify how many days a user will have disabled ratio,\nand when that period is over, the ratio AND downloads are automatically\nrestored to what they were before. So you don''t have to bother any more! You\ncan also give the user other time and byte limits for that period.\n\nDisable actually consists of two programs: a door ("Disable"), and a CLI\ncommand ("AntiDisable" or "Enable"). To GIVE free download, use the door.\nEnable should be run once a day. It checks if the period is over for any users,\nand then restores those accounts.\n\n\nRequirements\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nSo far, Disable has only been tested on AmiExpress v2.39 and v3.6, but I think\nit will work on ANY version. Please contact me if you encounter any problems.\n\nIt requires AmigaDOS 2.0 or higher.\n\n\nInstallation\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nCopy the door ("Disable.000" or "Disable.030" depending on what CPU you have)\nto "Doors:" or to "Doors:Disable". The CLI command, Enable, I suggest you put\nin "BBS:Utils" or in "C:".\n\nFor AmiExpress 2.xx, add a line to your BBS.CMD (or CustomCommands). For\nexample:\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n*DIS      XM240Doors:Disable/Disable\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^- File name of the door with full path.\n            ^^^\n ^^^        Access required to use the door.\n The command that will invoke the door.\n\nFor AmiExpress 3.x, just copy the included icon, "dis.info", to your\n"Commands/BBSCmd" drawer, and check that its tooltypes are correct for you.\n\nAs stated before, the AntiDisable program, Enable, should be run once a day.\nRun it sometime between, let''s say, 00:00 and 05:00. For that you could use a\ncron program. With CyberCron, an event in its config file (s:CronTab) would\nlook something like this:\n\n30 00 * * * BBS:Utils/Enable\n\nThis would execute "BBS:Utils/Enable" at 00:30 every day.\n\nIf your BBS location is other than "BBS:", write that directory as only\nargument to Enable, i.e. the command syntax for Enable is:\n\nEnable [BBS location]\n\n\nThe configuration file\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n"Disable.config" must be located in "Doors:" or in "Doors:Disable". It contains\npresets to make the procedure of giving a user free download much smoother. The\nincluded config file looks like this:\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\nComment    :One month, one hour\nDays       :32\nByte limit :10000000\nTime limit :3600\n\nComment    :One month, two hours\nDays       :32\nByte limit :10000000\nTime limit :7200\n\nComment    :Two months, one hour\nDays       :63\nByte limit :10000000\nTime limit :3600\n\nEOF\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n\nAs you can see, each preset consists of four lines. You can have a maximum of 9\npresets. There must be an empty line between them and the list must end with\nthe string "EOF". Everything after that will be ignored.\n\nThe COMMENTs will be displayed to you when you run the door and are asked to\nchoose preset.\n\nBYTE LIMIT is the daily byte limit for the period of free download.\n\nThe (daily) TIME LIMIT is specified in seconds.\n\nObserve that all preset data must start at column 13, i.e. right after the\ncolons.\n\n\nUsage\n~~~~~\nWhen you start Disable - the door, the main menu comes up:\n\n1. Give a user free download\n2. List users with free DL (given to them by this door)\n3. Check for "overdue" users\n4. Force restore a user\n5. Exit\n\nIt should be rather clear what the 1st menu choice means. But test it on\nyourself (write "*" as name...), all the way, and you''ll see how far it will\ngo. Don''t worry, the door won''t write to your account. Actually, it won''t write\nto ANY user who is currently logged in on the BBS, since that wouldn''t give him\nfree download anyway.\n\nIf you choose number 2 from the menu, it can look something like this:\n\nAccNo Name (Current)    Start date End date Days passed Days left Leeched bytes\n~~~~~ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~ ~~~~~~~~~~ ~~~~~~~~ ~~~~~~~~~~~ ~~~~~~~~~ ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  57  Mike               08-02-93  09-04-93      -1         32                0\n\nIn this example, only one user has got free download. He could have had another\nname/handle when he got it, but here the current name is displayed. "Days\npassed" says "-1" which means he was given free download today. Tomorrow it\nwill say "0" (no whole days have passed).\n\nThe 3rd menu choice acts like Enable: it checks if the period of free download\nis over for any users, i.e. if "Days left" is a negative number. It first lists\nthose users and then asks if you want their accounts to be restored.\n\nThe 4th menu choice, "Force restore a user", lets you restore ANY of the users\nwith free download, even those with time left.\n\nA TIP! When you are prompted for a user name (menu choice 1 and 4), you can use\nwildcards! Not only "*" is supported. You can use all the wildcard characters\nthat you can use with AmigaDOS files: "()|[]*?#%~". This has the disadvantage\nthat if a name contains one or more of those symbols, you can''t write the name\nexplicitly (in most cases). Write question marks instead of those characters.\nFor example, if you want to give "=( HAWK )=" free download, write "=? HAWK ?="\ninstead, or more comfortable: "*HAWK*". If you write a single "*" as name, you\ncan step through all your users.\n\nA TIP! Whenever you are asked to type in something (other than Y/N), you can\npress Enter alone to abort... Try it!\n\nA TIP! In this version of Disable/Enable, no log file is created (that will\ncome soon). But with Enable, you can still get a log, if you call it like this:\n\nEnable >>Doors:Disable/Enable.log [BBS location]\n\n\nDisable.Data\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nDisable saves its data in a file called "Disable.Data" in your BBS directory.\nWhen a user is given free download, his name (among other things) is saved in\nthat file. But neither Disable nor Enable has any use of that (they use the\nslot numbers instead). The name is saved only for the reason that if that file\ngets corrupted, as result of a HD crash or something, you can look in it to\n(hopefully) see what users you have given free download. But remember that the\nnames in Disable.Data are the names they had when they were given free download\n- not necessarily the current.\n\n\nNote!\n~~~~~\nDo NOT use any AmiExpress util that will "clean" your User.Data, when you have\nusers with free download. Disable/Enable uses the slot numbers to keep track of\nthose users, and if their slot numbers change, Enable will write to the wrong\nslots in User.Data, which will probably mean chaos.\n\n\nContact me!\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nIf you discover any bugs or you think Disable can be improved in some way,\nplease contact me. But also if you''re happy with it and you''re using it at your\nboard I''d like to hear from you.\n\nThe safest way to reach me is to call my friend Prof Do Right''s very nice board\nNightfall (+46-90-120369 or +46-90-178169), and leave me, Aquarius, a message.\nYou can also mail me through ALiENET, and through AmiXnet (@SWE001).\n\n\nSome greetings\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nProf Do Right/2ooo AD/PC <<< Thanx for the original idea, m8!\nAXL/iNDEPENDENT       <<< Life is sooo much easier with a 030, right?\nJAC/ADDONIC         <<< Still think this door would make things easier for ya..\nRaJ-Ban/Independent   <<< Hope you''ll be connected world-wide real soon...\nDANSKEN/Insane           <<< Lookin'' forward to calling your board again.\n\n\nDisclaimer\n~~~~~~~~~~\nNo responsibility is taken by the author for what effect this program might\nhave on your system.\n\n\nHistory\n~~~~~~~\n0.1  (1993-07-11) First version, used at Nightfall and Disneyland.\n\n1.0  (1993-07-19) Private version..\n\n     Menu (in the door): 1. Give a user free download\n                         2. List users with free DL\n                         3. Check for "overdue" users\n                         4. Force remove free DL for a user\n                         5. Exit\n     Before, only the 1st menu item existed.\n\n     Disable now shows the date when the user''s period will end, at the "last\n     chance" prompt.\n\n     Added current date and time output for Enable.\n\n1.1  (1993-08-04) First public release.\n\n     New look for the door. Coloured text, screen clears, pauses.\n\n     Added check for V36 of dos.library.\n\n     Now you can''t give free download to a deleted user.\n\n\n\nThat''s all! Keep your eyes open for new stunning creations by me!\n\n                  >>  Aquarius / No group  <<\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',50004,7,0,NULL,NULL,'aqdsbl11','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('aqdspl10','AQDSPL10.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/AQDSPL10.LHA','Displayer__','XIM','DisplayerXX',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/                        \ /    Displayer v1.0     /\/ /       _____  _____     \',replace('   /\__________/\________/\\n  /                        \\n /    Displayer v1.0     /\/\n/       _____  _____     \\n\/\  /\/     \/     \/\  /\n   \/                  \/\n   a door for AmiExpress \n Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n33.6 DigITal-X Prd/NirVana Dist Site! 33.6\n','\n',char(10)),'Displayer.doc',replace('1993-08-04\n\n             //\  // \\  //  //  //\   || ))  ||  //  //  / /\\n            //\ \ \\ //  \\_//  //\ \  || \\  ||  \\_//   \ \   / Independent\n           //  \ \    ~                                  \/ /\n                                presents:\n                           /\__________________\n                         //                    \\\n                         \\   Displayer v1.0   //\n                           \   ____________   /\n                            \_/            \_/\n                                              - for AmiExpress\n\n\n                        Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n\n               Permission is granted to freely distribute\n             this product for non-commercial purposes only,\n                as long as all files remain unmodified.\n\n\nArchive (AQDSPL10.LHA) contents\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nDisplayerXX             8280 bytes   Door file. Pauses before and after.\nDisplayerX_             8276 bytes   Door file. Pauses before only.\nDisplayer_X             8276 bytes   Door file. Pauses after only.\nDisplayer__             8272 bytes   Door file. Does not pause.\nDisplayer.doc           5570 bytes   This document file!\ndisp.info               1279 bytes   A sample command icon for AmiExpress 3.x.\n\n\nDescription\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nDisplayer is a textviewer which can make ordinary pauses ("Space to Resume")\nbefore and/or after the text, or not at all. There are four versions of the\ndoor because of that, which you can see above. Displayer makes it possible to\nhave as many texts as you like where for example the Logon.txt or BBSTitle.txt\nare shown. It looks for a text with the name "NAME{SEC}.txt(.gr)", i.e. it uses\nthe same "pattern" as for example the AmiExpress files LOGON, JOINCONF and\nBULL. If you want to use some MCI commands in a text like Logon.txt, and also\nwant to show a pure text file there, Displayer can be used so that the text\nwon''t be shown so SLOWLY (because of MCI). The version of Displayer that\ndoesn''t make any pauses ("Displayer__") is perfect as a door menu viewer as\nwell. You can then have different menus for users with different access levels.\nFor example, you could have one menu, "NAME200.txt", for sysops and cosysops,\nand another one, "NAME020.txt" for the other users.\n\n\nRequirements\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nSo far, Displayer has only been tested on AmiExpress v2.39 and v3.6, but I\nthink it will work on ANY version. Please contact me if you encounter any\nproblems.\n\n\nInstallation\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nMake a directory in "Doors:" called "Displayer". Then, put the four door files\nthere (or the ones you need). The TEXTS that you want Displayer to show must be\nlocated in that directory, and they must have the same name (plus suffix) as\nthe AmiExpress COMMAND that started Displayer.\n\nTo install a Displayer command for AmiExpress 2.xx, add a line to your SYS.CMD\nor BBS.CMD (or CustomCommands). For example:\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n*DISP     XM020Doors:Displayer/DisplayerX_\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^- File name of the door with full path.\n            ^^^\n ^^^        Access required to use the door.\n The command that will invoke the door.\n\nFor AmiExpress 3.x users, I have included a sample command icon ("disp.info")\nwhich you put in your "Commands/SysCmd" or "Commands/BBSCmd" drawer. Edit its\ntooltypes to suit your needs.\n\nIf you, with that icon or the above CMD line, use the DISP command, the door\nlooks in the "Doors:Displayer" directory for a text with the name\n"DISP{SEC}.txt(.gr)". Valid file names are for example "DISP.txt",\n"DISP225.txt" and "DISP050.txt.gr". Use SnoopDos if you don''t understand.\n\n\nExample of usage\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nIf you want some doors to be run right after a user has logged on, for example\na ''who'' door (like my AquaWho) and my AnnounceLogon door, but you want a nice\nlogo there too, then use MCI in Logon.txt to run the doors AND Displayer. The\nLogon.txt would look something like this:\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n~CC_WHO ~CC_ANNLOGON ~CC_DISPLOGO\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n\nNote that you must have a return, a space, or a ''|'' after the last MCI command\non a line. Else it will be ignored by AmiExpress. That''s why there is an empty\nrow in the example above.\n\nIn this case, it is suitable to make the DISPLOGO command call "DisplayerX_"\n(pause before but not after the logo).\n\nIf you want yet another text to be displayed, after the logo, just put a new\nDisplayer command in SYS.CMD (or wherever you want it), and call that too from\nthe Logon.txt, after DISPLOGO.\n\n\nContact me!\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nIf you discover any bugs or you think the door can be improved in some way,\nplease contact me. But also if you''re happy with it and you''re using it at your\nboard I''d like to hear from you.\n\nThe safest way to reach me is to call my friend Prof Do Right''s very nice board\nNightfall (+46-90-120369 or +46-90-178169), and leave me, Aquarius, a message.\nYou can also mail me through ALiENET, and through AmiXnet (@SWE001).\n\n\nSome greetings\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nProf Do Right/2ooo AD/PC <<< Why''s your name so looong? :)\nAXL/iNDEPENDENT          <<< Thanx for all your ideas!\nJAC/ADDONIC              <<< Too bad you''re not ''around'' so often...\n\n\nDisclaimer\n~~~~~~~~~~\nNo responsibility is taken by the author for what effect this program might\nhave on your system.\n\n\nHistory\n~~~~~~~\n1.0  (1993-08-04) Initial release.\n\n\n\nThat''s all! Keep your eyes open for new stunning creations by me!\n\n                  >>  Aquarius / No group  <<\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',31175,8,0,NULL,NULL,'aqdspl10','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('aqlspd12','AQLSPD12.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/AQLSPD12.LHA',NULL,'XIM','ListSpeeder.000',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/                        \ /   ListSpeeder v1.2    /\/ /       _____  _____     \',replace('   /\__________/\________/\\n  /                        \\n /   ListSpeeder v1.2    /\/\n/       _____  _____     \\n\/\  /\/     \/     \/\  /\n   \/                  \/\n   a door for AmiExpress \n Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n33.6 DigITal-X Prd/NirVana Dist Site! 33.6\n','\n',char(10)),'ListSpeeder.doc',replace('1993-08-04\n\n             //\  // \\  //  //  //\   || ))  ||  //  //  / /\\n            //\ \ \\ //  \\_//  //\ \  || \\  ||  \\_//   \ \   / Independent\n           //  \ \    ~                                  \/ /\n                                presents:\n\n                           _/\________________/\_\n                        __/                     /\n                        \   ListSpeeder v1.2    \\n                        / __________  ________/\/\n                        \/          \/\n                                         - THE filelist downloader for /X\n\n\n                        Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n\n               Permission is granted to freely distribute\n             this product for non-commercial purposes only,\n                as long as all files remain unmodified.\n\n\nArchive (AQLSPD12.LHA) contents\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nListSpeeder.000          19536 bytes     The all-processors version.\nListSpeeder.020          19504 bytes     Optimized version for 020 Amigas.\nListSpeeder.030          19504 bytes     Optimized version for 030 Amigas.\nListSpeeder.040          19504 bytes     Optimized version for 040 Amigas.\nListSpeeder.cfg           1355 bytes     A sample configuration file.\nListSpeeder.doc           7997 bytes     This document file!\nfd.info                   1281 bytes     Command icon for AmiExpress 3.x.\nNoPack.bat                 278 bytes     A no-packer script.\n\n\nDescription\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nListSpeeder lets the user download the filelists in the current conference so\nthat he can check them offline.\n\n\nFeatures\n~~~~~~~~\n- Multi-node compatible!\n\n- AmiExpress 3.x compatible (reads NDIRS from an icon for those versions).\n\n- Lets the user select for which directories in the current conference he wants\n  to download the filelist(s).\n\n- Converts the filelists to PC/IBM text format on request (adds a Carriage\n  Return before each Line Feed), so that they will look fine to PC users as\n  well.\n\n- The sysop can give the user up to 9 different packers to choose from.\n  Although ListSpeeder is made to CRUNCH the filelists, the user can choose\n  NOT to pack, with the help of a certain script, which is included.\n\n- Displays the name of the archive before sending it, so that the user can make\n  sure he doesn''t have a file with that name in the download path already.\n\n- Gives free download for the list(s).\n\n\nRequirements\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nIt requires AmigaDOS 2.0 or higher to run. Since it is made to gain time for\nthe user, the computer of the board should be rather fast, at least if the\nfilelists are big. A 68000 Amiga is probably too slow to have any use for this\ntool, but on 030s it should run fast enough.\n\nSo far, ListSpeeder has only been tested on AmiExpress v2.39 and v3.6, but I\nthink it will work on ANY version. Please contact me if you encounter any\nproblems.\n\n\nInstallation\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nI suggest you make a directory in "Doors:" called "ListSpeeder". Then copy\n"ListSpeeder.cfg", "NoPack.bat", and the suitable version of ListSpeeder\n(depending on what CPU you have) to that directory. You can have the files\ndirectly in "Doors:" instead if you want to. Rename the door (the executable)\nyou copied as "ListSpeeder" (not required though).\n\nFor AmiExpress 2.xx, add a line to your BBS.CMD (or Customcommands).\nFor example:\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n*FD       XM050Doors:ListSpeeder/ListSpeeder\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^- File name of the door with full path.\n            ^^^\n ^^^        Access required to use the door.\n The command that will invoke the door.\n\nFor AmiExpress 3.x, just copy "fd.info" to your "Commands/BBSCmd" drawer.\n\n\nThe configuration file\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n"ListSpeeder.cfg" must be in "Doors:" or in "Doors:ListSpeeder". The included\nconfig file looks like this:\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\nFILES\nYES\n\nLhA\nC:LHA -q a {d} {s}\n.LHA\n\nZip\nC:ZIP -q {d} {s}\n.ZIP\n\nNo packer\nexecute Doors:ListSpeeder/NoPack.bat {s} {d}\n.TXT\n\n*EOF*\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n\nThe first line contains the name of the destination file (the filelist archive)\nwithout suffix. This line must not contain any spaces! I suggest you change it\nto something that contains the name of your board, or an abbreviation of it. If\nyou have PC users on your board, make sure that the file name (without the\nsuffix) won''t be longer than 8 characters when the conference number has been\nadded to it, if you have that option turned on (see next paragraph).\n\nIf you want the conference number to be added to the filename, write YES on the\nsecond line, otherwise NO.\n\nAfter that, an empty line follows. It will be ignored by the door.\n\nThen, to only use LhA (with the default options) as packer, write "*EOF*".\n"LhA" must then be in your "C:" directory (at least for AmiExpress 3.6 because\nit doesn''t supply its doors with the system ''path''). If you want the user to be\nable to choose between different packers, you must write them with three lines\neach and an empty line between. The maximum number of packers is 9. End the\nlist of packers with the string "*EOF*".\n\nThe first line for a packer will be displayed to the user. Write for example\nthe name of the packer and perhaps a comment about it.\n\nThen, on the second line for that packer, you give ListSpeeder information\nabout how to use the packer, how to call it. Write "{d}" where the door should\nput the name of the destination file, and "{s}" for the source file. Turn off\nas much output as possible for the packer. For LhA, "-q" accomplishes that.\nNobody will see the output from the packer anyway, and turning it off will\nprobably make the packer a bit faster.\n\nOn the third and last packer line, write the suffix that you want archives made\nby that packer to have. No spaces are allowed on that line.\n\n\nContact me!\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nIf you discover any bugs or you think the door can be improved in some way,\nplease contact me. But also if you''re happy with it and you''re using it at your\nboard I''d like to hear from you.\n\nThe safest way to reach me is to call my friend Prof Do Right''s very nice board\nNightfall (+46-90-120369 or +46-90-178169), and leave me, Aquarius, a message.\nYou can also mail me through ALiENET, and through AmiXnet (@SWE001).\n\n\nSome greetings\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nProf Do Right/2ooo AD/PC <<< Mega-thanx for all your help and support!\nAXL/iNDEPENDENT          <<< You can''t say this door is slow now, can you? :)\nJAC/ADDONIC              <<< Thanx for convincing me to write a door of this\n                             kind. Hope it turned out like you wanted.\nRaJ-Ban/Independent      <<< Thanx for letting me test this door at your board.\nDANSKEN/Insane           <<< Hope ya get your board running soon again...\n\n\nDisclaimer\n~~~~~~~~~~\nNo responsibility is taken by the author for what effect this program might\nhave on your system.\n\n\nHistory\n~~~~~~~\n0.1  Non-public version used at Nightfall and Disneyland.\n\n1.0  Added check for V36.\n\n     The door now displays the name of the file before sending it.\n\n     Config file with different packers. User can choose packer.\n\n1.1  Made it /X 3.x compatible by reading ndirs from the conf icon\n     for those versions (and higher).\n\n     Forgot to check that no more than 9 packers were read from the\n     config file. Now it says "Error reading config file!" if you have\n     more than 9 packers there.\n\n     Added a CR at the beginning of the file lists, if PC format was\n     selected, which makes the no-packer script work better with PC format.\n\n     Speeded up the "preparing" for non-PC format.\n\n1.2  (1993-08-04) First release version.\n\n     Removed some unnecessary parts of the code, which decreased the size of\n     the executable a little.\n\n     The packers no longer have to junk directory names, because ListSpeeder\n     now makes the work directory the current.\n\n\n\nThat''s all! Keep your eyes open for new stunning creations by me!\n\n                  >>  Aquarius / No group  <<\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',57551,8,0,NULL,NULL,'aqlspd12','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('aqnete10','AQNETE10.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/AQNETE10.LHA','NetEdit','XIM','NetEdit',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/                        \ /       NetEdit v1.0    /\/ /       _____  _____     \',replace('   /\__________/\________/\\n  /                        \\n /       NetEdit v1.0    /\/\n/       _____  _____     \\n\/\  /\/     \/     \/\  /\n   \/                  \/\n   a door for AmiExpress \n Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n33.6 DigITal-X Prd/NirVana Dist Site! 33.6\n','\n',char(10)),'NetEdit.doc',replace('1993-08-01\n\n             //\  // \\  //  //  //\   || ))  ||  //  //  / /\\n            //\ \ \\ //  \\_//  //\ \  || \\  ||  \\_//   \ \   / Independent\n           //  \ \    ~                                  \/ /\n                                presents:\n\n                                |\n                          __  __|___|___|___   _\n                            |   |   |   |   |\n                        |___|___|___|___|___|___|_\n                        |   |               |   |\n                        |___| NetEdit v1.0  |___|__\n                        |   |               |   |\n                         ___|___|___|___|___|___|_\n                            |   |   |   |   |   |\n                                |   | __|___\n                                        |\n\n                                        - an ''e'' replacement for AmiExpress\n\n\n                        Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n\n               Permission is granted to freely distribute\n             this product for non-commercial purposes only,\n                as long as all files remain unmodified.\n\n\n\nArchive (AQNETE10.LHA) contents\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nNetEdit                  15164 bytes     The door, the executable.\nNetEdit.doc               3669 bytes     This document file!\ne.info                    1265 bytes     Command icon for AmiExpress 3.x.\n\n\nDescription\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nNetEdit is a message editor for AmiExpress.\n\nThe differences from the internal editor are:\n\n- It doesn''t check that the one you write to is a user on that board.\n\n- It adds an "origin" note at the end of the message when you save it - board\n  name and sysop name.\n\n- You can''t attach files.\n\nEverything else is the same. It is multi-node compatible, you can write EALLs,\nwrite private messages, and the actual editor is exactly the same as the one\nyou''re used to.\n\nThis makes NetEdit a perfect message editor for netmail systems, like the one\nby The Silencer / Equinox.\n\n\nRequirements\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\n- AmigaDOS 2.0\n\n- AmiExpress (I think it works on any version)\n\n\nHow to install it\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nFor AmiExpress 2.xx, add the following line to your BBS.CMD or CONF{x}.CMD:\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n*E        XM050Doors:NetEdit\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^- File name of the door with full path.\n            ^^^\n ^^         Access required to use the door.\n The command that will invoke the door.\n\nFor AmiExpress 3.x, just copy "e.info" to your "Commands/BBSCmd" drawer, or to\n"Commands/CONF{x}Cmd".\n\n\nContact me!\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nIf you discover any bugs or you think the door can be improved in some way,\nplease contact me. But also if you''re happy with it and you''re using it at your\nboard I''d like to hear from you.\n\nThe safest way to reach me is to call my friend Prof Do Right''s very nice board\nNightfall (+46-90-120369 or +46-90-178169), and leave me, Aquarius, a message.\nYou can also mail me through ALiENET, and through AmiXnet (@SWE001).\n\n\nSome greetings\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nProf Do Right/2ooo AD/PC <<< Why are there so few hours a day? Stay cool..\nAXL/iNDEPENDENT          <<< Thanx for talking me into writing this door.\nThe Silencer/Equinox     <<< Hope you don''t mind...\nALIS/Crystal             <<< I expect you to be using this door! :)\nDANSKEN/Insane           <<< Hope you get your board running soon again...\n\n\nDisclaimer\n~~~~~~~~~~\nNo responsibility is taken by the author for what effect this program might\nhave on your system.\n\n\nHistory\n~~~~~~~\n1.0  (1993-08-01) Initial release.\n\n\n\nThat''s all! Keep your eyes open for new stunning creations by me!\n\n                  >>  Aquarius / No group  <<\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',21943,8,0,NULL,NULL,'aqnete10','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('aqstrp11','AQSTRP11.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/AQSTRP11.LHA','Strip','XIM','Strip',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/                        \ /     Stripper v1.1     /\/ /       _____  _____     \',replace('   /\__________/\________/\\n  /                        \\n /     Stripper v1.1     /\/\n/       _____  _____     \\n\/\  /\/     \/     \/\  /\n   \/                  \/\n   a door for AmiExpress \n Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n33.6 DigITal-X Prd/NirVana Dist Site! 33.6\n','\n',char(10)),'Stripper.doc',replace('1993-08-04\n\n             //\  // \\  //  //  //\   || ))  ||  //  //  / /\\n            //\ \ \\ //  \\_//  //\ \  || \\  ||  \\_//   \ \   / Independent\n           //  \ \    ~                                  \/ /\n                                presents:\n\n                                   //\n                                   \\\n                                   //\n                          >>> Stripper v1.1 <<<\n                                 //\n                                 \\\n                                 //\n\n                        Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n\n               Permission is granted to freely distribute\n             this product for non-commercial purposes only,\n                as long as all files remain unmodified.\n\n\nArchive (AQSTRP11.LHA) contents\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nStrip                  10800 bytes      The CLI command.\nStripper.doc            4303 bytes      This document file.\n\n\nWhat it does\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nStripper simply cuts off the beginning of a file, strips it. The cut off part\ncan be moved to the end of another file, a "backup" file. You must specify how\nmany bytes you want the file you''re stripping to be. But Stripper won''t cut the\nfile in the middle of a row. It finds the nearest start of a row towards the\nbeginning of the file and cuts there instead. Hence, the stripped file will be\nbigger than, or equal to, the size you specified.\n\n\nWhy it could be useful\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nIf you''re running an AmiExpress BBS, some files tend to grow quite big, for\nexample the CallersLog and the UDLog. To keep them constantly at a reasonable\nsize, use Stripper! Many doors seek through these files, so they shouldn''t be\ntoo big, or else it will take a while for the doors to handle them. Stripping\nthe CallersLog was the original use for this program. Note that Stripper\ndoesn''t check if it cuts the files in the middle of a "user" (a call). You can\nfor example call it in your logoff script, or once a day (with some Cron\nprogram), or once a week. That''s up to you.\n\nThe terminal program TERM maintains, as default, a file called term-action.log\nwhere all your actions are recorded, such as connects, downloads, uploads and\ndisconnects. That file can eventually grow very big, and that''s where Stripper\ncomes into the picture...\n\nIt''s up to you to think of all the other possibilities.\n\n\nRequirements\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nStripper requires an Amiga with AmigaDOS 2.0 or higher to run.\n\n\nHow to use it\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nThe command line syntax is as follows:\n\nstrip <stripfile> <size> [BACKUP <backupfile>]\n\n<stripfile> is the name of the file that will get its beginning cut off.\n<size> is the desired size in bytes for the stripfile, which will be cut at the\nnearest beginning of a row, towards the beginning of the file. If the keyword\nBACKUP is submitted, the cut off part will be appended to <backupfile>.\n\nSome examples:\n\nstrip bbs:node0/CallersLog 100000\n\nThis call strips the file bbs:node0/CallersLog so that is becomes 100000 bytes\nbig, or perhaps some bytes bigger, so that it won''t be cut in the middle of a\nrow.\n\nstrip bbs:node0/CallersLog 100000 backup bbs:node0/CLogBackup\n\nThis accomplishes the same as the last call, but the cut off part will be\nappended to the file bbs:node0/CLogBackup.\n\n\nChitchat\n~~~~~~~~\nThis little tool was originally made for  Prof Do Right/2ooo AD/PC  only, but\nhe convinced me to release it. It is written in C and compiled with SAS/C 6.3.\n\n\nContact me!\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nIf you discover any bugs or you think the program can be improved in some way,\nplease contact me. But also if you''re happy with it and using it, I''d like to\nhear from you.\n\nThe safest way to reach me is to call my friend Prof Do Right''s very nice board\nNightfall (+46-90-120369 or +46-90-178169), and leave me, Aquarius, a message.\nYou can also mail be through ALiENET, and through AmixNet (@SWE001).\n\n\nDisclaimer\n~~~~~~~~~~\nNo responsability is taken by the author for what effect this program might\nhave on your system.\n\n\nHistory\n~~~~~~~\n1.0  (1993-06-29) First working version, made for Prof Do Right only.\n\n1.1  (1993-08-04) First public release.\n\n     Added CTRL-C disabling, a version string, and a check for OS V36.\n\n\n\nThat''s all! Keep your eyes open for new stunning creations by me!\n\n                  >>  Aquarius / No group  <<\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',15297,8,0,NULL,NULL,'aqstrp11','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('aquawh11','AQUAWH11.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/AQUAWH11.LHA',NULL,'XIM','AquaWho.000',NULL,NULL,NULL,'/                        \ /      AquaWho v1.1     /\/ /       _____  _____     \',replace('   /\__________/\________/\\n  /                        \\n /      AquaWho v1.1     /\/\n/       _____  _____     \\n\/\  /\/     \/     \/\  /\n   \/                  \/\n   a door for AmiExpress \n Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n33.6 DigITal-X Prd/NirVana Dist Site! 33.6\n','\n',char(10)),'AquaWho.doc',replace('1993-08-04\n\n             //\  // \\  //  //  //\   || ))  ||  //  //  / /\\n            //\ \ \\ //  \\_//  //\ \  || \\  ||  \\_//   \ \   / Independent\n           //  \ \    ~                                  \/ /\n                                presents:\n\n                                  /\        /\\n                               /\/  \  /\  /  \\n                          /\/\/      \/  \/    \\n                         /                     /\n                         \    AquaWho v1.1    /\n                          \              /\  /\n                           \  /\/\    /\/  \/\n                            \/    \  /\n                                   \/      - a who door for AmiExpress\n\n                        Copyright ©1993 Aquarius\n\n               Permission is granted to freely distribute\n             this product for non-commercial purposes only,\n                as long as all files remain unmodified.\n\n\nArchive (AQUAWH11.LHA) contents\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nAquaWho.000          18912 bytes     The all-processors version.\nAquaWho.030          18904 bytes     Optimized version for 030 Amigas.\nAquaWho.config         191 bytes     A sample configuration file.\nAquaWho.doc           8716 bytes     This document file!\nwho.info              1273 bytes     Command icon for AmiExpress 3.x.\n\n\nDescription\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nAquaWho is an AmiExpress door for multinode boards. Briefly you could say that\nit gives the user information about what''s happening on the other nodes.\n\n\nFeatures\n~~~~~~~~\n- It displays node number, phone number, user name, user location, what he''s\n  doing, and which conference he''s in. To my knowledge, this is the ONLY who\n  door that shows the current conference.\n\n- You can configure how high access level is required to see the conference\n  number.\n\n- You can configure how high access level is required to see the file names\n  when someone is uploading/downloading.\n\n- If a new user is logging into the BBS for the first time, AquaWho displays\n  "A new user!" in the "User" column when he is typing in answers to all the\n  new-user questions. When he is done, his name/handle is displayed, with the\n  note "(NEW)" added.\n\n- AquaWho is prepared for coming releases of AmiExpress in the way that it\n  doesn''t limit itself to 9 as the highest node number, or 9 as the highest\n  conference number.\n\n- Nice colours...\n\n\nRequirements\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nI know for sure that the door works on AmiExpress v2.35, v2.39, v3.5 and v3.6.\nI don''t think it will work on earlier versions than v2.04, but I haven''t been\nable to find a complete revision history for AmiExpress so I can''t be sure. It\nshouldn''t be dangerous to test it on ANY version though... Please contact me if\nyou encounter any problems.\n\n\nInstallation\n~~~~~~~~~~~~\nCopy the suitable version of AquaWho (depending on what CPU you have) to your\nharddisk and rename it as "AquaWho". I suggest you put it in "Doors:" or in\n"Doors:AquaWho".\n\nAquaWho looks for its configuration file, "AquaWho.config", in the following\ndirectories (in this order): "ENV:", "Doors:" and "Doors:AquaWho". To get the\nquickest startup, put it in "ENV:" ("ENVARC:"), if that''s in RAM, especially if\nyou have MANY nodes.\n\nFor AmiExpress 2.xx, add some lines to your BBS.CMD (or Customcommands).\nSuitable entries:\n--------------------------------------------\n*WH       XM020Doors:AquaWho/AquaWho\n*WHO      XM020Doors:AquaWho/AquaWho\n*NODE     XM020Doors:AquaWho/AquaWho\n--------------------------------------------\n               ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^- File name of the door with full path.\n            ^^^\n ^^^^       Access required to use the door.\n The command that will invoke AquaWho.\n\nFor AmiExpress 3.x, just copy the included icon ("who.info") to your\n"Commands/BBSCmd" directory, and clone it if you want more commands than WHO to\nstart AquaWho. Check the tooltypes so that they are correct for you.\n\n\nThe configuration file\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n"AquaWho.config" is a text file you''ll have to edit using a text editor. The\nconfig file included (which is Nightfall''s) looks like this:\n\n----Cut here------------------------------------\nFirst node to show :0\nLast node to show  :2\nPhone numbers      :LOCAL NODE\n                   :+46-90-120369\n                   :+46-90-178169\nMin ax for confs   :100\nMin ax for files   :100\n----Cut here------------------------------------\n\nRather self-explaining, don''t you think? This board has three nodes: 0, 1 and\n2. Node 0 is the local node.\n\nOn line one you write, after the colon, the number of your first node, usually\n"0". But if that, for example, is your local node and you don''t want the users\nto see what you''re doing when you''re there, you should write "1" instead.\n\nLine two should contain the number of your last node. Of course if you don''t\nwant one or more of your last nodes to be shown you could write a lower number\nhere.\n\nThen, starting on the third line, you enter the phone numbers for every node,\neach on an own line. So if you have four nodes (that you want to be shown), you\nmust use four lines for the phone numbers. For a local node, you could for\nexample write "SysOp''s node" or "LOCAL NODE", or whatever. Note that you must\nnot use more than 15 characters for the phone numbers. If you do, they will be\ntruncated.\n\nOn the line right below the last phone number, you write the access level\nrequired to see in which conferences other users online are.\n\nAs "Min ax for files" you write the access level required to see the names of\nthe files being uploaded or downloaded.\n\nObserve that all config entries must start at column 21, i.e. right after the\ncolons.\n\n\nNote!\n~~~~~\nIf you are using relative conferences, the conference numbers AquaWho shows\nmight not be the same for all users on your board. But if they all have access\nto the x first conferences, there''s no problem. For example, it''s not good if\nyou''re using relative conferences and some users'' access looks like this:\nXX_X__X__ or X_XXX____. Better: XXX______ and XXXX_____, as examples. (AE 3.x\ndon''t have these conference access strings, but you get the point anyway.) So\nif you have the "bad" case, either turn off relative conferences, or turn off\nconference showing by entering "300" as "Min ax for confs" in the config file.\n\n\nContact me!\n~~~~~~~~~~~\nIf you discover any bugs or you think the door can be improved in some way,\nplease contact me. But also if you''re happy with it and you''re using it at your\nboard I''d like to hear from you.\n\nThe safest way to reach me is to call my friend Prof Do Right''s very nice board\nNightfall (+46-90-120369 or +46-90-178169), and leave me, Aquarius, a message.\nYou can also mail me through ALiENET, and through AmiXnet (@SWE001).\n\n\nSome greetings\n~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\nProf Do Right/2ooo AD/PC <<< How would make it without you?\nAXL/iNDEPENDENT          <<< Thanx for all your help with this ''n that.\nJAC/ADDONIC              <<< Your Logon.txt looks GR8 now! hehe\nALIS/Crystal             <<< It''s nice you got time to install this thing.\nDANSKEN/Insane           <<< Hope ya get your board running soon again!\n\n\nDisclaimer\n~~~~~~~~~~\nNo responsibility is taken by the author for what effect this program might\nhave on your system.\n\n\nHistory\n~~~~~~~\n0.1  First working version, only for Nightfall.\n\n0.2  Added conference showing.\n\n0.3  Added filename showing and config file reading.\n\n1.0  (1993-06-29) First public release.\n\n     The config file got a new format - explanations for every line were added.\n\n     Some minor bugs were fixed and the source was cleaned up a bit.\n\n     More error checks added.\n\n     It no longer creates a temporary output file to show, because sysop could\n     then break in with F1 (chat) in the middle of the output.\n\n1.01 Released one day after v1.0, which I had only tested locally. I forgot to\n     use CR+LF instead of LF only, when I removed the temporary file. So the\n     output wasn''t very nice for remote users, even though it looked alright\n     for the sysop. Fixed that.\n\n1.02 Not a public version but some boards got it. The conf number for the door\n     user was retrieved using a built-in AmiExpress function. But if AquaWho\n     was used in the logon sequence, that function returned a conf number even\n     though the user hadn''t auto-rejoined a conf yet. So now is uses the same\n     method for that node as for the others, i.e. reading the Callerslog.\n\n1.1  (1993-08-04) Release version.\n\n     Some files were not closed under certain circumstances. Fixed that.\n\n     Added a version string.\n\n     Added "ENV:" as a directory where AquaWho looks for "AquaWho.config".\n\n     Fixed CTRL-C disabling.\n\n\n\nThat''s all! Keep your eyes open for new stunning creations by me!\n\n                  >>  Aquarius / No group  <<\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',33727,8,0,NULL,NULL,'aquawh11','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('aqwk24cr','AQWK24CR.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/AQWK24CR.LHA','AmiQWK','XIM','AmiQWK',NULL,NULL,NULL,'o--\       ///  _   \/  _   \\\_   \// \_--o |   \_/   /\/   /\   \  /\   \/     \  _/  | :   _/   / ·\   \/    \ \/   /   \     |   |',replace('   _________  ______  ______  _____  /\\no--\       ///  _   \/  _   \\\_   \// \_--o\n|   \_/   /\/   /\   \  /\   \/     \  _/  |\n:   _/   / ·\   \/    \ \/   /   \     |   |\n|   \_____   \________/______\  _|\____|   :\n`--------\_____/-÷S  o  f  T÷-\/-----------''\n    -> AmiQWK v2.4 + PseudoRegistered <-\n','\n',char(10)),'AmiQWK.guide',replace('@database AmiQWK.guide\n\n@Master amiqwk.texi\n\n@Width 72\n\n\nThis is the AmigaGuide® file AmiQWK.guide, produced by Makeinfo-1.55 from \nthe input file amiqwk.texi.\n\n\n@Node Main "AmiQWK.guide"\n@Next "Introduction"\n\n\n\n\n\nChapters:\n\n @{" Introduction " Link "Introduction"} \n @{" Legal Stuff " Link "Legal Stuff"} \n @{" System Requirements " Link "System Requirements"} \n @{" Installation and Quick Setup " Link "Installation and Quick Setup"} \n @{" Using AmiQWK " Link "Using AmiQWK"} \n @{" Reading and Writing Messages " Link "Reading and Writing Messages"} \n @{" Configuration " Link "Configuration"} \n\nAppendixes:\n\n @{" Advanced QWK features " Link "Advanced QWK features"}            Adding, Dropping conferences, etc.\n @{" The Future of AmiQWK " Link "The Future of AmiQWK"}             What to look for in future versions.\n @{" Technical Support " Link "Technical Support"}                How to get technical support.\n @{" Acknowledgments " Link "Acknowledgments"}                  Some of the people who made `AmiQWK'' possible.\n @{" Index " Link "Index"}                            Concept Index.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Introduction" "AmiQWK.guide/Introduction"\n@Next "Legal Stuff"\n@Prev "Main"\n@Toc "Main"\n\nIntroduction\n************\n\n\n @{" About this manual " Link "About this manual"}    About the `AmiQWK II'' Manual.\n @{" What is QWKMail? " Link "What is QWKMail?"}     Description of QWKMail.\n @{" What is AmiQWK II? " Link "What is AmiQWK II?"}   Description of `AmiQWK II''.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "About this manual" "AmiQWK.guide/About this manual"\n@Next "What is QWKMail?"\n@Toc "Introduction"\n\nAbout this manual\n=================\n\n   This manual assumes that you have at least a basic knowledge of the\nAmiga operating system. If you are not familiar with the Amiga I,\nrecommend reading the ''Basic Operations'' chapter of your AmigaDOS\nmanual.\n\n   This manual was written in `Texinfo'', the GNU documentation format,\nand processed through `MakeGuide'' to generate ASCII, `AmigaGuide'', TeX,\nand PostScript versions.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "What is QWKMail?" "AmiQWK.guide/What is QWKMail?"\n@Next "What is AmiQWK II?"\n@Prev "About this manual"\n@Toc "Introduction"\n\nWhat is QWKMail?\n================\n\n   QWKMail is an offline message format. Using QWKMail allows you to\ncall a BBS, collect all waiting messages, transfer them to your\ncomputer, and read them after you have logged off of the BBS. You can\nthen read them with a QWK compatible message reader such as `AmiQWK''.\nNew messages can be edited with your favorite text editor. Some of the\nadvantages of using QWKMail include:\n\n   * Collecting all waiting messages into a QWKMail packet usually\n     takes only a fraction of the time it takes to read messages\n     online, resulting in lower phone bills.\n\n   * Since your replies are edited offline, you can take as much time\n     as you need to enter and reply to messages. You also have the\n     advantage of using tools that may not be available when using BBS\n     editors, such as spelling and grammar checkers.\n\n   * You can easily save or print messages for future reference.\n\n   The QWK format was created in 1987 by Mark ''Sparky'' Herring. It is\nbased on Clark Development Corp.''s `PC-Board'' Version 12.0 BBS message\nbase format.  The QWKMail format is now supported by most BBS programs\neither internally or by an external ''door'' program.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "What is AmiQWK II?" "AmiQWK.guide/What is AmiQWK II?"\n@Prev "What is QWKMail?"\n@Toc "Introduction"\n\nWhat is AmiQWK II?\n==================\n\n   `AmiQWK II'' is a full featured QWKMail offline message reader for the\nCommodore Amiga computer. My goals for `AmiQWK'' are (in no particular\norder)\n\n   * To be the most stable and compatible QWKMail reader available for\n     the Commodore Amiga computer.\n\n   * To be the most full featured QWKMail reader for the Commodore Amiga\n     computer.\n\n   * To take advantage of features found Release 2 and higher of the\n     Amiga operating system.\n\n   * To be easy to use.\n\n   * To be highly configurable.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Legal Stuff" "AmiQWK.guide/Legal Stuff"\n@Next "System Requirements"\n@Prev "Introduction"\n@Toc "Main"\n\nLegal Stuff\n***********\n\n\n @{" About Shareware " Link "About Shareware"}      About the shareware concept.\n @{" Registration " Link "Registration"}         How to register `AmiQWK''.\n @{" Warranty " Link "Warranty"}             Warranty information.\n @{" Copyright " Link "Copyright"}            Copyright information.\n @{" Distribution " Link "Distribution"}         Distribution information.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "About Shareware" "AmiQWK.guide/About Shareware"\n@Next "Registration"\n@Toc "Legal Stuff"\n\nAbout Shareware\n===============\n\n   `AmiQWK II'' is released under the concept of Shareware. This means\nthat you are granted a license to use `AmiQWK II'' for a period of 30\ndays after the initial use. After 30 days you must either register the\nprogram or discontinue its use. See @{"Registration" Link "Registration"}, for more details.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Registration" "AmiQWK.guide/Registration"\n@Next "Warranty"\n@Prev "About Shareware"\n@Toc "Legal Stuff"\n\nRegistration\n============\n\n   To register `AmiQWK'', fill out the registration form (filename\n`Registration.form'') and send it along with your payment to the address\non the registration form. You will receive a 16 digit registration key\nbased on your name. Enter this number into the ''Register'' window of the\nconfiguration editor and `AmiQWK II'' will generate a keyfile\n(`AmiQWK.KEY'') that will remove the shareware registration notices.\n\n   Recommended method of payment is a check drwan from a U.S. bank or a\nmoney order in US dollars. (I can no longer accept personal checks\ndrawn from Canadian banks due to a change in bank policy.) Cash is\naccepted (US Funds only), however you send cash at your own risk.\n\n   AmiQWK users in New Zealand and Australia can register through my\nNew Zealand distributor. See the registration form (filename\n`registration.form'' for more details.\n\n   The registration key will work with all minor revisions of `AmiQWK\nII''.  (i.e. all versions up to, but not including, 3.0) When a new\nmajor revision becomes available, registered `AmiQWK II'' users will be\neligible for reduced cost updates.\n\n   Most registrations are processed within 2 working days. If a network\naddress is given, I will send your registration key via email in\naddition to postal mail.\n\n   Group registrations and site licenses are available, Contact the\nauthor for details. See @{"Technical Support" Link "Technical Support"}.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Warranty" "AmiQWK.guide/Warranty"\n@Next "Copyright"\n@Prev "Registration"\n@Toc "Legal Stuff"\n\nWarranty\n========\n\n   THIS PRODUCT IS PROVIDED "AS-IS" WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND. ALL\nRISKS AND DAMAGES, INCIDENTAL OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM THE USE,\nMISUSE, OR INABILITY TO USE THIS PROGRAM ARE ENTIRELY THE\nRESPONSIBILITY OF THE USER.  THE AUTHOR DOES NOT MAKE ANY GUARANTEES OR\nREPRESENTATIONS REGARDING THE CORRECTNESS, RELIABILITY, ACCURACY,\nCURRENTNESS, ETC. OF THIS PROGRAM. THE AUTHOR WILL NOT ACCEPT\nRESPONSIBILITY FOR ANY DAMAGE OR LOSSES RESULTING FORM THE USE, MISUSE,\nOR INABILITY TO USE THIS PRODUCT.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Copyright" "AmiQWK.guide/Copyright"\n@Next "Distribution"\n@Prev "Warranty"\n@Toc "Legal Stuff"\n\nCopyright\n=========\n\n   The `AmiQWK II'' software and documentation are copyright (C)\n1992,1993 Jim Dawson. All rights reserved.\n\n   `AmigaGuide'', `AmigaGuide.info'', `amigaguide.library'' (C) Copyright\n1992 Commodore-Amiga, Inc. All Rights Reserved. Reproduced and\ndistributed under license from Commodore.\n\n   `ReqTools'' and `reqtools.library'' are Copyright (C) Nico François.\n\n   All product names used in this document are trademarks of their\nrespective owners.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Distribution" "AmiQWK.guide/Distribution"\n@Prev "Copyright"\n@Toc "Legal Stuff"\n\nDistribution\n============\n\n   Regardless of the method of distribution, `AmiQWK II'' is NOT and\nnever has been public domain.\n\n   `AmiQWK II'' may be distributed freely, providing the following\ncriteria are met:\n\n   * Distribution is limited to the original shareware distribution\n     only. None of the files in the `AmiQWK II'' distribution archive\n     may be modified or omitted.\n\n   * No material gain may be realized from the distribution of `AmiQWK\n     II'', other than a small amount to cover distribution materials.\n\n   * `AmiQWK II'' may be included in freely distributable software\n     libraries, including the Fred Fish collection.\n\n   * `AmiQWK II'' may not be bundled with any hardware or software\n     product without prior written consent from Jim Dawson.\n\n   * Registration numbers for `AmiQWK'', and the key file that `AmiQWK\n     II'' generates from them (`AmiQWK.KEY'') may not be distributed.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "System Requirements" "AmiQWK.guide/System Requirements"\n@Next "Installation and Quick Setup"\n@Prev "Legal Stuff"\n@Toc "Main"\n\nSystem Requirements\n*******************\n\n\n @{" Hardware Requirements " Link "Hardware Requirements"} \n @{" Software Requirements " Link "Software Requirements"} \n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Hardware Requirements" "AmiQWK.guide/Hardware Requirements"\n@Next "Software Requirements"\n@Toc "System Requirements"\n\nHardware Requirements\n=====================\n\n   `AmiQWK'' requires the following hardware:\n\n   * Amiga computer.\n\n   * 512KB RAM. (1MB or more recommended.)\n\n   * Floppy drive (Second floppy drive or hard drive highly\n     recommended.)\n\n   * Printer. (if you want to print messages.)\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Software Requirements" "AmiQWK.guide/Software Requirements"\n@Prev "Hardware Requirements"\n@Toc "System Requirements"\n\nSoftware Requirements\n=====================\n\n   `AmiQWK'' requires the following software:\n\n   * Workbench 2.04 (V37) or higher.\n\n   * A text editor that can save ASCII text without any formatting\n     info. (ED and MEMACS that come with Workbench are fine.)\n\n   * Any archivers you plan to use. (`ZIP'', `LHA/LHArc'', `ZOO'', and\n     `ARC'' are currently supported)\n\n   * `reqtools.library''. (included with `AmiQWK II'' distribution\n     archive.)\n\n   * `amigaguide.library''. (included with `AmiQWK II'' distribution\n     archive, only required for online help.)\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Installation and Quick Setup" "AmiQWK.guide/Installation and Quick Setup"\n@Next "Using AmiQWK"\n@Prev "System Requirements"\n@Toc "Main"\n\nInstallation and Quick Setup\n****************************\n\n   To install `AmiQWK'', just double-click on the ''Install_AQ2'' icon.\nThis will copy the `reqtools.library'' and `amigaguide.library'' to your\n`libs:'' directory. If newer versions of either library are already\npresent, the library will not be copied.\n\n   Once the libraries have been installed, run `AmiQWK'' by\ndouble-clicking on it''s icon or by typing `AmiQWK'' from the `CLI''.\n\n   Once `AmiQWK'' is running, you will need to configure it to work on\nyour system. Click on the gadget labeled ''Config `AmiQWK''...'' or type\n''C'' (1) to bring up the configuration editor window.\n\n   The configuration editor window has 9 gadgets, the first 6 of which\nwhen clicked on will bring up a configuration sub-window. The remaining\n3 gadgets are used to save the configuration to disk, use the\nconfiguration without saving, and to cancel changes you have made to\nthe configuration. The following describes only the most basic\nconfiguration options needed to get `AmiQWK'' running, See\n@{"Configuration" Link "Configuration"}, for a more detailed description of all of the\nconfiguration options.\n\n`Operation''\n     Change the ''Editor'' string gadget to whatever editor you use.\n     About the only restrictions on the editor are that it must save\n     text in plain ASCII format (with no formatting codes) and that it\n     does not detach itself from the CLI process that spawned it. Here\n     are some examples:\n\n    `CygnusEd''\n          `c:ed -sticky'' (See @{"CygnusEd Notes" Link "CygnusEd Notes"}.)\n\n    `TurboText''\n          `c:ttx WAIT''\n\n    `Ed (AmigaDos)''\n          `c:ed''\n\n    `MEmacs (AmigaDos)''\n          `sys:tools/memacs''\n\n    `GoldEd''\n          `c:ed sticky''\n\n     See @{"Operation window" Link "Operation window"}, for more information.\n\n`Replies''\n     The default configuration for replies should not need to be\n     changed for most applications. See @{"Replies window" Link "Replies window"}, for more\n     information.\n\n`Paths''\n     In the Paths window you tell `AmiQWK'' where it should look for and\n     store certain files.\n\n    `Packet Dir''\n          This is where `AmiQWK'' should look for mail packets. This is\n          normally the same directory your term program uses for\n          downloads.\n\n    `Reply Dir''\n          This is where `AmiQWK'' stores replies. This is normally the\n          same directory your term program uses for uploads.\n\n    `Work Dir''\n          This is where `AmiQWK'' creates its work directory. If you\n          have enough memory, you might want to change this to `RAM:''\n          for best performance. If you leave it blank `AmiQWK'' will\n          create the work directory in the `AmiQWK'' program directory.\n\n     See @{"Paths window" Link "Paths window"}, for more information.\n\n`Archivers''\n     You will probably not need to change anything in the Archivers\n     window.  See @{"Archivers window" Link "Archivers window"}, for more information.\n\n`Graphics''\n     You will probably not need to make changes in the Graphics window\n     to get `AmiQWK'' to work. See @{"Graphics window" Link "Graphics window"}, for more information.\n\n`Register''\n     Enter your name in the gadget labeled ''Your Name''. If you have\n     registered, enter your registration key number in the\n     ''Registration Key'' gadget, otherwise leave it blank. See\n     @{"Register window" Link "Register window"}, for more information.\n\n   Once you have your configuration set, click on the ''Save'' gadget of\nthe ''Config'' window to save it to disk. If you think you messed up\nsomewhere and want to start again from scratch, click on ''Cancel''. If\nyou want to try out your configuration before you save it, click on\n''Use''.\n\n   ---------- Footnotes ----------\n\n   (1)  Most actions in `AmiQWK'' can be accomplished by using either\nthe mouse or the keyboard. Whenever a keyboard shortcut exists it will\nusually have the shortcut key underlined.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Using AmiQWK" "AmiQWK.guide/Using AmiQWK"\n@Next "Reading and Writing Messages"\n@Prev "Installation and Quick Setup"\n@Toc "Main"\n\nUsing AmiQWK\n************\n\n\n @{" Starting AmiQWK " Link "Starting AmiQWK"} \n @{" Select Packet window " Link "Select Packet window"} \n @{" Registered To " Link "Registered To"} \n @{" Open Packet... " Link "Open Packet..."} \n @{" Config AmiQWK... " Link "Config AmiQWK..."} \n @{" About AmiQWK... " Link "About AmiQWK..."} \n @{" Rename Packet... " Link "Rename Packet..."} \n @{" HELP!!!... " Link "HELP!!!..."} \n @{" Delete Packet... " Link "Delete Packet..."} \n @{" Quit and Exit... " Link "Quit and Exit..."} \n @{" Opening a packet " Link "Opening a packet"} \n @{" Select item to read window " Link "Select item to read window"} \n @{" Text Viewer " Link "Text Viewer"} \n @{" ANSI Viewer " Link "ANSI Viewer"} \n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Starting AmiQWK" "AmiQWK.guide/Starting AmiQWK"\n@Next "Select Packet window"\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nStarting AmiQWK\n===============\n\n   Before you use `AmiQWK'', You will need to download a QWKMail packet\nfrom your favorite BBS. Consult the online documentation for the BBS\nQWK mailer (or, if all else fails, the system operator) for\ninstructions on how to download a QWKMail packet. `AmiQWK'' will run\nwithout any packets, but you won''t be able to do much with it.\n\n     *TIP:* Do *NOT* use Zmodem Resume mode when downloading QWKMail\n     packets.\n     \n     Since most BBSes use the same filename for packets every time, if you\n     already have a QWK packet in your downloads directory your term program will\n     think you are trying to resume an aborted transfer, and the new packet will\n     be appended to the old one instead of creating a new file.\n\n   Once you have a QWKMail packet, start `AmiQWK'' by double clicking\nthe `AmiQWK'' icon or typing ''`AmiQWK'''' from the CLI. `AmiQWK'' will open\na custom, public screen (named ''AMIQWK'') with a window titled ''Select\nPacket''. (If you haven''t registered yet, a window will appear reminding\nyou to register before the ''Select Packet'' window (See\n@{"Select Packet window" Link "Select Packet window"}.) appears, as well as several other places in the\nprogram.) See @{"Registration" Link "Registration"}, for more details about registering `AmiQWK''.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Select Packet window" "AmiQWK.guide/Select Packet window"\n@Next "Registered To"\n@Prev "Starting AmiQWK"\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nSelect Packet window\n====================\n\n   From the Select Packet window, you can open a QWKMail packet for\nreading, delete or rename packets, enter the configuration editor, and\nget information about the program. The Select Packet window has the\nfollowing gadgets:\n\n`Registered To''\n     Name of the registered user. See @{"Registered To" Link "Registered To"}.\n\n`Open Packet...''\n     Open a packet for reading. See @{"Open Packet..." Link "Open Packet..."}.\n\n`Config AmiQWK...''\n     Open the configuration editor. See @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n`About AmiQWK...''\n     Displays version, and copyright information, as well as how to get\n     technical support and where to send your registration fees. See\n     @{"About AmiQWK..." Link "About AmiQWK..."}.\n\n`Rename Packet...''\n     Rename or move a packet. See @{"Rename Packet..." Link "Rename Packet..."}.\n\n`HELP!!!...''\n     Get online help. See @{"HELP!!!..." Link "HELP!!!..."}.\n\n`Delete Packet...''\n     Delete a packet. See @{"Delete Packet..." Link "Delete Packet..."}.\n\n`Quit and Exit...''\n     Exit program. See @{"Quit and Exit..." Link "Quit and Exit..."}.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Registered To" "AmiQWK.guide/Registered To"\n@Next "Open Packet..."\n@Prev "Select Packet window"\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nRegistered To\n=============\n\n   This read only text gadget displays the name of the person who has\nregistered this copy of `AmiQWK'', or ''** UNREGISTERED EVALUATION COPY\n**'' if `AmiQWK'' cannot process the registration key. This gadget is\nread-only. See @{"Registration" Link "Registration"}, for more information on registering\n`AmiQWK''.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Open Packet..." "AmiQWK.guide/Open Packet..."\n@Next "Config AmiQWK..."\n@Prev "Registered To"\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nOpen Packet...\n==============\n\n   When the ''Open Packet...'' gadget is selected, `AmiQWK'' opens a file\nrequester for you to select the mail packet to be opened. See\n@{"Opening a packet" Link "Opening a packet"}, for more information.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Config AmiQWK..." "AmiQWK.guide/Config AmiQWK..."\n@Next "About AmiQWK..."\n@Prev "Open Packet..."\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nConfig AmiQWK...\n================\n\n   Clicking on this gadget opens the configuration editor. See\n@{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}, for more information.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "About AmiQWK..." "AmiQWK.guide/About AmiQWK..."\n@Next "Rename Packet..."\n@Prev "Config AmiQWK..."\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nAbout AmiQWK...\n===============\n\n   This gadget opens a window containing version and copyright\ninformation, and where to send registrations, bug reports, suggestions,\netc.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Rename Packet..." "AmiQWK.guide/Rename Packet..."\n@Next "HELP!!!..."\n@Prev "About AmiQWK..."\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nRename Packet...\n================\n\n   This gadget opens a file requester to select a file to be renamed.\nOnce a file is selected, another file requester is opened to select the\nnew file name.\n\n   A file can be moved by using the rename function. Just enter a\ndifferent path in the ''Rename File As...'' file requester. You can even\n''Rename'' a file to another volume.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "HELP!!!..." "AmiQWK.guide/HELP!!!..."\n@Next "Delete Packet..."\n@Prev "Rename Packet..."\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nHELP!!!...\n==========\n\n   This gadget brings up `AmigaGuide'' help for the Packet Selection\nwindow. You can get help for most windows by pressing the ''HELP'' key.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Delete Packet..." "AmiQWK.guide/Delete Packet..."\n@Next "Quit and Exit..."\n@Prev "HELP!!!..."\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nDelete Packet...\n================\n\n   This gadget opens a file requester to select a file to be deleted.\nOnce a file is selected another requester will appear to ask for\nconfirmation before deleting the file.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Quit and Exit..." "AmiQWK.guide/Quit and Exit..."\n@Next "Opening a packet"\n@Prev "Delete Packet..."\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nQuit and Exit...\n================\n\n   Click on this gadget to exit the program. Unless ''Ask Before Exit''\nin the Operations config window (See @{"Operation window" Link "Operation window"}.) is turned off\nyou will be asked for confirmation before `AmiQWK'' closes.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Opening a packet" "AmiQWK.guide/Opening a packet"\n@Next "Select item to read window"\n@Prev "Quit and Exit..."\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nOpening a packet\n================\n\n   When ''Open Packet...'' is selected from the ''Select Packet'' window, a\nfile requester will appear to select the packet to open. Once a packet\nis selected, `AmiQWK'' closes the ''Select Packet'' window, extracts the\nmail packet into the work directory (automatically determining what\nmethod was used to compress the packet) as well as the reply packet, if\nit exists. The extraction procedure takes place in a CLI window that\nappears on the `AmiQWK'' screen. (assuming you haven''t changed the spawn\nwindow specifications in the Operations configuration window. (See\n@{"Operation window" Link "Operation window"}.))\n\n   Once the packet is extracted, `AmiQWK'' will scan the packet,\nbuilding lists of conferences and messages. This may take a while if\nyou are reading a large packet. If index files are not present in the\npacket or if the number of messages in the messages.dat file does not\nmatch the number of message indexes, `AmiQWK'' will attempt to generate\nthe indexes itself. (note that the indexes are not saved back to the\npacket, as `AmiQWK'' can generate the indexes very quickly.) Once the\npacket has been processed, the ''Select item to read'' window will open.\nSee @{"Select item to read window" Link "Select item to read window"}.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Select item to read window" "AmiQWK.guide/Select item to read window"\n@Next "Text Viewer"\n@Prev "Opening a packet"\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nSelect item to read window\n==========================\n\n   This window gives important information about the packet and the BBS\nthat it came from, and has several gadgets for selecting various parts\nof the mail packet for reading. (note that some of the gadgets may be\ndisabled if the corresponding item is not available in the packet.)\nThere is also a menu which can be used to generate requests to add,\ndrop, or reset conferences (assuming that the QWK packer that generated\nthe packet can process these requests, otherwise the menu items will be\ndisabled.)\n\n   The following information about the packet and the BBS it came from\nis displayed on the left part of the window.\n\n`Packet:''\n     The filename of the packet (not including path).\n\n`BBS:''\n     The name of the BBS that the packet came from.\n\n`Location:''\n     The location of the BBS.\n\n`Sysop:''\n     The name of the system operator of the BBS.\n\n`Phone:''\n     Primary phone number of the BBS.\n\n`Packet ID:''\n     Packet identifier for generating reply packets.\n\n`Time:''\n     Time that the packet was created.\n\n`Date:''\n     Date that the packet was created.\n\n`User Name:''\n     Name of the person who downloaded the packet.\n\n   And on the right are the following gadgets. Note that some of them\nmay be disabled if they are not available.\n\n`''Hello'' File...''\n     Displays welcome text using the internal text viewer. (See\n     @{"Text Viewer" Link "Text Viewer"}.) Usually is the login and welcome screen from the\n     BBS, but may contain other information.\n\n`Bulletins...''\n     Display bulletin file(s) using the internal text viewer. (See\n     @{"Text Viewer" Link "Text Viewer"}.) A file requester will be opened to select which file\n     to read. The bulletin files are named ''BLT-x.y'' where ''x'' is the\n     conference the bulletin came from and ''y'' is the actual number of\n     the bulletin. (i.e. BLT-3.1 indicates that it is the first\n     bulletin in conference 3.) Bulletins are usually only included if\n     they have been updated since the last time you called the BBS.\n     Select ''Cancel'' on the file requester to return to the ''Select\n     item to read'' window.\n\n`News...''\n     Display the News file using the internal text viewer. (See\n     @{"Text Viewer" Link "Text Viewer"}.) This file can contain anything the sysop chooses.\n     Usually this file, like bulletins, is only included if it has been\n     updated since the last mail packet was downloaded.\n\n`Read Mail...''\n     Read messages. (See @{"Reading Messages" Link "Reading Messages"}.)\n\n`New Files...''\n     Displays a list of files recently posted to the BBS using the\n     internal text viewer. (See @{"Text Viewer" Link "Text Viewer"}.)\n\n`''Goodbye'' Text''\n     Displays logout text using the internal text viewer. (See\n     @{"Text Viewer" Link "Text Viewer"}.) Usually this is the text that the BBS displays to\n     users logging off of the system, however it may contain other\n     information.\n\n`Close Packet...''\n     Closes the packet currently being read and returns to the Select\n     Packet window. (See @{"Select Packet window" Link "Select Packet window"}.) If there are any\n     replies, they will be packed into a reply packet for uploading.\n\n`Exit AmiQWK...''\n     Exit program. Unless ''Ask Before Exit'' in the Operations config\n     window is turned off (See @{"Operation window" Link "Operation window"}.) you will be asked for\n     confirmation before `AmiQWK'' closes. If there are any replies,\n     they will be packed into a reply packet for uploading.\n\n   And finally, there is a menu named ''Conference''. It will contain the\nfollowing menu items, however these items may be disabled if the QWK\npacker cannot handle these requests.\n\n`Add Conference''\n     Add a conference to your list of active conferences. A window will\n     appear listing all conferences that don''t have any messages in\n     them (if they have messages, you must already be subscribed to\n     that conference.) once a conference is selected, you will be asked\n     for confirmation before the add conference request is generated.\n\n`Drop Conference''\n     Remove a conference from your list of active conferences. A window\n     will appear listing all conferences. Once a conference is\n     selected, you will be asked for confirmation before the drop\n     conference request is generated.\n\n`Reset Pointer''\n     Reset last read pointer within a conference. A window will appear\n     listing all conferences. Once a conference is selected you will be\n     asked to enter a new last read message number. Once a last read\n     message number is selected, you will be asked for confirmation\n     before the reset request is generated.  See @{"Advanced QWK features" Link "Advanced QWK features"},\nfor more details on Adding, Dropping, and Reseting conferences.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Text Viewer" "AmiQWK.guide/Text Viewer"\n@Next "ANSI Viewer"\n@Prev "Select item to read window"\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nText Viewer\n===========\n\n   `AmiQWK'' has a built-in text viewer for reading text files included\nin the QWKMail packet, such as the Hello, Goodbye, News, Bulletins, and\nrecent uploads files. ANSI display sequences are filtered out before\nthe text is displayed. (Note that if the text uses ANSI cursor\npositioning codes, the text may be jumbled. However most ANSI text uses\nonly color codes.)\n\n   The following keys control movement in the text and message viewers:\n\nKeypad 8, Up Arrow\n     Scroll up one line of text.\n\nKeypad 2, Down Arrow\n     Scroll down one line of text.\n\nKeypad 9\n     Scroll up one page of text.\n\nKeypad 3 Space Bar\n     Scroll down one page of text.\n\nKeypad 7\n     Move to start of text.\n\nKeypad 1\n     Move to end of text.\n\n   In addition to the above keys, you can move through the current text\nby using the scroll bar at the bottom of the screen. (unless the scroll\nbar is disabled in the Operations configuration window. (See\n@{"Operation window" Link "Operation window"}.)\n\n   The following keys (and their corresponding menu items in the\n''Commands'' menu) also perform functions:\n\nQ\n     Quit reading text and return to the ''Select item to read'' window.\n     See @{"Select item to read window" Link "Select item to read window"}.\n\nP\n     Print the current text. See @{"Printing Text and Messages" Link "Printing Text and Messages"}.\n\nS\n     Save the current text. See @{"Saving Text and Messages" Link "Saving Text and Messages"}.\n\nA\n     ANSI display of the current message. See @{"ANSI Viewer" Link "ANSI Viewer"}.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "ANSI Viewer" "AmiQWK.guide/ANSI Viewer"\n@Prev "Text Viewer"\n@Toc "Using AmiQWK"\n\nANSI Viewer\n===========\n\n   The ANSI viewer will display the current message or text on a virtual\nterminal, as if it were being viewed on a terminal screen while\nconnected to a BBS. ANSI display sequences allow for changing the color\nof the text and background, cursor position, etc.\n\n   The ANSI viewer will correctly display most IBM-PC ANSI sequences.\nANSI is displayed on it''s own custom 16 color screen, the palette is\nhard-coded to match the default IBM-PC display.\n\n   The ANSI viewer uses the same font as is used for normal message\ntext.\n\n   When in the ANSI viewer the following functions are available:\n\nP\n     Pause the display. Display will be suspended until another key is\n     pressed.\n\nQ\n     Quit ANSI display and return to message or text reader.\n\nA\n     Display ANSI text again.\n\n0-9\n     Change display speed. 0 is fastest (no delay), 9 is slowest (max.\n     delay).\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Reading and Writing Messages" "AmiQWK.guide/Reading and Writing Messages"\n@Next "Configuration"\n@Prev "Using AmiQWK"\n@Toc "Main"\n\nReading and Writing Messages\n****************************\n\n\n @{" Reading Messages " Link "Reading Messages"} \n @{" Entering and re-editing new messages " Link "Entering and re-editing new messages"} \n @{" Writing messages and BBS Etiquette " Link "Writing messages and BBS Etiquette"} \n @{" Conference Window " Link "Conference Window"} \n @{" Message Index window " Link "Message Index window"} \n @{" Saving Text and Messages " Link "Saving Text and Messages"} \n @{" Printing Text and Messages " Link "Printing Text and Messages"} \n @{" New Tagline window " Link "New Tagline window"} \n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Reading Messages" "AmiQWK.guide/Reading Messages"\n@Next "Entering and re-editing new messages"\n@Toc "Reading and Writing Messages"\n\nReading Messages\n================\n\n   When ''Read Mail...'' is selected from the ''Select item to read''\nwindow, (See @{"Select item to read window" Link "Select item to read window"}.) the ''Select item to read''\nwindow is closed and you will enter the message reader.\n\n   When you first enter the message reader, the ''Select Conference''\nwindow (See @{"Conference Window" Link "Conference Window"}.) will appear for you to select the\nconference you want to start reading from. It will default to the first\nconference with messages other than the ''Replies'' conference. When you\nhave selected a conference, `AmiQWK'' will start displaying messages.\n\n   The message reader is divided into two sections. The top section\ncontains header information and several gadgets, and the bottom section\ncontains the message text.\n\n   The following header information is present in the header at the top\nof the screen:\n\nFrom:\n     Sender of the message.\n\nTo:\n     Person the message is addressed to.\n\nSubj:\n     Subject of the message.\n\nDate:\n     Date the message was entered.\n\nTime:\n     Time the message was entered.\n\nConference:\n     Name and number of the conference the message is in.\n\nMessage:\n     Message number, Number of this message out of the total available\n     in this conference, and the total number of messages in this\n     conference.  this packet.\n\nReply To:\n     Number of the message that this message is a reply to. (or blank\n     if it isn''t a reply to another message.)\n\nPRIVATE\n     If displayed, this message is tagged as being Private, usually\n     readable only by the sender, receiver, and system operator.\n\nREAD\n     If displayed, this message has already been read.\n\nPERSONAL\n     If displayed, this message is addressed to you personally.\n\n   In addition, there are six gadgets in the upper right of the screen.\n`Index''\n     Opens the Message Index window, to select a message to read. See\n     @{"Message Index window" Link "Message Index window"}.\n\n`Conf.''\n     Opens the Conference window, to select which conference to read.\n     See @{"Conference Window" Link "Conference Window"}.\n\n`Reply''\n     Reply to the current message. See\n     @{"Entering and re-editing new messages" Link "Entering and re-editing new messages"}.\n\n`Help''\n     Call up the `AmigaGuide'' help for the message viewer.\n\n`<<''\n     Read the previous message.\n\n`>>''\n     Read the next message.\n\n   At the bottom of the screen is the message text. There is a scroll\nbar at the bottom (unless the scroll bar is disabled in the Operations\nconfiguration window.) which can be used to move through the message\nusing the mouse. The following keys can also be used to move through\nthe message.\n\nKeypad 8, Up Arrow\n     Scroll up one line of message.\n\nKeypad 2, Down Arrow\n     Scroll down one line of message.\n\nKeypad 9\n     Scroll up one page of message.\n\nKeypad 3, Space Bar\n     Scroll down one page of message.\n\nKeypad 7\n     Move to start of message.\n\nKeypad 1\n     Move to end of message.\n\nKeypad 4, Left Arrow\n     Read the previous message in the current conference.\n\nKeypad 6, Right Arrow, RETURN\n     Read the next message in the current conference.\n\nSPACE BAR\n     Move forward 1 page. If the end of the message is passed the next\n     message is read.\n\n   The following keys also perform functions:\n\nA\n     ANSI display of the current message. See @{"ANSI Viewer" Link "ANSI Viewer"}.\n\nC\n     Open Conference window to select a new conference. See\n     @{"Conference Window" Link "Conference Window"}.\n\nE\n     Re-edit Reply. (only works in ''Replies'' conference.) See\n     @{"Entering and re-editing new messages" Link "Entering and re-editing new messages"}.\n\nI\n     Open Message Index window to select which message to read in the\n     current conference. See @{"Message Index window" Link "Message Index window"}.\n\nK\n     Kill (delete) the current message. (Only works in ''Replies''\n     conference.)\n\nN\n     Enter a new message. See @{"Entering and re-editing new messages" Link "Entering and re-editing new messages"}.\n\nP\n     Print the current message. See @{"Printing Text and Messages" Link "Printing Text and Messages"}.\n\nQ, ESC\n     Exit message reader and return to the ''Select item to read''\n     window. See @{"Select item to read window" Link "Select item to read window"}.\n\nR\n     Reply to the current message. See\n     @{"Entering and re-editing new messages" Link "Entering and re-editing new messages"}.\n\nS\n     Save the current message to disk. See @{"Saving Text and Messages" Link "Saving Text and Messages"}.\n\nT\n     Add a new tagline to the tagline file. See @{"New Tagline window" Link "New Tagline window"}.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Entering and re-editing new messages" "AmiQWK.guide/Entering and re-editing new messages"\n@Next "Writing messages and BBS Etiquette"\n@Prev "Reading Messages"\n@Toc "Reading and Writing Messages"\n\nEntering and re-editing new messages\n====================================\n\n   When you select ''Reply'' or ''New message'' when reading messages, the\n''New Message'' window is opened for information about the message. (such\nas who it is to, subject, etc.) and your editor is opened for you to\nenter your your message. (Note that you can alter the order the order\nin which the new message window is opened. You can configure `AmiQWK''\nto edit the message first then open the new message window, Open the\nnew message window then edit the message, or open the new message\nwindow both before and after the message is edited (the default). See\n@{"Replies window" Link "Replies window"}, for more information.)\n\n   If you are replying to an existing message, the original message\nwill be quoted in the new message text, with the quote prefix you\nselected in the Replies configuration window prefixed to each line. See\n@{"Replies window" Link "Replies window"}.  When re-editing messages the text will be sent to the\neditor as-is.\n\n`To''\n     This is the person the message is addressed to. If you are\n     replying to an existing message, this will default to the sender\n     of the message you are replying to. If it is a new message, this\n     field will be blank. This field is limited to 25 characters.\n\n`From''\n     This is normally your name. You can change this field, however\n     most QWKMail systems will not allow messages to be posted unless\n     this field matches your name. This field is limited to 25\n     characters due to constraints of the QWKMail format.\n\n`Subject''\n     This is the subject of the message. If you are replying to an\n     existing message this field will default to the subject of the\n     original message.  (with an optional ''RE:'' prefix, provided that\n     ''RE: prefix for reply subject'' is turned on in the ''Replies''\n     configuration window and the subject doesn''t already have a ''RE:''\n     prefix.) Otherwise this field is left blank so you can enter\n     whatever you want here. This field is limited to 25 characters due\n     to constraints of the QWKMail format.\n\n`Conference''\n     This is the conference you want the message to be posted to. It\n     defaults to the conference you are currently reading messages in.\n     You can enter the conference number in the integer string gadget,\n     or you can click on the ''Conference'' button and the Conference\n     window will appear for you to select a conference. (See\n     @{"Conference Window" Link "Conference Window"}.) The name of the conference will appear to the\n     right of the conference number gadget. `AmiQWK'' will check the\n     selected conference number against a list of conferences to make\n     sure you have selected a valid conference.\n\n`Public Status''\n     This is a cycle gadget that can be either set to ''Public'' or\n     ''Private''. A ''public'' message will be visible to everyone, while a\n     ''Private'' message is usually only visible to the sender, the\n     person who it is addressed to, and the system operator. Note that\n     not all conferences support private messages.\n\n`Return Receipt''\n     Tells the BBS to generate a return receipt message when this\n     message is received. This function is available only if it is\n     supported by the QWK mailer. Note that asking for a ''Return\n     Receipt'' will prefix the Subject field with ''RRR''.\n\n`Tagline''\n     This is a short one-line message that is appended to the end of\n     your message. You can select a tagline from the list gadget, or\n     type one into the string gadget below it. (If you enter a new\n     tagline, it will be appended to your tagline file for future use.)\n     Taglines are disabled when re-editing messages.\n\n`Select Another Tagline''\n     If you don''t like the tagline that `AmiQWK'' has selected for you,\n     click on this gadget and a new one will be selected.\n\n`OK''\n     Accept the current settings.\n\n`Cancel''\n     Cancel the new message.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Writing messages and BBS Etiquette" "AmiQWK.guide/Writing messages and BBS Etiquette"\n@Next "Conference Window"\n@Prev "Entering and re-editing new messages"\n@Toc "Reading and Writing Messages"\n\nWriting messages and BBS Etiquette\n==================================\n\n   When writing a message, Keep in mind that others will have to read\nit.  There are certain things that you can do to\n\n   * Avoid typing in all caps. Messages that are in all caps can be\n     difficult to read on some monitors.\n\n   * When entering a reply, quote only the relevant information.\n\n   * Try to keep your paragraphs short, and insert a blank line between\n     paragraphs.\n\n   * Try to keep on the subject and don''t post messages to areas where\n     they don''t belong.\n\n   Also remember that many BBSes are connected to BBS networks such as\nFidonet and Usenet. Additional rules may apply to network message\nareas, such as high and low ASCII characters (ASCII 0-31 and 128-255)\nand ANSI escape sequences may not be permitted, etc. If you are in\ndoubt as to what is and isn''t permitted in a certain conference, ask\nthe system operator or the network conference moderator for guidelines.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Conference Window" "AmiQWK.guide/Conference Window"\n@Next "Message Index window"\n@Prev "Writing messages and BBS Etiquette"\n@Toc "Reading and Writing Messages"\n\nConference Window\n=================\n\n   The Conference window is used to select a conference. It consists of\na list gadget listing all available conferences, and an OK and a cancel\ngadget.\n\n`Conference List''\n     Lists all available conferences by name and number, with the total\n     number of messages in each conference. You can select a conference\n     by double-clicking on the desired line of the conference or by\n     selecting a conference and clicking on the ''OK'' gadget.\n\n`OK''\n     Accept the currently selected conference and close the Conference\n     window.\n\n`Cancel''\n     Closes the Conference window and discards any conference selection.\n\n   You can also control the Conference window with the keyboard.\n\nC, Q, ESC\n     Closes the Conference window and discards any conference selection.\n\nO, ENTER\n     Accept the currently selected conference and close the Conference\n     window.\n\nKeypad 8, Up Arrow\n     Scroll up through the conference list.\n\nKeypad 2, Down Arrow\n     Scroll down through the conference list.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Message Index window" "AmiQWK.guide/Message Index window"\n@Next "Saving Text and Messages"\n@Prev "Conference Window"\n@Toc "Reading and Writing Messages"\n\nMessage Index window\n====================\n\n   The Message Index window  is used to select a message from within a\nconference. It consists of a list gadget listing all available messages,\nand an OK and a cancel gadget. The operation of the Message Index\nwindow is nearly identical to the Conference window.\n\n`message List''\n     Lists all available messages, by the To, From, Subject fields. The\n     following flags are present as well:\n\n    `*''\n          The message is addressed to you personally.\n\n    `R''\n          This message has been read.\n\n    `P''\n          This message is marked as being Private.\n\n     You can select a message by double-clicking on the desired line of\n     the message list, or by picking the desired conference with either\n     the mouse or keyboard and then clicking on the ''OK'' gadget.\n\n`OK''\n     Accept the currently selected message and close the Message Index\n     window.\n\n`Cancel''\n     Closes the Message Index window and discards any message selection.\n\n   You can also control the message window with the keyboard.\n\nC, Q, ESC\n     Closes the Message Index window and discards any message selection.\n\nO, ENTER\n     Accept the currently selected message and close the Message Index\n     window.\n\nKeypad 8, Up Arrow\n     Scroll up through the message list.\n\nKeypad 2, Down Arrow\n     Scroll down through the message list.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Saving Text and Messages" "AmiQWK.guide/Saving Text and Messages"\n@Next "Printing Text and Messages"\n@Prev "Message Index window"\n@Toc "Reading and Writing Messages"\n\nSaving Text and Messages\n========================\n\n   When you select ''Save'', A file requester will open for you to select\nthe file name to save the text or message to. If the file you selected\nalready exists the new text or message will be appended to the end of\nthe existing file. If you are saving a message the contents of the\nheader will be saved as well.\n\n   The default path and/or directory for the saved text can be set in\nthe ''Paths'' window of the config editor. See @{"Paths window" Link "Paths window"}.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Printing Text and Messages" "AmiQWK.guide/Printing Text and Messages"\n@Next "New Tagline window"\n@Prev "Saving Text and Messages"\n@Toc "Reading and Writing Messages"\n\nPrinting Text and Messages\n==========================\n\n   When you select ''Print'', the current text or message will be sent to\nthe printer. If you are printing a message the contents of the header\nwill be printed as well.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "New Tagline window" "AmiQWK.guide/New Tagline window"\n@Prev "Printing Text and Messages"\n@Toc "Reading and Writing Messages"\n\nNew Tagline window\n==================\n\n   The New Tagline window is used to enter a new tagline into the\ntaglines list file. It consists of three gadgets:\n\n`Tagline''\n     A string gadget to enter the new tagline into.\n\n`OK''\n     Close the New Tagline window and save the new tagline to the\n     tagline list file.\n\n`Cancel''\n     Close the New Tagline window and discard the new tagline.\n\n   One idea for those of you who like to steal taglines. Use a text\nsnapping program such as PowerSnap to cut taglines out of messages\nusing the mouse and then paste them into the New Tagline window.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Configuration" "AmiQWK.guide/Configuration"\n@Next "Advanced QWK features"\n@Prev "Reading and Writing Messages"\n@Toc "Main"\n\nConfiguration\n*************\n\n\n @{" Main Configuration Window " Link "Main Configuration Window"} \n @{" Operation window " Link "Operation window"} \n @{" CygnusEd Notes " Link "CygnusEd Notes"} \n @{" Replies window " Link "Replies window"} \n @{" Paths window " Link "Paths window"} \n @{" Archivers window " Link "Archivers window"} \n @{" Graphics window " Link "Graphics window"} \n @{" Register window " Link "Register window"} \n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Main Configuration Window" "AmiQWK.guide/Main Configuration Window"\n@Next "Operation window"\n@Toc "Configuration"\n\nMain Configuration Window\n=========================\n\n   The configuration editor consists of a main configuration window and\nseveral sub-windows. The main Configuration window has the following\ngadgets:\n\n`Operation''\n     Opens the Operation configuration window. The Operation window is\n     used to configure the general operation of `AmiQWK''. See\n     @{"Operation window" Link "Operation window"}.\n\n`Replies''\n     Opens the Replies configuration window. The Relies window is used\n     to configure how replies and new messages are processed. See\n     @{"Replies window" Link "Replies window"}.\n\n`Paths''\n     Opens the Paths configuration window. The Paths window is used to\n     tell `AmiQWK'' where it should look for and store files. See\n     @{"Paths window" Link "Paths window"}.\n\n`Archivers''\n     Opens the Archivers configuration window. The Archivers window is\n     used to specify which compression programs are used and their\n     command line options.  See @{"Archivers window" Link "Archivers window"}.\n\n`Graphics''\n     Opens the Graphics configuration window. The Graphics window is\n     used to specify which screen mode the `AmiQWK'' screen should open\n     in, the font that should be used for text displays, the screen\n     palette, etc. See @{"Graphics window" Link "Graphics window"}.\n\n`Register''\n     Opens the Registration configuration window. The Register window\n     is used to enter your registration key when you register `AmiQWK''.\n     See @{"Register window" Link "Register window"}.\n\n`Save''\n     Accepts the current configuration and saves it to disk and return\n     to the ''Select Packet'' window. See @{"Select Packet window" Link "Select Packet window"}.\n\n`Use''\n     Accepts the current configuration and returns you to the ''Select\n     Packet'' window See @{"Select Packet window" Link "Select Packet window"}. but does not save the new\n     configuration to disk. If the new configuration is not saved to\n     disk before exiting the program the new configuration will be lost.\n\n`Cancel''\n     Discard all changes to the configuration and return to the ''Select\n     Packet'' window. See @{"Select Packet window" Link "Select Packet window"}.\n\n   The configuration is saved in a file named ''Config.AQ'' in the same\ndirectory that `AmiQWK'' is located in.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Operation window" "AmiQWK.guide/Operation window"\n@Next "CygnusEd Notes"\n@Prev "Main Configuration Window"\n@Toc "Configuration"\n\nOperation window\n================\n\n   The Operation window is used to configure the general operation of\n`AmiQWK''.\n\n`Scroll bar in text window''\n     If this checkbox gadget is selected, a scroll bar will appear at\n     the bottom of the text and message windows. This scroll bar can be\n     used to move around the displayed text using the mouse.\n\n`Tab Size''\n     This slider gadget specifies the number of spaces between tab\n     stops.\n\n`Ask before Exit''\n     If this checkbox is selected, `AmiQWK'' will ask for confirmation\n     before exiting. Otherwise it will exit without asking first.\n\n`After Read''\n     This cycle gadget is used to tell `AmiQWK'' what to do with a\n     packet after you read it. It can be set to:\n\n    `No Action''\n          Nothing is done to the packet after you read it.\n\n    `Rename Packet''\n          You will be presented with a file requester to change the\n          name of the packet after you read it.\n\n    `Delete Packet.''\n          You will be asked if you want to delete the packet after you\n          read it.\n\n    `Ask''\n          You will be presented with a requester asking if you want to\n          Rename, Delete, or take no action at all on the packet after\n          you have read it.\n\n`Packet Search Pattern Match''\n     This is the pattern that `AmiQWK'' should use when looking for mail\n     packets.  It should normally be something like ''#?.Q#?'' as packet\n     names normally end with ''.QWK'' and some term programs may change\n     the extension if a duplicate file is downloaded.\n\n`Editor''\n     This is the command line used to start your editor for editing\n     messages. Be sure to specify any command line switches you need\n     here as well. The file name will be appended to the end of this\n     string.\n\n     Keep in mind that your text editor must NOT detach itself from the\n     process that started it.\n\n     Here are some example editor strings for various editors.\n\n    `CygnusEd''\n          `c:ed -sticky'' (See @{"CygnusEd Notes" Link "CygnusEd Notes"}.)\n\n    `TurboText''\n          `c:ttx WAIT''\n\n    `Ed''\n          `c:ed''\n\n    `MEmacs''\n          `sys:tools/memacs''\n\n    `GoldEd''\n          `c:ed sticky''\n\n     If you are using `CygnusEd'', See @{"CygnusEd Notes" Link "CygnusEd Notes"}.\n\n`Workbench to front on edit''\n     If this checkbox is selected, `AmiQWK'' will pull the workbench\n     screen to front when the editor is started. This is useful if you\n     are using an editor that opens on the workbench screen but does\n     not pull it to the front for you, such as the ''ed'' program that\n     comes with Workbench.\n\n`Spawn Window''\n     This string gadget is used to set the console specifications for\n     the spawn window for extracting packets and packing replies. See\n     your AmigaDos manual for more information on console\n     specifications. If you want the spawn window to open on the\n     `AmiQWK'' screen, make sure that ''SCREENAMIQWK'' appears somewhere\n     on this line.\n\n`Requesters''\n     Allows you to choose between ASL and `ReqTools'' file, font, and\n     screenmode requesters. (note that `AmiQWK'' will use the `ReqTools''\n     screenmode requester if you are using version 37 of ASL.library,\n     because ASL.library v37 does not have a screenmode selector.)\n\n`Use''\n     Use the current settings and return to the main configuration\n     window. See @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n`Cancel''\n     Discard changes and return to the main configuration window. See\n     @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "CygnusEd Notes" "AmiQWK.guide/CygnusEd Notes"\n@Next "Replies window"\n@Prev "Operation window"\n@Toc "Configuration"\n\nCygnusEd Notes\n==============\n\n   If you are using `CygnusEd'' version 2.12 or earlier, you may have\ntrouble getting `CygnusEd'' to open without detaching itself from it''s\nparent process due to a bug in the `ED'' activator program. If you are\nusing `CygnusEd'' 2.12, there are three options open to you.\n\n   * You can upgrade to a newer version of `CygnusEd''.\n\n   * You can get a third party replacement for the ''`ED'''' activator\n     program.  There are at least two in the public domain. At least\n     one of them will be available from the `AmiQWK'' support BBS at all\n     times. See @{"Technical Support" Link "Technical Support"}.\n\n   * You can use the included `CygnusEd'' starter script. (filename\n     ''StartCed'', set the ''Editor'' gadget in the ''Operation'' window of\n     the config editor to ''`StartCed'''' if you use this script. You may\n     also need to specify the full path to the `StartCed'' script.)\n\n   For best results, you will probably want to ''hot-start'' `CygnusEd''\nfrom the `s:user-startup'' file. See your `CygnusEd'' program for details\non ''hot-starting''.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Replies window" "AmiQWK.guide/Replies window"\n@Next "Paths window"\n@Prev "CygnusEd Notes"\n@Toc "Configuration"\n\nReplies window\n==============\n\n`Edit Header''\n     This cycle gadget has three settings. It controls when the message\n     editor and the ''New Message'' window are opened.\n\n    `Before Edit''\n          Edit message header in the ''New Message'' window before\n          editing the message text.\n\n    `After Edit''\n          Edit message header in the ''New Message'' window after editing\n          the message text.\n\n    `Before and After Edit''\n          Edit message header in the ''New Message'' window both before\n          and after editing the message text.\n\n`Quote Prefix''\n     This string is prefixed before each line of quoted text when\n     replying to a message.\n\n`Header Info in replies''\n     If this checkbox is selected, the information in the message\n     header will be included in the message text of a reply. Has no\n     effect on new messages.  (i.e. not a reply to an existing message.)\n\n`RE: prefix for reply subject''\n     If this checkbox is selected, the subject field of the new message\n     will be prefixed with ''RE:'' unless it already has a ''RE:'' prefix.\n\n`Trunc/Wrap reply text''\n     Specifies if quoted text in a reply is to be truncated or word\n     wrapped.\n\n`Col.''\n     Specifies column that quoted text in a reply is to be truncated or\n     word wrapped at.\n\n`Use''\n     Use the current settings and return to the main configuration\n     window. See @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n`Cancel''\n     Discard changes and return to the main configuration window. See\n     @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Paths window" "AmiQWK.guide/Paths window"\n@Next "Archivers window"\n@Prev "Replies window"\n@Toc "Configuration"\n\nPaths window\n============\n\n   The paths window is used to tell `AmiQWK'' where it should look for\nand store files. To the right of each path string gadget is a\n''pickfile'' button, which will open a file requester to select the\ndesired path. You can either type the path into the string gadget or\nselect the path from the file requester.\n\n`Packet Directory''\n     Tells `AmiQWK'' where it should look for mail packets. This should\n     be set to the downloads directory your terminal program uses.\n\n`Reply Directory''\n     Tells `AmiQWK'' where it should store reply packets. This should be\n     set to the uploads directory your terminal program uses.\n\n`Work Directory''\n     Tells `AmiQWK'' where it should create it''s work directory. If you\n     have enough memory, this should be set to `RAM:'' for best\n     performance. If this field is left blank, `AmiQWK'' will create\n     it''s work directory in the directory in the same directory as the\n     `AmiQWK'' executable file.\n\n`Saved Text''\n     Default path and/or filename for saved text. You can enter a\n     complete path with filename, just a filename (the files will be\n     saved to the `AmiQWK'' directory unless you specify a path when you\n     save messages or text files) or just a path (you will need to make\n     sure that the path name ends with a '':'' or a ''/'', otherwise\n     `AmiQWK'' will think that the last part of the path is actually a\n     file name.)\n\n`Use''\n     Use the current settings and return to the main configuration\n     window. See @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n`Cancel''\n     Discard changes and return to the main configuration window. See\n     @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Archivers window" "AmiQWK.guide/Archivers window"\n@Next "Graphics window"\n@Prev "Paths window"\n@Toc "Configuration"\n\nArchivers window\n================\n\n   The Archivers window is used to specify the command line strings for\nthe file compression/extraction programs. Currently `AmiQWK'' supports\n`LHA/LHArc'', `ZIP'', `ZOO'', and `ARC'' compression. The filename and\ndestination directory will be appended to the command line strings.\n\n`Use''\n     Use the current settings and return to the main configuration\n     window. See @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n`Cancel''\n     Discard changes and return to the main configuration window. See\n     @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Graphics window" "AmiQWK.guide/Graphics window"\n@Next "Register window"\n@Prev "Archivers window"\n@Toc "Configuration"\n\nGraphics window\n===============\n\n   The Graphics window is used to specify the general appearance of\n`AmiQWK''.\n\n`Screen Mode''\n     The name of the screen mode that `AmiQWK'' opens on. (Read only,\n     use the ''Select Screen Mode'' gadget to select a screen mode.)\n\n`Width''\n     The width of the `AmiQWK'' screen in pixels. (Read only)\n\n`Height''\n     The height of the `AmiQWK'' screen in pixels. (Read only)\n\n`Select Screen Mode''\n     Select a screen mode for `AmiQWK'' using the ASL or `ReqTools''\n     screenmode requester. (If you are using version 37 of ASL.library,\n     the `ReqTools'' screenmode requester will be used.) You can also\n     specify the number of colors the `AmiQWK'' screen uses. (I\n     recommend using 4 colors. At this time `AmiQWK'' does not use more\n     than four colors except for the ANSI display screen, which is hard\n     coded to use a 16 color screen.)\n\n`Shanghai''\n     If this checkmark is selected, the `AmiQWK'' screen will be opened\n     in ''Shanghai'' mode. If this option is set, older applications that\n     would normaly open on the Workbench screen instead open on the\n     default public screen, which may or may not be the Workbench\n     screen.\n\n`PopPubScreen''\n     If this checkmark is selected, the `AmiQWK'' screen will be opened\n     in ''PopPubScreen'' mode. This will cause the AmiQWK screen to ''pop''\n     to the front whenever a window is opened on the AmiQWK screen.\n\n`Edit Palette''\n     Change the color palette `AmiQWK'' uses using the `ReqTools''\n     palette requester.\n\n`Select Font''\n     Select the font `AmiQWK'' uses for message text and the ANSI\n     display, using the ASL or `ReqTools'' font requester. The selected\n     font and size will appear to the right of the ''Select Font''\n     gadget. I recommend using an IBM-PC style font as many packets\n     will contain characters that only appear properly when viewed with\n     an IBM-PC style font.\n\n`Use''\n     Use the current settings and return to the main configuration\n     window. See @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n`Cancel''\n     Discard changes and return to the main configuration window. See\n     @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Register window" "AmiQWK.guide/Register window"\n@Prev "Graphics window"\n@Toc "Configuration"\n\nRegister window\n===============\n\n   The Register window is used generate a registration keyfile. When you\nregister you will be given a 16 digit registration number which should\nbe entered into this window.\n\n`Your Name''\n     Enter your name into this gadget. It must be entered exactly as it\n     was specified on your registration form as the registration key is\n     matched against this field.\n\n`Registration Key''\n     Enter your 16 digit registration number in this gadget.\n\n`Status''\n     Registration status. Will either be a registration serial number\n     or ''** UNREGISTERED EVALUATION COPY **'' if a valid registration\n     key is not present.\n\n`Use''\n     Save your registration key to disk and return to the main\n     configuration window. See @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n`Cancel''\n     Discard changes and return to the main configuration window. See\n     @{"Main Configuration Window" Link "Main Configuration Window"}.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Advanced QWK features" "AmiQWK.guide/Advanced QWK features"\n@Next "The Future of AmiQWK"\n@Prev "Configuration"\n@Toc "Main"\n\nAdvanced QWK features\n*********************\n\n   Some BBSes allow for adding, dropping, and resetting of conferences,\ndownloading files attached to messages, and other functions by sending\nmessages to the BBS via a QWKmail packet. If the BBS supports these\nfeatures a file named DOOR.ID will be included in the QWKMail packet\nthat will tell `AmiQWK'' what functions are available and how to process\na request.\n\n   For example, if you had a packet from a Wildcat! BBS and had `AmiQWK''\ngenerate a request to add conference #25, `AmiQWK'' would generate a\nmessage with the following header information:\n\n   FROM: <your name>\nTO: TOMCAT\nSUBJ: ADD\nCONFERENCE 25\nIf you decide to abort the request, just enter the ''Replies'' conference\nand ''Kill'' the request message. (See @{"Reading Messages" Link "Reading Messages"}.)\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "The Future of AmiQWK" "AmiQWK.guide/The Future of AmiQWK"\n@Next "Technical Support"\n@Prev "Advanced QWK features"\n@Toc "Main"\n\nThe Future of AmiQWK\n********************\n\n   `AmiQWK III''will be a complete rewrite of the existing code. Some of\nthe features I plan on adding are:\n\n   * A whole new multi-window, multi-packet environment.\n        - Any number of packets can be opened at a time, limited only by\n          available memory.\n\n   * Database of services\n        - New messages can be created even if no packet is present.\n\n        - Replies can be sent to a different service than the packet is\n          from.\n\n   * Sorting\n        - Conferences by Name or Number.\n\n        - Messages by To, From, Subject, Message Number, etc.\n\n   * String search for message by to:, from:, subject:, message text.\n\n   * Improvement of quote system.\n        - Quote prefix may contain initials, first name of the person\n          who''se text is quoted, etc.\n\n   * Tagline manager\n        - Edit, Delete, Sort, Find tagline functions.\n\n        - multiple tagline files can be maintained. (such as a file\n          containing only Star Trek quotes, etc.)\n\n        - A true ''Steal Tagline'' function.\n\n   * Database of BBS and packet information\n        - Keep a record of which packets have been read, etc.\n\n        - Keep record of conferences and other information about the\n          packets a BBS generates, so that a reply packet can be\n          generated even if no packet exists for the BBS.\n\n        - Keep a address book of people you regularly send messages to,\n          including network addresses, phone and fax numbers, etc.\n\n        - Keep a ''Twit'' list. Ignore messages with certain topics or\n          from/to persons listed.\n\n   * ''Blue Wave'' and ''QWK-II'' packet support.\n\n   * Possible UUCP, TCP/IP, Fidonet, and FAX interfaces.\n\n   * UUEncode/UUDecode support.\n\n   * PGP (Pretty Good Privacy) support.\n\n   * ''Text Variables'' for inserting information into text, such as who\n     the message is to/from, etc.\n\n   * ARexx Interface\n\n   If there is a feature you would like to see in a future version, let\nme know about it, even if I listed it already. If I get a lot of\nrequests I may move it to a higher position on the list.\n\n   Remember, The future of `AmiQWK'' depends on YOU! Send in your\nregistration as soon as possible.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Technical Support" "AmiQWK.guide/Technical Support"\n@Next "Acknowledgments"\n@Prev "The Future of AmiQWK"\n@Toc "Main"\n\nTechnical Support\n*****************\n\n   Technical support for `AmiQWK'' is available through the following:\n\nElectronic Mail\n    INTERNET\n          [email removed]\nPostal Mail\n     Jim Dawson\n     509 S. Madison St.\n     Marion, IL. 62959\n     USA\n\nBBS\n     The Hackers Guild II BBS\n     (618)997-2591 300-14,400 BPS USR HST/v.32/v.32bis.\n     Full Duplex, 8 data bits, 1 stop bit, no parity.\n     24 Hrs, 7 Days. (Ex. Fidonet Zone Mail Hour.) You can download\n     from the AmiQWK file area on your first call.\n\n   For fastest and most reliable way to get technical support is to\ncall The Hackers Guild II BBS directly.\n\n   When asking for help, please give as complete description of your\nproblem as possible, as well as a complete description of your system\nand any software that might also be running at the same time as\n`AmiQWK''. The more information I have the faster I can suggest a\nsolution to your problem.\n\n   I will personally post new versions of AmiQWK to The Hackers Guild\nII BBS, Aminet FTP sites, and new versions will be submitted to Fred\nFish for inclusion in his library of freely distributable software.\nUsers of Fidonet mailers can FREQ the latest version of AmiQWK by\nrequesting ''AMIQWK'' from 1:2290/42.0 (or 618-997-2591 if you don''t have\na Fidonet nodelist.)\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Acknowledgments" "AmiQWK.guide/Acknowledgments"\n@Next "Index"\n@Prev "Technical Support"\n@Toc "Main"\n\nAcknowledgments\n***************\n\n   Special thanks go to:\n\n`Jefferey Foy''\n     For information on the QWKMail format and MSBIN-IEEE conversion\n     routines.\n\n`Greg Hewgill''\n     For specifications of the DOOR.ID file.\n\n`Patrick Y. Lee''\n     For his excellent document on the QWKMail format.\n\n`Marcus Trisdale''\n     For his ANSI display code.\n\n`Jan van den Baard''\n     For his GadToolsBox GUI builder program.\n\n`Nico François''\n     For `reqtools.library''\n\n`Commodore Business Machines''\n     for `AmigaGuide.library'' and for making the Amiga computer.\n\n`Robert J Chassell and Richard M. Stallman''\n     for the Texinfo documentation format.\n\n`Reinhard Spisser and Sebastiano Vigna''\n     for `MakeGuide''.\n\n`Chris Hurley, Guy Bartoli, Dave Birdsong, Dennis Jann, Mike Soucy,''\n`Kipling Cooper, Steve Stewart, and Matt Weatherford''\n     for Beta Testing `AmiQWK''\n\n   And of course, everyone who has sent in registrations.\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Index" "AmiQWK.guide/Index"\n@Prev "Acknowledgments"\n@Toc "Main"\n\nIndex\n*****\n\n@Index "Index"\n\n\n@EndNode\n\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',199943,6,0,NULL,NULL,'aqwk24cr','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('arcdoor','ARCDOOR.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/ARCDOOR.LHA','istrip','XIM','UNZIP',NULL,NULL,NULL,'from archives. (lha/lzh/lzx/zip)',replace('Download, Extract, Delete and View files\nfrom archives. (lha/lzh/lzx/zip)','\n',char(10)),'lOSeNDOS.guide',replace('                   \n                     __  _ __  /\__ _  _ __/\  __ _  __\n                    _\/___   \/              \/   ___\/_\n              ......\/\  /........................\  /\/......\n             :         \/                          \/         :\n             :      .___             .___         .___        :\n             :   _ _|   \      ______|   \  ______|   \       :\n             :   \\\__   7___  \______   /  \__   ____/       :\n             :      /.   |   \__/.  l/   \___/_____   \__ _   :\n             :     /::.  |    _/::.       _/::.  l/    _///   :\n             :     \___________\___________\____________\     :\n             :___        .___        .___        .___         :___\n     _ ______|   \ ______|   \ ______|   \ ______|   \  ______|   \\n     \\\__   ____/ \______   / \______   / \______   /  \__   ____/\n        /.   ___|___/.   |   \__/.  _|   \__/.  l/   \___/_____   \__ _\n       /::.  l/   _/::.  |    _/::. \___  _/::.       _/::.  l/    _///\n       \_ _________\_____|_____\___________\___________\___________ \\n         Y   :                                                :[nM$]Y\n      .------''                                                `------.\n      :eXON/rIOT^jORMAS · gHANDY/gODS · rAHIEM/eSSENCE · cELIC/rEKTUM: \n      `------.                                                .------''\n             `------------------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',114250,6,0,NULL,NULL,'arcdoor','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('arrange','ARRANGE.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/ARRANGE.LHA','arrange','XIM','arrange',NULL,NULL,NULL,'2 conferences as one',replace('  If you want to make \n 2 conferences as one\n','\n',char(10)),'arrange.doc',replace('\n\n If you want to make 2 conferences as one then this util is a must for you.\n this just sorts filedates.\n\n Usage: Arrage DirX  -  [X no. of dir to sort]\n\n\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',12851,7,0,NULL,NULL,'arrange','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('ars_popn','ARS_POPN.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/ARS_POPN.LHA',NULL,'XIM','TcTDemo.exe',NULL,NULL,'ARS','| > POWER OPEN < | | col Dor for /X, S-EXPRES |',replace('A-R-+- ----+    I   \n| > POWER OPEN < |    \n| col Dor for /X, S-EXPRES |  \n','\n',char(10)),'PowerOpen.guide',replace('@DATABASE PowerOpen.guide\n@NODE MAIN "Contents"\n\n                          »»» Power Open 1.o «««\n\n                      »» Contents of Documentation ««\n\n\n                              @{" Introduction " LINK int}\n                              @{" Requirements " LINK requ}\n                              @{" Installation " LINK ins}\n                              @{" Configuration" LINK Con}\n                              @{"   Credits    " LINK Credz}\n			      @{"  Greetings   " LINK Greets}\n                              @{"   History    " LINK Hist}\n                              @{"Other /X doors" LINK doors}\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Int "Introduction"\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n\nPower Open allows you to make a doormenu for each user by using only\none config file.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Requ "Requirements"\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n\nTo run Power Request 1.o you need:\n\n-An Amiga model (500/600/1000/1200/2000/2500/3000/4000/CDTV/CD³²)\n-AmiExpress (V2.xx or higher) or S-Express\n-The following command in your C: directory:\n  -Delete\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Ins "Installation"\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n\nTo install Power Request,just do the following things:\n\n\n-Decrunch the archive\n-Copy the <Doors> directories to your BBS directory\n\nAmiExpress 2.x or S-Express\n\n-Add the following line to your BBS commands:\n\n--- cut here ---\n\n*OPEN      Rm100Doors:ARISE/PowerOpen/PowerOpen.rexx\n\n--- cut here ---\n\nAmiExpress 3.x or S-Express\n\nCopy the icon <open.info> into your BBSCMD directory.\n\n-Copy the following command in your C: directory:\n  -Delete\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Con "Configuration"\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n\nIn the config file you need an entry for each of your doors, which has to\nlook like this:\n\nThe first line has to start with a "@" and then the doorname (how you\ncalled it in the BBS commands).Maximum length is 10 chars.\nIn the second line, you type a small description for the door (max. 60\nchars).\nNext follows the type of the door. If it should be shown to users with a\ndefinite access level you should write "AXS" here and the required access\nlevel in the next line.\nIf it should only be shown to some of your users, independent of their\naccess, you type "USERS" in this line and in the following lines the names\nof the users.\nThe config has to end with "@EndOfConfig"\n\nTo show a small text above the doorlist, it has to be named\n"DOORS:ARISE/PowerOpen/Doorheader.txt".\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Credz "Credits"\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n\n                            P-O-W-E-R  O-P-E-N\n\n                            V·E·R·S·I·O·N  1.o\n\n                          released on 17/02/1994\n\n                          Coding by : Kyle/ARÏSE\n\n                 It was done for Pater Pink''s Power Board.\n                           Call +49-(0)7244-2199\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Greets "Greetings"\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n\nGreetings fly to:\n\n@{"      SLAM/ESSENCE    " LINK Slam}\n@{"         KiWi         " LINK CALL}\n@{"       Gandalf        " LINK CALL}\n@{"        Ronny         " LINK CALL}\n@{"     Buster & Fuzz    " LINK CALL}\n@{"         Nose         " LINK CALL}\n@{"        Pencl         " LINK CALL}\n@{"    SMC & Skindiver   " LINK CALL}\n@{"all other cool Freaks!" LINK CALL}\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Slam "Hallo Slam"\n@TOC Greets\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n\nPater Pink meint,warum man hier den Slam Button anklickt,wenn man gar nicht\nSlam ist, und ueberhapt!\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE call "Vote for my BBS!"\n@TOC Greets\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n\n                  Call Pater Pink''s Power Board\n\n                     the official ARÏSE WHQ\n\n                    ask ELITE for the NUMBA!!\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Hist "History"\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n\nV1.0    Initial Release\n\nKnown bugs:\n-none\n\nTo be included:\n-submenus for the doors\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE doors "Other /X Doors by the ARÏSE /X Power Team"\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n\nReleased:\n\nVote,Du Schwein 3.0     THE best voting door around\n                        Features:\n                        -9 different charts\n                        -Upload Adverts\n                        -Easy to configurate\n                        -Special Editor&Sysop options (D/L Votes,ads...)\nPower Rulez V1.0        Displays a variable amount of rulez out of\n                        a rulez file.\nPower Request V1.0      Door to make and answer requests\n                        Features:\n                        -seperate checker, wich checks request, when a user\n                         enters the system\n                        -users can choose, how many times they give credits\nPower Page V1.0beta     Chat, even is the Sysop is not at home.\n                        (is being beta tested at the moment)\n                        Features:\n                        -various languages\n                        -handles up to 99 themes for each language\n                        -uses own userdatas with nicknames...\n                        -allows you to write a "message" to the each user\n                        -saves all answers, so you can implement them\n                         easiely\nPower Logoff V1.0beta   The best Logoff door\n                        (is being beta tested at the moment)\n                        Features:\n                        -Message to next user\n                        -BBS List/Adverts\n                        -Relogon\n                        -Comment to Sysop\n                        -Timebank\n                        -Online time\nPower Quest V1.0beta    Door for New User Questionaires\n                        Features:\n                        -Comment to sysop\n                        -Page sysop\n                        -Drop carrier on lame users\n                        -Different kinds of questions\n                         -Ask for string\n                         -Yes/No\n                         -multiple choice\n                         -ask for several strings\n\nPlanned:\n\nPower Status            hmm...The only cool Power Status\nPower Partner           Find your Dream Guy on the Board.\n                        (Spaetere Hochzeit nicht ausgeschlossen)\nPower Security          Security Door for LogIn. Will support the answers\n                        the user gave in Power Quest.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------{OPEN!}----\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',12270,4,0,NULL,NULL,'ars_popn','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('ars_preq','ARS-PREQ.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/ARS-PREQ.LHA',NULL,'XIM','+------------------------------------------+',NULL,'ARÏSE','ARS','| > POWER REQUEST <                        | | the Best Request Dor for /X, S-EXPRES    | | by ARÏSE :)                              |',replace('+------------------------------------------+\n| > POWER REQUEST <                        |\n| the Best Request Dor for /X, S-EXPRES    |\n| by ARÏSE :)                              |\n+------------------------------------------+\n','\n',char(10)),'PowerRequest.guide',replace('@DATABASE Vote.guide\n@NODE MAIN "Contents"\n\n                         »»» Power Request 1.o «««\n\n                      »» Contents of Documentation ««\n\n\n                              @{" Introduction " LINK int}\n                              @{" Requirements " LINK requ}\n                              @{" Installation " LINK ins}\n                              @{"     REQ      " LINK Req}\n                              @{"    REQCHK    " LINK ReqChk}\n                              @{"   Credits    " LINK Credz}\n			      @{"  Greetings   " LINK Greets}\n                              @{"   History    " LINK Hist}\n                              @{"Other /X doors" LINK doors}\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Credz "Credits"\n\n                         P-O-W-E-R  R-E-Q-U-E-S-T\n\n                            V·E·R·S·I·O·N  1.o\n\n                          released on 17/02/1994\n\n                          Coding by : Kyle/ARÏSE\n                         Ansis by :  Falcon/ARÏSE\n\n                 It was done for Pater Pink''s Power Board.\n                           Call +49-(0)7244-2199\n\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Int "Introduction"\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n\nPower Request allows the users to request files easiely.\nThese requests may be answered by other users, by uploading the requested\nfile. Power Request gives the users the credits, pronised by the user, who\nrequested the file.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Requ "Requirements"\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n\nTo run Power Request 1.o you need:\n\n-An Amiga model (500/600/1000/1200/2000/2500/3000/4000/CDTV/CD³²)\n-AmiExpress (V2.xx or higher) or S-Express\n-The following commands in your C: directory:\n  -Copy\n  -Delete\n  -List\n  -Makedir\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Ins "Installation"\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n\nTo install Power Request,just do the following things:\n\n\n-Decrunch the archive\n-Copy the <Doors> directories to your BBS directory\n\nAmiExpress 2.x or S-Express\n\n-Add the following lines to your BBS commands:\n\n--- cut here ---\n\n*REQ      Rm100Doors:ARISE/PowerRequest/PowerRequest.rexx\n*REQCHK   Rm100Doors:ARISE/PowerRequest/PowerRequestChk.rexx\n\n--- cut here ---\n\nAmiExpress 3.x or S-Express\n\nCopy the two icons <req.info> and <reqchk.info> into your BBSCMD directory.\n\n-Add the following line to your logontext:\n\n--- cut here ---\n\n~CC_REQCHK\n\n--- cut here ---\n\n-Copy the following commands in your C: directory:\n  -Copy\n  -Delete\n  -List\n  -Makedir\n  -Rename\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Req "Power Request"\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n\nThe Main Program of the Power Request package. Here the user is able to\nmake or view/answer/delete requests. It is really to use and I think, there\nis no need the explain the functions.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE ReqChk "Power Request Checker"\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n\nThis program is used to show the users their requests, which have been\nanswered and to show them the new requests.\nIt should be started, when a user enters the system (just put it in the \nLogon text). If there are requests, which the user hasn''t seen, it asks\nhim, if he wants to view them. When viewing the requests, he can also\nanswer them and upload the requested file.\nNext he will be asked, if Power Request Checker should search for answered\nrequests of him. When it found some request, which had been answered, it\nwill show the user the info about the request and the name&length of the\nfiles, which had been uploaded for this specific request and asks him if he\nwants to D/L the file(s). After downloading all files, the request can be\ndeleted or stay active.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Greets "Greetings"\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n\nGreetings fly to:\n\n@{"      SLAM/ESSENCE    " LINK Slam}\n@{"         KiWi         " LINK CALL}\n@{"       Gandalf        " LINK CALL}\n@{"        Ronny         " LINK CALL}\n@{"     Buster & Fuzz    " LINK CALL}\n@{"         Nose         " LINK CALL}\n@{"        Pencl         " LINK CALL}\n@{"    SMC & Skindiver   " LINK CALL}\n@{"all other cool Freaks!" LINK CALL}\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Slam "Hallo Slam"\n@TOC Greets\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n\nPater Pink meint,warum man hier den Slam Button anklickt,wenn man gar nicht\nSlam ist, und ueberhapt!\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE call "Vote for my BBS!"\n@TOC Greets\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n\n                  Call Pater Pink''s Power Board\n\n                     the official ARÏSE WHQ\n\n                    ask ELITE for the NUMBA!!\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE Hist "History"\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n\nV1.0    Initial Release\n\nKnown bugs:\n-If requesting user has not enough credits to D/L the file(s), it will\n be ignored\n\nTo be included:\n-credits check (see above)\n-request texts (to request infos(like BBS numbers) and not only files\n-answer with texts\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n@ENDNODE\n@NODE doors "Other /X Doors by the ARÏSE /X Power Team"\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n\nReleased:\n\nVote,Du Schwein 3.0     THE best voting door around\n                        Features:\n                        -9 different charts\n                        -Upload Adverts\n                        -Easy to configurate\n                        -Special Editor&Sysop options (D/L Votes,ads...)\nPower Rulez V1.0        Displays a variable amount of rulez out of\n                        a rulez file.\nPower Page V1.0beta     Chat, even is the Sysop is not at home.\n                        (is being beta tested at the moment)\n                        Features:\n                        -various languages\n                        -handles up to 99 themes for each language\n                        -uses own userdatas with nicknames...\n                        -allows you to write a "message" to the each user\n                        -saves all answers, so you can implement them\n                         easiely\nPower Logoff V1.0beta   The best Logoff door\n                        (is being beta tested at the moment)\n                        Features:\n                        -Message to next user\n                        -BBS List/Adverts\n                        -Relogon\n                        -Comment to Sysop\n                        -Timebank\nPower Quest V1.0beta    Door for New User Questionaires\n                        Features:\n                        -Comment to sysop\n                        -Page sysop\n                        -Drop carrier on lame users\n                        -Different kinds of questions\n                         -Ask for string\n                         -Yes/No\n                         -multiple choice\n                         -ask for several strings\nPower Open V1.0beta     Door to display a doormenu\n                        Features:\n                        -only one config file is needed\n                        -ability to show a door to only some users\n                         (co-sysops/members of your group...)\n\nPlanned:\n\nPower Status            hmm...The only cool Power Status\nPower Partner           Find your Dream Guy on the Board.\n                        (Spaetere Hochzeit nicht ausgeschlossen)\nPower Security          Security Door for LogIn. Will support the answers\n                        the user gave in Power Quest.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------{REQUEST!}----\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,NULL,12240,1,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,'scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('artm210','ARTM210.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/ARTM210.LHA','Artm','XIM','Artm',NULL,NULL,NULL,'Real Time Monitor v2.10',replace('  ARTM - AmiExpress \nReal Time Monitor v2.10\n','\n',char(10)),'Artm.Doc',replace('                 ARTM - AmiExpress Real Time Monitor v2.10\n\n                          (c) 1992 Chris Kawchuk\n                      KickStart BBS - (416) 279-0907\n                  AmiExpress Support BBS - 300/14400 HST\n\n	For quite some time now, I''ve always seen a need for a program like\n this. ARTM, quite simply, is an AmiExpress monitor. While it lacks the\n flare and gadgetry of Joseph Hodge''s "acp.ctrl", it does do it''s job, and\n does it nicely.\n\n System requirements:\n\n	- An Amiga with 512K+ Ram.\n	- A Registered AmiExpress BBS program, properly installed.\n	- Electricity.\n\n Optional:\n\n	- A monitor. (Only necessary if you actually want to see the\n	              information.)\n\n ARTM runs under:\n\n	- WB/KS 1.2, 1.3, 2.04, and 2.1\n	- 68000/68010/68020/68030/68040 compliant.\n	- Math Co-Processor is not necessary, although it does help when\n          rendering Imagine objects in the background.\n\n ARTM does not run under:\n\n	- IBM MS-DOS.\n	- OS/2 (Assuming your version actually works).\n	- WinDoze.\n	- MacIntosh.\n	- CPM.\n	- C-64.\n	- Atari GEM.\n	- Or any other non-Amiga system.\n\n	- It may run on a Cray XIV Supercomputer, bur unfortunately I did not\n          have $50 Million Dollars on me to buy one and test it out.\n\nHow to load ARTM:\n\n	- type "Artm"\n	- It is suggested that ARTM be placed a a lower priority than that\n	  of most of your other programs, since it''s a monitor. It does\n          not require access to the CPU when, for instance, there is\n          a lot of disk activity or processing going on. It will\n          hapily sit and wait until the more importnat tasks have finished\n	  before it does an update. Use the "ChangeTaskpri" command to set\n	  ARTM from a -5 to a  -10 priority.\n\n What ARTM displays:\n\n	- All current callers on all nodes.\n\n		This allows a multiline BBS Sysop to see, at a glance, all\n		the users currently logged into his/her BBS.\n\n		This is the ONE feature that influenced me to write this\n                program. Nothing else at the time could do this - See\n		everyone at one time. No unnecessary flipping of screens!\n\n	- Caller''s location.\n\n		Any out of town callers on your lines? Mabye you use the\n		"Location" entry for the users'' real name, if say you are\n		running a BBS that uses Aliases. It''s up to you. :-)\n\n	- Caller''s current action.\n\n		Shows what the user is doing without swapping a billion\n                screens. Also, when a user is paging you, one can see\n                instantly what user it is, and on what node.\n\n		Normal actions appear in green. When a user is logging\n		on or off, or paging the sysop for chat, the action will\n                appear yellow. Any sysop functions (like Emacs, remote\n                shell, file editing, account editing etc..) will appear\n		red. When you see an action that is red, it''s time to\n		pay attention! :-)\n\n		I may also add a warning bell whenever ARTM detects\n                a sysop function. This would allow the sysop to be\n                notified when a high lever command is executed, even\n                in the middle of the night. Let me know if you want this\n                feature.\n\n	- Total number of calls.\n\n		Self explanatory. Reads in your BBS:SystemStats. Anyone\n		could program this in their sleep. :-)\n\n	- Total number of messages.\n\n		This will search all your BBS:conf/MsgBases and count\n                up the current number of messages, wether they are active\n                or not. (They all get counted) You will need a standard\n		BBS:NET/NETMAIL.CFG file, which comes with my AmiExpress\n                NetMailer for this to work properly.\n\n	- NetMail NODEID number\n\n		Again, if you are using my ''AmiExpress NetMailer'' program,\n                this will read in and display your NetMail ID number.\n\n	- Last 8 callers & Node number\n\n		ARTM keeps track of the last callers since it was invoked.\n		The user''s name as well as the node number they logged in\n		on will be displayed.\n\n	- User account viewing\n\n		Currently a limited function. This allows a sysop to bring\n		up some details about a user''s account who is currently\n		online. Simply press the node number that the user is\n		logged into, and up comes the information. Any key\n		to return to the main screen.\n\n	- Definable ''peek'' time\n\n		Allows the Sysop to define at what intervals ARTM will\n		update it''s information. Allows the sysop to resort more or\n		less CPU time to ARTM, simply by changing the swait between\n		the scanning intervals. This delay is optional on the CLI\n		command line like such:\n\n		1> Artm <delay in seconds>\n\n		Therefore a command like "1> Artm 6" will load up ARTM\n		and then instruct artm to update every six seconds. Values\n		from 1 to 99 are valid, although anything over 20 is\n		aufully slow. If no value, or an invalid value is given,\n		it will defalut to a 3 second delay.\n\n	- Pulldown menus and keyboard equivalents:\n\n		"Quit    [Esc]"  - Exits the program.\n		"About  [Help]"  - Brings up an "About" window.\n\n	- Hmmm... Anything else?\n\n		Well, the opening animation on the "please register"\n		screen is another bonus. Would you belive that only\n		took 3 lines of code? :-)\n\n\n	That''s about it. Nothing fancy, nothing wild. If you have any\n suggestions or comments, I''d love to hear them. Call me at Kickstart BBS\n at (416) 279-0907. The number is also listed on the AmiExpress title\n screen. Either way, this is my contribution to AmiExpress.\n\n Please do not modify, change, edit, recompile, newzap, sublicence,\n rent, lease, convey, translate, convert, sell, re-sell, malform,\n mis-represent, deface, sabotage, deform, disassociate, burn in effigy,\n sacrifice, decompile, disassemble, and/or satire this program, and/or any\n copy of this program. ''Nuff said.\n\n As for all of you non-registered AmiExpress Sysops: Respect the author''s\n wishes, as piracy hurts everyone. Hey, one should be able to afford $50.00\n to support such a great BBS program. If you like it, buy it.\n\n              BullDog''s BBS    USA       (HST) 318-793-4101\n              KickStart        Canada    (HST) 416-279-0907\n              DownTown         Holland   (HST) ++31 (0)5750-29313\n              Pearl Harbor     Germany   (HST) +49-441-391199\n              Infinite Dreams  Italy     (HST) +39-2-58105803\n\n      Comments/suggestions/feedback is appreciated. Criticisms > nil:\n\n              Enjoy ARTM! Watch for new versions coming soon!\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n       Other software available from Chris Kawchuk / AmiCom Software:\n\n             ARTM - The AmiExpress Real Time Monitor.\n                    A ''must get'' for multinode AmiExpress sysops.\n\n             AMR  - AmiExpress Offline (ZOOM) Mail Reader\n                    A fully featured offline mail reader\n                    with threading, replying, editing, saving,\n                    quoting, and so on. Automatically generates\n                    ascii script files for any terminal program\n                    to automate and inser your replies to messages\n                    back into an AmiExpress BBS\n\n          NetMail - AmiExpress NetMailer\n                    Fully functional point-to-point netmailer. Works\n                    with any message bases, any configuration. Requires\n                    Arexx capable systems for DOOR scripting. Completely\n                    automated. Performs multiple conference packing\n                    and unpacking of mail files. No need for "special"\n                    netmail receiving conferences - It is completely\n                    integrated into the existing message bases.\n\n	Look for more soon! In the works:\n\n	UG - "Usage Grapher", which shows TRUE BBS usage as a percentage of\n total available time per hour. Keeps track of 6+ months of calls. Calculates\n full ANSI graphs, standard deviation, peak hours, etc.. etc...\n\n	MBP - "Message Base Packer" - renumbers all mail, removes deleted\n ones. Compatible with AmiExpress NetMailer. Also renumbers all user''s\n account pointers as to accurately reflect the correct "next message."\n\n	Fido-Compatible NetMail. Watch for it soon.\n\n\n                            - Chris Kawchuk. [Sysop: Kickstart BBS - Canada!]\n\n\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',43726,13,0,NULL,NULL,'artm210','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('as','AS.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/AS.LHA','as','XIM','LE-win5-96.exe',NULL,NULL,NULL,'|  |   | \      / ________ \   | \/  ___ _/ |  |  \   \| \_/  _|_/  |/ /  \   \ (_ | | |  |__:\__/|__|\____ \__|\ \__:\__/ (__: |',replace('_|__ ___:\______________________:\  ________\n |  |   | \      / ________ \   | \/  ___ _/\n |  |  \   \| \_/  _|_/  |/ /  \   \ (_ | |\n |  |__:\__/|__|\____ \__|\ \__:\__/ (__: |\n |__|================\/====\/=WiZZy\______:\nAminet Download Tool (ADT) Site Checker v1.0\n[iSDN][`] lEEChEd fRoM gOd''s hArEm [''][33.6]\n','\n',char(10)),'lOSeNDOS.guide',replace('                   \n                     __  _ __  /\__ _  _ __/\  __ _  __\n                    _\/___   \/              \/   ___\/_\n              ......\/\  /........................\  /\/......\n             :         \/                          \/         :\n             :      .___             .___         .___        :\n             :   _ _|   \      ______|   \  ______|   \       :\n             :   \\\__   7___  \______   /  \__   ____/       :\n             :      /.   |   \__/.  l/   \___/_____   \__ _   :\n             :     /::.  |    _/::.       _/::.  l/    _///   :\n             :     \___________\___________\____________\     :\n             :___        .___        .___        .___         :___\n     _ ______|   \ ______|   \ ______|   \ ______|   \  ______|   \\n     \\\__   ____/ \______   / \______   / \______   /  \__   ____/\n        /.   ___|___/.   |   \__/.  _|   \__/.  l/   \___/_____   \__ _\n       /::.  l/   _/::.  |    _/::. \___  _/::.       _/::.  l/    _///\n       \_ _________\_____|_____\___________\___________\___________ \\n         Y   :                                                :[nM$]Y\n      .------''                                                `------.\n      :eXON/rIOT^jORMAS · gHANDY/gODS · rAHIEM/eSSENCE · cELIC/rEKTUM: \n      `------.                                                .------''\n             `------------------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',12176,6,0,NULL,NULL,'as','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('asceol','ASCEOL.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/ASCEOL.LHA','ASCII_EOL','XIM','ASCII_EOL','1.0',NULL,NULL,NULL,replace('ASCII_EOL V1.0  (24-May-93)\n','\n',char(10)),'ASCII_EOL.doc',replace('ASCII_EOL V1.0  (24-May-93)\n---------------------------\n\nif you make an ASCII send, some terminalprograms(Term) send the text\nfile in the raw format. but they forget to send the right "end of line" code,\nso your uploaded text looks very strange. with ASCII_EOL you are able to\nconvert a text before sending it.\nnow you can send group logos (/X file description) or mails to an other user\nin the correct captured form!\nenjoy it!!!\n\n\nGadgets:\n--------\n\nQuit\n----\nquits ASCII_EOL V1.0\n\nSave Prefs\n----------\nsaves the configuration file into the current drawer\n\nfollowing datas will be saved:\n          content of "appendix"\n          current path (filerequester)\n          delete source file (yes/no)\n          \n\nDestination appendix\n--------------------\nappends the content of this gadget on destination filename\ne.g.:     testfile -> content=".ASC" -> testfile.ASC\n\nDelete source file\n------------------\nyes: deletes source file after writing destination file\nno:  no delete\n\nCONNVERT A FILE\n--------------\nstarts the converter\n\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',31798,8,0,NULL,NULL,'asceol','scan',1780048410);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('asend10','ASEND10.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/ASEND10.LHA','ASend','XIM','ASend',NULL,'Dux/TsL',NULL,'_/~____/\_/\_KZ_/~_______¬\_  _/~____/ 7___ ¬7  7  7__7  __)7~¬7  7  7___ ¬7 | ¬7  ¡  !  ¡ ¬¡    ¬|  |  !  ¡ ¬7  ¡',replace('     ______         _____________________\n   _/~____/\_/\_KZ_/~_______¬\_  _/~____/\n   7___ ¬7  7  7__7  __)7~¬7  7  7___ ¬7 \n   | ¬7  ¡  !  ¡ ¬¡    ¬|  |  !  ¡ ¬7  ¡ \n   l_____j__j_____j_____j__j__j__j_____j \n  ASend v1.0 by Dux/TsL - For AmiExpress!\n   Send the tempans to the SysOp - FAST\n','\n',char(10)),'ASend.doc',replace('x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x\nx                                                                           x\nx                                ASend v1.0                                 x\nx                                                                           x\nx                                By Dux/Tsl                                 x\nx                                                                           x\nx                           Mainline +45-981 53062                          x\nx                                                                           x\nx                            Amixnet: Dux@DK0001                            x\nx                                                                           x\nx×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x×x\n\n\n                            ### IMPORTANT NOTE ###\n\nIf you''re running a pirate version of AmiExpress, you may !NOT! use this\nutility! If you''re using AmiExpress - Then BUY it!\n\n\n                             ### INTRODUCTION ###\n\nThis utility will send the newuser questionaire to the SysOp as a message.\nThe utility is supposed to be really fast and will take almost no time to run.\n\n\n                             ### REQUIREMENTS ###\n\n- AmiExpress (with a REAL registration!)\n- You must have your nodes in BBS:\n- Your user.data must be in BBS: to find the name of the SysOp.\n\n                                ### USAGE ###\n\nThe utility is supposed to be placed in your logoffscript. The syntax is like\nthis:\n\nASEND <Node> <Conference path>\n\nThe <Node> is the node where the logoffscript was executed from. The\n<Conference path> is the conference you want the message to appear in.\n\nEg:\nASEND 0 BBS:Confs/Reception\n\nThis will check the Node0/TempAns and send it to the SysOp in the Reception\nconference.\n\n\n                                 ### BUGS ###\n\nThere''s no known bugs at the moment, but if any problems should occur, please\ncontact me at my board:\n\n                Mainline + 45-981 53062 - Amixnet: Dux@DK0001\n\n\n                             ### REGISTRATION ###\n\nI do no longer give out the free optimized and registred versions! BUT I would\nlike to know if you run my utils, so please contact me by calling my board or\nsend me a msg.\n\n\n                               ### HISTORY ###\n\nv1.0 - First release. No known bugs.\n\n\n                                ### HELLOS ###\n\n\nAlzheimer - No letters for a loooong time?!?\nVan Damme - I like your utils! Sorry that I can''t call ya board, but mail me a\n            msg on Amixnet please?\nEmpire - Cool utils - Will they ever be in C?\n\nTarzan - Frostie - Ramirez - Fun Fun - Scratch - Ice Q - Zany - Lector -\nYobbo - MR.Beer\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',22955,10,0,NULL,NULL,'asend10','scan',1780048410);
