INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('bb_h1o','BB_H1O.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/BB_H1O.LHA','BBhELP','XIM','BB_H1O.LHA BBhELP',NULL,'MuRpHy/???','BB','::/  \:\___ \___ \\__/::: o__(*_*)__o :::::: ::\/ /::| / /| / /:\ \::: (_   O   _) ::::: :::\_\::| \ \| \ \:/ /\:::  \_/U\_/  ::::',replace('.:.__:.__________.____:.:. \\\\|//// .::...\n::/  \:\___ \___ \\__/::: o__(*_*)__o ::::::\n::\/ /::| / /| / /:\ \::: (_   O   _) :::::\n:::\_\::| \ \| \ \:/ /\:::  \_/U\_/  ::::\n:::/__\/____/____/:\__/::::   ) (  ::::::::.\n--------------------------> PrEsEnTs <------\n           hELP V1.o - HELP MENU\n     Coded bY MuRpHy/??? BaD bRoThErS ¿¿¿\n--------------------------------------------\n','\n',char(10)),'BBhELP.gUiDe',replace('@database "BBhELP V1.o By MuRpHy Of ??? BaD bRoThErS ¿¿¿"\n@AUTHOR "MuRpHy"\n@(C) "MuRpHy/?BB¿"\n@Ver$ "- BBhELP V1.o - 18-07-94 -"\n@HEIGHT 30\n@Node MAIN "ThE MaIn MeNu"\n===========================================================================\n  __  __  __   ___/\     ^   ___/\\n /  \/  \/  \  \___ \   / \  \___ \\n \/ /\/ /\/ /   | / /  / ^ \  |  \ \\n  \_\ \_\ \_\   | \ \ / ___ \ | \/ /\n  /__\/__\/__\ /____//_/   \_\|___/\n						     _____\n	___/\  ___/\ ___/\__________    /\____ ___/\/ ___/ ____________\n	\___ \/    //  /  \_   __/\ \  / /   //    /\_ \   \__/\__/\__/\n	 | / /  / // / /  / | |   / /_/ / __//  / /   \ \   \ \ \ \ \ \\n	 | \ \ /  \\ \/  /  | |  / __  /| __\\ /  \/\_/ /   / /\/ /\/ /\\n	/____/ \___\\___/   |_| /_/ /_/ |____/ \___\___/    \__/\__/\__/\n	    /__|			    /__|\n===========================================================================\n    @{"*>     fEaTuReS      <*" link "Features"}               ??? BaD bRoThErS ¿¿¿ PrEsEnTs\n        @{"*>  hOw tO iNsTaLL   <*" link "Install"}\n            @{"*>   cOnFiGuRaTiOnS   <*" link "Configuration"}          ----->  hELP V1.o  <-----\n                @{"*>     GrEeTiNgS     <*" link "Greetings"}\n                    @{"*> CaLL ThEsE bOaRdS <*" link "Boards"}              bY MuRpHy/?BB¿\n                        @{"*>oThEr ?BB¿ /X Tools<*" link "Tools"}\n@endnode\n@Node Features "fEaTuReS"\n\n  - Fully coded in `C''\n\n  - Textfiles to extern commands can also be shown by this Help-Door.\n    So you can edit helptexts to your extern commands.\n\n  - Commands can be executed directly from the informations.\n    You can set the minimum level needed to execute a command.\n\n  - The sysop commands are only shown to users with sysop access.\n    You can set the SysOp Level.\n\n\n     @{"BaCk To ThE mAiN mEnU" link "MAIN"}    @{"hOw to iNsTaLL" link "Install"}\n@endnode\n@Node Install "hOw tO iNsTaLL"\n\nMake a drawer called: DOORS:BBHELP/\nCopy all files to this directory.\n\n\n/X 2.xx: Only add the following lines to your BBSCMD file:\n\n---------------------->> Cut Here <<--------------------------\nH         XM010DOORS:BBhELP/BBhELP\n---------------------->> Cut Here <<--------------------------\n\n\n/X 3.xx: Only copy the H.info to the BBSCMD drawer,.\n\n\n     @{"BaCk To ThE mAiN mEnU" link "MAIN"}    @{"cOnFiGuRaTiOnS" link "Configuration"}\n@endnode\n@Node Configuration "cOnFiGuRaTiOnS"\n\nThe hELP.cfg should be like this:\n\n---------------------->> Cut Here <<--------------------------\n10	;Execute Level\n200	;SysOp Level\n---------------------->> Cut Here <<--------------------------\n\nExecute level is the level needed by a user to execute a command out of\nthe BBhELP.\n\nSysOp Level is the level needed by a user to see the SysOp commands.\n\n\n\n\nMake your own textfiles and put them into the DOORS:BBHELP/HELPTXT/\ndirectory.\n\nFor example:\nDOORS:BBHELP/HELPTXT/H.TXT\n---------------------->> Cut Here <<--------------------------\n [ H ] - Help:\n\n Shows informations about the commands...\n---------------------->> Cut Here <<--------------------------\n\n\nThe standard commands texfile is called STANDARD.TXT and should be\nlocated at the DOORS:BBHELP/ directory. For Example\n---------------------->> Cut Here <<--------------------------\n     [<][>].....Fast Conf.joining       [ A ].......Alter File Flags  \n     [ B ]......Bulletins reading       [ C ].......Comment to Sysop  \n     [ D ].......Download File(s)       [ E ]..........Enter Message  \n     [ F ]..........File Listings       [ FS ]......Full Status View  \n     [ G ]........GoodBye(LOGOFF)       [ H ]..............Help Page  \n     [ J ]........Join Conference       [ M ]......(COLOR/MONO) Mode  \n     [ MC ].....Enter MCI Comment       [ ME ].....Enter MCI Message  \n     [ N ]...New Files Since Date       [ O ]..........Operator Page  \n     [ Q ].............Quiet Node       [ R ]..............Read Mail  \n     [ RL ]...............ReLogon       [ S ].Status of On-Line User  \n     [ T ]...................Time       [ U ].........Upload File(s)  \n     [ V ].......View a Text File       [ W ]..Write User Parameters  \n     [ WHO ].....Node Information       [ X ].....Expert Mode Toggle  \n     [ Z ]......Zippy Text Search       [ ZOOM ].........Zoo Mailing  \n---------------------->> Cut Here <<--------------------------\n\nThe sysop commands texfile is called SYSOP.TXT and should be\nlocated at the DOORS:BBHELP/ directory. For Example:\n---------------------->> Cut Here <<--------------------------\n     [ 1 ].........AccountEditing       [ 2 ].........CallerslogView\n     [ 3 ].........Edit File Dirs       [ 4 ].........Edit Text File\n     [ 5 ].........View Directory       [ 0 ]...........Remote Shell\n     [ DS ]........Sysop Download       [ VS ]............Sysop View\n---------------------->> Cut Here <<--------------------------\n\n\n     @{"BaCk To ThE mAiN mEnU" link "MAIN"}    @{"GrEeTiNgS" link "Greetings"}\n@endnode\n@node Greetings "GrEeTiNgS"\n\n\n	greetings goes to\n\n	InTeRcOdE - LAMERS INN  \n				-> cool betatester.....\n	[tHE aDDiCt/5D!] - SCUMWHERE\n				-> thx for your tools......\n				-> cool ansis...\n	[-jOE cOOl!] - WILD MARIA\n	Sisko - DEEP SPACE NINE\n	The Eremit - HIDDEN ENTRANCE\n\n\n	And to all Useres of the Homebase BBS...\n\n\n     @{"BaCk To ThE mAiN mEnU" link "MAIN"}    @{"CaLL ThEsE bOaRdS" link "Boards"}\n@endnode\n@Node Boards "CaLL ThEsE bOaRdS"\n=============================================================================\n  __  __  __   ___/\     ^   ___/\\n /  \/  \/  \  \___ \   / \  \___ \\n \/ /\/ /\/ /   | / /  / ^ \  |  \ \\n  \_\ \_\ \_\   | \ \ / ___ \ | \/ /\n  /__\/__\/__\ /____//_/   \_\|___/\n						     _____\n	___/\  ___/\ ___/\__________    /\____ ___/\/ ___/ ____________\n	\___ \/    //  /  \_   __/\ \  / /   //    /\_ \   \__/\__/\__/\n	 | / /  / // / /  / | |   / /_/ / __//  / /   \ \   \ \ \ \ \ \\n	 | \ \ /  \\ \/  /  | |  / __  /| __\\ /  \/\_/ /   / /\/ /\/ /\\n	/____/ \___\\___/   |_| /_/ /_/ |____/ \___\___/    \__/\__/\__/\n	    /__|			    /__|\n=============================================================================\n\n               CaLL tHeZe coOl ??? BaD bRoThErS ¿¿¿ BoaRDs\n\n________ _______________  ____ _______________________   ________________\n\__     \\__       __/  \/    |   ____)    __ |   ____) |  |   |  |   |  |\n |      / |     |   |         |  __/\_)   ____|_____ \  |  |   |  |   |  |\n |     /__|/\   |   |         |  \/  \   _  \         \ |  |  ||  |   |  |\n |    /      \      |  |  |   |       \  |   \         \|  |  ||  |  ||  |\n |            \ |   |  |  |   |        \ |    \        /|  |  |   |  |   |\n |____________/_|___|__|__|___|________/_|____/_______/ |__|__|___|__|___|\n\n  NoDe #1:  +49-711-ASK FOR IT	19K2 ZyXel	SysOp InTeRcOdE\n\n  NoDe #2:  +49-711-ASK FOR IT  28K8 V.FAST     CoSysOp MuRpHy & CaMpInO\n\n            ?BB¿ [ w·H·Q ]\n\n\n     \n  /\             ________                   ______\n /  \_________  /·       \___    _  _______/___   \______  _________________\n/_     |      |//  ______/   \  / \ \_____    /)   ).    \/  _____\____    /\n |:           |/  /   \ \:    \/   \/·  _____/    /||__|  \____  \/· _____/\n ||    |·     |  /    / /|  \       |  __|___     \||\__   | \/   \ __|___\n |·    |:     |\ \___/ /||   \  /   |  \    /  )   ):  |   |      / \    /\n |_____||     |\______/ |_____\/    |____  /______/|___|   |_____/____  /\n       |_______/               |____|    \/            |__/           \/\n\n  NoDe #1:  +49-711-ASK FOR IT	28K8 V.FAST	SysOp MuRpHy\n\n            ?BB¿ [ g·H·Q ]			CoSysOp InTeRcOdE & rAm\n\n     @{"BaCk To ThE mAiN mEnU" link "MAIN"}    @{"OtHeR ?BB¿ /X TooLS" link "Tools"}\n@endnode\n@Node Tools "OtHeR ?BB¿ /X ToolS"\n\n	?BB¿_oPeN	V1.o		Door Lister\n\n	?BB¿_hELP	V1.o		Help Menu\n	\n	?BB¿_fRoNtEnD	Soon		Frontend Door\n\n	?BB¿_rEqUeSt	Soon		Request Files\n\n	?BB¿_nUkE	Soon		Nuking/Commenting\n\n	?BB¿_sTaTuS	Soon		Informations Door\n\n	?BB¿_sEcUrItY   Soon		Security Tool\n\n	?BB¿_pAgE	Soon		SysOp Pager\n\n                                  @{"ByeBye" link "MAIN"}\n@endnode\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'XIM-BBSCmd',31153,4,0,NULL,NULL,'bb_h1o_bbhelp','scan',1780048423);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('bd_str20','BD-STR20.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/BD-STR20.LHA','TextStrip','XIM','TextStrip',NULL,NULL,'BD','/ __ \  / /\/\/____/\\ \\\ \ \ / /_/ /\/ / //\\  __\/ \ \/ / /_ / __  / / / //__\\ \ \   \   \__ \',replace('       ____    __    _____ ___ ____\n      / __ \  / /\/\/____/\\ \\\ \ \\n     / /_/ /\/ / //\\  __\/ \ \/ / /_\n    / __  / / / //__\\ \ \   \   \__ \\n   / /_/ / / /_/__    \ \ \___\  __ \ \\n  /_____/ /______/\____\ \/__/\\ \ \ \ \\n  \_____\/\______\/____/\____\/ \_\/\_\/\n __D·E·A·T·H - presents a new production__\n|\/¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯\/|\n|    TextStrip v0.20ß - check it out:     |\n|    ¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯     |\n| · Kill known banners from textfiles!    |\n| · Kill unused spaces and tabs!          |\n| · Add your own banners with date, time, |\n|   uploader, location & node! (/X)       |\n| · Supports two-digit nodes!             |\n| · Runs from AmiExpress or AmigaDOS!     |\n| · Nice design - just use it :)          |\n`-----------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),'TextStrip.guide',replace('@Database "TextStrip v0.16 Documentation"\n@Author "Black Death"\n@$VER: TextStrip Documentation v0.20 (10.11.94)"\n\n@Node Main "TextStrip v0.20 by Black Death"\n\n       @{b}Table of contents for TextStrip v0.20 by Black Death@{ub}\n\n    @{b}1.@{ub} @{"Introduction" Link "Intro"} .............. What is the purpose of this stripper?\n    @{b}2.@{ub} @{"Copyright" Link "Copy"} ........... Copyright information - skip this part! ;-)\n    @{b}3.@{ub} @{"Usage" Link "Usage"} ............................ How to use this program at all\n    @{b}4.@{ub} @{"Configuration" Link "Config"} ................. How to modify the recognitionfile\n    @{b}5.@{ub} @{"Adding" Link "Add"} .............................. How to add your own banners\n    @{b}6.@{ub} @{"Spaces/tabs" Link "Space"} ............................... Why are they removed?\n    @{b}7.@{ub} @{"Program status" Link "Status"} ............. Show me how many bytes I have saved!\n    @{b}8.@{ub} @{"History" Link "History"} ................................ Versions of this program\n    @{b}9.@{ub} @{"Greetings" Link "Greetings"} .................................... Personal greetings\n   @{b}10.@{ub} @{"Future plans" Link "Future"} ................. The future of this stripper if any\n\n       @{i}TextStrip is copyrighted © 1994 by Black Death, but may be spread\n       as long as all the files follow in unmodified form.@{ui}\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Intro" "Introduction"\n\n @{u}@{b}1. Introduction of this stripper@{uu}@{ub}\n\n I have only added some text to this documentation in this release. The\n old text is still the same shit, but in next release I think I will\n rewrite the whole documentation...\n\n I bet you have seen a textstripper before - you know - an utility that re-\n moves banners from textfiles? - Sure you have! But why then use mine and\n not all the others? Well, go ahead and use the others, but please keep\n these things in mind about my stripper:\n\n - It''s fast (written 100% in assembler!)\n - It''s easy to use and simple to configure.\n - It''s nice looking and very informative.\n - It runs as well from the CLI as from AmiExpress.\n - It also removes unused spaces and tabs.\n - It supports /X userdata.\n\n Still, why should you use my stripper? Well, carry on reading this docu-\n mentation, you might find some interesting things...\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Usage" "Usage"\n\n @{u}@{b}3. Program usage@{uu}@{ub}\n\n First of all, make sure that you have all the files included in this ar-\n chive. The files are:\n\n C:TextStrip\n S:TextStrip.config\n S:TextStrip.front\n S:TextStrip.tail\n\n You only need the program itself, but the other files demonstrates how to\n use the program. This program is not runable from WB, don''t try this, it\n won''t work! Try this from the CLI:\n\n TextStrip ?\n\n - And you will see some info about the program. You can also just type\n ''TextStrip'' and the usage will still show up:\n\n TextStrip [<Source>] [<Destination>] [?]\n           the destination file is optional!\n\n Remember to include the whole path of the filenames if you are not present\n in the right directory. And if you want the stripped file to overwrite the\n sourcefile, then just forget writing a destination filename. Let''s take a\n quick example, let''s say you want to strip the file ''DH1:Texts/MyFile.txt''\n to the file ''RAM:NewFile.txt''...\n\n TextStrip DH1:Texts/MyFile.txt RAM:NewFile.txt\n\n If you want to use the stripper from AmiExpress, you can either copy the\n icon included in this archive to your BBS:FCheck directory or you can make\n a script file like this:\n\n .key file\n .bra {\n .ket }\n\n C:TextStrip {file}\n\n If you don''t know how to do any of this, you shouldn''t be running a BBS!\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Copy" "Copyright"\n\n @{u}@{b}2. Copyright information about this program@{uu}@{ub}\n\n This program is not shareware, not buyware, it''s Public Domain. This means\n you can spread is to all your friends, enemies and everybody else you pos-\n sibly know! I don''t care, as long as somebody else but me find use of this\n program, I''m happy!\n\n The only thing I would not like is people performing changes to the object\n code (you know, the executable file of this archive...) If you find any\n errors or have any ideas for making this program better, please drop me a\n message at any danish elite board and I will see what I can do..\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Config" "Configuration"\n\n @{u}@{b}4. How to configure this util@{uu}@{ub}\n\n The file S:TextStrip.config contains all the banners you want the program\n to remove. All banners end with a null-byte.  Note: there should @{b}not@{ub} be a\n null-byte before the first banner and there must be one after the last one\n (otherwise this banner won''t be recognized later on).\n\n In CygnusEd you can easily make your own null-bytes in the menu ''Special/\n Enter ASCII...'', just enter a ''0'' in the requester. Or you can much faster\n copy the null-bytes already present in the config file to other positions.\n My CygnusEd 3.5 displays all null-bytes like a ''@'' character.\n\n You should not include empty lines before and after the add in the config\n file. These will automatically be removed after the stripping.\n\n In the configuration file you have the opportunity to use two different\n sorts of wildcards. One to strip a random character and one to strip a\n whole random line. If some bytes are unknown to you, you can just place a\n ''?'' instead of that byte. Let''s take an example. If you want to strip a\n line in a banner, which looks like this:\n\n         This file was uploaded at 18:11:47 on the 29-Oct-1994\n\n Just replace this line in your configfile with this:\n\n         This file was uploaded at ??:??:?? on the ??-???-????\n\n Or you can use the other built-in wildcard like this:\n\n         This file was uploaded at?#\n\n This will ignore the rest of the current line. The wilcard is the ''?#'' Now\n I''m sure you ask: "Why not use #? like in the normal AmigaDOS wildcards?"\n - Only because this is a bit easier! It doesn''t matter really, when just\n you know how to do it.. It doesn''t matter neither, if some banners have un-\n used @{"spaces/tabs" Link "Space"} included. These will be ignored, even if they are not in\n your config file. That''s because the configfile will be stripped first and\n all unused @{"spaces/tabs" Link "Space"} will not exist while stripping the file.\n\n - If the program can''t find any configuration file ''S:TextStrip.config'' it\n will simply just skip the banner remove part. It will still remove spaces\n and tabs and perhaps add your own banners, if they are present.\n\n The last configuration option you have is to @{"add your own banners" Link "Add"}. Please\n read the other section to find out how to do this..\n\n Important comment about the configuration file: always remove old banners,\n if the config file grows too big, the program will be slower and slower.\n If you only have an 68000/68020 machine the process on a 500 KB textfile,\n with 100 known banners can be extremely slow. Please cut it down often!\n You will have to make some sacrifices with your banners and your machine.\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Add" "Adding own banners"\n\n @{u}@{b}5. How to add your own banners to textfiles@{uu}@{ub}\n\n Two optional files must contain your banner, if you want to add your own\n banner:\n\n S:TextStrip.front     (This will be placed in top of textfile)\n S:TextStrip.tail      (And this in the bottom of the textfile)\n\n If one or two of these exists they will automatically be added to ALL pro-\n cessed textfiles and the TextStripper will signal this with ''ON'' or ''OFF''\n in the information border.\n\n @{u}The following strings can be inserted in your banners, explanation follows:@{uu}\n\n DD-MMM-YY                Date\n XX:XX:XX                 Time\n ==UPLOADERNAME==         Uploader name\n =L=O=C=A=T=I=O=N=!=      Uploader location\n §                        Nodenumber\n\n When these strings are found in your banner, they will be replaced with\n the data. The last three requires the program to be run from AmiExpress.\n The first two (date and time) will always work, also from the CLI. If\n you use one of the three which requires a node(x).user file without\n running the stripper from AmiExpress, you will get an error message.\n The stripper tries to find the node(x).user file in the complete path\n given to the program as argument. AmiExpress does this automatically,\n but from CLI you will have to write the whole path, e.g.\n\n TextStrip BBS:Node3/PlayPen/MyText.TXT\n\n You can also have more than 9 nodes. The stripper will work with up to\n 99 nodes, so this shouldn''t be a problem. If you have more than 9 nodes,\n the ''§'' should be replaced with ''§ '', because the stripper will overwrite\n the next byte. This means that borders around the nodenumber is also\n possible if the node sometimes can be 1 digit and sometimes 2. Check out\n this example:\n\n .--------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n | Date: DD-MMM-YY         Uploader: ==UPLOADERNAME==                       |\n | Time: XX:XX:XX          Location: =L=O=C=A=T=I=O=N=!=           Node: §  |\n `--------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n If you start the stripper from node 1, you could get this result:\n\n .--------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n | Date: 10-Nov-94         Uploader: Black Death                            |\n | Time: 19:07:43          Location: ·»              «·            Node: 1  |\n `--------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n And from node 18, this would be the result. Always remember an extra\n character after the ''§'' when running more than 9 nodes!\n\n .--------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n | Date: 10-Nov-94         Uploader: Black Death                            |\n | Time: 19:07:43          Location: ·»              «·            Node: 18 |\n `--------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n Enjoy these new options... Note: it is possible to have each entry more than\n one time! Check out the example I included in this archive. "Remote Panic"\n is the name of my board, which will open during ''95.\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Space" "The removal of spaces and tabulators"\n\n @{u}@{b}6. Why kill these?@{uu}@{ub}\n\n These are the things the stripper might kill:\n\n 1. Banners\n 2. Spaces\n 3. Tabs\n 4. Linefeeds\n\n Banners are only killed if they are present in the config file. Spaces and\n tabs will only be killed if they are the last bytes  on a line and nothing\n follows... they have nothing to do there then! When the file  is stripped,\n there might be some LF''s left in top and bottom of the file and these will\n also be removed.\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Status" "Program status"\n\n @{u}@{b}7. Showing the total number of removed bytes@{uu}@{ub}\n\n Each time the program have been used, a file called ''S:TextStrip.prefs''\n will be updated with the total number of removed bytes. The file always\n have the size of 4 bytes and will be created the first time you start\n the program. If the file does not exist, the program will create it and\n reset the counter.\n\n To show this number you will have to run the program from the CLI with\n this syntax: ''TextStrip ?'' - Among the information you will see the total\n number of removed bytes in the statistics - happiness ;-).\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "History" "Program history"\n\n @{u}@{b}8. Program history...@{uu}@{ub}\n\n There have been many betaversions of this program! - Just ask my friends,\n who betatested this for me (listed in @{"Greeting-part" Link "Greetings"}).\n\n It all started with a silly space-remover, just because I hate those edi-\n tors, which made tons of unused spaces on every single line. I gave this\n to some sysops, who didn''t even know what it was and why they should use\n it, but they started to bring me new ideas about killing banners as well,\n using wildcards, adding own banners and lots of bug-reports..\n\n I think it all came out pretty well, thanks to those, who supported the\n idea and used the stripper on their boards.\n\n If any of you have ANY ideas, ANY bugreports or ANY other things to say\n about this stripper, please leave me a message at one of these danish\n eliteboards: Static Chaos, Sky Tower, Bodycount, Mercyful Fate, The Judge.\n\n Program releases:\n ¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯\n v0.16  - First public release, no AmiExpress user.data support as well as\n          time/date. Executable size: 5112.\n\n v0.17ß - Enzo requested user.data support! So I made this and gave this\n          version to him (very buggy, I think!)\n\n v0.18ß - Enzo also wanted date/time adder, so I made this too! This\n          version was tested by Enzo and Mr.Orion. Thanks mates! ;-)\n\n v0.19ß - Hmm! Minor bug corrections. Now the program does not complain\n          about missing user.data if the file is not required, because\n          of a string replacement in the add. Date/time always available,\n          even if the user.data part failed.\n\n v0.20  - This release! Corrected some minor bugs and replaced 512\n          null-bytes in the program with AllocMem() .. hehe :)) Also\n          smashed some routines together to make the program code smaller.\n          Executable size: 6412.\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Greetings" "Personal greetings"\n\n @{u}I would like to thank the following for their support/friendship:@{uu}\n\n D-Siya   » Thanks for your betatesting and ideas and for using the\n            stripper on your board.\n\n Json     » Thanks for helping me out when I''m crying about a coding-\n            problem!\n\n Mr.Orion » Thanks for your betatesting and positive response on all my\n            work. I appreciate that. Also thank you for using my stripper\n            on your board.\n\n Enzo     » Thanks for finally using this stripper on your board! You\n            forced me to make it better! :)\n\n ZanthoR  » I appreciate your friendship - long time, no see mate. Call\n            me anytime and we will arrange a meeting between Rødovre and\n            Ebeltoft.. ;-)\n\n Daddy    » FatMan Productions rulez, eh! - Never forget that. See you in\n Cool     » two weeks... let''s party.. let''s EAT!! And visit Camilla Hove!\n\n Bonzo    » You are my stupid dog, thanks for not speaking to me!\n\n Leon     » You have got the place right after my dog, you really deserved\n            this :) - Keep using ProTracker, even though I don''t like your\n            music.. I like your company, friend!\n\n @{b}And last of all...@{ub}\n    __\n __///  Thanks to Amiga® for being the best computer ever!\n \XX/   And thanks to Pepsi® for being the best cola ever!\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Future" "Future plans"\n\n @{u}@{b}10. Future applications@{uu}@{ub}\n\n If I release new versions of this util, I hope to include the following\n features...\n\n Wildcard support on files to be stripped (nonBBS-use)\n · All files with .TXT extension in directory\n · Reqtools-requester to allow user to pick files easily\n\n Show percentage of bytes removed!\n\n Perhaps some random routines to make the banners different each time,\n hehe! - Maybe not, I hate this myself!\n\n And perhaps a new recognition routine, so it won''t be necesarry to go\n through all bytes in banners to be recognized.\n\n IMPORTANT: If you find THE bugs (!), please send me a message!\n@EndNode\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',21134,1,0,NULL,NULL,'bd_str20','scan',1780048423);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('bd_str21','BD-STR21.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/BD-STR21.LHA',NULL,'XIM','TextStrip',NULL,NULL,'BD',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'auto-added',23176,0,0,NULL,NULL,'bd_str21','scan',1780048423);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('bd_str27','BD-STR27.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/BD-STR27.LHA','TextStrip','XIM','TextStrip',NULL,NULL,'BD','\___ ¬\\_¬| \___¬\/  __/__ Y ¬\ /  ''  //  |/\/_Y  \  Y__/    _/ /  ,  ¬\   !  \\_   \ ! ¬\  | ¬\',replace('      ______ ___. _____  ________ __\n      \___ ¬\\_¬| \___¬\/  __/__ Y ¬\\n      /  ''  //  |/\/_Y  \  Y__/    _/\n     /  ,  ¬\   !  \\_   \ ! ¬\  | ¬\\n     \______/______/_l___/____/__l__/\n __   D·E·A·T·H - presents again the     __\n|\/¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯\/|\n|  TextStrip v0.27 - new features & more!  |\n|  ¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯  |\n| · Kill known banners and spaces/tabs!    |\n| · Add own banners with /X-support!       |\n| · Coded in assembler for maximum speed!  |\n`------------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),'TextStrip.guide',replace('@Database "TextStrip v0.27 Documentation"\n@Author "Black Death"\n@$VER: TextStrip Documentation v0.27 (07.12.94)"\n\n@Node Main "TextStrip v0.27 by Black Death"\n\n       @{b}Table of contents for TextStrip v0.27 by Black Death@{ub}\n\n    @{b}1.@{ub} @{"Introduction" Link "Intro"} .............. What is the purpose of this stripper?\n    @{b}2.@{ub} @{"Copyright" Link "Copy"} ........... Copyright information - skip this part! ;-)\n    @{b}3.@{ub} @{"Usage" Link "Usage"} ............................ How to use this program at all\n    @{b}4.@{ub} @{"Configuration" Link "Config"} ................. How to modify the recognitionfile\n    @{b}5.@{ub} @{"Adding" Link "Add"} .............................. How to add your own banners\n    @{b}6.@{ub} @{"Spaces/tabs" Link "Space"} ............................... Why are they removed?\n    @{b}7.@{ub} @{"Program status" Link "Status"} ............. Show me how many bytes I have saved!\n    @{b}8.@{ub} @{"History" Link "History"} ................................ Versions of this program\n    @{b}9.@{ub} @{"Greetings" Link "Greetings"} .................................... Personal greetings\n   @{b}10.@{ub} @{"Future plans" Link "Future"} ................. The future of this stripper if any\n\n   @{b}11.@{ub} @{"NEW!" Link "News"} ......................................... Since last version\n\n       @{i}TextStrip is copyrighted © 1994 by Black Death, but may be spread\n       as long as all the files follow in unmodified form.@{ui}\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Intro" "Introduction"\n\n @{u}@{b}1. Introduction of this stripper@{uu}@{ub}\n\n I bet you have seen a textstripper before - you know - an utility that re-\n moves banners from textfiles? - Sure you have! But why then use mine and\n not all the others? Well, go ahead and use the others, but please keep\n these things in mind about my stripper:\n\n - It''s fast (written 100% in assembler!)\n - It''s easy to use and simple to configure.\n - It''s nice looking and very informative.\n - It runs as well from the CLI as from AmiExpress.\n - It also removes unused spaces and tabs.\n - It supports /X userdata.\n\n Still, why should you use my stripper? Well, carry on reading this docu-\n mentation, you might find some interesting things...\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Usage" "Usage"\n\n @{u}@{b}3. Program usage@{uu}@{ub}\n\n First of all, make sure that you have all the files included in this ar-\n chive. The files are:\n\n BBS/FCheck/ASC.info        \\n BBS/FCheck/DOC.info         \  /X-filecheck icons, copy these to your\n BBS/FCheck/NFO.info         /  BBS:FCheck-directory for fast installation\n BBS/FCheck/TXT.info        /\n C/TextStrip                    The program itself - place this in C:\n S/TextStrip.config             Configuration file\n S/TextStrip.front           \  Your own banners to add\n S/TextStrip.tail            /  Edit these to your own needs\n BBS.info\n TextStrip.guide                This documentation\n TextStrip.guide.info\n TextStrip.iff                  A sample picture, shows the design\n\n You only need the program itself, but the other files demonstrates how to\n use the program. This program is not runable from WB, don''t try this, it\n won''t work! Try this from the CLI:\n\n ''TextStrip'' and this helpscreen will show up:\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTextStrip 0.26ß (adder/stripper) - © 1994 by Black Death\n\nThis tool removes all known banners from textfiles and adds your own.\nIf spaces and tabs  are found before a linefeed, these are removed as\nwell. Carriage returns will also be removed if specified.\n\n  Args:  [source]      - Textfile to be stripped\n         [destination] - Destination file (optional)\n\n         [NOSTRIP]     - Skip stripping part\n         [NOADD]       - Don''t add your own banners\n         [NOINFOADD]   - Don''t add tagline in bottom of file\n         [STRIPCR]     - Strip CR''s also\n         [BBS]         - Output information with nice ANSI\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n ''TextStrip ?'' for the usage:\n\n SOURCE/A,DESTINATION/M,NOSTRIP/S,NOADD/S,NOINFOADD/S,STRIPCR/S,BBS/S:\n\n Remember to include the whole path of the filenames if you are not present\n in the right directory. And if you want the stripped file to overwrite the\n sourcefile, then just forget writing a destination filename. Let''s take a\n quick example, let''s say you want to strip the file ''DH1:Texts/MyFile.txt''\n to the file ''RAM:NewFile.txt''...\n\n TextStrip DH1:Texts/MyFile.txt RAM:NewFile.txt\n Let''s take a look at the switches:\n\n NOSTRIP   - The configfile won''t be read/used and no banners will be\n             removed.\n NOADD     - Your own banners S:TextStrip.front and S:TextStrip.tail\n             won''t be added - useful when running a BBS and using the\n             stripper from the CLI on private files.\n NOINFOADD - My tagline won''t be added. Remember to include this line in\n             your configfile, so only one line will appear in every file.\n STRIPCR   - Strip those carriage returns from the PC (ascii-code #13)\n BBS       - Layout for BBS use (with ANSI-codes and stuff ;-)\n\n If you want to use the stripper from AmiExpress, you can either copy the\n icon included in this archive to your BBS:FCheck directory or you can make\n a script file like this:\n\n .key file\n .bra {\n .ket }\n\n C:TextStrip "{file}" STRIPCR BBS\n\n If you don''t know how to do any of this, you shouldn''t be running a BBS!\n Please note the new BBS-switch option. If you don''t use this the program\n won''t show ANSI''s but just some normal AmigaDOS-stuff. Actually I think\n this looks better from the CLI!\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Copy" "Copyright"\n\n @{u}@{b}2. Copyright information about this program@{uu}@{ub}\n\n This program is not shareware, not buyware, it''s Public Domain. This means\n you can spread is to all your friends, enemies and everybody else you pos-\n sibly know! I don''t care, as long as somebody else but me find use of this\n program, I''m happy!\n\n The only thing I would not like is people performing changes to the object\n code (you know, the executable file of this archive...) If you find any\n errors or have any ideas for making this program better, please drop me a\n message at any danish elite board and I will see what I can do..\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Config" "Configuration"\n\n @{u}@{b}4. How to configure this util@{uu}@{ub}\n\n The file S:TextStrip.config contains all the banners you want the program\n to remove. All banners end with a null-byte.  Note: there should @{b}not@{ub} be a\n null-byte before the first banner and there must be one after the last one\n (otherwise this banner won''t be recognized later on).\n\n In CygnusEd you can easily make your own null-bytes in the menu ''Special/\n Enter ASCII...'', just enter a ''0'' in the requester. Or you can much faster\n copy the null-bytes already present in the config file to other positions.\n My CygnusEd 3.5 displays all null-bytes like a ''@'' character.\n\n You should not include empty lines before and after the add in the config\n file. These will automatically be removed after the stripping.\n\n In the configuration file you have the opportunity to use two different\n sorts of wildcards. One to strip a random character and one to strip a\n whole random line. If some bytes are unknown to you, you can just place a\n ''?'' instead of that byte. Let''s take an example. If you want to strip a\n line in a banner, which looks like this:\n\n         This file was uploaded at 18:11:47 on the 29-Oct-1994\n\n Just replace this line in your configfile with this:\n\n         This file was uploaded at ??:??:?? on the ??-???-????\n\n Or you can use the other built-in wildcard like this:\n\n         This file was uploaded at?#\n\n This will ignore the rest of the current line. The wilcard is the ''?#'' Now\n I''m sure you ask: "Why not use #? like in the normal AmigaDOS wildcards?"\n - Only because this is a bit easier! It doesn''t matter really, when just\n you know how to do it.. It doesn''t matter neither, if some banners have un-\n used @{"spaces/tabs" Link "Space"} included. These will be ignored, even if they are not in\n your config file. That''s because the configfile will be stripped first and\n all unused @{"spaces/tabs" Link "Space"} will not exist while stripping the file.\n\n - If the program can''t find any configuration file ''S:TextStrip.config'' it\n will simply just skip the banner remove part. It will still remove spaces\n and tabs and perhaps add your own banners, if they are present.\n\n The last configuration option you have is to @{"add your own banners" Link "Add"}. Please\n read the other section to find out how to do this..\n\n Important comment about the configuration file: always remove old banners,\n if the config file grows too big, the program will be slower and slower.\n If you only have an 68000/68020 machine the process on a 500 KB textfile,\n with 100 known banners can be extremely slow. Please cut it down often!\n You will have to make some sacrifices with your banners and your machine.\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Add" "Adding own banners"\n\n @{u}@{b}5. How to add your own banners to textfiles@{uu}@{ub}\n\n Two optional files must contain your banner, if you want to add your own\n banner:\n\n S:TextStrip.front     (This will be placed in top of textfile)\n S:TextStrip.tail      (And this in the bottom of the textfile)\n\n If one or two of these exists they will automatically be added to ALL pro-\n cessed textfiles and the TextStripper will signal this with ''ON'' or ''OFF''\n in the information border.\n\n @{u}The following strings can be inserted in your banners, explanation follows:@{uu}\n\n String in configfile     Description         Example\n ------------------------ ------------------- ---------------\n MM-DD-YY                 Date 1              (12-07-94)\n DD-MM-YY                 Date 2              (07-12-94)\n DD-MMM-YY                Date 3              (07-Dec-94)\n YY-MMM-DD                Date 4              (94-Dec-07)\n HH:MM:SS                 Time 1              (21:08:13)\n HH:MM                    Time 2              (21:08)\n ===WEEKDAY===            Weekday             (Wednesday)\n\n ==UPLOADERNAME==         Uploader name       (Black Death)\n =L=O=C=A=T=I=O=N=!=      Uploader location   (Nowhere)\n §                        Nodenumber          (1)\n\n When these strings are found in your banner, they will be replaced with\n the data. The last three requires the program to be run from AmiExpress.\n The first seven (date and time) will always work, also from the CLI. If\n you use one of the three which requires a node(x).user file without\n running the stripper from AmiExpress, you will get an error message.\n The stripper tries to find the node(x).user file in the complete path\n given to the program as argument. AmiExpress does this automatically,\n but from CLI you will have to write the whole path, e.g.\n\n Please note that all the date/weekday replacements are supporting locale!\n So if you are using e.g. danish locale ''Sunday'' will become ''Søndag''..\n\n TextStrip BBS:Node3/PlayPen/MyText.TXT\n\n You can also have more than 9 nodes. The stripper will work with up to\n 99 nodes, so this shouldn''t be a problem. If you have more than 9 nodes,\n the ''§'' should be replaced with ''§ '', because the stripper will overwrite\n the next byte. This means that borders around the nodenumber is also\n possible if the node sometimes can be 1 digit and sometimes 2. Check out\n this example:\n\n .--------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n | Date: DD-MMM-YY         Uploader: ==UPLOADERNAME==                       |\n | Time: XX:XX:XX          Location: =L=O=C=A=T=I=O=N=!=           Node: §  |\n `--------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n If you start the stripper from node 1, you could get this result:\n\n .--------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n | Date: 10-Nov-94         Uploader: Black Death                            |\n | Time: 19:07:43          Location: ·»              «·            Node: 1  |\n `--------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n And from node 18, this would be the result. Always remember an extra\n character after the ''§'' when running more than 9 nodes!\n\n .--------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n | Date: 10-Nov-94         Uploader: Black Death                            |\n | Time: 19:07:43          Location: ·»              «·            Node: 18 |\n `--------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n Enjoy these new options... Note: it is possible to have each entry more than\n one time! Check out the example I included in this archive. "Remote Panic"\n is the name of my board, which will open during ''95.\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Space" "The removal of spaces and tabulators"\n\n @{u}@{b}6. Why kill these?@{uu}@{ub}\n\n These are the things the stripper might kill:\n\n 1. Banners\n 2. Spaces\n 3. Tabs\n 4. Linefeeds\n 5. Carriage returns\n\n Banners are only killed if they are present in the config file. Spaces and\n tabs will only be killed if they are the last bytes  on a line and nothing\n follows... they have nothing to do there then! When the file  is stripped,\n there might be some LF''s left in top and bottom of the file and these will\n also be removed.\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Status" "Program status"\n\n @{u}@{b}7. Showing the total number of removed bytes@{uu}@{ub}\n\n The opportunity to view the total number of removed bytes has been\n removed! So no S:TextStrip.prefs file is needed anymore, hope will\n live without this small option - I don''t think anybody used it anyway..\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "History" "Program history"\n\n @{u}@{b}8. Program history...@{uu}@{ub}\n\n There have been many betaversions of this program! - Just ask my friends,\n who betatested this for me (listed in @{"Greeting-part" Link "Greetings"}).\n\n It all started with a silly space-remover, just because I hate those edi-\n tors, which made tons of unused spaces on every single line. I gave this\n to some sysops, who didn''t even know what it was and why they should use\n it, but they started to bring me new ideas about killing banners as well,\n using wildcards, adding own banners and lots of bug-reports..\n\n I think it all came out pretty well, thanks to those, who supported the\n idea and used the stripper on their boards.\n\n If any of you have ANY ideas, ANY bugreports or ANY other things to say\n about this stripper, please leave me a message at one of these danish\n eliteboards: Static Chaos, Sky Tower, Bodycount, Mercyful Fate, The Judge.\n\n Program releases:\n ¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯¯\n v0.16  - First public release, no AmiExpress user.data support as well as\n          time/date. Executable size: 5112.\n\n v0.17ß - Enzo requested user.data support! So I made this and gave this\n          version to him (very buggy, I think!)\n\n v0.18ß - Enzo also wanted date/time adder, so I made this too! This\n          version was tested by Enzo and Mr.Orion. Thanks mates! ;-)\n\n v0.19ß - Hmm! Minor bug corrections. Now the program does not complain\n          about missing user.data if the file is not required, because\n          of a string replacement in the add. Date/time always available,\n          even if the user.data part failed.\n\n v0.20ß - Corrected some minor bugs and replaced 512 null-bytes in the\n          program with AllocMem() .. hehe :)) Also smashed some routines\n          together to make the program code smaller. Executable size: 6412.\n\n v0.21  - v0.20ß gave some sick dos-returncodes. This version doesn''t!\n          Executable size: 6404 bytes.\n\n v0.22ß - Now shows percent of file removed (19-11-94)\n\n v0.23ß - Added some new date/time-formats (19-11-94) - (20 minutes after\n          v0.22ß ;-)\n\n v0.24ß - New ReadArgs() funktion, now uses switches to different things.\n          Spaces in filename now allowed, but remember "" around the name.\n\n v0.25ß - Now CR''s will be removed as well, if selected with the STRIPCR\n          switch. This also infects the configfile, so if the configfile\n          has a CR in an add and the textfile doesn''t, the add will be\n          removed anyway.\n\n v0.26ß - Added new design so the program will look good from the CLI as\n          well as from AmiExpress with ANSI''s.. Use the ''BBS'' switch to\n          turn on BBS-mode.\n\n v0.27  - Didn''t really change important things.. just some minors,\n          perhaps some bugs..?! Executable size: 7844 bytes.\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Greetings" "Personal greetings"\n\n @{u}I would like to thank the following for their support/friendship:@{uu}\n\n No time for personal greetings, but here''s a list of those I want to\n greet/thank...\n\n Daddy Cool, Leon, Deckard, Gifair, Json, Devilstar, Yobbo, D-Siya,\n Doomlord, Relief & Mr. Orion   (no special order!)\n\n A greeting to all sysops who add pictures in their LHA archives! .. Stop\n this sick sence of humour! ... I hate buying new HD''s every week, because\n it''s filled with this kind of crap.\n\n @{b}And last of all...@{ub}\n    __\n __///  Thanks to Amiga® for being the best computer ever!\n \XX/   And thanks to Pepsi® for being the best cola ever!\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "Future" "Future plans"\n\n @{u}@{b}10. Future applications@{uu}@{ub}\n\n If I release new versions of this util, I hope to include the following\n features...\n\n Wildcard support on files to be stripped (nonBBS-use)\n · All files with .TXT extension in directory\n · Reqtools-requester to allow user to pick files easily\n\n Perhaps some random routines to make the banners different each time,\n hehe! - Maybe not, I hate this myself!\n\n And perhaps a new recognition routine, so it won''t be necesarry to go\n through all bytes in banners to be recognized.\n\n IMPORTANT: If you find THE bugs (!), please send me a message!\n@EndNode\n\n@Node "News" "New since last version"\n\n @{u}@{b}11. Short list of new features..@{uu}@{ub}\n\n · New time/date-formats:\n\n   HH:MM:SS         20:49:54     (used to be XX:XX:XX)\n   HH:MM            20:49\n   DD-MMM-YY        07-Dec-94    (same as before)\n   YY-MMM-DD        94-Dec-07\n   MM-DD-YY         12-07-94     (USA format)\n   DD-MM-YY         07-12-94     (European format)\n\n · New weekday option:\n   ===WEEKDAY===    Wednesday\n\n · Shows percent of bytes removed\n · New argumentroutine - new usage:\n\n   SOURCE/A,DESTINATION/M,NOSTRIP/S,NOADD/S,NOINFOADD/S,STRIPCR/S\n\n   Read about this in the @{"USAGE" Link "Usage"} chapter!\n\n · Adds tagline with name of stripper (optional)\n · Strips carriage returns as well (optional)\n@EndNode\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',34009,2,0,NULL,NULL,'bd_str27','scan',1780048423);
