7-GET-REAL   | pRINCE oF tHIEVES\n        rOYAL sCHQ  |   tHIRD  NODE:  T E L N E T!   | lABEL·jEDI!·uFOk\n          cRUX nHQ  |   fOURTH NODE:  T E L N E T!   | aXEN·mR.Z·rAJ!\n           lSD nHQ  |   fIFTH  NODE:  T E L N E T!   |\n           hORD hQ  ¦                                ¦ aMIGA/pC/pSX\n                    :  33.6 V.EVERYTHING oF cOURSE!  : oNLY!\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',15356,4,0,NULL,NULL,'ds_dp10','scan',1780048488);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('ds9_v1_2','DS9.V1.2.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DS9.V1.2.LHA','DeepHost','XIM','DeepHost','1','Node/Player',NULL,'Space Role Playing Game. DeepSpace is an ansi/ascii text space role playing game.',replace('DeepSpaceV1_2. Multi-Node/Player\nSpace Role Playing Game.\nDeepSpace is an ansi/ascii text\nspace role playing game.\nFrom Aminet by Gary. (20.05.94)\n','\n',char(10)),'DeepSpace.guide',replace('@database DeepSpace\n\n@node main "DeepSpace! Docs"\n                 \n @{" About DeepSpace " link about}\n\n @{" Legal Stuff " link leagalstuff}\n\n @{" Setting up DeepSpace " link setup}\n                         \n @{" Registering " link register}\n                         \n @{" Known Bugs " link bugs}\n        \n @{" History " link history}\n        \n @{" Future " link fut}\n                                \n@endnode\n@node about "About DeepSpace"\n\n     DeepSpace is a Multi-Player, Multi-Node Space Role Playing Game.\n     BBS''s that can run DOS doors (programs that use stdin & stdout) can\n     run this game.\n      \n     *Note a Paragon Version is also available.\n      DeepSpaceP will allow only one node at a time. \n      \n     Twenty nodes can play at one time. This has never been actually\n     tested though. Up to 100 users can join the game if @{"REGISTERED" link register}\n     If it isn''t @{"REGISTERED" link register} only 10 players is allowed.\n            \n     Enforcer & Mungwall has been used during development of this version. There should\n     not be any hits. Unless I mist one.\n     \n     DeepSpaceP (Paragon Version)   \n#    DeepSpace \n#    DeepHost\n#    DeepSet\n          Programmed by Todd Neumiller\n     GameSetUp\n          Programmed by Todd & Darrell Neumiller\n     Ansi GFX \n          Rod Whitney & Darren Campbell\n\n# = only in Dos version           \n                                 \n@endnode\n\n@node leagalstuff "Legal Stuff"\n \n     DeepSpace (c)\n \n     These Files and their related documentation are provided "AS-IS" and \n     are subject to change without notice; no warranties are made.  All \n     use is at your own risk.  No liability or responsibility is assumed.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node setup "Setting up DeepSpace"\n\n     WorkBench 2.0 or greater is required.\n\n     In the directory you unarced the DeepSpace archive, you will have\n     GameSetUp\n     DeepHost \n     DeepSet  ; used by DeepHost\n     DeepSpace\n     Text (dir) w/ numerous text files\n          Ones of interest for setting up DeepSpace are:\n          1: @{"AlienRaces" link araces}\n          2: @{"Sysop.txt " link sysop.txt}      \n     ENV: \n          3: @{"config    " link conf}\n\n     After the above mentioned files are edited to your liking run \n     GameSetup.  This program will generate all the files needed to\n     run DeepSpace.  GameSetup should also be ran when you wish to\n     re-roll the game.\n     * NEW V1.0 *\n     Also the path to which you have set up DeepSpace in can be\n     saved into your config file. It is optional. If the files \n     can not be found by DeepSpace fill in this option.\n     Give it the path you are running Gamesetup in.\n     Example: Dh0:Deepspace/\n     \n     DeepHost\n     This program is a background server for the DeepSpace game.\n     After gamesetup has been ran you can now start DeepHost. I\n     suggest you install it in your user-startup.\n     NOTE: It must remain in the same directory as the other files.\n           Do not copy it to another directory.\n           \n     Example: \n          run dh0:deepspace/deephost <nil: >nil:  \n     It runs DeepSet once to load all the data needed for the game.\n     This allows DeepHost to become somewhat smaller in size. \n     To shut down DeepHost just simply run DeepHost again. It will \n     signal the original DeepHost program to shut down. A requester\n     will pop up showing you that it is shutting down. If you do not\n     want a requester to pop up (via remote or ??) Give DeepHost an\n     argument. \n     Example:\n          DeepHost quiet\n     This will force the requester to stay off.\n          NOTE: Do not give an argument unless shutting DeepHost off!!!!\n     If any games are being played when you shut off the DeepHost program\n     they also will be signaled to stop.     \n                                         \n     You are ready to go! The program has only been tested on a Excelsior BBS\n     as of late. If you have ran it on any-other BBS please add a comment on the \n     bottom of this file giving directions if anything different in the\n     setup was required.\n     Also please let us know!\n\n     If you are able to adjust the priority DON''T. Just run DeepSpace at 0.\n  \nDeepSpace Arguments:\n--------------------\nUsage:\nDeepSpace Name/A/M,T=Time/A/N,Echo/S,D=Delay/N,A=Delay/N,CNET/S\n\nName      The users name\n            /A - Required\n            /M - Multiple strings allowed\nT=Time    The time allowed to play\n            /A - Required\n            /N - numbers\nEcho      Echo key press''s\n            /S - switch\nD=Delay   Fight delay instead of default (40000)\n            /N - numbers (1 to 200000000) \n          NOTE: If D is set to 0 it forces all players have the Auto-Attack on.\n                Do NOT set to a negative number.\nA=Delay   An alternate delay method. (1 - 200)\n                                             (ticks 50 = one second)\n          Don''t use this option unless you have to.                                   \n          This depends on the BBS you are using.                                   \n          During an attack sequence, numbers that range from 1 to 10 will be \n          printed. The object is to hit a key as close as possible to 10.\n          This is the percent power used during that fight wave. If all the\n          numbers flash by with out a delay you have to use this option.\n          If this occurs and you do not use this option the game, the program will \n          eat up a lot of CPU time. Have this set even if you want Auto-Attack on.\n                                                       (none interaction fight)\n          Set d to 0 if you want Auto-Attack on.\n          Set a to a min of 6.\n          @{"Testing setup delays" link sud}\n\nCNET      A flag for CNET BBS only!!!!\n          To use %7 for passing time left you have to use this switch.\n          I was told that this pass''s time in tenths of a minute. This\n          switch tell DeepSpace to convert the time.\n                     \nNOTE: Name & Time are REQUIRED!                         \n      Replace name(s) ,time with the proper BBS codes which are applicable. \nAn example:  \n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 Echo D=60000\n  Name is John Doe, Time allowed 120 minutes, Echo on, Delay set to 60000\n  * for most BBS''s\n\nOther examples:\n\n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 (Min arguments)\n  Echo off using default delay of 40000 for the Attack delay.\n             \n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 d=0\n  Auto-Attack always on: Which will take away this option for the player\n  to toggle themselves.\n  \n          DeepSpace John Doe t=30 echo a=8\n  Alternate delay method. Since d is at the default (40000) Auto-Attack off \n  (which will make it optional to the player).  Delay of 8 ticks for the attack \n  delay & echo on.        \n             \n          DeepSpace John Doe t=30 echo d=0 a=8\n  Alternate delay method. Auto-Attack always on. Delay of 8 ticks\n  for the attack delay & echo on.        \n        \n                   \n@endnode\n@node sud "Testing setup delays"\n\n  If you are using the D option for the attack delay set it very high at \n  first. (~ 100000)  Or the A option set at ~ 20.  Have someone attack an \n  alien via remote (to find the Alien use the sysop editor & check which \n  sector they are in or move them to your sector).  Check the speed during\n  the fight. Change the attacking ship to a Sprite (only 1 on offense) \n  against Fed ship (5 defense). This will be the fastest speed at which\n  the fight sequence will go. And adjust from there..   \n\n  *NOTE: if you are using the D option for the delay (at 100000) and the \n         fighting sequence flies by before you can push a key this indicates \n         that you have to use the A option.\n         \n@endnode \n@node araces "AlienRaces"\n\n     Text/AlienRaces\n     \n     AlienRaces is a simple text file. It can be edited with any type of text\n     editor.  It is a list of Alien names that will be given to the generated \n     alien''s in the game.  Example: Klingon\n     \n     You must have this file in the text directory or the GameSetup program \n     will fail. (you will read about GameSetup later). \n     \n     The maximum number of alien race names used is 50.  If you have 25 or \n     less names in the text file, 2 aliens will be generated for each race.\n     13 names = 26 total aliens\n     26 names = 26 total aliens ect..\n        \n@endnode\n@node sysop.txt "Sysop.txt"\n\n     Text/sysop.txt\n     \n     sysop.txt is a list of names that DeepSpace can access for\n     getting the sysop(s) name(s). The names in this simple text\n     file should match the name that will be past to DeepSpace\n     for the players name.\n     \n     For example you and your Co-Sysop''s handle''s were ''Roentgen'' \n     & ''Big Byte'' the text file would just contain these 2\n     names. Do not have trailing spaces behind these names. Hit \n     enter right after the full name is typed.\n\n     To enter the Planet editor hit ''!'' at the main menu. The \n     Planet editor is self explanatory.  To access the user\n     editor hit ''@'' at the main prompt.\n     \n                     \n@endnode\n@node conf "Config"\n\n     ENV:config \n     \n     This is a file that GameSetup will create. If you are registered\n     you have to make sure this file is not distributed.  \n\n@endnode\n\n@node register "Registering DeepSpace"\n\n     Registering DeepSpace can be done by sending $20 US dollars to:\n\n     Todd Neumiller\n     P.O. Box 18591\n     Spokane, WA 99208 USA                \n     \nInfo needed:\n     1: Your FULL name\n     2: Handle if used\n     3: BBS name (BBS phone #) \n     4: Mailing address\n\n     You can also get more information by calling\n     \n     Internet       [email removed] (Darrell Neumiller)\n     Fragmented BBS 509-926-5507 Sysop Darrell Neumiller AKA Big Byte\n                                 CoSysop ME! Todd Neumiller AKA Roentgen\n     Or                            \n     Ami-Tech BBS   509-244-2346 Sysop Rod Whitney AKA Spark Chaser\n     Planet Praxis  509-244-0336 Sysop Darren Cambpell AKA Commander Chang\n     or\n     Planet Praxis\n          FIDONET   \n               Todd Neumiller@1:346/31\n          INTERNET     \n               [email removed]\n     \n               \n@endnode\n\n@node bugs "Known BUGS"\n\n     I fix em as I find em! Or when someone else finds em.\n     Thanks for all the bug reports! \n     \n@endnode\n\n@node history "History"\n\nV.6  First working version.\n\nV.7  Found Mungwall hit & fixed it. Fixed the bug that only allowed a player\n     to e-mail someone with a user # with 1 digit.\n     *Note: Paragon version of DeepSpace created.\n\nV1.0 Big changes to timing method in DeepSpace. Herman Stevens from\n     Laukensstraat Belgium deserves all the credit. Informed me about\n     it and also told me how to fix it! (THANKS!) \n     In showing known/unknown/avoided sectors could crash. This was fixed.\n     Spelling fixes - Yah I can''t Spel \n     Other small odds and ends....\n     \n     GameSetup changed. Added the ability for the path to be saved into\n     the config file. The config file is saved in ENV: ENVARC: \n     This was done to hopefully fix the problem DLG BBS''s have running\n     DeepSpace in a directory besides the assigned DLG: directory.\n     \nV1.2 /* NOTE you do not have to re-roll your game if you were running the\n        last version (V1.0) */\n     MORE spelling fixes!\n     Are you sure (Y/n) prompts fixed so you really can change your mind.\n     Sysop editor: Can now move someone to sector 1001\n     ANSI files re-worked a little. \n     A switch for CNET to convert the time argument. \n     Some problems around porting at sector 1.\n     Can''t make a fortune selling your ship over & over.\n     GameSetup changed so you do not have to re-roll to add registration.\n         \n@endnode\n\n@node fut "Future"\n\n     We have had a lot of GREAT suggestions. I am keeping a list of them.\n     I wanted to get rid of all the problems I can find before adding\n     a lot more features in the game.\n     \n     Possible new features\n     \n     PhaseII:\n          Planet Praxis:\n          If you go through the BlackHole when it is un-stable \n          (and make it through) you probably have seen the planet ''Praxis''.\n          We plan on making this a ''second'' phase of the game. The player\n          could land on the planet & leave his ship.  Hostile Mutants & \n          Aliens would be populated on the planet.  Different objects\n          could be obtained there. Weapons? Better Scanners? ect..\n          An example being considered: A VERY VERY RARE weapon that can be\n          left at the DeepSpace port. The weapon could be programed to\n          detonate when a certain Explorer arrived. (DNA scan)\n          It would shoot a projectile at the head of the player.\n          This would be for you very best Explorer friends!\n             \n          Gambling:\n          Alot more ways to win $$. Possibly against other players as well.   \n          \n          Security:\n          The Shape Shifter could be anywhere! Selling illegal stuff from\n          planet Praxis? Could end up in jail with no way out, or\n          will there be a way out?\n           \n          \n@endnode\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',187672,3,0,NULL,NULL,'ds9_v1_2','scan',1780048488);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('ds9_v1_4','DS9.V1.4.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DS9.V1.4.LHA','DeepSpace','XIM','DeepHost','1.4',NULL,NULL,NULL,replace('DEEPSPACE V1.4 MULTI-NODE/PLAYER            \n','\n',char(10)),'DeepSpace.guide',replace('@database DeepSpace\n\n@node main "DeepSpace! Docs"\n                 \n @{" About DeepSpace " link about}\n\n @{" Legal Stuff " link leagalstuff}\n\n @{" Setting up DeepSpace " link setup}\n                         \n @{" Registering " link register}\n                         \n @{" Known Bugs " link bugs}\n        \n @{" History " link history}\n        \n @{" Future " link fut}\n                                \n@endnode\n@node about "About DeepSpace"\n\n     DeepSpace is a Multi-Player, Multi-Node Space Role Playing Game.\n     BBS''s that can run DOS doors (programs that use stdin & stdout) can\n     run this game.\n      \n     *Note a Paragon Version is also available. DeepSpaceP will allow only \n     one node at a time to run.  Enough said about this version..\n      \n     Twenty nodes can play at one time, this has never been actually\n     tested though. Up to 100 users can join the game if @{"REGISTERED" link register}\n     If it isn''t @{"REGISTERED" link register} only 10 players are allowed.\n            \n     Enforcer & Mungwall has been used during development of this version. There should\n     not be any hits, unless I missed one. We have also setup a 10 minute inactivity\n     timer in the game. This means that if a user playing the game doesn''t press \n     a key for 10 minutes the game will automatically clean up and exit.\n     \n     DeepSpaceP (Paragon Version)   \n#    DeepSpace \n#    DeepHost\n#    DeepSet\n          Programmed by Todd Neumiller\n     GameSetUp\n          Programmed by Todd & Darrell Neumiller\n     Ansi GFX \n          Rod Whitney & Darren Campbell\n\n# = only in Dos version           \n                                 \n@endnode\n\n@node leagalstuff "Legal Stuff"\n \n     DeepSpace (c)\n \n     These Files and their related documentation are provided "AS-IS" and \n     are subject to change without notice, no warranties are made.  All \n     use, is at your own risk.  No liability or responsibility is assumed.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node setup "Setting up DeepSpace"\n\n     WorkBench 2.0 or greater is required.\n\n     In the directory you unarced the DeepSpace archive, you will have\n     GameSetUp\n     DeepHost \n     DeepSet  - This is used by the DeepHost program.\n     DeepSpace\n     DeepSpace.guide (the file your reading)\n     Text (dir) w/ numerous text files\n          Ones of interest for setting up the config for DeepSpace are:\n          1: @{"Text/AlienRaces" link araces}\n          2: @{"Text/Sysop.txt " link sysop.txt}\n          3: @{"Text/Remarks.txt " link remarks.txt}            \n     ENV: \n          4: @{"ENV:config    " link conf}\n\n     After the above mentioned files are edited to your liking run \n     GameSetup.  This program will generate all the files needed to\n     run DeepSpace.  GameSetup should also be ran when you wish to\n     re-roll the game or when you receive the registraion information.\n\n     * NEW V1.0 *\n     Also the path to which you have set up DeepSpace in can be\n     saved into your config file. It is optional. If the files \n     can not be found by DeepSpace fill in this option.\n     Give it the path you are running Gamesetup in.\n     Example: Dh0:Doors/Deepspace/\n     \n     DeepHost\n     This program is a background server for the DeepSpace game.\n     After gamesetup has been ran you can now start DeepHost. I\n     suggest you run it from your S:User-Startup file.\n     NOTE: It must remain in the same directory as the other files.\n           Do not copy it to another directory.\n           \n     Example: \n          run dh0:doors/deepspace/deephost <nil: >nil:  \n     It runs DeepSet, which loads all the data needed for the game.\n     This allows DeepHost to become somewhat smaller in size.\n     To shut down DeepHost just simply run DeepHost again. It will \n     signal the original DeepHost program to shut down. A requester\n     will pop up showing you that it is shutting down. If you do not\n     want a requester to pop up (via remote or ??) Give DeepHost an\n     argument. \n     Example:\n          DeepHost quiet\n     This will force the requester to stay off.\n          NOTE: Do not give an argument unless shutting DeepHost off!!!!\n     If any games are being played when you shut off the DeepHost program\n     they also will be signaled to clean-up and stop.     \n                                         \n     You are ready to go! The program has been tested on Excelsior BBS, Cnet\n     and DLG. If you have ran it on any-other BBS please let us know and well\n     add your setup to our documentation for others to use.\n\n     If you are able to adjust the priority DON''T. Just run DeepSpace at 0.\n  \nDeepSpace Arguments:\n--------------------\nUsage:\nDeepSpace Name/A/M,T=Time/A/N,Echo/S,D=Delay/N,A=Delay/N,CNET/S\n\nName      The users name\n            /A - Required\n            /M - Multiple strings allowed\nT=Time    The time allowed to play\n            /A - Required\n            /N - numbers\nEcho      Echo key press''s\n            /S - switch\nD=Delay   Fight delay instead of default (40000)\n            /N - numbers (1 to 200000000) \n          NOTE: If D is set to 0 it forces all players have the Auto-Attack on.\n                Do NOT set to a negative number.\nA=Delay   An alternate delay method. (1 - 200)\n                                             (ticks 50 = one second)\n          Don''t use this option unless you have to.                                   \n          This depends on the BBS you are using.                                   \n          During an attack sequence, numbers that range from 1 to 10 will be \n          printed. The object is to hit a key as close as possible to 10.\n          This is the percent power used during that fight wave. If all the\n          numbers flash by with out a delay you have to use this option.\n          If this occurs and you do not use this option the game, the program will \n          eat up a lot of CPU time. Have this set even if you want Auto-Attack on.\n                                                       (none interaction fight)\n          Set d to 0 if you want Auto-Attack on.\n          Set a to a min of 6.\n          @{"Testing setup delays" link sud}\n\nCNET      A flag for CNET BBS only!!!!\n          To use %7 for passing time left you have to use this switch.\n          I was told that this pass''s time in tenths of a minute. This\n          switch tell DeepSpace to convert the time.\n                     \nNOTE: Name & Time are REQUIRED!                         \n      Replace name(s) ,time with the proper BBS codes which are applicable. \nAn example:  \n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 Echo D=60000\n  Name is John Doe, Time allowed 120 minutes, Echo on, Delay set to 60000\n  * for most BBS''s\n\nOther examples:\n\n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 (Min arguments)\n  Echo off using default delay of 40000 for the Attack delay.\n             \n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 d=0\n  Auto-Attack always on: Which will take away this option for the player\n  to toggle themselves.\n  \n          DeepSpace John Doe t=30 echo a=8\n  Alternate delay method. Since d is at the default (40000) Auto-Attack off \n  (which will make it optional to the player).  Delay of 8 ticks for the attack \n  delay & echo on.        \n             \n          DeepSpace John Doe t=30 echo d=0 a=8\n  Alternate delay method. Auto-Attack always on. Delay of 8 ticks\n  for the attack delay & echo on.        \n        \n                   \n@endnode\n@node sud "Testing setup delays"\n\n  If you are using the D option for the attack delay set it very high at \n  first. (~ 100000)  Or the A option set at ~ 20.  Have someone attack an \n  alien via remote (to find the Alien use the sysop editor & check which \n  sector they are in or move them to your sector).  Check the speed during\n  the fight.  Change the attacking ship to a Sprite (only 1 on offense) \n  against Fed ship (5 defense). This will be the fastest speed at which\n  the fight sequence will go, and adjust it from there..   \n\n  *NOTE: if you are using the D option for the delay (at 100000) and the \n         fighting sequence flies by before you can push a key this indicates \n         that you have to use the A option.\n         \n@endnode \n@node araces "AlienRaces"\n\n     Text/AlienRaces\n     \n     AlienRaces is a simple text file. It can be edited with any type of text\n     editor.  It is a list of Alien race names that will be given to the \n     generated alien''s in the game.  Example: Klingon\n     \n     You must have this file in the text directory or the GameSetup program \n     will fail. (you will read about GameSetup later). \n     \n     The maximum number of alien race names that can be used is 50.  If you \n     have 25 or less names in the text file, 2 aliens will be generated for \n     each race. Example:\n     13 names = 26 total aliens,\n     25 names = 50 total aliens,\n     26 names = 26 total aliens etc..\n        \n@endnode\n@node sysop.txt "Text/Sysop.txt"\n\n     Text/sysop.txt\n     \n     sysop.txt is a list of names that DeepSpace can access for\n     getting the sysop(s) name(s). The names in this simple text\n     file should match the name that will be passed to DeepSpace\n     for the players name.\n     \n     For example you and your Co-Sysop''s handle''s were ''Roentgen'' \n     & ''Big Byte'' the text file would just contain these 2\n     names. Do not have trailing spaces behind these names. Hit \n     enter right after the full name is typed.\n     \n     This file can also be edited while in the user editor under\n     defaults.\n     \n     EDITORS:\n     \n     To enter the Planet editor hit ''!'' at the main menu. The \n     Planet editor is self explanatory.  To access the user\n     editor hit ''@'' at the main prompt.\n     \n     PLAYING "GOD":\n     \n     To add a Planet is very simple through the planet editor.\n     At the start of the game you will notice that in the planet\n     editor, the numbers of the planets start at 135. If you want to \n     create a planet, select a number that''s not used from 0 - 134 and\n     fill in all of the information for the planet you wish to create.\n     \n     To create Aliens is about the same as stated above but through\n     the user editor. You will notice that all Aliens are over 99\n     in the list of users. 100 to 149. (50 Aliens MAX) To create an \n     Alien just select a number from 100 to 149 that is not used and \n     fill out all the info in the editor.\n                     \n@endnode\n@node remarks.txt "Text/Remarks.txt"\n\n     Text/Remarks.txt\n     \n     Remarks.txt is a list of strings that DeepSpace uses for insults.\n     When a player attacks someone and destroys their ship they will\n     insult the attacker.  This file can be edited inside the user editor\n     under defaults. The user editor can be entered at the main menu by\n     hitting @ at the main prompt.     \n     The strings you enter are used with the names of the attacker & the\n     attacked. These strings can only be 32 characters long!\n     Example:\n          If Roentgen attacked Big Byte & destroyed his ship. Your string \n          would be inserted like this...\n          \n          Big Bytes says Roentgen ''your string here''\n          \n     The format of Text/Remarks.txt\n          X\n          "first string"\n          "second string"\n          "third string"\n          ...\n          \n          Where X is the total number of strings you have in the file.\n                    \n     If you edit this file from the users editor you don''t have to worry\n     about the format of the text file.  It''s handled by the editor.    \n          \n@endnode\n@node conf "Config"\n\n     ENV:config \n     \n     This is a file that GameSetup will create. If you are registered\n     you have to make sure this file is not distributed.  \n\n@endnode\n\n@node register "Registering DeepSpace"\n\n     Registering DeepSpace can be done by sending $20 US dollars to:\n\n     Todd Neumiller\n     P.O. Box 18591\n     Spokane, WA 99208 USA                \n     \nInfo needed:\n     1: Your FULL name\n     2: Handle if used\n     3: BBS name (BBS phone #) \n     4: Mailing address\n\n     You can also get more information by calling or sending e-mail to:\n     \n     Internet       [email removed] (Darrell Neumiller)\n     Fragmented BBS 509-926-5507 Sysop Darrell Neumiller AKA Big Byte\n                                 CoSysop ME! Todd Neumiller AKA Roentgen\n     Or                            \n     Ami-Tech BBS   509-244-2346 Sysop Rod Whitney AKA Spark Chaser\n     Planet Praxis  509-244-0336 Sysop Darren Cambpell AKA Commander Chang\n     Or\n     Planet Praxis\n          FIDONET   \n               Todd Neumiller@1:346/31\n          INTERNET     \n               [email removed]\n     Updates are freqable from FIDONET 1:346/31 using the magic name DEEP \n               \n@endnode\n\n@node bugs "Known BUGS"\n\n     I fix em as I find em! Or when someone else finds em.\n     Thanks for all the bug reports! \n     \n@endnode\n\n@node history "History"\n\nV.6  First working version.\n\nV.7  Found Mungwall hit & fixed it. Fixed the bug that only allowed a player\n     to e-mail someone with a user # with 1 digit.\n     *Note: Paragon version of DeepSpace created.\n\nV1.0 Big changes to timing method in DeepSpace. Herman Stevens from\n     Laukensstraat Belgium deserves all the credit. Informed me about\n     it and also told me how to fix it! (THANKS!) \n     In showing known/unknown/avoided sectors could crash. This was fixed.\n     Spelling fixes - Yah I can''t Spel \n     Other small odds and ends....\n     GameSetup changed. Added the ability for the path to be saved into\n     the config file. The config file is saved in ENV: ENVARC: \n     This was done to hopefully fix the problem DLG BBS''s have running\n     DeepSpace in a directory besides the assigned DLG: directory.\n     \nV1.2 /* NOTE you do not have to re-roll your game if you were running the\n        last version (V1.0) */\n     MORE spelling fixes!\n     Are you sure (Y/n) prompts fixed so you really can change your mind.\n     Sysop editor: Can now move someone to sector 1001\n     ANSI files re-worked a little. \n     A switch for CNET to convert the time argument. \n     Some problems around porting at sector 1.\n     You can''t make a fortune selling your ship over & over.\n     GameSetup was changed so you do not have to re-roll to add registration.\n\nV1.3 Added contraband. \n     Fixed radio silence, now the command match''s the menu\n\nV1.4 We have heard a rumor that it was possible to get the program hung \n     on a disconnect during the game.  I Found two routines using Flush() \n     and took them out. Instead of just using ^C to abort the game, I have \n     also added ^D, & ^E.\n     \n     A "defaults" editor has been added.  This can be accessed from the users \n     editor, which is ''@'' at the main prompt. (see Text/Sysop.txt) The sysop \n     can select which ship and how much $$ a player will start with when they\n     first enter the game.\n\n     @{"Text/Remarks.txt " link remarks.txt} added. You can edit this through \n     the default editor.\n     \n     @{"Text/Sysop.txt " link sysop.txt} can also be edited through the default \n     editor.\n\n     We have also modified the player.directions file. It now contains a bit \n     more information on how to play the game and we went into detail on all \n     of the menu commands. This has also been archived in lha format so you \n     can put the file in your files directory for your users to download. The\n     name of this archive is ''DS_user_docs.lha''.\n     \n@endnode\n\n@node fut "Future"\n\n     We have had a lot of GREAT suggestions. I am keeping a list of them.\n     I wanted to get rid of all the problems I can find before adding\n     a lot more features to the game.\n     \n     Possible new features for phase II:\n          Planet Praxis:\n          If you go through the BlackHole when it is un-stable \n          (and make it through) you probably have seen the planet ''Praxis''.\n          We plan on making this a ''second'' phase of the game. The player\n          could beam to the planet from his ship.  Hostile Mutants & Aliens \n          would be populated on the planet, along with running into other \n          players from another bbs-node.  Different objects could be obtained\n          there. Weapons? Better Scanners? Crystals? etc..  An example that''s\n          being considered is: A VERY VERY RARE weapon that can be left at the\n          DeepSpace Port. The weapon could be programmed (via DNA scan) to\n          detonate when a certain Explorer arrived.  It would shoot a \n          projectile at the head of the player. This would be for your very \n          best Explorer friends! - NOT!\n             \n          Gambling:\n          A lot more ways to win $$. Possibly against other players as well. \n                    \n          Security:\n          The Shape Shifter could be anywhere!  Selling illegal stuff from\n          planet Praxis?  You could end up in jail with no way out, or\n          will there be a way out?\n          \n          RIPGfx ?\n          \n@endnode\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',199821,4,0,NULL,NULL,'ds9_v1_4','scan',1780048488);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('dse200','DSE200.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DSE200.LHA','DSE','XIM','Space Empire Version 2. Cool door game for',NULL,NULL,NULL,'BBS Systems.',replace('Space Empire Version 2. Cool door game for\nBBS Systems.\n','\n',char(10)),'DSEdoc.guide',replace('@database "DSE.doc"\n@Node Main "Dave''s Space Empire"\n        ==========================================================\n\n                     Dave''s Space Empire Version 2.00\n\n                         Written by David Prothero\n\n                Based on Ken''s Space Empire by Ken Schenke\n\n        ==========================================================\n\n\n                 @{"   Introduction    " link INTRO}   @{"   Distribution    " link DISTR}\n                 @{"   Installation    " link INSTL}   @{" Contacting Author " link AUTHR}\n                 @{" Obtaining Source  " link SOURC}   @{" What Took So Long " link LONG}\n\n\n Click the "Contents" button in the upper-left at any time to return here.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE INTRO "Introduction"\n\nIntroduction:\n\n   Dave''s Space Empire (DSE) is a "door" game for play online on Amiga\n   bulletin board systems (BBS''s).  The game is a multi-player game\n   where participants compete to build the largest "space empire".  The\n   game involves military strategy, cooperation with other players, and\n   you musn''t forget to keep the people in your own empire happy.  By\n   purchasing and/or conquering various types of planets, players gain\n   population, the resources of the particular planet, and points.\n   When a player reaches 100,000 points, they become "Space Lord".  They\n   do not automatically win, however, they must retain their Space Lord\n   status for one week (seven days).\n\n   Dave''s Space Empire is a "DOS" door game as to make it compatible\n   with as many BBS packages as possible.  It may also be run from a CLI\n   window.  It supports ANSI colors and IBM graphics, each of which can\n   be turned on/off by each individual user.  Also, all menus are ASCII\n   text files that may be changed with any text editor.  Also, EVERY\n   message displayed by the game is in one large text file so that\n   SysOps can customize the game and colors of the text as much as\n   possible.\n\n   To determine exactly which version you have, go into a Shell and type\n   "Version DSE" at the prompt.  Also, the DSEcfg and DSE-Msgs.txt files\n   have version strings in them.  Make sure all three files have the same\n   version number before using them together.  DSE-Msgs.txt is in the\n   Text directory.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE DISTR "Distribution"\n\nDistribution:\n\n   This program is freeware, however, if you distribute it, you MUST\n   distribute ALL files together.  A listing of the files is as follows:\n\n      DSE                 - Main executable program.\n      DSEcfg              - Sets up a few variables and resets the game.\n                            Mouse-driven, WB 2.04 and above, only.\n      DSEcfg.info         - Icon file for DSEcfg program.\n      DSEcfg-CLI          - CLI-based DSEcfg.  Also is Workbench 1.3 compatible.\n      DSEdoc.guide        - This file in AmigaGuide format.\n      DSEdoc.guide.info   - Icon file for AmigaGuide documentation.\n      powerpacker.library - Library for reading compressed Text/ files.\n      Text/               - Directory of text files.\n          |\n          Agricultural.txt   - Info file on Agricultural planets.\n          Bank.mnu           - Bank menu file.\n          Covert.mnu         - Covert Operations menu file.\n          Defense.txt        - Info file on Defense planets.\n          Documentation.txt  - Full DSE documentation file.\n          DSE-Msgs.txt       - DSE text messages file.\n          GovActions.mnu     - Government Actions menu file.\n          Intro.txt          - Introduction info file.\n          Main.mnu           - Main menu file (before game).\n          Mining.txt         - Info file on Ore Mining planets.\n          NewPlayer.txt      - New Player display file.\n          Radio.mnu          - Radio Network menu file.\n          Research.txt       - Info file on Research planets.\n          Special1.mnu       - Special Missions menu file.\n          Special2.mnu       - Special Missions menu file.\n          Special3.mnu       - Special Missions menu file.\n          Special4.mnu       - Special Missions menu file.\n          TBank.mnu          - Team Bank menu file.\n          TeamLeader.mnu     - Team menu file for a team leader.\n          TeamMember.mnu     - Team menu file for normal members.\n          TeamNone.mnu       - Team menu file for a non-member.\n          Training.txt       - Info file on Training planets.\n\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE INSTL "Installation"\n\nInstallation:\n\nNOTE: Pre-2.0 DSE users, this 2.0 version is *NOT* compatible with older\n      versions.  Please delete your old version and start over from\n      scratch.  My apologies, but it has undergone radical changes.\n\n\nTo install DSE follow these simple steps:\n\n\n1) Copy the included powerpacker.library to your LIBS: directory.  This\n   will allow you to use PowerPacker or other compression utility that uses\n   the powerpacker.library to compress any files in the Text/ directory.\n\n\n2) Create a directory somewhere on your hard-drive and put all of the\n   included files in that directory.  Make sure that subdirectories are\n   maintained.  For example, if you copy DSE to the directory DH0:DSE, then\n   there would have to be a DH0:DSE/Text directory as well, with all of the\n   text files under that directory.  Also, create a "Messages" (no quotes)\n   directory UNDER the DSE directory.  You will not copy any files here.\n\n   If you were using DH0:DSE for your path, your directory structure would\n   look like so:\n\n                DH0:\n                    DSE/\n                        Text/\n                        Messages/\n\n\n   NOTE: If your BBS program requires doors to be in a certain directory,\n   then you should follow those guidlines.\n\n\n3) Run the DSEcfg program from Workbench by double-clicking on its icon, or\n   from the Shell.  This program will only run under Workbench 2.04 or\n   above.  If you are still running 1.3, then you can use the DSEcfg-CLI\n   program which has been included.  It will only run from a shell.\n\n   When the program is run, do not reset the game, as there is no need to do\n   this.  Set the other options as you see fit.  Simply click on Save to\n   use the default values.\n\n\n4) Now select your BBS program here.  Select "General" if you do not see\n   your BBS software listed here.  You may also want to read "General" if\n   you want a more in depth description of how DSE can be run.\n\n       @{" General    " link GENRL}\n       @{" CNet AMIGA " link CNET}\n       @{" DLG-Pro    " link DLGPR}\n       @{" Excelsior! " link EXCEL}\n       @{" Starnet    " link STARN}\n       @{" TransAmiga " link TRANS}\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE GENRL "General Installation"\n\n   You need to find out how to run a "DOS" or "CLI" door from your BBS\n   program.  Refer to your documentation on how to do this.  Often times\n   there is a hack or third-party program for doing this that may not be\n   documented, so find someone who is a "guru" with your particular BBS\n   software and ask him/her.\n\n   Once you have discovered how to launch a DOS door from your BBS\n   software, then you need to setup your BBS to call the DSE executable\n   like so:\n\n   Path:To/DSE/Files/DSE <opt> <userid>\n\n   Obviously, the "Path:To/DSE/Files/" is where you stick the path where you\n   copied the DSE files and DSE is the executable filename.\n\n   Next, the <opt> is how you want DSE to do the I/O.  DSE _requires_ "RAW"\n   mode for operation, but there are two ways to accomplish this.  The\n   first way is by specifying "-d" (no quotes) as the <opt>.  This will\n   have DSE force RAW mode itself.  Some BBS programs don''t like this, but\n   do have the option of opening the I/O with RAW mode BEFORE running the\n   executable.  If your BBS is one of these, then tell your BBS to run the\n   door with RAW mode "ON" and use "-n" for the <opt>.\n\n   Last, you need to supply some sort of identification unique to the user\n   currently online.  This could be their name or ID number (possibly phone\n   number, but my system has different callers from one family that all\n   have the same phone number, of course, so be careful here).  To do this,\n   many BBS programs have special codes you supply.  For example, %3 is a\n   user''s name on CNet.  Remember that names may have spaces in them, so\n   you should put quotes around them.\n\n   Here''s an example of what you might put into the command line:\n\n   DH0:DSE/DSE -d "%NAME"\n\n   Note that this will run the DSE executable if it is in the DH0:DSE\n   directory with RAW mode forced and assumes that %NAME is the code to\n   insert the user online''s real name into the command line for the\n   particular BBS program.  Note that there are quotes around it.  This is\n   HIGHLY recommended, as names usually have spaces in them.\n\n\n   If you successfully install DSE for a BBS program that is not documented\n   in this file, then please consider writing a doc file for your BBS and\n   @{"sending it to me." link AUTHR}\n\n   @{"Continue at step 5 of main installation." link STEP5}\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE CNET "CNet AMIGA Installation"\n\n                          CNet v2.63 INSTALLATION\n             Written by Capt. Pez, Sysop of Needful Things BBS\n                    Needful Things BBS: 1-206-475-0884\n\n\n1) DSE _MUST_ be run as a "DOS" door from CNet. You would add it like this:\n\n   =========================================================================\n\n   DOS Filename: Pfiles:DSE/DSE        (The path and name of the file)\n   Description : Dave''s Space Empire   (What you are going to call it)\n   Arguments   : -n "%1"               (Options AND user ID - See Below)\n\n   =========================================================================\n   \n   DSE requires that RAW console mode be set to YES.  The -n option tells\n   DSE that you will be running the executable under RAW mode from CNet. \n   This is VERY important!  Make sure RAW mode is set to ''yes'' and that\n   you have the -n option set in the arguments.  \n   \n   Also make sure you have CNet''s "Echo Input" switch set to NO.\n   \n   Note that this will run the DSE executable if it is in the PFILES:DSE\n   directory with RAW mode forced and assumes that %1 is the code to insert \n   the user online''s real name into the command line for CNet.  Note that \n   there are quotes around it.  This is REQUIRED, as names usually have \n   spaces in them.\n\n   That''s all you need to do for the arguments!  Easy eh?\n\n\n2) If you are having ANY trouble with installing this on your BBS, feel free\n   to contact me (Capt. Pez) on Needful Things BBS (206) 475-0884 OR leave\n   me mail on Future World.  I call Future World at least once a month, so\n   if you want a quick reply, your best bet is to contact me on my BBS.\n\n\n   @{"Continue at step 5 of main installation." link STEP5}\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE DLGPR "DLG-Pro Installation"\n\n                           DLG Pro INSTALLATION\n                    DLG/DSE Beta Tester: John Freeland\n        Freeland''s Mainframe BBS: 1-206-438-2273 or 1-206-438-6013\n\n\nUnfortunately, I don''t have any specific docs for DLG, but, from what I''m\ntold, you want to run it like so:\n\n                            DSE -n %<var>\n\nReplace %<var> with a DLG variable for something like the user''s name or\nid number.  Also make sure you run the door with RAW mode _ON_.\n\nThat should do it!  If you have any problems, contact the BBS above, or\nJohn Freeland''s e-mail address is: [email removed].\n\n                                            -David Prothero\n\n   @{"Continue at step 5 of main installation." link STEP5}\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE EXCEL "Excelsior! Installation"\n\n                          Excelsior! INSTALLATION\n                  Excelsior/DSE Beta Tester: Scott Wiard\n                   Digital Dreams II BBS: 1-206-736-2480\n\nNOTE: This install was performed under Excelsior! version 0.996.  Future\n      versions will most likely be the same, or very similar.\n\n1) Login as SysOp and go into the <D>oors area.  If you wish, go into the\n   subdirectory you want to put DSE in.\n\n2) Type ''A'' to add a new door (this is the default command key, use the\n   appropriate key if you''ve changed it).\n\n3) It will then ask you the disk directory to search.  Enter the directory\n   you installed DSE in here.  Then, enter "DSE" (no quotes) for the pattern.\n\n4) Say ''Y'' to the DSE file and ''N'' to the other files.\n\n5) Enter the description and access levels you want.\n\n6) Now enter ''D'' for DOS door and then "-d %16" (no quotes) for the DOS\n   Arguments.  (%16 is the code for the user ID number.)\n\n   NOTE: Some versions of Excelsior! have a bug in the DOS door interface.\n   If the game appears double-spaced to REMOTE callers (not local), then\n   use "-e %16" instead for the arguments.  As of right now (0.996) this\n   bug has not been fixed.\n\n7) Don''t worry about the stack size, leave the priority at zero, and the\n   IPL variable at zero.\n\n8) Set the days to keep however you want (0 to keep forever) and set the\n   "Only one user at a time" to ''N'' (the game keeps it''s own locks).\n\n   @{"Continue at step 5 of main installation." link STEP5}\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE STARN "Starnet BBS Installation"\n\n                         STARNET BBS INSTALLATION\n            Written by Larry Cloud, Sysop of Cloud''s Corner BBS\n                    Cloud''s Corner BBS: 1-206-377-4290\n\n\nNOTE: This will ONLY work with Amigados 2.0 or higher due to the DOSDRIVER\n      program for Starnet.\n\n1) ADD the following lines to your LOGON_x.PRX file(s).  If you don''t HAVE a\n   LOGON_x.PRX file, call Cloud''s Corner and grab his "L-ON-OFF.LHA" archive\n   (make sure you change the _1 to _0 for line 0, _2 for line 2, etc):\n\n   ---> Cut Here <---\n\n   quote = d2c(34)\n   OPEN(''file'',''dosdrivers:dse_1'',''W'')\n   WRITELN(''file'',''cd doors:dse'')\n   WRITELN(''file'',''changetaskpri 2'')\n   WRITELN(''file'',''dse -d ''||quote||username||quote)\n   WRITELN(''file'',''changetaskpri 0'')\n   WRITELN(''file'',''endcli'')\n   CLOSE(''file'')\n   address command "protect dosdrivers:dse_1 +S"\n\n   ---> Cut Here <---\n\n2) In your DOORS:DOSDRIVERS directory, copy a copy of dosdrivers and rename\n   it to DSE.\n\n3) Create the following script in your DOORS:DOSDRIVERS directory and name\n   it "DSE.SCRIPT":\n\n   ---> Cut Here <---\n\n   .KEY NODE/A\n   .BRA {\n   .KET }\n   cd dosdrivers:\n   c:copy dse_{NODE} dse.sch\n   wait 1\n   run > NIL: dse {NODE}\n   endcli\n\n   ---> Cut Here <---\n\n4) Next, make a menu entry that launches it:\n\n   Option ''7'':\n   Function Rundoor dosdrivers:dse.script\n   end\n\n   You can of course assign a different key to it.\n\n\n   @{"Continue at step 5 of main installation." link STEP5}\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE TRANS "TransAmiga BBS Installation"\n\n                        TransAmiga BBS INSTALLATION\n          Written by David Prothero, Sysop of Digital Dreams III\n                    Digital Dreams III: 1-206-650-2582\n\n\n1) Install DSE in a directory called DSE under your BBS:Extras/ directory.\n\n2) Take the following, copy it to a file named DSE.trans and put it in your\n   BBS:Rexx/ directory:\n\n---> Cut Here <---\n\n/* Runs DSE */\n\noptions results\n\nsysteminfo 8 ; bbsline=result\nuserinfo a ; name = result\nid=time(s)\n\ncall open(fp,''t:dserun.''id,''W'')\ncall writeln(fp,''Failat 99999'')\ncall writeln(fp,''cd BBS:Extras/DSE'')\ncall writeln(fp,''DSE -d "''name''"'')\ncall writeln(fp,''endcli >nil:'')\ncall close(fp)\n\naddress command ''NewShell FIFO:trshell''id''/rwkecs from t:dserun.''id\naddress command ''BBS:Bin/TrShell -n''bbsline'' -ftrshell''id\naddress command ''delete t:dserun.''id\n\nexit\n\n---> Cut Here <---\n\n3) Next, add the DSE.trans to your BBS:Configs/Extras.Rx file.  Refer to\n   the TransAmiga documentation on how to do this.\n\n\n   @{"Continue at step 5 of main installation." link STEP5}\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE STEP5 "Installation"\n\n5) To run from a Shell, use -s for the <opt>, and manually enter the\n   <userid>.  Example:\n\n      1.dh0:DSE> DSE -s "David Prothero"\n\n   All this does is force RAW mode (which needs to be done from a shell)\n   and also remaps ANSI colors so that the text is readable from a normal\n   four color Workbench.  If you wish, you could run with a -d <opt> and\n   you would get 8 color ANSI, with the intense versions just being in bold.\n\n\n6) @{"Repeat step 3)" link INSTL} using a DIFFERENT path (with copies of all\n   the files also in that path) if you want to run multiple games at one\n   time.\n\n\n7) To reset the game, all you need to do is run the DSEcfg program and\n   click on the Reset Game gadget.  If you are using DSEcfg-CLI, then run\n   it from a Shell and say "Yes" to the first question where it asks if you\n   want to reset the current game.  You usually won''t have to do this, as the\n   game automatically resets after someone wins.\n\n\n8) You may customize any of the text files in the Text directory.  Please\n   note that these do not use ANSI codes, but special codes that are\n   interpretted by DSE.  This way you do NOT need two files, one for ANSI\n   and one for non-ANSI.  Also, any IBM style graphics are automatically\n   interpretted to dashes, etc. if the user turns off IBM graphics, so feel\n   free to use these as well.\n\n   @{"Info on text codes." link CODES}\n\n   IMPORTANT: When editing the DSE-Msgs.txt file, DO NOT INSERT OR DELETE\n              ANY LINES!!!  You will basically screw up the game BIG TIME!\n              Lines may be as long as your text editor will allow you to\n              type, but the bigger your DSE-Msgs.txt file is, the more\n              memory it takes up.  The amount of memory that the DSE-Msgs\n              file takes up is the same as the AmigaDOS filesize.\n\n   Again, you may compress any of the files in the Text directory with a\n   PowerPacker compatible utility, provided you''ve copied the included\n   powerpacker.library to your LIBS: directory.\n\n\n9) That''s it.  You''ve installed DSE on your system.  For those of you who\n   are wondering where DSEed and DSEmaint are (from older versions of DSE),\n   here''s what happened:  First of all, DSEmaint is obsolete.  Maintenance\n   is built into the game.  So no more nightly cron garbage.  Second, DSEed\n   is on the back burner right now.  I had some problems with getting it\n   finished and wanted to get DSE out the door, so I decided to release\n   version 2.0 without the editor.\n\n\n                                    -@{"David Prothero" link AUTHR}\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE CODES "Text File Codes"\n\n   Each code starts with a CONTROL-Y character.  Make sure your text editor\n   can handle this.  If you save a menu file in a text editor that does not\n   display the CONTROL-Y character, then all the codes will be RUINED.  I\n   know that CygnusEd works fine.\n\n   After the CONTROL-Y comes the one character code and then a one\n   character parameter.  Some codes don''t take parameters, so any\n   character will work here.\n\n   Available codes:\n\n   Code    Parameter(s)   Description\n\n   c       0-9 and a-f    Changes foreground color of all subsequent text.\n\n                          0 - Black      1 - Red        2 - Green   \n                          3 - Orange     4 - Blue       5 - Purple\n                          6 - Cyan       7 - Lt. Grey   8 - Dk. Grey\n                          9 - Lt. Red    a - Lt. Green  b - Yellow\n                          c - Lt. Blue   d - Lt. Purple e - Lt. Cyan\n                          f - White\n\n                          Colors are remapped when running from a Shell.\n\n\n   z       0-7            Changes background color of all subsequent text.\n                          Colors are the same as above, except, only the\n                          first eight may be used (ANSI restriction).\n                          Background colors are not seen from Shell mode.\n\n\n   n       1-9            Adds a "newline", which is a carriage return and\n                          a linefeed.  The parameter is the number of\n                          newlines to add.\n\n\n   g       0              Pauses for user to hit a key.  Just supply 0 as\n                          the parameter.\n\n\n   f       0-1            Supplying  the 0 parameter will just move the cursor\n                          back to "home" position (upper-left).  Supplying\n                          the 1 parameter will clear the screen completely.\n\n   q       1              Resets current color to "f" (white) and the\n                          background color to "0" (black).  Just supply 1\n                          as the parameter.\n\n\n   For example, the text: c9Hello, n2what is your name?g1f1\n\n   Would display the following in Light Red:\n\n   Hello,\n\n   What is your name?<wait for a keypress><clear screen>\n\n\n   Anyway, CNet SysOps will recognize these codes.  I''m using them because\n   this program was originally a CNet door and used those codes.  Instead\n   of re-writing all the text, I did it this way, which seemed more\n   flexible, anyway.  Sorry to Excelsior! users who''ll have to learn this\n   different code, but just remember to use a CONTROL-Y instead of a \\n   character.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE AUTHR "Contacting the Author"\n\nContacting the Author:\n\n   Please contact me if you find any bugs, have any suggestions, or need\n   help.\n\n   I am going to try to make this version as bug free as possible, and\n   will periodically add new features.  It''s not going to be my main\n   project (I am a student), so please be patient with me.\n\n\n   My Internet address or BBS is the best way to get a hold of me.  Please\n   remember that during quarter breaks (ie, Christmas, spring break,\n   summer, etc) that these addresses may not be valid.  I will try to keep\n   people updated.\n\n\n   Internet: [email removed]\n\n\n   My BBS: Digital Dreams III\n           (206)650-2582\n           This is where you can ALWAYS get the latest version.\n\n\n   US Snail: David Prothero\n             812A Buchanan Towers\n             Bellingham, WA 98225\n\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE SOURC "Obtaining Source"\n\nObtaining source:\n\n   To get the source code, just @{"ask me" link AUTHR}.\n\n   A donation wouldn''t be too bad, either :-).\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE LONG "What Took So Long"\n\nI don''t know!!\n\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,NULL,94384,1,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,'scan',1780048488);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('dspc193','DSPC193.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DSPC193.LHA','DeepSet','XIM','DeepSet',NULL,'Player/Node',NULL,'||   ||  ||    ||     ||    \ ||   ||  ||--  ||--   ||____/ + ||___||  ||___ ||___  ||        +',replace('+ ______   _____+_____  ______    +\n  ||   ||  ||    ||     ||    \\n  ||   ||  ||--  ||--   ||____/ +\n  ||___||  ||___ ||___  ||        +\n    Support BBS  (509) 926-5507\n  Multi-Player/Node Space BBS Door\n       Now with RIP Grafics!\n','\n',char(10)),'DeepSpace.guide',replace('@database DeepSpace\n\n@node main "DeepSpace! Docs"\n                 \n @{" About DeepSpace " link about}\n\n @{" Legal Stuff " link leagalstuff}\n\n @{" Setting up DeepSpace " link setup}\n                         \n @{" Registering " link register}\n                         \n @{" Known Bugs " link bugs}\n        \n @{" History " link history}\n        \n @{" Future " link fut}\n                                \n@endnode\n@node about "About DeepSpace"\n\n     DeepSpace is a Multi-Player, Multi-Node Space Role Playing Game.\n     BBS''s that can run DOS doors (programs that use stdin & stdout) can\n     run this game.\n      \n     *Note a Paragon Version is also available.\n      DeepSpaceP will allow only one node at a time to run. \n      \n     Twenty nodes can play at one time, this has never been actually\n     tested though. Up to 100 users can join the game if @{"REGISTERED"link register}\n     If it isn''t @{"REGISTERED" link register} only 5 players are allowed.\n            \n     Enforcer & Mungwall have been used during development of this version. \n     There should not be any hits, unless I missed one.  I have also \n     setup a 10 minute inactivity timer in the game.  This means that if \n     a user playing the game doesn''t press a key for 10 minutes the game \n     will automatically clean up and exit.\n     \n     DeepSpaceP (Paragon Version)   \n#    DeepSpace \n#    DeepHost\n#    DeepSet\n          \n          Programmed by Todd Neumiller\n     \n     \n     \n     GameSetUp\n          Programmed by Todd & Darrell Neumiller\n     Ansi GFX \n          Darren Campbell  &  Rod Whitney \n# = only in Dos version           \n                                 \n@endnode\n\n@node leagalstuff "Legal Stuff"\n \n     DeepSpace (c) Copyright 1994 \n \n     These Files and their related documentation are provided "AS-IS" and \n     are subject to change without notice, no warranties are made.  All \n     use, is at your own risk.  No liability or responsibility is assumed.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node setup "Setting up DeepSpace"\n\n     WorkBench 2.0 or greater is required.\n\n     In the directory you unarced the DeepSpace archive, you will have\n     GameSetUp\n     DeepHost \n     DeepSet  - This is used by the DeepHost program.\n     DeepSpace\n     DeepSpace.guide (the file your reading)\n     Text (dir) w/ numerous text files\n          Ones of interest for setting up the config for DeepSpace are:\n          1: @{"Text/AlienRaces" link araces}\n          2: @{"Text/Sysop.txt " link sysop.txt}\n          3: @{"Text/Remarks.txt " link remarks.txt}            \n          4: @{"Text/Defaults.txt " link defaults.txt}\n     ENV: \n          5: @{"ENV:config    " link conf}\n\n     After the above mentioned files are edited to your liking run \n     GameSetup.  This program will generate all the files needed to\n     run DeepSpace.  GameSetup should also be ran when you wish to\n     re-roll the game.\n\n     * NEW V1.0 *\n     Also the path to which you have set up DeepSpace in can be\n     saved into your config file. It is optional. If the files \n     can not be found by DeepSpace fill in this option.\n     Give it the path you are running Gamesetup in.\n     Example: Dh0:Doors/Deepspace/\n     \n     DeepHost\n     This program is a background server for the DeepSpace game.\n     After gamesetup has been ran you can now start DeepHost. I\n     suggest you run it from your S:User-Startup file.\n     NOTE: It must remain in the same directory as the other files.\n                Do not copy it to another directory.\n           \n     Example: \n          dh0:doors/deepspace/deephost  \n     It runs DeepSet, which loads all the data needed for the game.\n     This allows DeepHost to become somewhat smaller in size.\n     To shut down DeepHost just simply run DeepHost again. It will \n     signal the original DeepHost program to shut down. A requester\n     will pop up showing you that it is shutting down. If you do not\n     want a requester to pop up (via remote or ??) Give DeepHost the\n     argument of QUIET. \n     Example:\n          DeepHost quiet\n     This will force the requester to stay off.\n          NOTE: Do not give an argument unless shutting DeepHost off!!!!\n     If any games are being played when you shut off the DeepHost program\n     they also will be signaled to stop.     \n     As of version 2.0 of DeepHost, you can pass an argument for the\n     priority of DeepHost (from -5 to 100) this is not recomended!\n     Example: \n          DeepHost 50\n          This will run DeepHost with a priority of 50.\n     I did not put alot of code checking the arguments.. You can only pass\n     QUIET or intigers from -5 to 100. And not both..\n                                              \n     You are ready to go! The program has only been tested on a Excelsior \n     BBS as of late. If you have ran it on any-other BBS please add a \n     comment on the bottom of this file giving directions if anything \n     different in the setup was required.\n     Also please let us know!\n\n     If you are able to adjust the priority DON''T. Just run DeepSpace at 0.\n  \nDeepSpace Arguments:\n--------------------\nUsage:\nDeepSpace Name/A/M,T=Time/A/N,Echo/S,D=Delay/N,A=Delay/N,CNET/S,LOCAL/S\n\nName      The users name\n            /A - Required\n            /M - Multiple strings allowed\nT=Time    The time allowed to play\n            /A - Required\n            /N - numbers\nEcho      Echo key press''s\n            /S - switch\nD=Delay   Fight delay instead of default (40000)\n            /N - numbers (1 to 200000000) \n          NOTE: If D is set to 0 it forces all players have the Auto-Attack \n                on. Do NOT set to a negative number.\nA=Delay   An alternate delay method. (1 - 200)\n                                             (ticks 50 = one second)\n          Don''t use this option unless you have to.                        \n          This depends on the BBS you are using.                         \n          During an attack sequence, numbers that range from 1 to 10 will be \n          printed. The object is to hit a key as close as possible to 10.\n          This is the percent power used during that fight wave. If all the\n          numbers flash by with out a delay you have to use this option.\n          If this occurs and you do not use this option the game, the \n          program will eat up a lot of CPU time. Have this set even if you \n          want Auto-Attack on. (none interaction fight)\n          \n          Set d to 0 if you want Auto-Attack on.\n          Set a to a min of 6.\n          @{"Testing setup delays" link sud}\n\nCNET      A flag for CNET BBS only!!!!\n          To use %7 for passing time left you have to use this switch.\n          I was told that this pass''s time in tenths of a minute. This\n          switch tells DeepSpace to convert the time.\n\nLOCAL     flag to turn off RIP auto-sensing. For BBS''s that can have\n          RIP GFX played local use this.\n                       \nNOTE: Name & Time are REQUIRED!                         \n      Replace name(s) ,time with the proper BBS codes which are applicable. \nAn example:  \n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 Echo D=60000\n  Name is John Doe, Time allowed 120 minutes, Echo on, Delay set to 60000\n  * for most BBS''s\n\nOther examples:\n\n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 (Min arguments)\n  Echo off using default delay of 40000 for the Attack delay.\n             \n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 d=0\n  Auto-Attack always on: Which will take away this option for the player\n  to toggle themselves.\n  \n          DeepSpace John Doe t=30 echo a=8\n  Alternate delay method. Since d is at the default (40000) Auto-Attack off \n  (which will make it optional to the player).  Delay of 8 ticks for the \n  attack delay & echo on.        \n             \n          DeepSpace John Doe t=30 echo d=0 a=8\n  Alternate delay method. Auto-Attack always on. Delay of 8 ticks\n  for the attack delay & echo on.        \n        \n                   \n@endnode\n@node sud "Testing setup delays"\n\n  If you are using the D option for the attack delay set it very high at \n  first. (~ 100000)  Or the A option set at ~ 20.  Have someone attack an \n  alien via remote (to find the Alien use the sysop editor & check which \n  sector they are in or move them to your sector).  Check the speed during\n  the fight.  Change the attacking ship to a Sprite (only 1 on offense) \n  against Fed ship (5 defense). This will be the fastest speed at which\n  the fight sequence will go, and adjust it from there..   \n\n  *NOTE: if you are using the D option for the delay (at 100000) and the \n         fighting sequence flies by before you can push a key this indicates \n         that you have to use the A option.\n         \n  On our Excelsior BBS we use D=70000;\n  \n@endnode \n@node araces "AlienRaces"\n\n     Text/AlienRaces\n     \n     AlienRaces is a simple text file. It can be edited with any type of text\n     editor.  It is a list of Alien race names that will be given to the \n     generated alien''s in the game.  Example: Klingon\n     \n     You must have this file in the text directory or the GameSetup program \n     will fail. (you will read about GameSetup later). \n     \n     The maximum number of alien race names that can be used is 50.  If you \n     have 25 or less names in the text file, 2 aliens will be generated for \n     each race.\n     13 names = 26 total aliens,\n     25 names = 50 total aliens,\n     26 names = 26 total aliens etc..\n        \n@endnode\n@node sysop.txt "Text/Sysop.txt"\n\n     Text/sysop.txt\n     \n     sysop.txt is a list of names that DeepSpace can access for\n     getting the sysop(s) name(s). The names in this simple text\n     file should match the name that will be passed to DeepSpace\n     for the players name.\n     \n     For example you and your Co-Sysop''s handle''s were ''Roentgen'' \n     & ''Big Byte'' the text file would just contain these 2\n     names. Do not have trailing spaces behind these names. Hit \n     enter right after the full name is typed.\n     \n     This file can also be edited while in the user editor under\n     defaults.\n     \n     EDITORS:\n     \n     To enter the Planet/Port editor hit ''!'' at the main menu. The \n     Planet/Port editor is self explanatory.  To access the user\n     editor hit ''@'' at the main prompt.\n     \n     To add a Planet is very simple through the planet editor.\n     At the start of the game you will notice that in the planet\n     editor, the numbers of the planets start at about 135. To be \n     GOD and create a planet just select a number from 0 to 134 & fill \n     in all the information for the planet you wish to create.\n     \n     To create Aliens is about the same as stated above but through\n     the user editor. You will notice that all Aliens are over 99\n     in the list of users. 100 to 149.(50 Aliens MAX)\n     To create a Alien just select a number from 100 to 149 that is not\n     used and fill out all the info in the editor.\n          \n      \n                     \n@endnode\n@node remarks.txt "Text/Remarks.txt"\n\n     Text/Remarks.txt\n     \n     Remarks.txt is a list of strings that DeepSpace uses for insults.\n     When a player attacks someone and destroys their ship they will\n     insult the attacker.  This file can be edited inside the user editor\n     under defaults. The user editor can be entered at the main menu by\n     hitting @ at the main prompt.     \n     The strings you enter are used with the names of the attacker & the\n     attacked. These strings can only be 32 characters long!\n     Example:\n          If Roentgen attacked Big Byte & destroyed his ship. Your string \n          would be inserted like this...\n          \n          Big Bytes says Roentgen ''your string here''\n          \n     The format of Text/Remarks.txt\n          line 1 ''has the number of strings in the text file''\n          line 2 ''first string''\n          line 3 ''second string''\n          ect..\n          \n     If you edit this file with in the user editor you won''t even\n     have to worry about the format of the text file.      \n          \n@endnode\n@node defaults.txt "Text/Defaults.txt"\n\n     Text/Defaults.txt\n     \n     Defaults.txt is a list of variable''s that the Sysop can Edit. \n     \n     1: What ship the first time player will get.\n     2: How much cash the first time player will get.\n     3: How many ExaWatts the first time player will get.\n     4: How many turns the ships get. \n        This is based on a ratio. The default is 100.\n     5: How much Shields the first time player will get.\n     \n     These can be edited through the player editor. ''@'' at the main\n     prompt. You will see ''D'' for defaults as one of your available\n     selections.\n     \n               \n@endnode\n@node conf "Config"\n\n     ENV:DeepSpaceD/config \n     \n     This is a file that GameSetup will create. If you are registered\n     you have to make sure this file is not distributed.  \n\n@endnode\n\n@node register "Registering DeepSpace"\n\n     Registering DeepSpace can be done by sending $20 US dollars to:\n\n     Todd Neumiller\n     P.O. Box 18591\n     Spokane, WA 99208 USA                \n     \nInfo needed:\n     1: Your FULL name\n     2: Handle if used\n     3: BBS name (BBS phone #) \n     4: Mailing address\n\n     You can also get more information by calling or sending e-mail to:\n     \n     Internet:   (Darrell Neumiller)   [email removed] \n       \n\nPrimary Support BBS:   Fragmented BBS 509-926-5507\n                       FIDONET 1:346/47\n                       C-Link 911:6420/6.0\n                       AmigaNet 40:414/12.0\n                         \n                              Sysop Darrell Neumiller (AKA Big Byte)\n                              CoSysop ME! Todd Neumiller (AKA Roentgen)\n                                   \nAlternate Support BBS''s:   Ami-Tech BBS  509-838-6490 \n                              Sysop Rod Whitney  (AKA Spark Chaser)\n                           Stormfront BBS 509-533-2747 (CNET)\n                              Sysop Joe Kowtko (see CNET.docs) \n                              \n(You can also subscribe to DEEP_SPACE, DEEP_SYSOP, and DEEP_FILES to)\n(receive the latest info, contact Darrell at the internet address listed\nabove if interested.\n\n \n@endnode\n\n@node bugs "Known BUGS"\n\n     I fix em as I find em! Or when someone else finds em.\n     Thanks for all the bug reports! \n     \n@endnode\n\n@node history "History"\n\nV.6  First working version.\n\nV.7  Found Mungwall hit & fixed it. Fixed the bug that only allowed a player\n     to e-mail someone with a user # with 1 digit.\n     *Note: Paragon version of DeepSpace created.\n\nV1.0 Big changes to timing method in DeepSpace. Herman Stevens from\n     Laukensstraat Belgium deserves all the credit. Informed me about\n     it and also told me how to fix it! (THANKS!) \n     In showing known/unknown/avoided sectors could crash. This was fixed.\n     Spelling fixes - Yah I can''t Spel \n     Other small odds and ends....\n     GameSetup changed. Added the ability for the path to be saved into\n     the config file. The config file is saved in ENV: ENVARC: \n     This was done to hopefully fix the problem DLG BBS''s have running\n     DeepSpace in a directory besides the assigned DLG: directory.\n     \nV1.2 /* NOTE you do not have to re-roll your game if you were running the\n        last version (V1.0) */\n     MORE spelling fixes!\n     Are you sure (Y/n) prompts fixed so you really can change your mind.\n     Sysop editor: Can now move someone to sector 1001\n     ANSI files re-worked a little. \n     A switch for CNET to convert the time argument. \n     Some problems around porting at sector 1.\n     You can''t make a fortune selling your ship over & over.\n     GameSetup was changed so you do not have to re-roll to add registration.\n\nV1.3 Added contraband. \n     Fixed radio silence, now the command match''s the menu\n\nV1.4 We have heard a rumor that it was possible to get the program hung \n     on a disconnect during the game.  I Found two routines using Flush() \n     and took them out. Instead of just using ^C to abort the game, I have \n     added ^D, & ^E also. DeepSpace has always had a 10 minute inactivity\n     time-out.\n     \n     A ''default'' editor added. This can be accessed from the user editor.\n     ''@'' at the main prompt. (see Text/Sysop.txt)\n     The sysop can now select which ship, & how much $$ a player will \n     start with when they first enter the game.\n\n     @{"Text/Remarks.txt " link remarks.txt} added. Can edit through the default editor.\n     \n     @{"Text/Sysop.txt " link sysop.txt} can also be edited through the default editor.\n\nV1.6 Ooops! Forgot about 1.5.\n     The planet editor ''!'' from the main menu now also has a port editor.\n     RE_ROLL is required! Ports have been changed a bit. The creater of a\n     port can now make $$ from a share of its profits. The percentage\n     of the ports profits that a player can recieve is 10 by default.\n     This can be changed in the port editor. 0-100%\n     \n     When porting one turn gets used. Players that were able to steal/rob\n     ect. were getting rich with hardly any moves at all.\n     \n     BUGS fixed. Kicking player off a planet. (only 1 character was allowed\n     to be entered). Torpedoes can now be purchased fairly.\n     \n     Added to Text:Defaults.txt\n     In the player editor ''@'' from the main menu. You will get an option\n     ''D'' for defaults.txt. It is pretty straight forward. \n     You can edit how many turns a player can have. \n     \n     @{"Text/Defaults.txt " link defaults.txt}       \n\nV 1.9\n    \n     RIP Gfx is fully functional. More ships. More bug fixes. New\n     ANSI options. Just alot of different additions!\n     Non-Registered version only can have 5 players now. Also\n     only 2 players will be able to play at the same time.\n\nV 1.92\n\n    DeepHost will completely detach from stdin and out. All you\n    have to do is type DeepHost and the shell can be closed.\n    Can change the task priority of DeepHost by passing a number\n    from -5 to 100. (This is NOT recomended). To shut down the\n    host with out requesters pass QUIET.. DeepHost QUIET\n    \n    DeepSpace. Bug fixed for the argument ASKRIP. It asked but still\n    went into RIP anyways.. (Sorry) Fully functional on CNET now (RIP)\n\nV 1.93\n   \n    Found I Obtained a Semaphore & didnt Release it (sorry) It would\n    hang all other nodes..\n    \n    Fixed bug when transporting from one planet to another.. If you\n    would quit after transporting it would not save your sector\n    to the new location.\n    \n        \n@endnode\n\n@node fut "Future"\n\n     I have had a lot of GREAT suggestions. I am keeping a list of them.\n     I wanted to get rid of all the problems I can find before adding\n     a lot more features to the game.\n     \n     Possible new features for phase II:\n          Planet Praxis:\n          If you go through the BlackHole when it is un-stable \n          (and make it through) you probably have seen the planet ''Praxis''.\n          I plan on making this a ''second'' phase of the game. The player\n          could beam to the planet from his ship.  Hostile Mutants & Aliens \n          would be populated on the planet, along with running into other \n          players from another bbs-node.  Different objects could be obtained\n          there. Weapons? Better Scanners? Crystals? etc..  An example that''s\n          being considered is: A VERY VERY RARE weapon that can be left at \n          the DeepSpace Port. The weapon could be programmed (via DNA scan) \n          to detonate when a certain Explorer arrived.  It would shoot a \n          projectile at the head of the player. This would be for your very \n          best Explorer friends! - NOT!\n             \n          Gambling:\n          A lot more ways to win $$. Possibly against other players as well. \n                    \n@endnode\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',90686,1,0,NULL,NULL,'dspc193','scan',1780048488);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('dsv_ls10','DSV-LS10.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DSV-LS10.LHA','LogStrip','XIM','LogStrip',NULL,NULL,'DSV','|o:::  __    __  ___ __  _  ________  _:::o| |:::  /  \/\/ _)/__//  \// // __/, )\// :::| |:»»»/ / / /\ \/ \_/ / / \// _//  </ /«««::|',replace('.------------------------------------------.\n|o:::  __    __  ___ __  _  ________  _:::o|\n|:::  /  \/\/ _)/__//  \// // __/, )\// :::|\n|:»»»/ / / /\ \/ \_/ / / \// _//  </ /«««::|\n|::: \__/\/(__/\__/\__/\__/\__/\/\/\/   :::|\n|o:::                           -dMA!- :::o|\n`------------------------------------------''\n              --[ PresentS ]--\n      LogStripper V1.0 for AmiExpress\n           Released on 7-feb-95!\n          This is not Arexx shit!\n','\n',char(10)),'LogStrip.guide',replace('@database WiLDHeLP.guide\n@master Data:Work/LogStrip.guide\n@remark Created with Heddley v1.1 (c) Edd Dumbill 1994\n\n@node "MAIN" "LogStripper V1.0 by dR.dMA/DiScoVery"\n\n                     [---> ·D·i·S·c·o·V·e·r·y· <---]\n                                 presents\n\n                             LogStripper V1.0\n                       (C) 02/95 by dR.dMA/DiScoVery\n   ====================================================================\n\n                         @{" Introduction        " link "introduction" 0}\n                         @{" Installation        " link "installation" 0}\n                         @{" History             " link "history" 0}\n                         @{" Contact             " link "contact" 0}\n                         @{" Greetings           " link "Greetings" 0}\n@endnode\n\n@node "introduction" "Introduction"\n LogStripper is not a door.  What else then?  Ehh...one util for all versions\nof  AmiExpress  with you can keep small your logfiles.  It''s fully programmed\nin  SAS/C  6.50,  and  it''s  small  and  powerfull,  not  that  fucking Arexx\nlogstrippers.\n\n\n                have fun and a horny day\n\n                                          dR.dMA/DiScoVery\n@endnode\n\n@node "history" "history"\n                 -======================================-\n                               H I S T O R Y\n                 -======================================-\n\n-VeR----ReL.DaTe-----------------------CHaNGeD/uPDaTeD--------------------\n 1.0    07-02-95        first version\n@endnode\n\n@node "installation" "Installation"\n\n                             _________________  \n                            |~ |___________| ~|\n                            |  |___________|  |\n                            |  |___________|  |\n                            |  |___________|  |\n                            |  |___________|  |\n                            |    _________    |\n                            |   |~_    ~| |   |\n                            |   || |    | |   |\n                            l   || |    | |   |\n                             \_,||_|____|_|___|\n\nWhen  You  have  unpacked this archive, just copy the file called LogStrip to\nBBS:Utils dir.  In your logoff batch at the end place the following lines:\n\n\nBBS:Utils/LogStrip BBS:NodeX/CallersLog 50000\nBBS:Utils/LogStrip BBS:NodeX/UDLog 50000\n\n\nIf  you  want  to strip your CallersLog and UDLog.  That number at the end of\ncommandline is the size oy new logfile.  The default is 100000 bytes.\n\nAnd  don''t forget:  This little stuff does not requires OS2.0+ !  It works on\n1.3+...ehhe.:)\n@endnode\n\n@node "contact" "Contact me ..."\n-- Country -------- Board Name ------ Board Numba ----- Write to ----------\n\n  Yugoslavia         oBiTuArY       +38-124-821361       dR.dMA\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n@endnode\n\n@node "Greetings" "Greetings to:"\n dR.DMA/DiScoVery sends greetings to the following people:\n\n - Snake & Delboy / Prestige\n - MOK / Prestige\n - Mucsi / MuffBusters\n - Kozmo / Juice\n - Dr.Darcco / DiScoVery\n - WiZ / Hitchikkers\n - Mr. Return / Hitchikkers\n - WiLD THiNG / ¥oÐeL\n - P0T NOodle / Anthrox\n - Mack LifeGuard / Dual Crew-Shining\n - Dominator / CoRRoSioN\n \n@endnode\n\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',16256,8,0,NULL,NULL,'dsv_ls10','scan',1780048488);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('dsv_wall','DSV-WALL.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DSV-WALL.LHA','DSV-Wall','XIM','.------------------------------------------.',NULL,NULL,'DSV','|o:::  __    __  ___ __  _  ________  _:::o| |:::  /  \/\/ _)/__//  \// // __/, )\// :::| |:»»»/ / / /\ \/ \_/ / / \// _//  </ /«««::|',replace('.------------------------------------------.\n|o:::  __    __  ___ __  _  ________  _:::o|\n|:::  /  \/\/ _)/__//  \// // __/, )\// :::|\n|:»»»/ / / /\ \/ \_/ / / \// _//  </ /«««::|\n|::: \__/\/(__/\__/\__/\__/\__/\/\/\/   :::|\n|o:::                           -dMA!- :::o|\n`------------------------------------------''\n                 Presents:                  \n               DSV-Wall V1.0                \n          For /X 3.x & 4.x & OS2.0+         \n','\n',char(10)),'DSV-Wall.guide',replace('@database WiLDHeLP.guide\n@master Data:Work/bbs/DOORS/WiLDTools/WiLDHeLP/WiLDHeLP.GUiDe\n@remark Created with Heddley v1.1 (c) Edd Dumbill 1994\n\n@node "MAIN" "WiLD HeLP v0.1 (c) 09/94  WiLD THiNG/YoDeL"\n\n                     [---> ·D·i·S·c·o·V·e·r·y· <---]\n                                 presents\n\n                               DSV-Wall V1.0\n                       (c) 02/95 by dR.dMA/DiScoVery\n   ====================================================================\n\n                         @{" Introduction        " link "introduction" 0}\n                         @{" Installation        " link "installation" 0}\n                         @{" History             " link "history" 0}\n                         @{" Contact             " link "contact" 0}\n@endnode\n\n@node "introduction" "Introduction"\n DSV-Wall is an /X 3.xx & 4.xx XiM door. What is this?  What is a wall?  You\nknow it....\n\n                have fun and a horny day\n\n                                          dR.dMA/DiScoVery\n@endnode\n\n@node "history" "history"\n                 -======================================-\n                               H I S T O R Y\n                 -======================================-\n\n-VeR----ReL.DaTe-----------------------CHaNGeD/uPDaTeD--------------------\n 1.0    03-02-95        first version\n@endnode\n\n@node "installation" "Installation"\n\n                             _________________  \n                            |~ |___________| ~|\n                            |  |___________|  |\n                            |  |___________|  |\n                            |  |___________|  |\n                            |  |___________|  |\n                            |    _________    |\n                            |   |~_    ~| |   |\n                            |   || |    | |   |\n                            l   || |    | |   |\n                             \_,||_|____|_|___|\n\nWhen You have unpacked this little shit just copy the files to BBSCmd and\nDOORS: directory. If You don''t copy Wall.data, don''t be afraid, it will be\ninstalled automatically.\n\nAnd don''t forget: DSV-Wall requires OS2.0+ !\n@endnode\n\n@node "contact" "Contact me ..."\n-- Country -------- Board Name ------ Board Numba ----- Write to ----------\n\n  Yugoslavia         oBiTuArY       +38-124-821361       dR.dMA\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n@endnode\n\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,NULL,25397,6,0,NULL,NULL,'dsv_wall','scan',1780048488);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('dsv2_00','DSV2_00.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DSV2_00.LHA','DeepHost','XIM','DeepHost',NULL,'Player/Node','DSV2','||   ||  ||    ||     ||    \ ||   ||  ||--  ||--   ||____/ + ||___||  ||___ ||___  ||        +',replace('+ ______   _____+_____  ______    +\n  ||   ||  ||    ||     ||    \\n  ||   ||  ||--  ||--   ||____/ +\n  ||___||  ||___ ||___  ||        +\n+   DeepSpace Version 2.0! DOS\n  Multi-Player/Node Space BBS Door\n      Now with RIP Grafics!\n','\n',char(10)),'DeepSpace.guide',replace('@database DeepSpace\n\n@node main "DeepSpace! Docs"\n                 \n @{" About DeepSpace " link about}\n\n @{" Legal Stuff " link leagalstuff}\n\n @{" Setting up DeepSpace " link setup}\n                         \n @{" Registering " link register}\n                         \n @{" Known Bugs " link bugs}\n        \n @{" History " link history}\n        \n @{" Future " link fut}\n                                \n@endnode\n@node about "About DeepSpace"\n\n     DeepSpace is a Multi-Player, Multi-Node Space Role Playing Game.\n     BBS''s that can run DOS doors (programs that use stdin & stdout) can\n     run this game.\n      \n     *Note a Paragon Version is also available.\n      DeepSpaceP will allow only one node at a time to run. \n      \n     Twenty nodes can play at one time, this has never been actually\n     tested though. Up to 100 users can join the game if @{"REGISTERED"link register}\n     If it isn''t @{"REGISTERED" link register} only 5 players are allowed.\n            \n     Enforcer & Mungwall have been used during development of this version. \n     There should not be any hits, unless I missed one.  I have also \n     setup a 10 minute inactivity timer in the game.  This means that if \n     a user playing the game doesn''t press a key for 10 minutes the game \n     will automatically clean up and exit.\n     \n     DeepSpaceP (Paragon Version)   \n#    DeepSpace \n#    DeepHost\n#    DeepSet\n          \n          Programmed by Todd Neumiller\n     \n     \n     \n     GameSetUp\n          Programmed by Todd & Darrell Neumiller\n     Ansi GFX \n          Darren Campbell  &  Rod Whitney \n# = only in Dos version           \n                                 \n@endnode\n\n@node leagalstuff "Legal Stuff"\n \n     DeepSpace (c) Copyright 1994-1995\n \n     These Files and their related documentation are provided "AS-IS" and \n     are subject to change without notice, no warranties are made.  All \n     use, is at your own risk.  No liability or responsibility is assumed.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node setup "Setting up DeepSpace"\n\n     WorkBench 2.0 or greater is required.\n\n     In the directory you unarced the DeepSpace archive, you will have\n     GameSetUp\n     DeepHost \n     DeepSet  - This is used by the DeepHost program.\n     DeepSpace\n     DeepSpace.guide (the file your reading)\n     Text (dir) w/ numerous text files\n          Ones of interest for setting up the config for DeepSpace are:\n          1: @{"Text/AlienRaces" link araces}\n          2: @{"Text/Sysop.txt " link sysop.txt}\n          3: @{"Text/Remarks.txt " link remarks.txt}            \n          4: @{"Text/Defaults.txt " link defaults.txt}\n     ENV: \n          5: @{"ENV:config    " link conf}\n\n     After the above mentioned files are edited to your liking run \n     GameSetup.  This program will generate all the files needed to\n     run DeepSpace.  GameSetup should also be ran when you wish to\n     re-roll the game.\n\n     * NEW V1.0 *\n     Also the path to which you have set up DeepSpace in can be\n     saved into your config file. It is optional. If the files \n     can not be found by DeepSpace fill in this option.\n     Give it the path you are running Gamesetup in.\n     Example: Dh0:Doors/Deepspace/\n     \n     DeepHost\n     This program is a background server for the DeepSpace game.\n     After gamesetup has been ran you can now start DeepHost. I\n     suggest you run it from your S:User-Startup file.\n     NOTE: It must remain in the same directory as the other files.\n                Do not copy it to another directory.\n           \n     Example: \n          dh0:doors/deepspace/deephost  \n     It runs DeepSet, which loads all the data needed for the game.\n     This allows DeepHost to become somewhat smaller in size.\n     To shut down DeepHost just simply run DeepHost again. It will \n     signal the original DeepHost program to shut down. A requester\n     will pop up showing you that it is shutting down. If you do not\n     want a requester to pop up (via remote or ??) Give DeepHost the\n     argument of QUIET. \n     Example:\n          DeepHost quiet\n     This will force the requester to stay off.\n          NOTE: Do not give an argument unless shutting DeepHost off!!!!\n     If any games are being played when you shut off the DeepHost program\n     they also will be signaled to stop.     \n     As of version 2.0 of DeepHost, you can pass an argument for the\n     priority of DeepHost (from -5 to 100) this is not recomended!\n     Example: \n          DeepHost 50\n          This will run DeepHost with a priority of 50.\n     I did not put alot of code checking the arguments.. You can only pass\n     QUIET or intigers from -5 to 100. And not both..\n                                              \n     You are ready to go! The program has only been tested on a Excelsior \n     BBS as of late. If you have ran it on any-other BBS please add a \n     comment on the bottom of this file giving directions if anything \n     different in the setup was required.\n     Also please let us know!\n\n     If you are able to adjust the priority DON''T. Just run DeepSpace at 0.\n  \nDeepSpace Arguments:\n--------------------\nUsage:\nDeepSpace Name/A/M,T=Time/A/N,Echo/S,D=Delay/N,A=Delay/N,CNET/S,LOCAL/S\n\nName      The users name\n            /A - Required\n            /M - Multiple strings allowed\nT=Time    The time allowed to play\n            /A - Required\n            /N - numbers\nEcho      Echo key press''s\n            /S - switch\nD=Delay   Fight delay instead of default (40000)\n            /N - numbers (1 to 200000000) \n          NOTE: If D is set to 0 it forces all players have the Auto-Attack \n                on. Do NOT set to a negative number.\nA=Delay   An alternate delay method. (1 - 200)\n                                             (ticks 50 = one second)\n          Don''t use this option unless you have to.                        \n          This depends on the BBS you are using.                         \n          During an attack sequence, numbers that range from 1 to 10 will be \n          printed. The object is to hit a key as close as possible to 10.\n          This is the percent power used during that fight wave. If all the\n          numbers flash by with out a delay you have to use this option.\n          If this occurs and you do not use this option the game, the \n          program will eat up a lot of CPU time. Have this set even if you \n          want Auto-Attack on. (none interaction fight)\n          \n          Set d to 0 if you want Auto-Attack on.\n          Set a to a min of 6.\n          @{"Testing setup delays" link sud}\n\nCNET      A flag for CNET BBS only!!!!\n          To use %7 for passing time left you have to use this switch.\n          I was told that this pass''s time in tenths of a minute. This\n          switch tells DeepSpace to convert the time.\n\nLOCAL     flag to turn off RIP auto-sensing. For BBS''s that can have\n          RIP GFX played local use this.\n                       \nNOTE: Name & Time are REQUIRED!                         \n      Replace name(s) ,time with the proper BBS codes which are applicable. \nAn example:  \n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 Echo D=60000\n  Name is John Doe, Time allowed 120 minutes, Echo on, Delay set to 60000\n  * for most BBS''s\n\nOther examples:\n\n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 (Min arguments)\n  Echo off using default delay of 40000 for the Attack delay.\n             \n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 d=0\n  Auto-Attack always on: Which will take away this option for the player\n  to toggle themselves.\n  \n          DeepSpace John Doe t=30 echo a=8\n  Alternate delay method. Since d is at the default (40000) Auto-Attack off \n  (which will make it optional to the player).  Delay of 8 ticks for the \n  attack delay & echo on.        \n             \n          DeepSpace John Doe t=30 echo d=0 a=8\n  Alternate delay method. Auto-Attack always on. Delay of 8 ticks\n  for the attack delay & echo on.        \n        \n                   \n@endnode\n@node sud "Testing setup delays"\n\n  If you are using the D option for the attack delay set it very high at \n  first. (~ 100000)  Or the A option set at ~ 20.  Have someone attack an \n  alien via remote (to find the Alien use the sysop editor & check which \n  sector they are in or move them to your sector).  Check the speed during\n  the fight.  Change the attacking ship to a Sprite (only 1 on offense) \n  against Fed ship (5 defense). This will be the fastest speed at which\n  the fight sequence will go, and adjust it from there..   \n\n  *NOTE: if you are using the D option for the delay (at 100000) and the \n         fighting sequence flies by before you can push a key this indicates \n         that you have to use the A option.\n         \n  On our Excelsior BBS we use D=70000;\n  \n@endnode \n@node araces "AlienRaces"\n\n     Text/AlienRaces\n     \n     AlienRaces is a simple text file. It can be edited with any type of text\n     editor.  It is a list of Alien race names that will be given to the \n     generated alien''s in the game.  Example: Klingon\n     \n     You must have this file in the text directory or the GameSetup program \n     will fail. (you will read about GameSetup later). \n     \n     The maximum number of alien race names that can be used is 50.  If you \n     have 25 or less names in the text file, 2 aliens will be generated for \n     each race.\n     13 names = 26 total aliens,\n     25 names = 50 total aliens,\n     26 names = 26 total aliens etc..\n        \n@endnode\n@node sysop.txt "Text/Sysop.txt"\n\n     Text/sysop.txt\n     \n     sysop.txt is a list of names that DeepSpace can access for\n     getting the sysop(s) name(s). The names in this simple text\n     file should match the name that will be passed to DeepSpace\n     for the players name.\n     \n     For example you and your Co-Sysop''s handle''s were ''Roentgen'' \n     & ''Big Byte'' the text file would just contain these 2\n     names. Do not have trailing spaces behind these names. Hit \n     enter right after the full name is typed.\n     \n     This file can also be edited while in the user editor under\n     defaults.\n     \n     EDITORS:\n     \n     To enter the Planet/Port editor hit ''!'' at the main menu. The \n     Planet/Port editor is self explanatory.  To access the user\n     editor hit ''@'' at the main prompt.\n     \n     To add a Planet is very simple through the planet editor.\n     At the start of the game you will notice that in the planet\n     editor, the numbers of the planets start at about 135. To be \n     GOD and create a planet just select a number from 0 to 134 & fill \n     in all the information for the planet you wish to create.\n     \n     To create Aliens is about the same as stated above but through\n     the user editor. You will notice that all Aliens are over 99\n     in the list of users. 100 to 149.(50 Aliens MAX)\n     To create a Alien just select a number from 100 to 149 that is not\n     used and fill out all the info in the editor.\n          \n      \n                     \n@endnode\n@node remarks.txt "Text/Remarks.txt"\n\n     Text/Remarks.txt\n     \n     Remarks.txt is a list of strings that DeepSpace uses for insults.\n     When a player attacks someone and destroys their ship they will\n     insult the attacker.  This file can be edited inside the user editor\n     under defaults. The user editor can be entered at the main menu by\n     hitting @ at the main prompt.     \n     The strings you enter are used with the names of the attacker & the\n     attacked. These strings can only be 32 characters long!\n     Example:\n          If Roentgen attacked Big Byte & destroyed his ship. Your string \n          would be inserted like this...\n          \n          Big Bytes says Roentgen ''your string here''\n          \n     The format of Text/Remarks.txt\n          line 1 ''has the number of strings in the text file''\n          line 2 ''first string''\n          line 3 ''second string''\n          ect..\n          \n     If you edit this file with in the user editor you won''t even\n     have to worry about the format of the text file.      \n          \n@endnode\n@node defaults.txt "Text/Defaults.txt"\n\n     Text/Defaults.txt\n     \n     Defaults.txt is a list of variable''s that the Sysop can Edit. \n     \n     1: What ship the first time player will get.\n     2: How much cash the first time player will get.\n     3: How many ExaWatts the first time player will get.\n     4: How many turns the ships get. \n        This is based on a ratio. The default is 100.\n     5: How much Shields the first time player will get.\n     \n     These can be edited through the player editor. ''@'' at the main\n     prompt. You will see ''D'' for defaults as one of your available\n     selections.\n     \n               \n@endnode\n@node conf "Config"\n\n     ENV:DeepSpaceD/config \n     \n     This is a file that GameSetup will create. If you are registered\n     you have to make sure this file is not distributed.  \n\n@endnode\n\n@node register "Registering DeepSpace"\n\n     Registering DeepSpace can be done by sending $20 US dollars to:\n\n     Todd Neumiller\n     P.O. Box 18591\n     Spokane, WA 99208 USA                \n\nor   Europe/Germany \n  \n     Bj"orn Labitzke\n     Halemweg 43\n     13627 Berlin\n     Germany / Europe\n     Tel.: +49 30 382 29 78\n     E-Mail: [email removed]\n     \nInfo needed:\n     1: Your FULL name\n     2: Handle if used\n     3: BBS name (BBS phone #) \n     4: Mailing address\n\n     You can also get more information by calling or sending e-mail to:\n     \n     Internet:   (Todd Neumiller)   [email removed] \n       \n\n                \nSupport BBS:   Ami-Tech BBS  509-838-6490 \n                    Sysop Rod Whitney  (AKA Spark Chaser)\n            \n@endnode\n\n@node bugs "Known BUGS"\n\n     I fix em as I find em! Or when someone else finds em.\n     Thanks for all the bug reports! \n     \n@endnode\n\n@node history "History"\n\nV.6  First working version.\n\nV.7  Found Mungwall hit & fixed it. Fixed the bug that only allowed a player\n     to e-mail someone with a user # with 1 digit.\n     *Note: Paragon version of DeepSpace created.\n\nV1.0 Big changes to timing method in DeepSpace. Herman Stevens from\n     Laukensstraat Belgium deserves all the credit. Informed me about\n     it and also told me how to fix it! (THANKS!) \n     In showing known/unknown/avoided sectors could crash. This was fixed.\n     Spelling fixes - Yah I can''t Spel \n     Other small odds and ends....\n     GameSetup changed. Added the ability for the path to be saved into\n     the config file. The config file is saved in ENV: ENVARC: \n     This was done to hopefully fix the problem DLG BBS''s have running\n     DeepSpace in a directory besides the assigned DLG: directory.\n     \nV1.2 /* NOTE you do not have to re-roll your game if you were running the\n        last version (V1.0) */\n     MORE spelling fixes!\n     Are you sure (Y/n) prompts fixed so you really can change your mind.\n     Sysop editor: Can now move someone to sector 1001\n     ANSI files re-worked a little. \n     A switch for CNET to convert the time argument. \n     Some problems around porting at sector 1.\n     You can''t make a fortune selling your ship over & over.\n     GameSetup was changed so you do not have to re-roll to add registration.\n\nV1.3 Added contraband. \n     Fixed radio silence, now the command match''s the menu\n\nV1.4 We have heard a rumor that it was possible to get the program hung \n     on a disconnect during the game.  I Found two routines using Flush() \n     and took them out. Instead of just using ^C to abort the game, I have \n     added ^D, & ^E also. DeepSpace has always had a 10 minute inactivity\n     time-out.\n     \n     A ''default'' editor added. This can be accessed from the user editor.\n     ''@'' at the main prompt. (see Text/Sysop.txt)\n     The sysop can now select which ship, & how much $$ a player will \n     start with when they first enter the game.\n\n     @{"Text/Remarks.txt " link remarks.txt} added. Can edit through the default editor.\n     \n     @{"Text/Sysop.txt " link sysop.txt} can also be edited through the default editor.\n\nV1.6 Ooops! Forgot about 1.5.\n     The planet editor ''!'' from the main menu now also has a port editor.\n     RE_ROLL is required! Ports have been changed a bit. The creater of a\n     port can now make $$ from a share of its profits. The percentage\n     of the ports profits that a player can recieve is 10 by default.\n     This can be changed in the port editor. 0-100%\n     \n     When porting one turn gets used. Players that were able to steal/rob\n     ect. were getting rich with hardly any moves at all.\n     \n     BUGS fixed. Kicking player off a planet. (only 1 character was allowed\n     to be entered). Torpedoes can now be purchased fairly.\n     \n     Added to Text:Defaults.txt\n     In the player editor ''@'' from the main menu. You will get an option\n     ''D'' for defaults.txt. It is pretty straight forward. \n     You can edit how many turns a player can have. \n     \n     @{"Text/Defaults.txt " link defaults.txt}       \n\nV 1.9\n    \n     RIP Gfx is fully functional. More ships. More bug fixes. New\n     ANSI options. Just alot of different additions!\n     Non-Registered version only can have 5 players now. Also\n     only 2 players will be able to play at the same time.\n\nV 1.92\n\n    DeepHost will completely detach from stdin and out. All you\n    have to do is type DeepHost and the shell can be closed.\n    Can change the task priority of DeepHost by passing a number\n    from -5 to 100. (This is NOT recomended). To shut down the\n    host with out requesters pass QUIET.. DeepHost QUIET\n    \n    DeepSpace. Bug fixed for the argument ASKRIP. It asked but still\n    went into RIP anyways.. (Sorry) Fully functional on CNET now (RIP)\n\nV 1.93\n   \n    Found I Obtained a Semaphore & didnt Release it (sorry) It would\n    hang all other nodes..\n    \n    Fixed bug when transporting from one planet to another.. If you\n    would quit after transporting it would not save your sector\n    to the new location.\n    \n        \n@endnode\n\n@node fut "Future"\n\n     I have had a lot of GREAT suggestions. I am keeping a list of them.\n     I wanted to get rid of all the problems I can find before adding\n     a lot more features to the game.\n     \n     Possible new features for phase II:\n          Planet Praxis:\n          If you go through the BlackHole when it is un-stable \n          (and make it through) you probably have seen the planet ''Praxis''.\n          I plan on making this a ''second'' phase of the game. The player\n          could beam to the planet from his ship.  Hostile Mutants & Aliens \n          would be populated on the planet, along with running into other \n          players from another bbs-node.  Different objects could be obtained\n          there. Weapons? Better Scanners? Crystals? etc..  An example that''s\n          being considered is: A VERY VERY RARE weapon that can be left at \n          the DeepSpace Port. The weapon could be programmed (via DNA scan) \n          to detonate when a certain Explorer arrived.  It would shoot a \n          projectile at the head of the player. This would be for your very \n          best Explorer friends! - NOT!\n             \n          Gambling:\n          A lot more ways to win $$. Possibly against other players as well. \n                    \n@endnode\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',273897,4,0,NULL,NULL,'dsv2_00','scan',1780048488);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('dsy_ql1','DSY-QL1.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DSY-QL1.LHA','DSY-QUICK','XIM','DSY-QUICK',NULL,'KId/dSY·','DSY','___|__\__/ ___/__\  |  \_ _/   __/  \_____  \_  !    \_-[·1·9·9·5·]-. \    !     /   !   /_____.  / pRESENtS:   |',replace('      .__    _____  ___.__\n   ___|__\__/ ___/__\  |  \_\n _/   __/  \_____  \_  !    \_-[·1·9·9·5·]-.\n \    !     /   !   /_____.  / pRESENtS:   |\n .\________/_______/________/              |\n |   ANOTHER NICE LOOKING DOOR FROM DSY    |\n |      ·QUICKLOGIN v1.0 bY KId/dSY·       |\n `-----------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),'lOSeNDOS.guide',replace('                   \n                     __  _ __  /\__ _  _ __/\  __ _  __\n                    _\/___   \/              \/   ___\/_\n              ......\/\  /........................\  /\/......\n             :         \/                          \/         :\n             :      .___             .___         .___        :\n             :   _ _|   \      ______|   \  ______|   \       :\n             :   \\\__   7___  \______   /  \__   ____/       :\n             :      /.   |   \__/.  l/   \___/_____   \__ _   :\n             :     /::.  |    _/::.       _/::.  l/    _///   :\n             :     \___________\___________\____________\     :\n             :___        .___        .___        .___         :___\n     _ ______|   \ ______|   \ ______|   \ ______|   \  ______|   \\n     \\\__   ____/ \______   / \______   / \______   /  \__   ____/\n        /.   ___|___/.   |   \__/.  _|   \__/.  l/   \___/_____   \__ _\n       /::.  l/   _/::.  |    _/::. \___  _/::.       _/::.  l/    _///\n       \_ _________\_____|_____\___________\___________\___________ \\n         Y   :                                                :[nM$]Y\n      .------''                                                `------.\n      :eXON/rIOT^jORMAS · gHANDY/gODS · rAHIEM/eSSENCE · cELIC/rEKTUM: \n      `------.                                                .------''\n             `------------------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',32472,5,0,NULL,NULL,'dsy_ql1','scan',1780048488);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('ds_1_96','DS_1_96.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DS_1_96.LHA','DeepHost','XIM','DeepHost',NULL,'Player/Node','DS','||   ||  ||    ||     ||    \ ||   ||  ||--  ||--   ||____/ + ||___||  ||___ ||___  ||        +',replace('+ ______   _____+_____  ______    +\n  ||   ||  ||    ||     ||    \\n  ||   ||  ||--  ||--   ||____/ +\n  ||___||  ||___ ||___  ||        +\n    Support BBS  (509) 926-5507\n  Multi-Player/Node Space BBS Door\n       Now with RIP Grafics!\n','\n',char(10)),'DeepSpace.guide',replace('@database DeepSpace\n\n@node main "DeepSpace! Docs"\n                 \n @{" About DeepSpace " link about}\n\n @{" Legal Stuff " link leagalstuff}\n\n @{" Setting up DeepSpace " link setup}\n                         \n @{" Registering " link register}\n                         \n @{" Known Bugs " link bugs}\n        \n @{" History " link history}\n        \n @{" Future " link fut}\n                                \n@endnode\n@node about "About DeepSpace"\n\n     DeepSpace is a Multi-Player, Multi-Node Space Role Playing Game.\n     BBS''s that can run DOS doors (programs that use stdin & stdout) can\n     run this game.\n      \n     *Note a Paragon Version is also available.\n      DeepSpaceP will allow only one node at a time to run. \n      \n     Twenty nodes can play at one time, this has never been actually\n     tested though. Up to 100 users can join the game if @{"REGISTERED"link register}\n     If it isn''t @{"REGISTERED" link register} only 5 players are allowed.\n            \n     Enforcer & Mungwall have been used during development of this version. \n     There should not be any hits, unless I missed one.  I have also \n     setup a 10 minute inactivity timer in the game.  This means that if \n     a user playing the game doesn''t press a key for 10 minutes the game \n     will automatically clean up and exit.\n     \n     DeepSpaceP (Paragon Version)   \n#    DeepSpace \n#    DeepHost\n#    DeepSet\n          \n          Programmed by Todd Neumiller\n     \n     \n     \n     GameSetUp\n          Programmed by Todd & Darrell Neumiller\n     Ansi GFX \n          Darren Campbell  &  Rod Whitney \n# = only in Dos version           \n                                 \n@endnode\n\n@node leagalstuff "Legal Stuff"\n \n     DeepSpace (c) Copyright 1994 \n \n     These Files and their related documentation are provided "AS-IS" and \n     are subject to change without notice, no warranties are made.  All \n     use, is at your own risk.  No liability or responsibility is assumed.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node setup "Setting up DeepSpace"\n\n     WorkBench 2.0 or greater is required.\n\n     In the directory you unarced the DeepSpace archive, you will have\n     GameSetUp\n     DeepHost \n     DeepSet  - This is used by the DeepHost program.\n     DeepSpace\n     DeepSpace.guide (the file your reading)\n     Text (dir) w/ numerous text files\n          Ones of interest for setting up the config for DeepSpace are:\n          1: @{"Text/AlienRaces" link araces}\n          2: @{"Text/Sysop.txt " link sysop.txt}\n          3: @{"Text/Remarks.txt " link remarks.txt}            \n          4: @{"Text/Defaults.txt " link defaults.txt}\n     ENV: \n          5: @{"ENV:config    " link conf}\n\n     After the above mentioned files are edited to your liking run \n     GameSetup.  This program will generate all the files needed to\n     run DeepSpace.  GameSetup should also be ran when you wish to\n     re-roll the game.\n\n     * NEW V1.0 *\n     Also the path to which you have set up DeepSpace in can be\n     saved into your config file. It is optional. If the files \n     can not be found by DeepSpace fill in this option.\n     Give it the path you are running Gamesetup in.\n     Example: Dh0:Doors/Deepspace/\n     \n     DeepHost\n     This program is a background server for the DeepSpace game.\n     After gamesetup has been ran you can now start DeepHost. I\n     suggest you run it from your S:User-Startup file.\n     NOTE: It must remain in the same directory as the other files.\n                Do not copy it to another directory.\n           \n     Example: \n          dh0:doors/deepspace/deephost  \n     It runs DeepSet, which loads all the data needed for the game.\n     This allows DeepHost to become somewhat smaller in size.\n     To shut down DeepHost just simply run DeepHost again. It will \n     signal the original DeepHost program to shut down. A requester\n     will pop up showing you that it is shutting down. If you do not\n     want a requester to pop up (via remote or ??) Give DeepHost the\n     argument of QUIET. \n     Example:\n          DeepHost quiet\n     This will force the requester to stay off.\n          NOTE: Do not give an argument unless shutting DeepHost off!!!!\n     If any games are being played when you shut off the DeepHost program\n     they also will be signaled to stop.     \n     As of version 2.0 of DeepHost, you can pass an argument for the\n     priority of DeepHost (from -5 to 100) this is not recomended!\n     Example: \n          DeepHost 50\n          This will run DeepHost with a priority of 50.\n     I did not put alot of code checking the arguments.. You can only pass\n     QUIET or intigers from -5 to 100. And not both..\n                                              \n     You are ready to go! The program has only been tested on a Excelsior \n     BBS as of late. If you have ran it on any-other BBS please add a \n     comment on the bottom of this file giving directions if anything \n     different in the setup was required.\n     Also please let us know!\n\n     If you are able to adjust the priority DON''T. Just run DeepSpace at 0.\n  \nDeepSpace Arguments:\n--------------------\nUsage:\nDeepSpace Name/A/M,T=Time/A/N,Echo/S,D=Delay/N,A=Delay/N,CNET/S,LOCAL/S\n\nName      The users name\n            /A - Required\n            /M - Multiple strings allowed\nT=Time    The time allowed to play\n            /A - Required\n            /N - numbers\nEcho      Echo key press''s\n            /S - switch\nD=Delay   Fight delay instead of default (40000)\n            /N - numbers (1 to 200000000) \n          NOTE: If D is set to 0 it forces all players have the Auto-Attack \n                on. Do NOT set to a negative number.\nA=Delay   An alternate delay method. (1 - 200)\n                                             (ticks 50 = one second)\n          Don''t use this option unless you have to.                        \n          This depends on the BBS you are using.                         \n          During an attack sequence, numbers that range from 1 to 10 will be \n          printed. The object is to hit a key as close as possible to 10.\n          This is the percent power used during that fight wave. If all the\n          numbers flash by with out a delay you have to use this option.\n          If this occurs and you do not use this option the game, the \n          program will eat up a lot of CPU time. Have this set even if you \n          want Auto-Attack on. (none interaction fight)\n          \n          Set d to 0 if you want Auto-Attack on.\n          Set a to a min of 6.\n          @{"Testing setup delays" link sud}\n\nCNET      A flag for CNET BBS only!!!!\n          To use %7 for passing time left you have to use this switch.\n          I was told that this pass''s time in tenths of a minute. This\n          switch tells DeepSpace to convert the time.\n\nLOCAL     flag to turn off RIP auto-sensing. For BBS''s that can have\n          RIP GFX played local use this.\n                       \nNOTE: Name & Time are REQUIRED!                         \n      Replace name(s) ,time with the proper BBS codes which are applicable. \nAn example:  \n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 Echo D=60000\n  Name is John Doe, Time allowed 120 minutes, Echo on, Delay set to 60000\n  * for most BBS''s\n\nOther examples:\n\n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 (Min arguments)\n  Echo off using default delay of 40000 for the Attack delay.\n             \n          DeepSpace John Doe T=120 d=0\n  Auto-Attack always on: Which will take away this option for the player\n  to toggle themselves.\n  \n          DeepSpace John Doe t=30 echo a=8\n  Alternate delay method. Since d is at the default (40000) Auto-Attack off \n  (which will make it optional to the player).  Delay of 8 ticks for the \n  attack delay & echo on.        \n             \n          DeepSpace John Doe t=30 echo d=0 a=8\n  Alternate delay method. Auto-Attack always on. Delay of 8 ticks\n  for the attack delay & echo on.        \n        \n                   \n@endnode\n@node sud "Testing setup delays"\n\n  If you are using the D option for the attack delay set it very high at \n  first. (~ 100000)  Or the A option set at ~ 20.  Have someone attack an \n  alien via remote (to find the Alien use the sysop editor & check which \n  sector they are in or move them to your sector).  Check the speed during\n  the fight.  Change the attacking ship to a Sprite (only 1 on offense) \n  against Fed ship (5 defense). This will be the fastest speed at which\n  the fight sequence will go, and adjust it from there..   \n\n  *NOTE: if you are using the D option for the delay (at 100000) and the \n         fighting sequence flies by before you can push a key this indicates \n         that you have to use the A option.\n         \n  On our Excelsior BBS we use D=70000;\n  \n@endnode \n@node araces "AlienRaces"\n\n     Text/AlienRaces\n     \n     AlienRaces is a simple text file. It can be edited with any type of text\n     editor.  It is a list of Alien race names that will be given to the \n     generated alien''s in the game.  Example: Klingon\n     \n     You must have this file in the text directory or the GameSetup program \n     will fail. (you will read about GameSetup later). \n     \n     The maximum number of alien race names that can be used is 50.  If you \n     have 25 or less names in the text file, 2 aliens will be generated for \n     each race.\n     13 names = 26 total aliens,\n     25 names = 50 total aliens,\n     26 names = 26 total aliens etc..\n        \n@endnode\n@node sysop.txt "Text/Sysop.txt"\n\n     Text/sysop.txt\n     \n     sysop.txt is a list of names that DeepSpace can access for\n     getting the sysop(s) name(s). The names in this simple text\n     file should match the name that will be passed to DeepSpace\n     for the players name.\n     \n     For example you and your Co-Sysop''s handle''s were ''Roentgen'' \n     & ''Big Byte'' the text file would just contain these 2\n     names. Do not have trailing spaces behind these names. Hit \n     enter right after the full name is typed.\n     \n     This file can also be edited while in the user editor under\n     defaults.\n     \n     EDITORS:\n     \n     To enter the Planet/Port editor hit ''!'' at the main menu. The \n     Planet/Port editor is self explanatory.  To access the user\n     editor hit ''@'' at the main prompt.\n     \n     To add a Planet is very simple through the planet editor.\n     At the start of the game you will notice that in the planet\n     editor, the numbers of the planets start at about 135. To be \n     GOD and create a planet just select a number from 0 to 134 & fill \n     in all the information for the planet you wish to create.\n     \n     To create Aliens is about the same as stated above but through\n     the user editor. You will notice that all Aliens are over 99\n     in the list of users. 100 to 149.(50 Aliens MAX)\n     To create a Alien just select a number from 100 to 149 that is not\n     used and fill out all the info in the editor.\n          \n      \n                     \n@endnode\n@node remarks.txt "Text/Remarks.txt"\n\n     Text/Remarks.txt\n     \n     Remarks.txt is a list of strings that DeepSpace uses for insults.\n     When a player attacks someone and destroys their ship they will\n     insult the attacker.  This file can be edited inside the user editor\n     under defaults. The user editor can be entered at the main menu by\n     hitting @ at the main prompt.     \n     The strings you enter are used with the names of the attacker & the\n     attacked. These strings can only be 32 characters long!\n     Example:\n          If Roentgen attacked Big Byte & destroyed his ship. Your string \n          would be inserted like this...\n          \n          Big Bytes says Roentgen ''your string here''\n          \n     The format of Text/Remarks.txt\n          line 1 ''has the number of strings in the text file''\n          line 2 ''first string''\n          line 3 ''second string''\n          ect..\n          \n     If you edit this file with in the user editor you won''t even\n     have to worry about the format of the text file.      \n          \n@endnode\n@node defaults.txt "Text/Defaults.txt"\n\n     Text/Defaults.txt\n     \n     Defaults.txt is a list of variable''s that the Sysop can Edit. \n     \n     1: What ship the first time player will get.\n     2: How much cash the first time player will get.\n     3: How many ExaWatts the first time player will get.\n     4: How many turns the ships get. \n        This is based on a ratio. The default is 100.\n     5: How much Shields the first time player will get.\n     \n     These can be edited through the player editor. ''@'' at the main\n     prompt. You will see ''D'' for defaults as one of your available\n     selections.\n     \n               \n@endnode\n@node conf "Config"\n\n     ENV:DeepSpaceD/config \n     \n     This is a file that GameSetup will create. If you are registered\n     you have to make sure this file is not distributed.  \n\n@endnode\n\n@node register "Registering DeepSpace"\n\n     Registering DeepSpace can be done by sending $20 US dollars to:\n\n     Todd Neumiller\n     P.O. Box 18591\n     Spokane, WA 99208 USA                \n     \nInfo needed:\n     1: Your FULL name\n     2: Handle if used\n     3: BBS name (BBS phone #) \n     4: Mailing address\n\n     You can also get more information by calling or sending e-mail to:\n     \n     Internet:   (Darrell Neumiller)   [email removed] \n       \n\nPrimary Support BBS:   Fragmented BBS 509-926-5507\n                       FIDONET 1:346/47\n                       C-Link 911:6420/6.0\n                       AmigaNet 40:414/12.0\n                         \n                              Sysop Darrell Neumiller (AKA Big Byte)\n                              CoSysop ME! Todd Neumiller (AKA Roentgen)\n                                   \nAlternate Support BBS''s:   Ami-Tech BBS  509-838-6490 \n                              Sysop Rod Whitney  (AKA Spark Chaser)\n                           Stormfront BBS 509-533-2747 (CNET)\n                              Sysop Joe Kowtko (see CNET.docs) \n                              \n(You can also subscribe to DEEP_SPACE, DEEP_SYSOP, and DEEP_FILES to)\n(receive the latest info, contact Darrell at the internet address listed\nabove if interested.\n\n \n@endnode\n\n@node bugs "Known BUGS"\n\n     I fix em as I find em! Or when someone else finds em.\n     Thanks for all the bug reports! \n     \n@endnode\n\n@node history "History"\n\nV.6  First working version.\n\nV.7  Found Mungwall hit & fixed it. Fixed the bug that only allowed a player\n     to e-mail someone with a user # with 1 digit.\n     *Note: Paragon version of DeepSpace created.\n\nV1.0 Big changes to timing method in DeepSpace. Herman Stevens from\n     Laukensstraat Belgium deserves all the credit. Informed me about\n     it and also told me how to fix it! (THANKS!) \n     In showing known/unknown/avoided sectors could crash. This was fixed.\n     Spelling fixes - Yah I can''t Spel \n     Other small odds and ends....\n     GameSetup changed. Added the ability for the path to be saved into\n     the config file. The config file is saved in ENV: ENVARC: \n     This was done to hopefully fix the problem DLG BBS''s have running\n     DeepSpace in a directory besides the assigned DLG: directory.\n     \nV1.2 /* NOTE you do not have to re-roll your game if you were running the\n        last version (V1.0) */\n     MORE spelling fixes!\n     Are you sure (Y/n) prompts fixed so you really can change your mind.\n     Sysop editor: Can now move someone to sector 1001\n     ANSI files re-worked a little. \n     A switch for CNET to convert the time argument. \n     Some problems around porting at sector 1.\n     You can''t make a fortune selling your ship over & over.\n     GameSetup was changed so you do not have to re-roll to add registration.\n\nV1.3 Added contraband. \n     Fixed radio silence, now the command match''s the menu\n\nV1.4 We have heard a rumor that it was possible to get the program hung \n     on a disconnect during the game.  I Found two routines using Flush() \n     and took them out. Instead of just using ^C to abort the game, I have \n     added ^D, & ^E also. DeepSpace has always had a 10 minute inactivity\n     time-out.\n     \n     A ''default'' editor added. This can be accessed from the user editor.\n     ''@'' at the main prompt. (see Text/Sysop.txt)\n     The sysop can now select which ship, & how much $$ a player will \n     start with when they first enter the game.\n\n     @{"Text/Remarks.txt " link remarks.txt} added. Can edit through the default editor.\n     \n     @{"Text/Sysop.txt " link sysop.txt} can also be edited through the default editor.\n\nV1.6 Ooops! Forgot about 1.5.\n     The planet editor ''!'' from the main menu now also has a port editor.\n     RE_ROLL is required! Ports have been changed a bit. The creater of a\n     port can now make $$ from a share of its profits. The percentage\n     of the ports profits that a player can recieve is 10 by default.\n     This can be changed in the port editor. 0-100%\n     \n     When porting one turn gets used. Players that were able to steal/rob\n     ect. were getting rich with hardly any moves at all.\n     \n     BUGS fixed. Kicking player off a planet. (only 1 character was allowed\n     to be entered). Torpedoes can now be purchased fairly.\n     \n     Added to Text:Defaults.txt\n     In the player editor ''@'' from the main menu. You will get an option\n     ''D'' for defaults.txt. It is pretty straight forward. \n     You can edit how many turns a player can have. \n     \n     @{"Text/Defaults.txt " link defaults.txt}       \n\nV 1.9\n    \n     RIP Gfx is fully functional. More ships. More bug fixes. New\n     ANSI options. Just alot of different additions!\n     Non-Registered version only can have 5 players now. Also\n     only 2 players will be able to play at the same time.\n\nV 1.92\n\n    DeepHost will completely detach from stdin and out. All you\n    have to do is type DeepHost and the shell can be closed.\n    Can change the task priority of DeepHost by passing a number\n    from -5 to 100. (This is NOT recomended). To shut down the\n    host with out requesters pass QUIET.. DeepHost QUIET\n    \n    DeepSpace. Bug fixed for the argument ASKRIP. It asked but still\n    went into RIP anyways.. (Sorry) Fully functional on CNET now (RIP)\n        \n@endnode\n\n@node fut "Future"\n\n     I have had a lot of GREAT suggestions. I am keeping a list of them.\n     I wanted to get rid of all the problems I can find before adding\n     a lot more features to the game.\n     \n     Possible new features for phase II:\n          Planet Praxis:\n          If you go through the BlackHole when it is un-stable \n          (and make it through) you probably have seen the planet ''Praxis''.\n          I plan on making this a ''second'' phase of the game. The player\n          could beam to the planet from his ship.  Hostile Mutants & Aliens \n          would be populated on the planet, along with running into other \n          players from another bbs-node.  Different objects could be obtained\n          there. Weapons? Better Scanners? Crystals? etc..  An example that''s\n          being considered is: A VERY VERY RARE weapon that can be left at \n          the DeepSpace Port. The weapon could be programmed (via DNA scan) \n          to detonate when a certain Explorer arrived.  It would shoot a \n          projectile at the head of the player. This would be for your very \n          best Explorer friends! - NOT!\n             \n          Gambling:\n          A lot more ways to win $$. Possibly against other players as well. \n                    \n@endnode\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',264483,1,0,NULL,NULL,'ds_1_96','scan',1780048488);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('dtc_cd30','DTC-CD30.LZX','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DTC-CD30.LZX',NULL,'XIM','mCD',NULL,NULL,'DTC',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'auto-added',17027,0,0,NULL,NULL,'dtc_cd30','scan',1780048488);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('dtc_sr1','DTC-SR1.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DTC-SR1.LHA',NULL,'XIM','______________',NULL,'kRs!/DTC','DTC','============= |   \_   _/ __/ ============ DiGiTAl ChAoS | I> \| || (___ /X^DD  DiVSN ============= |____/|_| \___/ ============',replace('               ______________                \n============= |   \_   _/ __/ ============       \nDiGiTAl ChAoS | I> \| || (___ /X^DD  DiVSN\n============= |____/|_| \___/ ============                     \n             ..p.r.e.s.e.n.t.s..\n.----------------------------------------.\n|    SySTeM ReSeT  V1.0  by kRs!/DTC     |\n`----------------------------------------''	  \nSyStEM REbOotER TooL 4 /X aNd DaYDreAm BBS\nDiSplAys LaST SySTEm ReBooT - NiCE AnSIgfX\nPwPrOtECteD SySOp MOdE - FaST''n''EaZy 2 UsE\n-- AmIgaGUiDE DoC FiXeD In ThIS ReLEaSe --\n','\n',char(10)),'SR10.GuidE',replace('@DATABASE "Default"\n@NODE "MAIN" "SyStEm ReSEt  1.0"\n@TOC "MAIN"\n             .                          .\n             ¡\                         \\/.\n  _____/\____| \______/\_____/\  ___/\___\//\\n /  _____)   |  \   ____/    _ \/   . \_ \/  \           .-------.\n \______ \   i   \____. \____/  \_  i__/ ..   \_ .-------+-------+------.\n   /   /  \____.  /   i  \_/     /  ._)  ¡¡    / | D¡G¡TaL cHaOs |      |\n   \  /    /   i /        /\    /   i \_ ||   /  |     /X''n''DD D¡V¡S¡On |\n    \_____/\__  /\_______/  \  /\____  /_!|  /   `-------+-------+------''\n <---------. \_/ .-----------\/-----\_/--:l_/------------''       |\n           |     |                                               `--.\n         .-+-----+----.           .___/\____/\_____/\____/\______/\ |\n         | `-----''    |           ¡   _ \_  . \_ ____/   . \_    _ \`--.\n         | GfX:   LrY |           |   ___/  i__/___. \   i__/____/  \_ |\n         | cOdE:  kRs |           |  .  \   ._)    i  \_ ._)   /     / |\n     <---+------------+-------.   |  ¡   \_ i \_       / i \_  \    /  |\n         `------------''       |   l__|    /__  /______/___  /   \  /   |\n                              `------l___/--\_/----------\_/-----\/----''\n          \n @{" What iS It? " LINK "WHATIS"}  @{" InSTaLLaTioN " LINK "INSTALL"}  @{" D¡GiTaL ChAøS " LINK "DTC"}  @{" GrEetiNgS " LINK "GREETS"}  @{" CrEdItS " LINK "CREDS"}  \n\n           \n                                                             [ 24.4.95 ] \n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE "WHATIS" "WhAT Is It?"\n@TOC "MAIN"\n(-) What Is It? (-)\n\nSySteM ResEt can be used in two different ways:  normal users can use it to\nsee  when your system has been last rebooted, while co/sysops can use it to\nreset the system from remote.\n\nWhat  does this tool do?  You (Da SySoP) run it, it asks you the magic word\n(Ie:   a  password),  it  asks  you  if  you  are  really  sure  and then..\nthree..two..one...   the  computer is reset and you loose the CARRIER.  You\ncan  wait  a few minutes, then you can try to call your board again, to see\nif it has been able to reboot and to get online again..  Great..  you could\nsay..  but you could also have some doubts about the utility of this tool..\nWell,  imagine  you are on holiday or however far from home and you have to\ncontrol  your  board from remote or (in the worst way =) you have to let it\nin  the  hands  of  your  cosysops..   Now,  if  there''s the need to change\nsomething  in the board configuration (modify some conferences, some access\npresets,  some  commands..)  what  do  you  do?   You  can enter the shell,\ndownload  the  config  files (if u''re using DayDream) or the icons of which\nyou  need  to  change  tooltypes  (/X),  you modify them and in the end you\nupload  them  again  in the right directories.  But at this time the system\nwon''t  see  the changes until it is rebooted..  But you''re from remote, you\ncan''t  press  CTRL-A-A or quit your board and restart it..  Here it is, you\nuse SySTeM ReSeT.  Okee?  `)\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE "INSTALL" "InStAllATiON"\n@TOC "MAIN"\n\n(-) InSTaLL''n''CoNfIguRe (-)\n\nFirst  of  all:   this  is an /X XIM DOOR but you can use it 100% also on a\nDayDream  system, you only need the DD-Xim V2.0 by DarkElf/Access.  I think\nthat  if  you''re  using  DayDream  you''ll have it, ''cause it''s great and it\nallows  you  to use many /X doors.  However, if you haven''t it you can find\nit on DTC boards (Another reason to call them =).\n\nThe  file  SR.CFG  is  read  when  the door is run and if it''s not found in\nDOORS:DTC/  path,  the  door  exits.  This file is made up of 2 lines:  the\npassword required to reset the system and the minimum access level required\nto  enter SySoP mODe.  I strongly suggest you to install also SRL.X in your\nS:USER-STARTUP,  so that SyTeM ReSeT will be able to display date''n''time of\nyour last system reboot.\n\n\n+++ /X INSTALLATION: +++\n\nJust  copy SR.CFG in DOORS:DTC/ but consider that this path is HARDCODED in\nthe  door  and  you  can''t  change  it.   Put the icon file (RESET.INFO) in\nBBSCMD.  Put SR.x wherever you want.\n\n\n+++ DayDream INSTALLATION: +++\n\nCopy  SR.CFG  in  DOORS:DTC/  then  add  the  new  command to your daydream\nconfiguration file, compile it and dream =)..\n\nExample:\n\nDOOR_COMMAND.. RESET\nDOOR_TYPE..... 1\nDOOR_SECURITY. 50\nDOOR_EXECUTE.. Doors:XIM %N DOORS:DTC/SR10/SR.x  \n+\n\nGive  System  Reset  a  standard access level (users will see only when you\nlast rebooted) and make sure you set a co/sysop level for the SySOp MoDe in\nSR.CFG).\n\nThat''s all guys!\n\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE "DTC" "DiGiTal ChAOS"\n@TOC "MAIN"\n\n                  /\             /\   ___\n                 // \           // \_|   |___   ___               __\n        _/\____  \  /  _/\_____ \  /         )_|.  |             /_/\\n        ).     \ /\/\  ).      \/\/\__   ___/  \|  !             \_\/\n        |: ___  \    \//  _____//   \|   |  _   \  | __\n _  /\__|¦ \ /   \    \   |   \      \   |  Y.   \ |/. \          /\\n  \/    |   Y    /    /   |    \     /   |__||   