INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('dvd_asp1','DVD!ASP1.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DVD!ASP1.LHA',NULL,'XIM','Asp68000',NULL,NULL,'DVD','_____________|  _ /_____________ \____.\___   |  //__ ___.\  ___/ .:: |  | \\  |    ./   |  | \\  /\  ::.',replace('                 _____\n   _____________|  _ /_____________\n   \____.\___   |  //__ ___.\  ___/\n.:: |  | \\  |    ./   |  | \\  /\  ::.\n::  |_____/__|\___/ ___|_____/____\ic::\n::            .presents.             ::\n::         >> A-S-P V1.21! <<        ::\n::                                   ::\n::  Cool and powerful system-snooper ::\n::  Especially designed for hackers. ::\n::         >> NEEDS MUI! <<          ::\n`:::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::''\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n              _  _ __ ______________________\n   ________ _________ ________ ________    /____\n  \\__    /_\_______¬\______ ¬\_/    /    /    /     ·tRELLi·\n    /   ___/_/    /___/  \_____/    /    /    /     ·iKOjENiA·\n   /    /   /    /   /    /   /    /    /    /  \n  /____    /____/__ /____    /    /____/____/   \n ____/____/____/___ _  /____/____/    /                ·\n|   _____/    / ________       /\ ________ ________ __/__________ __\n|  /    / ___/¬\__   _ ¬\_____/  \______ ¬\______ ¬\_/   _______¬\_ |\n| /    /  \_  __/    /   /   /    /  \_____/    /   /    /   __/  //|\n|/    /    /  \/    /   /   /    /    /   /    /   /    /    /   /  |\n/____/____/   /____    /____    /____    /____/   /____/____    /   |\n|   /   /____/   /____/   /____/   /____/   /____/    /   /____/kRm |\n|___________________________________________________________________|\n\n                   DVD!ASP1.LHA 139560 bYTES   wAS                    \n                       d·O·W·N·L·O·A·D·E·D fROM                       \n\nboli                   cyberhouse bbs            02 Oct 1995 18:03:40\nDeeJayMix              -[ 4-WarD ]-              02 Oct 1995 18:29:28\n','\n',char(10)),'ASP.guide',replace('@DATABASE ASP.guide    -*-Text-*-\n@REMARK $VER: ASP.guide 1.21 (13.09.95)© 1995-1996 Zeno Montresor\n\n@NODE Stupid "The secret node!"\n\nWow... you found the secret node!\n\nYou really like pressing buttons, don''t you? Even if you don''t know\nwhat they do? You''re lucky that your harddrive didn''t get formatted...\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE MAIN "ASP version 1.21"\n@PREV Preview\n\n                                ASP version 1.21\n                                    13-09-95\n\n           ASP is a program to scan various OS lists and information\n                           ©1995-1996 Zeno Montresor\n\n\n@{" What does it do ?     " LINK Purpose}\n@{" How do I use it?      " LINK Usage}\n@{" New in this version   " LINK New}\n@{" Tips                  " LINK Tips}\n@{" How to reach me       " LINK Contact}\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Purpose "What does it do?"\n\nASP is a very intuitive scan program.\nASP is designed for coder and hacker, it permit to investigate all information\nabout task, libraries, port, resource, expansion board, monitor, memory and\nmuch more.\nEvery window is a graphics rapprese\nntation of one or more important structures.\nIf you don''t remeber the most important structure like Screen, Window, MsgPort,\nSemaphore, Libraries, Resource, ecc, the OS Include files are very useful to\ncomprise the information that ASP visualize.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Usage "What ????"\n\nIf you are not able to use ASP, please buy another machine, like a IBM clone\n(IBM stand for Idiotic Bullshit Machine)\n\nASP use MUI and it is very intuitive to use.\n\nTo Add iconification button run MuiPrefs and click on @{b}Iconify Gadget@{ub} and\n@{b}Iconify Icon@{ub}\n\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE New\n\n  bug fixed:\n\n	- Signal & Break work correctly\n	- You can Close libraries only if library Open Count is greater than 0\n	- You can Close fonts only if Open Count is greater than 0\n	- Now ShowBoard work correctly\n	- Removed Enforcer hit when you select a device (dh0: df0: ecc.)\n	- Removed initial Enforcer hit\n	- Removed GetCC() bug (try to double click on function @{b}GetCC()@{ub} in\n	  exec.library, is only @{b}Invalid@{ub} function in this library )\n	- renamed file offset/wb --> offset/workbench\n	- button GlobalVec work correctly\n	- Now when an error occurred, ASP exit correctly\n	- Removed Alloc memory bug\n\n  NEW:\n\n	- Now you can select if you want library function name or hexadecimal\n	  offset in jump library listview.\n	  Library function name is available if you install the directory @{b}offset@{ub}.\n	  In this directory there are ASCII file that contains function name,\n	  you can edit this file.\n\n	  Format is:\n\n	  16e PutMsg()\n	   | |   |    |\n	   | |   |   return no more character !!!!!!!!\n	   | | function name\n	   | @{b}ONE@{ub} space\n	  hexadecimal offset\n\n	  File name must respect this rule:\n\n	    File name is library name (or device or resource) without suffix.\n	    Example:\n\n	        battclock.resource ---> battclock\n	        expansion.library  ---> expansion\n\n	- Hexadecimal Dump now, if size is know, show only size bytes, otherwise\n	  show 1024 bytes.\n\n	- Add function Close Screen and Close Window. (@{b}USE WITH CARE@{ub})\n\n	- Jump Dump listview automatically update when you select a new library\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n\n@NODE Next "ASP 2.0"\n\nASP 2.0 will be the ultimate AMIGA MONITOR\n\n	- Disassembler\n	- More structure\n	- Save Window and screen as ilbm\n	- more parameter\n	- recognize expansion board\n	- and much more\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Tips "Some Infos"\n\n\nSometimes when Library Window and JumpDump Window are open and you double click\non new library to show, Asp Crash 8-(\nI have tasted a lot my source code whitout find any bug, sorry.\nIf this happened reboot your system and before a double click on new library\nclose JumpDump Window.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n\n@NODE Contact "How to reach me"\n\nIf you have anything to tell me (bugs, suggestions, ideas d:-), send it to:\n\n\n                                 Zeno Montresor\n                                Via Montorio 65\n                               37131 Verona Italy\n\n                              Phone +39-45-532190\n\n\nworthless thing, bomb, and other unhealthy stuff may be send to NIL: or\n/dev/null or whatever it is called on your system.\n\n\nCurrently I''m a student and can be reached at:\n\n\n                            [email removed]\n\n                                       or\n\n                           [email removed]\n\nPlease use the second only if the first is not available.\n\n\nI know, my english is terrible. 8-(\n\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',132137,1,0,NULL,NULL,'dvd_asp1','scan',1780048498);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('dvd_cfp1','DVD!CFP1.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DVD!CFP1.LHA','CrunchFileProtector','XIM','CrunchFileProtector',NULL,NULL,'DVD','\____.\/  .\  \/ ./  .\____.\/ic___/ |  | \\   \\   //    \\  | \\  _/ \ .:|_____/____/\__/\_____/_____/_____/:.',replace(' ______  ______  ____________  ______\n \____.\/  .\  \/ ./  .\____.\/ic___/\n  |  | \\   \\   //    \\  | \\  _/ \\n.:|_____/____/\__/\_____/_____/_____/:.\n::         .oO pRESETNS Oo.          ::\n:: CrunchFileProtector Version 1.35! ::\n::   >>> NO CHANCE FOR FAKERS! <<<   ::\n:: Protect you files against access  ::\n:: and against fakers...             ::\n:: - comfortable GUI, easy to use    ::\n::.  protect against XFD-Tools, too!.::\n·::::::::::::ReleaseDate:(26.09.95!)::·\n\n','\n',char(10)),'CrunchFileProtector.doc',replace('                              .                         :_______\n                __________    :___       _______________|    _ /\n       _  __  _/   ¬\_  _¬\___|  ¬\____ |   _ /   ¬\_ __|_   //_  __  _\n    / // / / /_  |___/    _/  | _ /  _ \|   //  |___/ \___   __/ / / // /\n             /   |  ¬\  | ¬\_   //   ¬\ ·   /   |  ¬\  ¬|   /\n             \_______/__|___/___/|____|\   /\_______/___|__/        __/\__\n      ...                           .   \_/                         \ || /\n     :::::.      ......... . . .    .         .  . . .. ............/_o _\.\n      ·::::......:     __________   :______                        :_ \/.:::\n       :·:::.         /_    ____/___|¬  _ /________                \/  .::·\n       :  ·:::_  __  _\\    __/    ¬|   //   _____/ __  _          :  .::·\n    ...:... \ \\ \ \ \__.    |     ||  ·/__   _/¬\ / / // /   .....:..::·\n    :  :  :  __/\__     \____|_ ____|_____/______/            :ic!dvd::·\n    :  :..:..\ oO /.....                                      :      :·\n    .        /_\ _\  ..:. .. . . .    .                       :\n     __________\/     _____________       _________________   :_____\n    /_  __ ¬\  _¬\____\____   ____/______/_   ¬\____   ___/___|   _¬\_ __  _\n_ __\\   ___/    _/¬   ¬\/    ¬\    ______/ |___/ /.   ¬\ ¬   ¬\    _// / //\n\\\ \\·   ¬||  | ¬\_  | \\     \\    _/¬\   |  ¬\//      \   |  \ | ¬\_\n      \____|___|___/_____/_______\______/_______/_________\_____/_|___/\n\n\n                  ........................................\n                  :                                      :\n               ...:     Version......: 1.35              :...\n               :  :     Release Date.: 26.09.1995         :.:\n               :..:..   Location.....: HnX/Germany        :\n                   _:   Author.......: iCARUS/diViDE!     : /\n                   \/                                     ://\n                    :.....................................://\n                                                         //\n\n\n\n                      1. What is CrunchFileProtector ?\n                      2. How to use it / to protect a file.\n\n                         a) "Load an executable file..."\n                         b) "Save a protected file..."\n                         c) "Protect an executable file..."\n                         d) "Clear buffer"\n                         e) "Buffer info"\n                         f) "About"\n                         g) "Quit"\n\n                      3. Error-Messages.\n                      4. About`n''Greetz.\n\n\n\n                  ...........................................\n                 _:                                         :\n                 \/    1. What is CrunchfileProtector ?     :...\n                  :    ­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­     :..:\n                  :.........................................:\n\nAs the name says, you can protect crunched files against recrunching\nand as a result against illegal accessing.\n\nI coded it because I saw many faked programms where a LAMER simply\nincreased the version-number in the executable file...\nI don`t worried about this Lamers... But: one day one of my programms\nappeared which was totally faked and EVEN MY NAME AS THE AUTHOR was\nreplaced by this sucker. That`s enough, don`t you think ???\n\nBefore I uploaded this file, I packed it with Imploder 4.0 so nobody\ncan see any strings or texts in the file. But the Lamer recrunched it\nwith a simple recrunch-tool (XPK or like.) and changed all with a HEX-\neditor.\n\n                    >>> BUT NOW CFP IS BORN !!! <<<\n\nAll you have to do is to crunch your files with any supported packer\n(Version 1.35 recognizes Imploder 4.0 and PowerPacker 4.0) and to\nprocess with it like descripted in Point 2. (see below.)\n\nAfter protecting a file, no one will be able to recrunch it and no\nrecruncher will be able to recognize this file as crunched, isn`t\nthat fine ??\n\nAnd your Greetz, Version-Strings, Names... are unreadable "crunched"\nin the file !!\n\n                     >>> GIVE FAKERS NO CHANCE <<<\n\n                                                  ... iCARUS/diViDE!\n\n\n\n\n                .............................................\n               _:                                           :\n               \/    2. How to use it / to protect a file   :...\n                :    ­­­­­­­--­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­--   :..:\n                :...........................................:\n\nIt`s very easy to protect a file.\nImagine you want to protect the file "dh0:work/testfile" which is Im-\nploder packed!\n\n- Click on the "Load an executable file..."-Gadget and select the file\n  you want (dh0:work/testfile).\n\n- If all went ok click on the "Protect anexecutable  file..."-Gadget.\n  -> A little status window appeares. Close it after all went ok by\n     pressing the left mousebutton.\n\n- Now click on the "Save a protected file..."-Gadget and enter your\n  savefilename (e.g. dh0:work/testfile2).\n\n\n\n\n                       .............................\n                      _:                           :\n                      \/    Gadget descriptions:   :...\n                       :    ­--­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­   :..:\n                       :...........................:\n\n    a) "Load an executable file..."\n       - If you click on this gadget a filerequest appeares.\n         Select the file you want to load and to protect later.\n\n    b) "Save a protected file..."\n       - You can the protected file from the buffer.\n\n    c) "Protect an executable file..."\n       - Use this button to protect a file which was loaded with\n         the help of gadget "a)" (see above.)\n\n    d) "Clear buffer"\n       - If you want to protect different files you must click on\n         gadget before you cna load a new file.\n\n    e) "Buffer info"\n       - If you click on this gadget a little status-window\n         appeares containing the following information:\n\n          >> FILENAME       : [- The filename.                        -]\n          >> Lenght(Before) : [- actuall filelenght.                  -]\n          >> Lenght(After!) : [- Filelenght after protection.         -]\n          >> MemoryAddress  : [- Startaddress of AllocMEM().          -]\n          >> Cruncher Used  : [- Which cruncher was used on this file -]\n\n    f) "About"\n       - Guess what.\n\n    g) "Quit"\n       - Guess again.\n\n\n\n\n\n                        ............................\n                       _:                          :\n                       \/    3. ERROR-Messages     :...\n                        :    ­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­     :..:\n                        :..........................:\n\n> "ERROR: File already protected..."\n  - You have already clicked on the "Protect an executable file..."\n    gadget. (see above c)\n\n> "ERROR: Please protect this file first..."\n  - You forgot to click on gadget c) before gadget b).\n\n> "ERROR: Could not open file..."\n  - There are different reasons: e.g. other programm uses this files\n\n> "ERROR: This file is not executable"\n  - YEP! Guess what: only executable files are supported...\n\n> "ERROR: Could not allocate enough memory for this file..."\n  - There are 2 solutions: 1. Buy more memory; 2. Close other windows...\n\n> "ERROR: No file loaded..."\n  - Hmmm.... load a file with gadget a) (see above).\n\n> "ERROR: Please clear the buffer first..."\n  - If you want to load a new file you must clear the buffer by clicking\n    on the gadget d) (see above)\n\n> "ERROR: Not crunched with one of the supported packers..."\n  - This is the worst message the programm shows you. This means the\n    selected file could not be processed because it isn`t crucnhed with\n    one of the supported packers... SORRY! (maybe next version this\n    cruncher will be recognized, too.)\n    To see which crunchers are supported click on gadget f)! (see above)\n\n\n\n\n                        ............................\n                       _:                          :\n                       \/    4.About`n''Greetz!     :...\n                        :    ­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­­     :..:\n                        :..........................:\n\nCoded by iCARUS/diVIDE in 1995 !!\nFirst some greetz to wENDiGO/mOTiON for helping me ...\n\nMy personal greetz are going to:\n--------------------------------\nwENDiGO/mOTiON (heheh.. thx!)\nthoraX (Du geiler PC-Co! 8)\neViL cHUCK ( 8) hey iNGO! los, geh in die Schule! hehehe....)\ntiGER 1 ( wann kommt endlich wipEout für die Playstation ???)\ncreep (see you at cc!)\nzombie of (p)ower (c)racking (b)oys (yEP! c64 ruled immernoch!)\ngarbor (weiss Dein Handle nicht einmal mehr !! c64 ruleZ!!!)\ngirelli (heheheh ...)\nnrg (hy Du alter tan^cru und dividler !!!)\ndope (heheheh...)\ngarfield (hy garfield, wo hat Dein vater nur die ganzen Hüte her??)\nflake/trsi (thx for keeping me up-to-date.)\ntanith/hectic (jaaaa: ich hab an Dich gedacht!!!!)\n\n              ALLE DIVIDE MEMBER SIND HIERMIT GEGRÜßT !!!!\n                     KEEP UP THE GOOD WORK!!\n\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',35252,4,0,NULL,NULL,'dvd_cfp1','scan',1780048498);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('dvd_fx17','DVD!FX17.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/DVD!FX17.LHA','aggrep','XIM','Barfly2Msg',NULL,NULL,'DVD','_____________|  _ /_____________ \____.\___   |  //__ ___.\  ___/ .:: |  | \\  |    ./   |  | \\  /\  ::.',replace('                 _____\n   _____________|  _ /_____________\n   \____.\___   |  //__ ___.\  ___/\n.:: |  | \\  |    ./   |  | \\  /\  ::.\n::  |_____/__|\___/ ___|_____/____\ic::\n::            .presents.             ::\n::       >> FREXX-ED V1.7! <<        ::\n::                                   ::\n::  Powerful editor like CygnusED!!  ::\n::      Support for C-Coders...      ::\n:: >> CRACKED! NO KEYFILE NEEDED  << ::\n`:::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::::''\n','\n',char(10)),'FPLuser.guide',replace('@database FPL\012@master FPL:docs/FPLuser.guide\012@$VER: 11.6\012@author "Daniel Stenberg"\012@(c) "1993-1995 by FrexxWare"\012@index HeddleyAutoIndex\012@width 78\012@remark Created with Heddley v1.20ß © Edd Dumbill 1994-5\012\012@node "Main" "FPL Users Documentation"\012 FPL is Copyright © 1992-1995 by FrexxWare. Permission is granted to freely\012distribute this program for non-commercial purposes only. FPL is distributed\012"as is" without warranty of any kind.\012\012 This documents the FPL language as it runs from version 11. If you happen to\012use any lower version, upgrade!\012\012-------------------------------------------------------------------------\012\012  FPL is very similar to C. If you know C, then FPL is very fast to learn!\012\012        Parts of the language:\012\012    @{" General           " link "General" 0}\012    @{" Expressions       " link "Expressions" 0}\012    @{" Functions         " link "Functions" 0}\012    @{" Keywords          " link "Keywords" 0}\012    @{" Line control      " link "LineControl" 0}\012    @{" Statements        " link "Statements" 0}\012    @{" Strings           " link "String" 0}\012    @{" Variables         " link "Variables" 0}\012    @{" Pragmas           " link "Pragmas" 0} (New from version 8)\012    \012    @{" Funclibs          " link "funclibs" 0} [Amiga only]\012\012        Subsequent information:\012\012    @{" About this manual " link "AboutManual" 0}\012    @{" Error messages    " link "Errors" 0}\012    @{" Examples          " link "Examples" 0}\012    @{" Hints and tricks  " link "Hints" 0}\012    @{" How to reach us   " link "Contact" 0}\012\012-------------------------------------------------------------------------\012\012For more information about the FPL distribution: @{" General information " link "FPLdoc.guide/Main" 0} \012\012And for programmers that want to implement FPL library support:\012@{" FPL implementation  " link "FPLlib.guide/Main" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "AboutManual" "About this manual"\012 This is the FPL LANGUAGE documentation. This deals with about everything that\012can be said about FPL from the user''s view. For information about how to\012implement fpl.library in your own code, see the FPLlib.guide and fpl.doc\012files.\012\012 Many examples provided are from the FrexxEd environment, meaning that several\012of the used functions in the examples are FrexxEd specific and not a part of\012FPL. (FrexxEd is Copyright © 1992-1994 by FrexxWare.)\012\012 There should be sufficient information in this manual to allow every user to\012fully understand and use FPL. If it should prove unsufficent, report it!\012\012 TECH Notes exist in some parts of the manual, explaining why I solved things\012the way I did and sometimes also how.\012\012 This documentation is written during a long time and during a lot of FPL\012updates. There might still be some old version thinking in this manual, but I\012hope it won''t destroy your ability to create something good with FPL.\012\012 This manual does only handle the language FPL. When FPL is used in real life,\012it will always be used in a software which is host to FPL. That software will\012affect FPL a lot and you should read the FPL chapters of that software''s\012documentation closely!\012@endnode\012\012@node "Contact" "How to reach me"\012 For private matters/discussions/questions/ideas, drop me a email/netmail at\012our own BBS: The Holy Grail (+46-(0)8-6121258, FidoNet 2:201/328, running\01228800 bps V34), or reach me at email: [email removed]!\012\012 Problems concerning FPL, the library, bug reports, other FrexxWare products\012or stuff like that, are dedicated to public message areas  making it possible\012for everyone to share, learn and participate.\012\012 If you have any ideas about things you''d like FPL to support, handle or run,\012don''t hesitate to contact me and share your visions. FPL is continuously\012developing and I need feedback to know in which directions you want it to go.\012\012 Swedish users of FPL can take advantage of the FidoNet echo mail area named\012R20_FPL. Available on backbone.\012\012 If there is enough interest shown, I will consider arranging a standard\012internet mailing list for FPL discussions.\012\012 All forthcoming updates and releases will be uploaded as fast as possible to\012the public ftp site known as AmiNet. (Thanks to Mattias Axelsson for his\012offering to do this on a regular basis!)\012\012 I''m very often on IRC, using the nick name ''Bagder'', meet me there!\012\012 Source code is very much available and if you didn''t get it in the package,\012get in touch!\012\012  Snail mail address:\012\012        Daniel Stenberg\012        Ankdammsgatan 36, 4tr\012        S-17143 SOLNA\012        Sweden\012@endnode\012\012@node "General" "General"\012* Every execution begins at the top of the program and interprets from\012  left to right, downwards.\012\012* All statements must be separated with a semicolon (;).\012\012* FPL is case sensitive. That makes the following two variable names\012  different:\012\012    hello  Hello\012\012* There is no maximum length of a script line. In fact, the entire\012  program is indeed as good in one single line as in several lines.\012\012* There is no line orientation in the language at all. Programs can be\012  written in almost any way provided that you follow the syntax rules\012  of the @{" keywords " link "Keywords" 0} and @{" functions " link "Functions" 0}. Whitespaces, comments and new lines\012  can be inserted anywhere to make the code more appealing to you.\012\012* Comments are allowed everywhere and are written exactly as in C and\012  Arexx as well as in C++; starting with a "/*" and ending with a "*/"\012  with no nesting possibilities(1):\012\012    /* This is a comment */\012\012  or starting with a "//" symbol and ending with a new line. Ex:\012\012    // This is also a comment\012\012  (Kjell, I hope you''re really happy with this feature!)\012\012  (1) - Since version 7, FPL can be made to accept nested comments.\012\012* Continuation of string lines is written with a backslash (\)\012  character followed by a newline. Ordinary statements don''t need\012  any continuation character at all. Just as in C... ;-) Ex:\012\012  a=b                   /* This is a fully working statement */\012   >\012   c;\012\012  b="hello\\012  world";               /* Continues a string assign.*/\012\012  output                /* Split the name and the arguments... */\012  ("hi");\012\012\012  /* The code below works just fine, but writing much of this */\012  /* kind might make your code rather confusing! */\012  output(\\012  "h\\012  ello\\012   wor\\012  ld"\\012  );\012\012  (In ''C'' you can continue *EVERYTHING* by simply writing a backslash\012  and continue on the next line (thanks to the preprocessor that merges\012  such lines into one for the compiler). FPL cannot perform the same\012  if you''re not using FPL together with a preprocessor that replaces\012  such sequences (such as FPP, the FrexxWare Pre Processor).)\012\012* All identifier names (labels/functions/variables) is limited to no\012  more than 64 significant characters. More characters can be used, but\012  identifiers with the same 64 first characters are considered\012  identical.\012\012* Identifiers can consist of both letters, numbers and underscores\012  ("_") but must not begin with a number. Letters are the 26 characters\012  from a to z and the 26 characters from A to Z.\012\012* There is no kind of unconditional jump or goto in FPL. In languages\012  like this (with different local levels), goto is most frequently\012  used in the wrong way and the use of it should anyhow have been very\012  limited (just look at the goto function in C). FPL doesn''t need any\012  goto/jump keyword.\012\012* Do not, I repeat, do not rely on undocumented features in the\012  language. FPL is constantly being changed and new error checks might\012  be implemented in the next release. The strange feature you may find\012  working in this version, might be a sever error in the next.\012  Code as stated in the manual and your FPL code will have a much\012  bigger change to stay accurate even when the version number ascends.\012@endnode\012\012@node "funclibs" "Funclibs"\012   Overview\012   ~~~~~~~~\012 To enable third party programs to add functions to already running FPL\012sessions, the ''funclib'' concept was invented. The inspiration source when\012designing the interface was to make it work like when using shared libraries\012(known as dynamic linking in some systems).\012\012 Using this technique, all FPL programmers can take advantage of functions\012that is placed in funclibs. By simply opening the funclib all its functions\012will exist and can get called. Any FPL program can open any funclib.\012\012 Funclibs could contain functions for bringing up requesters easier, for\012compression procedures, for serial port communication or for file handling.\012The limits are set by the funclib programmer, not by anyone else!\012\012    How to?\012    ~~~~~~~\012 By opening the desired funclib with the @{" openlib " link "openlib" 0} function, all function will\012be there. There''s nothing more to it than that!\012  After you''ve called the functions you wanted the funclib for, you simply\012call @{" closelib " link "closelib" 0} which concludes access to that funclib.\012\012 In some occasions, the host program might have already opened a funclib for\012you, and then you won''t need to open it and you shouldn''t close it.\012@endnode\012\012@node "LineControl" "Line Control"\012 I encourage the use of preprocessors together with FPL. FPL interprets the\012ANSI C standard "#line" instruction as a line number/file name changer.\012\012    #line\012    =====\012\012 A line control directive that supplies line numbers for FPL messages. It\012causes the next source line to be treated as having the specified number.\012\012 Syntax\012\012# line { decimal_constant [ "file_name" ] }\012\012 In order for FPL to produce meaningful references to line numbers in\012preprocessed source, the preprocessor inserts #line directives where necessary\012(for example, at the beginning at after the end of included text).\012\012 A file name specification enclosed in double quotation marks can follow the\012line number. If a file name is specified, FPL views the next line as part of\012the specified file. If a file name is not specified, FPL views the next line\012as part of the current source file.\012\012 Note that the keyword ''line'' is optional.  The directive\012\012        # line 300\012\012 is equivalent to\012\012        # 300\012\012 Example:\012\012 On Amiga, an FPL source could be preprocessed using SAS/C 6.x by entering the\012following line:\012\012  "sc PPONLY KEEPLINES <FPL program>"\012\012 (note that if ''KEEPLINES'' isn''t specified, SAS/C won''t output any #line\012  instructions!)\012@endnode\012\012@node "Pragmas" "Pragmas in FPL"\012Pragmas are specified by entering\012\012        #pragma <keyword>\012\012first in a line, and where the <keyword> is a compiler specific instruction.\012FPL supports the following pragmas:\012\012(NOTE: do only use the pragmas if you are very certain of what they are doing,\012they alter settings done by the FPL implementor.)\012\012 Keyword\012 -------\012 cache          Make FPL keep this file in memory after execution for faster\012                access (it may very well do it anyway, depending on the FPL\012                setup).\012\012 nocache        Opposite to ''cache''. Make FPL to *not* keep this file in\012                memory after usage, even if symbols were exported. The file\012                will be loaded into memory each time it is to be used, and is\012                flushed from memory when not in use.\012\012 reread         When executing cached files, the original file might be\012                changed. This pragma will force this file to be reread if\012                it is changed on disk while another version is cached and\012                then executed.\012\012 noreread       The opposite to ''reread''. Force FPL to ignore if the file has\012                been changed on disk or not.\012@endnode\012\012@node "Variables" "Variables"\012* There exist a few kind of variables in FPL:\012\012  integer       - holds a 32-bit signed numerical value.\012                  (maximum: 2147483647, minimum: -2147483648)\012                  Declarator: `int'' or `long''\012\012  short         - holds a 16-bit signed numerical value.\012                  (maximum: 32767, minimum: -32768)\012                  Declarator: `short''\012\012  char          - holds an 8-bit signed numerical value.\012                  (maximum: 127, minimum: -128)\012                  Declarator: `char''\012\012  string        - holds a contiguous sequence of characters.\012                  No length limit.\012                  Declarator: `string''. \012\012  Declare a varaible by using the declarator followed by the symbol name and\012  an optional initial assign. Multiple declarations can be done by comma\012  separating them.\012  Example:\012\012  int a;\012  string b;\012  int a=2;\012  string foobar="ninja", foo, bar;\012\012  Variables not assigned when declared, equals zero or zero length strings.\012\012* FPL includes NO floating point variables at all. The secret of still\012  making proper calculations is the @{" remainder operator " link "Remainder" 0}.\012  (Floating point numbers/expressions is expected to appear in a future FPL\012  version.)\012\012* Variables can be declared to exist in a few different ways:\012\012  `static''      - makes the variable to be remembered until the next time\012this\012                  function is invoked.\012  `const''       - constant variable. After the initialization assign, this\012                  cannot be changed.\012  `auto'' and\012  `register''    - make a varible declared as a global to exist as a non-\012                  global.\012  `volatile''    - volatile variables exist in C. They are written back to\012                  memory each time they are changed. FPL variables are always\012                  written back when changed. Implemented to make C ports\012                  easier.\012  `unsigned'' and\012  `signed''      - not currently implemented. These words are simply read and\012                  ignored.\012\012  These keywords can be used together with a declarator keyword in any\012  combination. Examples:\012\012  static int foobar;\012  int auto register foobar;\012  string static foobar;\012\012* Create declarator aliases by using the keyword `typedef''. Usage:\012  "typedef <declarator> <symbol>;". After such a typedef, the alternative\012  declarator can be used whereever the original declarator can be used.\012\012* Scope of FPL Identifiers\012\012  The region where an identifier is visible in a program is referred to\012  as the scope of the identifier.\012\012  The scope of an identifier is determined by where the identifier is\012  declared.\012\012  Block scope\012  -----------\012    The identifier''s declaration is located inside a statement\012  block.  An identifier with block scope is visible from the point where\012  it is declared to the closing brace (}) that ends the block.\012\012  You can nest block visibility. A block nested inside a block can\012  contain declarations that redeclare identifiers declared in the outer\012  block. The new declaration of the identifier applies to the inner\012  block. The original declaration is restored when program control\012  returns to the outer block. An identifier from the outer block is\012  visible inside inner blocks that do not redefine the variable.\012\012  File scope\012  ----------\012    The identifier''s declaration appears outside of any block, before the\012  start of the program.\012  An identifier with file scope is visible from the point where it is\012  declared to the end of the source file.\012\012  Example:\012\012    void cool(int);\012    void stupid(int);\012\012    int a=-50;\012    int b=1000;\012    cool(a):\012    exit;\012\012    void cool(int a)\012    {\012      stupid(a);\012    }\012\012    void stupid(int a)\012    {\012      {\012        int b=0;\012      }\012      output(b/a);\012    }\012\012* All FPL variables must be declared before use and they must be\012  declared first in the block. (Blocks are always started with an\012  an open brace and ended with return() or a close brace.)\012  (NOTE: The technique is like the one used in common C)\012  Ex:\012\012    int a;\012    for(a=0; a<10; a++) {\012      int b=0;\012      output(b);\012    }\012\012  is correct, but NOT this:\012\012    char a;\012    output(a);\012    short b=5;  /* the line above broke the chain of declarations */\012\012* Variables declared within loop braces will be declared and\012  assigned every loop, this is anyhow not good for execution speed! Ex:\012\012    int a;\012    for(a=0; a<6; a++) {\012      int b=a*2;\012      output(b, ", ");\012    }\012\012  This example will output() "0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 " !\012\012* Multi dimensional variable arrays are supported. To create e.g twenty\012  integers:\012\012    int hello[20];\012\012    (These will be accessible by the names hello[n], where n is a number\012    from 0 to 19.)\012\012  or\012\012    int hello[4][5];\012\012    (These will be accessible by the names hello[n][m], where n is a number\012    from 0 to 3, and m is a number from 0 to 4.)\012\012* Assign arrays using the {} operators. This example assigns var[3] to\012  100 and var[4] to 200:\012\012    var[3]={100, 200};\012\012  When declaring variables, the array assign always begins with the\012  first member of the array, this line assigns the four members a[0] to\012  a[3]:\012\012    string a[4]={"hi", "how", "are", "you?"};\012\012  The value of an array assign expression is always the last member of\012  the assign list. The following example will output the word "world"\012  on the screen:\012\012    output(a[0]={"hello", "world"});\012\012  Compound assigns on arrays are also valid! The following line adds 2,\012  3 and 4 to the variables length[3], length[4] and length[5]:\012\012    length[3]+={2, 3, 4};\012\012  The value of this expression will be the last addition (length[5]+4).\012\012  The string append operator is also array friendly. The following\012  example add strings to the strings names[1] and names[2].(The\012  returning string will be names[2]+=" (stupid)".):\012\012    names[1]+={" (ill)", " (stupid)"};\012\012  Of course, this works with array using more than one dimension too:\012\012    int foo[2][3]={\012      {0, 1, 2},\012      {2, 3, 4}\012    };\012\012  On the fly, things like this can (of course) be used:\012\012    foo[1]^={{2}, {3, 4}};\012\012* Attempts to create a variable with a name that is already being used\012  by another variable in the same local level will result in an error.\012\012* FPL supports variable referencing, more familiar to C programmers as\012  pointers to variables. Currently, though, you cannot declare or assign\012  them in any other way than through the parameters of a function call:\012\012    int foobar(int *barfoo) /* this function receives an integer reference */\012    {\012      *barfoo = 5; /* assign the variable ''barfoo'' is referencing! */\012      barfoo = 5;  /* ILLEGAL /\012    }\012    int ninja;\012    foobar(&ninja); /* make foobar() assign the ''ninja'' variable!\012\012  The same procedure is indeed possible to perform when referencing strings.\012@endnode\012\012@node "Strings" "Strings"\012* Strings are always written enclosed within quotation marks (").\012\012* To read the ASCII value of a single character within a string, use\012  square brackets in the form: name[index] where name is the name of\012  the string variable (could of course also be an array member) and\012  index is the column you want to check out. Index below zero or above\012  the length of the source string will result in error. The following\012  example outputs all ASCII codes in a string:\012\012     string  str = "hello world";\012     int  ascii, n;\012     while ( ascii = str[n++] ) {\012       output ( ascii "n" );\012    }\012\012  This example will output all ASCII values in column 2 in the strings\012  in the array:\012\012     string  str[3] = {"hello", "world", "string"};\012     int  n;\012     for ( n = 0; n < 3; n++ ) {\012       output ( str[n][2] "n" )\012    }\012\012  Reading beyond the end of a string simply returns a zero, while\012  reading a negative column will result in an error.\012\012* Strings not enclosed within quotes are assumed to be variable names.\012\012  Note the difference between:\012\012    output("Hello");\012\012  and\012\012    output(Hello);\012\012  (Example 1 outputs the actual string "Hello", while example 2 outputs\012  the contents of the variable *named* "Hello".)\012\012* Append strings to a string variable by using the ''+='' operator. Ex:\012\012    string a="Hello ";\012    a+="world";\012    output(a);\012\012  Outputs the string "Hello world" on the screen.\012\012    string a = "Hello ";\012    string b ="world";\012    output( a + b );\012\012* Special characters within strings should be symbolized with backslash\012  and a character just as in the good old C standard:\012  a    - Alert (bell)\012  b    - Backspace\012  f    - Form feed (new page)\012  n    - New-line\012  r    - Carriage return\012  t    - Horizontal tab\012  v    - Vertical tab\012  ''    - Single quotation mark\012  "    - Double quotation mark\012  ?    - Question mark\012  \    - Backslash\012  xhh  - hex number, where "hh" is a two digit hexadecimal value.\012  nnn  - octal number, where "nnn" is a three digit octal value.\012\012See also the @{"function reference" link "FunctionsR" 0} for string handling functions such as\012 @{"strcmp()" link "strcmp" 0}, @{"strncmp()" link "strncmp" 0}, @{"substr()" link "substr" 0}, @{"eval()" link "eval" 0} and more.\012@endnode\012\012@node "Functions" "Functions"\012If you are not aquinted to the use/calling of functions from FPL, read the\012@{" general function usage " link "generaluse" 0} paragraph.\012\012In a standard FPL environment there is a lot of functions that the FPL\012programs can use. They are added to FPL in three different ways:\012\012* The host program of FPL adds a lot of functions so that the programs really\012  can do anything fun with the software. Such functions are called "external"\012  functions and they can''t be described in this document but will be found in\012  the host programs documentation.\012\012* @{" Inside functions " link "declarefunctions" 0} that are declared and defined in an\012  FPL program. Any FPL program can create a function that can be called from\012  another function and that is treated and looks just like all the other\012  functions.\012\012* The @{" Internal functions " link "FunctionsR" 0} are supplied by FPL itself. Such functions\012  will always be there, no matter what the name or the purpose of the host\012  program is.\012@endnode\012\012@node "declarefunctions" "Declare inside functions"\012 This chapter handles FPL function declarations and definitions. A function\012declararation declares the format and existence of a function prior to its\012use. A function definition defines a function.\012\012 A function in FPL must always be declared (prototyped) or defined before it\012is used. Otherwise it doesn''t know where to find the function and how to\012interpret its parameters.\012\012    Function Declaration (prototyping)\012    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\012 A function declaration establishes the name of the function, the type of\012result that the function returns and the types of the arguments expected by\012the function when you call it.\012\012If the function does not return a value, declare it as a function returning\012void.\012\012If the function is to be accessed from other program than this, it should be\012declared `export <the rest>''.\012\012Example:\012\012      int func (int, string);\012\012    Function Definition\012    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\012 A function definition specifies the name, formal parameters, and body of a\012function. You must also specify the function''s return type.\012\012A function definition contains the following:\012\012* A type specifier, which determines the type of value that\012  the function returns. A function returning no value should be declared\012  to return `void''. A function can have any type specifier.\012\012* A function identifier, which provides the function with a name.\012\012* A list of parameters that the function expects and their types.\012\012* A block statement, which contains data definitions and code.\012\012 A function can be called by itself or by any function that appears in the\012same file as the function definition. If a function has been declared\012`export''ed, the function also can be called by functions that appear in other\012files, otherwise it can only be directly invoked from within the same source\012file.\012\012 The function definition or a declaration for the function must appear before,\012and in the same file as, a call to the function.  All declarations for a given\012function must be compatible with the function definition. They must have the\012same return type and the same parameter types.\012\012 The following example is a complete definition of the function sum:\012\012      int sum(int x,int y)\012      {\012        return(x + y);\012      }\012\012 The function sum returns int, and receives two values declared as x and y.\012The function body contains a single statement that returns the sum of x and\012y.\012\012 To indicate that a function accepts no parameters, use the keyword void as\012the type specifier in place of the parameter.  For example:\012\012      stop(void) { }\012\012 In the following example, the function f() takes one integer parameter and\012returns no value, while g() expects no parameters and returns an integer.\012\012      void f(int); int g(void);\012\012    Function Body\012    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\012 The body of a function is a  block statement.  The following function has an\012empty body:\012\012     void stub1(void) { }\012\012The body of the following function contains a definition for the integer\012variable big_num and a call to the function  printf:\012\012      void largest(int num1, int num2) {\012         int big_num;\012      \012         if (num1 >= num2)\012           big_num = num1;\012         else\012           big_num = num2;\012      \012         printf("big_num = %d\n", big_num);\012         return 0;\012      }\012\012Block statements are described in @{" Statements " link "blockstatement" 0}.\012\012* Prototypes *MUST* be first in the program. Before any program starts and\012  outside all braces to be global. Prototyping within the braces of a\012function\012  makes the function only local accessible.\012@endnode\012\012@node "generaluse" "General function use"\012 Here follows the ground rules when using/calling a function in FPL programs.\012\012* A function call has three major parts:\012  1 - The function identifier. The name of the function.\012  2 - A list of parameters. Most functions demands some kind of input.\012  3 - A return code from the function call.\012\012  In the example:\012\012                        retval = foobar ( "hello", 100 ) ;\012\012  Reference letter:        A        B   C    D   E   F G H\012\012  A - The variable that received the return code of the function. It must be\012      be of the type that the function returns. If the function returns a\012      string, the variable that receives the return code must be a string.\012      A return code from a function can always be ignored. Often it is wise\012      to check for progress, but it is always the choice of the programmer.\012\012  B - The function name. We call the "foobar" function in this example.\012\012  C - Always write the parameter list within parentheses. They tell FPL that\012      this really is a function. Even functions without in any input\012      parameters must be called with parentheses (but then without any\012      parameters in between)!\012\012  D - This is the first parameter to the function. Appearantly this function\012      accepts a string type as first parameter. Any string variable or string\012      expression is then a valid parameter.\012\012  E - Separate all parameters with a comma ",".\012\012  F - This is a second parameter. Appearantly this function accepts an\012      integer type as second parameter. Any integer variable or numerical\012      expression is then a valid parameter.\012\012  G - Conclude the parameter list with a closing parenthesis. Obviously, this\012      function is happy with two parameters.\012\012  H - End of statement is as always indicated with a semicolon ";".\012\012\012* Internal and external functions might have some arguments optional and some\012  functions may even accept parameter lists (an optional amount of parameters\012  of a certain type). All functions must always at least be called with the\012  number of required arguments as declared. Refer to the software docs.\012\012\012* There are four kinds of arguments possible to pass to a function :\012\012  1. Strings - constant strings or expressions returning strings.\012\012  2. Numeric arguments - are likewise read as @{" numerical expressions " link "Expressions" 0}\012     including everything true expressions consist of. An integer argument\012     can be sent as any kind of "char", "int" or "short" and can be received\012     by any one of those. FPL is tolerant when speaking about the mix of\012     such.\012\012  3. String variable references - pointer to a @{" string variable " link "Variables" 0}.\012\012  4. Numeric variable references - pointer to an @{" integer variable " link "Variables" 0}.\012     Just as with integer arguments, the use of the integers can be any of\012     the three integer types "char", "short" and "int"/"long".\012\012\012* All arguments that should be sent to and received in the function must\012  be declared by comma separating "int", "string", "int *" or "string *".\012  (Using "char", "short" or "long" is of course working too.)\012\012    "int"       - sends an integer result of an expression.\012    "string"    - sends a string result.\012    "int *"     - sends a named integer variable.\012    "string *"  - sends a named string variable.\012\012  Sending "int *" or "string *" makes the function able to change the\012  contents of the variables used in the calling function.\012\012  All declared arguments are required. Optionals are not possible to declare.\012\012  C programmers see the obvious inspiration programming language.\012@endnode\012\012@node "Constants" "Constants"\012  Sepcifying constants in FPL can be done in several ways.\012\012  Numeric expression constants can be written as:\012\012  WHAT                  HOW                             EXAMPLE\012  ------------------------------------------------------------------\012\012  * octal number        an octal number with a zero     012\012                        prefix\012\012  * binary number       a binary number with a "0b"     0b011010\012                        prefix\012\012  * hexadecimal number  a hexadecimal number with a     0xDEADBEEF\012                        "0x" prefix\012\012  * decimal number      a number                        129\012\012  * ASCII code          a character within apostrophes  ''a''\012\012\012  String constants can also be written as:\012\012  * octal numbers       "\nnn" where nnn is an 1-3      "\12"\012                        digit octal number\012\012  * hexadecimal numbers "\xhh" where hh is a two digit  "\xea"\012                        hexdecimal number\012\012  * a string            anything within quots           "k i ll^e(rn/injax"\012@endnode\012\012@node "blockstatement" "Blockstatement"\012  A block statement lets you to group any number of data definitions,\012declarations, and statements into one statement.  All definitions,\012declarations, and statements enclosed within a single set of braces are\012treated as a single statement.  You can place a block wherever a single\012statement is allowed.\012\012  All definitions and declarations occur at the beginning of a block before\012statements.  Statements must follow the definitions and declarations.  A block\012is treated as a single statement.\012\012  If you redefine a data object inside a nested block, the inner object hides\012the outer object while the inner block is processed.  Defining several\012variables that have the same identifier can make a program difficult to\012understand and maintain.  Therefore, you should limit such redefinitions of\012identifiers within nested blocks.\012\012  If a data object is usable within a block and its identifier is not\012redefined, all nested blocks can use that data object.\012\012  Initialization of an auto or register variable occurs each time the block is\012run from the beginning.  If you transfer control from one block to the middle\012of another block, initializations are not always performed.  You cannot\012initialize an extern variable within a block.\012\012  Examples\012\012The following example shows how the values of data objects change in nested\012blocks:\012\012  int main(void)\012  {\012     int x = 1;               /* Initialize x to 1 */\012     int y = 3;\012\012     if (y > 0)\012     {\012       int x = 2;           /* Initialize x to 2 */\012       output("second x = ", x, "\n");\012     }\012     output("first x = ", x, "\n");\012  }\012\012The example produces the following output:\012\012  second x = 2\012  first x = 1\012\012  Two variables named x are defined in main.  The definition of x on line 5\012retains storage throughout the execution of main.  However, because the\012definition of x on line 10 occurs within a nested block, line 11 recognizes x\012as the variable defined on line 10.  Line 13 is not part of the nested block.\012Thus, line 13 recognizes x as the variable defined on line 5.\012@endnode\012\012@node "Statements" "Statements"\012  These are the statements that FPL includes:\012\012        - @{" Block      " link "blockstatement" 0}\012        - @{" break      " link "break" 0}\012        - @{" continue   " link "continue" 0}\012        - @{" do         " link "do" 0}\012        - @{" Expression " link "ExpressionStatement" 0}\012        - @{" for        " link "for" 0}\012        - @{" if         " link "if" 0}\012        - @{" Null       " link "Nullstatement" 0}\012        - @{" return     " link "return" 0}\012        - @{" switch     " link "switch" 0} (New from version 7)\012        - @{" while      " link "while" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "Nullstatement" "Null statement"\012  The null statement performs no operation;\012\012SYNTAX\012        ;\012\012EXAMPLE\012        int i;\012        for(i=0; i<100; function(i++))\012          ; /* null statement */\012@endnode\012\012@node "ExpressionStatement" "Expression statement"\012  Expression statements performs some kind of evaluation of expression(s).\012\012* Statements must feature an action or cause an error.\012\012  int a;        /***************************************/\012  a++;          /* These are examples of statements    */\012  a=2;          /* that include some kind of "action". */\012  go(a);        /***************************************/\012\012  2+2;          /************************************************/\012  a;            /* These lines are NOT valid stand aloners      */\012  (c>22)*3;     /* since they don''t perform any kind of change. */\012  a-14*a;       /************************************************/\012\012\012  Since the release of version 10, FPL does now feature yet another ANSI C\012feature:\012\012  Expressions containing the operators &&, || or ?: can be only partly\012evaluated depending on the results of the other parts. Ex, in the expression\012"(a || b)" a is true, and then FPL won''t execute/evaluate b. Likewise "(a &&\012b)" where a is FALSE, will never reach b. Also "a=b?c:d" will reach c only if\012b is true, and d only if b is false!!\012@endnode\012\012@node "Keywords" "Keywords"\012Detailed information is obtained in the @{" keyword reference " link "KeywordsR" 0}.\012\012    Summary:\012    ========\012\012* Loop statements are done very similarly as in C, using the ''for'', ''while''\012  or ''do'' statements. They are used as in the C programming language:\012  (with exception for the wonderful "else" statement in "while"... )\012\012    @{" do " link "do" 0} { @{" statement " link "Statements" 0};}  while ( @{" expression " link "Expressions" 0} )\012\012    @{" while " link "while" 0} ( @{" expression " link "Expressions" 0} ) { @{" statement " link "Statements" 0}; } [ @{" else " link "else" 0} { @{" statement " link "Statements" 0}: } ]\012\012    @{" for " link "for" 0} (@{" expression1 " link "Expressions" 0}; @{" expression2 " link "Expressions" 0}; @{" expression3 " link "Expressions" 0}) { @{" statement " link "Statements" 0}; }\012\012  A feature (which I''ve always been missing when coding C) has been\012  added, and that''s `else'' after `while''! If the condition never goes\012  true, the ''else'' statement will be executed.\012\012  @{" continue " link "continue" 0} works 100% as in C.\012\012  @{" break " link "break" 0} is like break in C, but extended with an optional level\012  argument.\012\012* Take care of exceptions just as you do in C using statements like:\012\012  @{" if " link "if" 0} ( @{" expression " link "Expressions" 0} ) { @{" statement " link "Statements" 0}; } [ @{" else " link "else" 0} { @{" statement " link "Statements" 0}; } ]\012\012* Declare variables and functions by using the keywords:\012\012  @{" int " link "int" 0}, @{" char " link "char" 0}, @{" short " link "short" 0} and @{" long " link "int" 0} that all creates numerical variables.\012\012  @{" string " link "string" 0} declares a string variable.\012\012  @{" resize " link "resize" 0} changes the size of an already existing array.\012\012  @{" export " link "export" 0} tells FPL that the following global declaration\012  is to be exported. Exported global symbols are accessible in any file.\012\012  @{" More variable declaration details " link "Variables" 0}.\012\012* Exit programs or functions by using:\012\012  @{" exit " link "exit" 0} or @{" return " link "return" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "Hints" "Hints and tricks"\012  Since I''ve created FPL and know about its inner workings, I''ll try to sum a\012few words about what could be useful to think of when coding FPL.\012\012    General\012    =======\012\012 Parenthesized expressions will help you from making mistakes in expressions\012due to lack of operator precedence knowledge.\012\012 Do not declare unnecessary variables or variables you don''t have to use. Try\012to reuse already declared ones and stay away from the nasty variable\012declaration @{" keywords " link "Keywords" 0}!\012\012 Of course FPL is a perfect example of using a C programming preprocessor if\012you want preprocessor features like include files, macros and such goodies.\012FPL currently works perfect running preprocessed programs including the\012preprocessor instruction "#line".\012\012    Interpreting speed\012    ==================\012\012 FPL Performance philosophy is a structured programmer''s nightmare coming\012true. What is said to be ugly or even dirty coding in C, in fact often is to\012prefer in FPL since it''s interpreting one statement at a time.\012\012 Do as much as you can in as few @{" statements " link "Statements" 0} as possible is a golden rule.\012Use as few function calls as possible. Try to append everything possible to\012the function''s argument and call it only once.\012\012 Use @{" compound " link "CompoundAssign" 0} and nested @{" variable assigns " link "Assignment" 0}! FPL has no compiler, which\012makes statements like "a=a+2; b=b+a; function(a);" interpret MUCH slower than\012"function(b+=(a+=2))" even though it might look better and be more readable.\012(If it was hard to code it should be hard to read, right?)\012\012 Avoid long and complicated @{" string handlings " link "String" 0}. If you must use a string, try\012putting it in a variable and refer to that variable as much as possible.\012Especially loops will benefit a *LOT* in speed. Future version of FPL might\012deal with static strings (non-variable referenced) in a better way.\012\012    Bug hunting\012    ===========\012\012  Of course even FPL routines will be coded incorrect from time to time.\012\012 Comment your code. It''s not hard, decreases performance only slightly and\012makes it so very much easier to change the code. And who knows, perhaps\012someone else would want to change it in the future!\012\012 Wait for the soon appearing FPL debugger!\012@endnode\012\012@node "Expressions" "Numeric expressions (and operators)"\012  Numeric expressions are a basic part of FPL programs. Expressions are\012evaluated on the basis of the operators that the expressions contain and the\012contexts where the expressions can be used.\012\012    @{" Constants               " link "Constants" 0} Not changing values\012    \012    @{" Grouping and evaluating " link "Grouping" 0} Precedence, associativity, etc\012    \012    @{" Primary Expressions     " link "Primary" 0} Parentheses, function calls\012    @{" Unary Expressions       " link "Unary" 0} ++, --, +, -, !, ~\012    @{" Binary Expressions      " link "Binary" 0} (*, /, %, +, -, <<, >>, <, >, <=, >=, ==, etc)\012    @{" Assignment expressions  " link "Assignment" 0} (+=, -=, *=, /=, %=, etc)\012    @{" Conditional Expressions " link "Conditional" 0} (?, :)\012    @{" Comma expressions       " link "Comma" 0} (,)\012@endnode\012\012@node "Grouping" "Grouping and evaluating"\012 Two operator characteristics determine how operands group with operators:\012precedence and associativity. Precedence provides a priority system for\012grouping different types of operators with their operands. Associativity\012provides a left-to-right order for grouping operands to operators that have\012the same precedence.\012\012 You can explicitly state the grouping of operands with operators by using\012parentheses.\012\012 In the expression\012\012 a + b * c / d\012\012 the * and / operations are evaluated before + because of precedence. b is\012multiplied by c before it is divided by d because of associativity.\012\012 The following table lists the FPL language operators in their order of\012precedence. The operators are listed in order of precedence: @{" primary " link "Primary" 0}\012operators have the highest precedence, and the @{" comma operator " link "Comma" 0} has the lowest\012precedence. Operators that appear in the same group have the same precedence.\012\012 FPL''s operator precedence is 100% ANSI C compatible.\012\012    Operator Precedence and Associativity (decreasing order):\012    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\012\012    Operator Type           Associativity   Operators\012    -------------           -------------   ---------\012    @{" Primary              " link "Primary" 0} left to right   (), function calls\012    @{" Unary                " link "Unary" 0} right to left   ++ -- - + ! ~ []\012    @{" Multiplicative       " link "Multiplication" 0} left to right   * / %\012    @{" Additive             " link "Addition" 0} left to right   + -\012    @{" Bitwise Shift        " link "Shift" 0} left to right   << >>\012    @{" Relational           " link "Relational" 0} left to right   < > <= >=\012    @{" Equality             " link "Equality" 0} left to right   == !=\012    @{" Bitwise Logical AND  " link "Band" 0} left to right   &\012    @{" Bitwise Exclusive OR " link "Beor" 0} left to right   ^\012    @{" Bitwise Inclusive OR " link "Bor" 0} left to right   |\012    @{" Logical AND          " link "Land" 0} left to right   &&\012    @{" Logical OR           " link "Lor" 0} left to right   ||\012    @{" Conditional          " link "Conditional" 0} right to left   ? :\012    @{" Assignment           " link "Assignment" 0} right to left   = += -= *= /= <<= &= ^= |= >>=\012    @{" Comma                " link "Comma" 0} left to right   ,\012\012\012 The order of evaluation for the operands of the logical AND (&&) and the\012logical OR (||) operators is always left-to-right. If the operand on the left\012side of a && operator evaluates to 0 (zero), the operator on the right side is\012not evaluated. If the operand on the left side of a || operator evaluates to\012nonzero, the operator on the right side is not evaluated.\012\012\012    Examples\012\012The parentheses in the following expressions explicitly show how FPL groups\012operands and operators:\012\012    total = (4 + (5 * 3));\012    total = (((8 * 5) / 10) / 3);\012    total = (10 + (5/3));\012\012 FPL group operands with operators that are both associative and commutative\012in a simple left-to-right order. We group the operands and operators of the\012expression\012\012    total = price + prov_tax + city_tax;\012\012in the following way (as indicated by parentheses):\012\012    total = ((price + prov_tax) + city_tax);\012\012 TECH NOTE:\012 ~~~~~~~~~~\012 In C, the example above could have been evaluated the following ways too\012    total = ((price + city_tax) + prov_tax);\012    total = (price + (prov_tax + city_tax));\012\012 But that''s not too good if you want to use expressions like\012    a = b++ + (b>10?10:20);\012 since you wouldn''t know in which order the b variable is read and used... FPL\012reads and uses it in a left-to-right order.\012@endnode\012\012@node "Primary" "Primary expressions"\012 All primary operators have the same precedence and have left-to-right\012associativity. See @{" Grouping and Evaluating FPL Expressions " link "Grouping" 0}\012\012 For detailed information on primary operators, see\012\012    @{" Parenthesized Expressions " link "ParenExp" 0}\012    @{" Function calls " link "FuncCalls" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "ParenExp" "Parenthesized Expressions"\012 You can use parentheses to explicitly state how operands group with\012operators. The following expression does not contain any parentheses used for\012grouping operands and operators. The parentheses surrounding weight and\012zipcode form a function call. Notice how FPL groups the operands and operators\012in this expression:\012\012 Expression without Parentheses:\012\012       -discount * item + handling(weight, zipcode) > 10 * item\012\012 The following expression is similar, but contains parentheses that change how\012the operands and operators are grouped:\012\012 Expression with Parentheses:\012\012      (-discount * (item + handling(weight, zipcode))) > (10 * item)\012@endnode\012\012@node "FuncCalls" "Function calls"\012 A function call is a primary expression followed by a parenthesized argument\012list. The argument list can contain any number of expressions separated by\012commas, or it can be empty.\012\012 For example:\012\012      stub()\012\012      overdue(account, date, amount)\012\012      notify(name, (date+5))\012\012      report(error, time, date, (num++))\012\012 The arguments are evaluated, and each parameter is assigned the value of the\012corresponding argument. Assigning a value to a parameter changes the value\012with in the function, but has no effect on the argument.\012@endnode\012\012@node "Unary" "Unary Expression"\012\012 An unary expression contains one operand of scalar type and an unary\012operator. All unary operators have the same @{" precedence " link "Grouping" 0}. As indicated in the\012following descriptions, the usual arithmetic conversions are performed on the\012operands of most unary expressions.\012\012     @{" Bitwise Negation ~ " link "BNegation" 0}\012     @{" Decrement --       " link "Decrement" 0}\012     @{" Increment ++       " link "Increment" 0}\012     @{" Logical Negation ! " link "LNegation" 0}\012     @{" Unary Minus -      " link "UMinus" 0}\012     @{" Unary Plus +       " link "UPlus" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "Increment" "Increment ++"\012 The ++ (increment) operator adds 1 (one) to the value of the operand. The\012operand receives the result of the increment operation.\012\012 You can place the ++ before or after the operand. If the ++ appears before\012the operand, the operand is incremented; then the incremented value is used in\012the expression. If you place the ++ after the operand, the current value of\012the operand is used in the expression; then the operand is incremented. For\012example:\012\012      play = ++play1 + play2++;\012\012 is equivalent to the following sequence of expressions:\012\012      play1 = play1 + 1;\012      play = play1 + play2;\012      play2 = play2 + 1;\012\012 In the C language they say "avoid using a variable more than once in an\012expression where the variable is incremented". But that''s not necessary in\012FPL! I''ve created it to work as I wanted it, and so it does...\012\012      y = x(i) + i++;\012\012Does work exactly as you think; first calls the function x(), adds the value\012of i and finally increases i.\012@endnode\012\012@node "Decrement" "Decrement --"\012Acts as the @{" ++ operator " link "Increment" 0}, but instead of adding it''s subtracting!\012@endnode\012\012@node "UPlus" "Unary plus +"\012The + (unary plus) operator maintains the value of the operand.\012\012The result of applying the unary plus operator to a signed operand is\012equivalent to the promoted type of the operand.\012@endnode\012\012@node "UMinus" "Unary minus -"\012The - (unary minus) operator negates the value of the operand.\012\012The result of applying the unary minus operator to a signed operand is\012equivalent to the negative promoted type of the operand.\012\012For example, if quality has the value 100, then -quality has the value -100.\012@endnode\012\012@node "LNegation" "Logical negation !"\012The ! (logical negation) operator determines whether the operand evaluates to\0120 (false). If so, the operation yields the value 1 (true). If the expression\012evaluates to a nonzero value, the operation yields the value 0 (false).\012\012If right is not equal to 0, the following two expressions are equivalent:\012\012  !right;\012  right == 0;\012@endnode\012\012@node "BNegation" "Bitwise negation ~"\012The ~ (bitwise negation) operator yields the ones complement of the operand.\012In the binary representation of the result, every bit has the opposite value\012of the same bit in the operand. \012\012Suppose x represents the decimal value 5. The 32-bit binary representation of\012x is:\012\01200000000000000000000000000000101\012\012The expression ~x yields the following result, represented here as a 32-bit\012binary number:\012\01211111111111111111111111111111010\012\012The 32-bit binary representation of ~0 is:\012\01211111111111111111111111111111111\012@endnode\012\012@node "Binary" "Binary expressions"\012 A binary expression contains two operands separated by one operator.\012\012 Not all binary operators have the same @{" precedence " link "Grouping" 0}.\012\012 To ensure correct results, avoid creating expressions that depend on the\012order in which FPL evaluates the operands.\012\012    @{" Addition +             " link "Addition" 0}\012    @{" Bitwise AND &          " link "Band" 0}\012    @{" Bitwise Exclusive OR ^ " link "Beor" 0}\012    @{" Bitwise Inclusive OR | " link "Bor" 0}\012    @{" Bitwise Shift << >>    " link "Shift" 0}\012    @{" Division /             " link "Division" 0}\012    @{" Equality == !=         " link "Equality" 0}\012    @{" Logical AND &&         " link "Land" 0}\012    @{" Logical OR ||          " link "Lor" 0}\012    @{" Multiplication *       " link "Multiplication" 0}\012    @{" Relational < > <= >=   " link "Relational" 0}\012    @{" Remainder %            " link "Remainder" 0}\012    @{" Subtraction -          " link "Subtraction" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "Multiplication" "Multiplication *"\012The * (multiplication) operator yields the product of its operands.\012\012Because multiplication has both associative and commutative properties, the\012operands will be grouped in a left-to-right order. For example, the\012expression:\012\012  sites * number * cost\012\012is interpreted in  the following way:\012\012  (sites * number) * cost\012@endnode\012\012@node "Division" "Division /"\012The / (division) operator yields the quotient of its operands.\012\012If both operands are positive integers and the operation produces a remainder,\012FPL ignores the remainder. Thus, the expression 7 / 4 yields the value 1\012(rather than 1.75 or 2).\012\012The C language does not define how the compiler treats the quotient when\012either of the operands has a negative value.  Thus, -7 / 4 can yield either -1\012or -2. However, on all IBM C compilers, -7 / 4 always results in a quotient of\012-1 and a @{" remainder " link "Remainder" 0} of -3, and that''s the rule FPL has been following.\012\012It ends up in an error message if the second operand (the denominator)\012evaluates to 0 (zero).\012@endnode\012\012@node "Remainder" "Remainder %"\012The % (remainder) operator yields the remainder from the @{" division " link "Division" 0} of the\012left operand by the right operand. For example, the expression 5 % 3 yields\0122.\012\012If the right operand evaluates to 0 (zero), it results in an error message. If\012either operand has a negative value, the result is such that the following\012expression always yields the value of a if b is not 0 (zero):\012\012  ( a / b ) * b + a % b;\012@endnode\012\012@node "Addition" "Addition +"\012The + (addition) operator yields the sum of its operands.\012@endnode\012\012@node "Subtraction" "Subtraction -"\012The - (subtraction) operator yields the difference of its operands.\012@endnode\012\012@node "Shift" "Bitwise left and right shift << >>"\012The bitwise shift operators move the bit values of a binary object. The left\012operand specifies the value to be shifted. The right operand specifies the\012number of positions that the bits in the value are to be shifted.\012\012The << (bitwise left shift) operator indicates the bits are to be shifted to\012the left. The >> (bitwise right shift) operator indicates the bits are to be\012shifted to the right.\012\012The right operand should not have a negative value or a value that is greater\012than the width in bits of the expression being shifted. Bitwise shifts on such\012values give unpredictable results.\012\012If the right operand has the value 0 (zero), the result is the value of the\012left operand (after the usual arithmetic conversions).\012\012The << operator fills vacated bits with zeros. For example, if l_op has the\012value 4019, the bit pattern (in 32-bit format) of l_op is:\012\01200000000000000000000111110110011\012\012The expression l_op << 3 yields:\012\01200000000000000000111110110011000\012\012The result is the integral part of the quotient of the left operand divided by\012the quantity, 2 raised to the power of the right operand. If the left operand\012has a negative value, the vacated bits of a signed value are filled with a\012copy of the sign bit of the unshifted value. For example, if l_op has the\012value -25, the bit pattern (in 32-bit format) of l_op is:\012\01211111111111111111111111111100111\012\012Vacated bits are filled with ones, and the expression l_op >> 3 yields:\012\01211111111111111111111111111111100\012@endnode\012\012@node "Relational" "Relational < > <= >="\012The relational operators compare two operands for the validity of a\012relationship. If the relationship stated by the operator is true, the value of\012the result is 1 (one). Otherwise, the value of the result is 0 (zero).\012\012FPL has the following relational operators:\012\012Operator        Usage\012\012<       Indicates whether the value of the left operand is less than\012        the value of the right operand.\012\012>       Indicates whether the value of the left operand is greater than\012        the value of the right operand.\012\012<=      Indicates whether the value of the left operand is less than or\012        equal to the value of the right operand.\012\012>=      Indicates whether the value of the left operand is greater than\012        or equal to the value of the right operand.\012\012Relational operators have also left-to-right associativity. Therefore, the\012expression:\012\012  a < b <= c\012\012is interpreted as:\012\012  (a < b) <= c\012\012If the value of a is less than the value of b, the first relationship is true\012and yields the value 1 (one).  The value 1 (one) is then compared with the\012value of c.\012@endnode\012\012@node "Equality" "Equality == !="\012The equality operators, like the relational operators, compare two operands\012for the validity of a relationship. The equality operators, however, have a\012lower precedence than the relational operators. If the relationship stated by\012an equality operator is true, the value of the result is 1 (one). Otherwise,\012the value of the result is 0 (zero).\012\012FPL has the following equality operators:\012\012Operator        Usage\012\012==      Indicates whether the value of the left operand is equal to the\012        value of the right operand.\012\012!=      Indicates whether the value of the left operand is not equal to\012        the value of the right operand.\012\012The following expressions contain examples of equality and relational\012operators:\012\012  time < max_time == status < complete\012  hello != world\012@endnode\012\012@node "Band" "Bitwise AND &"\012The & (bitwise AND) operator compares each bit of its first operand to the\012corresponding bit of the second operand. If both bits are 1''s, the\012corresponding bit of the result is set to 1. Otherwise, it sets the\012corresponding result bit to 0.\012\012The following example shows the values of a, b, and the result of a & b\012represented as 32-bit binary numbers:\012\012bit pattern of a        00000000000000000000000001011100\012\012bit pattern of b        00000000000000000000000000101110\012\012bit pattern of a & b    00000000000000000000000000001100\012@endnode\012\012@node "Beor" "Bitwise Exclusive OR ^"\012The ^ (bitwise exclusive OR) operator compares each bit of its first operand\012to the corresponding bit of the second operand. If both bits are 1''s or both\012bits are 0''s, the corresponding bit of the result is set to 0. Otherwise, this\012operator sets the corresponding result bit to 1.\012\012The following example shows the values of a, b, and the result of a ^ b\012represented as 32-bit binary numbers:\012\012bit pattern of a        00000000000000000000000001011100\012\012bit pattern of b        00000000000000000000000000101110\012\012bit pattern of a^b      00000000000000000000000001110010\012@endnode\012\012@node "Bor" "Bitwise inclusive OR |"\012The | (bitwise inclusive OR) operator compares the values (in binary format)\012of each operand and yields a value whose bit pattern shows which bits in\012either of the operands has the value 1 (one). If both of the bits are 0\012(zero), the result of the comparison is 0 (zero); otherwise, the result is 1\012(one).\012\012The following example shows the values of a, b, and the result of a | b\012represented as 32-bit binary numbers:\012\012bit pattern of a        00000000000000000000000001011100\012\012bit pattern of b        00000000000000000000000000101110\012\012bit pattern of a | b    00000000000000000000000001111110\012@endnode\012\012@node "Land" "Logical AND &&"\012 The && (logical AND) operator indicates whether both operands have a nonzero\012value. If both operands have nonzero values, the result has the value 1 (one).\012Otherwise, the result has the value 0 (zero).\012\012 The following examples show how the language evaluates expressions that\012contain the logical AND operator:\012\012 Expression      Result\012 ~~~~~~~~~~      ~~~~~~\012 1 && 0          0\012\012 1 && 4          1\012\012 0 && 0          0\012\012\012 Expressions like "a && b" will only evaluate b if a is false!\012\012\012NOTE: The logical AND (&&) should not be confused with the bitwise AND (&)\012operator.  For example,\012\012  1 && 4 evaluates to 1\012\012while\012\012  1 & 4 evaluates to 0\012@endnode\012\012@node "Lor" "Logical OR ||"\012 The || (logical OR) operator indicates whether either operand has a nonzero\012value. If either operand has a nonzero value, the result has the value 1\012(one). Otherwise, the result has the value 0 (zero).\012\012 The following examples show how expressions that contain the logical OR\012operator are evaluated:\012\012 Expression      Result\012 ~~~~~~~~~~      ~~~~~~\012 1 || 0          1\012\012 1 || 4          1\012\012 0 || 0          0\012\012\012Expressions like "a || b" will only reach b if a is false.\012\012\012NOTE: The logical OR (||) should not be confused with the bitwise OR (|)\012operator. For example,\012\012  1 || 4 evaluates to 1\012\012while\012\012  1 | 4 evaluates to 5\012@endnode\012\012@node "Conditional" "Conditional expressions"\012\012 (operand1?operand2:operand3)\012\012 A conditional expression is a compound expression that contains a condition\012(operand1), an expression to be evaluated if the condition has a nonzero value\012(operand2), and an expression to be evaluated if the condition has the value 0\012(zero) (operand3).\012\012 The conditional expression contains one two-part operator. The ? symbol\012follows the condition, and the : symbol appears between the two action\012expressions. All expressions between the operators ? and : are treated as one\012expression.\012\012    Examples\012    ~~~~~~~~\012 The following expression determines which variable has the greater value, y\012or z, and assigns the greater value to the variable x:\012\012      x = (y > z) ? y : z;\012\012 The following is an equivalent expression:\012\012      if (y > z) {\012        x = y;\012      } else {\012        x = z;\012      }\012\012 The following expression assigns an integer. If the variable c is less than\012zero, output receives the value of c. If not, output receives the return code\012from the search function.\012\012      c = c<0?c:search("hello");\012\012 If the last operand of a conditional expression contains an assignment\012operator, use parentheses to ensure the expression evaluates properly. For\012example, the = operator has higher precedence than the ?: operator in the\012following expression:\012\012      (i == 7) ? j ++ : k = j;\012\012 This expression generates an error because it is interpreted as if it were\012parenthesized this way:\012\012      ((i == 7) ? j ++ : k) = j;\012\012 That is, k is treated as the third operand, not the entire assignment\012expression k = j.  The error arises because a conditional expression is not an\012lvalue, and the assignment is not valid.\012\012 To make the expression evaluate correctly, enclose the last operand in\012parentheses:\012\012      (i == 7) ? j ++ : (k = j);\012@endnode\012\012@node "Assignment" "Assignment expressions"\012 An assignment expression gives a value to the left operand.\012\012 The left operand in all assignment expressions must be a variable. The value\012of the expression is the value of the left operand after the assignment is\012completed.\012\012 The language contains two types of assignment operators, @{" simple assignment " link "SimpleAssign" 0}\012and @{" compound assignment " link "CompoundAssign" 0} operators.\012@endnode\012\012@node "SimpleAssign" "Simple assignment ="\012 The simple assignment operator gives the value of the right operand to the\012left operand.\012\012 The following example assigns in order the value 0 (zero) to strangeness, the\012value of strangeness to charm, the value of charm to beauty, and the value of\012beauty to truth:\012\012    truth = beauty = charm = strangeness = 0;\012@endnode\012\012@node "CompoundAssign" "Compound assignment"\012 The compound assignment operators perform an operation on both operands and\012give the result of that operation to the left operand.\012\012 The following table lists the compound assignment operators and shows an\012expression using each operator:\012\012 Operator        Example                 Equivalent Expression\012 --------        -------                 ---------------------\012 +=              index   +=  2           index   = index + 2\012 -=              balance -=  debit       balance = balance - debit\012 *=              bonus   *=  increase    bonus   = bonus * increase\012 /=              time    /=  hours       time    = time / hours\012 %=              allow   %=  1000        allow   = allow % 1000\012 <<=             result  <<= num         result  = result << num\012 >>=             form    >>= 1           form    = form >> 1\012 &=              mask    &=  2           mask    = mask &  2\012 ^=              test    ^= pre_test     test    = test ^ pre_test\012 |=              flag    |=  on          flag    = flag | on\012\012\012 Although the equivalent expression column shows the left operands (from the\012example column) evaluated twice, the left operand is evaluated only once.\012\012Note that the expression\012\012    a *= b + c\012\012is equivalent to\012\012    a = a * (b + c)\012\012and NOT\012\012    a = a * b + c.\012@endnode\012\012@node "Comma" "Comma expressions"\012 A comma expression contains two operands separated by a comma operator.\012Although both operands are evaluated, the value of the right operand is the\012value of the expression. The left operand is evaluated, possibly producing\012side effects, and then the value is discarded.\012\012 In the following example, if omega has the value 11, the expression\012increments delta and assigns the value 3 to alpha:\012\012      alpha = (delta++, omega % 4);\012\012 Any number of expressions separated by commas can form a single expression.\012The leftmost expression is evaluated first. The value of the rightmost\012expression becomes the value of the entire expression. For example, the value\012of the expression\012\012      intensity++, shade * increment, rotate(direction);\012\012is the value of the expression\012\012      rotate(direction);\012\012Restrictions\012\012 You can place comma expressions within lists that contain commas (for\012example, argument lists and initializer lists). However, because the comma has\012a special meaning, you must place parentheses around comma expressions in\012these lists. The comma expression t = 3, t + 2 is contained in the following\012function call:\012\012      f(a, (t = 3, t + 2), c);\012\012The arguments to the function f are: the value of a, the value 5, and the\012value of c.\012@endnode\012\012@node "KeywordsR" "Keywords"\012 FPL supplies a number of keywords that''ll help executing the program. (You\012might recognize several of them from the C programming language, but please\012read the descriptions carefully cause some of the keywords behave in a\012slightly different way in FPL.)\012\012 These keywords are e.g looping @{" functions " link "Functions" 0} and conditional checks etc. The\012"arguments" to these keywords are a bit special and tricky, they are therefore\012not included in this table. Refer to keyword reference for proper syntax.\012\012  Keyword        Short Description\012  --------       -----------\012  @{" auto     " link "Variables" 0}     No global variable.\012  @{" break    " link "break" 0}     Break out of a number of levels of loop braces.\012  @{" case     " link "case" 0}     Used in switch() statements for a specific case.\012  @{" char     " link "char" 0}     Create a 8-bit signed numerical variable.\012  @{" const    " link "Variables" 0}     Constant variable.\012  @{" continue " link "continue" 0}     Continue the loop.\012  @{" debug    " link "debug" 0}     Control debug mode.\012  @{" default  " link "default" 0}     Specifies default action in a switch() statement.\012  @{" do       " link "do" 0}     do {statement} while (expression);\012  @{" exit     " link "exit" 0}     Stop executing this FPL routine.\012  @{" for      " link "for" 0}     for(statement1;statement2;statement3)\012  @{" if       " link "if" 0}     if(condition) {statement} [else {statement}]\012  @{" int      " link "int" 0}     Creates an 32-bit integer variable.\012  @{" long     " link "int" 0}     Equivalent to `int''.\012  @{" register " link "Variables" 0}     No global variable.\012  @{" resize   " link "resize" 0}     Resize a variable array.\012  @{" return   " link "return" 0}     Return from subroutine.\012  @{" short    " link "short" 0}     Create a 16-bit signed numerical variable.\012  @{" static   " link "Variables" 0}     Remember local variables between invokes.\012  @{" string   " link "string" 0}     Creates a string variable named [name].\012  @{" switch   " link "switch" 0}     Do different actions on different results.\012  @{" typedef  " link "typedef" 0}     Make declarator aliases.\012  @{" volatile " link "Variables" 0}     FPL variables are always volatile.\012  @{" while    " link "while" 0}     while(condition) {statement} [else {statement}]\012\012These keywords are reserved for FPL use, but do nothing today:\012\012  ''double''     Not implemented.\012  ''enum''       Not implemented.\012  ''float''      Not implemented.\012  ''signed''     ALL variables are always signed.\012  ''struct''     Not implemented.\012  ''union''      Not implemented.\012  ''unsigned''   Not implemented.\012@endnode\012\012@node "break" "break"\012SYNTAX  break [expression];\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        A break statement enables you to end iterative (do, for, while,\012        switch statements and exit from them at any point other than the\012        logical end.\012\012        The break statement ends the loop or switch and moves control to the\012        next statement outside the loop/switch. Within nested statements,\012        break ends only the smallest enclosing do, for, while or switch\012        statement as default.\012\012        The possible "else" statement after a while, is *NOT* a loop.\012\012        Specifying a following expression breaks out of a number of iterative\012        statements.\012\012RESTRICTIONS\012        Place a break statement only in the body of an iterative statement.\012\012EXAMPLES\012\012        The following example shows a break statement in the action part of a\012        for statement. If the i*3 is equal to 9, the break statement causes\012        the for statement to end.\012\012          for (i = 0; i < 5; i++) {\012            if (i*3 == 9)\012              break;\012            length++;\012          }\012\012        Break out of a number of loops by giving an argument:\012\012          for(i = 0; i < 5 ; i++) {\012            for(j = 0; j < 5 ; j++) {\012              if(i*5+j>18) {\012                break 2; // Breaks out of two `for'' loops!\012              }\012            }\012          }\012\012        Break out of a switch() statement:\012\012          switch(i) {\012            case 2:\012              break;\012\012            default:\012          }\012\012SEE     @{" do " link "do" 0}, @{" for " link "for" 0}, @{" while " link "while" 0}, @{" switch " link "switch" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "case" "case"\012SYNTAX  case expression: statement;\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        The ''case'' statement can only be used within a switch statement. It\012        tells that the following statements should be run if the result of\012        the case expression is the result of the switch expression.\012\012        The expression must be followed by a colon '':''.\012\012        Break the case statement with a ''break''. Then the execution will\012        continue after the switch() statement.\012\012NOTE\012        If the expression contains a colon '':'', it must be with parentheses\012        or it can confuse the interpreter under certain conditions.\012\012EXAMPLE\012        The following program show a switch() statement with three cases:\012\012          switch( Character() ) {\012            case ''\t'':\012            case '' '':\012              a = 0;\012              break;\012\012            case ''\n'':\012              a = -1;\012              break;\012\012            default:\012              a = 1;\012              break;\012          }\012\012SEE ALSO\012        @{" switch " link "switch" 0}, @{" break " link "break" 0}, @{" default " link "default" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "char" "char"\012SYNTAX  char name [= expression];\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        Declares a char (signed 8-bit) variable (and assign a value to it).\012        Read more about it the chapter discussing variables. Not assigned\012        variables equals zero (0) after declaration.\012\012INPUTS  string name     - The variable name.\012        char expression - Initial expression.\012\012SEE     @{" int    " link "int" 0}\012        @{" short  " link "short" 0}\012        @{" string " link "string" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "continue" "continue"\012SYNTAX  continue;\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        A continue statement enables you to stop the current iteration of a\012        loop. Program control is passed from the location in the body of the\012        loop where the continue is found, to the condition part of the loop.\012\012        The continue statement ends the execution of the action part of a do,\012        for, or while statement and moves control to the condition part of\012        the statement. If the iterative statement is a for statement, control\012        moves to the third expression in the condition part of the statement,\012        then to the second expression (the test) in the condition part of the\012        statement.\012\012        Within nested statements, the continue statement ends only the\012        current iteration of the do, for or while statement immediately\012        enclosing it.\012\012RESTRICTIONS\012        Place a continue statement only within the body of an iterative\012        statement.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        The following example shows a continue statement in a for statement.\012        The continue statement causes the system to skip over those elements\012        of the formula that have values less than or equal to 100.\012\012          output("Try this calculation while <=100.\n");\012          for (i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {\012            if (i*(3-b/c+a) <= 100)\012              continue;\012            output("The number ", i, " makes it no longer below!");\012          }\012\012SEE     @{" do " link "do" 0}, @{" for " link "for" 0}, @{" while " link "while" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "default" "default"\012SYNTAX  default: statement;\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        The ''default'' statement can only be used within a switch statement.\012        It tells that the following statements is the default action if no\012        case expressions match the switch expression.\012\012        The keyword must be followed by a colon '':''.\012\012        Break the default statement with a ''break''. Then the execution will\012        continue after the switch() statement.\012\012EXAMPLE\012        This program executes the ''default'' statement if ''x'' does not match\012        "foo" or "bar":\012\012          switch(x) {\012            case "foo":         /* if x is "foo" */\012            case "bar":         /* if x is "bar" */\012              /* nananana */\012              break;            /* break out of switch */\012\012            default:\012              foobar();         /* if x isn''t "foo" or "bar" */\012              break;\012          }\012\012SEE ALSO\012        @{" switch " link "switch" 0}, @{" break " link "break" 0}, @{" case " link "case" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "do" "do"\012SYNTAX  do statement; while ( expression ) ;\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        A do statement repeatedly executes a statement until a test\012        expression evaluates to 0 (zero). Because of this order of\012        processing, the statement is processed at least once.\012\012        The body of the loop is run before the controlling while clause is\012        evaluated. Further processing of the do statement depends on the\012        value of the while clause. If the while clause does not evaluate to\012        0 (zero), the statement runs again. Otherwise, processing of the\012        statement ends.\012\012        A break or return statement can cause the processing of a do\012        statement to end, even when the while clause does not evaluate to 0\012        (zero).\012\012EXAMPLES\012        The following statement prompts the system user to enter a 1. If the\012        system user enters a 1, the statement ends execution.  If not, the\012        statement displays another prompt.\012\012          do {\012            input("Enter a 1!", reply);\012          } while (reply != 1);\012\012SEE     @{" break " link "break" 0}, @{" continue " link "continue" 0}, @{" return " link "return" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "debug" "debug    "\012SYNTAX  int debug ( Enable );                                   (V9)\012\012DESCRIPTION\012         Toggles the "debug mode" state. When FPL runs in the "debug mode",\012        the debugger is capable of tracing the FPL executions.\012\012         If called without parameter, it will return the current "debug mode"\012        status.\012\012NOTE\012        The FPL debugger is not extensively used, developed or spread. Much\012        more documentation regarding this function and FPL debugging concepts\012        and procedures will appear in future versions of this document.\012\012INPUTS\012        int Enable      - Zero to disable, non-zero to enable.\012\012RETURNS\012        It returns the "debug mode" state that was when the function was\012        called. That is, if you enable debug mode, it will return the state\012        previous to this function call.\012\012SEE\012@endnode\012\012@node "else" "else"\012SYNTAX  <special>\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        Used after condition checks to make FPL execute certain parts of the\012        program if the previous check did not evaluate true.\012\012        It always belongs to the last `if''/`while'' in this block.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        It can be used in simple one time checks like:\012\012          if(a!=2)\012            break;\012          else\012            output("hello");  /* only executed if variable a equals 2 */\012\012        and even if a while loop never is executed:\012\012          while(a--)\012            output(a);\012          else\012            output("hello"); // only executed if the while condition never\012                             // was true.\012\012        Take a look at the two examples below and note the difference!\012\012        1. If both "a" and "b" are true, then invoke "build_new_things". if\012           "a" is false, invoke "crash_mostofit":\012\012          if(a) { // braces are required if we want the `else'' to affect this\012            if(b) \012              build_new_things();\012          } else\012            crash_mostofit();\012\012        2. If "a" and "b" are true, then invoke "build_new_things". If "a" is\012           true and "b" is false, invoke "crash_mostofit":\012\012          if(a) // without braces, the else suddenly affects the other if!\012            if(b) \012              build_new_things();\012            else\012              crash_mostofit();\012@endnode\012\012@node "exit" "exit"\012SYNTAX  exit( return_code );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        Stop execution of the current FPL routine.\012\012INPUTS  int/string return_code  - Return code to return to the calling\012                                  process.\012\012        See the documentation for the software you''re controlling for the\012        exact meaning of these return codes. The return code can be excluded\012        as well as the parenthesis can when returning a value.\012\012RETURNS\012        A result code to the invoking environment.\012\012SEE ALSO\012        @{" return " link "return" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "export" "export"\012SYNTAX  export [ global declaration ];\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        Make the following global symbol available to all programs.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        The following statements make the function "foobar" and the string\012        variable "String" accessiable to all other FPL programs that will\012        succeed this program:\012\012          export string String;\012          export int foobar(int);\012          /* the actual function foobar must reside in the same source file */\012@endnode\012\012@node "for" "for"\012SYNTAX  for ([expression1] ; [expression2] ; [expression3]) statement;\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        Expression1\012          is evaluated only before the statement is processed for the first\012          time. You can use this expression to initialize a variable. If you\012          do not want to evaluate an expression prior to the first iteration\012          of the statement, you can omit this expression.\012\012        Expression2\012          is evaluated before each iteration of the statement. The expression\012          must evaluate to a scalar type. If it evaluates to 0 (zero), the\012          statement is not processed and control moves to the next statement\012          following the for statement. If expression2 does not evaluate to 0,\012          the statement is processed. If you omit expression2, it is as if\012          the expression had been replaced by a nonzero constant and the for\012          statement is not terminated by failure of this condition.\012\012        Expression3\012          is evaluated after each iteration of the statement. You can use\012          this expression to increase, decrease, or reinitialize a variable.\012          If you do not want to evaluate an expression after each iteration\012          of the statement, you can omit this expression.\012\012        A break or return statement can cause the processing of a for\012        statement to end, even when the second expression does not evaluate\012        to 0 (zero). If you omit expression2, you must use a break or a\012        return statement to stop the the for statement from running.\012\012EXAMPLES\012\012        The following for statement prints the value of count 20 times. The\012        for statement initially sets the value of count to 1. After each\012        iteration of the statement, count is incremented.\012\012          for (count = 1; count <= 20; count++) {\012            output("count =", count);\012          }\012\012        The following sequence of statements accomplishes the same task. Note\012        the use of the while statement instead of the for statement.\012\012          count = 1;\012          while (count <= 20) {\012            output("count = ", count);\012            count++;\012          }\012\012        The following for statement does not contain an initialization\012        expression.\012\012          for (; index > 10; index--) {\012            list= var1 + index;\012            output("list = ", list, "\n");\012          }\012\012        The following for statement continues running until input receives\012        the letter e:\012\012          for (;;) {\012            input("Gimme an e!", letter);\012            if (!strcmp(letter, "\n"))\012              continue;\012            else if (!strcmp(letter, "e"))\012              break;\012            output("You entered the letter", letter);\012          }\012\012        The following for statement contains multipart initializations and\012        increments.  The comma operator makes this construction possible.\012\012          for (i = 0, j = 50; i < 10; i++, j += 50) {\012            output("i = ", i, " and j = ", j);\012          }\012\012        The following example shows a nested for statement. The outer\012        statement is run as long as the value of row is less than 5. Each\012        time the outer for statement is processed, the inner for statement\012        sets the initial value of column to zero and the statement of the\012        inner for statement is run three times. The inner statement is run\012        as long as the value of column is less than 3.\012\012          for (row = 0; row < 5; row++) {\012            for (column = 0; column < 3; column++)\012              output("column * row =", row * column, "\n");\012\012        A never-ending loop using `for'':\012\012          for(;;)\012            perform_until_death();\012\012SEE     @{" break " link "break" 0}, @{"continue" link "continue" 0}, @{"do" link "do" 0}, @{"while" link "while" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "if" "if"\012SYNTAX  if ( expression ) statement; [ else  statement;  ]\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        An if statement allows you to conditionally process a statement if\012        the specified test expression evaluates to a nonzero value. You can\012        optionally specify an else clause on the if statement. If the test\012        expression evaluates to 0 (zero), and an else clause exists, the\012        statement in the else clause is run. If the test expression evaluates\012        to a nonzero value, the statement following the expression runs and\012        the else clause is ignored.\012\012        When if statements are nested and else clauses are present, a given\012        else is associated with the closest preceding if statement within the\012        same block.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        The following example causes grade to receive the value A if the\012        value of score is greater than or equal to 90.\012\012          if (score >= 90)\012            grade = A;\012\012        The following example displays number is positive if the value of\012        number is greater or equal to 0 (zero). Otherwise, the example\012        displays number is negative.\012\012          if (number >= 0)\012            output("number is positive\n");\012          else\012            output("number is negative\n");\012\012\012        The following example shows a nested if statement:\012\012          if (paygrade == 7)\012            if (level >= 0 && level <= 8)\012              salary *= 2;\012            else\012              salary *= 3;\012          else\012             salary *= 4;\012\012\012        The following example shows an if statement that does not have an\012        else clause.\012\012          if (gallons > 0) {\012            if (miles > gallons)\012              mpg = miles/gallons;\012          } else\012            mpg = 0;\012\012\012        The following example shows an if statement nested within an else\012        clause. This example tests multiple conditions.  The tests are made\012        in order of their appearance. If one test evaluates to a nonzero\012        value, an action statement runs and the entire if statement ends.\012\012          if (value > 0)\012             increase++;\012          else if (value == 0)\012             breaeven++;\012          else\012             ++decrease;\012@endnode\012\012@node "int" "int"\012SYNTAX  int name [= expression];\012\012          or\012\012        long name [= expression];\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        Declare an integer (signed 32-bit) variable and assign a value to it.\012        Read more about it the chapter discussing variables. Not assigned\012        variables equals zero (0) after declaration.\012\012        ANSI C specifies that `long'' should be a 32-bit number and `int'' is\012        more vague. The MC680x0 processor series has - as all decent\012        processors have - 32-bit integers, which make them the exact same\012        data type. FPL is mostly run on such processors making this\012        equivalence natural.\012\012INPUTS  string name             - The variable name.\012        int/long expression     - Initial expression.\012\012SEE     @{" string " link "string" 0}, @{" char   " link "char" 0}, @{" short  " link "short" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "resize" "resize"\012SYNTAX  resize name [ new_size ] ;\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        A common problem when using arrays is that you don''t know the size of\012        it already from the start. This keyword brings a solution to that\012        problem. This one resizes the named array to the new size you specify\012        inside the square brackets. Notice that it''s also possible to reduce\012        the size, so be careful not to destroy data you like to reuse.\012\012        WARNING: Using multi dimensional arrays and `resize'' is a dangerous\012        combination. E.g when changing an array from "[4][5]" to "[5][5]",\012        the old values will not any longer be readable in their old array\012        members. A resize of this kind makes the data move around a bit\012        depending on the internal data array storage. DO NOT USE `resize'' IN\012        MULTI DIMENSIONAL ARRAYS IF YOU WANT THE DATA TO STAY INTACT!\012\012INPUTS\012        name            - The name of the array you''d like to resize.\012        new_size        - The new size of the array.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        To make a list of all the names the user enters, you need an array as\012        big as the user pleases. But declaring an enormous size from the\012        start really isn''t to recommend. This example shows one way to solve\012        such a problem:\012\012          int roof=10, num;\012          string names[roof];\012          do {\012            if(num==roof)\012              resize names[roof+=10];\012            names[num]=input("Insert name number " num ":");\012          } while(names[num++]!="");\012\012\012        Multi dimensional arrays looses their proper values when resizing, do\012        copy the array to preserve to contents:\012\012          int a[DIM1][DIM2];       // This is the old array construction\012          {\012            /* This could with benefit be written into a function to be\012               called from several instances */\012\012            int n1, n2;              // counter variables\012            int b[NEWDIM1][NEWDIM2]; // The temporary new array outfit!\012\012            for(n1=0; n1<DIM1 && n1<NEWDIM1; n1++)   // temporary store all\012              for(n2=0; n2<DIM2 && n2<NEWDIM2; n2++) // vital data in array b\012                b[n1][n2]=a[n1][n2];\012\012            resize a[NEWDIM1][NEWDIM2]; // resize a\012\012            for(n1=0; n1<NEWDIM1; n1++)   // restore all data to array a\012              for(n2=0; n2<NEWDIM2; n2++)\012                a[n1][n2]=b[n1][n2];\012          }\012@endnode\012\012@node "return" "return"\012SYNTAX  return [ return_code ];\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        A return statement ends the execution of the current function and\012        returns a return code and control to the caller of it. The return\012        code is written exact as in C, within parentheses or not. It can\012        also be totally excluded.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        We use a function to print the result of the formula, but only if\012        the result is even:\012\012          int n;\012          int print(int);\012          for(n=0; n<10; n++)\012            n+=printf(n*n);\012          exit;\012\012          int print(int a)\012          {\012            if(!(a&1))\012              output(a);\012             return(a&2);  /* Get back to the for loop */\012          }\012\012SEE     @{" exit " link "exit" 0}, @{" break " link "break" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "short" "short"\012SYNTAX  short name [= expression];\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        Declares a short (signed 16-bit) variable (and assigns a value to\012        it). Read more about it the chapter discussing @{"variables." link "Variables" 0} Not\012        assigned variables equal zero (0) after declaration.\012\012INPUTS  string name        - The variable name.\012        short expression   - Initial expression.\012\012SEE     @{"int " link "int" 0}, @{"char" link "char" 0}, @{"string" link "string" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "string" "string"\012* Strings are always written enclosed within quotation marks (").\012\012* To read the ASCII value of a single character within a string, use\012  square brackets in the form: name[index] where name is the name of\012  the string variable (could of course also be an array memb@{"er) and" link "Functions" 0}\012@{"  i" link "Functions" 0}ndex is the column you want to check out. Index below zero or above\012  the length of the source string will result in error. The following\012  @{"examp" link "int" 0}le@{" outputs all ASCII c" link "Variables" 0}odes in a string:\012\012     string  str = "hello world";\012     int  ascii, n;\012     while ( ascii = str[n++] ) {\012       output ( ascii "n" );\012    }\012\012  This example will output all ASCII values in column 2 in the strings\012  in the array:\012\012     string  str[3] = {"hello", "world", "string"};\012     int  n;\012     for ( n = 0; n < 3; n++ ) {\012       output ( str[n][2] "n" )\012    }\012\012  Reading beyond the end of a string simply returns a zero, while\012  reading a negative column will result in an error.\012\012* Strings not enclosed within quotes are assumed to be variable names.\012\012  Note the difference between:\012\012    output("Hello");\012\012  and\012\012    output(Hello);\012\012  (Example 1 outputs the actual string "Hello", while example 2 outputs\012  the contents of the variable *named* "Hello".)\012\012* Append strings to a string variable by using the ''+='' operator. Ex:\012\012    string a="Hello ";\012    a+="world";\012    output(a);\012\012  Outputs the string "Hello world" on the screen.\012\012    string a = "Hello ";\012    string b ="world";\012    output( a + b );\012\012* Special characters within strings should be symbolized with backslash\012  and a character just as in the good old C standard:\012  a    - Alert (bell)\012  b    - Backspace\012  f    - Form feed (new page)\012  n    - New-line\012  r    - Carriage return\012  t    - Horizontal tab\012  v    - Vertical tab\012  ''    - Single quotation mark\012  "    - Double quotation mark\012  ?    - Question mark\012  \    - Backslash\012  xhh  - hex number, where "hh" is a two digit hexadecimal value.\012  nnn  - octal number, where "nnn" is a three digit octal value.\012\012See also the function reference for string handling functions such as\012 strcmp(), strncmp(), substr(), eval() and more.\012@endnode\012\012@node "typedef" "typedef"\012SYNTAX  typedef <declarator> <symbol>;\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        `typedef'' allows you to define your own identifiers that can be used\012        in place of FPL type specifiers such as int, string and char. The\012        data types you define using `typedef'' are not new data types; they\012        are synonyms for the primary data types used by FPL.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        This example creates a synonym for int and then use that to declare\012        three integral variables:\012\012                typedef int LENGTH;     /* new declarator named `LENGTH'' */\012                LENGTH length, width, height;\012@endnode\012\012@node "switch" "switch"\012SYNTAX  switch ( expression ) { [case expression:] [default:expression] }\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        A switch statement lets you define actions to different results of\012        the switch expression.\012\012        The result of the expression written within the parentheses following\012        the switch keyword is matched against the expressions following the\012        ''@{" case " link "case" 0}'' keywords within the braces. If no match is found the action\012        specified after ''@{" default " link "default" 0}'' will be run. If no ''default'' is defined\012        either, nothing will happen.\012\012        The switch expression may be either a string or numerical expression.\012        The expressions in the following case statements have to be of the\012        same expression type as the initial one, or FPL will report error.\012\012        Multiple cases may be specified with the same expression, but only\012        the first one that match the switch expression will be run.\012\012        A case statement is aborted by a @{" break " link "break" 0} statement or the brace\012        ending the switch statement.\012\012NOTE\012        Unlike C, FPL handles dynamic expressions in case-statements very\012        well!\012\012        The case statements will be parsed/scanned from the top to bottom.\012        For speed reasons, you should put the case-statements that occur\012        most likely at the top and the least likely case-statements at\012        the bottom.\012\012EXAMPLES\012\012        In the following program the result from the equation ''x+5'' is\012        controlling what text to assign to the ''output'' variable:\012\012          int check = x + 5;\012          string output;\012          switch( check ) {\012            case 5:     /* notice the colon that follows the expression */\012              output = "x was zero";\012              break;    /* escape from switch! */\012\012            case 4:\012              break;    /* do nothing */\012\012            case 7:\012              /* fall through! */\012\012            case 8:\012              output = "x was two or three";\012              break;\012\012            default:\012              output = "x was not from -1 to 3";\012          }   /* breaks out of ''default'' */\012\012        In the following program, the content of the variable ''username'' is\012        controlling which function to invoke:\012\012          string username = getname();  /* get user name */\012          switch(username) {\012            case "superuser":\012              GetSuperUser();\012              break;\012\012            case "normal":\012              GetNormalUser();\012              break;\012\012            case "unworthy":\012              GetUnworthyUser();\012              break;\012          }\012@endnode\012\012@node "while" "while"\012SYNTAX  while (@{" expression " link "Expressions" 0}) @{" statement " link "Statements" 0}; [ @{" else " link "else" 0} statement; ]\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        A while statement enables you to repeatedly run the body of a loop\012        until the controlling expression is no longer met or evaluates to 0\012        (zero.)\012\012        The expression is evaluated to determine whether or not the body of\012        the loop should be run. If the expression evaluates to 0 (zero), the\012        body of the loop never runs. If not, the body is processed. After the\012        body has run, control passes back to the expression. Further\012        processing depends on the value of the condition.\012\012        When coding in ''C'', I''ve always missed the "while else" function. FPL\012        includes that feature! If the test expression evaluates to false\012        (zero) the FIRST TIME it''s evaluated, and an "else" clause exists,\012        the statement in the "else" clause is run. If the test expression\012        evaluates to a nonzero value, the statement following the expression\012        runs and the "else" clause is ignored.\012\012        A break or return statement can cause the processing of a while\012        statement to end, even when the condition does not evaluate to 0\012        (zero).\012\012        NOTE: The else clause is *NOT* a loop. "break" is not a valid move to\012        break out of such.\012\012EXAMPLES\012\012        In the following program, item triples each time the value of the\012        expression index++ is less than MAX_INDEX. When index++ evaluates to\012        MAX_INDEX, the while statement ends.\012\012          while (index < MAX_INDEX) {\012            item *= 3;\012            output("item = ", item, "\n");\012            index++;\012          }\012\012        The following program first checks if a<0 and if it is, increases a\012        with 100 until it isn''t any longer less than zero. If not, it''ll\012        output a message saying so.\012\012          while(a<0)\012            a+=100;\012          else\012            output("a was never below 0\n");\012\012\012SEE     @{" break " link "break" 0}, @{" return " link "return" 0}, @{"continue" link "continue" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "FunctionsR" "Functions"\012  These are the internal functions supplied from the start by FPL:\012\012  Function          Argument(s)     Short Description\012  --------          -----------     -----------------\012  \012  @{" abs       " link "abs" 0}       int           Absolute value of integer operand.\012  @{" atoi      " link "atoi" 0}       str           Convert a string to an integer.\012  @{" eval      " link "eval" 0}       str           Calculate a string.\012  @{" interpret " link "interpret" 0}       str           Interpret string as program.\012  @{" itoa      " link "itoa" 0}       int           Convert an integer to string.\012  @{" itoc      " link "itoc" 0}       int           Convert an integer to ASCII.\012  @{" joinstr   " link "joinstr" 0}       str, ...      Joins strings!\012  @{" ltostr    " link "ltostr" 0}       n1, n2        Convert an integer to string.\012  @{" sprintf   " link "sprintf" 0}                     Formatted print to a string.\012  @{" strcmp    " link "strcmp" 0}       str1, str2    Compare two strings.\012  @{" stricmp   " link "strcmp" 0}       str1, str2    Compare two strings. CI(*)\012  @{" strlen    " link "strlen" 0}       str]          String length.\012  @{" strncmp   " link "strncmp" 0}       str1, str2, n Compare two strings a certain length.\012  @{" strnicmp  " link "strncmp" 0}       str1, str2, n Compare two strings a certain length. CI(*)\012  @{" strstr    " link "strstr" 0}       str1, str2    Find substring str1 in string str2.\012  @{" stristr   " link "strstr" 0}       str1, str2    Find substring str1 in string str2. CI(*)\012  @{" strtol    " link "strtol" 0}       str, base     Convert string to integer.\012  @{" substr    " link "substr" 0}       s1, col, len  Get part of string.\012\012 [Amiga only:]\012\012  @{" openlib   " link "openlib" 0}       lib, ver        Open funclib.\012  @{" closelib  " link "closelib" 0}       lib             Close funclib.\012\012(*) = Case insensitive. It doesn''t make any difference to ''A'' or ''a''.\012      It does only apply to ASCII characters; that is only a-z and A-Z.\012@endnode\012\012@node "abs" "abs"\012SYNTAX  int abs( Int );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        Returns the absolute value of the input integer.\012\012INPUTS  int Int;\012\012RETURNS see description.\012@endnode\012\012@node "atoi" "atoi"\012SYNTAX  int atoi( String );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        The atoi keyword returns an integer whose value is represented by the\012        character string pointed to by the String parameter. atoi scans the\012        string up to the first character that is inconsistent. Leading\012        white-space characters are ignored, and an optional sign may precede\012        the digits.\012\012INPUTS  string String;\012\012RETURNS\012        Upon successful completion, atoi returns the converted value. If no\012        conversion could be performed, 0 (zero) is returned. If the correct\012        value is outside the range of representable values, maximum or\012        minimum value is returned according to the sign of the value.\012\012SEE\012        @{"strtol()" link "strtol" 0}, @{"itoa()" link "itoa" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "closelib" "closelib"\012SYNTAX  int closelib( Name );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012         Closes a specified @{" funclib " link "funclibs" 0}. Funclibs are third\012        party programs that add functions to the FPL session. The funclibs\012        should be stored in FPLLIBS:.\012\012         A closelib() concludes access to the functions of the specified\012        funclib. Each call to @{" openlib() " link "openlib" 0} should have a matching call to\012        closelib().\012\012         For information regarding specified funclibs, refer to the manual of\012        the particular funclib.\012\012INPUTS\012        string Name     - The name of the funclib.\012\012RETURNS\012        It return 0 on success. Otherwise see ''openlib'' for closer\012        description.\012\012SEE     @{" openlib " link "openlib" 0} and the @{" funclibs " link "funclibs" 0} section.\012@endnode\012\012@node "eval" "eval"\012SYNTAX  int eval( String );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        The mathematical expression in String is evaluated and returned.\012        eval handles all variables declared before this point of the\012        execution.\012        An error in the expression will unfortunately return 0 which will\012        look like the result of the expression.\012\012INPUTS  string String   - A string including a valid numerical expression.\012\012RETURNS\012        The result of the mathematical expression in String or 0 if there was\012        any error in the expression.\012@endnode\012\012@node "interpret" "interpret"\012SYNTAX  int interpret( String );                        (V3)\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        Interprets the argument as if it is a continuation of the program.\012        The argument must be a 100% correct FPL program statement. All\012        variables and functions usable in the program at this point will be\012        usable in the argument-statement too.\012\012        Any error in this function will get reported at the position right\012        after the function call.\012\012INPUTS  string String   - FPL statement\012\012RETURNS\012        At this moment: always zero. But do not depend upon that since it\012        might get changed in the future.\012\012EXAMPLE\012        This example calls a function via the interpret() function:\012\012                int foobar(int);\012                interpret("foobar(2);");\012                exit;\012\012                int foobar(int b)\012                {\012                  output(b);\012                }\012\012        We can create a function that accepts a function name as parameter\012        and then is able to call that named function:\012\012                int foobar(int);\012                int foo(int);\012                int bar(int);\012                int call(string, int);\012\012                call("foobar", 1);\012                call("foo", 2);\012                call("bar", 3);\012                exit;\012\012                int call(string command, int parameter)\012                {\012                  int ret;      /* declare return code variable */\012                  string statement=joinstr("ret=", command, "(",\012                                           ltostr(parameter), ");");\012\012                  output(statement);    /* output the statement */\012                  interpret(statement); /* interpret it */\012                  /*\012                   * At this point `ret'' has got the return code\012                   * of the called function.\012                   */\012                  return(ret);\012                }\012\012                /*\012                 * Here should the definitions of the functions\012                 * "foobar", "foo" and "bar" be programmed.\012                 */\012\012SEE     @{" eval " link "eval" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "itoa" "itoa"\012SYNTAX  string itoa( Integer );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        The itoa function converts the integer given as argument to a string\012        and returns it. The convertion will use base 10, which creates a\012        decimal number.\012\012INPUTS\012        int Integer     - Number to be converted.\012\012RETURNS\012          Upon successful completion, the function returns the converted\012        string. If no conversion could be performed, the returned string is\012        zero-length.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        Convert the number the variable ''num'' holds, to a string:\012\012          string result= itoa(num);\012\012SEE     @{"ltostr()" link "ltostr" 0}, @{"atoi()" link "atoi" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "itoc" "itoc"\012SYNTAX  string itoc( Integer );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012          The itoc function returns the character with the ASCII code\012        given as argument. Any argument number higher than 255 will be ANDed\012        with 255 internally.\012\012INPUTS\012        int Integer     - ASCII code of the desired character.\012\012RETURNS\012          Upon successful completion, the function returns the character\012        as a string.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        To get the character with ASCII number 137:\012\012          string result= itoc( 137 );\012\012SEE     @{" itoa " link "itoa" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "joinstr" "joinstr"\012SYNTAX  string joinstr( String, ... );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012         This function lets you specify any amount of strings, and returns\012        them all as one concatenated single string.\012\012INPUT   string String   - Strings to be merged.\012\012RETURNS\012        A string holding all joined strings.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        Merge three strings into one:\012\012          string a="one ", c="two ", b="three", d;\012          d=joinstr(a, b, c); /* ''d'' now holds "one two three" */\012@endnode\012\012@node "ltostr" "ltostr"\012SYNTAX  string ltostr( Integer, Base );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012          The ltostr subroutine returns a string which is the first parameter\012        converted using the second parameter as base.\012\012          If the Base parameter is positive and not greater than 36, then it\012        is used as the base for conversion.\012\012INPUTS\012        int Integer     - Number to be converted.\012        int Base        - Specifies the base to use for the conversion.\012\012RETURNS\012          Upon successful completion, the ltostr subroutine returns the\012        converted string. If no conversion could be performed, the returned\012        string is zero-length.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        Convert the number 1993 to hexadecimal:\012\012          string result= ltostr(1993, 16);\012\012SEE     @{"strtol()" link "strtol" 0}, @{"itoa()" link "itoa" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "openlib" "openlib"\012SYNTAX  int openlib( Name, Version );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012         Opens a specified @{" funclib " link "funclibs" 0}. Funclibs are third\012        party programs that add functions to the FPL session. The funclibs\012        should be stored in FPLLIBS:.\012\012         An openlib() gains access to the functions of the specified funclib.\012        After finished using the functions of that funclib, the session\012should\012        be concluded with a call to @{" closelib() " link "closelib" 0}. Each\012        call to openlib() should have a matching call to closelib().\012\012         For information regarding specified funclibs, refer to the manual of\012        the particular funclib.\012\012INPUTS\012        string Name     - The name of the funclib.\012        int Version     - The lowest acceptable version of the funclib.\012\012RETURNS\012        It return 0 on success. Otherwise a non-zero number where the numbers\012        have the following meaning:\012        1 - funclib parameter error\012        2 - internal funclib error\012        3 - failed getting a system resource\012        4 - out of memory error\012        5 - failed loading the funclib (occurs if you open a non-existant\012            funclib).\012        6 - the requested version didn''t exist\012        More error codes are likely to be added within short.\012\012SEE     @{" closelib " link "closelib" 0} and the @{" funclibs " link "funclibs" 0} section.\012@endnode\012\012@node "sprintf" "sprintf"\012SYNTAX  string sprintf (format, arg1, arg2, ...);\012\012DESCRIPTION\012\012        This function produces a string of ASCII characters.\012\012        The format argument holds a string that contains ordinary\012        characters and conversion specifications that indicate how you\012        want the arguments arg1, arg2, and so on to be printed. The\012        ordinary characters are copied to the result string, but the\012        conversion specifications are replaced with the correctly formatted\012        values of the arguments arg1, arg2, and so on. The first conversion\012        specification is replaced with the formatted value of arg1, the\012        second specification is replaced with the value of arg2, and so\012        on. In some cases, as described below, a conversion specification\012        may process more than one argument.\012\012        Each conversion specification must begin with a percent character\012        (%). To place an ordinary percent into the output stream, precede\012        it with another percent in the fmt string. That is, %% will send\012        a single percent character to the output stream. A specification\012        has the following format:\012\012            %[arg][flags][width][.precision][size]type\012\012        The brackets ([]) indicate optional fields.  Each field is defined\012        as follows:\012\012            arg\012                FPL supports the argument number selection style $<arg>.\012                When an argument number has been given, that specified\012                argument will be used instead of the next one in turn.\012                I.e "%$2d" will produce a formatted integer found as the\012                second argument. You should *not* mix specified arugment\012                numbers with %-codes with unspecified arugment numbers.\012\012            flags\012                controls output justification and the printing of signs,\012                blanks, decimal places, and hexadecimal prefixes.\012\012                If any flag characters are used, they must appear after\012                the percent.  Valid flags are as follows:\012\012                - (minus)\012                    causes the result to be left-justified within the\012                    field specified by width or within the default width.\012\012                + (plus)\012                    causes a plus or minus sign to be placed before the\012                    result.  This flag is used in conjunction with the\012                    various numeric conversion types.  If it is absent,\012                    the sign character is generated only for a negative\012                    number.\012\012                blank\012                    causes a leading blank for a positive number and a\012                    minus sign for a negative number.  This flag is\012                    similar to the plus.  If both the plus and the blank\012                    flags are present, the plus takes precedence.\012\012                # (pound)\012                    causes special formatting.  With the o, x, and X\012                    types, the pound flag prefixes any nonzero output with\012                    0, 0x, or 0X, respectively.  With the f, e, and E\012                    conversion types, the pound flag forces the result to\012                    contain a decimal point.  With the g and G types, the\012                    pound flag forces the result to contain a decimal\012                    point and retain trailing zeroes.\012\012                0 (zero)\012                    pads the field width with leading zeros instead of\012                    spaces for the d, i, o, u, x, X, e, E, f, g, and G\012                    conversion types.  If the minus flag is also used, the\012                    zero flag is ignored.  If a precision is specified,\012                    the zero flag is ignored for conversion types d, i, o,\012                    u, x, and X.  Behavior of the zero flag is undefined\012                    for the remaining conversion types.\012\012            width\012                specifies the field width, which is the minimum number of\012                characters to be generated for this format item.\012\012                The width is a nonnegative number that specifies the\012                minimum field width.  If fewer characters are generated by\012                the conversion operation, the result is padded on the left\012                or right (depending on the minus flag described above).  A\012                blank is used as the padding character unless width begins\012                with a zero.  In that case, zero padding is performed.  If\012                the minus flag appears, padding is performed with blanks.\012                width specifies the minimum field width, and it will not\012                cause lengthy output to be truncated.  Use the precision\012                specifier for that purpose.\012\012                If you do not want to specify the field width as a\012                constant in the format string, you can code it as an\012                asterisk (*), with or without a leading zero.  The\012                asterisk indicates that the width value is an integer in\012                the argument list. See the examples for more information\012                on this technique. If the asterisk is followed by a $ sign\012                and a digit 1-9, you can specify which argument that holds\012                the width.\012\012            precision\012                specifies the field precision, which is the required\012                precision of numeric conversions or the maximum number of\012                characters to be copied from a string, depending on the\012                type field.\012\012                The meaning of the precision item depends on the field\012                type, as follows:\012\012                 Type                  Meaning\012                 ----                  -------\012                  c                    The precision item is ignored.\012                  b, d, i, o, u, x, X  The precision is the minimum number\012                                       of digits to appear.  If fewer\012                                       digits are generated, leading\012                                       zeroes are supplied.\012                  s                    The precision is the maximum number\012                                       of characters to be copied from the\012                                       string.\012\012                As with the width item, you can use an asterisk for the\012                precision to indicate that the value should be picked up\012                from the next argument.\012\012            size\012                Not used by FPL, though all the ANSI C modifiers are still\012                read and ignored for compatibility.\012                (Can be either L for long double, l for large size, or h\012                for small size.)\012\012            type\012                specifies the type of argument conversion to be done.\012                Valid conversion types are as follows:\012\012                    b\012                        specifies binary-integer conversion. The\012                        associated argument is taken as an unsigned\012                        integer, and it is converted to a string of binary\012                        digits.  This conversion type is an extension to\012                        the ANSI standard.\012                    c\012                        specifies single-character conversion. The\012                        associated argument must be an integer.  The\012                        single character in the right-most byte of the\012                        integer is copied to the output.\012                    d\012                        specifies decimal-integer conversion. The\012                        associated argument must be an integer, and the\012                        result is a string of digits preceded by a sign.\012                        If the plus and blank flags are absent, the sign\012                        is produced only for a negative integer.\012                    i\012                        specifies decimal-integer conversion. The\012                        associated argument must be an integer, and the\012                        result is a string of digits preceded by a sign.\012                        If the plus and blank flags are absent, the sign\012                        is produced only for a negative integer.\012                    n\012                        specifies the argument will be a pointer to an\012                        integer into which is written the number of\012                        characters written so far by this call to the\012                        sprintf function.\012                    o\012                        specifies octal-integer conversion. The\012                        associated argument is taken as an unsigned\012                        integer, and it is converted to a string of octal\012                        digits.\012                    p\012                        specifies pointer conversion.  The associated\012                        argument is taken as a data pointer, and it is\012                        converted to hexadecimal representation.  Under\012                        AmigaDOS, the pointer is printed as 8 hexadecimal\012                        digits, with leading zeroes if necessary.\012                    P\012                        specifies pointer conversion. This is the same as\012                        the p format, except that uppercase letters are\012                        used as hexadecimal digits. This conversion type\012                        is an extension to the ANSI standard.\012                    s\012                        specifies string conversion. The associated argument\012                        must be a string!\012                    u\012                        specifies unsigned decimal integer conversion.\012                        The associated argument is taken as an unsigned\012                        integer, and it is converted to a string of\012                        decimal digits.\012                    x\012                        specifies hexadecimal-integer conversion.  The\012                        associated argument is taken as an unsigned\012                        integer, and it is converted to a string of\012                        hexadecimal digits with lowercase letters.\012                    X\012                        specifies hexadecimal-integer conversion.  This is\012                        the same as the x format, except that uppercase\012                        letters are used as hexadecimal digits.\012\012RETURNS\012\012        This function returns the formatted string.\012\012EXAMPLE\012\012        /* This example prints a message indicating whether */\012        /* the function argument is positive or negative.   */\012        /* In the second sprintf, the width and precision   */\012        /* are 15 and 8, respectively.                      */\012        string pneg(int value)\012        {\012            string sign;\012\012            if (value < 0) {\012              sign = "negative";\012            }\012            else {\012              sign = "not negative";\012            }\012            return sprintf("The number %d is %s.n",value,sign);\012        }\012\012        Output(pneg(37));\012        Output(pneg(-18));\012\012        /* This example outputs 12 and 8 in reversed order by using the\012           specified argument number syntax: */\012\012        Output( sprintf( " %$2d %$1d ", 12, 8 ) );\012@endnode\012\012@node "strcmp" "strcmp"\012SYNTAX  int strcmp ( String1, String2 );\012\012        int stricmp ( String1, String2 );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        strcmp lexicographically compares the string in the String1\012        parameter to the string in the String2 parameter. stricmp() does\012        the same but does a case insensitive compare, which makes uppercase\012        letters equal their lowercase correspondance.\012\012        Case insensitive means that the strings "HELLO" and "hello" are\012        treated as identical.\012\012INPUTS  string String1, String2;\012\012RETURNS\012        strcmp and stricmp return values that are:\012\012        Less than 0 (zero) if String1 is less than String2\012        Equal to 0 (zero) if String1 is equal to String2\012        Greater than 0 (zero) if String1 is greater than String2.\012\012SEE     @{"strncmp and strnicmp" link "strncmp" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "strlen" "strlen"\012SYNTAX  int strlen( String );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        Returns the length of the string String.\012\012INPUTS  string  String;\012\012RETURNS\012        The length of the string String.\012@endnode\012\012@node "strncmp" "strncmp"\012SYNTAX  int strncmp ( String1, String2, Num );\012\012        int strnicmp ( String1, String2, Num );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        The strncmp and strnicmp subroutines make the same comparisons as\012        strcmp and stricmp, but they compare at most ''Num'' characters.\012\012INPUTS  string String1, String2;\012        int Number      - Maximum number of characters to compare.\012\012RETURNS\012        they return values that are:\012\012        Less than 0 (zero) if String1 is less than String2\012        Equal to 0 (zero) if String1 is equal to String2\012        Greater than 0 (zero) if String1 is greater than String2.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        Compare the string variable Str1 and check if it matches Str2, but\012        check no more than 12 characters:\012\012          if(!strncmp(Str1, Str2, 12))\012            output("MATCH!");\012\012        This example returns zero (0) in result.\012\012          result = strncmp("realization", "really", 4);\012\012        This example returns a value less than zero in result.\012\012          result = strncmp("world", "worldgames", 100);\012\012\012SEE     @{"strcmp() and stricmp()" link "strcmp" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "strstr" "strstr"\012SYNTAX  int strstr( String1, String2, Column );\012\012        int stristr ( String1, String2, Column );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        The strstr and stristr functions find occurrences of String2 in\012        String1.\012\012        The ''Column'' parameter can be set optionally. It specifies the start\012        searching column!\012\012        strstr() requires the substring to match exactly, while the stristr()\012        performs a case insentive search. Case insensitive means that the\012        strings "HELLO" and "hello" are treated as identical.\012\012INPUTS\012        string String1  - The string to search within.\012\012        string String2  - The substring.\012\012        int Column      - Start search column.\012\012RETURNS\012        They return in which column they found String2 or -1 if the string\012        was not found. If String2 is a 0 (zero) length string, the functions\012        return 0.\012\012SEE     @{"strncmp and strnicmp" link "strncmp" 0}, @{"strcmp and stricmp" link "strcmp" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "strtol" "strtol"\012SYNTAX  int strtol( String, Base );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012          The strtol subroutine returns an integer whose value is represented\012        by the character string pointed to by the String parameter. The\012        strtol subroutine scans the string up to the first character that is\012        inconsistent with the Base parameter. Leading white-space characters\012        are ignored, and an optional sign may precede the digits.\012\012          If the Base parameter is positive and not greater than 36, then it\012        is used as the base for conversion. After an optional leading sign,\012        leading zeros are ignored. "0x" or "0X" is ignored if Base is 16\012        (sixteen).\012  \012          If the Base parameter is 0 (zero), the string determines the base.\012        Thus, after an optional leading sign, a leading 0 (zero) indicates\012        octal conversion, a leading "0x" or "0X" indicates hexadecimal\012        conversion and a leading "0b" or "0B" indicates a binary conversion.\012        The default is to use decimal conversion.\012\012INPUTS  string String   - Character string to be converted.\012        int Base        - Specifies the base to use for the conversion.\012\012RETURNS\012          Upon successful completion, the strtol subroutine return the\012        converted value. If no conversion could be performed, 0 (zero) is\012        returned. If the correct value is outside the range of representable\012        values, maximum or minimum value is returned according to the sign of\012        the value.\012\012EXAMPLES\012        Convert the string holding the hexadecimal "0xdeadbeef" to decimal:\012\012          string String = "0xdeadbeef"\012          int num = strtol(String, 16);\012\012SEE     @{" ltostr " link "ltostr" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "substr" "substr"\012SYNTAX  string substr( Source, Column, Length );\012\012DESCRIPTION\012        The substr function returns a substring to a destination string \012        variable. Giving a length of -1 will make substr get the rest\012        of the string from the column specified.\012\012        - If the substring is told to keep on beyond the end of the Source,\012          the substring will end where the Source string does.\012\012        - If the column parameter is below zero or larger than the length of\012          the source string, a zero length string is returned.\012\012        - The first column of the source is column 0 (zero).\012\012INPUTS\012        string Source   - The source string from which the substring\012                          will be copied from. */\012        int Column      - Start column of the substring.\012        int Length      - The Length of the substring.\012\012RETURNS\012        The substring!\012\012EXAMPLES\012        Extract a substring of 3 characters from column 4 of the source\012        string:\012\012          string Dest;\012          Dest=substr("One Two Three", 4, 3);\012\012        Get the right part of a line split by the character "|":\012\012          string Line="Names     |Addresses", Dest;\012          int pos;\012          pos=strstr(Line, "|");\012          if(pos<0)                     /* if we can''t find a "|" */\012            exit;\012\012          pos++;                        /* We don''t want to include the "|"\012                                           Character in our substring! */\012          Dest=substr(Line,             /* The source string */\012                      pos,              /* where from */\012                      -1);               /* To the end of the string. */\012\012        It''s OK to try getting more than you will get. This example get the\012        rest of the line too, but maximum 10000 characters...:\012\012          Dest=substr(Line, pos, 10000);\012\012SEE     @{"strstr()" link "strstr" 0} and @{"stristr()" link "strstr" 0}\012@endnode\012\012@node "Examples" "Examples"\0121. Ask the user for his age and output the number of days old he is:\012\012  int a;                            /* Create an integer called "a"  */\012  a=num_input("Age in years:");     /* Get age of user in variable a */\012  output("Makes " a*365 " days!\n");/* Output proper text            */\012\012\0122. Ask the user for his name and output it a hundred times with spaces\012   between:\012\012  string name;                          /* Create a string variable */\012  name=str_input("What''s your name?");  /* Input name               */\012  int i;                                /* Create an integer        */\012  for (i=0; i<100; i++)                 /* Count 100 times          */\012    output(name " ");                   /* Output the name          */\012\012\0123. Replace all form feed characters with the proper number of newlines\012   to suit the page length given by the user (for guys using\012   printers which by some reason don''t handle form feeds):\012\012  int len, i, page;                     // Create a number of ints\012  len=num_input("Page length?");        // Get page length\012  while(search_for("\f", "S")) {        // Search for form feed\012    page=len-line()%len;                // Calc number of newlines\012    for(i=0, i<page, i++)               // Count to number above\012      output("\n");                     // Output a newline\012  }                                     // End of while\012  status("Done!");                      // Text in status line.\012\012\0124. Make a nice little table with temperatures in Celsius and\012   Fahrenheit. Count from 0 to 100 Celsius with ten degrees steps:\012\012  int c, f;                     /* Declare two variables             */\012  while(c<=100) {               /* while c less than or equal to 100 */\012    output(c" degrees Celsius = ");/* output Celsius                 */\012    f=c*18/10+32;               /* The formula. Made to work\012                                   without floating points           */\012    output(f" degrees Fahrenheit! \n"); /* output Fahrenheit result  */\012    c+=10;                      /* Add ten to c.                     */\012  }                             /* loop                              */\012\0125. Create a routine that make a division with an innumerable number of\012   decimals:\012\012  int a, b, n;                  /* Declare all variables needed     */\012  a=355;                        /* Set the operands of the division */\012  b=14;\012  output(a " / " b " = ");      /* View the evaluation to the user  */\012  output(a/b);                  /* Calculate...                     */\012  if(a%b) {                     /* If there remains anything...     */\012    output(".");                /* Display a dot.                   */\012    for(n=0;n<100;n++) {        /* Only 100 decimals this time!     */\012      a=a%b*10;                 /* Evaluate next left operator      */\012      output(a/b);              /* View the next figure             */\012    }\012  }\012  output("\n");                 /* Make an extra newline            */\012\012\0126. Perform the same task as above. This is an example program showing\012   some simple moves to make the code run faster:\012\012  int a=355, b=14, n;\012  string s=a" / "b" = "(a/b);\012  if(a%b)\012    for(s+=".";n++<100;)\012      s+=((a=a%b*10)/b);\012  output(s"\n");\012\0127. Try out the recursive possibilities of FPL:\012\012  int b;\012  int label(int &);\012  label(b);\012  output(b"\n");\012  exit;\012\012  int label(int &b)\012  {\012    if(++b<5) {\012      while(1) {{{{{{label(b);break;}}}}}}\012    }\012    return;\012  }\012@endnode\012\012@node "Errors" "Error messages"\012DIVISION BY ZERO\012  Division by zero is not a permitted mathematical move (note that this may\012  also appear with the remainder operator "%").\012  See @{" division " link "Division" 0} or @{" remainder " link "Remainder" 0}.\012\012FILE\012  Something about the specified file is wrong. Check the file name again.\012\012IDENTIFIER NOT FOUND\012  The variable or function is not found!\012\012IDENTIFIER ALREADY USED\012  This name is already used by an identifier. You cannot use it too!\012\012ILLEGAL ARRAY\012  You cannot create an array with less than one element, access elements with\012  higher number than you declared it to or access negative elements. When\012  using the resize keyword the variable name must already be an array.\012  See @{" variables " link "Variables" 0} or @{" resize " link "resize" 0}.\012\012ILLEGAL ASSIGN\012  FPL does not support that type of @{" compound assignment " link "CompoundAssign" 0}.\012\012ILLEGAL BREAK\012  @{" Break " link "break" 0} can ONLY be used within some kind of loop (@{" while " link "while" 0}, @{" for " link "for" 0} or @{" do " link "do" 0})\012  or @{" switch " link "switch" 0}, and this wasn''t such a @{" statement " link "Statements" 0}.\012\012ILLEGAL CASE\012  The ''case'' keyword can only be used within a @{"switch()" link "switch" 0} statement!\012\012ILLEGAL CONDITION OPERATOR\012  @{"Conditional operators" link "Conditional" 0} should be used like a ? b : c. If any of the ? or :\012  characters are missing, this might become the error message!\012\012ILLEGAL CONTINUE\012  @{" Continue " link "continue" 0} can ONLY be used within some kind of loop (@{" while " link "while" 0}, @{" for " link "for" 0} or\012  @{" do " link "do" 0}), and this wasn''t such a @{" statement " link "Statements" 0}.\012\012ILLEGAL DECLARATION\012  The @{" declaration " link "Variables" 0} was not placed in the beginning of a block,\012  or a function isn''t declared the way it was prototyped.\012\012ILLEGAL DEFAULT\012  The ''default'' keyword can only be used within a @{"switch()" link "switch" 0} statement!\012\012ILLEGAL PROTOTYPE\012  Something in the just parsed function prototype was not using\012  correct syntax and/or keywords.\012\012ILLEGAL REFERENCE\012  A variable reference was used the wrong way!\012\012ILLEGAL RESIZE\012  @{"Resizing" link "resize" 0} of an array did not follow correct syntax!\012\012ILLEGAL STATEMENT\012  The @{" statement " link "Statements" 0} was not allowed at this point. It might\012  be a numerical expresion where a string expresion was expected or vice\012  versa. It might also be a statement that cannot be placed here.\012\012ILLEGAL STRING INDEX\012  Trying to read a negative column off a string will most likely cause this.\012\012ILLEGAL VARIABLE TYPE\012  You mixed @{" string " link "Strings" 0} and @{" integer " link "Integers" 0} variables/@{" expressions" link "Expressions" 0} illegally\012  or you tried to send to wrong kind of variable to a function.\012\012INTERNAL ERROR\012  The library''s internal data/structures has not been handled properly by\012  the coder of the software you''re using. Report this immediately to\012  him/her. (See the docs of the software you''re controlling with FPL.) This\012  is not the FPL programmer''s fault.\012\012INSIDE FUNCTION NOT FOUND\012  A function that has been prototyped to be an `inside'' function was not\012  found!\012  Notice that the function *MUST* be prototyped and declared the same way. A\012  function prototyped as `int'' but declared as `void'', will *NOT* be found by\012  the FPL interpreter!\012\012INCOMPLETE STATEMENT\012  The @{" statement " link "Statements" 0} just parsed did not include a required action such as a\012  variable change or a function call.\012\012MISSING APOSTROPHE\012  The program most likely contains a character within single quotes (''),\012  where the following quote is missing.\012\012MISSING ARGUMENT\012  The @{" function " link "FunctionsR" 0}/@{" keyword " link "KeywordsR" 0} you tried to invoke, requires more arguments!\012  (Or refer to the docs of the software you''re controlling with FPL.)\012\012MISSING BRACE\012  Some places just require braces. I.e block statements that have an initial\012  brace or @{"Array assigns" link "Variables" 0} that must end with a close\012  brace.\012\012MISSING BRACKET\012  Array references and other need an open bracket ([) and a close bracket\012  (]).\012\012MISSING COLON\012  There should be a colon (:) here, but it''s not!\012\012MISSING OPERAND\012  Making an expression that starts with an operator most likely causes this\012  error.\012\012MISSING PARENTHESES\012  The @{"statement" link "Statements" 0} just interpreted had a lack of parenthesis.\012\012MISSING SEMICOLON\012  All @{"statements" link "General" 0} must be separated with semicolons. This wasn''t!\012\012MISSING WHILE\012  There is a missing ''while'' keyword after the @{"do-while" link "do" 0} statement!\012\012OUT OF MEMORY\012  The system has run out of memory. This isn''t your fault, and there is\012  nothing you can do about it but decrease memory usage. Note that if *any*\012  internal FPL allocation call fails, the program stops with this error code.\012\012OUT OF STACK\012  The stack size hit the roof. The program is too recursive!\012\012PARAMETER IS OUT OF RANGE\012  The parameter(s) specified must be within a certain range, which you\012  clearly did not manage. (Refer to the docs of the action you just tried.)\012\012PROGRAM STOPPED\012  The program was stopped by a force outside the library. This is not\012  a real error message but more like information.\012\012READ ONLY VIOLATION\012  You tried changing the contents of a @{"read-only variable" link "Variables" 0}.\012\012SYNTAX ERROR\012  Severe error in your writing. Rethink and try again. All @{" functions " link "Functions" 0},\012  labels or variable @{" identifiers MUST start with a letter " link "General" 0}.\012\012TOO MANY PARAMETERS\012  Either a function is called with too many parameters, or there is a missing\012  paranthesis after the parameters.\012\012UNBALANCED COMMENT\012  There is no corresponding end of comment.\012\012UNEXPECTED END OF PROGRAM\012  The program ended where it wasn''t supposed to! Probable cause is a\012  lack of closing comment sign or lack of a closing paren of some kind.\012\012UNEXPECTED INTEGER STATEMENT\012  The interpreter expected a @{"string expression" link "String" 0}, but read an integer one.\012\012UNEXPECTED STRING STATEMENT\012  The interpreter expected an @{"integer expression" link "ExpressionStatement" 0}, but read a string one.\012\012UNKNOWN\012  The programmer of the host software sent a strange error code to FPL which\012  there is no corresponding error message to!\012\012UNMATCHED BRACE\012  This brace has no corresponding one.\012@endnode\012\012@node HeddleyAutoIndex "Index"\012@remark Index auto-generated by Heddley\012Index of database FPL@{PAR}\012@{PAR}\012Documents\012@{PAR}\012@{"About this manual" link "AboutManual"}@{LINE}\012@{"abs" link "abs"}@{LINE}\012@{"Addition +" link "Addition"}@{LINE}\012@{"Assignment expressions" link "Assignment"}@{LINE}\012@{"atoi" link "atoi"}@{LINE}\012@{"Binary expressions" link "Binary"}@{LINE}\012@{"Bitwise AND &" link "Band"}@{LINE}\012@{"Bitwise Exclusive OR ^" link "Beor"}@{LINE}\012@{"Bitwise inclusive OR |" link "Bor"}@{LINE}\012@{"Bitwise left and right shift << >>" link "Shift"}@{LINE}\012@{"Bitwise negation ~" link "BNegation"}@{LINE}\012@{"Blockstatement" link "blockstatement"}@{LINE}\012@{"break" link "break"}@{LINE}\012@{"case" link "case"}@{LINE}\012@{"char" link "char"}@{LINE}\012@{"closelib" link "closelib"}@{LINE}\012@{"Comma expressions" link "Comma"}@{LINE}\012@{"Compound assignment" link "CompoundAssign"}@{LINE}\012@{"Conditional expressions" link "Conditional"}@{LINE}\012@{"Constants" link "Constants"}@{LINE}\012@{"continue" link "continue"}@{LINE}\012@{"debug    " link "debug"}@{LINE}\012@{"Decl