         | | | |\n                | | | `---------> Seconds\n                | | `-----------> Minutes\n                | `-------------> Day\n                `---------------> Conference Number\n\n     If there are more then 100 Conferences then the first character\n     becomes a ''A''....''J''.\n\n  o To know from Which board you got the Dirs a Text-File will be added\n    with the following information:\n\n      [email removed]:\n\n       BBS       :  LaST ouTPoST\n       CONFERENCE:  SNES <OUTPOST>\n       DATE      :  01-29-94\n\n  o I''ve been thinking about adding a Global Conference Downloader but I\n    think it''s a bit useless becoz overhere the Archive would be about\n    4 Mb in size!\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n REGISTRATION:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  If YOU use this Program please let me know by Calling Last Outpost or\n  to send me a Message on AmiXnet @NL0004 [EMPiRE] Coz I want to know\n  how many Sysops are using my Tools!!!\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n HISTORY:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  V1.0  02-24-93 - First Release\n\n  V1.1  04-01-93 - Release Version\n                 - Changed the Name of the DL-File\n\n  V1.2  04-20-93 - Added NonStop Txt Showing\n        05-11-93 - /X 3.x Compatible\n        06-01-93 - Command Line Usage is now Possible! (dd 1 2 4)\n        06-12-93 - Release Version\n\n  V1.3  06-21-93 - Fixed a small Error found with the RexxDoorDebugger.\n\n  V2.0ß 01-17-94 - Rewritten to XIM\n        01-18-94 - Added a Better OutPut FileName\n        01-25-94 - Fixed the ''10-Node'' BUG.\n        01-27-94 - Added the BBS InfoFile to the Archive.\n\n  V2.0  01-29-94 - Release Version\n\n  V2.1ß 04-01-94 - Bug Fix: Command-Line fucked up when entering a character\n                            and not a DirNumber.\n        04-14-94 - Bug Fix: Command Line Usage only accepted 1 dir.\n        04-18-94 - ReCompiled with the Latest AE.Includes\n        05-04-94 - Bug Fix: Entering garbage would result in the command line\n                            jumping 1 line lower then it should.\n        06-20-94 - Changed Temporary directory from RAM: to T:\n\n  V2.1  07-01-94 - Release Version.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n EMPiRE /X DOORS/UTILS COLLECTION UNTIL NOW!:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - AboStats V0.2                        AIM Door\n  - AE2PCE V1.0                          CLI Util\n  - AutoOnline V1.3                      XIM Door/Util\n  - Bulls V2.2                           XIM Door\n  - CheckDir V1.0                        CLI Util\n  - CheckNU V1.0                         XIM Door\n  - ConfAccess V1.1                      ServerUtil\n  - CopyDate V1.3                        CLI Util\n  - DlDir V2.1                           XIM Door\n  - DoorKIT 1.01                         XIM Doors/Includes\n  - DMenu V1.0                           AIM Door\n  - DutchNET V1.0  [AmiXnet]             AIM Door\n  - Front V2.0                           XIM Door\n  - JoinCnf V3.6                         XIM Door\n  - LastCalls & TopUps V1.2              LogOffUtil\n  - LastDL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - LastUL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - MakeFree V1.1                        AIM Door\n  - MemberList V1.0                      AIM Door\n  - MonthTopUp V2.0                      ServerUtil\n  - MultiNodeLastCaller V1.1             LogOffUtil\n  - Page V2.1                            XIM Door\n  - PMail V1.1                           AIM Door\n  - Remote Maintenance V1.3              AIM Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.3                 AIM Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.2 [Party]         CLI Door\n  - SecLevChk V1.0                       ServerUtil\n  - Sent FrondEnd 1.0  [Sent_FE]         XIM Door\n  - StripCR V1.0                         CLI Util\n  - SysopCommands V1.0                   AIM Door\n  - UserData Cleaner/Repair V2.0         ServerUtil\n  - UserStats V2.0                       ServerUtil\n  - UsrCnfAx V1.1                        CLI Util\n  - View & Check Archive/Text V1.7       AIM Door\n  - WhoNode V1.3                         AIM Door\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n THANX MUST GO TO ALL OFFICIAL BETA-TESTERS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   Tycoon/Mystic /X-PoWeR!   [Channel X]\n   JPS                       [Red October]\n   Sal-One/TRSi              [BoonDocks]\n   Blue Bird/Mystic          [Tau Ceti]\n   Beast/Partek              [Sequencial]\n   SpeedDevil/Partek         [Rising Sun]\n   Galaxy                    [Ulixys]\n\n\n GREETZ/TNX GO TO:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   Killraven/MST /X-PoWeR! - For giving me sum Great Pointers/Sources on ''C''\n   ByteAndi/TRSI           - Tnx for all ya ''C'' Help & Sources!\n   Easy Rider/Mystic       - hope ya are back soon!\n   JAF/G.O.D.              - For some good REXX ideas\n   Bad Sector/Hidden       - Still Using your Sort-Routine. It''s the Fastest!\n   B.O.B./CyberForce       - For (Mis)Using/Testing my tools! :)\n   Aragorn/Vision          - Tnx for Testing\n   Camelot/Complex         - Tnx for Testing\n   Sharon/5D               - Tnx for Testing\n   Telekinetic/T.F.A.      - Tnx for Testing\n   Alcoholic               - Tnx for Testing\n   Price/T.F.A.            - Great job on your {F}ull {S}creen {E}ditor!\n   Van Damme               - Nice Doors!!!\n   DR.G/IBB                - Hope to see Check''N Add work on a 040\n   SiGMA SEVEN             - Nice job on SiGMA-EXPRESS! Tempted to ''Convert''\n   911/Movement            - Tnx For ya Ansi Logo''s!! You''re the Greatest!\n   Mike                    - Tnx for calling!\n   Amicom/TRSi             - Tnx for calling!\n   THC/Legend              - Tnx for Beta-Testing!\n   Mega Fortress           - Tnx for Beta-Testing!\n   SkIn                    - Tnx for ya Description Logo! Looks Great!!\n   Shadow Man/S8           - Nice Doors & Tnx for ya greetz! & Calling ofcoz\n   [MAZ!^PHAZE]            - Tnx for calling!\n   Shuyuki Tanaka          - Tnx for your Messages!\n   AGONY/cOmpLeX           - Tnx for calling!\n   HUMANOiD & jABBA /MST &\n    SCANDAL                - Tnx for calling!\n   LECTOR                  - Tnx for your NetMail\n   Koo Koo Ka Choo         - Tnx for calling!\n   TaRzAN                  - Tnx for your NetMail. Yezz 040 Speed Rulez!!\n   Crayor/Scx              - Tnx for calling!\n   Foxx''n''Lynxx            - Tnx for your BugReports!!!\n   ThE BiRD                - Tnx for your NetMail on UsrCnfAx\n   Dux                     - Tnx for your NetMail\n   dR.dRE!/fAITH           - Tnx for da greetz, Nice Doors btw\n   [-RUNT&SCOTTIE-]        - Tnx for calling!\n   sPAcEcAKe/aF            - Tnx for calling!\n   Freddy Kreuger/GOD      - Tnx for your NetMail on JoinCnf 3.2\n   MF1                     - Tnx for calling!\n   AtomiX/N.A.S.A          - Hope the ''C'' archive was sume help for ya!\n   MAD HARLEQUiN [ITPH01]  - Tnx for your Nice AmiXnet Messy''s\n   Mike Hoffren  [SF0001]  - Tnx for the BugReport on JoinCnf 3.3 [100+ Cnfs]\n   Galaxy/TFA              - Nice job on da SentCleaner\n   SvEN tHE CREAToR/5D     - ''Tnx'' for forcing me to use Cursor-Keys!\n   Diogenes/TPY/5D         - Tnx for calling and for your BugReport!\n   L.A.Style               - Tnx For Calling/Beta-Testing!\n\n   & Ofcoz to ALL MYSTiC Members all over the Globe!\n\n                        ______  __________________                            \n.----------------+-----/    \ \/ / __/_  __/ / __/----------+----------------.\n|                |----/ / / /\  /\  /-/ /-/ / /----[ /X ]---|                |\n|       /\    /\ |-EmP\/ / /-/ /\  /-/ /-/ /\ \--[ PoWeR! ]-|       /\    /\ |\n|      /C \  /!/ +-----\/\/--\/--\/--\/--\/--\/-------------+      /C \  /!/ |\n|     /i/\ \/R/  |                                          |     /i/\ \/R/  |\n|    /T/  \ E/   |   EMPiRE   -   KiLLRAVEN  -   TYCOON     |    /T/  \ E/   |\n|   /S/   /W \   |                                          |   /S/   /W \   |\n|  /Y/   /O/\ \  |   RE/Y\o   -   INCOGNiTO  -   HARDBYTE   |  /Y/   /O/\ \  |\n| /M/   /P/  \ \ |                                          | /M/   /P/  \ \ |\n| \/    \/    \/ |                   BoBo                   | \/    \/    \/ |\n|                |                                          |                |\n`----------------+----------[  MYSTiC /X PoWeR!  ]----------+----------------''\n\n\n \n\n                       ____ _________ _________ ________ _____\n            MYSTiC    /   / \\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /     MYSTiC\n                     /   /   \\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n         CONSOLE    /   / /\  /   __   \ \____   \   /   /      AMIGA\n                   /   /_/  \/   / /   /_____/   /  /   /\n       WORLD-HQ    \________X___/ /___/\________/   \__/      EURO-HQ\n      ____ _________ ________ _________ _________ _________ ________ _____\n     /    \\\  /   / \\  /   \\\  /    \\\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /\n    /   /\ \\\/   / /\\\/__   \\\/   /\ \\\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n   /   / /  \/   / /  \   /   / /   _____\/   / /  \ \____   \   /   /\n  /   /_/   /   /_/   /  /   / /   /     /   /_/   /_____/   /  /   /\n  \________X_________/   \__/  \__/      \________/\________/   \__/\n\n\nAMIGA - SNES - MEGADRIVE - GAMEBOY - GAMEGEAR - PC ENGINE - LYNX NEOGEO - C64\n\n4.5 Gb ONLINE             SYSOP:  EMPiRE / MYSTiC               4.5 Gb ONLINE\n\nHOLLAND:              +31-72-157682  &  +31-72-201182               :HOLLAND\n\n','\n',char(10)),'{"ACCESS":"010","LOCATION":"DOORS:EmP_Tools/DlDir","PRIORITY":"0","STACK":"50000","TYPE":"XIM"}','auto-added',17379,2,0,NULL,NULL,'emp_dd21','scan',1780048498);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('emp_jc34','EMP-JC34.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/EMP-JC34.LHA','JoinCnf','XIM','JoinCnf',NULL,NULL,'EMP','|                   The already ''Famous''   | ¦_/\___/\___/\___   JoinCnf got better!!   ¦ |\   /\ __/_  __/                          |',replace('+[ MYSTiC /X-DESiGN ]----[ JoinCnf 3.4 ]---+\n|                   The already ''Famous''   |\n¦_/\___/\___/\___   JoinCnf got better!!   ¦\n|\   /\ __/_  __/                          |\n|/ Y \/_.¬\/  \  Added Features:           |\n/  | \\¬¦ \\  \\ o Better Password Handling|\n\__l_//___//__// o Relative.Cnf BUG-Fix    ¦\n|dL!\/   \/  \/  o Better Command-Line     |\n+-[ EMPiRE/MYSTiC ]-[ XIM - /X 2.xx/3.xx ]-+\n','\n',char(10)),'JoinCnf.DOC',replace('\n                  ###### ###   ## ######  ### ######  ######              \n    _______|\     ###    #### ### ###  ## ### ###  ## ###       /|________\n   |         >    ####   ######## ######  ### ######  ####     <         |\n   | |¯¯¯¯¯|/     ###    ### # ## ###     ### #####   ###       \|¯¯¯¯¯| |\n   | |            ###### ###   ## ###     ### ### ##  ######           | |\n   | |_________________________________________________________________| |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |                            JoinCnf V3.4                             |\n   |                            ~~~~~~~~~~~~                             |\n   |                             TYPE:  XIM                              |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |                   ©1994  EMPiRE / MYSTiC /X-DESiGN                  |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |       LaSt OuTpOsT  +31-72-157682 & +31-72-201182  -  HOLLAND       |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |       AmixNet:  @NL0004   -   /X-Fido:  44:100/102     EMPiRE       |\n   |_____________________________________________________________________|\n\n===============================================================================\n\n PURPOSE:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  o This DOOR is a alternative to the ''j'' command in /X 2.xx/3.xx\n\n  o At the moment i''m using 36 Conferences and I don''t want Users\n    to ''See'' Conferences where they don''t have Access to, like the Private\n    Conferences.\n\n  o SplitUp between the File and the NetMail Conferences (optional)\n\n  o Passwords Checking for Access to Private Conferences.\n    If a User fails to enter the right Conference Password it will be Logged\n    in DOORS:Doors.LOG and/or he/she will be Kicked-Off (optional)\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n REQUIREMENTS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  Ami Express 2.xx/3.xx\n  It Doesn''t work with Ami Express 1.1x!!\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n INSTALLATION:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  o Copy the EmP_Tools Directory to DOORS:\n  o Make sure ENV: exists! (standard with OS2.x+)\n  o Merge your NodeX/JoinConf.txt with my DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf.CFG and\n    Edit it to your Needs. (Check the Existing JoinCnf.CFG!)\n\n JoinCnf.CFG LayOut:\n\n##[ start: cut here ]#########################################################\nLOGFILE            Doors:Doors.LOG  ; Log File Name for Password Failures\nDUAL_LISTING       YES              ; Use 2 Listings [YES/NO]\nCURRENT_CNF        YES              ; Show Current Conferences [YES/NO]\nCNF_NAMES          NO               ; Use Names from ConfConfig.info Icon [/X 3.x]\nPASSWORD_LEVEL     255              ; Password Protection Below Level\nNETMAIL_STARTCNF   18               ; First NetMail Conference Number\nDROP_DTR           3                ; Drop Carrier after 3 Password Failures\n[Init_END] ====================================================================\nCNF_NAME.1 =...................... NEW USER\nCNF_NAME.2 =......................... AMiGA\nCNF_NAME.3 =............... AMiGA <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.4 =................ SNES <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.5 =........ MEGADRIVE/MS <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.6 =............. GAMEBOY <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.7 =........... GAME GEAR <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.8 =........... PC-ENGINE <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.9 =................ LYNX <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.10=.................. PC <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.11=................. C64 <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.12=...................... REQUESTS\nCNF_NAME.13=.................... /X SUPPORT\nCNF_NAME.14=.......... EmP /X BeTaTeST SiTe\nCNF_NAME.15=.............. MYSTiC <PRiVATE>\nCNF_PASS.15=PASSWORD\nCNF_NAME.16=.............. MYSTiC SCENETALK\nCNF_PASS.16=PASSWORD\nCNF_NAME.17=...................... ROM BASE\nCNF_NAME.18=[AX0001]. /X Net Administration\nCNF_NAME.19=[LS0001]......... C Programming\nCNF_NAME.20=[LS0002]....... ASM Programming\nCNF_NAME.21=[LS0003]...... REXX Programming\nCNF_NAME.22=[PN0001]............ Pirate NET\nCNF_NAME.23=[HLT001]............. DuTCH NeT\nCNF_NAME.24=[PFT001].......... Amiga Topics\n[Cnf_END]=====================================================================\n  [36m___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\ ___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\\n  \[35m_ [36m. \ \_ . \ \_ . \ \  __\\  __\ \_ . \ \  __\ \_ . \ \  . \ \  __\ \  __\\n  [35m/  |__\/  |  \/  |  \/  ._l/  ._l_/  |__\/  ._l_/  |  \/  |__\/  ._l_/__.  \\n /   ¡   \  ¡   \  |   \  | /   ¡   \  .   \  ¡   \  |   \  ¡   \  ¡   \  ¡   \\n [34m\_____  /______/__¡_  /__¡ \_____  /__¡_  /____  /__¡_  /____  /____  /____  /\n-------\/------------\/-----------\/-----\/-----\/[Ð!]-\/-----\/-----\/-----\/--\n\n[Title_END]===================================================================\n\n[34m---------------------------[  [36mNETMAIL CONFERENCES  [34m]----------------------------[0m\n\n[NetMail_END]==================================================================\n\n[34m--------------------------------------------------------------------------------[0m\n[CurCnf_END]==================================================================\n[34m--------------------------------------------------------------------------------[0m\n[EndTitle_END]================================================================\n##[ end: cut here ]###########################################################\n\n\n\n  - If ''CNF_NAMES'' is set to YES then the CNF_NAME.xx is taken from\n    the BBS:ConfConfig.info Icon [/X 3.x] or from s:Acp.Startup [/X 2.xx].\n    This makes the CNF_NAME.xx in JoinCnf.CFG obsolete.\n    It''s also possible to Mix this with the Names from JoinCnf.CFG, first\n    the Names from BBS:ConfConfig.info or s:Acp.Startup are read and then\n    the names from JoinCnf.CFG are written over the old ones.\n  - CNF_NAME.x can''t be longer the 80 Characters.\n  - CNF_PASS.x can''t be longer the 40 Characters.\n\n\n[Init_END] ====================================================================\nCNF_NAME.2 =........... AMiGA PUBLIC DOMAIN\nCNF_PASS.15=PASSWORD\nCNF_PASS.16=PASSWORD\nCNF_NAME.18=[AX0001]. /X Net Administration\nCNF_NAME.19=[LS0001]......... C Programming\nCNF_NAME.20=[LS0002]....... ASM Programming\nCNF_NAME.21=[LS0003]...... REXX Programming\nCNF_NAME.22=[PN0001]............ Pirate NET\nCNF_NAME.23=[HLT001]............. DuTCH NeT\nCNF_NAME.24=[PFT001].......... Amiga Topics\n[Cnf_END]=====================================================================\n\n\n  - If you don''t want to Use the ''PASSWORD_LEVEL'' Option then set is to a\n    value greater than 255, this way Everyone has to enter the  Private \n    Conference Password.\n\n  - For now I expect that all Conferences above ''NETMAIL_STARTCNF'' are\n    NetMail Conferences so if this isn''t the case or you don''t want to use\n    the File/NetMail Splitup then set NETMAIL_STARTCNF to 0\n\n  - Set ''DROP_DTR'' to the number of Password-tries before the User is Kicked.\n    He/she will not be able to use JoinCnf until ENV:JC-PWFAIL.{SLotNr} has\n    been deleted (for example after a SystemReset).\n    Use ''0'' to disable this option.\n\n\n  AE 2.xx:\n  ~~~~~~~~\n  - Append/Join the BBS.cmd to BBS:Commands/BBS.cmd\n\n    Or add:            111111111122222222223333333333  <-- Position!\n              123456789012345678901234567890123456789\n\n              *J        XM001DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf\n              *<        XM001DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf\n              *>        XM001DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf\n\n  - If you want ''Relative Conferences'' then you must add the following\n    to your s:ACP.Startup:\n\n      NODE* RELATIVE_CONFERENCES\n\n    This way:  ______X__  -> j 1 !!! Now it looks a bit better when a user\n    hasn''t got access to all Conferences.\n\n  - If you are still using the ''LOADCONFIG'' option in ACP.Startup make sure\n    that you Include all ConferenceNames in JoinCnf.CFG coz i don''t use the\n    old config.1 files to read the Names from.\n\n\n  AE 3.xx:\n  ~~~~~~~~\n  - Copy J.info,<.info & >.info to BBS:Commands/BBScmd/\n\n    Or add to J.info, <.info & >.info ICON ToolType Info:\n\n      ACCESS=001\n      LOCATION=DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf\n      MULTINODE=YES\n      PRIORITY=0\n      STACK=50000\n      TYPE=XIM\n\n  - If you want ''Relative Conferences'' then you add the following to\n    BBS:CONFCONFIG.INFO IconToolType Info: \n\n      RELATIVE_CONFERENCES\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n USAGE:\n ~~~~~~\n  Just enter J, < or > on the MAIN screen in AmiExpress.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n ERROR MESSAGES:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\n  o ERROR:  DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf.CFG Not Found !!!\n  |\n  `--> JoinCnf.CFG can''t be found in DOORS:EmP_Tools !?\n\n  o ERROR:  BBS:ConfConfig Not Found !!!\n  |\n  `--> BBS:ConfConfig can''t be found in BBS:                         [/X 3.xx]\n\n  o ERROR:  JoinCnf 3.0 ONLY Works with /X 2.x/3.x!!\n  |\n  `--> Obvious\n\n  o ERROR:  UnKnown Keyword: XXXXXXXX in JoinCnf.CFG\n  |\n  `--> There must be 7 different lines in the INIT part (JoinCnf.CFG)\n       Compare this with the original JoinCnf.CFG\n       Check that the KeyWords are:\n\n         LOGFILE\n         DUAL_LISTINGS\n         CURRENT_CNF\n         CNF_NAMES\n         PASSWORD_LEVEL\n         NETMAIL_STARTCNF\n         DROP_DTR\n\n  o ERROR:  CNF_NAME part of JoinCnf.CFG is NOT Valid!!\n  |\n  `--> Check that all CNF_NAME.xx are correct and that xx is a Valid Conf.\n       The Line has to start with CNF_NAME.xx=  (example: CNF_NAME.1 =)\n\n  o ERROR:  CNF_PASS part of JoinCnf.CFG is NOT Valid!!\n  |\n  `--> Check that all CNF_PASS.x are correct and that x is a Valid Conference.\n       The Line has to start with CNF_PASS.xx=  (example: CNF_PASS.1 =)\n\n  o ERROR:  BBS:Access/AREA.x Not Found!!                            [/X 3.xx]\n  |\n  `--> An User got an ConferencesAccess (x) that doesn''t exist (anymore)!!\n\n  o ERROR:  ToolType NCONFS doesn''t Exist in BBS:ConfConfig.info!!\n  |\n  `--> Should/Will never happen... /X Wouldn''t have started!         [/X 3.xx]\n\n  o ERROR:  CNF_PASS.x in JoinCnf.CFG is LONGER then 40 Characters!!!\n  |\n  `--> Edit CNF_PASS.x password to a smaller one (< 40 Characters)!\n\n  o ERROR:  Only 256 Conferences are Supported for now!!!\n  |\n  `--> Contact EMPiRE and Blackmail/Bribe him in making a ''Bigger'' version.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n BUG REPORT & SUGGESTIONS:                                                                   \n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  It''s possible that a mistake will occur.... Nobody is Perfect.\n  If you have any Suggestions or Comments Please Leave a Message on my Board:\n\n   LaSt OuTpOsT  N1: +31-72-157682  N2: +31-72-201182  -  HOLLAND\n   ~~~~~~~~~~~~  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~     ~~~~~~~\n       AmixNet:  @NL0004   -   /X-Fido:  44:100/102   EMPiRE\n       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~   ~~~~~~\n\n===============================================================================\n\n NOTE:\n ~~~~~\n  o This Program Has been tested on a /X 2.0 - /X 3.32 Configuration.\n  o If you don''t use Conference Passwords then the < and > references are\n    not necessary and can be left out if you want, because the internal\n    < and > are always a bit faster.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n REGISTRATION:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  If YOU use this Program please let me know by Calling Last Outpost or\n  to send me a Message by NetMail @NL0004 - EMPiRE  Coz I want to know\n  how many Sysops are using my Tools!!!\n \n\n===============================================================================\n\n HISTORY:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  V1.0  07-05-92 - Release Version\n\n  V1.1  07-15-92 - Improved Password Checking\n\n  V1.2  07-28-92 - The internal j(oin) command can be changed.\n                 - Relative Conferences: ConfAccess _______X_ -> j 1 !!!\n\n  V1.3  07-29-92 - Made ''Relative Conferences'' selectable. Default OFF\n                 - Improved Conference selection.\n\n  V1.4  08-03-92 - Default ''Relative Conferences'' is YES. As it''s now Default\n                   in /X 2.1 [1 Aug''92]\n                 - Moved ''Relative Conferences'' to the Config file\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Bug Removed:\n                    o The Door Failed when e.g ''J 1abcd'' was entered.\n                    o Minor Bug removed in the Password Protection.\n\n  V1.5  08-23-92 - Changed the ColorLayout a bit.\n\n  V1.6  09-11-92 - Request Update for THC/DESiRE: 2 Conference Lists.\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n\n  V1.7  10-21-92 - Small Improvements.\n\n  V1.8  01-23-93 - Small Improvements in the 2 Conference Lists.\n\n  V1.9  01-25-93 - Bug Report from JPS (Tnx!):\n                    o 1 Cnf List Failed, 1 Line of Code was Accidentally\n                      Killed from the Source.... \n\n  V1.10 02-22-93 - Changed Layout a bit\n\n  V2.ß  04-22-93 - /X 3.1 UPDATE!!! - It''s Still BETA coz I Just got /X 3.1\n                   2 Hours ago!\n        04-29-93 - Added < and > Support because of Password Protection\n        05-03-93 - Better Error Checking (''Fool'' Proof?!)\n        05-04-93 - Changed whole Config (JoinCnf.CFG) Layout!!\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Added ''Current Confererence'' Option\n        05-06-93 - Bug Report from Aragorn/Vision (Tnx!):\n                    o Rexx claims that 2a,2b,.. exist between 1 to 20 (dec)\n\n  V2.0  05-09-93 - Release Version\n\n  V2.1  05-10-93 - Minor Update. The date in Doors.LOG file was wrong.\n        05-20-93 - Added ''Password Level'', this way the Sysop/CoSysop doesn''t\n                   need to enter a Conferences Password eachtime he Joins\n                   that Conference. (Thanx to Alcoholic for the Idea!!)\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n        05-27-93 - Fixed the UpperCase Problem in JoinCnf.CFG and all used\n                   Icon Files [/X 3.x]\n\n  V2.2  06-18-93 - Updated the ''<'' & ''>'' code.\n        06-21-93 - Fixed a small Error found with the RexxDoorDebugger.\n        06-28-93 - Added the ErrorMessages in the doc.\n\n  V2.3  07-23-93 - Better Error handling\n\n  V3.ß1 08-10-93 - At last the XIM Version! Rexx is a thing of the Past!\n                 - Changed ALL References from AIM to XIM !!!\n                 - Keywords ''NRSPACES'', ''RELATIVE_CONFERENCE'', ''NCONFS'' are\n                   deleted from JoinCnf.CFG (Obsolete) because they are now\n                   ''read'' from the BBS:ConfConfig.info [/X 3.x] or\n                   s:ACP.Startup [/X 2.xx]\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n\n  V3.ß2 08-13-93 - Keyword ''NRLINES'' is deleted from JoinCnf.CFG.\n                 - Keyword ''NETMAIL_STARTCNF'' added to JoinCnf.CFG\n                   If there are NetMail Conferences then the Conference\n                   showing can be split in 2 (Files & Netmail).\n                 - Renamed Keyword ''2_LISTINGS'' to ''DUAL_LISTING''\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Included Optimized 030 Version!\n\n  V3.ß3 08-15-93 - Added the ''LOGFILE'' Option. Password Failures on Private\n                   Conferences will be logged in DOORS:Doors.LOG (default)\n                 - Added the ''CNF_NAMES'' Keyword (was ''CONFCONFIG_NAMES'')\n                   Now ConferenceNames from BBS:ConfConfig tooltype [/X 3.x]\n                   (or s:ACP.Startup [/X 2.xx]) and CNF_NAME.xx from\n                   JoinCnf.CFG can be Mixed in the Output.\n\n  V3.ß4 08-23-93 - Optimized the code and it''s now about 2 kb smaller\n                 - Bug Report from Beast (Tnx!):\n                    o JoinCnf failed completely when the OLD ''LOADCONFIG''\n                      option in ACP.Startup was used [/X 2.xx]\n                 - DEBUG OPTION: enter !% as Conference and you get some info\n                                 when all goes wrong.\n\n  V3.0  08-26-93 - Release Version.\n                 - All Debug routines removed.\n                 - Optimized 030 Version Included.\n\n  V3.1  09-04-93 - Bug Reports from ByteAndi/MST:\n                    o The Last conference couldn''t be joined when Not using\n                      ''RELATIVE_CONFERENCE'' (only happend in the Menu)\n        09-05-93 -  o Sometimes when only entering ''J'' a Conference would be\n                      joined. This is due to a BUG in /X... Fixed for now!\n        09-11-93 - Release Version.\n\n  V3.2  10-02-93 - /X 3.11 Update... AE Returned a different VersionNumber\n                   then in previous versions... this made JoinCnf think that\n                   it wasn''t /X 3.xx but /X 2.xx... FIXED!\n\n  V3.3ß 10-09-93 - Bug Report from ByteAndi/MST:\n                    o Conference Passwords bigger than 80 Chars would Halted\n                      the system! The maximum CNF_PASS size is now 40 Chars.\n                 - Updated some more code. Needs Lesser memory to run.\n                 - Better Error checking.\n        10-14-93 - Bug Report from Sal1/TRSI:\n                    o /X 2.xx & NOT using ''RELATIVE_CONFERENCE'' still failed.\n                      Should be fixed now!\n                 - When the KeyWord ''LOADCONFIG'' is used in the Acp.Startup\n                   [/X 2.xx] then the ''Current Conference'' Option will be\n                   Disabled! \n                 - Some more Code-Optimizing.\n\n  V3.3  12-01-93 - Release Version\n                 - Included an Optimized 020 Version for A1200/A4000 Users.\n                 - Just replace the Old JoinCnf with the New One!\n\n  V3.4ß 01-16-93 - Bug Report from Sal1/TRSI:\n                    o When Using /X 3.20+ & ''NETMAIL_STARTCNF  0'' & Not Using\n                      ''RELATIVE_CONFERENCE'' the Conferences were not shown.\n                 - Added ''DROP_DTR'' option, it Drops the Carrier of the\n                   User after he/she fails to enter the Right Password for\n                   a number of times. After this he/she won''t be able to use\n                   JoinCnf until the file ENV:JC_PWFAIL.{SlotNr} doesn''t exist\n                   anymore (for example after a systemreset). When he tries\n                   he will be Kicked-Off right away!\n        01-17-94 - Bug Report from Mike Hoffren:\n                    o Using 108 Conferences will Crash the System!\n                      In the past it was ''Only'' possible to use 100 Confs,\n                      I never thought there would be more than that!\n                      It''s been Fixed to 256 Conferences for now\n                 - When JoinCnf is run, it will ShowUp on the Server.\n        01-21-94 - Change the Command-Line a bit.\n                 - JoinCnf will now make Use of the full 80 Chars/Line, this\n                   means that JoinCnf.CFG Changed a bit!\n        01-25-94 - Fixed the ''10-Node'' BUG.\n\n  V3.4  01-29-94 - Release Version.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n EMPiRE /X DOORS/UTILS COLLECTION UNTIL NOW!:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - AboStats V0.2                        AIM Door\n  - AutoOnline V1.1                      XIM Door/Util\n  - Bulls V2.0                           XIM Door\n  - CheckNU V1.0                         XIM Door\n  - ConfAccess V1.1                      ServerUtil\n  - CopyDate V1.3                        CLI Util\n  - DlDir V2.0                           XIM Door\n  - DoorKIT 1.01                         XIM Doors/Includes\n  - DMenu V1.0                           AIM Door\n  - DutchNET V1.0  [AmiXnet]             AIM Door\n  - Front V2.0                           XIM Door\n  - JoinCnf V3.4                         XIM Door\n  - LastCalls & TopUps V1.2              LogOffUtil\n  - LastDL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - LastUL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - MakeFree V1.1                        AIM Door\n  - MemberList V1.0                      AIM Door\n  - MonthTopUp V2.0                      ServerUtil\n  - MultiNodeLastCaller V1.1             LogOffUtil\n  - Page V2.0                            XIM Door\n  - PMail V1.1                           AIM Door\n  - Remote Maintenance V1.3              AIM Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.3                 AIM Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.2 [Party]         CLI Door\n  - SecLevChk V1.0                       ServerUtil\n  - Sent FrondEnd 1.0  [Sent_FE]         XIM Door\n  - SysopCommands V1.0                   AIM Door\n  - UserData Cleaner/Repair V2.0         ServerUtil\n  - UserStats V2.0                       ServerUtil\n  - UsrCnfAx V1.1                        CLI Util\n  - View & Check Archive/Text V1.7       AIM Door\n  - WhoNode V1.3                         AIM Door\n\n===============================================================================\n\n THANX MUST GO TO ALL OFFICIAL BETA-TESTERS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   Tycoon/Mystic             [Channel X]\n   ByteAndi/Mystic           [Diabolo]\n   JPS                       [Red October]\n   Sal-One/TRSi              [BoonDocks]\n   Sharon/Scx                [The Unknown]\n   Blue Bird/Mystic          [Tau Ceti]\n   Easy Rider/Mystic         [Mamba]\n   Mega Fortress             [MF Enterprise]\n   Beast/Partek              [Sequencial]\n   SpeedDevil/Partek         [Rising Sun]\n\n\n GREETZ/TNX GO TO:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   ByteAndi/MST /X-DESiGN  - NewFiles is the best! And Tnx for all ya ''C''\n                             Help & Sources! You''re (almost) the Greatest!\n   Killraven/MST /X-DESiGN - For giving me sum Great Pointers/Sources on ''C''\n   CrackDown/MST /X-DESiGN - Nice Doors, Keep it up!\n   JAF/G.O.D.              - For some good REXX ideas\n   Bad Sector/Hidden       - Still Using your Sort-Routine. It''s the Fastest!\n   B.O.B./Paradox          - For (Mis)Using/Testing my tools!\n   Aragorn/Vision          - Tnx for Testing in the past\n   Camelot/Complex         - Tnx for Testing in the past\n   Telekinetic/T.F.A.      - Tnx for Testing in the past\n   Alcoholic               - Tnx for Testing in the past\n   Price/T.F.A.            - Great job on your {F}ull {S}creen {E}ditor!\n   Van Damme               - Nice Doors!!!\n   DR.G/IBB                - Hope to see Check''N Add work on a 040\n   SiGMA SEVEN             - Nice job on SiGMA-EXPRESS!\n   911/Movement            - Tnx For ya Ansi Logo''s!! You''re the Greatest!\n   Mike                    - Tnx for calling!\n   Amicom/TRSi             - Tnx for calling!\n   SkIn                    - Tnx for ya Description Logo! Looks Great!!\n   Shadow Man/S8           - Nice Doors & Tnx for ya greetz! & Calling ofcoz\n   [MAZ!^PHAZE]            - Tnx for calling!\n   Shuyuki Tanaka          - Tnx for your Messages!\n   AGONY/cOmpLeX           - Tnx for calling!\n   HUMANOiD & RU$tY/AFL    - Tnx for calling!\n   LECTOR                  - Tnx for your NetMail\n   Koo Koo Ka Choo         - Tnx for calling!\n   TaRzAN                  - Tnx for your NetMail. Yezz 040 Speed Rulez!!\n   Crayor/Scx              - Tnx for calling!\n   Foxx''n''Lynxx            - Tnx for your BugReports!!!\n   ThE BiRD                - Tnx for your NetMail on UsrCnfAx\n   Dux                     - Tnx for your NetMail\n   dR.dRE!/fAITH           - Tnx for da greetz, Nice Doors btw\n   [-RUNT&SCOTTIE-]        - Tnx for calling!\n   sPAcEcAKe/aF            - Tnx for calling!\n   Freddy Kreuger/GOD      - Tnx for your NetMail on JoinCnf 3.2\n   MF1                     - Tnx for calling!\n   AtomiX/N.A.S.A          - Hope the ''C'' archive was sume help for ya!\n   MAD HARLEQUiN [ITPH01]  - Tnx for your Nice AmiXnet Messy''s\n   Mike Hoffren  [SF0001]  - Tnx for the BugReport on JoinCnf 3.3 [100+ Cnfs]\n   Galaxy/TFA              - Nice job on da SentCleaner\n\n\n   & Ofcoz to ALL MYSTiC Members all over the Globe!\n\n\n                        ______  __________________                            \n.----------------+-----/    \ \/ / __/_  __/ / __/----------+----------------.\n|                |----/ / / /\  /\  /-/ /-/ / /----[ /X ]---|                |\n|       /\    /\ |-EmP\/ / /-/ /\  /-/ /-/ /\ \--[ DESiGN ]-|       /\    /\ |\n|      /C \  /N/ +-----\/\/--\/--\/--\/--\/--\/-------------+      /C \  /N/ |\n|     /i/\ \/G/  |                                          |     /i/\ \/G/  |\n|    /T/  \ i/   |                                          |    /T/  \ i/   |\n|   /S/   /S \   |   EMPiRE   -   BYTEANDi   -  CRACKDOWN   |   /S/   /S \   |\n|  /Y/   /E/\ \  |   TYCOON   -   KiLLRAVEN  -   SiEGEL     |  /Y/   /E/\ \  |\n| /M/   /D/  \ \ |   FUZZY    -      SPY     -  INCOGNiTO   | /M/   /D/  \ \ |\n| \/    \/    \/ |   RE/Y\o   -     BoBo     -   HARDBYTE   | \/    \/    \/ |\n|                |                                          |                |\n`----------------+----------[  MYSTiC /X DESiGN  ]----------+----------------''\n\n \n\n                       ____ _________ _________ ________ _____\n            MYSTiC    /   / \\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /     MYSTiC\n                     /   /   \\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n         CONSOLE    /   / /\  /   __   \ \____   \   /   /      AMIGA\n                   /   /_/  \/   / /   /_____/   /  /   /\n       WORLD-HQ    \________X___/ /___/\________/   \__/      EURO-HQ\n      ____ _________ ________ _________ _________ _________ ________ _____\n     /    \\\  /   / \\  /   \\\  /    \\\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /\n    /   /\ \\\/   / /\\\/__   \\\/   /\ \\\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n   /   / /  \/   / /  \   /   / /   _____\/   / /  \ \____   \   /   /\n  /   /_/   /   /_/   /  /   / /   /     /   /_/   /_____/   /  /   /\n  \________X_________/   \__/  \__/      \________/\________/   \__/\n\n\n  AMIGA - SNES - MEGADRIVE - GAMEBOY - GAMEGEAR - PC ENGINE - LYNX - C64\n\n  3.5 Gb ONLINE          SYSOP:  EMPiRE / MYSTiC           3.5 Gb ONLINE\n\n  HOLLAND:          +31-72-157682  &  +31-72-201182             :HOLLAND\n\n','\n',char(10)),'{"ACCESS":"001","LOCATION":"DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf","PRIORITY":"0","STACK":"50000","TYPE":"XIM"}','auto-added',28674,6,0,NULL,NULL,'emp_jc34','scan',1780048498);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('emp_jc35','EMP_JC35.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/EMP_JC35.LHA','JoinCnf','XIM','JoinCnf',NULL,NULL,'EMP','|                                          | ¦_/\___/\___/\___   The already ''Famous''   ¦ |\   /\ __/_  __/   JoinCnf got better!!   |',replace('+[ MYSTiC /X-DESiGN ]----[ JoinCnf 3.5 ]---+\n|                                          |\n¦_/\___/\___/\___   The already ''Famous''   ¦\n|\   /\ __/_  __/   JoinCnf got better!!   |\n|/ Y \/_.¬\/  \                            |\n/  | \\¬¦ \\  \\ Added Features:           |\n\__l_//___//__//  o Cursor-Key Handling    ¦\n|dL!\/   \/  \/   o Small Bug Fixes        |\n+-[ EMPiRE/MYSTiC ]-[ XIM - /X 2.xx/3.xx ]-+\n','\n',char(10)),'JoinCnf.DOC',replace('\n                  ###### ###   ## ######  ### ######  ######              \n    _______|\     ###    #### ### ###  ## ### ###  ## ###       /|________\n   |         >    ####   ######## ######  ### ######  ####     <         |\n   | |¯¯¯¯¯|/     ###    ### # ## ###     ### #####   ###       \|¯¯¯¯¯| |\n   | |            ###### ###   ## ###     ### ### ##  ######           | |\n   | |_________________________________________________________________| |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |                            JoinCnf V3.5                             |\n   |                            ~~~~~~~~~~~~                             |\n   |                             TYPE:  XIM                              |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |                   ©1994  EMPiRE / MYSTiC /X-DESiGN                  |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |       LaSt OuTpOsT  +31-72-157682 & +31-72-201182  -  HOLLAND       |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |       AmixNet:  @NL0004   -   /X-Fido:  44:100/102     EMPiRE       |\n   |_____________________________________________________________________|\n\n===============================================================================\n\n PURPOSE:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  o This DOOR is a alternative to the ''j'' command in /X 2.xx/3.xx\n\n  o At the moment i''m using 36 Conferences and I don''t want Users\n    to ''See'' Conferences where they don''t have Access to, like the Private\n    Conferences.\n\n  o SplitUp between the File and the NetMail Conferences (optional)\n\n  o Passwords Checking for Access to Private Conferences.\n    If a User fails to enter the right Conference Password it will be Logged\n    in DOORS:Doors.LOG and/or he/she will be Kicked-Off (optional)\n\n  o Cursor-Key control for Conference selecting.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n REQUIREMENTS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  Ami Express 2.xx/3.xx\n  It Doesn''t work with Ami Express 1.1x!!\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n INSTALLATION:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  o Copy the EmP_Tools Directory to DOORS:\n  o Make sure ENV: exists! (standard with OS2.x+)\n  o Merge your NodeX/JoinConf.txt with my DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf.CFG and\n    Edit it to your Needs. (Check the Existing JoinCnf.CFG!)\n\n JoinCnf.CFG LayOut:\n\n##[ start: cut here ]#########################################################\nLOGFILE            Doors:Doors.LOG  ; Log File Name for Password Failures\nDUAL_LISTING       YES              ; Use 2 Listings [YES/NO]\nCURRENT_CNF        YES              ; Show Current Conferences [YES/NO]\nCNF_NAMES          NO               ; Use Names from ConfConfig.info Icon [/X 3.x]\nPASSWORD_LEVEL     255              ; Password Protection Below Level\nNETMAIL_STARTCNF   18               ; First NetMail Conference Number\nDROP_DTR           3                ; Drop Carrier after 3 Password Failures\nCURSOR_KEYS        YES              ; Use the Cursors to select a Cnf [YES/NO]\n[Init_END] ====================================================================\nCNF_NAME.1 =...................... NEW USER\nCNF_NAME.2 =......................... AMiGA\nCNF_NAME.3 =............... AMiGA <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.4 =................ SNES <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.5 =........ MEGADRIVE/MS <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.6 =............. GAMEBOY <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.7 =........... GAME GEAR <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.8 =........... PC-ENGINE <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.9 =................ LYNX <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.10=.................. PC <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.11=................. C64 <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.12=...................... REQUESTS\nCNF_NAME.13=.................... /X SUPPORT\nCNF_NAME.14=. EmP & /X DESiGN BeTaTeST SiTe\nCNF_NAME.15=.............. MYSTiC <PRiVATE>\nCNF_PASS.15=PASSWORD\nCNF_NAME.16=.............. MYSTiC SCENETALK\nCNF_PASS.16=PASSWORD\nCNF_NAME.17=...................... ROM BASE\nCNF_NAME.18=[AX0001]. /X Net Administration\nCNF_NAME.19=[LS0001]......... C Programming\nCNF_NAME.20=[LS0002]....... ASM Programming\nCNF_NAME.21=[LS0003]...... REXX Programming\nCNF_NAME.22=[PN0001]............ Pirate NET\nCNF_NAME.23=[HLT001]............. DuTCH NeT\nCNF_NAME.24=[PFT001].......... Amiga Topics\nCNF_NAME.26=[MOV001].......... Movie Topics\nCNF_NAME.27=[AA0001]........... ANSI Topics\nCNF_NAME.28=[WBS001]............ Buy & Sell\nCNF_NAME.29=[CHBX01]............ ChatterBox\n[Cnf_END]=====================================================================\n  [36m___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\ ___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\\n  \[35m_ [36m. \ \_ . \ \_ . \ \  __\\  __\ \_ . \ \  __\ \_ . \ \  . \ \  __\ \  __\\n  [35m/  |__\/  |  \/  |  \/  ._l/  ._l_/  |__\/  ._l_/  |  \/  |__\/  ._l_/__.  \\n /   ¡   \  ¡   \  |   \  | /   ¡   \  .   \  ¡   \  |   \  ¡   \  ¡   \  ¡   \\n [34m\_____  /______/__¡_  /__¡ \_____  /__¡_  /____  /__¡_  /____  /____  /____  /\n-------\/------------\/-----------\/-----\/-----\/[Ð!]-\/-----\/-----\/-----\/-\n\n[Title_END]===================================================================\n\n[34m---------------------------[  [36mNETMAIL CONFERENCES  [34m]---------------------------[0m\n\n[NetMail_END]==================================================================\n\n[34m-------------------------------------------------------------------------------[0m\n[CurCnf_END]==================================================================\n[34m-------------------------------------------------------------------------------[0m\n[EndTitle_END]================================================================\n##[ end: cut here ]###########################################################\n\n\n\n  - If ''CNF_NAMES'' is set to YES then the CNF_NAME.xx is taken from\n    the BBS:ConfConfig.info Icon [/X 3.x] or from s:Acp.Startup [/X 2.xx].\n    This makes the CNF_NAME.xx in JoinCnf.CFG obsolete.\n    It''s also possible to Mix this with the Names from JoinCnf.CFG, first\n    the Names from BBS:ConfConfig.info or s:Acp.Startup are read and then\n    the names from JoinCnf.CFG are written over the old ones.\n  - CNF_NAME.x can''t be longer the 80 Characters.\n  - CNF_PASS.x can''t be longer the 40 Characters.\n\n\n[Init_END] ====================================================================\nCNF_NAME.2 =........... AMiGA PUBLIC DOMAIN\nCNF_PASS.15=PASSWORD\nCNF_PASS.16=PASSWORD\nCNF_NAME.18=[AX0001]. /X Net Administration\nCNF_NAME.19=[LS0001]......... C Programming\nCNF_NAME.20=[LS0002]....... ASM Programming\nCNF_NAME.21=[LS0003]...... REXX Programming\nCNF_NAME.22=[PN0001]............ Pirate NET\nCNF_NAME.23=[HLT001]............. DuTCH NeT\nCNF_NAME.24=[PFT001].......... Amiga Topics\n[Cnf_END]=====================================================================\n\n\n  - If you don''t want to Use the ''PASSWORD_LEVEL'' Option then set is to a\n    value greater than 255, this way Everyone has to enter the Private \n    Conference Password.\n\n  - For now I expect that all Conferences above ''NETMAIL_STARTCNF'' are\n    NetMail Conferences so if this isn''t the case or you don''t want to use\n    the File/NetMail Splitup then set NETMAIL_STARTCNF to 0\n\n  - Set ''DROP_DTR'' to the number of Password-tries before the User is Kicked.\n    He/she will not be able to use JoinCnf until ENV:JC-PWFAIL.{SLotNr} has\n    been deleted (for example after a SystemReset).\n    Use ''0'' to disable this option.\n\n  - Set ''CURSOR_KEYS'' to ''YES'' if you want to use the Cursor-Keys to select a\n    Conference. Keep in mind that it only will work if the Output-Screen\n    ''fits'' in the Users TerminalScreen!\n    Set it to ''NO'' to disable this option.\n\n\n  AE 2.xx:\n  ~~~~~~~~\n  - Append/Join the BBS.cmd to BBS:Commands/BBS.cmd\n\n    Or add:            111111111122222222223333333333  <-- Position!\n              123456789012345678901234567890123456789\n\n              *J        XM001DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf\n              *<        XM001DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf\n              *>        XM001DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf\n\n  - If you want ''Relative Conferences'' then you must add the following\n    to your s:ACP.Startup:\n\n      NODE* RELATIVE_CONFERENCES\n\n    This way:  ______X__  -> j 1 !!! Now it looks a bit better when a user\n    hasn''t got access to all Conferences.\n\n  - If you are still using the ''LOADCONFIG'' option in ACP.Startup make sure\n    that you Include all ConferenceNames in JoinCnf.CFG coz i don''t use the\n    old config.1 files to read the Names from.\n\n\n  AE 3.xx:\n  ~~~~~~~~\n  - Copy J.info,<.info & >.info to BBS:Commands/BBScmd/\n\n    Or add to J.info, <.info & >.info ICON ToolType Info:\n\n      ACCESS=001\n      LOCATION=DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf\n      MULTINODE=YES\n      PRIORITY=0\n      STACK=50000\n      TYPE=XIM\n\n  - If you want ''Relative Conferences'' then you add the following to\n    BBS:CONFCONFIG.INFO IconToolType Info: \n\n      RELATIVE_CONFERENCES\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n USAGE:\n ~~~~~~\n  Just enter J, < or > on the MAIN screen in AmiExpress.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n ERROR MESSAGES:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\n  o ERROR:  DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf.CFG Not Found !!!\n  |\n  `--> JoinCnf.CFG can''t be found in DOORS:EmP_Tools !?\n\n  o ERROR:  BBS:ConfConfig Not Found !!!\n  |\n  `--> BBS:ConfConfig can''t be found in BBS:                         [/X 3.xx]\n\n  o ERROR:  JoinCnf 3.0 ONLY Works with /X 2.x/3.x!!\n  |\n  `--> Obvious\n\n  o ERROR:  UnKnown Keyword: XXXXXXXX in JoinCnf.CFG\n  |\n  `--> There must be 8 different KeyWords in the INIT part (JoinCnf.CFG)\n       Compare this with the original JoinCnf.CFG\n       Check that the KeyWords are:\n\n         LOGFILE\n         DUAL_LISTINGS\n         CURRENT_CNF\n         CNF_NAMES\n         PASSWORD_LEVEL\n         NETMAIL_STARTCNF\n         DROP_DTR\n         CURSOR_KEYS\n\n  o ERROR:  CNF_NAME part of JoinCnf.CFG is NOT Valid!!\n  |\n  `--> Check that all CNF_NAME.xx are correct and that xx is a Valid Conf.\n       The Line has to start with CNF_NAME.xx=  (example: CNF_NAME.1 =)\n\n  o ERROR:  CNF_PASS part of JoinCnf.CFG is NOT Valid!!\n  |\n  `--> Check that all CNF_PASS.x are correct and that x is a Valid Conference.\n       The Line has to start with CNF_PASS.xx=  (example: CNF_PASS.1 =)\n\n  o ERROR:  BBS:Access/AREA.x Not Found!!                            [/X 3.xx]\n  |\n  `--> An User got an ConferencesAccess (x) that doesn''t exist (anymore)!!\n\n  o ERROR:  ToolType NCONFS doesn''t Exist in BBS:ConfConfig.info!!\n  |\n  `--> Should/Will never happen... /X Wouldn''t have started!         [/X 3.xx]\n\n  o ERROR:  CNF_PASS.x in JoinCnf.CFG is LONGER then 40 Characters!!!\n  |\n  `--> Edit CNF_PASS.x password to a smaller one (< 40 Characters)!\n\n  o ERROR:  Only 100 Conferences are Supported for now!!!\n  |\n  `--> Contact EMPiRE and Blackmail/Bribe him in making a ''Bigger'' version.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n BUG REPORT & SUGGESTIONS:                                                                   \n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  It''s possible that a mistake will occur.... Nobody is Perfect.\n  If you have any Suggestions or Comments Please Leave a Message on my Board:\n\n   LaSt OuTpOsT  N1: +31-72-157682  N2: +31-72-201182  -  HOLLAND\n   ~~~~~~~~~~~~  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~     ~~~~~~~\n       AmixNet:  @NL0004   -   /X-Fido:  44:100/102   EMPiRE\n       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~   ~~~~~~\n\n===============================================================================\n\n NOTE:\n ~~~~~\n  o This Program Has been tested on a /X 2.0 - /X 3.36 Configuration.\n  o If you don''t use Conference Passwords then the < and > references are\n    not necessary and can be left out if you want, because the internal\n    < and > are always a bit faster.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n REGISTRATION:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  If YOU use this Program please let me know by Calling Last Outpost or\n  to send me a Message by NetMail @NL0004 - EMPiRE  Coz I want to know\n  how many Sysops are using my Tools!!!\n \n\n===============================================================================\n\n HISTORY:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  V1.0  07-05-92 - Release Version\n\n  V1.1  07-15-92 - Improved Password Checking\n\n  V1.2  07-28-92 - The internal j(oin) command can be changed.\n                 - Relative Conferences: ConfAccess _______X_ -> j 1 !!!\n\n  V1.3  07-29-92 - Made ''Relative Conferences'' selectable. Default OFF\n                 - Improved Conference selection.\n\n  V1.4  08-03-92 - Default ''Relative Conferences'' is YES. As it''s now Default\n                   in /X 2.1 [1 Aug''92]\n                 - Moved ''Relative Conferences'' to the Config file\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Bug Removed:\n                    o The Door Failed when e.g ''J 1abcd'' was entered.\n                    o Minor Bug removed in the Password Protection.\n\n  V1.5  08-23-92 - Changed the ColorLayout a bit.\n\n  V1.6  09-11-92 - Request Update for THC/DESiRE: 2 Conference Lists.\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n\n  V1.7  10-21-92 - Small Improvements.\n\n  V1.8  01-23-93 - Small Improvements in the 2 Conference Lists.\n\n  V1.9  01-25-93 - Bug Report from JPS (Tnx!):\n                    o 1 Cnf List Failed, 1 Line of Code was Accidentally\n                      Killed from the Source.... \n\n  V1.10 02-22-93 - Changed Layout a bit\n\n  V2.ß  04-22-93 - /X 3.1 UPDATE!!! - It''s Still BETA coz I Just got /X 3.1\n                   2 Hours ago!\n        04-29-93 - Added < and > Support because of Password Protection\n        05-03-93 - Better Error Checking (''Fool'' Proof?!)\n        05-04-93 - Changed whole Config (JoinCnf.CFG) Layout!!\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Added ''Current Confererence'' Option\n        05-06-93 - Bug Report from Aragorn/Vision (Tnx!):\n                    o Rexx claims that 2a,2b,.. exist between 1 to 20 (dec)\n\n  V2.0  05-09-93 - Release Version\n\n  V2.1  05-10-93 - Minor Update. The date in Doors.LOG file was wrong.\n        05-20-93 - Added ''Password Level'', this way the Sysop/CoSysop doesn''t\n                   need to enter a Conferences Password eachtime he Joins\n                   that Conference. (Thanx to Alcoholic for the Idea!!)\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n        05-27-93 - Fixed the UpperCase Problem in JoinCnf.CFG and all used\n                   Icon Files [/X 3.x]\n\n  V2.2  06-18-93 - Updated the ''<'' & ''>'' code.\n        06-21-93 - Fixed a small Error found with the RexxDoorDebugger.\n        06-28-93 - Added the ErrorMessages in the doc.\n\n  V2.3  07-23-93 - Better Error handling\n\n  V3.ß1 08-10-93 - At last the XIM Version! Rexx is a thing of the Past!\n                 - Changed ALL References from AIM to XIM !!!\n                 - Keywords ''NRSPACES'', ''RELATIVE_CONFERENCE'', ''NCONFS'' are\n                   deleted from JoinCnf.CFG (Obsolete) because they are now\n                   ''read'' from the BBS:ConfConfig.info [/X 3.x] or\n                   s:ACP.Startup [/X 2.xx]\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n\n  V3.ß2 08-13-93 - Keyword ''NRLINES'' is deleted from JoinCnf.CFG.\n                 - Keyword ''NETMAIL_STARTCNF'' added to JoinCnf.CFG\n                   If there are NetMail Conferences then the Conference\n                   showing can be split in 2 (Files & Netmail).\n                 - Renamed Keyword ''2_LISTINGS'' to ''DUAL_LISTING''\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Included Optimized 030 Version!\n\n  V3.ß3 08-15-93 - Added the ''LOGFILE'' Option. Password Failures on Private\n                   Conferences will be logged in DOORS:Doors.LOG (default)\n                 - Added the ''CNF_NAMES'' Keyword (was ''CONFCONFIG_NAMES'')\n                   Now ConferenceNames from BBS:ConfConfig tooltype [/X 3.x]\n                   (or s:ACP.Startup [/X 2.xx]) and CNF_NAME.xx from\n                   JoinCnf.CFG can be Mixed in the Output.\n\n  V3.ß4 08-23-93 - Optimized the code and it''s now about 2 kb smaller\n                 - Bug Report from Beast (Tnx!):\n                    o JoinCnf failed completely when the OLD ''LOADCONFIG''\n                      option in ACP.Startup was used [/X 2.xx]\n                 - DEBUG OPTION: enter !% as Conference and you get some info\n                                 when all goes wrong.\n\n  V3.0  08-26-93 - Release Version.\n                 - All Debug routines removed.\n                 - Optimized 030 Version Included.\n\n  V3.1  09-04-93 - Bug Reports from ByteAndi/MST:\n                    o The Last conference couldn''t be joined when Not using\n                      ''RELATIVE_CONFERENCE'' (only happend in the Menu)\n        09-05-93 -  o Sometimes when only entering ''J'' a Conference would be\n                      joined. This is due to a BUG in /X... Fixed for now!\n        09-11-93 - Release Version.\n\n  V3.2  10-02-93 - /X 3.11 Update... AE Returned a different VersionNumber\n                   then in previous versions... this made JoinCnf think that\n                   it wasn''t /X 3.xx but /X 2.xx... FIXED!\n\n  V3.3ß 10-09-93 - Bug Report from ByteAndi/MST:\n                    o Conference Passwords bigger than 80 Chars would Halted\n                      the system! The maximum CNF_PASS size is now 40 Chars.\n                 - Updated some more code. Needs Lesser memory to run.\n                 - Better Error checking.\n        10-14-93 - Bug Report from Sal1/TRSI:\n                    o /X 2.xx & NOT using ''RELATIVE_CONFERENCE'' still failed.\n                      Should be fixed now!\n                 - When the KeyWord ''LOADCONFIG'' is used in the Acp.Startup\n                   [/X 2.xx] then the ''Current Conference'' Option will be\n                   Disabled! \n                 - Some more Code-Optimizing.\n\n  V3.3  12-01-93 - Release Version\n                 - Included an Optimized 020 Version for A1200/A4000 Users.\n                 - Just replace the Old JoinCnf with the New One!\n\n  V3.4ß 01-16-93 - Bug Report from Sal1/TRSI:\n                    o When Using /X 3.20+ & ''NETMAIL_STARTCNF  0'' & Not Using\n                      ''RELATIVE_CONFERENCE'' the Conferences were not shown.\n                 - Added ''DROP_DTR'' option, it Drops the Carrier of the\n                   User after he/she fails to enter the Right Password for\n                   a number of times. After this he/she won''t be able to use\n                   JoinCnf until the file ENV:JC_PWFAIL.{SlotNr} doesn''t exist\n                   anymore (for example after a systemreset). When he tries\n                   he will be Kicked-Off right away!\n        01-17-94 - Bug Report from Mike Hoffren:\n                    o Using 108 Conferences will Crash the System!\n                      In the past it was ''Only'' possible to use 100 Confs,\n                      I never thought there would be more than that!\n                      It''s been Fixed to 256 Conferences for now\n                 - When JoinCnf is run, it will ShowUp on the Server.\n        01-21-94 - Change the Command-Line a bit.\n                 - JoinCnf will now make Use of the full 80 Chars/Line, this\n                   means that LayOut of JoinCnf.CFG Changed a bit!\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n        01-25-94 - Fixed the ''10-Node'' BUG. Using Node 10+ would result in\n                   JoinCnf thinking it was started from Node 1.... FIXED!\n\n  V3.4  01-29-94 - Release Version.\n\n  V3.5ß 02-01-94 - Change the ''Full Use of 80 Chars/Line'' to 79 Chars/Line\n                   because some Terminals on Amiga/PC can''t handle the 80th\n                   character on the right (extra linefeeds)!\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Took out all 100+ Conferencing coz /X-Fido is on it''s way\n                   now (so no need for 100+ Conferences) and otherwise the\n                   Code becomes too big! A 100+ Version Can be REQUESTED!\n        02-06-94 - Changed the CommandLine-Input so only Numbers can be\n                   selected.\n                 - Added some BETA-Code to use the Cursor-Keys to select a\n                   Conference. The only BIG problem exists when there are too\n                   many Conferences and the Screen starts scrolling....\n        02-09-94 - Updated the Cursor-Key Code so it''s now possible to Use\n                   Cursor-Keys only if the JoinCnf output-screen fits in the\n                   Users terminalscreen (without scrolling). If it doesn''t\n                   ''fit'' then the Cursor-Keys are disabled otherwise it would\n                   mess up the screen!\n                 - Added the ''CURSOR_KEYS'' KeyWord, when it''s ''NO'' then there\n                   will never be made use of ANSI when using the Cursors.\n                   When the KeyWord is ''YES'' or is leftout then ANSI is used\n                   when the JoinCnf-Screen ''fits'' in the Users TerminalScreen.\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Note: For now the Cursor-Code works only with Relative\n                         Conferences.\n        02-16-94 - Fixed a Small Bug in the Password Handling.\n        02-20-94 - Fixed the Non-Relative Conferences problems so now it will\n                   work on ANY /X 2.xx or 3.xx configuration.\n        02-21-94 - Fixed a Bug in printing the Command-Line to Screen. It\n                   failed when there where Non-Even FileConfs & NetMailConfs.\n                 - Changed the Cursor-Handling, the ''jump'' from FileConf to \n                   NetMailConf has been changed.\n        03-08-94 - Optimized the Cursor-Key Routine.\n\n  V3.5  03-13-94 - Release Version.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n EMPiRE /X DOORS/UTILS COLLECTION UNTIL NOW!:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - AboStats V0.2                        AIM Door\n  - AE2PCE V1.0                          CLI Util\n  - AutoOnline V1.2                      XIM Door/Util\n  - Bulls V2.1                           XIM Door\n  - CheckDir V1.0                        CLI Util\n  - CheckNU V1.0                         XIM Door\n  - ConfAccess V1.1                      ServerUtil\n  - CopyDate V1.3                        CLI Util\n  - DlDir V2.0                           XIM Door\n  - DoorKIT 1.01                         XIM Doors/Includes\n  - DMenu V1.0                           AIM Door\n  - DutchNET V1.0  [AmiXnet]             AIM Door\n  - Front V2.0                           XIM Door\n  - JoinCnf V3.5                         XIM Door\n  - LastCalls & TopUps V1.2              LogOffUtil\n  - LastDL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - LastUL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - MakeFree V1.1                        AIM Door\n  - MemberList V1.0                      AIM Door\n  - MonthTopUp V2.0                      ServerUtil\n  - MultiNodeLastCaller V1.1             LogOffUtil\n  - Page V2.0                            XIM Door\n  - PMail V1.1                           AIM Door\n  - Remote Maintenance V1.3              AIM Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.3                 AIM Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.2 [Party]         CLI Door\n  - SecLevChk V1.0                       ServerUtil\n  - Sent FrondEnd 1.0  [Sent_FE]         XIM Door\n  - StripCR V1.0                         CLI Util\n  - SysopCommands V1.0                   AIM Door\n  - UserData Cleaner/Repair V2.0         ServerUtil\n  - UserStats V2.0                       ServerUtil\n  - UsrCnfAx V1.1                        CLI Util\n  - View & Check Archive/Text V1.7       AIM Door\n  - WhoNode V1.3                         AIM Door\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n THANX MUST GO TO ALL OFFICIAL BETA-TESTERS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   Tycoon/Mystic             [Channel X]\n   ByteAndi/Mystic           [Diabolo]\n   JPS                       [Red October]\n   Sal-One/TRSi              [BoonDocks]\n   Sharon/5D                 [The Unknown]\n   Blue Bird/Mystic          [Tau Ceti]\n   Easy Rider/Mystic         [Mamba]\n   Mega Fortress             [MF Enterprise]\n   Beast/Partek              [Sequencial]\n   SpeedDevil/Partek         [Rising Sun]\n   Galaxy                    [Ulixys]\n\n\n GREETZ/TNX GO TO:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   ByteAndi/MST /X-DESiGN  - NewFiles is the best! And Tnx for all ya ''C''\n                             Help & Sources! You''re (almost) the Greatest!\n   Killraven/MST /X-DESiGN - For giving me sum Great Pointers/Sources on ''C''\n   CrackDown/MST /X-DESiGN - Nice Doors, Keep it up!\n   JAF/G.O.D.              - For some good REXX ideas\n   Bad Sector/Hidden       - Still Using your Sort-Routine. It''s the Fastest!\n   B.O.B./CyberForce       - For (Mis)Using/Testing my tools! :)\n   Aragorn/Vision          - Tnx for Testing in the past\n   Camelot/Complex         - Tnx for Testing in the past\n   Telekinetic/T.F.A.      - Tnx for Testing in the past\n   Alcoholic               - Tnx for Testing in the past\n   Price/T.F.A.            - Great job on your {F}ull {S}creen {E}ditor!\n   Van Damme               - Nice Doors!!!\n   DR.G/IBB                - Hope to see Check''N Add work on a 040\n   SiGMA SEVEN             - Nice job on SiGMA-EXPRESS! Tempted to ''Convert''\n   911/Movement            - Tnx For ya Ansi Logo''s!! You''re the Greatest!\n   Mike                    - Tnx for calling!\n   Amicom/TRSi             - Tnx for calling!\n   SkIn                    - Tnx for ya Description Logo! Looks Great!!\n   Shadow Man/S8           - Nice Doors & Tnx for ya greetz! & Calling ofcoz\n   [MAZ!^PHAZE]            - Tnx for calling!\n   Shuyuki Tanaka          - Tnx for your Messages!\n   AGONY/cOmpLeX           - Tnx for calling!\n   HUMANOiD & jABBA /MST &\n    SCANDAL                - Tnx for calling!\n   LECTOR                  - Tnx for your NetMail\n   Koo Koo Ka Choo         - Tnx for calling!\n   TaRzAN                  - Tnx for your NetMail. Yezz 040 Speed Rulez!!\n   Crayor/Scx              - Tnx for calling!\n   Foxx''n''Lynxx            - Tnx for your BugReports!!!\n   ThE BiRD                - Tnx for your NetMail on UsrCnfAx\n   Dux                     - Tnx for your NetMail\n   dR.dRE!/fAITH           - Tnx for da greetz, Nice Doors btw\n   [-RUNT&SCOTTIE-]        - Tnx for calling!\n   sPAcEcAKe/aF            - Tnx for calling!\n   Freddy Kreuger/GOD      - Tnx for your NetMail on JoinCnf 3.2\n   MF1                     - Tnx for calling!\n   AtomiX/N.A.S.A          - Hope the ''C'' archive was sume help for ya!\n   MAD HARLEQUiN [ITPH01]  - Tnx for your Nice AmiXnet Messy''s\n   Mike Hoffren  [SF0001]  - Tnx for the BugReport on JoinCnf 3.3 [100+ Cnfs]\n   Galaxy/TFA              - Nice job on da SentCleaner\n   SvEN tHE CREAToR/5D     - ''Tnx'' for forcing me to use Cursor-Keys!\n   Diogenes/TPY/5D         - Tnx for calling and for your BugReport!\n\n   & Ofcoz to ALL MYSTiC Members all over the Globe!\n\n\n                        ______  __________________                            \n.----------------+-----/    \ \/ / __/_  __/ / __/----------+----------------.\n|                |----/ / / /\  /\  /-/ /-/ / /----[ /X ]---|                |\n|       /\    /\ |-EmP\/ / /-/ /\  /-/ /-/ /\ \--[ DESiGN ]-|       /\    /\ |\n|      /C \  /N/ +-----\/\/--\/--\/--\/--\/--\/-------------+      /C \  /N/ |\n|     /i/\ \/G/  |                                          |     /i/\ \/G/  |\n|    /T/  \ i/   |                                          |    /T/  \ i/   |\n|   /S/   /S \   |   EMPiRE   -   BYTEANDi   -  CRACKDOWN   |   /S/   /S \   |\n|  /Y/   /E/\ \  |   TYCOON   -   KiLLRAVEN  -   SiEGEL     |  /Y/   /E/\ \  |\n| /M/   /D/  \ \ |   FUZZY    -      SPY     -  INCOGNiTO   | /M/   /D/  \ \ |\n| \/    \/    \/ |   RE/Y\o   -     BoBo     -   HARDBYTE   | \/    \/    \/ |\n|                |                                          |                |\n`----------------+----------[  MYSTiC /X DESiGN  ]----------+----------------''\n\n \n\n                       ____ _________ _________ ________ _____\n            MYSTiC    /   / \\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /     MYSTiC\n                     /   /   \\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n         CONSOLE    /   / /\  /   __   \ \____   \   /   /      AMIGA\n                   /   /_/  \/   / /   /_____/   /  /   /\n       WORLD-HQ    \________X___/ /___/\________/   \__/      EURO-HQ\n      ____ _________ ________ _________ _________ _________ ________ _____\n     /    \\\  /   / \\  /   \\\  /    \\\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /\n    /   /\ \\\/   / /\\\/__   \\\/   /\ \\\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n   /   / /  \/   / /  \   /   / /   _____\/   / /  \ \____   \   /   /\n  /   /_/   /   /_/   /  /   / /   /     /   /_/   /_____/   /  /   /\n  \________X_________/   \__/  \__/      \________/\________/   \__/\n\n\n  AMIGA - SNES - MEGADRIVE - GAMEBOY - GAMEGEAR - PC ENGINE - LYNX - C64\n\n  3.5 Gb ONLINE          SYSOP:  EMPiRE / MYSTiC           3.5 Gb ONLINE\n\n  HOLLAND:          +31-72-157682  &  +31-72-201182             :HOLLAND\n\n','\n',char(10)),'{"ACCESS":"001","LOCATION":"DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf","PRIORITY":"0","STACK":"50000","TYPE":"XIM"}','auto-added',33184,6,0,NULL,NULL,'emp_jc35','scan',1780048504);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('emp_jc36','EMP-JC36.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/EMP-JC36.LHA','JoinCnf','XIM','JoinCnf',NULL,NULL,'EMP','|            The Ultimate ''J'' Replacement  | ¦_/\___/\___/\___                          ¦ |\   /\ __/_  __/ Added Features:          |',replace('+[ MYSTiC /X-PoWeR! ]----[ JoinCnf 3.6 ]---+\n|            The Ultimate ''J'' Replacement  |\n¦_/\___/\___/\___                          ¦\n|\   /\ __/_  __/ Added Features:          |\n|/ Y \/_.¬\/  \   o Jump more Cnfs with < >|\n/  | \\¬¦ \\  \\  o Faster Startup         |\n\__l_//___//__//  o Removed some ''C'' Bugs  ¦\n|dL!\/   \/  \/   o All 2.xx Support KICKED|\n+-[ EMPiRE/MYSTiC ]----[ XIM - /X 3.38+ ]--+\n','\n',char(10)),'JoinCnf.DOC',replace('\n                  ###### ###   ## ######  ### ######  ######              \n    _______|\     ###    #### ### ###  ## ### ###  ## ###       /|________\n   |         >    ####   ######## ######  ### ######  ####     <         |\n   | |¯¯¯¯¯|/     ###    ### # ## ###     ### #####   ###       \|¯¯¯¯¯| |\n   | |            ###### ###   ## ###     ### ### ##  ######           | |\n   | |_________________________________________________________________| |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |                            JoinCnf V3.6                             |\n   |                            ~~~~~~~~~~~~                             |\n   |                             TYPE:  XIM                              |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |                   ©1994  EMPiRE / MYSTiC /X-PoWeR!                  |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |       LaSt OuTpOsT  +31-72-157682 & +31-72-201182  -  HOLLAND       |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |       AmixNet:  @NL0004   -   /X-Fido:  44:100/102     EMPiRE       |\n   |_____________________________________________________________________|\n\n===============================================================================\n\n PURPOSE:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  o This DOOR is a alternative to the ''j'' command in /X 3.xx\n\n  o At the moment i''m using 36 Conferences and I don''t want Users\n    to ''See'' Conferences where they don''t have Access to, like the Private\n    Conferences.\n\n  o SplitUp between the File and the NetMail Conferences (optional)\n\n  o Passwords Checking for Access to Private Conferences.\n    If a User fails to enter the right Conference Password it will be Logged\n    in DOORS:Doors.LOG and/or he/she will be Kicked-Off (optional)\n\n  o Cursor-Key control for Conference selecting.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n REQUIREMENTS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  Ami Express 3.xx\n  It Doesn''t work with Ami Express 1.1x or 2.xx!!\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n INSTALLATION:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  o Copy the EmP_Tools Directory to DOORS:\n  o Make sure ENV: exists! (standard with OS2.x+)\n  o Merge your NodeX/JoinConf.txt with my DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf.CFG and\n    Edit it to your Needs. (Check the Existing JoinCnf.CFG!)\n\n JoinCnf.CFG LayOut:\n\n##[ start: cut here ]#########################################################\nLOGFILE            Doors:Doors.LOG  ; Log File Name for Password Failures\nDUAL_LISTING       YES              ; Use 2 Listings [YES/NO]\nCURRENT_CNF        YES              ; Show Current Conferences [YES/NO]\nCNF_NAMES          NO               ; Use Names from ConfConfig.info Icon [/X 3.x]\nPASSWORD_LEVEL     255              ; Password Protection Below Level\nNETMAIL_STARTCNF   18               ; First NetMail Conference Number\nDROP_DTR           3                ; Drop Carrier after 3 Password Failures\nCURSOR_KEYS        YES              ; Use the Cursors to select a Cnf [YES/NO]\n[Init_END] ====================================================================\nCNF_NAME.1 =...................... NEW USER\nCNF_NAME.2 =......................... AMiGA\nCNF_NAME.3 =............... AMiGA <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.4 =................ SNES <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.5 =........ MEGADRIVE/MS <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.6 =............. GAMEBOY <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.7 =........... GAME GEAR <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.8 =........... PC-ENGINE <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.9 =................ LYNX <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.10=.................. PC <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.11=................. C64 <OUTPOST>\nCNF_NAME.12=...................... REQUESTS\nCNF_NAME.13=.................... /X SUPPORT\nCNF_NAME.14=. EmP & /X DESiGN BeTaTeST SiTe\nCNF_NAME.15=.............. MYSTiC <PRiVATE>\nCNF_PASS.15=PASSWORD\nCNF_NAME.16=.............. MYSTiC SCENETALK\nCNF_PASS.16=PASSWORD\nCNF_NAME.17=...................... ROM BASE\nCNF_NAME.18=[AX0001]. /X Net Administration\nCNF_NAME.19=[LS0001]......... C Programming\nCNF_NAME.20=[LS0002]....... ASM Programming\nCNF_NAME.21=[LS0003]...... REXX Programming\nCNF_NAME.22=[PN0001]............ Pirate NET\nCNF_NAME.23=[HLT001]............. DuTCH NeT\nCNF_NAME.24=[PFT001].......... Amiga Topics\nCNF_NAME.26=[MOV001].......... Movie Topics\nCNF_NAME.27=[AA0001]........... ANSI Topics\nCNF_NAME.28=[WBS001]............ Buy & Sell\nCNF_NAME.29=[CHBX01]............ ChatterBox\n[Cnf_END]=====================================================================\n  [36m___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\ ___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\  ___/\\n  \[35m_ [36m. \ \_ . \ \_ . \ \  __\\  __\ \_ . \ \  __\ \_ . \ \  . \ \  __\ \  __\\n  [35m/  |__\/  |  \/  |  \/  ._l/  ._l_/  |__\/  ._l_/  |  \/  |__\/  ._l_/__.  \\n /   ¡   \  ¡   \  |   \  | /   ¡   \  .   \  ¡   \  |   \  ¡   \  ¡   \  ¡   \\n [34m\_____  /______/__¡_  /__¡ \_____  /__¡_  /____  /__¡_  /____  /____  /____  /\n-------\/------------\/-----------\/-----\/-----\/[Ð!]-\/-----\/-----\/-----\/-\n\n[Title_END]===================================================================\n\n[34m---------------------------[  [36mNETMAIL CONFERENCES  [34m]---------------------------[0m\n\n[NetMail_END]==================================================================\n\n[34m-------------------------------------------------------------------------------[0m\n[CurCnf_END]==================================================================\n[34m-------------------------------------------------------------------------------[0m\n[EndTitle_END]================================================================\n##[ end: cut here ]###########################################################\n\n\n\n  - If ''CNF_NAMES'' is set to YES then the CNF_NAME.xx is taken from\n    the BBS:ConfConfig.info Icon [/X 3.x].\n    This makes the CNF_NAME.xx in JoinCnf.CFG obsolete.\n    It''s also possible to Mix this with the Names from JoinCnf.CFG, first\n    the Names from BBS:ConfConfig.info or s:Acp.Startup are read and then\n    the names from JoinCnf.CFG are written over the old ones.\n  - CNF_NAME.x can''t be longer the 80 Characters.\n  - CNF_PASS.x can''t be longer the 40 Characters.\n\n\n[Init_END] ====================================================================\nCNF_NAME.2 =........... AMiGA PUBLIC DOMAIN\nCNF_PASS.15=PASSWORD\nCNF_PASS.16=PASSWORD\nCNF_NAME.18=[AX0001]. /X Net Administration\nCNF_NAME.19=[LS0001]......... C Programming\nCNF_NAME.20=[LS0002]....... ASM Programming\nCNF_NAME.21=[LS0003]...... REXX Programming\nCNF_NAME.22=[PN0001]............ Pirate NET\nCNF_NAME.23=[HLT001]............. DuTCH NeT\nCNF_NAME.24=[PFT001].......... Amiga Topics\n[Cnf_END]=====================================================================\n\n\n  - If you don''t want to Use the ''PASSWORD_LEVEL'' Option then set is to a\n    value greater than 255, this way Everyone has to enter the Private \n    Conference Password.\n\n  - For now I expect that all Conferences above ''NETMAIL_STARTCNF'' are\n    NetMail Conferences so if this isn''t the case or you don''t want to use\n    the File/NetMail Splitup then set NETMAIL_STARTCNF to 0\n\n  - Set ''DROP_DTR'' to the number of Password-tries before the User is Kicked.\n    He/she will not be able to use JoinCnf until ENV:JC-PWFAIL.{SLotNr} has\n    been deleted (for example after a SystemReset).\n    Use ''0'' to disable this option.\n\n  - Set ''CURSOR_KEYS'' to ''YES'' if you want to use the Cursor-Keys to select a\n    Conference. Keep in mind that it only will work if the Output-Screen\n    ''fits'' in the Users TerminalScreen!\n    Set it to ''NO'' to disable this option.\n\n  AE 3.xx:\n  ~~~~~~~~\n  - Copy J.info,<.info & >.info to BBS:Commands/BBScmd/\n\n    Or add to J.info, <.info & >.info ICON ToolType Info:\n\n      ACCESS=001\n      LOCATION=DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf\n      MULTINODE=YES\n      PRIORITY=0\n      STACK=50000\n      TYPE=XIM\n\n  - If you want ''Relative Conferences'' then you add the following to\n    BBS:CONFCONFIG.INFO IconToolType Info: \n\n      RELATIVE_CONFERENCES\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n USAGE:\n ~~~~~~\n  Just enter J, < or > on the MAIN screen in AmiExpress.\n\n  Selecting > x or < x  will make JoinCnf jump x Conferences Up or Down.\n\n===============================================================================\n\n ERROR MESSAGES:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\n  o ERROR:  DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf.CFG Not Found !!!\n  |\n  `--> JoinCnf.CFG can''t be found in DOORS:EmP_Tools !?\n\n  o ERROR:  BBS:ConfConfig Not Found !!!\n  |\n  `--> BBS:ConfConfig can''t be found in BBS:\n\n  o ERROR:  JoinCnf 3.0 ONLY Works with /X 3.xx!!\n  |\n  `--> Obvious\n\n  o ERROR:  UnKnown Keyword: XXXXXXXX in JoinCnf.CFG\n  |\n  `--> There must be 8 different KeyWords in the INIT part (JoinCnf.CFG)\n       Compare this with the original JoinCnf.CFG\n       Check that the KeyWords are:\n\n         LOGFILE\n         DUAL_LISTINGS\n         CURRENT_CNF\n         CNF_NAMES\n         PASSWORD_LEVEL\n         NETMAIL_STARTCNF\n         DROP_DTR\n         CURSOR_KEYS\n\n  o ERROR:  CNF_NAME part of JoinCnf.CFG is NOT Valid!!\n  |\n  `--> Check that all CNF_NAME.xx are correct and that xx is a Valid Conf.\n       The Line has to start with CNF_NAME.xx=  (example: CNF_NAME.1 =)\n\n  o ERROR:  CNF_PASS part of JoinCnf.CFG is NOT Valid!!\n  |\n  `--> Check that all CNF_PASS.x are correct and that x is a Valid Conference.\n       The Line has to start with CNF_PASS.xx=  (example: CNF_PASS.1 =)\n\n  o ERROR:  BBS:Access/AREA.x Not Found!!\n  |\n  `--> An User got an ConferencesAccess (x) that doesn''t exist (anymore)!!\n\n  o ERROR:  ToolType NCONFS doesn''t Exist in BBS:ConfConfig.info!!\n  |\n  `--> Should/Will never happen... /X Wouldn''t have started!\n\n  o ERROR:  CNF_PASS.x in JoinCnf.CFG is LONGER then 40 Characters!!!\n  |\n  `--> Edit CNF_PASS.x password to a smaller one (< 40 Characters)!\n\n  o ERROR:  Only 100 Conferences are Supported for now!!!\n  |\n  `--> Contact EMPiRE and Blackmail/Bribe him in making a ''Bigger'' version.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n BUG REPORT & SUGGESTIONS:                                                                   \n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  It''s possible that a mistake will occur.... Nobody is Perfect.\n  If you have any Suggestions or Comments Please Leave a Message on my Board:\n\n   LaSt OuTpOsT  N1: +31-72-157682  N2: +31-72-201182  -  HOLLAND\n   ~~~~~~~~~~~~  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~     ~~~~~~~\n   AmixNet:  @NL0004\n   /X-Fido:  44:100/102\n   UUCP   :  [email removed]\n\n===============================================================================\n\n NOTE:\n ~~~~~\n  o This Program Has been tested on a /X 2.0 - /X 3.38 Configuration.\n  o If you don''t use Conference Passwords then the < and > references are\n    not necessary and can be left out if you want, because the internal\n    < and > are always a bit faster.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n REGISTRATION:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  If YOU use this Program please let me know by Calling Last Outpost or\n  to send me a Message by NetMail @NL0004 - EMPiRE  Coz I want to know\n  how many Sysops are using my Tools!!!\n \n\n===============================================================================\n\n HISTORY:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  V1.0  07-05-92 - Release Version\n\n  V1.1  07-15-92 - Improved Password Checking\n\n  V1.2  07-28-92 - The internal j(oin) command can be changed.\n                 - Relative Conferences: ConfAccess _______X_ -> j 1 !!!\n\n  V1.3  07-29-92 - Made ''Relative Conferences'' selectable. Default OFF\n                 - Improved Conference selection.\n\n  V1.4  08-03-92 - Default ''Relative Conferences'' is YES. As it''s now Default\n                   in /X 2.1 [1 Aug''92]\n                 - Moved ''Relative Conferences'' to the Config file\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Bug Removed:\n                    o The Door Failed when e.g ''J 1abcd'' was entered.\n                    o Minor Bug removed in the Password Protection.\n\n  V1.5  08-23-92 - Changed the ColorLayout a bit.\n\n  V1.6  09-11-92 - Request Update for THC/DESiRE: 2 Conference Lists.\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n\n  V1.7  10-21-92 - Small Improvements.\n\n  V1.8  01-23-93 - Small Improvements in the 2 Conference Lists.\n\n  V1.9  01-25-93 - Bug Report from JPS (Tnx!):\n                    o 1 Cnf List Failed, 1 Line of Code was Accidentally\n                      Killed from the Source.... \n\n  V1.10 02-22-93 - Changed Layout a bit\n\n  V2.ß  04-22-93 - /X 3.1 UPDATE!!! - It''s Still BETA coz I Just got /X 3.1\n                   2 Hours ago!\n        04-29-93 - Added < and > Support because of Password Protection\n        05-03-93 - Better Error Checking (''Fool'' Proof?!)\n        05-04-93 - Changed whole Config (JoinCnf.CFG) Layout!!\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Added ''Current Confererence'' Option\n        05-06-93 - Bug Report from Aragorn/Vision (Tnx!):\n                    o Rexx claims that 2a,2b,.. exist between 1 to 20 (dec)\n\n  V2.0  05-09-93 - Release Version\n\n  V2.1  05-10-93 - Minor Update. The date in Doors.LOG file was wrong.\n        05-20-93 - Added ''Password Level'', this way the Sysop/CoSysop doesn''t\n                   need to enter a Conferences Password eachtime he Joins\n                   that Conference. (Thanx to Alcoholic for the Idea!!)\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n        05-27-93 - Fixed the UpperCase Problem in JoinCnf.CFG and all used\n                   Icon Files [/X 3.x]\n\n  V2.2  06-18-93 - Updated the ''<'' & ''>'' code.\n        06-21-93 - Fixed a small Error found with the RexxDoorDebugger.\n        06-28-93 - Added the ErrorMessages in the doc.\n\n  V2.3  07-23-93 - Better Error handling\n\n  V3.ß1 08-10-93 - At last the XIM Version! Rexx is a thing of the Past!\n                 - Changed ALL References from AIM to XIM !!!\n                 - Keywords ''NRSPACES'', ''RELATIVE_CONFERENCE'', ''NCONFS'' are\n                   deleted from JoinCnf.CFG (Obsolete) because they are now\n                   ''read'' from the BBS:ConfConfig.info [/X 3.x] or\n                   s:ACP.Startup [/X 2.xx]\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n\n  V3.ß2 08-13-93 - Keyword ''NRLINES'' is deleted from JoinCnf.CFG.\n                 - Keyword ''NETMAIL_STARTCNF'' added to JoinCnf.CFG\n                   If there are NetMail Conferences then the Conference\n                   showing can be split in 2 (Files & Netmail).\n                 - Renamed Keyword ''2_LISTINGS'' to ''DUAL_LISTING''\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Included Optimized 030 Version!\n\n  V3.ß3 08-15-93 - Added the ''LOGFILE'' Option. Password Failures on Private\n                   Conferences will be logged in DOORS:Doors.LOG (default)\n                 - Added the ''CNF_NAMES'' Keyword (was ''CONFCONFIG_NAMES'')\n                   Now ConferenceNames from BBS:ConfConfig tooltype [/X 3.x]\n                   (or s:ACP.Startup [/X 2.xx]) and CNF_NAME.xx from\n                   JoinCnf.CFG can be Mixed in the Output.\n\n  V3.ß4 08-23-93 - Optimized the code and it''s now about 2 kb smaller\n                 - Bug Report from Beast (Tnx!):\n                    o JoinCnf failed completely when the OLD ''LOADCONFIG''\n                      option in ACP.Startup was used [/X 2.xx]\n                 - DEBUG OPTION: enter !% as Conference and you get some info\n                                 when all goes wrong.\n\n  V3.0  08-26-93 - Release Version.\n                 - All Debug routines removed.\n                 - Optimized 030 Version Included.\n\n  V3.1  09-04-93 - Bug Reports from ByteAndi/MST:\n                    o The Last conference couldn''t be joined when Not using\n                      ''RELATIVE_CONFERENCE'' (only happend in the Menu)\n        09-05-93 -  o Sometimes when only entering ''J'' a Conference would be\n                      joined. This is due to a BUG in /X... Fixed for now!\n        09-11-93 - Release Version.\n\n  V3.2  10-02-93 - /X 3.11 Update... AE Returned a different VersionNumber\n                   then in previous versions... this made JoinCnf think that\n                   it wasn''t /X 3.xx but /X 2.xx... FIXED!\n\n  V3.3ß 10-09-93 - Bug Report from ByteAndi/MST:\n                    o Conference Passwords bigger than 80 Chars would Halted\n                      the system! The maximum CNF_PASS size is now 40 Chars.\n                 - Updated some more code. Needs Lesser memory to run.\n                 - Better Error checking.\n        10-14-93 - Bug Report from Sal1/TRSI:\n                    o /X 2.xx & NOT using ''RELATIVE_CONFERENCE'' still failed.\n                      Should be fixed now!\n                 - When the KeyWord ''LOADCONFIG'' is used in the Acp.Startup\n                   [/X 2.xx] then the ''Current Conference'' Option will be\n                   Disabled! \n                 - Some more Code-Optimizing.\n\n  V3.3  12-01-93 - Release Version\n                 - Included an Optimized 020 Version for A1200/A4000 Users.\n                 - Just replace the Old JoinCnf with the New One!\n\n  V3.4ß 01-16-93 - Bug Report from Sal1/TRSI:\n                    o When Using /X 3.20+ & ''NETMAIL_STARTCNF  0'' & Not Using\n                      ''RELATIVE_CONFERENCE'' the Conferences were not shown.\n                 - Added ''DROP_DTR'' option, it Drops the Carrier of the\n                   User after he/she fails to enter the Right Password for\n                   a number of times. After this he/she won''t be able to use\n                   JoinCnf until the file ENV:JC_PWFAIL.{SlotNr} doesn''t exist\n                   anymore (for example after a systemreset). When he tries\n                   he will be Kicked-Off right away!\n        01-17-94 - Bug Report from Mike Hoffren:\n                    o Using 108 Conferences will Crash the System!\n                      In the past it was ''Only'' possible to use 100 Confs,\n                      I never thought there would be more than that!\n                      It''s been Fixed to 256 Conferences for now\n                 - When JoinCnf is run, it will ShowUp on the Server.\n        01-21-94 - Change the Command-Line a bit.\n                 - JoinCnf will now make Use of the full 80 Chars/Line, this\n                   means that LayOut of JoinCnf.CFG Changed a bit!\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n        01-25-94 - Fixed the ''10-Node'' BUG. Using Node 10+ would result in\n                   JoinCnf thinking it was started from Node 1.... FIXED!\n\n  V3.4  01-29-94 - Release Version.\n\n  V3.5ß 02-01-94 - Change the ''Full Use of 80 Chars/Line'' to 79 Chars/Line\n                   because some Terminals on Amiga/PC can''t handle the 80th\n                   character on the right (extra linefeeds)!\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Took out all 100+ Conferencing coz /X-Fido is on it''s way\n                   now (so no need for 100+ Conferences) and otherwise the\n                   Code becomes too big! A 100+ Version Can be REQUESTED!\n        02-06-94 - Changed the CommandLine-Input so only Numbers can be\n                   selected.\n                 - Added some BETA-Code to use the Cursor-Keys to select a\n                   Conference. The only BIG problem exists when there are too\n                   many Conferences and the Screen starts scrolling....\n        02-09-94 - Updated the Cursor-Key Code so it''s now possible to Use\n                   Cursor-Keys only if the JoinCnf output-screen fits in the\n                   Users terminalscreen (without scrolling). If it doesn''t\n                   ''fit'' then the Cursor-Keys are disabled otherwise it would\n                   mess up the screen!\n                 - Added the ''CURSOR_KEYS'' KeyWord, when it''s ''NO'' then there\n                   will never be made use of ANSI when using the Cursors.\n                   When the KeyWord is ''YES'' or is leftout then ANSI is used\n                   when the JoinCnf-Screen ''fits'' in the Users TerminalScreen.\n                   [ JoinCnf.CFG CHANGED! ]\n                 - Note: For now the Cursor-Code works only with Relative\n                         Conferences.\n        02-16-94 - Fixed a Small Bug in the Password Handling.\n        02-20-94 - Fixed the Non-Relative Conferences problems so now it will\n                   work on ANY /X 2.xx or 3.xx configuration.\n        02-21-94 - Fixed a Bug in printing the Command-Line to Screen. It\n                   failed when there where Non-Even FileConfs & NetMailConfs.\n                 - Changed the Cursor-Handling. The ''jump'' from FileConf to \n                   NetMailConf has been changed.\n        03-08-94 - Optimized the Cursor-Key Routine.\n\n  V3.5  03-13-94 - Release Version.\n\n  V3.6ß 04-11-94 - Added the ''Q'' to the CommandLine on Tycoon''s Request:\n                    o Pressing ''Q'' will now Exit JoinCnf Immediately, even\n                      when a Conference is selected.\n        04-14-94 - Killed all sscanf''s in the Source coz of Bugs in the\n                   SAS Compiler and this way JoinCnf gets 2 kb smaller!\n        04-17-94 - After a long talk with ByteMaster I managed to kill some\n                   obsolete code, this will make JoinCnf startup Faster!\n                 - Added an New CommandLine Option: \n                    ''> 4''  will Join: Current Conference + 4 (if it Exists)\n                    ''< 9''  will Join: Current Conference - 9 (if it Exists)\n                    ''< -2'' will Join: Current Conference + 2 (if it Exists)\n                 - ReCompiled with the Latest AE.Includes\n        06-28-94 - Fixed some code that can cause random guru''s (SAS C Bug!)\n                 - Removed ALL /X 2.xx support because of the new AE.Includes\n                   JoinCnf won''t work on any /X version before /X 3.38, if\n                   you still have not upgraded then this is the time to do it!\n\n  V3.6  07-01-94 - Release Version.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n EMPiRE /X DOORS/UTILS COLLECTION UNTIL NOW!:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - AboStats V0.2                        AIM Door\n  - AE2PCE V1.0                          CLI Util\n  - AutoOnline V1.3                      XIM Door/Util\n  - Bulls V2.2                           XIM Door\n  - CheckDir V1.0                        CLI Util\n  - CheckNU V1.0                         XIM Door\n  - ConfAccess V1.1                      ServerUtil\n  - CopyDate V1.3                        CLI Util\n  - DlDir V2.1                           XIM Door\n  - DoorKIT 1.01                         XIM Doors/Includes\n  - DMenu V1.0                           AIM Door\n  - DutchNET V1.0  [AmiXnet]             AIM Door\n  - Front V2.0                           XIM Door\n  - JoinCnf V3.6                         XIM Door\n  - LastCalls & TopUps V1.2              LogOffUtil\n  - LastDL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - LastUL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - MakeFree V1.1                        AIM Door\n  - MemberList V1.0                      AIM Door\n  - MonthTopUp V2.0                      ServerUtil\n  - MultiNodeLastCaller V1.1             LogOffUtil\n  - Page V2.1                            XIM Door\n  - PMail V1.1                           AIM Door\n  - Remote Maintenance V1.3              AIM Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.3                 AIM Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.2 [Party]         CLI Door\n  - SecLevChk V1.0                       ServerUtil\n  - Sent FrondEnd 1.0  [Sent_FE]         XIM Door\n  - StripCR V1.0                         CLI Util\n  - SysopCommands V1.0                   AIM Door\n  - UserData Cleaner/Repair V2.0         ServerUtil\n  - UserStats V2.0                       ServerUtil\n  - UsrCnfAx V1.1                        CLI Util\n  - View & Check Archive/Text V1.7       AIM Door\n  - WhoNode V1.3                         AIM Door\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n THANX MUST GO TO ALL OFFICIAL BETA-TESTERS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   Tycoon/Mystic /X-PoWeR!   [Channel X]\n   JPS                       [Red October]\n   Sal-One/TRSi              [BoonDocks]\n   Blue Bird/Mystic          [Tau Ceti]\n   Beast/Partek              [Sequencial]\n   SpeedDevil/Partek         [Rising Sun]\n   Galaxy                    [Ulixys]\n\n\n GREETZ/TNX GO TO:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   Killraven/MST /X-PoWeR! - For giving me sum Great Pointers/Sources on ''C''\n   ByteAndi/TRSI           - Tnx for all ya ''C'' Help & Sources!\n   Easy Rider/Mystic       - hope ya are back soon!\n   JAF/G.O.D.              - For some good REXX ideas\n   Bad Sector/Hidden       - Still Using your Sort-Routine. It''s the Fastest!\n   B.O.B./CyberForce       - For (Mis)Using/Testing my tools! :)\n   Aragorn/Vision          - Tnx for Testing\n   Camelot/Complex         - Tnx for Testing\n   Sharon/5D               - Tnx for Testing\n   Telekinetic/T.F.A.      - Tnx for Testing\n   Alcoholic               - Tnx for Testing\n   Price/T.F.A.            - Great job on your {F}ull {S}creen {E}ditor!\n   Van Damme               - Nice Doors!!!\n   DR.G/IBB                - Hope to see Check''N Add work on a 040\n   SiGMA SEVEN             - Nice job on SiGMA-EXPRESS! Tempted to ''Convert''\n   911/Movement            - Tnx For ya Ansi Logo''s!! You''re the Greatest!\n   Mike                    - Tnx for calling!\n   Amicom/TRSi             - Tnx for calling!\n   THC/Legend              - Tnx for Beta-Testing!\n   Mega Fortress           - Tnx for Beta-Testing!\n   SkIn                    - Tnx for ya Description Logo! Looks Great!!\n   Shadow Man/S8           - Nice Doors & Tnx for ya greetz! & Calling ofcoz\n   [MAZ!^PHAZE]            - Tnx for calling!\n   Shuyuki Tanaka          - Tnx for your Messages!\n   AGONY/cOmpLeX           - Tnx for calling!\n   HUMANOiD & jABBA /MST &\n    SCANDAL                - Tnx for calling!\n   LECTOR                  - Tnx for your NetMail\n   Koo Koo Ka Choo         - Tnx for calling!\n   TaRzAN                  - Tnx for your NetMail. Yezz 040 Speed Rulez!!\n   Crayor/Scx              - Tnx for calling!\n   Foxx''n''Lynxx            - Tnx for your BugReports!!!\n   ThE BiRD                - Tnx for your NetMail on UsrCnfAx\n   Dux                     - Tnx for your NetMail\n   dR.dRE!/fAITH           - Tnx for da greetz, Nice Doors btw\n   [-RUNT&SCOTTIE-]        - Tnx for calling!\n   sPAcEcAKe/aF            - Tnx for calling!\n   Freddy Kreuger/GOD      - Tnx for your NetMail on JoinCnf 3.2\n   MF1                     - Tnx for calling!\n   AtomiX/N.A.S.A          - Hope the ''C'' archive was sume help for ya!\n   MAD HARLEQUiN [ITPH01]  - Tnx for your Nice AmiXnet Messy''s\n   Mike Hoffren  [SF0001]  - Tnx for the BugReport on JoinCnf 3.3 [100+ Cnfs]\n   Galaxy/TFA              - Nice job on da SentCleaner\n   SvEN tHE CREAToR/5D     - ''Tnx'' for forcing me to use Cursor-Keys!\n   Diogenes/TPY/5D         - Tnx for calling and for your BugReport!\n   L.A.Style               - Tnx For Calling/Beta-Testing!\n\n   & Ofcoz to ALL MYSTiC Members all over the Globe!\n\n                        ______  __________________                            \n.----------------+-----/    \ \/ / __/_  __/ / __/----------+----------------.\n|                |----/ / / /\  /\  /-/ /-/ / /----[ /X ]---|                |\n|       /\    /\ |-EmP\/ / /-/ /\  /-/ /-/ /\ \--[ PoWeR! ]-|       /\    /\ |\n|      /C \  /!/ +-----\/\/--\/--\/--\/--\/--\/-------------+      /C \  /!/ |\n|     /i/\ \/R/  |                                          |     /i/\ \/R/  |\n|    /T/  \ E/   |   EMPiRE   -   KiLLRAVEN  -   TYCOON     |    /T/  \ E/   |\n|   /S/   /W \   |                                          |   /S/   /W \   |\n|  /Y/   /O/\ \  |   RE/Y\o   -   INCOGNiTO  -   HARDBYTE   |  /Y/   /O/\ \  |\n| /M/   /P/  \ \ |                                          | /M/   /P/  \ \ |\n| \/    \/    \/ |                   BoBo                   | \/    \/    \/ |\n|                |                                          |                |\n`----------------+----------[  MYSTiC /X PoWeR!  ]----------+----------------''\n\n\n \n\n                       ____ _________ _________ ________ _____\n            MYSTiC    /   / \\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /     MYSTiC\n                     /   /   \\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n         CONSOLE    /   / /\  /   __   \ \____   \   /   /      AMIGA\n                   /   /_/  \/   / /   /_____/   /  /   /\n       WORLD-HQ    \________X___/ /___/\________/   \__/      EURO-HQ\n      ____ _________ ________ _________ _________ _________ ________ _____\n     /    \\\  /   / \\  /   \\\  /    \\\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /\n    /   /\ \\\/   / /\\\/__   \\\/   /\ \\\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n   /   / /  \/   / /  \   /   / /   _____\/   / /  \ \____   \   /   /\n  /   /_/   /   /_/   /  /   / /   /     /   /_/   /_____/   /  /   /\n  \________X_________/   \__/  \__/      \________/\________/   \__/\n\n\nAMIGA - SNES - MEGADRIVE - GAMEBOY - GAMEGEAR - PC ENGINE - LYNX NEOGEO - C64\n\n4.5 Gb ONLINE             SYSOP:  EMPiRE / MYSTiC               4.5 Gb ONLINE\n\nHOLLAND:              +31-72-157682  &  +31-72-201182               :HOLLAND\n\n','\n',char(10)),'{"ACCESS":"001","LOCATION":"DOORS:EmP_Tools/JoinCnf","PRIORITY":"0","STACK":"50000","TYPE":"XIM"}','auto-added',29201,4,0,NULL,NULL,'emp_jc36','scan',1780048498);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('emp_mt17','EMP-MT17.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/EMP-MT17.LHA','MonthTopUp','XIM','CyberCron',NULL,'EMPiRE/[SBS]','EMP','/~ __/~V~X~. \/~V~\/~X~___/~__/~\/~. \/~  \ \__  \ |  \ _ \ _  \  \_  \_  \  \ |  \ | / ___/____/___/_V__/__/___/___/__/____/_|__',replace(' ______ _ ___  _ _  _ __________  ___  ___\n/~ __/~V~X~. \/~V~\/~X~___/~__/~\/~. \/~  \\n\__  \ |  \ _ \ _  \  \_  \_  \  \ |  \ |\n/ ___/____/___/_V__/__/___/___/__/____/_|__\n\/--\/---\/--\/---\/-\/--\/--\/-\/---\/----\nAE UTIL: MonthTopUp V1.7 - by EMPiRE/[SBS]\n','\n',char(10)),'CyberCron.guide',replace('@database "CyberCron.guide"\n\n@width 80\n\n@node Main "CyberCron documentation"\n@prev CAW\n\n                                 CyberCron\n\n                Copyright © 1992 by Christopher A. Wichura\n                           All rights reserved.\n\n@{" Legal Mumbo Jumbo " link LegalStuff}\n@{" Supplied Files " link SuppliedFiles}\n@{" What is a cron? " link DefineCron}\n@{" Why another cron for the Amiga? " link WhyCyberCron}\n@{" Starting CyberCron " link StartingUp}\n@{" Creating a crontab file " link CronTabFile}\n@{" CyberCron as an ARexx Function Host " link RexxPort}\n@{" How job queues work in CyberCron " link JobQueues}\n@{" The internal naming scheme used by CyberCron " link InternalNaming}\n@{" How to make CyberCron speak your language " link Localization}\n@{" The revision history of CyberCron " link ChangeLog}\n@{" Credits " link Credits}\n@{" Contacting the author " link CAW}\n@endnode\n\n@node LegalStuff "Legal Mumbo Jumbo"\n\nCyberCron is not in the public domain.  All source files, along with the\nresulting executable, are copyright by C. Wichura.  You may not sell\nCyberCron.  The only allowed charge that may be placed on CyberCron is for\nmedia and/or mailing costs.\n\nCyberCron may be freely redistributed via BBSs, InterNet/Usenet, and disk\nlibraries such as Fred Fish''s, as long as the archive is not modified.\nDisk magazines and services that charge extra for file transfers may not\ndistribute CyberCron.\n\nIn using CyberCron, you accept the responsibility for any damage or loss of\nproductivity/money that may occur through or during its use.  C. Wichura\nis not and cannot be held accountable.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node SuppliedFiles "Supplied Files"\n\nAll source to CyberCron is included in the Source/* directory.  Please do\nnot redistribute changes you make.  Instead, send them to me for inclusion\nin the master distribution.\n\nThe s directory contains a sample crontab file.  This is the one I am\ncurrently using on my system.  It contains a number of comments that give\nsome helpful hints on how to use some of the more advanced features of\nCyberCron.\n\nIn RexxScripts is CCShow.rexx, based on the NCShow.rexx script that came with\n@{"NazCron" link NazCron}.  It will call @{"LIST_EVENTS" link RCmdListEvents} and if there are active events, it will\nloop through the list calling @{"SHOW_EVENT" link RCmdShowEvent} to display each one.\n\nAlso in the rexx directory you will find the files at.rexx and batch.rexx.\nThese are the files Loren Rittle sent me that implement the UNIX @{"at" link AtCommand}\nand @{"batch" link BatchCommand} commands using CyberCron.  They have since\nbeen updated by Graham Walter to be much more complete.\n\nThe Catalogs directory is where @{"translation catalogs" link Localization} for languages that\nCyberCron has been converted to are found.  If you are running under\nWorkbench 2.1 and would like to use CyberCron in your language, copy the\nappropriate file into your LOCALE:Catalogs/<language> directory.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node DefineCron "What is a cron?"\n\nA cron is a program that runs in the background (i.e., requires no user\nintervention) and executes commands at specific times, as defined in a\n@{"crontab file" link CronTabFile}.  It allows the automation of jobs that one runs regularily.\n\nFor example, many maintenance tasks can be handled by a cron event.\nIncremental system backups to a streaming tape could be started at a time\nwhen one is generally away from the machine every day or every week, for\nexample.  Or you might have a cron entry run a script that logs onto BIX,\nCompuServe, or wherever and downloads all your mail and unread messages\nautomatically during the night when rates are lower.\n\nA cron can also be used as a means of reminding yourself to do things.  You\nmight add an event that runs an alarm clock program in the morning to wake\nyou up, for example.  Or you might have it throw a requester up telling you\nto go to bed when it is getting late.  The possibilities are endless.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node WhyCyberCron "Why another cron for the Amiga?"\n\nThere have been several crons for the Amiga already.  These include\nAmiCron, dcron, TPTCron and @{"NazCron" link NazCron}, to name a few.  So what''s the\nneed for yet another cron?\n\nCyberCron was written to take advantage of the new features of Workbench\n2.04 (or greater), and thus has many advanced options that previous crons\ndon''t.  It''s this new functionality that makes switching over to CyberCron\nworthwhile.\n\nCyberCron uses the new System() call in dos.library to launch events.  This\ncall is far superior to the Execute() call of Workbench 1.3 days which the\nother crons use.  Through the use of the System() call, CyberCron can\nsupport options such as the stack size or priority to launch specific\nevents at.\n\nCyberCron also understands ARexx.  It can start events as ARexx commands\nand/or scripts.  It also has an ARexx port which can accept a number of\n@{"ARexx commands" link RexxPort} to control it while running.\n\nCyberCron supports @{"localization" link Localization}.  If you are running it on a machine with\nlocale.library and have a translation catalog for your local language,\nCyberCron''s messages will appear in your language instead of English.\n\nFinally, CyberCron also uses @{"file notification" link FileNotification} to determine when the\ncrontab file has been changed.  This eliminates the performance hit that\nprevious Amiga crons have suffered from due to the fact that they had to\ncheck the crontab file for updates once a minute.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node StartingUp "Starting CyberCron"\n@next CronTabFile\n\nYou can start CyberCron from either the CLI or Workbench.\n\nWhen CyberCron is started from the Workbench, it trys to clone a copy of\nthe Workbench''s CLI structure.  What this means is that the command path\nthat existed at the time LoadWB was run will be propagated on to commands\nCyberCron launches.\n\nCyberCron understands the following arguments:\n\n    [@{" CRONTAB " link ArgCronTab} <filespec>] [@{" LOGFILE " link ArgLogFile} <filespec>]\n    [@{" DEFSTACK " link ArgDefStack} <Default Stack>] [@{" DEFPRI " link ArgDefPriority} <Default Priority>]\n    [@{" SENDMAIL " link ArgSendMail} <"{command} {from switch} %s {name switch} *"%s*"">]\n    [@{" CRONPRI " link ArgCronPriority} <Cron''s Priority>] [@{" PORTNAME " link ArgPortName} <ARexx Portname>]\n\nAll arguments are optional.  If not specified, CyberCron will use an\nappropriate default.  See the specific options'' documentation for more\ninformation.\n\nTo use these options from a Workbench icon, simply make each tooltype be in\nthe form of <option>=<setting>.  For example, if you wanted to specify a\nlogfile of T:CronLog, you would have a tooltype that looked like\n\n	LOGFILE=T:CronLog\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##      These nodes are for the various command line argument links         ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node ArgCronTab "The »CronTab« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n@prev ArgPortName\n\nThis specifies the @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile} that CyberCron will try and read.  If not\nspecified, s:CronTab will be used as the default.\n\nThe crontab file may also be changed while CyberCron is running by using\nthe @{"NEW_EVENT_FILE" link RCmdNewEventFile} ARexx command.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	CRONTAB/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	CronTab cron:ctab\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ArgLogFile "The »LogFile« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n\nThis indicates where CyberCron is to log activities.  If no filespec is\ngiven, logging will be turned off.\n\nThe logfile may also be changed while CyberCron is running by using the\n@{"NEW_LOG_FILE" link RCmdNewLogFile} ARexx command.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	LOGFILE/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	LogFile T:CronLog\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ArgSendMail "The »SendMail« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n\nCyberCron supports sending the output of a command it has launched to a\nuser via e-mail.  For this to work, however, it needs to know how to send\nmail on the local system.  This command tells CyberCron how to do this.\n\nIf the sendmail option is not specified then entries which try to direct\ntheir output to e-mail will not be able to.  File @{"output redirection" link EArgReDirOut} will\nbe honored instead.\n\nThe argument for this command should be in the form of\n\n    "<sendmail command> <from switch> %s <realname switch> *"%s*""\n\nFor example, for AmigaUUCP''s sendmail one would use\n\n    "UUCP:c/SendMail -f %s -R *"%s*""\n\nFor CyberCron to be able to send mail, the sendmail command must read the\nmail message from its standard input.  This is because CyberCron sets up a\npipe to redirect output into sendmail.  This also means that you must have\n@{"PIPE:" link PipeDevice} mounted before mail can be sent.\n\nThe sendmail command must also expect the To: and Subject: fields to be in\nits input instead of on the command line, and must generate the Message-ID:\nfield itself.\n\nAmigaUUCP''s SendMail command meets all the above criteria.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	SENDMAIL/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	SendMail "UUCP:c/SendMail -f %s -R *"%s*""\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ArgDefStack "The »DefStack« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n\nThis is the default stack size to launch executables with.  Values less\nthan 2048 will be rejected.\n\nThis argument is ignored when launching ARexx events.\n\nIf not specified, the stack size that CyberCron was started with will be\nused as the default.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	DEFSTACK/K/N\n\nEXAMPLE\n	DefStack 25000\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ArgDefPriority "The »DefPri« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n\nThis specifies the default priority to launch executables at.\n\nThis option is ignored by ARexx events.\n\nIf not specified, this option defaults to zero.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	DEFPRI/K/N\n\nEXAMPLE\n	DefPri -1\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ArgCronPriority "The »CronPri« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n\nThis is the priority that CyberCron should run at.\n\nIf not specified, this option defaults to no change.  I.e., CyberCron will\nremain that the priority it was started at.\n\nToolPri is accepted as an alias for CronPri.  This is for conformity with\nthe @{"Style Guide" link StyleGuide}.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	TOOLPRI=CRONPRI/K/N\n\nEXAMPLE\n	CronPri 4\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ArgPortName "The »PortName« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n@next ArgCronTab\n\nThis option specifies the ARexx portname that CyberCron will use.\n\nIf not specified, CyberCron defaults to a portname of CYBERCRON.\n\nCyberCron will not let you start it multiple times with the same port name.\nIt does, however, support the use of concurrent CyberCron tasks which each\nhave their own portname.\n\nUsing this option, one could have multiple crontab files.  One might also\nchoose to have one CyberCron task that handles their @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile} while\na second is used as a server for script commands such as the @{"at" link AtCommand} command.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	PORTNAME/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	PortName CyberCronAtServer\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##      End of nodes describing the various command line arguments          ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node CronTabFile "Creating a crontab file"\n@prev StartingUp\n@next RexxPort\n\nLines in this file must be blank, a comment, or an event.  Comments are\ndenoted by lines that start with a "#" symbol.  Events take the form of\n\n	min hour day month dow command <command''s args, if any>\n\nin their simplest form.  Each of min, hour, day, month and dow (day of the\nweek) are specifications for what range of values this command is to be run\nat.  A * indicates any value is acceptable.  Numeric digits specify\nspecific values and can be seperated by a , (seperates distinct entries) or\na - (used to indicate a range of entries).  For example,\n\n	* * * * * Date\n\nwould run the command date every minute of every hour of every day of every\nmonth regardless of the day of the week.\n\n	0 * * * * Date\n\nis similar, but only runs the date command on the zeroth minute of each\nhour.\n\n	1,5-10,25 * * * * Date\n\nwould run the date command on minutes 1, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 and 25 of each\nhour.\n\nMonths range from 1 (January) to 12 (December).  The day of the week ranges\nfrom 0 (Sunday) to 6 (Saturday).\n\nCyberCron also supports a number of extensions to the traditional event\nformat.  These may occur anywhere in the event, and are:\n\n    @{" :NAME " link EArgName} <name>\n    @{" < " link EArgReDirIn} <filespec>\n    @{" > " link EArgReDirOut} <filespec>\n    @{" 2> " link EArgReDirErr} <filespec>\n    @{" :MAILUSER " link EArgMailUser} <user name>\n    @{" :EXECONCE " link EArgExecOnce}\n    @{" :OBEYQUEUE " link EArgObeyQueue} <queue name>\n    @{" :NOLOG " link EArgNoLog}\n    @{" :REXX " link EArgRexx}\n    @{" :STACK " link EArgStack} <bytes>\n    @{" :PRI " link EArgPriority} <priority>\n    @{" :CUSTOMSH " link EArgCustomShell} <shell name>\n    @{" :SYSSH " link EArgSystemShell}\n\nPlease note that these above options are parsed with the dos.library\nReadArgs() command.  Because of this, those options which take a variable\nmust have whitespace between the option''s keyword and its assignment.  This\ngenerally isn''t a problem, except that most people I know are used to\nspecifying redirection as\n\n	@{">" link EArgReDirOut}ram:foo\n\nwhile the proper method for CyberCron would be\n\n	@{">" link EArgReDirOut} ram:foo\n\nThe other implication of using ReadArgs() is that quotes will be stripped\nout, as will extra spaces.  To retain spaces, you must inclose the element\nthey proceed or follow with quotes.  For example, one might use\n\n	date @{">" link EArgReDirOut} "Ram:Two  Spaces"\n\nTo retain quotes, you will have to quote the argument as you would for\nspaces, but escape a quote character withing the quoted string.  For\nexample.\n\n	"This has a *" quote in it"\n\n\nThere is one aditional special case that needs to be observed when creating\nan event.  If the command you are running is actually a script _and_ it\ncontains a space in it''s filename, you must explicitly call the AmigaDOS\nExecute command for it to work.  For example, you''d have to use\n\n	execute "foo bar script"\n\ninstead of\n\n	"foo bar script"\n\n(those people with WShell who want to execute an ARexx script from a WShell\ncontext would use WShell''s Rexx command instead of Execute.)\n\nThis is due to a problem that the AmigaDOS shell has executing script''s\nwhose command name has been quoted.  This only applies to scripts which\nhave spaces in their name, so very few people should be affected by this\nlimitation.\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##             These nodes descibe the various event options                ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node EArgName "The »:NAME« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n@prev EArgSystemShell\n\nSpecifies the formal name the event will be known under when CyberCron is\naccessed via its @{"ARexx port" link RexxPort}.\n\nYou may use the same name multiple time.  However, this is not recommened.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:NAME/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:Name WakeMeUp\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgReDirIn "The input redirection event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nThis specifies where input is to be redirected from.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	</K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	< "CON:0/0/300/100/Gimme some input"\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgReDirOut "The output redirection event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nSpecifies where output is to be redirected to.\n\nIf >> is used instead of >, the filespec will be appended to instead of\noverwritten.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	>/K or >>/K\n\nEXAMPLES\n	> RAM:job.out\n	>> RAM:job.logfile\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgReDirErr "The error redirection event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nSpecifies where standard error is to be redirected to.\n\nIf 2>> is used instead of 2>, the filespec will be appended to instead of\noverwritten.\n\nNote that this is currently ignored by the event launcher per a discussion\nI had with Randell Jesup.  Standard error support wasn''t really finished\nfor 2.0, and that makes it difficult to support this at this time.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	2>/K or 2>>/K\n\nEXAMPLES\n	2> RAM:job.err\n	2>> RAM:job.errlog\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgMailUser "The »:MAILUSER« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nSpecifies a user to send mail to with the output of the command being run.\nIf the @{"SENDMAIL" link ArgSendMail} command line option was not specified, this will be ignored\nwhen the event is started, and any @{"output redirection" link EArgReDirOut} specified will be\nused, instead.\n\nThe username may be any e-mail address, so mail can be sent to both local\nand remote systems.  If your sendmail supports it, you could even specify\nan alias and have it send mail to multiple people.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:MAILUSER/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:MailUser root\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgExecOnce "The »:EXECONCE« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nAfter the event has been started, remove it from the event list.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:EXECONCE/S\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:ExecOnce\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgObeyQueue "The »:OBEYQUEUE« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nSelect a specific queue to schedule the event under.  See the section on\n@{"queues" link JobQueues} for more information.\n\nUsing this, @{":EXECONCE" link EArgExecOnce}, @{":MAILUSER" link EArgMailUser} and some ARexx macros, one could simulate\nthe @{"at" link AtCommand} and @{"batch" link BatchCommand} commands under UNIX.  (See Loren''s @{"at" link AtCommand} stuff for an\nexample of how.)\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:OBEYQUEUE/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:ObeyQueue c\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgNoLog "The »:NOLOG« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nIf you specified a @{"logfile" link ArgLogFile} then every time an event is started or ends a\nmessage gets sent to the log file.  This turns off logging for the specific\nevent.  Handy for things one runs many, many times, in keeping the size of\nyour logfile from exploding.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:NOLOG/S\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:NoLog\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgRexx "The »:REXX« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nStart this command using ARexx rather than trying to run it as a system\ncall.  If the first character of the command is "`" then it will be started\nas an ARexx string rather than a command.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:REXX/S\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:Rexx\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgStack "The »:STACK« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nThe stacksize to use when launching the event.\n\nThis option is ignored for ARexx events.\n\nIf the value specified is less than 2048, or this option is omitted, then\nthe @{"default stack size" link ArgDefStack} will be used instead.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:STACK/K/N\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:Stack 50000\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgPriority "The »:PRI« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nThe priority to launch the event at.\n\nThis option is ignored for ARexx events.\n\nIf this option is omitted then the @{"default priority" link ArgDefPriority} will be used.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:PRI/K/N\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:PRI -5\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgCustomShell "The »:CUSTOMSH« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nUse a specific custom shell when launching the event.  See your "Using the\nSystem Software" manual for more information on custom shells.\n\nThis option is ignored by ARexx events.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:CUSTOMSH/K\n\nEXAMPLES\n	:CustomSH WShell\n	:CustomSH BootShell\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgSystemShell "The »:SYSSH« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n@next EArgName\n\nIf no custom shell is specified, CyberCron will try and launch the command\nusing the specified User Shell.  If you do not want the User Shell to be\nused for a specific event, specifying this flag will cause CyberCron to use\nthe system shell.\n\nThis option is ignored by ARexx events.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:SYSSH/S\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:SysSH\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##           End of nodes descibing the various event options               ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node RexxPort "CyberCron as an ARexx Function Host"\n@prev CronTabFile\n@next JobQueues\n\nCyberCron''s default ARexx port name is CYBERCRON.  However, this can be\nchanged with the @{"PORTNAME" link ArgPortName} command line option.\n\nCyberCron supports the following ARexx commands:\n\n    @{" SHUTDOWN " link RCmdShutdown}\n    @{" VERSION " link RCmdVersion}\n    @{" SUSPEND " link RCmdSuspend}\n    @{" RESUME " link RCmdResume}\n    @{" NEW_EVENT_FILE " link RCmdNewEventFile} <filespec>\n    @{" CLOSE_EVENT_FILE " link RCmdCloseEventFile}\n    @{" NEW_LOG_FILE " link RCmdNewLogFile} <filespec>\n    @{" CLOSE_LOG_FILE " link RCmdCloseLogFile}\n    @{" SHOW_STATUS " link RCmdShowStatus}\n    @{" PURGE_REXX_EVENTS " link RCmdPurgeRexxEvents}\n    @{" ADD_EVENT " link RCmdAddEvent} <event>\n    @{" DELETE_REXX_EVENT " link RCmdDeleteRexxEvent} <event name>\n    @{" DELETE_EVENT " link RCmdDeleteEvent} <event name>\n    @{" LIST_EVENTS " link RCmdListEvents}\n    @{" SHOW_EVENT " link RCmdShowEvent} <event name>\n    @{" SET_QUEUE_MAX " link RCmdSetQueueMax} <queue name> <max jobs executable at a time>\n    @{" GET_QUEUE_MAX " link RCmdGetQueueMax} <queue name>\n    @{" EVENT_NEXT_EXEC " link RCmdEventNextExec} <event name>\n    @{" NEXT_EVENT_TO_EXEC " link RCmdNextEventToExec}\n    @{" EXPAND_SSSC " link RCmdExpandSSSC} <seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation}>\n    @{" SSSC_TO_ASCII " link RCmdSSSCtoASCII} <seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation}>\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##            These nodes describe the various ARexx commands               ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node RCmdShutdown "The »SHUTDOWN« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n@prev RCmdSSSCtoASCII\n\nCauses CyberCron to quit.  Can also be accomplished by sending a break\nsignal to CyberCron.\n\nOnce SHUTDOWN is started, CyberCron will not accept any more ARexx\ncommands.\n\nIt may take a while for CyberCron to actually quit, however, because it\nmust wait for any currently running events to terminate.\n\nQUIT is accepted as a synonym of SHUTDOWN to provide comformity with the\n@{"Style Guide" link StyleGuide}.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	QUIT=SHUTDOWN\n\nEXAMPLES\n	Shutdown\n	Quit\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdVersion "The »VERSION« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nReturns the version, revision and date of CyberCron in the form\n\n    <version>.<revision> (<day>.<month>.<year>)\n\nThis is returned in the RESULT string, so you must issue an @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults}\ncommand before you can use this command.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	VERSION\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} Version\n	say result\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdSuspend "The »SUSPEND« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nStop processing events.  @{"File notifcation" link FileNotification} on the @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile}\nremains active, however, so changes will be processed normally.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	SUSPEND\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Suspend\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdResume "The »RESUME« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nResume processing of events after a @{"SUSPEND" link RCmdSuspend} has been issued.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	RESUME\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Resume\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdNewEventFile "The »NEW_EVENT_FILE« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nChange the @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile} to <filespec>.  This checks to make sure the file\nactually exists before making the change.  However, parse errors will not\nbe noticed until the current crontab has been purged and CyberCron actually\nstarts reading the new file.\n\nIf something goes wrong (crontab file not found or its name is too long)\nwhich doesn''t cause the previous crontab file to have been purged yet then\nthis will return RC_WARN (5).\n\nIf a fatal error occurs (couldn''t restart @{"notification" link FileNotification} on the new file)\nand the previous crontab has been purged, but the new one not read in, this\nwill return RC_ERROR (10).\n\nTEMPLATE\n	NEW_EVENT_FILE FILESPEC/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	New_Event_File S:CronTab2\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdCloseEventFile "The »CLOSE_EVENT_FILE« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nFree all entries from the @{"CronTab" link ArgCronTab}.  Processing of entries added by the\nARexx @{"ADD_EVENT" link RCmdAddEvent} command will still continue.  Use @{"NEW_EVENT_FILE" link RCmdNewEventFile} to read a\n@{"crontab file" link CronTabFile} again, if you so desire.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	CLOSE_EVENT_FILE\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Close_Event_File\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdNewLogFile "The »NEW_LOG_FILE« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nChanges the @{"logfile" link ArgLogFile} to <filespec>.\n\nIf an error occurs (the new name is too long, for example) then RC_WARN (5)\nwill be returned and the old logfile, if specified, will remain active.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	NEW_LOG_FILE FILESPEC/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	New_Log_File T:CronLog\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdCloseLogFile "The »CLOSE_LOG_FILE« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nTurns off logging.  You can re-enable logging with the @{"NEW_LOG_FILE" link RCmdNewLogFile}\ncommand, if you so desire.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	CLOSE_LOG_FILE\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Close_Log_File\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdShowStatus "The »SHOW_STATUS« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nReturns a couple bits of information about CyberCron''s status.  This is in\nthe form of\n\n    @{"<activity>" link RCmdSuspend} @{"<crontab filespec>" link ArgCronTab} @{"<logfile filespec>" link ArgLogFile}\n\nwhere <activity> is either ACTIVE or SUSPENDED.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	SHOW_STATUS\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} Show_Status\n	say result\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdPurgeRexxEvents "The »PURGE_REXX_EVENTS« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nAll events added with the ARexx @{"ADD_EVENT" link RCmdAddEvent} command will be purged.\nProcessing of entries from the @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile} will continue normally.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	PURGE_REXX_EVENTS\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Purge_Rexx_Events\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdAddEvent "The »ADD_EVENT« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nAdds an event to CyberCron''s active list.  Use the same format as if you\nwere specifying an event in a @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile}.  Comments are not allowed.\n\nIf an error occurs in parsing the event, RC_ERROR (10) will be returned.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	ADD_EVENT EVENT/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	ADD_EVENT ''0 8 * * 1-5 Echo "Go to work!" @{">" link EArgReDirOut} CON:0/0/200/50/Hey!/CLOSE/WAIT''\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdDeleteRexxEvent "The »DELETE_REXX_EVENT« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nDeletes the specified event, but only if it is was added with the ARexx\n@{"ADD_EVENT" link RCmdAddEvent} command.\n\nIf the event was not found, RC_ERROR (10) will be returned.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	DELETE_REXX_EVENT @{"EVENT_NAME/A" link InternalNaming}\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Delete_Rexx_Event ''the event''\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdDeleteEvent "The »DELETE_EVENT« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nSame as @{"DELETE_REXX_EVENT" link RCmdDeleteRexxEvent}, but can be used to delete any event, even those\nread from the @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile}.\n\nIf the event was not found, RC_ERROR (10) will be returned.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	DELETE_EVENT @{"EVENT_NAME/A" link InternalNaming}\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Delete_Event ''the event''\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdListEvents "The »LIST_EVENTS« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nReturns a list of all the current events CyberCron is tracking, seperated\nby spaces.  Note that the events are listed using CyberCron''s internal\n@{"naming scheme" link InternalNaming}.\n\nThe result is returned in a result string, so you must use @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults}\nbefore calling this command, or you will get an error.\n\nIf there are no events, then the string "<None>" is returned.  If an error\noccured, an empty string will be returned.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	LIST_EVENTS\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} List_Events\n	say result\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdShowEvent "The »SHOW_EVENT« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nReturns the full specification for the specified event, as it would be\ngiven in a @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile}, except that it will have the event''s @{"internal name" link InternalNaming}\nprepended.\n\nNote that events may not look exactly the same as when they were added.\nThis is because CyberCron converts events into an internalized structure\nand unconverts them when this command is called.\n\nIf the event was not found then an empty string will be returned.\n\nYou must specify @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults} before using this command, or you will get\nan error.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	SHOW_EVENT @{"EVENT_NAME/A" link InternalNaming}\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} Show_Event ''the event''\n	say result\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdSetQueueMax "The »SET_QUEUE_MAX« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nSet''s the maximum number of jobs that may be run under the specified queue\nat any given time.\n\nIf an illegal queue name is specified, RC_ERROR (10) will be returned.\n\nSetting the maximum to zero is legal, and will result in no jobs for the\nspecified queue being executed until such a time as the queue maximum is\nraised to greater than zero again.\n\nSee the section on @{"queues" link JobQueues} for more information.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	SET_QUEUE_MAX QUEUE_NAME/A MAX_JOBS_EXECUTABLE_AT_A_TIME/N/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Set_Queue_Max a 3\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdGetQueueMax "The »GET_QUEUE_MAX« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nGet the current maximum number of jobs that can be run under the specified\nqueue at any given time.\n\nIf an illegal queue name is specified, -1 is returned.\n\nSee the section on @{"queues" link JobQueues} for more information.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	GET_QUEUE_MAX QUEUE_NAME/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	GET_QUEUE_MAX a\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdEventNextExec "The »EVENT_NEXT_EXEC« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nReturns the next time at which the specified event will execute at.\nThis is returned as the number of seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation}.\n\nIf the event cannot be found, a null string will be returned.\n\nCyberCron will look up to seven years into the future trying to find a time\nthat satisfies the requirements of the event''s timespec.  This is to allow\nfor the effect of leap years.\n\nIf CyberCron cannot determine a time at which the event would next execute,\nzero will be returned.\n\nThe result is returned in a result string, so you must use @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults}\nbefore calling this command, or you will get an error.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	EVENT_NEXT_EXEC @{"EVENT_NAME/A" link InternalNaming}\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Event_Next_Exec ''the event''\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdNextEventToExec "The »NEXT_EVENT_TO_EXEC« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nThis will scan through the event list, looking for the event that is due up\nfor execution next.  It returns a string in the form of\n\n	@{"<event name>" link InternalNaming} <SSSC>\n\nThe <SSSC> field is the seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation} that the event will\nexecute at.\n\nIf no event is found then the string "<None>" will be returned.\n\nThe result is returned in a result string, so you must use @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults}\nbefore calling this command, or you will get an error.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	NEXT_EVENT_TO_EXEC\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} Next_Event_To_Exec\n	nextevent = result\n	if (nextevent ~= ''<None>'') then\n		parse var nextevent event sssc .\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdExpandSSSC "The »EXPAND_SSSC« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nThis command will take the number of seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation} and\nexpand it into seperate fields for seconds, minutes, etc.  The result is\nreturned in a string in the form of\n\n	<sec> <min> <hour> <day> <month> <year> <day of the week>\n\nThis is useful for scripts that want to make decisions based on the results\nof @{"EVENT_NEXT_EXEC" link RCmdEventNextExec} or @{"NEXT_EVENT_TO_EXEC" link RCmdNextEventToExec}.\n\nThe result is returned in a result string, so you must use @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults}\nbefore calling this command, or you will get an error.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	EXPAND_SSSC SECONDS_SINCE_@{"SYSTEM_CREATION" link SystemCreation}/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} @{"Next_Event_To_Exec" link RCmdNextEventToExec}\n	nextevent = result\n	if (nextevent ~= ''<None>'') then do\n		parse var nextevent event sssc .\n		Expand_SSSC sssc\n		parse var result sec min hour day month year dow .\n	end\n		\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdSSSCtoASCII "The »SSSC_TO_ASCII« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n@next RCmdShutdown\n\nThis command will take the number of seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation} and\nreturn a human readable ASCII date string.\n\nThis is useful for scripts that want to display the results of @{"EVENT_NEXT_EXEC" link RCmdEventNextExec}\nor @{"NEXT_EVENT_TO_EXEC" link RCmdNextEventToExec} to the user.\n\nThe result is returned in a result string, so you must use @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults}\nbefore calling this command, or you will get an error.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	SSSC_TO_ASCII SECONDS_SINCE_@{"SYSTEM_CREATION" link SystemCreation}/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} @{"Next_Event_To_Exec" link RCmdNextEventToExec}\n	nextevent = result\n	if (nextevent ~= ''<None>'') then do\n		parse var nextevent event sssc .\n		@{"Show_Event" link RCmdShowEvent} event\n		eventDisp = result\n		if (eventDispl ~= '''') then do\n			say ''->'' || eventDisp\n			SSSC_To_ASCII sssc\n			say ''  next execution at" result\n		end\n	end\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##          End of nodes describing the various ARexx commands              ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node JobQueues "How job queues work in CyberCron"\n@prev RexxPort\n\nCyberCron supports 26 job queues, named ''a'' through ''z''.  Each queue has a\nmaximum number of jobs associated with it.  If an event has a queue\nspecified, not only must it be the appropriate time to run that event, but\nthe number of events currently running under that queue must be less than\nthe maximum allowed for the queue for the event to actually be executed.\n\nEvents that don''t specify an @{":OBEYQUEUE" link EArgObeyQueue} argument are run under the default\nqueue (not included in the 26 named queues that are user accessable), which\nallows an unlimited number of jobs to be executed at the same time.\n\nAll job queues are initilized with their maximum number of events to\nexecute set to one.  ARexx commands exist to @{"read" link RCmdGetQueueMax} and @{"change" link RCmdSetQueueMax} the maximum\nnumber for specific queues.\n\nJobs that could not be started because the queue maximum would be exceeded\nare marked as delayed.  They will be run the next time the queue is\navailable, regardless of whether or not their time spec matches the current\ntime they are up for scheduling.\n\nJob queues were originally added to support writting ARexx scripts that\nsimulate the UNIX @{"at" link AtCommand} and @{"batch" link BatchCommand} commands.  However, I''ve tried to make\nthe interface flexible and useful in other areas.  For example, queues can\nbe used to insure sequencing of events.  Make sure that the queue you will\nuse has its maximum set to one (which is the default for all queues).\nSpecify when the first job is to run.  Specify that the second job should\nrun one minute after the first job.  If the first job is still running when\nthe second comes up for scheduling, the second job will be delayed.\nCyberCron will check every minute after that to see if the first job has\ncompleted and will run the second job when it is safe.  Any number of jobs\ncould be sequenced together using a queue in this manner.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node InternalNaming "The internal naming scheme used by CyberCron"\n\nCyberCron''s @{"LIST_EVENTS" link RCmdListEvents} command will return event names in an internalized\nformat.  These names are in the form of 0x???????? where ? indicates a hex\ndigit.  This is done so that events that have no formal name can be\nspecified from ARexx.  It also allows events to be specified unambigously,\nwhich is useful if you have more than one event with the same formal name\nand you wish to delete/show/etc a specific one.  To prevent naming\nconflicts, CyberCron will not let you specify a formal name that begins\nwith the characters 0x (or 0X, as the case of the "x" doesn''t matter).\n\nWhen asking CyberCron to delete/show/etc an event, you can either use its\ninternal name, or, if it has a formal name, you may use that.  Internal\nnames are cumbersome to use by hand, and are impractical to handle except by\nusing console snap or under the control of an ARexx script.  Formal names\nare much easier to deal with, which is why they are present.  If you wanted\nto add a quick test event, you might give it a formal name of TestEvent and\nthen use that to delete it instead of having to figure out it''s internal\nname and then using it to delete the event.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Localization "How to make CyberCron speak your language"\n\nWhen running under Workbench 2.1, CyberCron has the ability to use a\ntranslation catalog which contains the text strings it uses in a different\nlanguage.  These translation files are stored in the user''s\nLOCALE:Catalogs/<language> directory.  For example, the French string\ncatalog for CyberCron would be the file:\n\n	LOCALE:Catalogs/français/CyberCron.catalog\n\nPlease note that case is important for the filename portion of the above\nfilespec.\n\nIt is also possible to make a Catalogs sub-directory in the directory where\nthe CyberCron executable resides.  This reduces the amount of clutter in\nthe user''s LOCALE: volume.  If you placed the CyberCron executable in\nSYS:WBStartup, then the corresponding location of the French catalog would\nbe:\n\n	SYS:WBStartup/Catalogs/français/CyberCron.catalog\n\nIf there isn''t a translation file for your language present in this\ndistribution (and there most likely won''t be as I don''t know any foreign\nlanguages :->), you can make your own.  To do this, you need to:\n\n	1) edit the dummy.ct file in the Source directory, translating\n	   it as appropriate.  you should be looking at the\n	   Source/CyberCronStrings.cd file as you do this since it\n	   contains many important notes about what the different\n	   strings are used for.\n\n	2) run CatComp (a Commodore developer tool which I can''t include)\n	   to make the actual catalog that CyberCron will use.  For\n	   example:\n\n		catcomp Source/CyberCronStrings.cd <language>.ct catalog\n		    locale:Catalogs/<language>/CyberCron.catalog\n\n		(the above is all on one CLI line, of course)\n\nIf you don''t have access to CatComp then feel free to @{"e-mail" link CAW} me your\ntranslation (lha and uuencode it, though, or any special accent marks, etc,\nwill be lost) and I''ll run it through CatComp.  I''d like any translation\nfiles people come up with, anyway, so I can distribute them with future\nversions of CyberCron.  Also, if you feel that one of the currently\navailable translation files has something which could be translated in a\nbetter way, feel free to mail me those changes as well.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ChangeLog "The history of CyberCron"\n@next Credits\n\nVersion 1.0 -- Initial release\n\nVersion 1.1 -- Maintenance release the day after 1.0 was released.\n               Don''t remember what it fixed, though... :-)\n\n@{" Version 1.2 " link Change1p2}\n\n@{" Version 1.3 " link Change1p3}\n\n@{" Version 1.4 " link Change1p4}\n\n@{" Version 1.5 " link Change1p5}\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##             Start of nodes describing CyberCron revisions                ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node Change1p2 "Changes in version 1.2 of CyberCron"\n@toc ChangeLog\n@prev Change1p5\n\nIt has been pointed out to me that I forgot to specify the default rexx\nextension of ".rexx".  Thus, scripts in the form of\n\n	foo.rexx\n\nwould not be started unless you actually stated @{":REXX" link EArgRexx} foo.rexx instead of\nbeing able to use @{":REXX" link EArgRexx} foo alone.\n\nAlso added a semaphore lock in the GetJobNum()/FreeJobNum() routines to\nprevent the job table from possibly being clobbered by EndSystemJob()\ngetting called at the same time CyberCron was trying to start another job.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Change1p3 "Changes in version 1.3 of CyberCron"\n@toc ChangeLog\n\nIt has been requested that CyberCron become sensitive to small changes\nbackwards in time.  This is usually caused by reloading the system clock\nfrom the battery backed clock once a day (as the battery backed clock tends\nto keep better time on some machines).  Before running the event scan,\nCyberCron will now check to see if time has gone backwards and will delay\nevent scans until it reaches the next minute that it would have actually\nrun a scan at if the time hadn''t shifted backwards.  There is a 5 minute\nthreshhold limit, however, so if time goes backwards more than that (for\ninstance, daylight savings time changes state and you go back an hour) then\nCyberCron assumes you know what you are doing and will use the new time\nwithout delay.\n\nI also switched the static declarations of struct RDArgs to\nAllocDosObject()/FreeDosObject() pairs.  This should help insure that\nCyberCron will run on any future versions of the OS, even if it will be a\nfew eons before I actually would have had to worry about this... :-)\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Change1p4 "Changes in version 1.4 of CyberCron"\n@toc ChangeLog\n\nFixed handling of commands with a space in them.  Previously, they would be\nindicated as not found.  Now they will work correctly.  Also modified the\nlog output a bit to indicate the command name portion of the command.  The\ncommand is quoted with »« characters.\n\nWhen CyberCron is first started, it will now spit an "I''m alive" message\nout to the logfile.  Brian Vargyas wanted this so he could tell when his\nBBS had rebooted by reading through the log.\n\nCyberCron now checks to see if it is already running and will exit\ngracefully if so.  Added new @{"PORTNAME" link ArgPortName} option to startup arguments to allow\nmultiple CyberCrons to be run if needed by specifying different ARexx port\nnames for each.\n\nModified CCShow.rexx to accept one argument when started to be used as the\nportname to address.  This allows one to easly display the active jobs for\nany CyberCron task running.  If no portname is specified, CCShow defaults\nto CYBERCRON, which is CyberCron''s @{"PORTNAME" link ArgPortName} default.\n\nAliased the @{"CRONPRI" link ArgCronPriority} startup option to TOOLPRI to try and be a bit more\ncomformant with the @{"Style Guide" link StyleGuide}.  Similarily, QUIT is now recognized as a\nsynonym for the @{"SHUTDOWN" link RCmdShutDown} ARexx command.\n\nBecause CyberCron now officially supports multiple copies of itself\nrunning, I have made it use a public locking mechanism for the @{"logfile" link ArgLogFile}.\nThis way, one can have the same logfile for all instances of CyberCron and\nnot have to worry about it getting clobbered.  The job number list is also\nshared between concurrent CyberCrons as well.\n\nDocumentation redone as an AmigaGuide file.  The hypertext nature of\nAmigaGuide makes it a lot easier to navigate around the CyberCron\ndocumentation and find information quickly.\n\nMike Sinz has provided me with wb2cli.o, a linker object that creates a CLI\nstructure for me when CyberCron is started from the Workbench.  This allows\nCyberCron to propagate the path that existed at LoadWB time to jobs it\nstarts.\n\nUpdated the ErrorMsg() function to throw up an EasyRequester when CyberCron\nis started from the WorkBench.  This was done because there is no StdErr to\nwrite errors to in this case.  Once CyberCron is up and fully running,\nErrorMsg() calls will not use a requester, however.  At this point, only\nthings like "Crontab changed, re-reading" are sent to ErrorMsg().  Since\nthese are also sent to the Log() routine, the information isn''t lost and it\nallows CyberCron to remain non-interactive.\n\nAdded @{"localization" link Localization} support.  Now I need to find people to translate the\nstring catalog to other languages for me... :-)\n\nEnhanced the WBtoCLIargs() routine.  Specifically, it now calls FindArg()\non each tooltype to decide if it should copy the tooltype over into the\nargument string.  This prevents ReadArgs() from seeing arguments it doesn''t\nknow about and generating a "wrong number of arguments" error.  This was\ndone to make it possible to include tooltypes such as DONOTWAIT in\nCyberCron''s .info file.  This makes it easy to drop CyberCron in one''s\nWBStartup drawer.\n\nModified the lmkfile to run source code through the indent filter.  This\nhelps make the code look a bit nicer by cleaning up comments as well as\ninsuring that the proper indentation levels are in use.\n\nBy popular demand, removed the ANSI control sequences from the startup and\nhelp messages.\n\nNoticed the Amiga2Date() function in utility.library.  Changed\nGetSystemTime() to make use of it, saving 350+ bytes of code.\n\nFound and squashed an Enforcer hit that would occur in DoMsg() when a\nroutine wanted to return a null string.\n\nAdded four new ARexx commands.  Two, @{"EVENT_NEXT_EXEC" link RCmdEventNextExec} and @{"NEXT_EVENT_TO_EXEC" link RCmdNextEventToExec},\ndeal with determining the time at which an event will execute at.  Both\nreturn their results as the number of seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation}.\nTo make dealing with this result easier, the @{"EXPAND_SSSC" link RCmdExpandSSSC} and @{"SSSC_TO_ASCII" link RCmdSSSCtoASCII}\ncommands were added.\n\nUpdated CCShow.rexx to display the time each job is next scheduled to\nexecute at by using the new @{"EVENT_NEXT_EXEC" link RCmdEventNextExec} and @{"SSSC_TO_ASCII" link RCmdSSSCtoASCII} Rexx commands.\n\nIf OwnDevUnit.library is present, CyberCron will now try and obtain a lock\non LOG-UPDATE.LOCK before trying to write to its logfile.  This will\nprotect its log updates from AmigaUUCP''s trimfile executable.  For this to\nwork properly, however, you must be using v2.1 or better of\nOwnDevUnit.library.\n\nIn previous versions of CyberCron, if a @{"queue" link JobQueues} was already executing the\nmaximum number of jobs it was set to and more jobs were due to be\nscheduled, then only the first job would be delayed and the rest would be\nskipped.  Now all such jobs will be delayed.\n\nRecompiled under SAS/C 6.0.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Change1p5 "Changes in version 1.5 of CyberCron"\n@toc ChangeLog\n@next Change1p2\n\nImplemented some suggestions that Martin Taillefer sent me.  Specifically:\n\n	Fixed the documentation to refer to Workbench ?.? instead of\n	KickStart ?.?, which is the proper terminology.\n\n	Removed the OC_BuiltInLanguage tag from the OpenCatalog() call\n	since this is the default prodided by locale.library anyway.\n\n	Eliminated MySPrintf() and now use sprintf() from amiga.lib since\n	it''s smaller.\n\n	Removed some checks before freeing items in the cleanup code since\n	as of V37 things like CloseLibrary(), FreeVec(), etc, all let you\n	free things that weren''t previously allocated.\n\n	Modified the aborting of timer I/O so that the Disable()/Enable()\n	are no longer used.\n\nGot the new version of CatComp.  Fixed CyberCron to compile using it.\nReduces the size of the executable by about 300 bytes due to removal of\nmany unneeded reloc-32''s.\n\nFor @{"v1.4" link Change1p4}, I made the routine that starts system jobs always enclose the\ncommand name in quotes.  It has been brought to my attention that this\nbreaks many scripts.  As of v1.5, CyberCron no longer places quotes around\nthe command name indiscriminately.  Quotes are now inserted only when the\ncommand name actually has a space in it.\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##              End of nodes describing CyberCron revisions                 ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node Credits "Credits"\n@prev ChangeLog\n\nDon Nafis, author of @{"NazCron" link NazCron}, from which I pretty much stole the idea for\nthe initial ARexx command set recognized by CyberCron.\n\nThomas Rokicki for a little bit of magic in the GetSystemTime() routine.\n(Although this is no longer used as of v1.4.)\n\nLoren Rittle for, well, many things.  He''s responsible for getting me to\nadd the stuff needed to support writing @{"at" link AtCommand} and @{"batch" link BatchCommand} UNIX like commands\n(though I hope to have done it in a means general and flexible enough that\nothers may be able to use the event options in their own, new ways) and\nwriting the at.rexx and batch.rexx scripts, despite the time presure he is\nunder right now.  He also helped catch a number of bugs, offered little\nsuggestions about functionality here and there and then there''s always the\n__emit() trick in MySPrintfSupp() (I nearly cried when I saw it in his\nls-4.5 code --- it never dawned on me to do anything so simple :->).\n\nMike Sinz for providing the wb2cli.o linker module.  This actually makes\nrunning CyberCron from the Workbench a useful thing to do.\n\nMike Koch for drawing the icon that I finally selected for CyberCron.  I''d\nalso like to thank the other people who sent in icons, particularily Pang Sie\nPiau, who sent me a couple rounds of icons as I made suggestions to him.\nHis icons have a much better Workbench 2.0 look to them, but in the end I\ndecided that Mike''s better denotes the idea of a cron.\n\nAmish Dave for moral support and helping me clean up the documentation a\nbit.\n\nMartin Taillefer for sending me some comments that helped tweak the code\nin various places to make CyberCron slightly smaller and more efficient.\n\nGeorg Sassen for tracking down the problem with scripts that v1.4\nintroduced.  (See the @{"v1.5 changes" link Change1p5} for how this was fixed.)\n\nSebastiano Vigna and Paolo Lenzi for the Italian translation catalog.\n\nXavier Drèze for the French translation catalog.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node CAW "Contacting the author"\n@next main\n\nIf you happen to find a bug or have a suggestion for CyberCron, or just\nwant to say "hey, cool program", please contact me using one of the ways\nlisted below.  Even if you wanna say "CyberCron sucks", let me know and be\nsure to say why you feel this way so that I might be able to fix what you\nthink is wrong with the program.\n\nThese electronic forms are the most prefered means of contacting me.  They\nwill get you a response pretty quick.\n\n	e-mail: [email removed]\n	BIX: caw\n\nSnail Mail is pretty slow and I''m not known for being very good about\nresponding to it...  :-)\n\n	Christopher A. Wichura\n	5450 East View Park\n	Chicago, Il.  60615\n	USA\n\nYou can also reach me by phone.  However, please try to limit your calling\nto evening hours (I''m in the central time zone).  If I''m not home and you\nleave a message, call back again anyway.  Around here, one tends to get\nmaybe 5% of the messages left for them, if lucky...  :-)\n\n	(312)/684-2941\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##                 Misc nodes for informational purposes                    ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node OptionsResults "The use of »OPTIONS RESULTS« in an ARexx script"\n@prev OptionsResults\n@next OptionsResults\n\nARexx requires that one use the OPTIONS RESULTS command before making any\ncalls that return a result string (as opposed to a result code/number).\n\n@endnode\n\n@node FileNotification "What file notification does under Workbench 2.04 or greater"\n@prev FileNotification\n@next FileNotification\n\nFile notification is a new feature of Workbench 2.04.  It allows a program\nto ask the operating system to tell it when a file has been created,\nchanged or deleted.\n\nBy using file notification, it is no longer necessary for programs such as\ncrons to constantly check if a critical configuration file has been\nupdated.  This results in a significant increase in system performance.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node NazCron "NazCron"\n@prev NazCron\n@next NazCron\n\nNazCron is a cron for the Amiga that was written by Don Nafis.  It was\nreleased as Shareware.\n\nCyberCron has strong roots in NazCron, primarily in it''s @{"ARexx command set" link RexxPort}.\nAlmost all its commands are the same as under NazCron.  The new commands I\nadded to cover features in CyberCron that NazCron does not have are done in\na style very similar to the way NazCron handled things.\n\nPlease note that CyberCron is based on NazCron in spirit only.  No code\nfrom NazCron has been incorporated into CyberCron.\n\nIf you are someone still using Workbench 1.3 (god forbid!) and are looking\nfor a rather nice cron, you might consider trying to find a copy of\nNazCron.\n\nThe last information I have on how to contact Don Nafis is from the NazCron\ndocumentation, dated 1989.  It is as follows:\n\n               Don Nafis\n               Nazlo Associates Ltd.\n               P.O. Box 1515\n               Laurel Springs, NJ  08021\n\n               (609) 228-8088 Voice\n\n               (609) 227-8278 BBS -\n                               Viva! Amiga!\n                         3/12/24 - 8/N/1 - 24 hours\n                            130 Megabytes Online\n\n               Compuserve 70656, 133\n\n@endnode\n\n@node AtCommand "The »at« command"\n@prev BatchCommand\n\nThe UNIX ''at'' command executes a command (or a sequence of commands) at a\nspecific time.  For example, you can do something like\n\n	prompt>at next tuseday 8\n	echo "Tomorrow is your wife''s birthday.  Did you get the present?"\n	EOF (the keystroke that indicates end of file, CTRL-\ on the Amiga)\n\nand at 8am next tuesday the echo command would be run.  One might think\nthat the output of this echo command would never be seen, right?  Wrong.\n''At'' allows one to specify where the output is to be redirected.  If it is\nnot redirected then the system e-mails the output of the command to the\nuser who submitted the ''at'' job.\n\nFor a more complete description, consult the Unix man pages on the ''at''\ncommand.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node BatchCommand "The »Batch« command"\n@next AtCommand\n\nThe UNIX ''batch'' command is actually a special case of the @{"at" link AtCommand} command.\nInstead of executing a job at a specific time, the batch command submits\nthe job for immediate execution.  @{"Job queues" link JobQueues} insure that only a few\n''batch''ed jobs are actually running at one time, however.\n\n''batch'' is intended for running jobs that require a lot of CPU time.\n\nFor more information, consult the Unix man pages on the ''batch'' command.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node PipeDevice "The Commodore PIPE: device"\n@prev PipeDevice\n@next PipeDevice\n\nThe Commodore supplied PIPE: device can be used to route the output of one\nprogram into the input stream of another.  This is done in a memory\nefficient way which does not require large temporary files to be created.\n\nFor more information on the PIPE: device and how to mount it, see your\n"Using the System Software" manual.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node StyleGuide "AMIGA User Interface Style Guide"\n@prev StyleGuide\n@next StyleGuide\n\nThe "Amiga User Interface Style Guide" is a reference manual Commodore has\nwritten that has many useful suggestions on making Amiga applications more\nsimilar.  The idea is that it makes it easy for a user to use a new\napplication because many of the things they have learned from previous\napplications will still be valid.\n\nThe style guide is published by Addison-Wesley Publishing Company, Inc.\nIt''s ISBN number is 0-201-57757-7.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node SystemCreation "System Creation"\n@prev SystemCreation\n@next SystemCreation\n\nSystem creation on the Amiga is the zeroth minute of the zeroth hour on\nJanuary 1st, 1978.\n\n@endnode\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',42864,2,0,NULL,NULL,'emp_mt17','scan',1780048498);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('emp_mt20','EMP-MT20.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/EMP-MT20.LHA','MonthTop','XIM','CyberCron',NULL,'EMPiRE/[SBS]','EMP','/~ __/~V~X~. \/~V~\/~X~___/~__/~\/~. \/~  \ \__  \ |  \ _ \ _  \  \_  \_  \  \ |  \ |  \ / ___/____/___/_V__/__/___/___/__/____/_|__/',replace(' ______ _ ___  _ _  _ __________  ___  ___  \n/~ __/~V~X~. \/~V~\/~X~___/~__/~\/~. \/~  \ \n\__  \ |  \ _ \ _  \  \_  \_  \  \ |  \ |  \\n/ ___/____/___/_V__/__/___/___/__/____/_|__/\n\/--\/---\/--\/---\/-\/--\/--\/-\/---\/----\nAE UTIL: MonthTop V2.0   - by EMPiRE/[SBS]  \n - Now Includes Upload & Download Showing!\n','\n',char(10)),'CyberCron.guide',replace('@database "CyberCron.guide"\n\n@width 80\n\n@node Main "CyberCron documentation"\n@prev CAW\n\n                                 CyberCron\n\n                Copyright © 1992 by Christopher A. Wichura\n                           All rights reserved.\n\n@{" Legal Mumbo Jumbo " link LegalStuff}\n@{" Supplied Files " link SuppliedFiles}\n@{" What is a cron? " link DefineCron}\n@{" Why another cron for the Amiga? " link WhyCyberCron}\n@{" Starting CyberCron " link StartingUp}\n@{" Creating a crontab file " link CronTabFile}\n@{" CyberCron as an ARexx Function Host " link RexxPort}\n@{" How job queues work in CyberCron " link JobQueues}\n@{" The internal naming scheme used by CyberCron " link InternalNaming}\n@{" How to make CyberCron speak your language " link Localization}\n@{" The revision history of CyberCron " link ChangeLog}\n@{" Credits " link Credits}\n@{" Contacting the author " link CAW}\n@endnode\n\n@node LegalStuff "Legal Mumbo Jumbo"\n\nCyberCron is not in the public domain.  All source files, along with the\nresulting executable, are copyright by C. Wichura.  You may not sell\nCyberCron.  The only allowed charge that may be placed on CyberCron is for\nmedia and/or mailing costs.\n\nCyberCron may be freely redistributed via BBSs, InterNet/Usenet, and disk\nlibraries such as Fred Fish''s, as long as the archive is not modified.\nDisk magazines and services that charge extra for file transfers may not\ndistribute CyberCron.\n\nIn using CyberCron, you accept the responsibility for any damage or loss of\nproductivity/money that may occur through or during its use.  C. Wichura\nis not and cannot be held accountable.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node SuppliedFiles "Supplied Files"\n\nAll source to CyberCron is included in the Source/* directory.  Please do\nnot redistribute changes you make.  Instead, send them to me for inclusion\nin the master distribution.\n\nThe s directory contains a sample crontab file.  This is the one I am\ncurrently using on my system.  It contains a number of comments that give\nsome helpful hints on how to use some of the more advanced features of\nCyberCron.\n\nIn RexxScripts is CCShow.rexx, based on the NCShow.rexx script that came with\n@{"NazCron" link NazCron}.  It will call @{"LIST_EVENTS" link RCmdListEvents} and if there are active events, it will\nloop through the list calling @{"SHOW_EVENT" link RCmdShowEvent} to display each one.\n\nAlso in the rexx directory you will find the files at.rexx and batch.rexx.\nThese are the files Loren Rittle sent me that implement the UNIX @{"at" link AtCommand}\nand @{"batch" link BatchCommand} commands using CyberCron.  They have since\nbeen updated by Graham Walter to be much more complete.\n\nThe Catalogs directory is where @{"translation catalogs" link Localization} for languages that\nCyberCron has been converted to are found.  If you are running under\nWorkbench 2.1 and would like to use CyberCron in your language, copy the\nappropriate file into your LOCALE:Catalogs/<language> directory.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node DefineCron "What is a cron?"\n\nA cron is a program that runs in the background (i.e., requires no user\nintervention) and executes commands at specific times, as defined in a\n@{"crontab file" link CronTabFile}.  It allows the automation of jobs that one runs regularily.\n\nFor example, many maintenance tasks can be handled by a cron event.\nIncremental system backups to a streaming tape could be started at a time\nwhen one is generally away from the machine every day or every week, for\nexample.  Or you might have a cron entry run a script that logs onto BIX,\nCompuServe, or wherever and downloads all your mail and unread messages\nautomatically during the night when rates are lower.\n\nA cron can also be used as a means of reminding yourself to do things.  You\nmight add an event that runs an alarm clock program in the morning to wake\nyou up, for example.  Or you might have it throw a requester up telling you\nto go to bed when it is getting late.  The possibilities are endless.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node WhyCyberCron "Why another cron for the Amiga?"\n\nThere have been several crons for the Amiga already.  These include\nAmiCron, dcron, TPTCron and @{"NazCron" link NazCron}, to name a few.  So what''s the\nneed for yet another cron?\n\nCyberCron was written to take advantage of the new features of Workbench\n2.04 (or greater), and thus has many advanced options that previous crons\ndon''t.  It''s this new functionality that makes switching over to CyberCron\nworthwhile.\n\nCyberCron uses the new System() call in dos.library to launch events.  This\ncall is far superior to the Execute() call of Workbench 1.3 days which the\nother crons use.  Through the use of the System() call, CyberCron can\nsupport options such as the stack size or priority to launch specific\nevents at.\n\nCyberCron also understands ARexx.  It can start events as ARexx commands\nand/or scripts.  It also has an ARexx port which can accept a number of\n@{"ARexx commands" link RexxPort} to control it while running.\n\nCyberCron supports @{"localization" link Localization}.  If you are running it on a machine with\nlocale.library and have a translation catalog for your local language,\nCyberCron''s messages will appear in your language instead of English.\n\nFinally, CyberCron also uses @{"file notification" link FileNotification} to determine when the\ncrontab file has been changed.  This eliminates the performance hit that\nprevious Amiga crons have suffered from due to the fact that they had to\ncheck the crontab file for updates once a minute.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node StartingUp "Starting CyberCron"\n@next CronTabFile\n\nYou can start CyberCron from either the CLI or Workbench.\n\nWhen CyberCron is started from the Workbench, it trys to clone a copy of\nthe Workbench''s CLI structure.  What this means is that the command path\nthat existed at the time LoadWB was run will be propagated on to commands\nCyberCron launches.\n\nCyberCron understands the following arguments:\n\n    [@{" CRONTAB " link ArgCronTab} <filespec>] [@{" LOGFILE " link ArgLogFile} <filespec>]\n    [@{" DEFSTACK " link ArgDefStack} <Default Stack>] [@{" DEFPRI " link ArgDefPriority} <Default Priority>]\n    [@{" SENDMAIL " link ArgSendMail} <"{command} {from switch} %s {name switch} *"%s*"">]\n    [@{" CRONPRI " link ArgCronPriority} <Cron''s Priority>] [@{" PORTNAME " link ArgPortName} <ARexx Portname>]\n\nAll arguments are optional.  If not specified, CyberCron will use an\nappropriate default.  See the specific options'' documentation for more\ninformation.\n\nTo use these options from a Workbench icon, simply make each tooltype be in\nthe form of <option>=<setting>.  For example, if you wanted to specify a\nlogfile of T:CronLog, you would have a tooltype that looked like\n\n	LOGFILE=T:CronLog\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##      These nodes are for the various command line argument links         ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node ArgCronTab "The »CronTab« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n@prev ArgPortName\n\nThis specifies the @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile} that CyberCron will try and read.  If not\nspecified, s:CronTab will be used as the default.\n\nThe crontab file may also be changed while CyberCron is running by using\nthe @{"NEW_EVENT_FILE" link RCmdNewEventFile} ARexx command.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	CRONTAB/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	CronTab cron:ctab\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ArgLogFile "The »LogFile« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n\nThis indicates where CyberCron is to log activities.  If no filespec is\ngiven, logging will be turned off.\n\nThe logfile may also be changed while CyberCron is running by using the\n@{"NEW_LOG_FILE" link RCmdNewLogFile} ARexx command.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	LOGFILE/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	LogFile T:CronLog\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ArgSendMail "The »SendMail« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n\nCyberCron supports sending the output of a command it has launched to a\nuser via e-mail.  For this to work, however, it needs to know how to send\nmail on the local system.  This command tells CyberCron how to do this.\n\nIf the sendmail option is not specified then entries which try to direct\ntheir output to e-mail will not be able to.  File @{"output redirection" link EArgReDirOut} will\nbe honored instead.\n\nThe argument for this command should be in the form of\n\n    "<sendmail command> <from switch> %s <realname switch> *"%s*""\n\nFor example, for AmigaUUCP''s sendmail one would use\n\n    "UUCP:c/SendMail -f %s -R *"%s*""\n\nFor CyberCron to be able to send mail, the sendmail command must read the\nmail message from its standard input.  This is because CyberCron sets up a\npipe to redirect output into sendmail.  This also means that you must have\n@{"PIPE:" link PipeDevice} mounted before mail can be sent.\n\nThe sendmail command must also expect the To: and Subject: fields to be in\nits input instead of on the command line, and must generate the Message-ID:\nfield itself.\n\nAmigaUUCP''s SendMail command meets all the above criteria.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	SENDMAIL/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	SendMail "UUCP:c/SendMail -f %s -R *"%s*""\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ArgDefStack "The »DefStack« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n\nThis is the default stack size to launch executables with.  Values less\nthan 2048 will be rejected.\n\nThis argument is ignored when launching ARexx events.\n\nIf not specified, the stack size that CyberCron was started with will be\nused as the default.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	DEFSTACK/K/N\n\nEXAMPLE\n	DefStack 25000\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ArgDefPriority "The »DefPri« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n\nThis specifies the default priority to launch executables at.\n\nThis option is ignored by ARexx events.\n\nIf not specified, this option defaults to zero.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	DEFPRI/K/N\n\nEXAMPLE\n	DefPri -1\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ArgCronPriority "The »CronPri« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n\nThis is the priority that CyberCron should run at.\n\nIf not specified, this option defaults to no change.  I.e., CyberCron will\nremain that the priority it was started at.\n\nToolPri is accepted as an alias for CronPri.  This is for conformity with\nthe @{"Style Guide" link StyleGuide}.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	TOOLPRI=CRONPRI/K/N\n\nEXAMPLE\n	CronPri 4\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ArgPortName "The »PortName« command line argument"\n@toc StartingUp\n@next ArgCronTab\n\nThis option specifies the ARexx portname that CyberCron will use.\n\nIf not specified, CyberCron defaults to a portname of CYBERCRON.\n\nCyberCron will not let you start it multiple times with the same port name.\nIt does, however, support the use of concurrent CyberCron tasks which each\nhave their own portname.\n\nUsing this option, one could have multiple crontab files.  One might also\nchoose to have one CyberCron task that handles their @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile} while\na second is used as a server for script commands such as the @{"at" link AtCommand} command.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	PORTNAME/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	PortName CyberCronAtServer\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##      End of nodes describing the various command line arguments          ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node CronTabFile "Creating a crontab file"\n@prev StartingUp\n@next RexxPort\n\nLines in this file must be blank, a comment, or an event.  Comments are\ndenoted by lines that start with a "#" symbol.  Events take the form of\n\n	min hour day month dow command <command''s args, if any>\n\nin their simplest form.  Each of min, hour, day, month and dow (day of the\nweek) are specifications for what range of values this command is to be run\nat.  A * indicates any value is acceptable.  Numeric digits specify\nspecific values and can be seperated by a , (seperates distinct entries) or\na - (used to indicate a range of entries).  For example,\n\n	* * * * * Date\n\nwould run the command date every minute of every hour of every day of every\nmonth regardless of the day of the week.\n\n	0 * * * * Date\n\nis similar, but only runs the date command on the zeroth minute of each\nhour.\n\n	1,5-10,25 * * * * Date\n\nwould run the date command on minutes 1, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 and 25 of each\nhour.\n\nMonths range from 1 (January) to 12 (December).  The day of the week ranges\nfrom 0 (Sunday) to 6 (Saturday).\n\nCyberCron also supports a number of extensions to the traditional event\nformat.  These may occur anywhere in the event, and are:\n\n    @{" :NAME " link EArgName} <name>\n    @{" < " link EArgReDirIn} <filespec>\n    @{" > " link EArgReDirOut} <filespec>\n    @{" 2> " link EArgReDirErr} <filespec>\n    @{" :MAILUSER " link EArgMailUser} <user name>\n    @{" :EXECONCE " link EArgExecOnce}\n    @{" :OBEYQUEUE " link EArgObeyQueue} <queue name>\n    @{" :NOLOG " link EArgNoLog}\n    @{" :REXX " link EArgRexx}\n    @{" :STACK " link EArgStack} <bytes>\n    @{" :PRI " link EArgPriority} <priority>\n    @{" :CUSTOMSH " link EArgCustomShell} <shell name>\n    @{" :SYSSH " link EArgSystemShell}\n\nPlease note that these above options are parsed with the dos.library\nReadArgs() command.  Because of this, those options which take a variable\nmust have whitespace between the option''s keyword and its assignment.  This\ngenerally isn''t a problem, except that most people I know are used to\nspecifying redirection as\n\n	@{">" link EArgReDirOut}ram:foo\n\nwhile the proper method for CyberCron would be\n\n	@{">" link EArgReDirOut} ram:foo\n\nThe other implication of using ReadArgs() is that quotes will be stripped\nout, as will extra spaces.  To retain spaces, you must inclose the element\nthey proceed or follow with quotes.  For example, one might use\n\n	date @{">" link EArgReDirOut} "Ram:Two  Spaces"\n\nTo retain quotes, you will have to quote the argument as you would for\nspaces, but escape a quote character withing the quoted string.  For\nexample.\n\n	"This has a *" quote in it"\n\n\nThere is one aditional special case that needs to be observed when creating\nan event.  If the command you are running is actually a script _and_ it\ncontains a space in it''s filename, you must explicitly call the AmigaDOS\nExecute command for it to work.  For example, you''d have to use\n\n	execute "foo bar script"\n\ninstead of\n\n	"foo bar script"\n\n(those people with WShell who want to execute an ARexx script from a WShell\ncontext would use WShell''s Rexx command instead of Execute.)\n\nThis is due to a problem that the AmigaDOS shell has executing script''s\nwhose command name has been quoted.  This only applies to scripts which\nhave spaces in their name, so very few people should be affected by this\nlimitation.\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##             These nodes descibe the various event options                ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node EArgName "The »:NAME« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n@prev EArgSystemShell\n\nSpecifies the formal name the event will be known under when CyberCron is\naccessed via its @{"ARexx port" link RexxPort}.\n\nYou may use the same name multiple time.  However, this is not recommened.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:NAME/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:Name WakeMeUp\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgReDirIn "The input redirection event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nThis specifies where input is to be redirected from.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	</K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	< "CON:0/0/300/100/Gimme some input"\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgReDirOut "The output redirection event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nSpecifies where output is to be redirected to.\n\nIf >> is used instead of >, the filespec will be appended to instead of\noverwritten.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	>/K or >>/K\n\nEXAMPLES\n	> RAM:job.out\n	>> RAM:job.logfile\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgReDirErr "The error redirection event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nSpecifies where standard error is to be redirected to.\n\nIf 2>> is used instead of 2>, the filespec will be appended to instead of\noverwritten.\n\nNote that this is currently ignored by the event launcher per a discussion\nI had with Randell Jesup.  Standard error support wasn''t really finished\nfor 2.0, and that makes it difficult to support this at this time.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	2>/K or 2>>/K\n\nEXAMPLES\n	2> RAM:job.err\n	2>> RAM:job.errlog\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgMailUser "The »:MAILUSER« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nSpecifies a user to send mail to with the output of the command being run.\nIf the @{"SENDMAIL" link ArgSendMail} command line option was not specified, this will be ignored\nwhen the event is started, and any @{"output redirection" link EArgReDirOut} specified will be\nused, instead.\n\nThe username may be any e-mail address, so mail can be sent to both local\nand remote systems.  If your sendmail supports it, you could even specify\nan alias and have it send mail to multiple people.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:MAILUSER/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:MailUser root\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgExecOnce "The »:EXECONCE« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nAfter the event has been started, remove it from the event list.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:EXECONCE/S\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:ExecOnce\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgObeyQueue "The »:OBEYQUEUE« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nSelect a specific queue to schedule the event under.  See the section on\n@{"queues" link JobQueues} for more information.\n\nUsing this, @{":EXECONCE" link EArgExecOnce}, @{":MAILUSER" link EArgMailUser} and some ARexx macros, one could simulate\nthe @{"at" link AtCommand} and @{"batch" link BatchCommand} commands under UNIX.  (See Loren''s @{"at" link AtCommand} stuff for an\nexample of how.)\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:OBEYQUEUE/K\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:ObeyQueue c\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgNoLog "The »:NOLOG« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nIf you specified a @{"logfile" link ArgLogFile} then every time an event is started or ends a\nmessage gets sent to the log file.  This turns off logging for the specific\nevent.  Handy for things one runs many, many times, in keeping the size of\nyour logfile from exploding.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:NOLOG/S\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:NoLog\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgRexx "The »:REXX« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nStart this command using ARexx rather than trying to run it as a system\ncall.  If the first character of the command is "`" then it will be started\nas an ARexx string rather than a command.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:REXX/S\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:Rexx\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgStack "The »:STACK« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nThe stacksize to use when launching the event.\n\nThis option is ignored for ARexx events.\n\nIf the value specified is less than 2048, or this option is omitted, then\nthe @{"default stack size" link ArgDefStack} will be used instead.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:STACK/K/N\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:Stack 50000\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgPriority "The »:PRI« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nThe priority to launch the event at.\n\nThis option is ignored for ARexx events.\n\nIf this option is omitted then the @{"default priority" link ArgDefPriority} will be used.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:PRI/K/N\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:PRI -5\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgCustomShell "The »:CUSTOMSH« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n\nUse a specific custom shell when launching the event.  See your "Using the\nSystem Software" manual for more information on custom shells.\n\nThis option is ignored by ARexx events.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:CUSTOMSH/K\n\nEXAMPLES\n	:CustomSH WShell\n	:CustomSH BootShell\n\n@endnode\n\n@node EArgSystemShell "The »:SYSSH« event line option"\n@toc CronTabFile\n@next EArgName\n\nIf no custom shell is specified, CyberCron will try and launch the command\nusing the specified User Shell.  If you do not want the User Shell to be\nused for a specific event, specifying this flag will cause CyberCron to use\nthe system shell.\n\nThis option is ignored by ARexx events.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	:SYSSH/S\n\nEXAMPLE\n	:SysSH\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##           End of nodes descibing the various event options               ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node RexxPort "CyberCron as an ARexx Function Host"\n@prev CronTabFile\n@next JobQueues\n\nCyberCron''s default ARexx port name is CYBERCRON.  However, this can be\nchanged with the @{"PORTNAME" link ArgPortName} command line option.\n\nCyberCron supports the following ARexx commands:\n\n    @{" SHUTDOWN " link RCmdShutdown}\n    @{" VERSION " link RCmdVersion}\n    @{" SUSPEND " link RCmdSuspend}\n    @{" RESUME " link RCmdResume}\n    @{" NEW_EVENT_FILE " link RCmdNewEventFile} <filespec>\n    @{" CLOSE_EVENT_FILE " link RCmdCloseEventFile}\n    @{" NEW_LOG_FILE " link RCmdNewLogFile} <filespec>\n    @{" CLOSE_LOG_FILE " link RCmdCloseLogFile}\n    @{" SHOW_STATUS " link RCmdShowStatus}\n    @{" PURGE_REXX_EVENTS " link RCmdPurgeRexxEvents}\n    @{" ADD_EVENT " link RCmdAddEvent} <event>\n    @{" DELETE_REXX_EVENT " link RCmdDeleteRexxEvent} <event name>\n    @{" DELETE_EVENT " link RCmdDeleteEvent} <event name>\n    @{" LIST_EVENTS " link RCmdListEvents}\n    @{" SHOW_EVENT " link RCmdShowEvent} <event name>\n    @{" SET_QUEUE_MAX " link RCmdSetQueueMax} <queue name> <max jobs executable at a time>\n    @{" GET_QUEUE_MAX " link RCmdGetQueueMax} <queue name>\n    @{" EVENT_NEXT_EXEC " link RCmdEventNextExec} <event name>\n    @{" NEXT_EVENT_TO_EXEC " link RCmdNextEventToExec}\n    @{" EXPAND_SSSC " link RCmdExpandSSSC} <seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation}>\n    @{" SSSC_TO_ASCII " link RCmdSSSCtoASCII} <seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation}>\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##            These nodes describe the various ARexx commands               ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node RCmdShutdown "The »SHUTDOWN« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n@prev RCmdSSSCtoASCII\n\nCauses CyberCron to quit.  Can also be accomplished by sending a break\nsignal to CyberCron.\n\nOnce SHUTDOWN is started, CyberCron will not accept any more ARexx\ncommands.\n\nIt may take a while for CyberCron to actually quit, however, because it\nmust wait for any currently running events to terminate.\n\nQUIT is accepted as a synonym of SHUTDOWN to provide comformity with the\n@{"Style Guide" link StyleGuide}.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	QUIT=SHUTDOWN\n\nEXAMPLES\n	Shutdown\n	Quit\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdVersion "The »VERSION« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nReturns the version, revision and date of CyberCron in the form\n\n    <version>.<revision> (<day>.<month>.<year>)\n\nThis is returned in the RESULT string, so you must issue an @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults}\ncommand before you can use this command.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	VERSION\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} Version\n	say result\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdSuspend "The »SUSPEND« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nStop processing events.  @{"File notifcation" link FileNotification} on the @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile}\nremains active, however, so changes will be processed normally.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	SUSPEND\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Suspend\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdResume "The »RESUME« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nResume processing of events after a @{"SUSPEND" link RCmdSuspend} has been issued.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	RESUME\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Resume\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdNewEventFile "The »NEW_EVENT_FILE« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nChange the @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile} to <filespec>.  This checks to make sure the file\nactually exists before making the change.  However, parse errors will not\nbe noticed until the current crontab has been purged and CyberCron actually\nstarts reading the new file.\n\nIf something goes wrong (crontab file not found or its name is too long)\nwhich doesn''t cause the previous crontab file to have been purged yet then\nthis will return RC_WARN (5).\n\nIf a fatal error occurs (couldn''t restart @{"notification" link FileNotification} on the new file)\nand the previous crontab has been purged, but the new one not read in, this\nwill return RC_ERROR (10).\n\nTEMPLATE\n	NEW_EVENT_FILE FILESPEC/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	New_Event_File S:CronTab2\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdCloseEventFile "The »CLOSE_EVENT_FILE« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nFree all entries from the @{"CronTab" link ArgCronTab}.  Processing of entries added by the\nARexx @{"ADD_EVENT" link RCmdAddEvent} command will still continue.  Use @{"NEW_EVENT_FILE" link RCmdNewEventFile} to read a\n@{"crontab file" link CronTabFile} again, if you so desire.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	CLOSE_EVENT_FILE\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Close_Event_File\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdNewLogFile "The »NEW_LOG_FILE« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nChanges the @{"logfile" link ArgLogFile} to <filespec>.\n\nIf an error occurs (the new name is too long, for example) then RC_WARN (5)\nwill be returned and the old logfile, if specified, will remain active.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	NEW_LOG_FILE FILESPEC/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	New_Log_File T:CronLog\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdCloseLogFile "The »CLOSE_LOG_FILE« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nTurns off logging.  You can re-enable logging with the @{"NEW_LOG_FILE" link RCmdNewLogFile}\ncommand, if you so desire.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	CLOSE_LOG_FILE\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Close_Log_File\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdShowStatus "The »SHOW_STATUS« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nReturns a couple bits of information about CyberCron''s status.  This is in\nthe form of\n\n    @{"<activity>" link RCmdSuspend} @{"<crontab filespec>" link ArgCronTab} @{"<logfile filespec>" link ArgLogFile}\n\nwhere <activity> is either ACTIVE or SUSPENDED.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	SHOW_STATUS\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} Show_Status\n	say result\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdPurgeRexxEvents "The »PURGE_REXX_EVENTS« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nAll events added with the ARexx @{"ADD_EVENT" link RCmdAddEvent} command will be purged.\nProcessing of entries from the @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile} will continue normally.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	PURGE_REXX_EVENTS\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Purge_Rexx_Events\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdAddEvent "The »ADD_EVENT« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nAdds an event to CyberCron''s active list.  Use the same format as if you\nwere specifying an event in a @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile}.  Comments are not allowed.\n\nIf an error occurs in parsing the event, RC_ERROR (10) will be returned.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	ADD_EVENT EVENT/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	ADD_EVENT ''0 8 * * 1-5 Echo "Go to work!" @{">" link EArgReDirOut} CON:0/0/200/50/Hey!/CLOSE/WAIT''\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdDeleteRexxEvent "The »DELETE_REXX_EVENT« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nDeletes the specified event, but only if it is was added with the ARexx\n@{"ADD_EVENT" link RCmdAddEvent} command.\n\nIf the event was not found, RC_ERROR (10) will be returned.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	DELETE_REXX_EVENT @{"EVENT_NAME/A" link InternalNaming}\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Delete_Rexx_Event ''the event''\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdDeleteEvent "The »DELETE_EVENT« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nSame as @{"DELETE_REXX_EVENT" link RCmdDeleteRexxEvent}, but can be used to delete any event, even those\nread from the @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile}.\n\nIf the event was not found, RC_ERROR (10) will be returned.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	DELETE_EVENT @{"EVENT_NAME/A" link InternalNaming}\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Delete_Event ''the event''\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdListEvents "The »LIST_EVENTS« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nReturns a list of all the current events CyberCron is tracking, seperated\nby spaces.  Note that the events are listed using CyberCron''s internal\n@{"naming scheme" link InternalNaming}.\n\nThe result is returned in a result string, so you must use @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults}\nbefore calling this command, or you will get an error.\n\nIf there are no events, then the string "<None>" is returned.  If an error\noccured, an empty string will be returned.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	LIST_EVENTS\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} List_Events\n	say result\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdShowEvent "The »SHOW_EVENT« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nReturns the full specification for the specified event, as it would be\ngiven in a @{"crontab file" link CronTabFile}, except that it will have the event''s @{"internal name" link InternalNaming}\nprepended.\n\nNote that events may not look exactly the same as when they were added.\nThis is because CyberCron converts events into an internalized structure\nand unconverts them when this command is called.\n\nIf the event was not found then an empty string will be returned.\n\nYou must specify @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults} before using this command, or you will get\nan error.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	SHOW_EVENT @{"EVENT_NAME/A" link InternalNaming}\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} Show_Event ''the event''\n	say result\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdSetQueueMax "The »SET_QUEUE_MAX« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nSet''s the maximum number of jobs that may be run under the specified queue\nat any given time.\n\nIf an illegal queue name is specified, RC_ERROR (10) will be returned.\n\nSetting the maximum to zero is legal, and will result in no jobs for the\nspecified queue being executed until such a time as the queue maximum is\nraised to greater than zero again.\n\nSee the section on @{"queues" link JobQueues} for more information.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	SET_QUEUE_MAX QUEUE_NAME/A MAX_JOBS_EXECUTABLE_AT_A_TIME/N/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Set_Queue_Max a 3\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdGetQueueMax "The »GET_QUEUE_MAX« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nGet the current maximum number of jobs that can be run under the specified\nqueue at any given time.\n\nIf an illegal queue name is specified, -1 is returned.\n\nSee the section on @{"queues" link JobQueues} for more information.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	GET_QUEUE_MAX QUEUE_NAME/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	GET_QUEUE_MAX a\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdEventNextExec "The »EVENT_NEXT_EXEC« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nReturns the next time at which the specified event will execute at.\nThis is returned as the number of seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation}.\n\nIf the event cannot be found, a null string will be returned.\n\nCyberCron will look up to seven years into the future trying to find a time\nthat satisfies the requirements of the event''s timespec.  This is to allow\nfor the effect of leap years.\n\nIf CyberCron cannot determine a time at which the event would next execute,\nzero will be returned.\n\nThe result is returned in a result string, so you must use @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults}\nbefore calling this command, or you will get an error.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	EVENT_NEXT_EXEC @{"EVENT_NAME/A" link InternalNaming}\n\nEXAMPLE\n	Event_Next_Exec ''the event''\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdNextEventToExec "The »NEXT_EVENT_TO_EXEC« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nThis will scan through the event list, looking for the event that is due up\nfor execution next.  It returns a string in the form of\n\n	@{"<event name>" link InternalNaming} <SSSC>\n\nThe <SSSC> field is the seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation} that the event will\nexecute at.\n\nIf no event is found then the string "<None>" will be returned.\n\nThe result is returned in a result string, so you must use @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults}\nbefore calling this command, or you will get an error.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	NEXT_EVENT_TO_EXEC\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} Next_Event_To_Exec\n	nextevent = result\n	if (nextevent ~= ''<None>'') then\n		parse var nextevent event sssc .\n\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdExpandSSSC "The »EXPAND_SSSC« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n\nThis command will take the number of seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation} and\nexpand it into seperate fields for seconds, minutes, etc.  The result is\nreturned in a string in the form of\n\n	<sec> <min> <hour> <day> <month> <year> <day of the week>\n\nThis is useful for scripts that want to make decisions based on the results\nof @{"EVENT_NEXT_EXEC" link RCmdEventNextExec} or @{"NEXT_EVENT_TO_EXEC" link RCmdNextEventToExec}.\n\nThe result is returned in a result string, so you must use @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults}\nbefore calling this command, or you will get an error.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	EXPAND_SSSC SECONDS_SINCE_@{"SYSTEM_CREATION" link SystemCreation}/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} @{"Next_Event_To_Exec" link RCmdNextEventToExec}\n	nextevent = result\n	if (nextevent ~= ''<None>'') then do\n		parse var nextevent event sssc .\n		Expand_SSSC sssc\n		parse var result sec min hour day month year dow .\n	end\n		\n@endnode\n\n@node RCmdSSSCtoASCII "The »SSSC_TO_ASCII« ARexx command"\n@toc RexxPort\n@next RCmdShutdown\n\nThis command will take the number of seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation} and\nreturn a human readable ASCII date string.\n\nThis is useful for scripts that want to display the results of @{"EVENT_NEXT_EXEC" link RCmdEventNextExec}\nor @{"NEXT_EVENT_TO_EXEC" link RCmdNextEventToExec} to the user.\n\nThe result is returned in a result string, so you must use @{"OPTIONS RESULTS" link OptionsResults}\nbefore calling this command, or you will get an error.\n\nTEMPLATE\n	SSSC_TO_ASCII SECONDS_SINCE_@{"SYSTEM_CREATION" link SystemCreation}/A\n\nEXAMPLE\n	@{"options results" link OptionsResults}\n	address @{"CYBERCRON" link ArgPortName} @{"Next_Event_To_Exec" link RCmdNextEventToExec}\n	nextevent = result\n	if (nextevent ~= ''<None>'') then do\n		parse var nextevent event sssc .\n		@{"Show_Event" link RCmdShowEvent} event\n		eventDisp = result\n		if (eventDispl ~= '''') then do\n			say ''->'' || eventDisp\n			SSSC_To_ASCII sssc\n			say ''  next execution at" result\n		end\n	end\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##          End of nodes describing the various ARexx commands              ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node JobQueues "How job queues work in CyberCron"\n@prev RexxPort\n\nCyberCron supports 26 job queues, named ''a'' through ''z''.  Each queue has a\nmaximum number of jobs associated with it.  If an event has a queue\nspecified, not only must it be the appropriate time to run that event, but\nthe number of events currently running under that queue must be less than\nthe maximum allowed for the queue for the event to actually be executed.\n\nEvents that don''t specify an @{":OBEYQUEUE" link EArgObeyQueue} argument are run under the default\nqueue (not included in the 26 named queues that are user accessable), which\nallows an unlimited number of jobs to be executed at the same time.\n\nAll job queues are initilized with their maximum number of events to\nexecute set to one.  ARexx commands exist to @{"read" link RCmdGetQueueMax} and @{"change" link RCmdSetQueueMax} the maximum\nnumber for specific queues.\n\nJobs that could not be started because the queue maximum would be exceeded\nare marked as delayed.  They will be run the next time the queue is\navailable, regardless of whether or not their time spec matches the current\ntime they are up for scheduling.\n\nJob queues were originally added to support writting ARexx scripts that\nsimulate the UNIX @{"at" link AtCommand} and @{"batch" link BatchCommand} commands.  However, I''ve tried to make\nthe interface flexible and useful in other areas.  For example, queues can\nbe used to insure sequencing of events.  Make sure that the queue you will\nuse has its maximum set to one (which is the default for all queues).\nSpecify when the first job is to run.  Specify that the second job should\nrun one minute after the first job.  If the first job is still running when\nthe second comes up for scheduling, the second job will be delayed.\nCyberCron will check every minute after that to see if the first job has\ncompleted and will run the second job when it is safe.  Any number of jobs\ncould be sequenced together using a queue in this manner.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node InternalNaming "The internal naming scheme used by CyberCron"\n\nCyberCron''s @{"LIST_EVENTS" link RCmdListEvents} command will return event names in an internalized\nformat.  These names are in the form of 0x???????? where ? indicates a hex\ndigit.  This is done so that events that have no formal name can be\nspecified from ARexx.  It also allows events to be specified unambigously,\nwhich is useful if you have more than one event with the same formal name\nand you wish to delete/show/etc a specific one.  To prevent naming\nconflicts, CyberCron will not let you specify a formal name that begins\nwith the characters 0x (or 0X, as the case of the "x" doesn''t matter).\n\nWhen asking CyberCron to delete/show/etc an event, you can either use its\ninternal name, or, if it has a formal name, you may use that.  Internal\nnames are cumbersome to use by hand, and are impractical to handle except by\nusing console snap or under the control of an ARexx script.  Formal names\nare much easier to deal with, which is why they are present.  If you wanted\nto add a quick test event, you might give it a formal name of TestEvent and\nthen use that to delete it instead of having to figure out it''s internal\nname and then using it to delete the event.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Localization "How to make CyberCron speak your language"\n\nWhen running under Workbench 2.1, CyberCron has the ability to use a\ntranslation catalog which contains the text strings it uses in a different\nlanguage.  These translation files are stored in the user''s\nLOCALE:Catalogs/<language> directory.  For example, the French string\ncatalog for CyberCron would be the file:\n\n	LOCALE:Catalogs/français/CyberCron.catalog\n\nPlease note that case is important for the filename portion of the above\nfilespec.\n\nIt is also possible to make a Catalogs sub-directory in the directory where\nthe CyberCron executable resides.  This reduces the amount of clutter in\nthe user''s LOCALE: volume.  If you placed the CyberCron executable in\nSYS:WBStartup, then the corresponding location of the French catalog would\nbe:\n\n	SYS:WBStartup/Catalogs/français/CyberCron.catalog\n\nIf there isn''t a translation file for your language present in this\ndistribution (and there most likely won''t be as I don''t know any foreign\nlanguages :->), you can make your own.  To do this, you need to:\n\n	1) edit the dummy.ct file in the Source directory, translating\n	   it as appropriate.  you should be looking at the\n	   Source/CyberCronStrings.cd file as you do this since it\n	   contains many important notes about what the different\n	   strings are used for.\n\n	2) run CatComp (a Commodore developer tool which I can''t include)\n	   to make the actual catalog that CyberCron will use.  For\n	   example:\n\n		catcomp Source/CyberCronStrings.cd <language>.ct catalog\n		    locale:Catalogs/<language>/CyberCron.catalog\n\n		(the above is all on one CLI line, of course)\n\nIf you don''t have access to CatComp then feel free to @{"e-mail" link CAW} me your\ntranslation (lha and uuencode it, though, or any special accent marks, etc,\nwill be lost) and I''ll run it through CatComp.  I''d like any translation\nfiles people come up with, anyway, so I can distribute them with future\nversions of CyberCron.  Also, if you feel that one of the currently\navailable translation files has something which could be translated in a\nbetter way, feel free to mail me those changes as well.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node ChangeLog "The history of CyberCron"\n@next Credits\n\nVersion 1.0 -- Initial release\n\nVersion 1.1 -- Maintenance release the day after 1.0 was released.\n               Don''t remember what it fixed, though... :-)\n\n@{" Version 1.2 " link Change1p2}\n\n@{" Version 1.3 " link Change1p3}\n\n@{" Version 1.4 " link Change1p4}\n\n@{" Version 1.5 " link Change1p5}\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##             Start of nodes describing CyberCron revisions                ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node Change1p2 "Changes in version 1.2 of CyberCron"\n@toc ChangeLog\n@prev Change1p5\n\nIt has been pointed out to me that I forgot to specify the default rexx\nextension of ".rexx".  Thus, scripts in the form of\n\n	foo.rexx\n\nwould not be started unless you actually stated @{":REXX" link EArgRexx} foo.rexx instead of\nbeing able to use @{":REXX" link EArgRexx} foo alone.\n\nAlso added a semaphore lock in the GetJobNum()/FreeJobNum() routines to\nprevent the job table from possibly being clobbered by EndSystemJob()\ngetting called at the same time CyberCron was trying to start another job.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Change1p3 "Changes in version 1.3 of CyberCron"\n@toc ChangeLog\n\nIt has been requested that CyberCron become sensitive to small changes\nbackwards in time.  This is usually caused by reloading the system clock\nfrom the battery backed clock once a day (as the battery backed clock tends\nto keep better time on some machines).  Before running the event scan,\nCyberCron will now check to see if time has gone backwards and will delay\nevent scans until it reaches the next minute that it would have actually\nrun a scan at if the time hadn''t shifted backwards.  There is a 5 minute\nthreshhold limit, however, so if time goes backwards more than that (for\ninstance, daylight savings time changes state and you go back an hour) then\nCyberCron assumes you know what you are doing and will use the new time\nwithout delay.\n\nI also switched the static declarations of struct RDArgs to\nAllocDosObject()/FreeDosObject() pairs.  This should help insure that\nCyberCron will run on any future versions of the OS, even if it will be a\nfew eons before I actually would have had to worry about this... :-)\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Change1p4 "Changes in version 1.4 of CyberCron"\n@toc ChangeLog\n\nFixed handling of commands with a space in them.  Previously, they would be\nindicated as not found.  Now they will work correctly.  Also modified the\nlog output a bit to indicate the command name portion of the command.  The\ncommand is quoted with »« characters.\n\nWhen CyberCron is first started, it will now spit an "I''m alive" message\nout to the logfile.  Brian Vargyas wanted this so he could tell when his\nBBS had rebooted by reading through the log.\n\nCyberCron now checks to see if it is already running and will exit\ngracefully if so.  Added new @{"PORTNAME" link ArgPortName} option to startup arguments to allow\nmultiple CyberCrons to be run if needed by specifying different ARexx port\nnames for each.\n\nModified CCShow.rexx to accept one argument when started to be used as the\nportname to address.  This allows one to easly display the active jobs for\nany CyberCron task running.  If no portname is specified, CCShow defaults\nto CYBERCRON, which is CyberCron''s @{"PORTNAME" link ArgPortName} default.\n\nAliased the @{"CRONPRI" link ArgCronPriority} startup option to TOOLPRI to try and be a bit more\ncomformant with the @{"Style Guide" link StyleGuide}.  Similarily, QUIT is now recognized as a\nsynonym for the @{"SHUTDOWN" link RCmdShutDown} ARexx command.\n\nBecause CyberCron now officially supports multiple copies of itself\nrunning, I have made it use a public locking mechanism for the @{"logfile" link ArgLogFile}.\nThis way, one can have the same logfile for all instances of CyberCron and\nnot have to worry about it getting clobbered.  The job number list is also\nshared between concurrent CyberCrons as well.\n\nDocumentation redone as an AmigaGuide file.  The hypertext nature of\nAmigaGuide makes it a lot easier to navigate around the CyberCron\ndocumentation and find information quickly.\n\nMike Sinz has provided me with wb2cli.o, a linker object that creates a CLI\nstructure for me when CyberCron is started from the Workbench.  This allows\nCyberCron to propagate the path that existed at LoadWB time to jobs it\nstarts.\n\nUpdated the ErrorMsg() function to throw up an EasyRequester when CyberCron\nis started from the WorkBench.  This was done because there is no StdErr to\nwrite errors to in this case.  Once CyberCron is up and fully running,\nErrorMsg() calls will not use a requester, however.  At this point, only\nthings like "Crontab changed, re-reading" are sent to ErrorMsg().  Since\nthese are also sent to the Log() routine, the information isn''t lost and it\nallows CyberCron to remain non-interactive.\n\nAdded @{"localization" link Localization} support.  Now I need to find people to translate the\nstring catalog to other languages for me... :-)\n\nEnhanced the WBtoCLIargs() routine.  Specifically, it now calls FindArg()\non each tooltype to decide if it should copy the tooltype over into the\nargument string.  This prevents ReadArgs() from seeing arguments it doesn''t\nknow about and generating a "wrong number of arguments" error.  This was\ndone to make it possible to include tooltypes such as DONOTWAIT in\nCyberCron''s .info file.  This makes it easy to drop CyberCron in one''s\nWBStartup drawer.\n\nModified the lmkfile to run source code through the indent filter.  This\nhelps make the code look a bit nicer by cleaning up comments as well as\ninsuring that the proper indentation levels are in use.\n\nBy popular demand, removed the ANSI control sequences from the startup and\nhelp messages.\n\nNoticed the Amiga2Date() function in utility.library.  Changed\nGetSystemTime() to make use of it, saving 350+ bytes of code.\n\nFound and squashed an Enforcer hit that would occur in DoMsg() when a\nroutine wanted to return a null string.\n\nAdded four new ARexx commands.  Two, @{"EVENT_NEXT_EXEC" link RCmdEventNextExec} and @{"NEXT_EVENT_TO_EXEC" link RCmdNextEventToExec},\ndeal with determining the time at which an event will execute at.  Both\nreturn their results as the number of seconds since @{"system creation" link SystemCreation}.\nTo make dealing with this result easier, the @{"EXPAND_SSSC" link RCmdExpandSSSC} and @{"SSSC_TO_ASCII" link RCmdSSSCtoASCII}\ncommands were added.\n\nUpdated CCShow.rexx to display the time each job is next scheduled to\nexecute at by using the new @{"EVENT_NEXT_EXEC" link RCmdEventNextExec} and @{"SSSC_TO_ASCII" link RCmdSSSCtoASCII} Rexx commands.\n\nIf OwnDevUnit.library is present, CyberCron will now try and obtain a lock\non LOG-UPDATE.LOCK before trying to write to its logfile.  This will\nprotect its log updates from AmigaUUCP''s trimfile executable.  For this to\nwork properly, however, you must be using v2.1 or better of\nOwnDevUnit.library.\n\nIn previous versions of CyberCron, if a @{"queue" link JobQueues} was already executing the\nmaximum number of jobs it was set to and more jobs were due to be\nscheduled, then only the first job would be delayed and the rest would be\nskipped.  Now all such jobs will be delayed.\n\nRecompiled under SAS/C 6.0.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Change1p5 "Changes in version 1.5 of CyberCron"\n@toc ChangeLog\n@next Change1p2\n\nImplemented some suggestions that Martin Taillefer sent me.  Specifically:\n\n	Fixed the documentation to refer to Workbench ?.? instead of\n	KickStart ?.?, which is the proper terminology.\n\n	Removed the OC_BuiltInLanguage tag from the OpenCatalog() call\n	since this is the default prodided by locale.library anyway.\n\n	Eliminated MySPrintf() and now use sprintf() from amiga.lib since\n	it''s smaller.\n\n	Removed some checks before freeing items in the cleanup code since\n	as of V37 things like CloseLibrary(), FreeVec(), etc, all let you\n	free things that weren''t previously allocated.\n\n	Modified the aborting of timer I/O so that the Disable()/Enable()\n	are no longer used.\n\nGot the new version of CatComp.  Fixed CyberCron to compile using it.\nReduces the size of the executable by about 300 bytes due to removal of\nmany unneeded reloc-32''s.\n\nFor @{"v1.4" link Change1p4}, I made the routine that starts system jobs always enclose the\ncommand name in quotes.  It has been brought to my attention that this\nbreaks many scripts.  As of v1.5, CyberCron no longer places quotes around\nthe command name indiscriminately.  Quotes are now inserted only when the\ncommand name actually has a space in it.\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##              End of nodes describing CyberCron revisions                 ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node Credits "Credits"\n@prev ChangeLog\n\nDon Nafis, author of @{"NazCron" link NazCron}, from which I pretty much stole the idea for\nthe initial ARexx command set recognized by CyberCron.\n\nThomas Rokicki for a little bit of magic in the GetSystemTime() routine.\n(Although this is no longer used as of v1.4.)\n\nLoren Rittle for, well, many things.  He''s responsible for getting me to\nadd the stuff needed to support writing @{"at" link AtCommand} and @{"batch" link BatchCommand} UNIX like commands\n(though I hope to have done it in a means general and flexible enough that\nothers may be able to use the event options in their own, new ways) and\nwriting the at.rexx and batch.rexx scripts, despite the time presure he is\nunder right now.  He also helped catch a number of bugs, offered little\nsuggestions about functionality here and there and then there''s always the\n__emit() trick in MySPrintfSupp() (I nearly cried when I saw it in his\nls-4.5 code --- it never dawned on me to do anything so simple :->).\n\nMike Sinz for providing the wb2cli.o linker module.  This actually makes\nrunning CyberCron from the Workbench a useful thing to do.\n\nMike Koch for drawing the icon that I finally selected for CyberCron.  I''d\nalso like to thank the other people who sent in icons, particularily Pang Sie\nPiau, who sent me a couple rounds of icons as I made suggestions to him.\nHis icons have a much better Workbench 2.0 look to them, but in the end I\ndecided that Mike''s better denotes the idea of a cron.\n\nAmish Dave for moral support and helping me clean up the documentation a\nbit.\n\nMartin Taillefer for sending me some comments that helped tweak the code\nin various places to make CyberCron slightly smaller and more efficient.\n\nGeorg Sassen for tracking down the problem with scripts that v1.4\nintroduced.  (See the @{"v1.5 changes" link Change1p5} for how this was fixed.)\n\nSebastiano Vigna and Paolo Lenzi for the Italian translation catalog.\n\nXavier Drèze for the French translation catalog.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node CAW "Contacting the author"\n@next main\n\nIf you happen to find a bug or have a suggestion for CyberCron, or just\nwant to say "hey, cool program", please contact me using one of the ways\nlisted below.  Even if you wanna say "CyberCron sucks", let me know and be\nsure to say why you feel this way so that I might be able to fix what you\nthink is wrong with the program.\n\nThese electronic forms are the most prefered means of contacting me.  They\nwill get you a response pretty quick.\n\n	e-mail: [email removed]\n	BIX: caw\n\nSnail Mail is pretty slow and I''m not known for being very good about\nresponding to it...  :-)\n\n	Christopher A. Wichura\n	5450 East View Park\n	Chicago, Il.  60615\n	USA\n\nYou can also reach me by phone.  However, please try to limit your calling\nto evening hours (I''m in the central time zone).  If I''m not home and you\nleave a message, call back again anyway.  Around here, one tends to get\nmaybe 5% of the messages left for them, if lucky...  :-)\n\n	(312)/684-2941\n\n@endnode\n\n##############################################################################\n##                 Misc nodes for informational purposes                    ##\n##############################################################################\n\n@node OptionsResults "The use of »OPTIONS RESULTS« in an ARexx script"\n@prev OptionsResults\n@next OptionsResults\n\nARexx requires that one use the OPTIONS RESULTS command before making any\ncalls that return a result string (as opposed to a result code/number).\n\n@endnode\n\n@node FileNotification "What file notification does under Workbench 2.04 or greater"\n@prev FileNotification\n@next FileNotification\n\nFile notification is a new feature of Workbench 2.04.  It allows a program\nto ask the operating system to tell it when a file has been created,\nchanged or deleted.\n\nBy using file notification, it is no longer necessary for programs such as\ncrons to constantly check if a critical configuration file has been\nupdated.  This results in a significant increase in system performance.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node NazCron "NazCron"\n@prev NazCron\n@next NazCron\n\nNazCron is a cron for the Amiga that was written by Don Nafis.  It was\nreleased as Shareware.\n\nCyberCron has strong roots in NazCron, primarily in it''s @{"ARexx command set" link RexxPort}.\nAlmost all its commands are the same as under NazCron.  The new commands I\nadded to cover features in CyberCron that NazCron does not have are done in\na style very similar to the way NazCron handled things.\n\nPlease note that CyberCron is based on NazCron in spirit only.  No code\nfrom NazCron has been incorporated into CyberCron.\n\nIf you are someone still using Workbench 1.3 (god forbid!) and are looking\nfor a rather nice cron, you might consider trying to find a copy of\nNazCron.\n\nThe last information I have on how to contact Don Nafis is from the NazCron\ndocumentation, dated 1989.  It is as follows:\n\n               Don Nafis\n               Nazlo Associates Ltd.\n               P.O. Box 1515\n               Laurel Springs, NJ  08021\n\n               (609) 228-8088 Voice\n\n               (609) 227-8278 BBS -\n                               Viva! Amiga!\n                         3/12/24 - 8/N/1 - 24 hours\n                            130 Megabytes Online\n\n               Compuserve 70656, 133\n\n@endnode\n\n@node AtCommand "The »at« command"\n@prev BatchCommand\n\nThe UNIX ''at'' command executes a command (or a sequence of commands) at a\nspecific time.  For example, you can do something like\n\n	prompt>at next tuseday 8\n	echo "Tomorrow is your wife''s birthday.  Did you get the present?"\n	EOF (the keystroke that indicates end of file, CTRL-\ on the Amiga)\n\nand at 8am next tuesday the echo command would be run.  One might think\nthat the output of this echo command would never be seen, right?  Wrong.\n''At'' allows one to specify where the output is to be redirected.  If it is\nnot redirected then the system e-mails the output of the command to the\nuser who submitted the ''at'' job.\n\nFor a more complete description, consult the Unix man pages on the ''at''\ncommand.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node BatchCommand "The »Batch« command"\n@next AtCommand\n\nThe UNIX ''batch'' command is actually a special case of the @{"at" link AtCommand} command.\nInstead of executing a job at a specific time, the batch command submits\nthe job for immediate execution.  @{"Job queues" link JobQueues} insure that only a few\n''batch''ed jobs are actually running at one time, however.\n\n''batch'' is intended for running jobs that require a lot of CPU time.\n\nFor more information, consult the Unix man pages on the ''batch'' command.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node PipeDevice "The Commodore PIPE: device"\n@prev PipeDevice\n@next PipeDevice\n\nThe Commodore supplied PIPE: device can be used to route the output of one\nprogram into the input stream of another.  This is done in a memory\nefficient way which does not require large temporary files to be created.\n\nFor more information on the PIPE: device and how to mount it, see your\n"Using the System Software" manual.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node StyleGuide "AMIGA User Interface Style Guide"\n@prev StyleGuide\n@next StyleGuide\n\nThe "Amiga User Interface Style Guide" is a reference manual Commodore has\nwritten that has many useful suggestions on making Amiga applications more\nsimilar.  The idea is that it makes it easy for a user to use a new\napplication because many of the things they have learned from previous\napplications will still be valid.\n\nThe style guide is published by Addison-Wesley Publishing Company, Inc.\nIt''s ISBN number is 0-201-57757-7.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node SystemCreation "System Creation"\n@prev SystemCreation\n@next SystemCreation\n\nSystem creation on the Amiga is the zeroth minute of the zeroth hour on\nJanuary 1st, 1978.\n\n@endnode\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',41439,1,0,NULL,NULL,'emp_mt20','scan',1780048498);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('emp_sf10','EMP-SF10.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/EMP-SF10.LHA','Sent_FE','XIM','Sent_FE',NULL,NULL,'EMP','¦_/\___/\___/\___                          ¦ |\   /\ __/_  __/    ''AutoSent'' FrontEnd   | |/ Y \/_.¬\/  \       will start from a    |',replace('+[ MYSTiC /X-DESiGN ]----[ Sent_FE 1.0 ]---+\n¦_/\___/\___/\___                          ¦\n|\   /\ __/_  __/    ''AutoSent'' FrontEnd   |\n|/ Y \/_.¬\/  \       will start from a    |\n/  | \\¬¦ \\  \\     Bulletin and Forces   |\n\__l_//___//__//    Every User to Use his  ¦\n|dL!\/   \/  \/        Own SentBy-Line     |\n+-[ EMPiRE/MYSTiC ]------[ XIM - /X 3.xx ]-+\n','\n',char(10)),'Sent_FE.DOC',replace('\n                  ###### ###   ## ######  ### ######  ######              \n    _______|\     ###    #### ### ###  ## ### ###  ## ###       /|________\n   |         >    ####   ######## ######  ### ######  ####     <         |\n   | |¯¯¯¯¯|/     ###    ### # ## ###     ### #####   ###       \|¯¯¯¯¯| |\n   | |            ###### ###   ## ###     ### ### ##  ######           | |\n   | |_________________________________________________________________| |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |                          Sent FrontEnd 1.0                          |\n   |                          ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~                          |\n   |                             TYPE:  XIM                              |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |                  ©1994  EMPiRE / MYSTiC /X-DESiGN                   |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |       LaSt OuTpOsT  +31-72-157682 & +31-72-201182  -  HOLLAND       |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |       AmixNet:  @NL0004   -   /X-Fido:  44:100/102     EMPiRE       |\n   |_____________________________________________________________________|\n\n===============================================================================\n\n PURPOSE:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  With Sent_FE you are able to start ByteAndi''s AutoSent from a Bulletin.\n  This way EVERYBODY has to enter his Private SentBy-Line for use with\n  FILE_ID.DIZ.\n\n  When a valid SentBy-Line already exists then you don''t get to see any\n  output of this Door!\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n REQUIREMENTS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  Ami Express 3.xx\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n INSTALLATION:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  o Copy the EmP_Tools Directory to DOORS:\n\n  o Sent_FE should be started from a Bulletin:\n\n    as BBS:Bull.txt\n\n--[ start: cut here ]-------------------------\n~CC_FE_Sent\n--[ stop: cut here ]--------------------------\n\n    or with a txtfile (BBS:Bulletins/bull.txt)\n\n--[ start: cut here ]-------------------------\n~SS_BBS:Bulletins/bull.txt|~CC_FE_Sent\n--[ stop: cut here ]--------------------------\n\n\n  AE 3.xx:\n  ~~~~~~~~\n  - Copy Sent_FE.info to BBS:Commands/SYScmd/\n\n    Or add to Sent_FE.info ICON ToolType Info:\n\n      ACCESS=001\n      LOCATION=DOORS:EmP_Tools/Sent_FE\n      MULTINODE=YES\n      PRIORITY=0\n      STACK=50000\n      TYPE=XIM\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n ERROR MESSAGES:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n\n  o ERROR:  DOORS:FILEID/Sent.DAT Not Found !!!\n  |\n  `--> JoinCnf.CFG can''t be found in DOORS:EmP_Tools !?\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n BUG REPORT & SUGGESTIONS:                                                                   \n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  It''s possible that a mistake will occur.... Nobody is Perfect.\n  If you have any Suggestions or Comments Please Leave a Message on my Board:\n\n   LaSt OuTpOsT  N1: +31-72-157682  N2: +31-72-201182  -  HOLLAND\n   ~~~~~~~~~~~~  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~     ~~~~~~~\n       AmixNet:  @NL0004   -   /X-Fido:  44:100/102   EMPiRE\n       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~   ~~~~~~\n\n===============================================================================\n\n NOTE:\n ~~~~~\n  o This Program Has been tested on a /X 3.32 Configuration.\n\n  o Sent_FE will startup ByteAndi''s AutoSent when:\n\n     - There isn''t a Valid SentBy-Line\n     - The User never entered a SentBy-Line\n     - The UserName in Sent.DAT is different to the one he/she is using now.\n\n    else Sent_FE will stay silent and won''t put Any text on screen.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n REGISTRATION:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  If YOU use this Program please let me know by Calling Last Outpost or\n  to send me a Message by NetMail @NL0004 - EMPiRE  Coz I want to know\n  how many Sysops are using my Tools!!!\n \n\n===============================================================================\n\n HISTORY:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  V0.1  01-16-94 - Release Version.\n        01-17-94 - Added NONSTOP TextShowing.\n        01-25-94 - Fixed the ''10-Node'' BUG.\n\n  V1.0  01-29-94 - Release Version.\n\n\n===============================================================================\n\n EMPiRE /X DOORS/UTILS COLLECTION UNTIL NOW!:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - AboStats V0.2                        AIM Door\n  - AutoOnline V1.1                      XIM Door/Util\n  - Bulls V2.0                           XIM Door\n  - CheckNU V1.0                         XIM Door\n  - ConfAccess V1.1                      ServerUtil\n  - CopyDate V1.3                        CLI Util\n  - DlDir V2.0                           XIM Door\n  - DoorKIT 1.01                         XIM Doors/Includes\n  - DMenu V1.0                           AIM Door\n  - DutchNET V1.0  [AmiXnet]             AIM Door\n  - Front V2.0                           XIM Door\n  - JoinCnf V3.4                         XIM Door\n  - LastCalls & TopUps V1.2              LogOffUtil\n  - LastDL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - LastUL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - MakeFree V1.1                        AIM Door\n  - MemberList V1.0                      AIM Door\n  - MonthTopUp V2.0                      ServerUtil\n  - MultiNodeLastCaller V1.1             LogOffUtil\n  - Page V2.0                            XIM Door\n  - PMail V1.1                           AIM Door\n  - Remote Maintenance V1.3              AIM Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.3                 AIM Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.2 [Party]         CLI Door\n  - SecLevChk V1.0                       ServerUtil\n  - Sent FrondEnd 1.0  [Sent_FE]         XIM Door\n  - SysopCommands V1.0                   AIM Door\n  - UserData Cleaner/Repair V2.0         ServerUtil\n  - UserStats V2.0                       ServerUtil\n  - UsrCnfAx V1.1                        CLI Util\n  - View & Check Archive/Text V1.7       AIM Door\n  - WhoNode V1.3                         AIM Door\n\n===============================================================================\n\n THANX MUST GO TO ALL OFFICIAL BETA-TESTERS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   Tycoon/Mystic             [Channel X]\n   ByteAndi/Mystic           [Diabolo]\n   JPS                       [Red October]\n   Sal-One/TRSi              [BoonDocks]\n   Sharon/Scx                [The Unknown]\n   Blue Bird/Mystic          [Tau Ceti]\n   Easy Rider/Mystic         [Mamba]\n   Mega Fortress             [MF Enterprise]\n   Beast/Partek              [Sequencial]\n   SpeedDevil/Partek         [Rising Sun]\n\n\n GREETZ/TNX GO TO:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   ByteAndi/MST /X-DESiGN  - NewFiles is the best! And Tnx for all ya ''C''\n                             Help & Sources! You''re (almost) the Greatest!\n   Killraven/MST /X-DESiGN - For giving me sum Great Pointers/Sources on ''C''\n   CrackDown/MST /X-DESiGN - Nice Doors, Keep it up!\n   JAF/G.O.D.              - For some good REXX ideas\n   Bad Sector/Hidden       - Still Using your Sort-Routine. It''s the Fastest!\n   B.O.B./Paradox          - For (Mis)Using/Testing my tools!\n   Aragorn/Vision          - Tnx for Testing in the past\n   Camelot/Complex         - Tnx for Testing in the past\n   Telekinetic/T.F.A.      - Tnx for Testing in the past\n   Alcoholic               - Tnx for Testing in the past\n   Price/T.F.A.            - Great job on your {F}ull {S}creen {E}ditor!\n   Van Damme               - Nice Doors!!!\n   DR.G/IBB                - Hope to see Check''N Add work on a 040\n   SiGMA SEVEN             - Nice job on SiGMA-EXPRESS!\n   911/Movement            - Tnx For ya Ansi Logo''s!! You''re the Greatest!\n   Mike                    - Tnx for calling!\n   Amicom/TRSi             - Tnx for calling!\n   SkIn                    - Tnx for ya Description Logo! Looks Great!!\n   Shadow Man/S8           - Nice Doors & Tnx for ya greetz! & Calling ofcoz\n   [MAZ!^PHAZE]            - Tnx for calling!\n   Shuyuki Tanaka          - Tnx for your Messages!\n   AGONY/cOmpLeX           - Tnx for calling!\n   HUMANOiD & RU$tY/AFL    - Tnx for calling!\n   LECTOR                  - Tnx for your NetMail\n   Koo Koo Ka Choo         - Tnx for calling!\n   TaRzAN                  - Tnx for your NetMail. Yezz 040 Speed Rulez!!\n   Crayor/Scx              - Tnx for calling!\n   Foxx''n''Lynxx            - Tnx for your BugReports!!!\n   ThE BiRD                - Tnx for your NetMail on UsrCnfAx\n   Dux                     - Tnx for your NetMail\n   dR.dRE!/fAITH           - Tnx for da greetz, Nice Doors btw\n   [-RUNT&SCOTTIE-]        - Tnx for calling!\n   sPAcEcAKe/aF            - Tnx for calling!\n   Freddy Kreuger/GOD      - Tnx for your NetMail on JoinCnf 3.2\n   MF1                     - Tnx for calling!\n   AtomiX/N.A.S.A          - Hope the ''C'' archive was sume help for ya!\n   MAD HARLEQUiN [ITPH01]  - Tnx for your Nice AmiXnet Messy''s\n   Mike Hoffren  [SF0001]  - Tnx for the BugReport on JoinCnf 3.3 [100+ Cnfs]\n   Galaxy/TFA              - Nice job on da SentCleaner\n\n\n   & Ofcoz to ALL MYSTiC Members all over the Globe!\n\n\n                        ______  __________________                            \n.----------------+-----/    \ \/ / __/_  __/ / __/----------+----------------.\n|                |----/ / / /\  /\  /-/ /-/ / /----[ /X ]---|                |\n|       /\    /\ |-EmP\/ / /-/ /\  /-/ /-/ /\ \--[ DESiGN ]-|       /\    /\ |\n|      /C \  /N/ +-----\/\/--\/--\/--\/--\/--\/-------------+      /C \  /N/ |\n|     /i/\ \/G/  |                                          |     /i/\ \/G/  |\n|    /T/  \ i/   |                                          |    /T/  \ i/   |\n|   /S/   /S \   |   EMPiRE   -   BYTEANDi   -  CRACKDOWN   |   /S/   /S \   |\n|  /Y/   /E/\ \  |   TYCOON   -   KiLLRAVEN  -   SiEGEL     |  /Y/   /E/\ \  |\n| /M/   /D/  \ \ |   FUZZY    -      SPY     -  INCOGNiTO   | /M/   /D/  \ \ |\n| \/    \/    \/ |   RE/Y\o   -     BoBo     -   HARDBYTE   | \/    \/    \/ |\n|                |                                          |                |\n`----------------+----------[  MYSTiC /X DESiGN  ]----------+----------------''\n\n \n\n                       ____ _________ _________ ________ _____\n            MYSTiC    /   / \\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /     MYSTiC\n                     /   /   \\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n         CONSOLE    /   / /\  /   __   \ \____   \   /   /      AMIGA\n                   /   /_/  \/   / /   /_____/   /  /   /\n       WORLD-HQ    \________X___/ /___/\________/   \__/      EURO-HQ\n      ____ _________ ________ _________ _________ _________ ________ _____\n     /    \\\  /   / \\  /   \\\  /    \\\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /\n    /   /\ \\\/   / /\\\/__   \\\/   /\ \\\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n   /   / /  \/   / /  \   /   / /   _____\/   / /  \ \____   \   /   /\n  /   /_/   /   /_/   /  /   / /   /     /   /_/   /_____/   /  /   /\n  \________X_________/   \__/  \__/      \________/\________/   \__/\n\n\n  AMIGA - SNES - MEGADRIVE - GAMEBOY - GAMEGEAR - PC ENGINE - LYNX - C64\n\n  3.5 Gb ONLINE          SYSOP:  EMPiRE / MYSTiC           3.5 Gb ONLINE\n\n  HOLLAND:          +31-72-157682  &  +31-72-201182             :HOLLAND\n\n','\n',char(10)),'{"ACCESS":"001","LOCATION":"DOORS:EmP_Tools/Sent_FE","PRIORITY":"0","STACK":"50000","TYPE":"XIM"}','auto-added',35769,8,1,'Sent_FE','Doors/Sent_FE','emp_sf10','scan',1780048498);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('emp_ua10','EMP_UA10.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/EMP_UA10.LHA','UsrCnfAx','XIM','UsrCnfAx',NULL,'EMPiRE/MYSTiC','EMP','+--------/    \ \/ / __/_  __/ / __/-------+ |-------/ / / /\  /\  /-/ /-/ / /----------| |-------\/ / /-/ /\  /-/ /-/ /\ \-EmP------|',replace('          ______  __________________\n+--------/    \ \/ / __/_  __/ / __/-------+\n|-------/ / / /\  /\  /-/ /-/ / /----------|\n|-------\/ / /-/ /\  /-/ /-/ /\ \-EmP------|\n+--------\/\/--\/--\/--\/--\/--\/----------+\n| AE UTIL: UsrCnfAx V1.0  by EMPiRE/MYSTiC |\n| Create Bulls with User Conference Access |\n','\n',char(10)),'UsrCnfAx.doc',replace('\n                  ###### ###   ## ######  ### ######  ######              \n    _______|\     ###    #### ### ###  ## ### ###  ## ###       /|________\n   |         >    ####   ######## ######  ### ######  ####     <         |\n   | |¯¯¯¯¯|/     ###    ### # ## ###     ### #####   ###       \|¯¯¯¯¯| |\n   | |            ###### ###   ## ###     ### ### ##  ######           | |\n   | |_________________________________________________________________| |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |                           UsrCnfAx V1.0                             |\n   |                           ~~~~~~~~~~~~~                             |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |                        BY EMPiRE / MYSTiC                           |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |       LaSt OuTpOsT  +31-72-157682 & +31-72-201182  -  HOLLAND       |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |       AmixNet:  @NL0004   -   /X-Fido:  44:100/102   [EMPiRE]       |\n   |_____________________________________________________________________|\n\n\n PURPOSE:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  - Creates Bulletins with All Users who have access to a certain Conference.\n\n\n REQUIREMENTS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - A Running RexxMast\n  - /X 2.xx & /X 3.x\n\n\n INSTALLATION:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - Copy All the Files to BBS:Utils/EmP :\n\n     BBS:Utils/EmP/UsrCnfAx\n     BBS:Utils/EmP/UsrCnfAx.doc\n\n  - Add the BBS:Utils/EmP Directory to your Path (Startup-Seqeunce)\n\n\n USAGE:\n ~~~~~~\n  - CLI Usage   :  UsrCnfAx <Conference Nr> <Output File> <ViewFlag>\n\n    Optional <ViewFlag>:  1  - 3 Colums Usernames (default if ommited)\n                          2  - 2 Colums Usernames & Ul/Dl Stats\n\n    - Example #1:  UsrCnfAx 10 BBS:CNF_EmP_TOOLS/bull.txt 1\n\n    - Output:\n    _____________    ______________________________    _____________________\n /\/             \/\/                              \/\/                     \/\\n<    CoNFeReNCe:          EmP /X BeTaTeST SiTe             UsrCnfAx V1.0       >\n \/\_____________/\/\______________________________/\/\_____________________/\/\n\n\n           EMPiRE                     JAF                   AlCoHoLiC        \n           B.O.B.                   ARAGORN          [-sAl-øNe!/TRS¡!/dP!-]  \n        Tycoon/desire         CaMeLoT/Ch/\oS BBS               JPS           \n         TELEKINETIC                 WOODY                   -E/\S-          \n         AmIcOm/TrSi               DR. G/IBB                  Mike           \n\n    _____________________________________    _______________________________\n /\/                                     \/\/                               \/\\n<        15 uSeRS iN THiS CoNFeReNCe              DoNe By EMPiRE/MYSTiC        >\n \/\_____________________________________/\/\_______________________________/\/\n\n\n\n\n    - Example #2:  UsrCnfAx 10 BBS:CNF_EmP_TOOLS/bull.txt 2\n\n    - Output:\n    _____________    ______________________________    _____________________\n /\/             \/\/                              \/\/                     \/\\n<    CoNFeReNCe:          EmP /X BeTaTeST SiTe             UsrCnfAx V1.0       >\n \/\_____________/\/\______________________________/\/\_____________________/\/\n\n                              UL    DL                               UL    DL\n\n   EMPiRE                    8.0     0    JAF                      18.0   0.2\n   AlCoHoLiC                24.4  55.4    B.O.B.                   77.5  26.4\n   ARAGORN                 170.7  50.1    [-sAl-øNe!/TRS¡!/dP!-]   29.4  12.5\n   Tycoon/desire            24.0  22.4    CaMeLoT/Ch/\oS BBS       46.3  15.3\n   JPS                      71.6  30.7    TELEKINETIC               3.2   2.3\n   WOODY                    15.8   6.2    -E/\S-                   20.9   3.0\n   AmIcOm/TrSi               1.0   0.1    DR. G/IBB                   0     0\n   Mike                        0     0\n\n    _____________________________________    _______________________________\n /\/                                     \/\/                               \/\\n<        15 uSeRS iN THiS CoNFeReNCe              DoNe By EMPiRE/MYSTiC        >\n \/\_____________________________________/\/\_______________________________/\/\n\n\n\n\n BUG REPORT & SUGGESTIONS:                                                                   \n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  It''s possible that a mistake will occure.... Nobody is Perfect.\n  If you have any Suggestions or Comments Please Leave a Message on my Board:\n\n   LaSt OuTpOsT  N1: +31-72-157682  N2: +31-72-201182  -  HOLLAND\n   ~~~~~~~~~~~~  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~     ~~~~~~~\n      AmixNet:  @NL0004   -   /X-Fido:  44:100/102   [EMPiRE]\n      ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~   ~~~~~~~~\n\n\n NOTE:\n ~~~~~\n  - This Tool works GREAT on a 68040 or higher. But on a 68000\n    it''s a bit slow! So be patient or buy a 020+!\n  - This Program Has been Made for use with a /X 3.6 Configuration. But it\n    will Work on a /X 2.xx Setup too.\n\n\n HISTORY:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  beta1 05-20-93  - Beta Release\n  beta2 05-21-93  - Added extra options for Ul/Dl Viewing\n  beta3 05-22-93  - Forced Deletes of Temp.Files\n\n  V1.0  07-18-93  - Release Version\n\n\n\n EMPiRE /X DOORS/UTILS COLLECTION UNTIL NOW!:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - AboStats V0.2                        Door\n  - Bulls V1.2                           Door\n  - ConfAccess V1.0                      ServerUtil\n  - CopyDate V1.3                        Util\n  - DlDir V1.2                           Door\n  - DMenu V1.0                           Door\n  - DutchNET V1.0                        Door\n  - JoinCnf V2.2                         Door\n  - LastCalls & TopUps V1.2              LogOffUtil\n  - LastDL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - LastUL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - MakeFree V1.1                        Door\n  - MemberList V1.0                      Door\n  - MonthTopUp V2.0                      ServerUtil\n  - MultiNodeLastCaller V1.1             LogOffUtil\n  - Page V1.3                            Door\n  - PMail V1.1                           Door\n  - Remote Maintenance V1.3              Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.2                 Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.2 [Party]         Util\n  - SecLevChk V1.0                       ServerUtil\n  - SysopCommands V1.0                   Door\n  - UserData Cleaner/Repair V2.0         ServerUtil\n  - UserStats V2.0                       ServerUtil\n  - UsrCnfAx V1.0                        Util\n  - View & Check Archive/Text V1.6       Door\n  - WhoNode V1.3                         Door\n\n\n THANX MUST GO TO ALL OFFICIAL BETA-TESTERS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   Aragorn/Vision           [Terminal Zone]\n   Camelot/Endless Piracy   [Chaos]\n   Telekinetic/T.F.A.       [Total Confusion]\n   Alcoholic                [Sticky Fingers]\n   Tycoon/Desire            [Channel X]\n   Mike                     [Global Chaos]\n   Amicom/TRSi              [Skara Brae]\n   JPS/Mystic               [Red October]\n   Sal-One/TRSi             [BoonDocks]\n   \n\n GREETZ/TNX GO TO:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   JAF/G.O.D.              - For some good REXX ideas\n   Bad Sector/Hidden       - Still Using your Sort-Routine. It''s the Fastest!\n   B.O.B./Paradox          - For Using my tools!\n   Price/T.F.A.            - Great job on your {F}ull {S}creen {E}ditor!\n   Van Damme               - Nice Doors!!!\n   DR.G/IBB                - Hope to see Check''N Add work on a 040\n   SiGMA SEVEN             - Long time no ''See''\n   911/Movement            - Tnx For ya Ansi Logo''s!!\n   SkIn                    - Tnx for ya Decription Logo! Looks Great!!\n   Shadow Man/S8           - Nice Doors & Tnx for ya greetz!\n   [MAZ!^PHAZE]            - Tnx for calling!\n   Shuyuki Tanaka          - Tnx for your Messages!\n   AGONY/cOmpLeX           - Tnx for calling!\n   HUMANOiD & RU$tY AFL    - Tnx for calling!\n   LECTOR                  - Tnx for your NetMail\n   Koo Koo Ka Choo         - Tnx for calling!\n   TaRzAN                  - Tnx for your NetMail. Yezz 040 Speed Rulez!!\n\n   & Ofcoz to ALL Mystic Members all over the Globe!\n\n\n\n                       ____ _________ _________ ________ _____\n        AMIGA         /   / \\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /     AMIGA\n       SNES          /   /   \\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/     SNES\n      MEGADRIVE     /   / /\  /   __   \ \____   \   /   /       MEGADRIVE\n     GAMEBOY       /   /_/  \/   / /   /_____/   /  /   /       GAMEBOY\n    GAMEGEAR      /_________/___/ /___/_________/  /___/       GAMEGEAR\n      ____ _________ ________ _________ _________ _________ ________ _____\n     /    \\\  /   / \\  /   \\\  /    \\\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /\n    /   /\ \\\/   / /\\\/__   \\\/   /\ \\\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n   /   / /  \/   / /  \   /   / /   _____\/   / /  \ \____   \   /   /\n  /   /_/   /   /_/   /  /   / /   /     /   /_/   /_____/   /  /   /\n /_________/_________/  /___/ /___/     /_________/_________/  /___/\n\n\n                         SYSOP:  EMPiRE / MYSTiC\n\n               HOLLAND:    +31-72-157682  &  +31-72-201182\n\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',10762,5,0,NULL,NULL,'emp_ua10','scan',1780048504);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('emp_uc10','EMP-UC10.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/EMP-UC10.LHA','UdClean','XIM','UdClean',NULL,NULL,'EMP',NULL,replace('User Data Util Delete Inactive Accounts\n','\n',char(10)),'illumination.txt',replace('\n                    Chaos.  Live it.  Learn it.  Be it.\n\n                     _\!/_ The Illuminated Ones _\!/_\n                      /!\  ««««««««««»»»»»»»»»»  /!\\n\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',13371,4,0,NULL,NULL,'emp_uc10','scan',1780048504);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('emp_uc20','EMP-UC20.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/EMP-UC20.LHA','UdClean','XIM','PrIvAtE!.eXe',NULL,'EMPiRE/MYSTiC','EMP','+--------/    \ \/ / __/_  __/ / __/-------+ |-------/ / / /\  /\  /-/ /-/ / /----------| |-------\/ / /-/ /\  /-/ /-/ /\ \-EmP------|',replace(replace('          ______  __________________        \r\n+--------/    \ \/ / __/_  __/ / __/-------+\r\n|-------/ / / /\  /\  /-/ /-/ / /----------|\r\n|-------\/ / /-/ /\  /-/ /-/ /\ \-EmP------|\r\n+--------\/\/--\/--\/--\/--\/--\/----------+\r\n| AE UTIL: UdClean V2.0 - by EMPiRE/MYSTiC |\r\n| - CleanUp User.Data/Conf.DB''s   [/X 3.x] |\r\n+------------------------------------------+\r\n','\r',char(13)),'\n',char(10)),'UdClean.doc',replace('\n                  ###### ###   ## ######  ### ######  ######              \n    _______|\     ###    #### ### ###  ## ### ###  ## ###       /|________\n   |         >    ####   ######## ######  ### ######  ####     <         |\n   | |¯¯¯¯¯|/     ###    ### # ## ###     ### #####   ###       \|¯¯¯¯¯| |\n   | |            ###### ###   ## ###     ### ### ##  ######           | |\n   | |_________________________________________________________________| |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |                 UserData Clean/Repair V2.0  [UdClean]               |\n   |                 ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~               |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |                         BY EMPiRE / MYSTiC                          |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |             LaSt OuTpOsT  +31-72-157682 & +31-72-201182             |\n   |                                                                     |\n   |       AmiXnet:  @NL0004   -   /X-Fido:  44:100/102   [EMPiRE]       |\n   |_____________________________________________________________________|\n\n\n\n PURPOSE:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  1. Delete InActive/Terminated Accounts.\n  2. Delete Accounts at a Specific Level.\n  3. Clean User.Data.MONTH file for use with MonthTopUp/MonthTop\n  4. Make UserData/UserKeys/Conf.DB BACKUPs\n  5. Salving/Repairing a Corrupted User.Data/User.Keys\n     When UdClean Finds a Incorrect Filesize of the User.Data/User.Keys\n     then it will TRY to Salvage/Repair Them as much as Possible!\n  6. ReSizing Conf.DB Files (No Need to run RATIOS Afterwards!)\n\n  - The major Difference with this Version and the V1.5 Version is when\n    the User.Data/User.Keys is cleaned  All Conf.DB Files in Every\n    Conference have to be Cleaned too!!\n\n\n\n REQUIREMENTS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - /X 3.x  (It Will NOT work with /X 2.xx, Use UdClean V1.5)\n  - A Running RexxMast\n\n\n INSTALLATION:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - Copy All the Files to BBS:Utils/EmP :\n\n     BBS:Utils/EmP/UdClean\n     BBS:Utils/EmP/UdClean.doc\n\n  - Create a Backup Directory for the User.Data/User.Keys/Conf.DB Files:\n\n     BBS:BACKUP\n\n  - Add the BBS:Utils/EmP Directory to your Path (Startup-Sequence)\n\n  - Add to BBS:ACP.info ICON ToolType Info:\n\n      BUTTON_NAME.<num>=UD_CLean\n      BUTTON_COMMAND.<num>=run >NIL: BBS:Utils/EmP/UdClean\n\n       <num>=1-15\n\n\n USAGE:\n ~~~~~~\n  - Server Usage:  Select <UD_CLean> Button\n\n  - CLI Usage   :  UDCLean\n\n\n OPTIONS:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  UdClean [DEVICE RAM:] [DBSIZE 1000]\n\n  - Options:\n\n      DEVICE  Select Device where UdClean can put its TEMP-Files\n              Keep in mind that a System with 25 Conferences will eat up\n              about 2 Mb of RAM!! (25 * 74000 + 100000 Bytes)\n              DEFAULT=RAM:         ^    ^       ^\n                                   |    |       |\n                                   |    |       `---- User.Data + User.Keys\n                                   |    `------------ Conf.DB Size\n                                   `----------------- Nr Of Conferences\n\n      DBSIZE  Select Number of Users in your Conf.DB Files\n              DEFAULT=1000 \n              This way All the Conf.DB Files can be resized to make them\n              bigger or smaller!\n\n  - Example:  UdClean DEVICE DH0: DBSIZE 500\n\n                TEMP-File to DH0:  (Very SLOW!!!)\n                500 Users in the Conf.DB Files\n\n\n IMPORTANT:\n ~~~~~~~~~~\n  - ALWAYS make Sure that NO Users are Online while Cleaning!!\n  - ALWAYS make a BACKUP (option 4) BEFORE you SAVE the NEW\n    User.Data & User.Keys & Conf.DB Files!!! Don''t blame me if all Fails!\n  - Keep in Mind that after an UdClean the User (Slot)Numbers may have been\n    changed, so PartUploads will not be found by the right Uploader!!\n\n\n ERROR MESSAGES:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - DBSIZE Must be Greater than the Number of Users in the User.Data!!!\n    At present there are 372 Users in the User.Data\n\n  - ERROR: NCONFS must Exist in BBS:ConfConfig.info IconInfo!!!\n\n  - ERROR: UdClean V2.0 is for /X 3.x ONLY !!!\n\n  - ERROR: BBS:CNF_AMIGA/Conf.DB doesn''t Exist!!\n\n  - MAJOR ERROR: There is Something WRONG with your User.Data & User.Keys !!!\n\n      User.Data FileSize is xxxxx Bytes And Should be at Least xxxxx Bytes!!\n      User.Keys FileSize is xxxxx Bytes And Should be at Least xxxxx Bytes!!\n\n  - MAJOR ERROR: There is Something WRONG with your User.Data !!!\n\n      User.Data FileSize is xxxxx Bytes And Should be at Least xxxxx Bytes!!\n\n  - MAJOR ERROR: There is Something WRONG with your User.Keys !!!\n\n      User.Keys FileSize is xxxxx Bytes And Should be at Least xxxxx Bytes!!\n\n  - If there still are problems Check if you are using a Valid OS 2.x+\n    with the OS 2.x+ commands: Copy, Rename & Delete\n    And try to run it in CLI, it will returns error messages.\n\n\n BUG REPORT & SUGGESTIONS:                                                                   \n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  It''s possible that a mistake will occur.... Nobody is Perfect.\n  If you have any Suggestions or Comments Please Leave a Message on my Board:\n\n   LaSt OuTpOsT  N1: +31-72-157682  N2: +31-72-201182  -  HOLLAND\n   ~~~~~~~~~~~~  ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~     ~~~~~~~\n      AmiXnet:  @NL0004   -   /X-Fido:  44:100/102   [EMPiRE]\n      ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~       ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~   ~~~~~~~~\n\n\n NOTE:\n ~~~~~\n  - This Tool works GREAT on a 68040 or higher. But on a 68000\n    it''s a bit slow! So be patient or buy a 020+!\n  - This Program Has been tested on a /X 3.6 Configuration.\n\n\n REGISTRATION:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - If YOU use this Program please let me know by Calling Last Outpost or\n    to send me a Message by NetMail @NL0004 [EMPiRE] Coz I want to know\n    how many Sysops are using my Tools!!!\n\n\n\n HISTORY:\n ~~~~~~~~\n  V1.0  01-24-93  - Release Version\n  V1.1  01-25-93  - Forgot to Mention in This Doc to Create BBS:BACKUP\n                  - Changed LayOut\n                  - Added:  User.Data/User.Keys Salvage/Repair\n  V1.2  02-26-93  - Minor Update\n  V1.3  03-01-93  - Added:  Auto User.Data/User.Keys Clean for MonthTopUp\n                  - Added:  User.Data.MONTH Clean for MonthTopUp\n  V1.4  04-27-93  - Minor Update\n  V1.5  05-22-93  - Forced Deletes of Temp.Files\n                  - This is the EndVersion for use with /X 2.xx\n\n  V2.0  07-06-93  - Major Update for use with /X 3.x (Only)\n                  - It will NOT work anymore with /X 2.xx, use UdClean V1.5\n        08-28-93  - Added User.Data.MONTH [MonthTop/MonthTopUp] Backup\n        08-29-93  - Release Version\n                  - Tnx to JAF & Alcoholic for BetaTesting!\n\n\n\n EMPiRE /X DOORS/UTILS COLLECTION UNTIL NOW!:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n  - AboStats V0.2                        Door\n  - Bulls V1.2                           Door\n  - ConfAccess V1.0                      ServerUtil\n  - CopyDate V1.3                        Util\n  - DlDir V1.2                           Door\n  - DMenu V1.0                           Door\n  - DutchNET V1.0                        Door\n  - JoinCnf V2.3                         Door\n  - LastCalls & TopUps V1.2              LogOffUtil\n  - LastDL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - LastUL V0.1                          LogOffUtil\n  - MakeFree V1.1                        Door\n  - MemberList V1.0                      Door\n  - MonthTopUp V2.0                      ServerUtil\n  - MultiNodeLastCaller V1.1             LogOffUtil\n  - Page V1.3                            Door\n  - PMail V1.1                           Door\n  - Remote Maintenance V1.3              Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.2                 Door\n  - SceneTalk Voter V2.2 [Party]         Util\n  - SecLevChk V1.0                       ServerUtil\n  - SysopCommands V1.0                   Door\n  - UserData Cleaner/Repair V2.0         ServerUtil\n  - UserStats V2.0                       ServerUtil\n  - UsrCnfAx V1.1                        Util\n  - View & Check Archive/Text V1.7       Door\n  - WhoNode V1.3                         Door\n\n\n THANX MUST GO TO ALL OFFICIAL BETA-TESTERS:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   Aragorn/Vision           [Terminal Zone]\n   Camelot/Endless Piracy   [Chaos]\n   Telekinetic/T.F.A.       [Total Confusion]\n   Alcoholic                [Sticky Fingers]\n   Tycoon/Desire            [Channel X]\n   Mike                     [Global Chaos]\n   Amicom/TRSi              [Skara Brae]\n   JPS/Mystic               [Red October]\n   Sal-One/TRSi             [BoonDocks]\n   \n\n GREETZ/TNX GO TO:\n ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~\n   JAF/G.O.D.              - For some good REXX ideas\n   Bad Sector/Hidden       - Still Using your Sort-Routine. It''s the Fastest!\n   B.O.B./Paradox          - For Using my tools!\n   Price/T.F.A.            - Great job on your {F}ull {S}creen {E}ditor!\n   Van Damme               - Nice Doors!!!\n   DR.G/IBB                - Hope to see Check''N Add work on a 040\n   SiGMA SEVEN             - Long time no ''See''\n   911/Movement            - Tnx For ya Ansi Logo''s!!\n   SkIn                    - Tnx for ya Decription Logo! Looks Great!!\n   Shadow Man/S8           - Nice Doors & Tnx for ya greetz!\n   ByteAndi/AFL            - NewFiles is the best!\n   [MAZ!^PHAZE]            - Tnx for calling!\n   Shuyuki Tanaka          - Tnx for your Messages!\n   AGONY/cOmpLeX           - Tnx for calling!\n   HUMANOiD & RU$tY AFL    - Tnx for calling!\n   LECTOR                  - Tnx for your NetMail\n   Koo Koo Ka Choo         - Tnx for calling!\n   TaRzAN                  - Tnx for your NetMail. Yezz 040 Speed Rulez!!\n   Crayor/Scx              - Tnx for calling!\n   Foxx''n''Lynxx            - Tnx for your BugReport!!!\n   ThE BiRD                - Tnx for your NetMail on UsrCnfAx\n\n\n   & Ofcoz to ALL MYSTiC Members all over the Globe!\n\n\n\n                       ____ _________ _________ ________ _____\n            MYSTiC    /   / \\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /     MYSTiC\n                     /   /   \\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n         CONSOLE    /   / /\  /   __   \ \____   \   /   /      CONSOLE\n                   /   /_/  \/   / /   /_____/   /  /   /\n       WHQ        /_________/___/ /___/_________/  /___/      WHQ\n      ____ _________ ________ _________ _________ _________ ________ _____\n     /    \\\  /   / \\  /   \\\  /    \\\  /    \\\  /   _\\\  /   \\\  /\n    /   /\ \\\/   / /\\\/__   \\\/   /\ \\\/   /\ \\\/   /___\\/__   \\\/\n   /   / /  \/   / /  \   /   / /   _____\/   / /  \ \____   \   /   /\n  /   /_/   /   /_/   /  /   / /   /     /   /_/   /_____/   /  /   /\n /_________/_________/  /___/ /___/     /_________/_________/  /___/\n\n\n  AMIGA - SNES - MEGADRIVE - GAMEBOY - GAMEGEAR - PC ENGINE - LYNX - C64\n\n  3.5 Gb ONLINE          SYSOP:  EMPiRE / MYSTiC           3.5 Gb ONLINE\n\n  HOLLAND:          +31-72-157682  &  +31-72-201182             :HOLLAND\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',17879,5,0,NULL,NULL,'emp_uc20','scan',1780048504);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('emt_anwl','EMT-ANWL.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/EMT-ANWL.LHA','AnarchyWall','XIM','AnarchyWall',NULL,NULL,'EMT','___ /_  /__   __ __   ___   __    /_ | / __\ / / __\ /  Y  \ / __\ /  \___ / |_ / /__/_ / /__/_  \_/  \ /__/_  _ \ // __|__f',replace('       ____                          ___\n  ___ /_  /__   __ __   ___   __    /_ |\n / __\ / / __\ /  Y  \ / __\ /  \___ / |_\n/ /__/_ / /__/_  \_/  \ /__/_  _ \ // __|__f\n\_____//\_____/___|    \____/__/   \  |   /®\n                  |_____\     /_____\____/ z\n       -=^÷A·N·A·R·C·H·Y÷^W·A·L·L÷^=-\nCOOL WALL (XIM) WITH COLOUR CHOOSE FUNCTION.\n       CONFIGURABLE WITH NICE DEZIGN!\nDESIGN:                                CODE:\nTRIVIAL/dS!                   aPACHE/eLEMENT\n','\n',char(10)),'AnarchyWall.doc',replace('        _____   __________      ______   ________        _____  ________\nt^______\__ /_._\   __    \.____\__  /_._\  _   `---/   _\ __/__\_.    /-----.\n /    _       |     |/     |   _       |    _______/---/   \      |____      |\n/_____________|_____/______|___________|---//_________/\__________| //_______|\n\n                                  W A L L\n\n                           Code  : Apache/Element  \n                           Dezign: Trivial/dS!\n\n\n                               [$] What? [$]\n\nIt''s THE wall for AmigaExpress.\n\n                               [$] Why? [$]\n\nBecause most wall''s out there are ugly, and the nice one''s didn''t have the\ncolour effects.\n\n                            [$] Installing [$]\n\nI presume you run AmigaExpress, and if you don''t know how to install an \nordinary XIM door, I don''t wan''t you to use it. \n(That includes you, saboteur/afl)\n\n                            [$] Compability [$]\n\nThis door works 100% on both Daydream (using XIM by Darkelf) and S!X.\n\n                               [$] Ehum! [$]\n\nIf you use this door, I wouldn''t be asking for too much if I got free leech\non your board, would I? That''s all you have to do.\n\n                              [$] Thanks [$]\n\nTrivial, Tommy, Ventura, Kid Curry, Greedysnitch, Aryan, Meanace, Bea\/is,\nB.B King, Desoto, IronNuke, XTC, Voodoo, MadMax, Snafu, Cluster, Kim Strid,\nArcane, Bc, Chriz, Des Legumes, Frenzy, Immortal, Injector J, Jmf, Joe, Madsen,\nNecroman, Neptune, Numen, Psycho, Raptor, Sth, Ten, Tomentor, Neville, kID,\nD-Fekt, Cricket, Moonlighter, Orlando, Or¡on, the Holiday Inn sysop,\nfor the moment I just can''t remember your name, I got it on my tongue! :)\nAnd to Saboteur/Afl (or Ice, linkan, Nazi Nöff, randy, exstacy, did I forget\nany of your names?) for being so fucking lame.\n\n                            [$] Contacting [$]\n\nTo contact me, leave me (Apache) a message at any of these boards:\n\nPet Sematary, Outpost, Global Explorer, City Of Joy, Babylon Zoo, Nile\nCity, Lies Unlimited, City Dump, Holliday Inn.\n\nNow, have fun!\n\n                                                  / Apache/Element -96!\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',99295,3,0,NULL,NULL,'emt_anwl','scan',1780048504);
