LL ONE OF THE KEKSDESIGN-BOARD OR LEAVE A MESSAGE AT THE\nCOCO-NET! BUG-REPORT AND SUGGESTIONS TO:\n\nCount Dan:       Confusion BBS: (0341 - 6896640) \n				leave a message at the logon-prompt!\n				running latest version of CNET !\n\n2. Contents of this Archive\n===========================\n\n		sysud     - main program (don''t change anything!)\n		sysud.cfg - config file\n		temp/     - required dir !\n		sysud.doc - this doc!\n\n3.Installation\n==============\n\nCopy all the files to pfiles: ! THE DIR pfiles:KEKSDESIGN/sysud/TEMP/ must\nexist !!\n\nAdd in your systext:bbsmenu following line where you want to have it\n(if you don''t know where to place it, read in your CNET-manual ;) )\n\nSD	#0pfiles:keksdesign/sysud/sysud}\n	^                               ^\n	|                               ` SHIFT + ALT + "+"\n	` CTRL + "Q"\n\nNow load the sysud.cfg into an editor:\n------------------- cut here -------------------------\n31\nleech\n4free\ndelete\n0\n0\n------------------- cut here -------------------------\n1. line: Level you have to have to use SD without knowing the Password\n	 [If you use 32 everyone have to enter the pw!]\n2. line: The password you have to know to use SD !\n	 [NONE=No Password to know]\n3. line: The password to leech the files for FREE!\n	 [NONE=always for free Dl]\n4. line: The password to delete a file!\n	 [NONE=no password needed to delete the file(s)]\n5. line: 0=Only shows Files ending with .txt, .doc, .dok, .nfo\n	 1=Shows all files (shows also e.g. c:dir,c:list,c:lha)\n6. line: 0=QUICKMODE ACTIVE\n	 1=QUICKMODE INACTIVE\n	\n\nATTENTION: PASSES MUST BE DIFFERENT FROM THE CHAR "Q", BECAUSE WITH Q\n	   YOU CAN QUIT THE PROGRAM AND FROM "NONE", BECAUSE WITH\n	   NONE YOU DISABLE THE PASSWORD-PROTECTION!\n\nTHat''s all folks!\n\n\n4.Requirements\n==============\nAn Amiga (i coded this fine tool on a A1200 with no extras,that means \n14,4 MHZ and 2 MB ram! and it all works fine !)- CNET and an AREXX-Server\n\n!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!\nATTENTION: With 2 MB RAM and running rpstart, CNET, CED i had not have \n	   enough ram for crunching the progs and the computer went down\n	   (GURUUUUUUU , GURUUUUUUUU!! ;) ). Well be carefully !\n!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!\n\nCOMMANDS:\n	c:LIST	  - oN aLL wBs\n	c:DELETE  - oN aLL wBs\n	c:ZIP     - yOU hAVE tO hAVE oNE\n	c:LHA	  - yOU hAVE tO hAVE oNE\n	c:echo    - oN aLL wBs\n\nWhy i have decided to use c-commands? Yes why not? they are written in ASS\n(CNET didn''t support ASS-PROGS, or can you write cNET-tOOLS in ASS? tHEN\n cONTACT mE!), they are fast. And the script get faster and shorter !\n\nYou can get lha- and the zip-command in every PD-Board ! So leech it, if\nyou don''t have it !\n\n5.History\n=========\n\n05-??-95        v0.1ß   Now i remember me ! My first try to copy the\n			Sysopdownload from Byteandi! Very lame (slow \n			also .) ). I never ended it really, but when\n			i installed my new harddisk, i couldn''t save\n			it ! BETTER THAN EVER:\n\n12-03-95	v0.1	first release - only one cruncher available\n			hardly no design - very slow\n			RELEASE DOESN''T WORK (I OPTIMIZED IT WITH \n			REXXOPT V1.6 AND THEN IT WON''T WORK, DON''T\n			KNOW WHY! ;()\n\n12-04-95	v0.2	now 2 cruncher (PC-AMIGA/ZIP-LHA) \n			          !more design ! \n			you can now deselect your selected files\n			in the normal selection base- cleans up the\n			filebuffer, no longer filenumbers like:\n			1 3 5 8! \n			BUG REMOVED: NOW CHECKING LOST CARRIER\n\n12-05-95	v0.3	now you can ONLINE-delete files, after entering\n			a password! You can see all the Textfiles (files)\n			on the hd! Checks if the Dir is empty (BUG)\n			Shows selected files in the FILE-/DIRLISTING\n			\n			\n6.FEATURES\n==========\n\n	What will be new in version 0.4?\n\n		a) TESTING ARCS bEFORE DOWNLOADING!\n		b) yOUR sUGGESTIONS tO cONFUSION bBS!\n\n\n7.WANTED!\n=========\n\nKeks Design is in need of more members! We only search talented CNet-Tools-\nCoders and Ascii Artists! If you can code in C, ASM or Arexx, please contact\nus at the following boards:\n\nSeraph:		Mystic BBS:    (030  - offline)\nCount Dan:	Confusion BBS: (0341 - 6896640)\n\nLeave a message at the logon-prompt - we''ll call you back!\nThanx a lot!\n\n8.Keks Design Memberlist\n========================\n\nCount Dan:	Leader, Ascii Artist, CNet-Tools-Coder\nSeraph:		German Headquarter Sysop, CNet-Tools-Coder\nMalcolm X:	Ascii Artist, CNet-Tools-Coder\nMeucci:         Script-writer !\n\nPlease note: because of less coding and supporting KeksDesign, TINIC is\nno longer Keks Design Member! But have a look at his great program\nTinyMeter!\n\n9.Bye bye!\n==========\n\nWell, that''s the end of this doc! Have fun with our productions and\nremember: Save the scene! Don''t be lazy! Move your ass!\n\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,NULL,16630,5,0,NULL,NULL,NULL,'scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('klr_bd14','KLR_BD14.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KLR_BD14.LHA','KiLLER_Baud','XIM','KiLLER_Baud',NULL,NULL,'KLR','.-\_  \/  _/   __/______\/___/-------------. | /   \/   \______  \_  \/  \_   mYSTIC!   | :/     \____/   _____/___    / /X dIVISION .',replace('  __/\  __________ _ ___  /\__              \n.-\_  \/  _/   __/______\/___/-------------.\n| /   \/   \______  \_  \/  \_   mYSTIC!   |\n:/     \____/   _____/___    / /X dIVISION .\n/_______\-\_____/--sTZ--l___/--------------:\n| KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC BRiNGS: KiLLER-BAUD 1.4 |\n| *Creates bulls about users'' baud-rates!* :\n: MAJOR BUGFiX AND CORRECTED SUPPLiED iCON !\n| o Exclude baud-rates  o Nice lay-out     ¡\n: o Two pages of info!  o Future proof!    |\n`------------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),'KiLLER_Baud.Guide',replace('@DATABASE "KiLLER-Comment.GUiDE"\n@REMARK	.-------------------------------------------------------.\n@REMARK  | This document should be read with AmigaGuide (OS 2.x) |\n@REMARK  | or MultiView (OS 3.x) or any other guide reader. Tnx. |\n@REMARK  `-------------------------------------------------------''\n@REMARK	$VER: KiLLER Baud 1.4.0\n@WIDTH 80\n@NODE MAIN "KiLLER Comment - MYSTiC 1994 - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             Guide to KiLLER Baud\n                 for Ami-Express 3.38+ Bulletin Board Systems\n\n                             Program version: 1.4.0\n                Compilation Date/Time: Thu Feb  2 21:30:48 1995\n\n                         © 1994/1995 by @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n\n                   ... another fine MYSTiC /X production ...\n\n      @{" First words... " link WhatIs}    What is KiLLER Baud\n      @{" Features       " link Features}    What can it do?\n      @{" Installation   " link Install}    How to install KiLLER Baud on your system\n      @{" Example 1      " link BaudUsage}    Example output of bulletin 1\n      @{" Example 2      " link LastBauds}    Example output on bulletin 2\n      @{" History        " link History}    History of KiLLER Baud\n      @{" Greetings      " link Greetings}    Big hellos and messages\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Contact "How to contact KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC - © Me me me! :)"\n                            .--------------------.\n                            |  Contacting me...  |\n                            `--------------------''\n\n       For FAST response (preferred) please contact me on Internet:\n\n                               [email removed]\n\n                    ( [email removed] -- backup )\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                 I can be reached on the following boards:\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                The Undiscovered       +31-2294-1442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-4442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-tRY! (USR 28k8)\n\n                Last Outpost           +31-72-201182 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-72-157682 (USR 28k8)\n\n         I know, you have to call The Netherlands for it, but it''s\n            the only boards I call on a frequent basis, sorry.\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n        You can also find me on IRC once in a while on the channels\n                   #amigascne, #amiga, #rave and #megawatt\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatIs "What is KiLLER Baud - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                        .------------------------.\n                        |  What is KiLLER Baud?  |\n                        `------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Baud is (another) statistical mumbo jumbo door.  It is able to\n   create  some  nice  statistics  about  the baud rate your users use to\n   logon your system.  It is styled in the same way as @{"KiLLER Comment" link KiLLERComment}, so\n   if  you  are familiar with that door, you will know that the output of\n   it will be fair enough :-)\n\n   I  made  this  door  as a simple diversion of some other projects I am\n   working  on.   This  door  was created in two days and I don''t feel it\n   should  be  released  to  the  public  because  it is too simple to be\n   released.   The  idea  is not my own, I saw another ''accumulative baud\n   report''  door  and  I  am  still  recovering from the output that door\n   creates.   I  don''t  know where some people get their inspiration from\n   but that output reminded me of kindergarten ;-)\n\n   And  because  of  the  nice  respons  I  got from some sysops about my\n   previous  releases,  I  thought  it  would  be  nice  to  make another\n   statistical  door,  sysops can''t get enough of BBS statistics.\n\n   Anyway,  I hope you like it and if you do, please give me a yell.\n\n                                                         @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Features "Features of KiLLER Baud - © Me me me and nobody else! :)"\n                           .--------------------------.\n                           |  Features of KiLLER Baud |\n                           `--------------------------''\n\n   ­ Create two pages of statistical data in one bulletin\n     (With a ''Space to Resume'' between them ofcourse)\n\n   ­ Create two pages of statistical data divided over 2 bulletins\n     (So two separate files)\n     \n   ­ Create either one of the two possible bulletins.\n     (You can choose between @{"Baud Usage" link BaudUsage} and @{"Last Bauds" link LastBauds})\n\n   ­ Only show the output on screen, don''t create the bulletins.\n     (Two pages, not selectable).\n\n   Other program features are:\n\n   · Fast baud detecting, virtually no delay for users.\n   · Very small data file to create bulletins\n   · You can remove baud rates from the bulletin(s).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Install "How to Install KiLLER Baud - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC (that''s me)"\n                      .------------------------------.\n                      |  How to install KiLLER Baud  |\n                      `------------------------------''\n\n   You are free to call the programs whatever you want and you can locate\n   the  program  wherever  you want.  I hate programs that have hardcoded\n   paths in their source.\n\n   The KiLLER_Baud executable has got a double function:  update the data\n   file  and create the bulletin(s).  Depending on available tooltypes in\n   the command icon, KiLLER Baud will know what to do.\n\n   ­­ Baud Detection ­­\n\n   First  of  all,  make  sure  that  KiLLER_Baud is run at least once to\n   update the data file.  I suggest you install it as an MCI command in a\n   bulletin  that  is  shown  to  every  user  that logs in (or logs out,\n   whatever fits you best).\n\n   · Example ·\n\n   ~CC_KB-DETECT\n   \n   · End ·\n\n   To  have  KiLLER_Baud  only  update the data file, create an icon that\n   holds  the tooltype "DETECT_BAUD".  If this tooltype is present in the\n   icon with what KiLLER_Baud was started, the data file will be updated.\n   This data file will reside in the same directory as the program itself\n   and will  be called "KiLLER_Baud.Data" and is 484 bytes in size.  Note\n   that  the  bulletin(s)  will not be created!  An example icon has been\n   provided called "KB-DETECT.info".\n   \n   Important tooltypes for that command are:\n   \n   DETECT_BAUD             ­ This tooltype MUST be present if you want KB\n                             to update the data file.\n                             \n   LOCAL_NODE=<number>     ­ KiLLER_Baud  will  not add your local logins\n                             to its data file.  Normally,  the local node\n                             would be node 0, but certain boards that run\n                             /X-Fido  for example have their last node as\n                             their local node.  So with this tooltype you\n                             can specify which node is the local node.\n                             \n                             You  can  also specify a node which does not\n                             exist,  and  your local logins will be added\n                             to the data file.\n                             \n                             Default value is node 0.\n\n   ­­ Bulletin Creation ­­\n\n   Unless  you  want  to install KiLLER_Baud as a regular /X command that\n   can  be  started  from  the  main  prompt,  you  also  need to install\n   KiLLER_Baud  as  another  MCI command, but this time to have it create\n   the bulletin(s).\n\n   You  can  specify the name(s) of the bulletin(s) with tooltypes.  Here\n   are the important tooltypes for this command:\n\n   BULL=<filename>         ­ When specified, one bulletin will be created\n                             containing 2 pages of statistics,  separated\n                             by a "Space to Resume".\n\n   BULL.1=<filename>       ­ This  will  result in one bulletin that only\n                             contains the @{"Baud Usage" link BaudUsage} information.\n\n   BULL.2=<filename>       ­ This  induces KiLLER_Baud to create only the\n                             @{"Last Bauds" link LastBauds} bulletin and nothing more.\n\n   NOTE¹ : The `BULL'' tooltype overrides both BULL.1 and BULL.2 !!\n\n   NOTE² : If you ommit all of these tooltypes, KiLLER_Baud will show the\n           bulletins on screen and will not create any bulletin.\n           \n   If  you  wish,  you  can  have  certain  baud  rates  removed from the\n   bulletins.   Normally,  the  bulletin would contain baud rates ranging\n   from  SLOW  to FAST, whereas SLOW means all baud rates below 9600 baud\n   and  FAST all baudrates above the highest speed available. Please note\n   that both the SLOW and FAST baud rates will not be put in the bulletin\n   until they are actually used.\n\n   For  example,  there  is  a BBS somewhere in Finland that has no 12000\n   baud users.  As strange as it is, this was true.  But the bulletin did\n   contain  the  12000  baud  line.  It was much disliked by the sysop of\n   that  board.   It even got to a point where he get himself drunk, felt\n   bad (a la Michael Jackson) and called The Undiscovered BBS.\n\n   There  he  wrote me a message saying that he hated the 12000 baud line\n   and  that  it  should  be removed.  I made him clear that although his\n   board  did  not  have 12000 baud users, the Undiscovered has got them!\n   He felt like an ass and left crying.  I felt so bad about this, that I\n   made it possible to leave out any baudrate you like.  However, this is\n   purely  COSMETIC.  The 12000 connects are still being counted and they\n   are  also used for the total number of connects.  But it was the least\n   I could do for such a lonely guy (snif).\n\n   So  without telling you the name of the sysop (QUARTZ QUARTZ QUARTZ!),\n   this is how to disable baudrates:\n\n   NO_SLOW     - Disable  SLOW baudrate\n   NO_9600     - Disable  9600 baudrate\n   NO_12000    - Disable 12000 baudrate (see! :) )\n   NO_14400    - Disable 14400 baudrate\n   ...\n   NO_FAST     - Disable FAST baudrate\n   \n   These  tooltypes  can be put in the K-Baud tooltype and remember, they\n   are  purely  cosmetic,  if  you  disable all baudrates and look at the\n   bulletin,  there  will  be NO baudrates mentioned, but there will be a\n   total number of connects and a favourite baudrate.\n\n   ­­ Regular /X Command ­­\n\n   If  you want it to install like a regular command, install it like any\n   other  command but make sure you remove the BULL#?  tooltypes from the\n   icon or it will create bulletins instead of showing them.\n\n   ­­ Overview ­­\n\n   ONCE AGAIN (but in short):\n   \n      · K-Baud can either create bulletins or display them pronto\n      · To make it create bulletins, either put the BULL tooltype\n        or BULL.x tooltypes in the icon (NOT BOTH!).\n      · To disable baudrates use tooltype: NO_<baudrate>\n      · If  you  remove all BULL* tooltypes from the K-Baud icon,\n        K-Baud will show the results on screen right away.\n\n   ­­ Reset the counting ­­\n\n   If  you  want  to reset the counting, simply remove the data file.  It\n   will  detect itself that the file is gone and create a new one (with a\n   new starting date).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE LastBauds "Example Output: Last Baud (Bull.2) - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n.-----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER BAUD v1.4  ^  <> Baud Rate Statistical View <>  ^  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC! |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------.--------------------------.---------------------------------.\n| Connect String | Date/Time last Connect   | Last Connect made by            |\n:----------------:--------------------------:---------------------------------:\n|   9.600 baud   | Fri Dec 16 12:14:45 1994 | ArCaDe/MYSTiC                   |\n|  12.000 baud   | Never                    | Nobody                          |\n|  14.400 baud   | Sun Dec 18 19:35:31 1994 | Kerstman                        |\n|  16.800 baud   | Sat Dec 17 09:03:12 1994 | Sal-One                         |\n|  19.200 baud   | Sun Dec 18 15:39:33 1994 | Incognito/MST                   |\n|  21.600 baud   | Sun Dec 18 11:32:50 1994 | WiSHBRiNGER                     |\n|  24.000 baud   | Fri Dec 16 23:11:22 1994 | skate BOB                       |\n|  26.400 baud   | Sun Dec 18 19:59:58 1994 | Motive Power/MST                |\n|  28.800 baud   | Sun Dec 18 02:13:26 1994 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC                |\n:----------------^--------------------------^---------------------------------:\n| Total Connects :  64041 ^ Favourite speed: 21.600 baud ^ Since: 16-Dec-1994 |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n                     This is bulletin 2 (BULL.2 tooltype).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE BaudUsage "Example Output: Baud Usage (Bull.1) - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n.-----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER BAUD v1.4  ^  <> Baud Rate Statistical View <>  ^  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC! |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------.----------------------.-------.-------.---------------------.\n| Connect String | 0%--------------100% | Total | Perc% | Overall             |\n:----------------:----------------------:-------:-------:---------------------:\n|   9.600 baud   | ####                 |    52 |    5% | Doing fine          |\n|  12.000 baud   | ######               |    86 |    9% | Popular rate        |\n|  14.400 baud   | #########            |   120 |   13% | Steady size.        |\n|  16.800 baud   | ############         |   154 |   17% | Substantial         |\n|  19.200 baud   | #                    |    17 |    1% | Among others        |\n|  21.600 baud   | #####                |    66 |    7% | Steady basis        |\n|  24.000 baud   | ####                 |    59 |    6% | Steady basis        |\n|  26.400 baud   | #################### |   255 |   29% | THE baud rate!      |\n|  28.800 baud   | ##                   |    34 |    3% | Doing fine          |\n:----------------^----------------------^-------^-------^---------------------:\n| Total Connects :    867 ^ Favourite speed: 26.400 baud ^ Since: 16-Dec-1994 |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n   The  graphical percentages shown are a RELATIVE comparison between the\n   most used baud rates and the rest.\n\n   Both  the  `Slower''  and  `Faster'' baudrate are ONLY shown when it has\n   been actually used by someone.\n\n                   (And yes, this output is a fraud :) )\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE History "History of KiLLER Baud - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                          .--------------------------.\n                          |  History of KiLLER Baud  |\n                          `--------------------------''\n\n   \n   1.4.0    02-Feb-95   Fixed       After spending two hours with on the\n                                    with Muad''Dib, we finally discovered\n                                    that I forgot to put the tooltype\n                                    TYPE=XIM in the icon that I delivered\n                                    in the package. This caused quite\n                                    some problems on some boards, but not\n                                    on mine ;) But we found it, and I\n                                    fixed it so it works again.\n                                    *Reported by Muad''Dib*\n\n   1.3.1    02-Feb-95   Fixed       I misplaced the code from the KiLLER\n                                    detect program causing two errors ;(\n                                    This has been fixed, sorry dudes.\n                                    *Detected by NightShade & Motive Power*\n\n   1.3.0    05-Jan-95   Improved    Put the code to update the data file\n                                    into the main KiLLER_Baud program to\n                                    save space. This saved about 14KB.\n\n   1.2.2    04-Jan-95   NEW         You can now have baud rates removed\n                                    from the bulletin with the tooltypes:\n                                    NO_<baudrate>.\n                                    *Cried for by Quartz/MST*\n\n   1.2.1    25-Dec-94   Improved    Added two more checks that prevent a\n                                    division by zero (guru #80000005).\n\n   1.2.0    21-Dec-94   Improved    The baud rates "Slower" and "Faster" are\n                                    now only shown when they are actually in\n                                    use. It''s a cosmetic change.\n                                    *Requested by Muad''Dib*\n\n   1.1.0    20-Dec-94   Improved    The graphical percentage bar is now made\n                                    relative to the most used baud rate for\n                                    better representation.\n\n   1.0.0    18-Dec-94   Release     Muad''Dib got the first version to test.\n\n   1.0.0    18-Dec-94   NEW         Created KiLLER Baud.\n\n                                    -*-\n\n                                    OBSOLETE! Put the code into the\n                                    main executable (saving about 14KB).\n\n   1.2.1    25-Dec-94   Improved    When the data file is first created,\n                                    all variables will be cleared now on\n                                    a more firm basis.\n\n   1.2.0    21-Dec-94   Improved    Made it work with the new structure and\n                                    revised it to 1.2.0 for compatibility\n                                    reasons.\n\n   1.0.1    18-Dec-94   Fixed       KiLLER Detect now detects the line speed\n                                    and not the speed between modem and\n                                    computer (sigh).\n                                    Reported by: Muad''Dib/DLT\n\n   1.0.0    16-Dec-94   NEW         Created KiLLER Detect.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Greetings "Aloha! - By KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                                .-------------.\n                                |  Greetings  |\n                                `-------------''\n\n   Muad''Dib/DLT/iFT     Let''s crash your machine! Moehaha! :)\n   \n   Quartz/MYSTiC        You cry baby! :)\n\n   Motive Power/MST     28k8 beats 26k4 bud... ;)\n   \n   I-GO/Perseus         To bad you weren''t at the O22VW\n   \n   Zed                  I hope to contact you soon!\n   \n   Jake/Prestige        One day I will come and leech, don''t worry :)\n   \n   Tycoon/MYSTiC        It was fun, but to all good things come an end\n\n   Shuyuki Tanaka       Try to contact me at my Internet address! Japan\n                        is very very very cool!\n\n                      ...and to all my other good friends!\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE KiLLERComment "KiLLER-WHAT? - © 1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n         «« THIS IS AN EXCERPT OUT OF THE GUIDE OF KILLER COMMENT ««\n\n                         .---------------------------.\n                         |  What is KiLLER Comment?  |\n                         `---------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER Comment is number 1001 in the row of a [C]omment doors.  But this\n   is not just *a* comment door, otherwise I wouldn''t have made it.  I have\n   some profound reasons.\n\n   Despite  the  fact  that  there  must be over a 1000 different [C]omment\n   doors,  there  is not one comment door that suits my needs.  I have seen\n   plenty of them but to me they all look ugly, have static interfaces, are\n   as flexible as a log of wood and generally suck :-).\n\n   With  this mind I''ve tried to make KiLLER Comment as smart and pretty as\n   I  can  and  I  feel I have succeeded.  I have never seen a comment door\n   that  works  as  simple  as  this  one  with  a fair amount of so-called\n   ''intelligence'' and comes with a nice GUI.\n\n   What  is  this  ''intelligence'' I speak about you say?  Well, all comment\n   doors  sofar  weren''t  able  to see that if I typed "c kill" I wanted to\n   comment to "KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" nor where they able to see that if I typed\n   "c  amiga" I wanted to comment to the Amiga co-sysop.  KiLLER Comment is\n   able to detect this!\n\n   Another  thing  is  security.   Every  new  user  was  able to see which\n   cosysops  you  had,  but  by giving every cosysop a security level, they\n   will  not see their names/jobs.  And if there is only one (co)sysop they\n   are  allowed  to  see,  they  will  not  even  see it is done via KiLLER\n   Comment.\n\n   Also,  per user in the [C]omment list there is a check wether he/she has\n   got access to the conference that KiLLER [C]omment is called from, so it\n   is  impossible  to write a message to someone who doesn''t have access to\n   that conference.\n\n   What I personally also dislike are comment doors that only fill like 25%\n   of  your  screen.   Not  that  you  should put a lot of nonsense in your\n   comment  door,  no,  but  KiLLER  Comment puts an extra line between the\n   names  of  the  (co-)sysops  if  that''s possible with the user''s maximum\n   numbers  of  lines  he  can  handle  on his screen.  It''s these kinds of\n   things I miss in those 13-in-a-dozen [C]omment doors.  That''s why I made\n   KiLLER Comment.\n\n   And  oh, just like I first introduced cursor control in ConfScan, KiLLER\n   comment also uses cursor control for selecting a user you can write to.\n\n                             »» END OF EXCERPT ««\n\n   To get hold of this tool, simply @{"contact" link Contact} me.\n\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),'{"TYPE":"XIM"}','auto-added',34973,1,0,NULL,NULL,'klr_bd14','scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('klr_chat','KLR-CHAT.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KLR-CHAT.LHA','Chat-O-Meter','XIM','Chat-O-Meter',NULL,'kLR','KLR','cHAT-O-mETER eXEC & source bY kLR ---------------------------------',replace('---------------------------------\ncHAT-O-mETER eXEC & source bY kLR\n---------------------------------\n','\n',char(10)),'Chat-O-Meter.GUIDE',replace('@DATABASE "Chat-O-Meter.GUiDE"\n@REMARK  .-------------------------------------------------------.\n@REMARK  | This document should be read with AmigaGuide (OS 2.x) |\n@REMARK  | or MultiView (OS 3.x) or any other guide reader. Tnx. |\n@REMARK  `-------------------------------------------------------''\n@REMARK  $VER: Chat-O-Meter 2.2.3\n@WIDTH 80\n@NODE MAIN "Chat-O-Meter - MYSTiC ''94/''95 - © ''94/''95 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            Guide to Chat-O-Meter\n                 for Ami-Express 3.38+ Bulletin Board Systems\n\n                            Program version: 2.2.3\n               Compilation Date/Time: Mon Jun 26 21:50:06 1995\n\n                        © 1994/1995 by @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n\n                   ... another fine MYSTiC /X production ...\n\n                       @{"       UPDATE iNFORMATiON        " link WhatsNew}\n\n      @{" Welcome Sysop! " link WhatIs}    What is Chat-O-Meter?\n      @{" Features       " link Features}    What can it do?\n      @{" Installation   " link Install}    How to install Chat-O-Meter\n      @{" Conversion     " link Conversion}    How to convert your old 1.x bulletin to 2.x\n\n      @{" Example 1      " link BullExample}    Example output the bulletin of Chat-O-Meter\n      @{" Example 2      " link ExitChatExample}    Example output when you exit a chat\n\n      @{" History        " link History}    History of Chat-O-Meter\n      @{" Greetings      " link Greetings}    Greetings, messages and contact info\n      @{" Troubles?      " link Troubles}    Trouble-shooting\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatIs "What is Chat-O-Meter - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                          - -----------------------.\n                         |  What Is Chat-O-Meter?  |\n                         `----------------------- -\n\n   It  started out as a tool to keep track of the time you spent chatting\n   with  your users and to be able to make a nice looking @{"bulletin" link BullExample} out of\n   that  information.   But  things  have  changed, not only does it keep\n   track  of  the  time  the sysop spends chatting, it also registers the\n   time every user ever spent chatting with you, the sysop, in a separate\n   data  file.   So  for every user there is information available on how\n   long  s/he  chatted with you and how many times s/he chatted with you.\n\n   Needless to say that it''s also possible to create a full ANSI bulletin\n   based upon the users'' data file.\n\n   At  the  end  of  every  chat  you  will  get  to see a banner holding\n   information  about  your  latest  chat  and  accumulative  info  about\n   previous  chats.   At  your  request,  it  can  also  show  the users''\n   accumulated information.\n\n   Together  with  Chat-O-Top  you  are  able  to  create a wide range of\n   bulletins  from  the  data  gathered while you chat.  Examples of such\n   bulletins are:\n\n   · Top 10 of the longest chats ever.\n   · Top xx of the users that have chatted the longest/sportest with you.\n   · Top xx of the users that have chatted the most/least with you.\n\n   How  will  this  affect  your  chats  you say?  Well, I think you will\n   hardly  notice  a difference since the creation and/or updating of the\n   data  files  is  done  in  a  most  efficient way with as less fuzz as\n   possible.   Creation  of  the  Top Ten Longest Chats only takes upto 1\n   second.\n\n                                ** NOTICE **\n\n   Chat-O-Meter  is  giftware,  this means that you can use it as much as\n   you  like,  where  you like, dressed as you like and how you like, but\n   YOU MAY NOT SELL IT IN ANY WAY!  Permission is granted for distribtion\n   on AmiNet.\n\n                                ** NOTICE **\n\n                                   Enjoy!\n\n                                                        @{"-KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatsNew "What''s New - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                           - --------------------.\n                          |  New in version 2.2  |\n                          `-------------------- -\n\n   Again,  I  have  optimized the executable and I am sure it can be even\n   more optimized, maybe in the future (and without startup-code).\n\n   I  also rewrote the routine that would keep track of your daily chats,\n   as a result it is much faster and more reliable.  Please note that the\n   first  time  you  use  this  version  and  a  new bulletin needs to be\n   generated  by  Chat-O-Meter (because of a new record) there might be a\n   record of 0 seconds and 0 chats.  This will only last for a day.\n\n   The  total  time  that  you''ve  used  chatting with your users used to\n   displayed  in  hours,  minutes and seconds (such as:  2254:33:15), but\n   because  it  lacked clearity on the long run, I''ve altered it a bit so\n   it is now displayed in days, hours and minutes (i.e. 93d 11h 23m).\n\n   And  also  new  in  the  version  is the arrival of Chat-O-Top, a neat\n   little  thingy  that  is  able  to  create  several  very  interesting\n   bulletins, among others:\n\n   * Bulletin of total chatting times with the users\n   * Bulletin of the number of chats with the users\n   * Bulletin of the average time per chat with the users\n\n   These  bulletins  can be sorted either from high to low or vice versa.\n   It  is able to handle custom designs and has some other handy options.\n\n   Please check out the guide to Chat-O-Top for more information.\n\n                           - --------------------.\n                          |  New in version 2.1  |\n                          `-------------------- -\n\n   Added  a  routine  to  keep  track of your daily chats.  At the end of\n   every day (that is, when you start to chat for the first time on a new\n   day)  it  will  calculate  your  total chatting time and if it''s a new\n   record it will be added at the bottom of the Chat-O-Meter bulletin.\n   See supplied @{ "example." link BullExample }\n\n   Also,  at the end of every chat, you will get to see the current score\n   of today''s chatting time.\n   See supplied @{ "example." link ExitChatExample }\n\n   And due to some optimising the executable has become smaller.\n\n                           - --------------------.\n                          |  New in version 2.0  |\n                          `-------------------- -\n\n   The  previous  version ( 1.2 ) was released over one year ago (October\n   1993)  and  to tell you the truth, the coding was lousy and it was not\n   that  sysop  friendly.   But  do  not  despair,  this version has been\n   totally rewritten (from scratch!) and is much smarter and has got some\n   nice new options.  Better forget all about the previous version (1.2).\n\n   It  has  to  be  said  that I would have never created this version if\n   Quartz  hadn''t  asked  for  it  AND if Stezothetic hadn''t made such an\n   awesome logo.  It seems people use my tools after all :-)\n\n   First  of  all,  the best improvement is that it is now able to detect\n   wether you started or ended a chat.  So if you were a previous user of\n   Chat-O-Meter,  the  times  of  deleting  the  old data file before you\n   started another chat are over :) Lost carriers are no problem anymore.\n\n   Another  *BIG* improvement is the @{"new layout" link BullExample}, done by Stezo (Stz).  It\n   now  has  got a really great logo and nice colours.  You''ll love it, I\n   do!  Many thanks to Stezothetic for creating the logo!\n\n   But  that''s not all, another exciting new feature is the creation of a\n   data  file  that  contains  data about chats and times from every user\n   individually  (but  stored  in one single file).  I will also create a\n   door/tool  that creates a nice bulletin out of that one.  If you think\n   this  is nonsense, you are able to turn it off so no data file will be\n   created.\n\n   The  previous  version also did not set the ENV:  variable of AMi-X to\n   the right value, so users that performed a ''who'' saw that the user you\n   were  chatting  with was ''using a door''.  This has been fixed.  But if\n   you  liked  that ''feature'' of Chat-O-Meter, it is still able to hide a\n   chat. Privacy above all (nope, there is no PGP function :) ).\n\n   That''s it.  I hope you''ll like it, it''s one of those nice little doors\n   that  makes  just  the  difference  between your board and your follow\n   sysops'' boards.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Features "Features of Chat-O-Meter - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                      - ------------------------------.\n                     |  Features of Chat-O-Meter 2.0  |\n                     `------------------------------ -\n\n   · Super  Location(tm),  place  the  executable wherever you want (ENV:\n     will do just fine ;) ).\n\n   · Incredible  Creation(tm),  the  Chat-O-Meter bulletin can be created\n     anywhere  you  like it, there is no discrimination of directories at\n     all!  A first in Ami-X land!  :)\n\n   · Ultra  Cool  Layout(tm), thanks to the Great Stezothetic a fantastic\n     layout accompanies this package.\n\n   · Top Security(tm), prevent users from detecting you are chatting with\n     someone (optionable, but comes free with the package as a bonus).\n\n   · Speed  Creation(tm), you have never seen such fast creation/updating\n     of the data files and the bulletin.\n\n   · Space  Saver(tm),  miniscule  files to store the data.  Now you have\n     more room for your public domain software :)\n   \n   · Big  Brother(tm), not only YOUR chats are recorded, a record is kept\n     per  user  for  ultimate  security and reference.  Now you can proof\n     with  whom  you  have  chatted.   A  wide  variety of reports can be\n     generated about this subject (Chat-O-Top).\n\n   · Banner  Baffler(tm), comes in the famous Ultra Cool Layout(tm) style\n     and  tells  you  all  about  your  latest  chats  together  with the\n     accumulated info from the user you''ve chatted with (so you can check\n     if  you''ve  chatted too much with him/here this month, remember your\n     quota!  :) ).\n     \n   · Artificial  Intelligence,  Chat-O-Meter is able to detect wether you\n     want  to  have  yourself asked the question "Show Bulletin [Y/n]" or\n     not when a user hits the Top Ten.\n\n   · Spelling Checker, optionable removal of plural ''s''s (no, this is not\n     a typo) from words like "mINS", "hOURS" and "sECS".\n\n   · Once installed, you will never have to do any maintenance!\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE ExitChatExample "Exit Chat Example - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n   Every time you exit a chat, you will get to see this small bulletin.\n\n                                 FULL SIZE\n :                                                                         :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :  /X MYSTiC       Chat-O-Meter v2.2  by  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     /X MYSTiC  :\n ¡                                                                         .\n :  tIME: 00:02:24 ^ cHAT #: 12404 ^ tODAY: 00:02:26 ^ oVERALL: 1199:04:34 ¡\n .            pRIVATE sCORE: 0000:07:42 ^ pRIVATE chAT #: 22               :\n .                                                                         |\n :   sORRY dUDE, nO Chat-O-Meter tOP tIME tODAY, tRY aGAIN sOON, oKAY?!    :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :                                                                         :\n\n                                 NO_USERFILE\n\n :                                                                         :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :  /X MYSTiC       Chat-O-Meter v2.2  by  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     /X MYSTiC  :\n ¡                                                                         .\n :  tIME: 00:02:24 ^ cHAT #: 12404 ^ tODAY: 00:02:26 ^ oVERALL: 1199:04:34 ¡\n .                                                                         |\n :   sORRY dUDE, nO Chat-O-Meter tOP tIME tODAY, tRY aGAIN sOON, oKAY?!    :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :                                                                         :\n\n                                NO_DAY_RECORD\n\n :                                                                         :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :  /X MYSTiC       Chat-O-Meter v2.2  by  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     /X MYSTiC  :\n ¡                                                                         .\n :    tHIS cHAT:  0:00:51 ^ oVERALL tIME: 1199:04:35 ^ cHAT #: 12406       ¡\n .            pRIVATE sCORE: 0000:07:42 ^ pRIVATE chAT #: 22               :\n .                                                                         |\n :   sORRY dUDE, nO Chat-O-Meter tOP tIME tODAY, tRY aGAIN sOON, oKAY?!    :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :                                                                         :\n\n                         NO_USERFILE & NO_DAY_RECORD\n\n :                                                                         :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :  /X MYSTiC       Chat-O-Meter v2.2  by  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     /X MYSTiC  :\n ¡                                                                         .\n :    tHIS cHAT:  0:00:51 ^ oVERALL tIME: 1199:04:35 ^ cHAT #: 12406       ¡\n .                                                                         |\n :   sORRY dUDE, nO Chat-O-Meter tOP tIME tODAY, tRY aGAIN sOON, oKAY?!    :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :                                                                         :\n\n                                   QUIET\n\n                                    :-)\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Install "Installation of Chat-O-Meter - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                      - -----------------------------.\n                     |  How to install Chat-O-Meter  |\n                     `----------------------------- -\n\n   For the illiterate sysops among us:  smack the executable anywhere you\n   like  but  it''s  better you put it in DOORS:Chat-O-Meter/ because then\n   you  will not have to mess with those nasty icons!  Drag those vicious\n   icons  to  your  SYSCmd  directory  as  quick as you can.  After that,\n   modify  your  STARTCHAT.TXT  and  ENDCHAT.TXT  so  that  CHAT_START is\n   started  when you start a chat and END_CHAT is started when your END a\n   chat.  Now, that wasn''t too hard was it?  :-)\n\n   And now, for those of you who like to read huge guides, here comes the\n   verbose version of the above alinea.\n\n   Step 1 - Place  the  executable any place on this earth.  The supplied\n            icons  have  been setup to find it in DOORS:Chat-O-Meter, but\n            if  you  don''t  mind editing the icons, place it anywhere you\n            like.   Please  be  aware of the fact that the Chat-O-Meter''s\n            data  files  will  be created in the same directory you place\n            it.\n\n            Note  to  other  programmers:  Haven''t you guys ever heard of\n            the  PROGDIR:   assign?   Come  on, hard coded paths in doors\n            suck big time, it lacks knowledge of the system.\n\n   Step 2 - Copy   the   two   supplied   icons,   "Chat_Start.info"  and\n            "Chat_End.info" to your SYSCmd directory.\n\n   Step 3 - For  Chat-O-Meter  to do its job, you will have to alter your\n            STARTCHAT.TXT  and  ENDCHAT.TXT  file(s).  These files can be\n            found  in  your  BBS:NODEx/  directory(-ies), as you probably\n            know (well, did ya?).\n            \n            Make  sure  you  call the "Chat_Start" command when you start\n            chatting and call the "End_Chat" command when exiting a chat.\n            You  can do this with MCI commands.  Consult your Ami-Express\n            manual on how to do this (nah nah :) ).\n\n   Step 4 - We''re  done  with  the  steps. For ultimate performance, make\n            sure  to  check  out  the tooltypes described below, they can\n            really make your day.\n            \n   Step 5 - Oops, sorry.\n\n   Chat_Start.info\n   ---------------\n   STACK=10000          ; Should be enough.\n\n   There  are  no more tooltypes for this icon.  Sorry, I promise to work\n   on it :)\n   \n   Chat_End.info\n   -------------\n   CHAT_END             : This  is how Chat-O-Meter recognizes the end of\n                          a chat. Make sure it is in the icon.\n\n   BULLETIN=            : Use  this  tooltype  to set the name (including\n                          path)  of  the  bulletin that Chat-O-Meter will\n                          create.\n                          (i.e. BULLETIN=BBS:Bulletins/Chat-O-Meter.TXT)\n\n   DONT_ASK             : By  default, whenever a user enters the Top Ten\n                          of  longest  chats  s/he  will be asked if s/he\n                          wants  to see the updated Top Ten.  If you have\n                          something  against  that, you can override this\n                          question  with  this  tooltype,  it will NOT be\n                          shown.\n\n   DONT_TRUNC           : By  default  all plural ''s''-es will be removed.\n                          So  for  example:  1 hOURS  becomes 1 hOUR  and\n                          1 sECS  becomes  1 sEC.  However,  if you think\n                          that  ruins  the  bulletin (cosmetically),  you\n                          can  override  it  by specifying this tooltype.\n\n   QUIET                : If you are one of those quiet types, and do NOT\n                          wish  to see that ugly banner after every chat,\n                          quickly  use this tooltype to make sure it will\n                          never happen (god forbid!) :-).\n\n   NO_USERFILE          : As from version 2.0.3 a file is created holding\n                          all  information  about your users'' chats.  But\n                          if  you  think  that  the  8 bytes per user are\n                          taking  up  way  to much of your precious drive\n                          space,  you  can stop the creation of this file\n                          with  this  tooltype. \n\n   NO_DAY_RECORD        : And  if  it  isn''t enough already, Chat-O-Meter\n                          creates  another  file  of  20 bytes (ack) that\n                          holds info about the time you''ve spent chatting\n                          today  with  users  and  it  also harbours your\n                          tODAY  rECORD.   Again,  for  you poor guys out\n                          there  with  too  little  drive  space, you can\n                          prevent the creation of this file by using this\n                          tooltype.  Goodluck!  :)\n\n   That are all tooltypes for this moment.  When you are done setting the\n   tooltypes  you  are ready with the installation of Chat-O-Meter.  Have\n   fun and chat your head off!  :-)\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE BullExample "Bulletin Example Output - Design by Stezothetic!"\n\n   If  your  viewer  is  just as lousy as mine (AmigaGuide) then you will\n   agree  with  me  that the bulletin looks just plain ugly!  But believe\n   me, in Ami-Express or a good viewer/editor it looks way better.\n\n   And  yes,  it  is  possible for one user to be in the Top Ten multiple\n   times.   Why?   Well obviously I don''t check for it :) But why don''t I\n   check  for  it?   Easy,  how would you feel when you''ve just chatted 4\n   hours  with  the  sysop and it''s not put in the Top Ten because you''ve\n   chatted for 5 hours a previous time?  Not happy, let me tell you that.\n\n   What?  You would like to decide that for me?  Oh.  Well, tell it to me\n   in  my  face  and  maybe, just maybe I will fix it.  Pfwah, I''m amazed\n   that  you are even reading this, 99% of sysops don''t read guides, lazy\n   bastards.\n\n .                          _  __           /        __  _      ·          .\n-O-_____   ___.___  ___________ //\___   / ·   ______\  .        \ _      -O-\n :/  __/__/   |  ¬\/   _ ¬\____\/____/  · /   /  __ ¬\ -:---------\/-----. :\n_/   |   ¬\_  _    \_  _   \_  \/  ¬\_ / / /_/   \/   \_| >cHAT·O·mETER< | :\n\_____     /__|     /__|    /___     //_/ / \_____     /`----------------'' ¦\n-:---l____/---l____/---l___//\-l____/-\_\/-------l____/------ ---  --   -  ¡_\n ¦                       __/  \  ___  ___________  /\_________    _______  \/\n | .------------------.  \_    \/  ¬\/  __ ¬\____\/____/  __ ¬\___\____ ¬\_¦\n ¡ :   ·/X mYSTIC!·   :  /     \/    \_ \____/__ \/  ¬\_  \____/__   \/  _/:\n :_`---------------- - _/       \_____/___     /__     /____     /___/    \_\n \/ -  -- --- ---------\_________/--Stz--\____/--l____/----\____/----\_____/-\n :                                                                         |\n !   o1. Veteran              03 hOURS, 42 mINS aND 42 sECS  (06-jAN-95)   :\n |   o2. Veteran              03 hOURS, 28 mINS aND 53 sECS  (29-dEC-93)   :\n O   o3. skate BOB            03 hOURS, 08 mINS aND 03 sECS  (25-sEP-94)   ¡\n |   o4. Monte Q              03 hOURS, 07 mINS aND 03 sECS  (11-dEC-93)   O\n ¦   o5. SKATE BOB            03 hOURS, 03 mINS aND 40 sECS  (22-dEC-93)   |\n :   o6. KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     02 hOURS, 52 mINS aND 01 sEC   (28-aPR-94)   ¦\n ¦   o7. skate BOB            02 hOURS, 50 mINS aND 55 sECS  (22-oCT-94)   :\n ¡   o8. Sir IQ               02 hOURS, 48 mINS aND 52 sECS  (28-oCT-93)   ·\n_:   o9. bULLOCK/jETSET       01 hOUR , 08 mINS aND 15 sECS  (05-jUN-95)   :_\n\:   1o. [MEGA^FORTRESS]      01 hOUR , 08 mINS aND 09 sECS  (20-jUN-95)   :/\n \                                                                         /\n :\        tOTAL # oF cHATS: 22866       tOTAL cHAT tIME: 2190:19:18      /:\n-O-\---------------------------------------------------------------------/-O-\n :  ·          dAY rECORD: 04:15:10 oN 23-jUN-95 ( 44 cHATS)            ·  :\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE History "History of Chat-O-Meter - Amazing... :)"\n                    - ---------------------------------.\n                   |  History: Something irreversible  |\n                   `-------------------------------- -\n\n                        Chat-O-Meter Ami-Express Door\n                             (more history below)\n\n   26-Jun-95   2.2.3    Chat-O-Meter  can  now also be used in the year\n                        2000 and up, inserted a simple check.\n\n   26-Jun-95   2.2.2    The  total  chatting  time  is now displayed in\n                        units of days, hours and minutes to enhance the\n                        clearity  of  the  time  spent chatting (entire\n                        days are wasted chatting :) ).\n\n                        Combined  some fprintf()''s so the executable is\n                        a few bytes smaller.\n\n   24-Jun-95   2.2.1    Finally  found  that  damn  bug  in  the  today\n                        routine.   How  the hell was I supposed to know\n                        that  time()  works from 1970 and not from 1978\n                        as  demanded  by Date2Amiga()?  Sigh.  But it''s\n                        fixed now, no more passing days at 18:00 hours.\n\n   22-Jun-95   2.2.0    Rewrote the ''today'' routine and replaced some\n                        of the fopen()-fread()-fwrite()''s with AmigaDOS\n                        function calls (Open, Read, Write) for speed.\n\n                        Rewrote  some  parts of the guide, my English\n                        has improved and I think it should show :)\n                        (Hi Radavi, better this way?)\n\n   07-Feb-95   2.1.1    Fixed the ''today''-routine. It could totally ruin\n                        your Chat-O-Meter.Data if things went wrong. The\n                        day-record is now stored in the .today file and\n                        no longer in the Chat-O-Meter.Data file (as it\n                        did in version 2.1.0).\n\n   06-Feb-95   2.1.0    Chat-O-Meter also keeps track of the total chat\n                        time per day. This can be turned off with:\n                        New tooltype: NO_DAY_RECORD\n\n                        Optimized /X link code. Smaller executable.\n\n   25-Dec-94   2.0.9    Chat-O-Meter can also run in silent mode now.\n                        New tooltype: QUIET\n\n   25-Dec-94   2.0.8    Merry X-Mas :) Made some internal changes, removed\n                        the stack-check (smaller executable).\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.7    Found the bug that caused the strange resets.\n                        I adjusted the wrong variable... sigh... it\n                        should be fixed now.\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.6    Allocated memory for some structures by myself\n                        instead of allocating them on the stack. Hope\n                        to have fixed the strange guru''s.\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.5ß   Special version for Muad''Dib with DEBUG info.\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.5    Tried to fix a bug that caused strange resets on\n                        Muad''Dibs machine (040) and weird crashes on my\n                        own system (total freeze and I had to turn my\n                        computer off for at least one minute!).\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.4    Fixed a bug that caused the bulletin only the be\n                        updated in the event of a new entry in the Top\n                        Ten. The Total Chat Time was never updated.\n                        *Reported by Quartz*\n\n   23-Dec-94   2.0.3    Built in the User file, now a record is kept of\n                        every user. A new bulletin perhaps.\n                        New tooltype: NO_USERFILE\n                        \n                        Altered the output after the chat again, now also\n                        contains info about the user him/herself.\n\n   22-Dec-94   2.0.2    Altered the output a bit and made some minor\n                        internal changes that caused a smaller executable.\n\n   21-Dec-94   2.0.1    Altered the output after a chat.\n\n   21-Dec-94   2.0.0    Totally rewrote it from scratch:\n                        - END_CHAT tooltype fixes the problem with lost\n                          carriers and then starting a chat again causing\n                          the start of the chat being the end of a chat.\n                          (Never mind if you don''t get it, it''s fixed!)\n                        *Requested by Quartz*\n\n                        New layout from Stezothetic implemented.\n                        *Thanks to Stezothetic*\n\n   26-Oct-93   1.2      Output file is now sysop-configurable. Now you can\n                        have it saved anywhere you like!\n                        Released: 30-Oct-93\n\n   23-Oct-93   1.1      Implemented safety-procedure so when user drops\n                        carrier in chat the Chat-O-Top doesn''t get mixed up.\n                        Released at: 27-Oct-93\n\n   17-Oct-93   1.0      First release to friends.\n\n                          Chat-O-Meter Convert Util\n\n   22-Jan-95   1.0.1    Fixed CoM-Convert; it now reads the name of the\n                        user from the correct place.\n\n   04-Jan-95   1.0.0    Completed CoM-Convert and restyled it.\n\n   03-Jan-95   1.0.0    Created CoM-Convert.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Greetings "Greetings and thanks - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            - --------------------.\n                           |  Greetings & Thanks  |\n                           `-------------------- -\n\n   A *BIG* THANKS TO STEZOTHETIC (Stz) FOR CREATING THE DESiGN, THANKS MATE!\n\n                                      :\n                                     -O-\n                                      :\n\n   Muad''Dib       -  Gheez,  using  custom  code to use linked lists is a\n                     bit unneccessary, but it sure shows that your skills\n                     are  improving.   Before  you know it you can finish\n                     KiLLER all by yourself :)\n\n   Wishbringer    -  Congratulations with passing your examinations.  Now\n                     it''s  time  for  the  REAL work at school or are you\n                     going  to  do  labour,  maybe working as a gardener?\n                     :-)\n   \n   Motive Power   -  Lost  your data line?  Last your data line?  How can\n                     you  be  a  good MYSTiC board then?  Naughty boy for\n                     not  telling me, I had to find out via xxxxx, afraid\n                     I  would kick you?  Well, you are right (not)!  It''s\n                     the  friendship that counts (together with megabytes\n                     :) ).\n   \n   I-GO           -  He  man,  let''s  puke  all  over this place!  Puking\n                     rules!   Let''s  inhale the odor and maybe we can get\n                     high  on  that,  woooow, sounds like the trip of our\n                     life times (and the last...  barf!).\n         \n   Exotic         -  My dear, dear friend. How is life treating you?\n   \n   Zed            -  Let  me  get this straight, you''ve made X-Caller for\n                     both XLK and AMi-X?  Why?\n   \n   Nocturn        -  Earth  calling  Nocturn,  Earth calling Nocturn.  If\n                     you  are  reading  this,  please  contact me.  Since\n                     you''ve  moved,  you seemed to have vanished from the\n                     Earth (did the gays get you in Philly?  :) ).\n                     \n   Empire         -  Why  work  for  the  money?   We  work for pleasure,\n                     pleasure  is  what  we  want out of life, so we work\n                     with  pleasure  (not).   Here''s  a  piece of advice:\n                     work to live, don''t live to work.\n   \n   bObO/MST       -  Ofcourse  you  send me 1000+ messages, they all just\n                     happened  to  be  re-routed to NIL:  ?  Oh well, I''m\n                     just  happy  your e-mail address works again.  Where\n                     the hell is File-ID 2.0 with colour support? :)\n   \n   Stezothetic    -  You''re  in the army now, wo oh oh, he''s in the army,\n                     nohow...  snief!\n   \n   UFOk           -  You  are the fastest trader I''ve ever seen!  And you\n                     don''t  trade  little  as well, almost 1 gigabyte per\n                     month.  Wouzers...  you rule for sure!\n\n   Radavi         -  You  don''t  give up easily, do you now?  You have to\n                     find EVERY tiny error I''ve made in my guide(s).  But\n                     now  I  am  convinced  that this file is free of any\n                     mistakes,  I''ve even corrected some that you did not\n                     find  (moeha!).   But  it''s all for the best, even a\n                     perfectionist  like  myself can make mistakes (did I\n                     tell you about my finest quality?  Modesty :) ).\n\n   QuartZ         -  You  thought  I  had  forgotten all about you didn''t\n                     you?   You  are thinking "why am I mentioned last in\n                     the  list?"  Getting paranoid?  No friends?  :) Nah,\n                     just teasing you my friend.\n   \n                      and to all my friends on Internet!\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Contact "How to get in touch with me - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             - ------------------.\n                            |  Contacting me...  |\n                            `------------------ - \n\n       For FAST response (preferred) please contact me on Internet:\n\n                               [email removed]\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                 I can be reached on the following boards:\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                The Undiscovered       +31-2294-1442 (USR 33k6)\n                                       +31-2294-4442 (USR 33k6)\n                                       +31-2294-tRY! (USR 33k6)\n\n                Last Outpost           +31-72-201182 (USR 33k6)\n                                       +31-72-157682 (USR 33k6)\n\n         I know, you have to call The Netherlands for it, but it''s\n            the only boards I call on a frequent basis, sorry.\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n        You can also find me on IRC once in a while on the channels\n                   #amigascne, #amiga, #rave and #megawatt\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Conversion "How to convert your old bulletin - © 1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                           - ---------------------.\n                          |  Bulletin Conversion  |\n                          `--------------------- -\n\n   For  sysops  that  used  Chat-O-Meter  1.x, there is a special program\n   called  "CoM-Convert"  that allows you to convert your old bulletin to\n   the  new  data  file  that  Chat-O-Meter  2.0  uses.  If you are not a\n   previous user of Chat-O-Meter, you can skip this part.\n\n   To  convert  it,  simply  start  the  program  "CoM-Convert"  from the\n   directory  where  you  have Chat-O-Meter 2.0 installed.  The data file\n   will be created in the directory where you started "CoM-Convert".\n\n   Once  you  have  started the program, a file requester will pop up and\n   you  must select the old bulletin.  Press `OK'' and you will get to see\n   the names (plus additional info) from that bulletin.\n\n   After that, the program will exit (`Ready.'') and you can find the data\n   file  called  `Chat-O-Meter.Data''  in that same directory.  Move it if\n   you  have  to.  The new bulletin will not be created yet!  To do this,\n   you  have  to  start Chat-O-Meter 2.0 from /X (start and end a chat is\n   sufficient).\n\n   That''s it, easy a as a fiddle.\n\n   NOTE : To use this tool, you must have req-tools installed.\n\n   NOTE¹: There  is *NO* check whether the file you feed to "CoM-Convert"\n          really  is an old bulletin from 1.x.  You can even cheat if you\n          want to ;-)\n\n   NOTE²: To  all beta-testers, this conversion util takes in account any\n          old  `Chat-O-Meter.Data'' file.  That is, it doesn''t recalculate\n          a new Top-Ten (it simply converts the old one), but it does ADD\n          the  old  `Total Chat Time''  to the new time and that also goes\n          for the total number of chats.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Troubles "Troubles with Chat-O-Meter? - © 1995 by KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            - ------------------.\n                           |  Trouble-Shooting  |\n                           `------------------ -\n\n   Q: Chat-O-Meter does not generate the bulletin, nor does it show\n      it on screen. What should I do?\n\n   A: Remove the "CHAT_END" tooltype from your CHAT_END icon.\n\n                               - -- ---- -- -\n\n   Q: Chat-O-Meter says it has got a daily record of 0 seconds? How\n      is that possible?\n\n   A: This can only happen the first day when a new record from a user\n      has been set, but there has not been a daily record yet. This\n      will be fixed by tomorrow, be patient :)\n\n                               - -- ---- -- -\n\n   Q: Why doesn''t Chat-O-Top show the total number of chats?\n   \n   A: Because there was no room, but if you contact me I might\n      consider doing a new version.\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),'{"STACK":"10000"}','auto-added',120747,6,0,NULL,NULL,'klr_chat','scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('klr_chsr','KLR-CHSR.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KLR-CHSR.LHA','Chat-O-Meter','XIM','Chat-O-Meter',NULL,NULL,'KLR',NULL,replace('Chat-O-Meter Exec & Source.\n','\n',char(10)),'Chat-O-Meter.GUIDE',replace('@DATABASE "Chat-O-Meter.GUiDE"\n@REMARK  .-------------------------------------------------------.\n@REMARK  | This document should be read with AmigaGuide (OS 2.x) |\n@REMARK  | or MultiView (OS 3.x) or any other guide reader. Tnx. |\n@REMARK  `-------------------------------------------------------''\n@REMARK  $VER: Chat-O-Meter 2.2.3\n@WIDTH 80\n@NODE MAIN "Chat-O-Meter - MYSTiC ''94/''95 - © ''94/''95 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            Guide to Chat-O-Meter\n                 for Ami-Express 3.38+ Bulletin Board Systems\n\n                            Program version: 2.2.3\n               Compilation Date/Time: Mon Jun 26 21:50:06 1995\n\n                        © 1994/1995 by @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n\n                   ... another fine MYSTiC /X production ...\n\n                       @{"       UPDATE iNFORMATiON        " link WhatsNew}\n\n      @{" Welcome Sysop! " link WhatIs}    What is Chat-O-Meter?\n      @{" Features       " link Features}    What can it do?\n      @{" Installation   " link Install}    How to install Chat-O-Meter\n      @{" Conversion     " link Conversion}    How to convert your old 1.x bulletin to 2.x\n\n      @{" Example 1      " link BullExample}    Example output the bulletin of Chat-O-Meter\n      @{" Example 2      " link ExitChatExample}    Example output when you exit a chat\n\n      @{" History        " link History}    History of Chat-O-Meter\n      @{" Greetings      " link Greetings}    Greetings, messages and contact info\n      @{" Troubles?      " link Troubles}    Trouble-shooting\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatIs "What is Chat-O-Meter - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                          - -----------------------.\n                         |  What Is Chat-O-Meter?  |\n                         `----------------------- -\n\n   It  started out as a tool to keep track of the time you spent chatting\n   with  your users and to be able to make a nice looking @{"bulletin" link BullExample} out of\n   that  information.   But  things  have  changed, not only does it keep\n   track  of  the  time  the sysop spends chatting, it also registers the\n   time every user ever spent chatting with you, the sysop, in a separate\n   data  file.   So  for every user there is information available on how\n   long  s/he  chatted with you and how many times s/he chatted with you.\n\n   Needless to say that it''s also possible to create a full ANSI bulletin\n   based upon the users'' data file.\n\n   At  the  end  of  every  chat  you  will  get  to see a banner holding\n   information  about  your  latest  chat  and  accumulative  info  about\n   previous  chats.   At  your  request,  it  can  also  show  the users''\n   accumulated information.\n\n   Together  with  Chat-O-Top  you  are  able  to  create a wide range of\n   bulletins  from  the  data  gathered while you chat.  Examples of such\n   bulletins are:\n\n   · Top 10 of the longest chats ever.\n   · Top xx of the users that have chatted the longest/sportest with you.\n   · Top xx of the users that have chatted the most/least with you.\n\n   How  will  this  affect  your  chats  you say?  Well, I think you will\n   hardly  notice  a difference since the creation and/or updating of the\n   data  files  is  done  in  a  most  efficient way with as less fuzz as\n   possible.   Creation  of  the  Top Ten Longest Chats only takes upto 1\n   second.\n\n                                ** NOTICE **\n\n   Chat-O-Meter  is  giftware,  this means that you can use it as much as\n   you  like,  where  you like, dressed as you like and how you like, but\n   YOU MAY NOT SELL IT IN ANY WAY!  Permission is granted for distribtion\n   on AmiNet.\n\n                                ** NOTICE **\n\n                                   Enjoy!\n\n                                                        @{"-KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatsNew "What''s New - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                           - --------------------.\n                          |  New in version 2.2  |\n                          `-------------------- -\n\n   Again,  I  have  optimized the executable and I am sure it can be even\n   more optimized, maybe in the future (and without startup-code).\n\n   I  also rewrote the routine that would keep track of your daily chats,\n   as a result it is much faster and more reliable.  Please note that the\n   first  time  you  use  this  version  and  a  new bulletin needs to be\n   generated  by  Chat-O-Meter (because of a new record) there might be a\n   record of 0 seconds and 0 chats.  This will only last for a day.\n\n   The  total  time  that  you''ve  used  chatting with your users used to\n   displayed  in  hours,  minutes and seconds (such as:  2254:33:15), but\n   because  it  lacked clearity on the long run, I''ve altered it a bit so\n   it is now displayed in days, hours and minutes (i.e. 93d 11h 23m).\n\n   And  also  new  in  the  version  is the arrival of Chat-O-Top, a neat\n   little  thingy  that  is  able  to  create  several  very  interesting\n   bulletins, among others:\n\n   * Bulletin of total chatting times with the users\n   * Bulletin of the number of chats with the users\n   * Bulletin of the average time per chat with the users\n\n   These  bulletins  can be sorted either from high to low or vice versa.\n   It  is able to handle custom designs and has some other handy options.\n\n   Please check out the guide to Chat-O-Top for more information.\n\n                           - --------------------.\n                          |  New in version 2.1  |\n                          `-------------------- -\n\n   Added  a  routine  to  keep  track of your daily chats.  At the end of\n   every day (that is, when you start to chat for the first time on a new\n   day)  it  will  calculate  your  total chatting time and if it''s a new\n   record it will be added at the bottom of the Chat-O-Meter bulletin.\n   See supplied @{ "example." link BullExample }\n\n   Also,  at the end of every chat, you will get to see the current score\n   of today''s chatting time.\n   See supplied @{ "example." link ExitChatExample }\n\n   And due to some optimising the executable has become smaller.\n\n                           - --------------------.\n                          |  New in version 2.0  |\n                          `-------------------- -\n\n   The  previous  version ( 1.2 ) was released over one year ago (October\n   1993)  and  to tell you the truth, the coding was lousy and it was not\n   that  sysop  friendly.   But  do  not  despair,  this version has been\n   totally rewritten (from scratch!) and is much smarter and has got some\n   nice new options.  Better forget all about the previous version (1.2).\n\n   It  has  to  be  said  that I would have never created this version if\n   Quartz  hadn''t  asked  for  it  AND if Stezothetic hadn''t made such an\n   awesome logo.  It seems people use my tools after all :-)\n\n   First  of  all,  the best improvement is that it is now able to detect\n   wether you started or ended a chat.  So if you were a previous user of\n   Chat-O-Meter,  the  times  of  deleting  the  old data file before you\n   started another chat are over :) Lost carriers are no problem anymore.\n\n   Another  *BIG* improvement is the @{"new layout" link BullExample}, done by Stezo (Stz).  It\n   now  has  got a really great logo and nice colours.  You''ll love it, I\n   do!  Many thanks to Stezothetic for creating the logo!\n\n   But  that''s not all, another exciting new feature is the creation of a\n   data  file  that  contains  data about chats and times from every user\n   individually  (but  stored  in one single file).  I will also create a\n   door/tool  that creates a nice bulletin out of that one.  If you think\n   this  is nonsense, you are able to turn it off so no data file will be\n   created.\n\n   The  previous  version also did not set the ENV:  variable of AMi-X to\n   the right value, so users that performed a ''who'' saw that the user you\n   were  chatting  with was ''using a door''.  This has been fixed.  But if\n   you  liked  that ''feature'' of Chat-O-Meter, it is still able to hide a\n   chat. Privacy above all (nope, there is no PGP function :) ).\n\n   That''s it.  I hope you''ll like it, it''s one of those nice little doors\n   that  makes  just  the  difference  between your board and your follow\n   sysops'' boards.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Features "Features of Chat-O-Meter - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                      - ------------------------------.\n                     |  Features of Chat-O-Meter 2.0  |\n                     `------------------------------ -\n\n   · Super  Location(tm),  place  the  executable wherever you want (ENV:\n     will do just fine ;) ).\n\n   · Incredible  Creation(tm),  the  Chat-O-Meter bulletin can be created\n     anywhere  you  like it, there is no discrimination of directories at\n     all!  A first in Ami-X land!  :)\n\n   · Ultra  Cool  Layout(tm), thanks to the Great Stezothetic a fantastic\n     layout accompanies this package.\n\n   · Top Security(tm), prevent users from detecting you are chatting with\n     someone (optionable, but comes free with the package as a bonus).\n\n   · Speed  Creation(tm), you have never seen such fast creation/updating\n     of the data files and the bulletin.\n\n   · Space  Saver(tm),  miniscule  files to store the data.  Now you have\n     more room for your public domain software :)\n   \n   · Big  Brother(tm), not only YOUR chats are recorded, a record is kept\n     per  user  for  ultimate  security and reference.  Now you can proof\n     with  whom  you  have  chatted.   A  wide  variety of reports can be\n     generated about this subject (Chat-O-Top).\n\n   · Banner  Baffler(tm), comes in the famous Ultra Cool Layout(tm) style\n     and  tells  you  all  about  your  latest  chats  together  with the\n     accumulated info from the user you''ve chatted with (so you can check\n     if  you''ve  chatted too much with him/here this month, remember your\n     quota!  :) ).\n     \n   · Artificial  Intelligence,  Chat-O-Meter is able to detect wether you\n     want  to  have  yourself asked the question "Show Bulletin [Y/n]" or\n     not when a user hits the Top Ten.\n\n   · Spelling Checker, optionable removal of plural ''s''s (no, this is not\n     a typo) from words like "mINS", "hOURS" and "sECS".\n\n   · Once installed, you will never have to do any maintenance!\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE ExitChatExample "Exit Chat Example - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n   Every time you exit a chat, you will get to see this small bulletin.\n\n                                 FULL SIZE\n :                                                                         :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :  /X MYSTiC       Chat-O-Meter v2.2  by  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     /X MYSTiC  :\n ¡                                                                         .\n :  tIME: 00:02:24 ^ cHAT #: 12404 ^ tODAY: 00:02:26 ^ oVERALL: 1199:04:34 ¡\n .            pRIVATE sCORE: 0000:07:42 ^ pRIVATE chAT #: 22               :\n .                                                                         |\n :   sORRY dUDE, nO Chat-O-Meter tOP tIME tODAY, tRY aGAIN sOON, oKAY?!    :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :                                                                         :\n\n                                 NO_USERFILE\n\n :                                                                         :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :  /X MYSTiC       Chat-O-Meter v2.2  by  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     /X MYSTiC  :\n ¡                                                                         .\n :  tIME: 00:02:24 ^ cHAT #: 12404 ^ tODAY: 00:02:26 ^ oVERALL: 1199:04:34 ¡\n .                                                                         |\n :   sORRY dUDE, nO Chat-O-Meter tOP tIME tODAY, tRY aGAIN sOON, oKAY?!    :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :                                                                         :\n\n                                NO_DAY_RECORD\n\n :                                                                         :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :  /X MYSTiC       Chat-O-Meter v2.2  by  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     /X MYSTiC  :\n ¡                                                                         .\n :    tHIS cHAT:  0:00:51 ^ oVERALL tIME: 1199:04:35 ^ cHAT #: 12406       ¡\n .            pRIVATE sCORE: 0000:07:42 ^ pRIVATE chAT #: 22               :\n .                                                                         |\n :   sORRY dUDE, nO Chat-O-Meter tOP tIME tODAY, tRY aGAIN sOON, oKAY?!    :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :                                                                         :\n\n                         NO_USERFILE & NO_DAY_RECORD\n\n :                                                                         :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :  /X MYSTiC       Chat-O-Meter v2.2  by  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     /X MYSTiC  :\n ¡                                                                         .\n :    tHIS cHAT:  0:00:51 ^ oVERALL tIME: 1199:04:35 ^ cHAT #: 12406       ¡\n .                                                                         |\n :   sORRY dUDE, nO Chat-O-Meter tOP tIME tODAY, tRY aGAIN sOON, oKAY?!    :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :                                                                         :\n\n                                   QUIET\n\n                                    :-)\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Install "Installation of Chat-O-Meter - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                      - -----------------------------.\n                     |  How to install Chat-O-Meter  |\n                     `----------------------------- -\n\n   For the illiterate sysops among us:  smack the executable anywhere you\n   like  but  it''s  better you put it in DOORS:Chat-O-Meter/ because then\n   you  will not have to mess with those nasty icons!  Drag those vicious\n   icons  to  your  SYSCmd  directory  as  quick as you can.  After that,\n   modify  your  STARTCHAT.TXT  and  ENDCHAT.TXT  so  that  CHAT_START is\n   started  when you start a chat and END_CHAT is started when your END a\n   chat.  Now, that wasn''t too hard was it?  :-)\n\n   And now, for those of you who like to read huge guides, here comes the\n   verbose version of the above alinea.\n\n   Step 1 - Place  the  executable any place on this earth.  The supplied\n            icons  have  been setup to find it in DOORS:Chat-O-Meter, but\n            if  you  don''t  mind editing the icons, place it anywhere you\n            like.   Please  be  aware of the fact that the Chat-O-Meter''s\n            data  files  will  be created in the same directory you place\n            it.\n\n            Note  to  other  programmers:  Haven''t you guys ever heard of\n            the  PROGDIR:   assign?   Come  on, hard coded paths in doors\n            suck big time, it lacks knowledge of the system.\n\n   Step 2 - Copy   the   two   supplied   icons,   "Chat_Start.info"  and\n            "Chat_End.info" to your SYSCmd directory.\n\n   Step 3 - For  Chat-O-Meter  to do its job, you will have to alter your\n            STARTCHAT.TXT  and  ENDCHAT.TXT  file(s).  These files can be\n            found  in  your  BBS:NODEx/  directory(-ies), as you probably\n            know (well, did ya?).\n            \n            Make  sure  you  call the "Chat_Start" command when you start\n            chatting and call the "End_Chat" command when exiting a chat.\n            You  can do this with MCI commands.  Consult your Ami-Express\n            manual on how to do this (nah nah :) ).\n\n   Step 4 - We''re  done  with  the  steps. For ultimate performance, make\n            sure  to  check  out  the tooltypes described below, they can\n            really make your day.\n            \n   Step 5 - Oops, sorry.\n\n   Chat_Start.info\n   ---------------\n   STACK=10000          ; Should be enough.\n\n   There  are  no more tooltypes for this icon.  Sorry, I promise to work\n   on it :)\n   \n   Chat_End.info\n   -------------\n   CHAT_END             : This  is how Chat-O-Meter recognizes the end of\n                          a chat. Make sure it is in the icon.\n\n   BULLETIN=            : Use  this  tooltype  to set the name (including\n                          path)  of  the  bulletin that Chat-O-Meter will\n                          create.\n                          (i.e. BULLETIN=BBS:Bulletins/Chat-O-Meter.TXT)\n\n   DONT_ASK             : By  default, whenever a user enters the Top Ten\n                          of  longest  chats  s/he  will be asked if s/he\n                          wants  to see the updated Top Ten.  If you have\n                          something  against  that, you can override this\n                          question  with  this  tooltype,  it will NOT be\n                          shown.\n\n   DONT_TRUNC           : By  default  all plural ''s''-es will be removed.\n                          So  for  example:  1 hOURS  becomes 1 hOUR  and\n                          1 sECS  becomes  1 sEC.  However,  if you think\n                          that  ruins  the  bulletin (cosmetically),  you\n                          can  override  it  by specifying this tooltype.\n\n   QUIET                : If you are one of those quiet types, and do NOT\n                          wish  to see that ugly banner after every chat,\n                          quickly  use this tooltype to make sure it will\n                          never happen (god forbid!) :-).\n\n   NO_USERFILE          : As from version 2.0.3 a file is created holding\n                          all  information  about your users'' chats.  But\n                          if  you  think  that  the  8 bytes per user are\n                          taking  up  way  to much of your precious drive\n                          space,  you  can stop the creation of this file\n                          with  this  tooltype. \n\n   NO_DAY_RECORD        : And  if  it  isn''t enough already, Chat-O-Meter\n                          creates  another  file  of  20 bytes (ack) that\n                          holds info about the time you''ve spent chatting\n                          today  with  users  and  it  also harbours your\n                          tODAY  rECORD.   Again,  for  you poor guys out\n                          there  with  too  little  drive  space, you can\n                          prevent the creation of this file by using this\n                          tooltype.  Goodluck!  :)\n\n   That are all tooltypes for this moment.  When you are done setting the\n   tooltypes  you  are ready with the installation of Chat-O-Meter.  Have\n   fun and chat your head off!  :-)\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE BullExample "Bulletin Example Output - Design by Stezothetic!"\n\n   If  your  viewer  is  just as lousy as mine (AmigaGuide) then you will\n   agree  with  me  that the bulletin looks just plain ugly!  But believe\n   me, in Ami-Express or a good viewer/editor it looks way better.\n\n   And  yes,  it  is  possible for one user to be in the Top Ten multiple\n   times.   Why?   Well obviously I don''t check for it :) But why don''t I\n   check  for  it?   Easy,  how would you feel when you''ve just chatted 4\n   hours  with  the  sysop and it''s not put in the Top Ten because you''ve\n   chatted for 5 hours a previous time?  Not happy, let me tell you that.\n\n   What?  You would like to decide that for me?  Oh.  Well, tell it to me\n   in  my  face  and  maybe, just maybe I will fix it.  Pfwah, I''m amazed\n   that  you are even reading this, 99% of sysops don''t read guides, lazy\n   bastards.\n\n .                          _  __           /        __  _      ·          .\n-O-_____   ___.___  ___________ //\___   / ·   ______\  .        \ _      -O-\n :/  __/__/   |  ¬\/   _ ¬\____\/____/  · /   /  __ ¬\ -:---------\/-----. :\n_/   |   ¬\_  _    \_  _   \_  \/  ¬\_ / / /_/   \/   \_| >cHAT·O·mETER< | :\n\_____     /__|     /__|    /___     //_/ / \_____     /`----------------'' ¦\n-:---l____/---l____/---l___//\-l____/-\_\/-------l____/------ ---  --   -  ¡_\n ¦                       __/  \  ___  ___________  /\_________    _______  \/\n | .------------------.  \_    \/  ¬\/  __ ¬\____\/____/  __ ¬\___\____ ¬\_¦\n ¡ :   ·/X mYSTIC!·   :  /     \/    \_ \____/__ \/  ¬\_  \____/__   \/  _/:\n :_`---------------- - _/       \_____/___     /__     /____     /___/    \_\n \/ -  -- --- ---------\_________/--Stz--\____/--l____/----\____/----\_____/-\n :                                                                         |\n !   o1. Veteran              03 hOURS, 42 mINS aND 42 sECS  (06-jAN-95)   :\n |   o2. Veteran              03 hOURS, 28 mINS aND 53 sECS  (29-dEC-93)   :\n O   o3. skate BOB            03 hOURS, 08 mINS aND 03 sECS  (25-sEP-94)   ¡\n |   o4. Monte Q              03 hOURS, 07 mINS aND 03 sECS  (11-dEC-93)   O\n ¦   o5. SKATE BOB            03 hOURS, 03 mINS aND 40 sECS  (22-dEC-93)   |\n :   o6. KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     02 hOURS, 52 mINS aND 01 sEC   (28-aPR-94)   ¦\n ¦   o7. skate BOB            02 hOURS, 50 mINS aND 55 sECS  (22-oCT-94)   :\n ¡   o8. Sir IQ               02 hOURS, 48 mINS aND 52 sECS  (28-oCT-93)   ·\n_:   o9. bULLOCK/jETSET       01 hOUR , 08 mINS aND 15 sECS  (05-jUN-95)   :_\n\:   1o. [MEGA^FORTRESS]      01 hOUR , 08 mINS aND 09 sECS  (20-jUN-95)   :/\n \                                                                         /\n :\        tOTAL # oF cHATS: 22866       tOTAL cHAT tIME: 2190:19:18      /:\n-O-\---------------------------------------------------------------------/-O-\n :  ·          dAY rECORD: 04:15:10 oN 23-jUN-95 ( 44 cHATS)            ·  :\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE History "History of Chat-O-Meter - Amazing... :)"\n                    - ---------------------------------.\n                   |  History: Something irreversible  |\n                   `-------------------------------- -\n\n                        Chat-O-Meter Ami-Express Door\n                             (more history below)\n\n   26-Jun-95   2.2.3    Chat-O-Meter  can  now also be used in the year\n                        2000 and up, inserted a simple check.\n\n   26-Jun-95   2.2.2    The  total  chatting  time  is now displayed in\n                        units of days, hours and minutes to enhance the\n                        clearity  of  the  time  spent chatting (entire\n                        days are wasted chatting :) ).\n\n                        Combined  some fprintf()''s so the executable is\n                        a few bytes smaller.\n\n   24-Jun-95   2.2.1    Finally  found  that  damn  bug  in  the  today\n                        routine.   How  the hell was I supposed to know\n                        that  time()  works from 1970 and not from 1978\n                        as  demanded  by Date2Amiga()?  Sigh.  But it''s\n                        fixed now, no more passing days at 18:00 hours.\n\n   22-Jun-95   2.2.0    Rewrote the ''today'' routine and replaced some\n                        of the fopen()-fread()-fwrite()''s with AmigaDOS\n                        function calls (Open, Read, Write) for speed.\n\n                        Rewrote  some  parts of the guide, my English\n                        has improved and I think it should show :)\n                        (Hi Radavi, better this way?)\n\n   07-Feb-95   2.1.1    Fixed the ''today''-routine. It could totally ruin\n                        your Chat-O-Meter.Data if things went wrong. The\n                        day-record is now stored in the .today file and\n                        no longer in the Chat-O-Meter.Data file (as it\n                        did in version 2.1.0).\n\n   06-Feb-95   2.1.0    Chat-O-Meter also keeps track of the total chat\n                        time per day. This can be turned off with:\n                        New tooltype: NO_DAY_RECORD\n\n                        Optimized /X link code. Smaller executable.\n\n   25-Dec-94   2.0.9    Chat-O-Meter can also run in silent mode now.\n                        New tooltype: QUIET\n\n   25-Dec-94   2.0.8    Merry X-Mas :) Made some internal changes, removed\n                        the stack-check (smaller executable).\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.7    Found the bug that caused the strange resets.\n                        I adjusted the wrong variable... sigh... it\n                        should be fixed now.\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.6    Allocated memory for some structures by myself\n                        instead of allocating them on the stack. Hope\n                        to have fixed the strange guru''s.\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.5ß   Special version for Muad''Dib with DEBUG info.\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.5    Tried to fix a bug that caused strange resets on\n                        Muad''Dibs machine (040) and weird crashes on my\n                        own system (total freeze and I had to turn my\n                        computer off for at least one minute!).\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.4    Fixed a bug that caused the bulletin only the be\n                        updated in the event of a new entry in the Top\n                        Ten. The Total Chat Time was never updated.\n                        *Reported by Quartz*\n\n   23-Dec-94   2.0.3    Built in the User file, now a record is kept of\n                        every user. A new bulletin perhaps.\n                        New tooltype: NO_USERFILE\n                        \n                        Altered the output after the chat again, now also\n                        contains info about the user him/herself.\n\n   22-Dec-94   2.0.2    Altered the output a bit and made some minor\n                        internal changes that caused a smaller executable.\n\n   21-Dec-94   2.0.1    Altered the output after a chat.\n\n   21-Dec-94   2.0.0    Totally rewrote it from scratch:\n                        - END_CHAT tooltype fixes the problem with lost\n                          carriers and then starting a chat again causing\n                          the start of the chat being the end of a chat.\n                          (Never mind if you don''t get it, it''s fixed!)\n                        *Requested by Quartz*\n\n                        New layout from Stezothetic implemented.\n                        *Thanks to Stezothetic*\n\n   26-Oct-93   1.2      Output file is now sysop-configurable. Now you can\n                        have it saved anywhere you like!\n                        Released: 30-Oct-93\n\n   23-Oct-93   1.1      Implemented safety-procedure so when user drops\n                        carrier in chat the Chat-O-Top doesn''t get mixed up.\n                        Released at: 27-Oct-93\n\n   17-Oct-93   1.0      First release to friends.\n\n                          Chat-O-Meter Convert Util\n\n   22-Jan-95   1.0.1    Fixed CoM-Convert; it now reads the name of the\n                        user from the correct place.\n\n   04-Jan-95   1.0.0    Completed CoM-Convert and restyled it.\n\n   03-Jan-95   1.0.0    Created CoM-Convert.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Greetings "Greetings and thanks - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            - --------------------.\n                           |  Greetings & Thanks  |\n                           `-------------------- -\n\n   A *BIG* THANKS TO STEZOTHETIC (Stz) FOR CREATING THE DESiGN, THANKS MATE!\n\n                                      :\n                                     -O-\n                                      :\n\n   Muad''Dib       -  Gheez,  using  custom  code to use linked lists is a\n                     bit unneccessary, but it sure shows that your skills\n                     are  improving.   Before  you know it you can finish\n                     KiLLER all by yourself :)\n\n   Wishbringer    -  Congratulations with passing your examinations.  Now\n                     it''s  time  for  the  REAL work at school or are you\n                     going  to  do  labour,  maybe working as a gardener?\n                     :-)\n   \n   Motive Power   -  Lost  your data line?  Last your data line?  How can\n                     you  be  a  good MYSTiC board then?  Naughty boy for\n                     not  telling me, I had to find out via xxxxx, afraid\n                     I  would kick you?  Well, you are right (not)!  It''s\n                     the  friendship that counts (together with megabytes\n                     :) ).\n   \n   I-GO           -  He  man,  let''s  puke  all  over this place!  Puking\n                     rules!   Let''s  inhale the odor and maybe we can get\n                     high  on  that,  woooow, sounds like the trip of our\n                     life times (and the last...  barf!).\n         \n   Exotic         -  My dear, dear friend. How is life treating you?\n   \n   Zed            -  Let  me  get this straight, you''ve made X-Caller for\n                     both XLK and AMi-X?  Why?\n   \n   Nocturn        -  Earth  calling  Nocturn,  Earth calling Nocturn.  If\n                     you  are  reading  this,  please  contact me.  Since\n                     you''ve  moved,  you seemed to have vanished from the\n                     Earth (did the gays get you in Philly?  :) ).\n                     \n   Empire         -  Why  work  for  the  money?   We  work for pleasure,\n                     pleasure  is  what  we  want out of life, so we work\n                     with  pleasure  (not).   Here''s  a  piece of advice:\n                     work to live, don''t live to work.\n   \n   bObO/MST       -  Ofcourse  you  send me 1000+ messages, they all just\n                     happened  to  be  re-routed to NIL:  ?  Oh well, I''m\n                     just  happy  your e-mail address works again.  Where\n                     the hell is File-ID 2.0 with colour support? :)\n   \n   Stezothetic    -  You''re  in the army now, wo oh oh, he''s in the army,\n                     nohow...  snief!\n   \n   UFOk           -  You  are the fastest trader I''ve ever seen!  And you\n                     don''t  trade  little  as well, almost 1 gigabyte per\n                     month.  Wouzers...  you rule for sure!\n\n   Radavi         -  You  don''t  give up easily, do you now?  You have to\n                     find EVERY tiny error I''ve made in my guide(s).  But\n                     now  I  am  convinced  that this file is free of any\n                     mistakes,  I''ve even corrected some that you did not\n                     find  (moeha!).   But  it''s all for the best, even a\n                     perfectionist  like  myself can make mistakes (did I\n                     tell you about my finest quality?  Modesty :) ).\n\n   QuartZ         -  You  thought  I  had  forgotten all about you didn''t\n                     you?   You  are thinking "why am I mentioned last in\n                     the  list?"  Getting paranoid?  No friends?  :) Nah,\n                     just teasing you my friend.\n   \n                      and to all my friends on Internet!\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Contact "How to get in touch with me - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             - ------------------.\n                            |  Contacting me...  |\n                            `------------------ - \n\n       For FAST response (preferred) please contact me on Internet:\n\n                               [email removed]\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                 I can be reached on the following boards:\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                The Undiscovered       +31-2294-1442 (USR 33k6)\n                                       +31-2294-4442 (USR 33k6)\n                                       +31-2294-tRY! (USR 33k6)\n\n                Last Outpost           +31-72-201182 (USR 33k6)\n                                       +31-72-157682 (USR 33k6)\n\n         I know, you have to call The Netherlands for it, but it''s\n            the only boards I call on a frequent basis, sorry.\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n        You can also find me on IRC once in a while on the channels\n                   #amigascne, #amiga, #rave and #megawatt\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Conversion "How to convert your old bulletin - © 1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                           - ---------------------.\n                          |  Bulletin Conversion  |\n                          `--------------------- -\n\n   For  sysops  that  used  Chat-O-Meter  1.x, there is a special program\n   called  "CoM-Convert"  that allows you to convert your old bulletin to\n   the  new  data  file  that  Chat-O-Meter  2.0  uses.  If you are not a\n   previous user of Chat-O-Meter, you can skip this part.\n\n   To  convert  it,  simply  start  the  program  "CoM-Convert"  from the\n   directory  where  you  have Chat-O-Meter 2.0 installed.  The data file\n   will be created in the directory where you started "CoM-Convert".\n\n   Once  you  have  started the program, a file requester will pop up and\n   you  must select the old bulletin.  Press `OK'' and you will get to see\n   the names (plus additional info) from that bulletin.\n\n   After that, the program will exit (`Ready.'') and you can find the data\n   file  called  `Chat-O-Meter.Data''  in that same directory.  Move it if\n   you  have  to.  The new bulletin will not be created yet!  To do this,\n   you  have  to  start Chat-O-Meter 2.0 from /X (start and end a chat is\n   sufficient).\n\n   That''s it, easy a as a fiddle.\n\n   NOTE : To use this tool, you must have req-tools installed.\n\n   NOTE¹: There  is *NO* check whether the file you feed to "CoM-Convert"\n          really  is an old bulletin from 1.x.  You can even cheat if you\n          want to ;-)\n\n   NOTE²: To  all beta-testers, this conversion util takes in account any\n          old  `Chat-O-Meter.Data'' file.  That is, it doesn''t recalculate\n          a new Top-Ten (it simply converts the old one), but it does ADD\n          the  old  `Total Chat Time''  to the new time and that also goes\n          for the total number of chats.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Troubles "Troubles with Chat-O-Meter? - © 1995 by KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            - ------------------.\n                           |  Trouble-Shooting  |\n                           `------------------ -\n\n   Q: Chat-O-Meter does not generate the bulletin, nor does it show\n      it on screen. What should I do?\n\n   A: Remove the "CHAT_END" tooltype from your CHAT_END icon.\n\n                               - -- ---- -- -\n\n   Q: Chat-O-Meter says it has got a daily record of 0 seconds? How\n      is that possible?\n\n   A: This can only happen the first day when a new record from a user\n      has been set, but there has not been a daily record yet. This\n      will be fixed by tomorrow, be patient :)\n\n                               - -- ---- -- -\n\n   Q: Why doesn''t Chat-O-Top show the total number of chats?\n   \n   A: Because there was no room, but if you contact me I might\n      consider doing a new version.\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),'{"STACK":"10000"}','auto-added',115018,3,0,NULL,NULL,'klr_chsr','scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('klr_co12','KLR-CO12.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KLR-CO12.LHA','KC-Config','XIM','KC-Config',NULL,NULL,'KLR','Amiexpress Door',replace('Best Comment door around with cursor-control\nAmiexpress Door\n','\n',char(10)),'KiLLER-Comment.GUiDE',replace('@DATABASE "KiLLER-Comment.GUiDE"\n@REMARK	.-------------------------------------------------------.\n@REMARK  | This document should be read with AmigaGuide (OS 2.x) |\n@REMARK  | or MultiView (OS 3.x) or any other guide reader. Tnx. |\n@REMARK  `-------------------------------------------------------''\n@REMARK	$VER: KiLLER Comment 1.2.0\n@WIDTH 80\n@NODE MAIN "KiLLER Comment - MYSTiC 1994/1995 - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            Guide to KiLLER Comment\n                     for Ami-Express Bulletin Board Systems\n\n                             Program version: 1.2.0\n                Compilation Date/Time: Thu Feb  2 15:23:39 1995\n\n                         © 1994/1995 by @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n\n                   ... another fine MYSTiC /X production ...\n\n      @{" First words... " link WhatIs}    What/Why is KiLLER Comment?\n      @{" Features       " link Features}    What can it do?\n      @{" Installation   " link Install}    How to install KiLLER Comment on your BBS\n      @{" Usage /X       " link AmiUsage}    How things go with Ami-Express\n      @{" Usage Config   " link Config}    How to use the Config GUI\n      @{" History        " link History}    History of KiLLER Comment\n      @{" History Config " link HistoryGUI}    History of KiLLER Config\n      @{" Greetings      " link Greetings}    Greetings, messages and contact info\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatIs "What/Why is KiLLER Comment? - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                         .---------------------------.\n                         |  What is KiLLER Comment?  |\n                         `---------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER Comment is number 1001 in the row of a [C]omment doors.  But this\n   is not just *a* comment door, otherwise I wouldn''t have made it.  I have\n   some profound reasons.\n\n   Despite  the  fact  that  there  must be over a 1000 different [C]omment\n   doors,  there  is not one comment door that suits my needs.  I have seen\n   plenty of them but to me they all look ugly, have static interfaces, are\n   as flexible as a log of wood and generally suck :-).\n\n   With  this mind I''ve tried to make KiLLER Comment as smart and pretty as\n   I  can  and  I  feel I have succeeded.  I have never seen a comment door\n   that  works  as  simple  as  this  one  with  a fair amount of so-called\n   ''intelligence'' and comes with a nice GUI.\n\n   What  is  this  ''intelligence'' I speak about you say?  Well, all comment\n   doors  sofar  weren''t  able  to see that if I typed "c kill" I wanted to\n   comment to "KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" nor where they able to see that if I typed\n   "c  amiga" I wanted to comment to the Amiga co-sysop.  KiLLER Comment is\n   able to detect this!\n\n   Another  thing  is  security.   Every  new  user  was  able to see which\n   cosysops  you  had,  but  by giving every cosysop a security level, they\n   will  not see their names/jobs.  And if there is only one (co)sysop they\n   are  allowed  to  see,  they  will  not  even  see it is done via KiLLER\n   Comment.\n\n   Also,  per user in the [C]omment list there is a check wether he/she has\n   got access to the conference that KiLLER [C]omment is called from, so it\n   is impossible to write a person to someone in the wrong conference.\n\n   What I personally also dislike are comment doors that only fill like 25%\n   of  your  screen.   Not  that  you  should put a lot of nonsense in your\n   comment  door,  no,  but  KiLLER  Comment puts an extra line between the\n   names  of  the  (co-)sysops  if  that''s possible with the user''s maximum\n   numbers  of  lines  he  can  handle  on his screen.  It''s these kinds of\n   things I miss in those 13-in-a-dozen [C]omment doors.  That''s why I made\n   KiLLER Comment.\n\n   And  oh, just like I first introduced cursor control in ConfScan, KiLLER\n   comment also uses cursor control for selecting a user you can write to.\n\n   Anyway,  I  hope  you  will  enjoy KiLLER Comment as much as I will.  Go\n   check out the features if you want to know more.\n\n                                                          -KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Features "Features of KiLLER Comment - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                        .------------------------------.\n                        |  Features of KiLLER Comment  |\n                        `------------------------------''\n\n   Although  the  list  of  features isn''t big, it''s enough for a [C]omment\n   door.  Remember, it''s only a [C]omment door.\n\n   » GUI Config\n\n   A small program comes with it to set it up the way you want it as easy\n   as possible.  It''s just click and save, you probably won''t even need the\n   keyboard but to fill in some personal info.\n\n   » Security\n\n   Users  with  low  access will not see all cosysops in the [C]omment list\n   (esspecially useful with new users).\n\n   » Known as\n\n   Very often your cosysops will have names that contain some sort of group\n   name  in  it  (like  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC), but it''s not always that nice or\n   handy  to  have  that group name in the comment door (be it for cosmetic\n   reasons,  be  it  for  some  other reason).  That''s why you can enter an\n   alias  for  each of your cosysops (and for yourself ofcourse) which will\n   be used to display in the comment list.\n\n   » Info\n\n   For  every (co)sysop you can enter one line of information regarding why\n   he  is in the comment list.  This can be something like "Amiga Co-Sysop"\n   or "Takes care of users / files / lamers".\n\n   » Comments\n\n   Most  comment doors offer you the possibility to display a message after\n   the  user has selected someone to write to.  This is generally something\n   like  "<name>  says:   bla bla".  With KiLLER Comment you can make lines\n   like  this:   "And  there  goes another message to <name>" or "<name> is\n   cheering of joy!".\n   \n   An example file with fortune cookies has been supplied (Comments.ASCII).\n\n   » Cursor Control / Numeric entries / Default sysop\n\n   Selecting  a  user  can  be done with the cursor keys or by entering the\n   corresponding number.  A default sysop is also available (space).\n\n   » Comment Statistics\n\n   KiLLER  Comment  keeps record of the number of messages written to every\n   user  and  also knows when every user received his last message plus the\n   user  who  wrote  it to him.  So with this information it is possible to\n   create  some  nice  looking  statistics.  These statistics can be viewed\n   within /X or with the Config program.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Install "How to install KiLLER Comment - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                      .----------------------------------.\n                      |  Installation of KiLLER Comment  |\n                      `----------------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Comment  can  be installed like any /X 3.x command, but please\n   take notice of these tooltypes:\n\n   "KC-Config.info" - The icon of the GUI config program\n   \n   · ACP_LOCATION=<path>      - Location of your ACP.info file.\n                                (eg. ACP_LOCATION=SYS:WBStartUp)\n\n   · KC_LOCATION=<path>       - Location of the /X version\n                                (eg. KC_LOCATION=DOORS:K-Comment)\n                                ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE **\n                                 THIS SHOULD BE THE PATH TO THE DIRECTORY\n                                 AND SHOULD _NOT_ INCLUDE THE NAME OF THE\n                                 EXECUTABLE! DON''T FORGET THIS! (HI JAKE)\n                                ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE **\n\n   · KC_VIEWER=<full name>    - External viewer for the Commentistics\n                                (eg. KC_VIEWER=C:PPMORE)\n\n   · PUB_SCREEN=<name>        - Optional(!) name of public screen that the\n                                config program will open on instead of the\n                                Workbench.\n                                (eg. PUB_SCREEN=ECS_PUB)\n\n   "C.info"          - The icon in your BBS command directory\n\n   · ACS_STATS=<level>        - The minimum level required to view the\n                                statiscal mumbo jumbo that KiLLER Comment\n                                is able to create for you (with ''C ?'').\n                                (eg. ACS_STATS=50)\n\n   · BBS_LOCATION=<path>      - This should be the path to your BBS:, or\n                                if you keep your USER.DATA located some-\n                                where else, the path to your USER.DATA.\n\n   · SEPARATOR_LINES          - An empty line is by default put  between\n                                two sysops if possible, but if you don''t\n                                like  that,  you can also have a line of\n                                ''-''s put between them.  To do so, simply\n                                this tooltype.\n\n   I  have  found  it  very  useful  to  put the Config program in my ACP\n   command list.  Maybe this can be useful for you too.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE AmiUsage "Usage in Ami-Express - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                          .------------------------.\n                          |  Usage in Ami-Express  |\n                          `------------------------''\n\n                          ^-^  Selecting a user  ^-^\n\n   Once  installed, the usage of the [C]omment door is (and should be) real\n   easy.  You can simply start it with ''C'' and you will be presented with a\n   menu from which you can select a user to send some mail to.\n\n   However,  like I said in the first words, KiLLER comment is a bit smart.\n   If  you enter a parameter after the ''C'', you can skip the menu and still\n   select  the  person you want to write to.  For example, suppose you want\n   to write a message to "Muad''Dib".  We all know that this is a very lousy\n   name  to spell, so we can suffice by entering "C Muad" and Muad''Dib will\n   be selected.\n\n   However,  if  you''ve  got  another  cosysop  in  the  list that is named\n   Muad''Dab,  KiLLER  Comment  will  take  the first name that has the part\n   "muad"  in it.  This is just the way it goes, so if you want to write to\n   Muad''Dab  instead  of  Muad''Dib  you  have  to enter another string that\n   uniquely identifies Muad''Dab, for instance:  "C Dab" :)\n\n   Another  way to select a user is entering his or her appropriate number.\n   If  you  know  that Muad''Dib is number 2 in the list, you can suffice by\n   entering  "C 2".   And  if  you  know that Muad''Dab is number 5, you can\n   enter  "C 5"  to  write  to Muad''Dab.  Real easy.  If you enter a number\n   that does not exist, you will be presented with the menu again.\n\n   But  that''s not all, let''s say you got separate sysops (or cosysops) for\n   different  conferences;  Amiga,  SNES  and  PC.  And suppose you want to\n   write  to  the Amiga cosysop, you can simply enter "C Amiga" or "C Ami".\n   Make  sure  the  length  of  the  string  after  the  "C"  is at least 3\n   characters,  otherwise  it will be marked as ambigious and the menu will\n   be  prompted.   And  yes,  that means that "C PC" will NOT work (haha!).\n   But  hey,  there  are  3  other  ways of selecting that sysop.  So don''t\n   despair (or enter "C p PC" if the text is "Cosysop PC").\n\n                           ^-^  Commentistics  ^-^\n\n   The  commentistics  can be viewed by entering a question mark behind the\n   "C".  Couldn''t be more simple.  Just enter "C ?" and you will be able to\n   view  all  sorts  of  statistic mumbo jumbo on your screen.  Once you''ve\n   seen the first screen, you can go to the next screen by pressing any key\n   BUT Q, ESC can CTRL-C.  Than the screen will be cleared in a fashionable\n   style and the last page will be shown, containing the times and names of\n   users who wrote last to the users in the Comment list.\n\n                           ^-^  Syntax Summary  ^-^\n\n   For all you folks that didn''t bother to read the upper texts, here''s the\n   brief list of all commands:\n\n   C        - A menu will be shown from which you can select a user\n\n   C <text> - The  <text> will be compared to the names of the users in the\n              list  and  if  that fails, it will be compared to the info of\n              each user in the list. This comparing is case insensitive.\n\n   C <#>    - The corresponding name with the number will be selected.\n\n   C ?      - The commentistics will be shown.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Config "The Config Editor - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             .-----------------.\n                             |  Config Editor  |\n                             `-----------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Comment  comes  with  a  full C= compliant Guided User Interface\n   (commonly  known  as  GUI).  With this Configuration Editor you are able\n   to setup KiLLER Comment the way YOU want.\n\n   When  you  start  the  Config  Editor for the first time, the sysop will\n   automatically  added as the first entry in the list of users that can be\n   commented  to.   There  should  at  least  be  ONE  entry in the list or\n   otherwise the user can''t comment to anybody :-)\n\n   Okay,  I  think it is best if I just tell you what every button does and\n   how to do things.  Here we go...\n\n   » Known As\n\n   You  can  enter an alias for every user that is in the list.  By default\n   the  alias  is  the  same  as  their real name in /X but you are free to\n   change  it.  KiLLER Comment will still know their real name, don''t worry\n   about that.  This function can be handy if you got a cosysop with a name\n   like [-Bad Boy^Lamers-R-Us^I-Suck-] but you don''t want to have that damn\n   ugly handle in your comment door.  Fill in whatever you like.\n\n   » Info\n\n   As  usual  you are able to enter one line of text that somehow describes\n   why  this  user  is put in the list of ''commenteers'' :) You can put info\n   like "Amiga Cosysop" in here or "Nuking is my nick-name!".  You are free\n   to  use  any colours in it, just enter them in the gadget like you would\n   do  in  a  text  file.   If pressing the ESC removes the cursor from the\n   gadget,  you  need to TURN OF THE TEXT-FILTER OPTION IN YOUR PREFERENCES\n   FROM YOUR AMIGA (Prefs/IControl)!!\n\n   Although  you  can only have like 34 chars on screen for info about that\n   user,  the  Config  Editor  takes 80 chars.  You are responsible for the\n   length  of  the  text.   If you put 80 chars of text in it, it will look\n   like a mess on screen in Ami-Express.\n\n   » Comments\n\n   A  comment is a line of text that is shown to the user who just selected\n   somebody.   This  can  be  a  message  like  "Muad''Dib says:  Thanks for\n   nothing!"  or  "You can hear Muad''Dib shout with joy.".  Or you can show\n   him fortune cookie (max.  1 line!).  An example file has been included.\n\n   This gadget wants a full path plus filename to a file that contains such\n   messages.   This can be a different file for every user or the same file\n   for all users.  The lines in the text file can contain anything you like\n   where you can use ''%s'' to represent the name of the choosen user.\n\n   Examples:   %s says: Thanks for your mail bud.\n               You can hear %s scream with joy.\n               %s is overwhelmed with tears by your gesture.\n               Who is General Failure and why is he reading my hard drive?\n\n   Be creative!\n\n   With  the  [S]  button  you  can select a file via a file-requester than\n   rather typing in its full name.\n\n   » Req. Acs\n\n   For  security  reasons,  you  are able to set a minimum access level for\n   every  user in the comment list.  This way you are able to have multiple\n   cosysops and not let all the users know what they all do.\n\n   Imagine  you''ve got a cosysop for the Amiga section, SNES section and PC\n   section.   You''ve  got  a lot of Amiga users, but you don''t want them to\n   know  that  you  got  a  PC  section.   If  they would use the ''C'' door,\n   normally  they  would  see  that  you  got a cosysop for the PC section.\n   "What  PC  section?"  they will think.  Or even worse, new users who use\n   the door can easily spot your conferences.  But not with KiLLER Comment,\n   just  make  sure that the Amiga users have a lower access level than the\n   PC users.\n\n   It''s  not  perfect yet, but it''s a start.  I am thinking of a better way\n   to secure this (it''s possible, I just have to work it out a bit more).\n\n   By  the  way,  cosysops  with  "C"  access  will  also NOT see the other\n   cosysop(s) (not even in the Commentistics).\n\n   » Commentistics\n\n   Not  only in Ami-Express, but also in the Config Editor, you are able to\n   look  up  the statistics that KiLLER Comment can create.  The statistics\n   are  put  into  a  file  and that file is shown to you with the external\n   viewer  you specified.  For your information, that file is always called\n   T:KC_Commentistics,  does  NOT  contain  any  ANSI  codes and is removed\n   afterwards.\n\n   » Add / Del\n\n   Adding  a  user  to  the  list is quite easy, select Add and you will be\n   presented  with  a  list  of all your users in your USER.DATA.  This may\n   take  a  while,  depending on your type (read:  speed) of computer, hard\n   drive  and number of users in your USER.DATA.  A new user will always be\n   inserted at the END of the list.\n\n   To  remove  a user from the list, make it the active user (its name will\n   be  shown  below the listview) and press Del.  Easy as peanuts.  The new\n   current user will be the last user in the list.\n\n   You can not remove all users from the list.  A protection has been built\n   in  so please try to do so :) It''s impossible to remove them all because\n   to who should the users in your system comment to?\n\n   » Up / Down\n\n   With  those  gadgets  you  are  able to determine the order in which the\n   users  will  appear a) in the list and b) on screen in Ami-Express.  The\n   most upper user will be the default sysop!\n\n   To  move  a user, make it the current one by selecting it and then press\n   either up or down (repeatively if neccessary).\n\n   » Save\n\n   Guess what?\n\n   » &\n   \n   Sorry? What did you say? :)\n\n   » Quit\n\n   You  guessed  it.   By  the  way,  there  is  NO  warning if you changed\n   something and did not save it before quitting.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Greetings "Greetings go to... - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            .-------------------.\n                            |  Greetings to...  |\n                            `-------------------''\n\n                            In no specific order:\n\n            Muad''Dib/DLT ^ Motive Power/MST ^ QuartZ/MST ^ Jabbah \n\n            Exotic ^ UFOk/MST ^ Easy Rider/MST ^ I-GO/MW ^ Galaxy\n\n        bObO/MST ^ Famous''O''Hara ^ Zed ^ Green Beret/MST ^ Arcade/MST\n\n                     ... and to all my other friends ...\n\n\n   If you want to contact me, read @{"this!" link Contact}\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Contact "How to get in touch with me - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             - ------------------.\n                            |  Contacting me...  |\n                            `------------------ - \n\n       For FAST response (preferred) please contact me on Internet:\n\n                               [email removed]\n\n                    ( [email removed] -- backup )\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                 I can be reached on the following boards:\n\n                The Undiscovered       +31-2294-1442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-4442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-tRY! (USR 28k8)\n\n                Last Outpost           +31-72-201182 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-72-157682 (USR 28k8)\n\n         I know, you have to call The Netherlands for it, but it''s\n            the only boards I call on a frequent basis, sorry.\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n        You can also find me on IRC once in a while on the channels\n                     #amigascne, #amiga and #megawatt\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE History "History of KiLLER Comment - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n   1.2.0    03-02-95    Fixed the version numbers in the executable ;)\n                        REPORTED BY: MOTiVE POWER\n                        \n                        You can now enter an asterisk behind the name of\n                        the person you want to write to (ie. C MUAD*).\n                        REQUESTED BY: MUAD''DiB\n                        \n                        Made the percentage bar in the commentistics a\n                        relative one, like in KiLLER-Baud. The user with\n                        the most messages will always have 100% and the\n                        rest will be a relative representation to that\n                        user.\n\n   1.1.0    26-12-94    KiLLER [C]omment now checks wether the persons in\n                        the [C]omment list have access to the current conf\n                        that KC is called from. If not, that person will\n                        not be put in the list.\n                        REQUESTED BY: QuartZ\n\n   1.0.5    02-11-94    You can now have lines used as a separator on screen\n                        instead of a blank line.\n                        New tooltype: SEPARATOR_LINES\n                        REQUESTED BY: JAKE\n\n   1.0.4    20-10-94    Added an access level check. If a user does not have\n                        the minimal required access to actually see a cosysop\n                        in the Comment list, than that user will not see\n                        that cosysop. See also @{"History Gui" link HistoryGUI}.\n                        REQUESTED BY: TYCOON/MYSTiC & MUAD''DiB\n\n            20-10-94    The program now attempts to write messages with the\n                        external "E" door (if any). This makes it more\n                        flexible for sysop, allowing them to have external\n                        "E" doors installed, like the one from 5D and the\n                        external one from Tycoon/MYSTiC (/X-Fido).\n                        REQUESTED BY: TYCOON/MYSTiC\n\n   1.0.3    15-10-94    A bug in the "Commentistics" routine caused a guru\n                        when there were users with no messages. A division\n                        by zero was the result -> guru :( Fixed that.\n\n   1.0.0    14-10-94    First initial release to friends.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE HistoryGUI "History of KiLLER Config Editor - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n   1.0.3    14-02-95    Fixed a bug that caused all users with a name\n                        shorter than 4 characters to be left out of the\n                        user-list when you wanted to add a user.\n                        REPORTED BY: PHANTOM/OVERLORDS\n\n   1.0.2    20-10-94    Restyled the GUI bit. I removed the "Show Comments"\n                        gadget because I found it not useful. Removed the\n                        code also (ofcoz, but you never know who reads\n                        this :) ).\n\n            20-10-94    New option: [ Req. Acs ] (Required Access)\n                        You can set a minimum access level per user in the\n                        list. Users with an access level lower than that\n                        level will NOT see him in the [C]omment door.\n                        This enables you to let even new users use this\n                        door and not reveal to them that you (for instance)\n                        have an Amiga cosysop and/or SNES cosysop and/or\n                        PC cosysop.\n                        REQUESTED BY: TYCOON/MYSTIC & MUAD''DIB/DLT\n\n            20-10-94    New option: [ Clear Statistical Data ]\n                        With this option you can clear all statistical data\n                        that is kept for every user. This means that the\n                        total # of msg-s, last msg date and user will be set\n                        to 0 (or None).\n\n            20-10-94    Another restyle: [ Save ][ & ][ Quit ]\n                        Save -> Saves the (new) prefs\n                        &    -> Save and Quit\n                        Quit -> Quit the program\n\n            20-10-94    Another restyle: I changed the placed of the [ Add ]\n                        [ Del ] [ Up ] [ Down ] a bit, to make it a bit more\n                        logical.\n\n   1.0.1    14-10-94    First bug found in 3 secs by Muad''Dib :) If you\n                        selected "Commentistics" from the GUI Config and\n                        there were no messages written yet, a crash was\n                        the result (division by zero). Fixed it.\n\n   1.0.0    14-10-94    First initial release to friends.\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),'{"LOCATION":"<path>"}','auto-added',56625,2,0,NULL,NULL,'klr_co12','scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('klr_kbau','KLR-KBAU.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KLR-KBAU.LHA','Baud','XIM','Baud',NULL,'KLR','KLR','kILLER-bAUD eXEC & sOURCE bY KLR --------------------------------',replace('--------------------------------\nkILLER-bAUD eXEC & sOURCE bY KLR\n--------------------------------\n','\n',char(10)),'KiLLER_Baud.Guide',replace('@DATABASE "KiLLER-Comment.GUiDE"\n@REMARK	.-------------------------------------------------------.\n@REMARK  | This document should be read with AmigaGuide (OS 2.x) |\n@REMARK  | or MultiView (OS 3.x) or any other guide reader. Tnx. |\n@REMARK  `-------------------------------------------------------''\n@REMARK	$VER: KiLLER Baud 1.5.0\n@WIDTH 80\n@NODE MAIN "KiLLER Comment - MYSTiC 1994 - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             Guide to KiLLER Baud\n                 for Ami-Express 3.38+ Bulletin Board Systems\n\n                             Program version: 1.5.0\n                Compilation Date/Time: Thu Jun  1 15:45:51 1995\n\n                         © 1994/1995 by @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n\n                   ... another fine MYSTiC /X production ...\n\n                        @{"     UPDATE INFORMATION     " link Update}\n\n      @{" First words... " link WhatIs}    What is KiLLER Baud\n      @{" Features       " link Features}    What can it do?\n      @{" Installation   " link Install}    How to install KiLLER Baud on your system\n      @{" Example 1      " link BaudUsage}    Example output of bulletin 1\n      @{" Example 2      " link LastBauds}    Example output on bulletin 2\n      @{" History        " link History}    History of KiLLER Baud\n      @{" Greetings      " link Greetings}    Big hellos and messages\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Contact "How to contact KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC - © Me me me! :)"\n                            .--------------------.\n                            |  Contacting me...  |\n                            `--------------------''\n\n       For FAST response (preferred) please contact me on Internet:\n\n                               [email removed]\n\n                    ( [email removed] -- backup )\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                 I can be reached on the following boards:\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                The Undiscovered       +31-2294-1442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-4442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-tRY! (USR 28k8)\n\n                Last Outpost           +31-72-201182 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-72-157682 (USR 28k8)\n\n         I know, you have to call The Netherlands for it, but it''s\n            the only boards I call on a frequent basis, sorry.\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n        You can also find me on IRC once in a while on the channels\n                            #amigascne and #rave\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatIs "What is KiLLER Baud - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                        .------------------------.\n                        |  What is KiLLER Baud?  |\n                        `------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Baud is (another) statistical mumbo jumbo door.  It is able to\n   create  some  nice  statistics  about  the baud rate your users use to\n   logon your system.  It is styled in the same way as @{"KiLLER Comment" link KiLLERComment}, so\n   if  you  are familiar with that door, you will know that the output of\n   it will be fair enough :-)\n\n   I  made  this  door  as a simple diversion of some other projects I am\n   working  on.   This  door  was created in two days and I don''t feel it\n   should  be  released  to  the  public  because  it is too simple to be\n   released.   The  idea  is not my own, I saw another ''accumulative baud\n   report''  door  and  I  am  still  recovering from the output that door\n   creates.   I  don''t  know where some people get their inspiration from\n   but that output reminded me of kindergarten ;-)\n\n   And  because  of  the  nice  respons  I  got from some sysops about my\n   previous  releases,  I  thought  it  would  be  nice  to  make another\n   statistical  door,  sysops can''t get enough of BBS statistics.\n\n   Anyway,  I hope you like it and if you do, please give me a yell.\n\n                                                         @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Features "Features of KiLLER Baud - © Me me me and nobody else! :)"\n                           .--------------------------.\n                           |  Features of KiLLER Baud |\n                           `--------------------------''\n\n   ­ Create two pages of statistical data in one bulletin\n     (With a ''Space to Resume'' between them ofcourse)\n\n   ­ Create two pages of statistical data divided over 2 bulletins\n     (So two separate files)\n     \n   ­ Create either one of the two possible bulletins.\n     (You can choose between @{"Baud Usage" link BaudUsage} and @{"Last Bauds" link LastBauds})\n\n   ­ Only show the output on screen, don''t create the bulletins.\n     (Two pages, not selectable).\n\n   Other program features are:\n\n   · Fast baud detecting, virtually no delay for users.\n   · Very small data file to create bulletins\n   · You can remove baud rates from the bulletin(s).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Install "How to Install KiLLER Baud - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC (that''s me)"\n                      .------------------------------.\n                      |  How to install KiLLER Baud  |\n                      `------------------------------''\n\n   You are free to call the programs whatever you want and you can locate\n   the  program  wherever  you want.  I hate programs that have hardcoded\n   paths in their source.\n\n   The KiLLER_Baud executable has got a double function:  update the data\n   file  and create the bulletin(s).  Depending on available tooltypes in\n   the command icon, KiLLER Baud will know what to do.\n\n   ­­ Baud Detection ­­\n\n   First  of  all,  make  sure  that  KiLLER_Baud is run at least once to\n   update the data file.  I suggest you install it as an MCI command in a\n   bulletin  that  is  shown  to  every  user  that logs in (or logs out,\n   whatever fits you best).\n\n   · Example ·\n\n   ~CC_KB-DETECT\n   \n   · End ·\n\n   To  have  KiLLER_Baud  only  update the data file, create an icon that\n   holds  the tooltype "DETECT_BAUD".  If this tooltype is present in the\n   icon with what KiLLER_Baud was started, the data file will be updated.\n   This data file will reside in the same directory as the program itself\n   and will  be called "KiLLER_Baud.Data" and is 484 bytes in size.  Note\n   that  the  bulletin(s)  will not be created!  An example icon has been\n   provided called "KB-DETECT.info".\n   \n   Important tooltypes for that command are:\n   \n   DETECT_BAUD             ­ This tooltype MUST be present if you want KB\n                             to update the data file.\n                             \n   LOCAL_NODE=<number>     ­ KiLLER_Baud  will  not add your local logins\n                             to its data file.  Normally,  the local node\n                             would be node 0, but certain boards that run\n                             /X-Fido  for example have their last node as\n                             their local node.  So with this tooltype you\n                             can specify which node is the local node.\n                             \n                             You  can  also specify a node which does not\n                             exist,  and  your local logins will be added\n                             to the data file.\n                             \n                             Default value is node 0.\n\n   ­­ Bulletin Creation ­­\n\n   Unless  you  want  to install KiLLER_Baud as a regular /X command that\n   can  be  started  from  the  main  prompt,  you  also  need to install\n   KiLLER_Baud  as  another  MCI command, but this time to have it create\n   the bulletin(s).\n\n   You  can  specify the name(s) of the bulletin(s) with tooltypes.  Here\n   are the important tooltypes for this command:\n\n   BULL=<filename>         ­ When specified, one bulletin will be created\n                             containing 2 pages of statistics,  separated\n                             by a "Space to Resume".\n\n   BULL.1=<filename>       ­ This  will  result in one bulletin that only\n                             contains the @{"Baud Usage" link BaudUsage} information.\n\n   BULL.2=<filename>       ­ This  induces KiLLER_Baud to create only the\n                             @{"Last Bauds" link LastBauds} bulletin and nothing more.\n\n   NOTE¹ : The `BULL'' tooltype overrides both BULL.1 and BULL.2 !!\n\n   NOTE² : If you ommit all of these tooltypes, KiLLER_Baud will show the\n           bulletins on screen and will not create any bulletin.\n           \n   If  you  wish,  you  can  have  certain  baud  rates  removed from the\n   bulletins.   Normally,  the  bulletin would contain baud rates ranging\n   from  SLOW  to FAST, whereas SLOW means all baud rates below 9600 baud\n   and  FAST all baudrates above the highest speed available. Please note\n   that both the SLOW and FAST baud rates will not be put in the bulletin\n   until they are actually used.\n\n   For  example,  there  is  a BBS somewhere in Finland that has no 12000\n   baud users.  As strange as it is, this was true.  But the bulletin did\n   contain  the  12000  baud  line.  It was much disliked by the sysop of\n   that  board.   It even got to a point where he get himself drunk, felt\n   bad (a la Michael Jackson) and called The Undiscovered BBS.\n\n   There  he  wrote me a message saying that he hated the 12000 baud line\n   and  that  it  should  be removed.  I made him clear that although his\n   board  did  not  have 12000 baud users, the Undiscovered has got them!\n   He felt like an ass and left crying.  I felt so bad about this, that I\n   made it possible to leave out any baudrate you like.  However, this is\n   purely  COSMETIC.  The 12000 connects are still being counted and they\n   are  also used for the total number of connects.  But it was the least\n   I could do for such a lonely guy (snif).\n\n   So  without telling you the name of the sysop (QUARTZ QUARTZ QUARTZ!),\n   this is how to disable baudrates:\n\n   NO_SLOW     - Disable  SLOW baudrate\n   NO_9600     - Disable  9600 baudrate\n   NO_12000    - Disable 12000 baudrate (see! :) )\n   NO_14400    - Disable 14400 baudrate\n   ...\n   NO_FAST     - Disable FAST baudrate\n   \n   These  tooltypes  can be put in the K-Baud tooltype and remember, they\n   are  purely  cosmetic,  if  you  disable all baudrates and look at the\n   bulletin,  there  will  be NO baudrates mentioned, but there will be a\n   total number of connects and a favourite baudrate.\n\n   ­­ Regular /X Command ­­\n\n   If  you want it to install like a regular command, install it like any\n   other  command but make sure you remove the BULL#?  tooltypes from the\n   icon or it will create bulletins instead of showing them.\n\n   ­­ Overview ­­\n\n   ONCE AGAIN (but in short):\n   \n      · K-Baud can either create bulletins or display them pronto\n      · To make it create bulletins, either put the BULL tooltype\n        or BULL.x tooltypes in the icon (NOT BOTH!).\n      · To disable baudrates use tooltype: NO_<baudrate>\n      · If  you  remove all BULL* tooltypes from the K-Baud icon,\n        K-Baud will show the results on screen right away.\n\n   ­­ Reset the counting ­­\n\n   If  you  want  to reset the counting, simply remove the data file.  It\n   will  detect itself that the file is gone and create a new one (with a\n   new starting date).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE LastBauds "Example Output: Last Baud (Bull.2) - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n.-----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER BAUD v1.5  ^  <> Baud Rate Statistical View <>  ^  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC! |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------.--------------------------.---------------------------------.\n| Connect String | Date/Time last Connect   | Last Connect made by            |\n:----------------:--------------------------:---------------------------------:\n|  Slower baud   | Sat May 27 10:56:37 1995 | celtic                          |\n|   9.600 baud   | Thu Jun 01 21:49:11 1995 | Alf                             |\n|  12.000 baud   | Thu Jun 01 17:51:58 1995 | wHIRLWIND                       |\n|  14.400 baud   | Thu Jun 01 18:00:15 1995 | yhatware                        |\n|  16.800 baud   | Thu Jun 01 16:45:12 1995 | UFOk                            |\n|  19.200 baud   | Thu Jun 01 20:46:07 1995 | D`sTyLE                         |\n|  21.600 baud   | Fri Jun 02 16:02:02 1995 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC!               |\n|  24.000 baud   | Thu Jun 01 01:32:36 1995 | I-GO                            |\n|  26.400 baud   | Thu Jun 01 21:26:36 1995 | Arlette                         |\n|  28.800 baud   | Thu Jun 01 17:39:38 1995 | [MEGA^FORTRESS]                 |\n|  31.200 baud   | Thu Jun 01 17:56:02 1995 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC                |\n|  33.600 baud   | Fri Jun  2 16:03:29 1995 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC                |\n|  Faster baud   | Thu Jun 01 18:00:36 1995 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC                |\n:----------------^--------------------------^---------------------------------:\n| Total Connects :  14352 ^ Favourite speed: 14.400 baud ^ Since: 25-Dec-1994 |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n                     This is bulletin 2 (BULL.2 tooltype).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE BaudUsage "Example Output: Baud Usage (Bull.1) - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n.-----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER BAUD v1.5  ^  <> Baud Rate Statistical View <>  ^  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC! |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------.----------------------.-------.-------.---------------------.\n| Connect String | 0%--------------100% | Total | Perc% | Overall             |\n:----------------:----------------------:-------:-------:---------------------:\n|  Slower baud   |                      |    75 |    0% | Private             |\n|   9.600 baud   |                      |   127 |    0% | Private             |\n|  12.000 baud   |                      |    50 |    0% | Hardly used         |\n|  14.400 baud   | #################### |  5724 |   39% | The Ruler!          |\n|  16.800 baud   | ###                  |   869 |    6% | Used by few         |\n|  19.200 baud   | ##                   |   728 |    5% | Used by few         |\n|  21.600 baud   | ####                 |  1424 |    9% | Not too bad         |\n|  24.000 baud   | ##                   |   656 |    4% | Used by few         |\n|  26.400 baud   | ######               |  1975 |   13% | Popular rate        |\n|  28.800 baud   | #########            |  2704 |   18% | Much liked          |\n|  31.200 baud   |                      |     2 |    0% | Hardly used         |\n|  33.600 baud   |                      |     5 |    0% | Hardly used         |\n|  Faster baud   |                      |    13 |    0% | Hardly used         |\n:----------------^----------------------^-------^-------^---------------------:\n| Total Connects :  14352 ^ Favourite speed: 14.400 baud ^ Since: 25-Dec-1994 |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n   The  graphical percentages shown are a RELATIVE comparison between the\n   most used baud rates and the rest.\n\n   Both  the  `Slower''  and  `Faster'' baudrate are ONLY shown when it has\n   been actually used by someone.\n\n                   (And yes, this output is a fraud :) )\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE History "History of KiLLER Baud - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                      -//- History of KiLLER Baud -//-\n   \n   1.5.0    01-Jun-95   Improved    33.600 baud is recognized.\n\n            01-Jun-95   Improved    Changed the texts in KiLLER Baud.\n\n   1.4.0    02-Feb-95   Fixed       After spending two hours with on the\n                                    with Muad''Dib, we finally discovered\n                                    that I forgot to put the tooltype\n                                    TYPE=XIM in the icon that I delivered\n                                    in the package. This caused quite\n                                    some problems on some boards, but not\n                                    on mine ;) But we found it, and I\n                                    fixed it so it works again.\n                                    *Reported by Muad''Dib*\n\n   1.3.1    02-Feb-95   Fixed       I misplaced the code from the KiLLER\n                                    detect program causing two errors ;(\n                                    This has been fixed, sorry dudes.\n                                    *Detected by NightShade & Motive Power*\n\n   1.3.0    05-Jan-95   Improved    Put the code to update the data file\n                                    into the main KiLLER_Baud program to\n                                    save space. This saved about 14KB.\n\n   1.2.2    04-Jan-95   NEW         You can now have baud rates removed\n                                    from the bulletin with the tooltypes:\n                                    NO_<baudrate>.\n                                    *Cried for by Quartz/MST*\n\n   1.2.1    25-Dec-94   Improved    Added two more checks that prevent a\n                                    division by zero (guru #80000005).\n\n   1.2.0    21-Dec-94   Improved    The baud rates "Slower" and "Faster" are\n                                    now only shown when they are actually in\n                                    use. It''s a cosmetic change.\n                                    *Requested by Muad''Dib*\n\n   1.1.0    20-Dec-94   Improved    The graphical percentage bar is now made\n                                    relative to the most used baud rate for\n                                    better representation.\n\n   1.0.0    18-Dec-94   Release     Muad''Dib got the first version to test.\n\n   1.0.0    18-Dec-94   NEW         Created KiLLER Baud.\n\n                                    -*-\n\n                                    OBSOLETE! Put the code into the\n                                    main executable (saving about 14KB).\n\n   1.2.1    25-Dec-94   Improved    When the data file is first created,\n                                    all variables will be cleared now on\n                                    a more firm basis.\n\n   1.2.0    21-Dec-94   Improved    Made it work with the new structure and\n                                    revised it to 1.2.0 for compatibility\n                                    reasons.\n\n   1.0.1    18-Dec-94   Fixed       KiLLER Detect now detects the line speed\n                                    and not the speed between modem and\n                                    computer (sigh).\n                                    Reported by: Muad''Dib/DLT\n\n   1.0.0    16-Dec-94   NEW         Created KiLLER Detect.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Greetings "Aloha! - By KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                                .-------------.\n                                |  Greetings  |\n                                `-------------''\n\n   Muad''Dib/MST/DLT     USR sure as hell rulez!\n\n   UFOk/MST^LSD^PSG     Trying to break the world-record? ;)\n\n   QuartZ/MYSTiC        Let''s get it going! Intro''s!\n\n   Motive Power/MST     33k6 beats 26k4 bud... ;)\n   \n   I-GO^MDMA            0.25 is not enough for energy.\n   \n   Zed                  I hope to contact you soon!\n   \n                      ...and to all my other good friends!\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE KiLLERComment "KiLLER-WHAT? - © 1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n         «« THIS IS AN EXCERPT OUT OF THE GUIDE OF KILLER COMMENT ««\n\n                     -//- What is KiLLER Comment? -//-\n\n   KiLLER Comment is number 1001 in the row of a [C]omment doors.  But this\n   is not just *a* comment door, otherwise I wouldn''t have made it.  I have\n   some profound reasons.\n\n   Despite  the  fact  that  there  must be over a 1000 different [C]omment\n   doors,  there  is not one comment door that suits my needs.  I have seen\n   plenty of them but to me they all look ugly, have static interfaces, are\n   as flexible as a log of wood and generally suck :-).\n\n   With  this mind I''ve tried to make KiLLER Comment as smart and pretty as\n   I  can  and  I  feel I have succeeded.  I have never seen a comment door\n   that  works  as  simple  as  this  one  with  a fair amount of so-called\n   ''intelligence'' and comes with a nice GUI.\n\n   What  is  this  ''intelligence'' I speak about you say?  Well, all comment\n   doors  sofar  weren''t  able  to see that if I typed "c kill" I wanted to\n   comment to "KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" nor where they able to see that if I typed\n   "c  amiga" I wanted to comment to the Amiga co-sysop.  KiLLER Comment is\n   able to detect this!\n\n   Another  thing  is  security.   Every  new  user  was  able to see which\n   cosysops  you  had,  but  by giving every cosysop a security level, they\n   will  not see their names/jobs.  And if there is only one (co)sysop they\n   are  allowed  to  see,  they  will  not  even  see it is done via KiLLER\n   Comment.\n\n   Also,  per user in the [C]omment list there is a check wether he/she has\n   got access to the conference that KiLLER [C]omment is called from, so it\n   is  impossible  to write a message to someone who doesn''t have access to\n   that conference.\n\n   What I personally also dislike are comment doors that only fill like 25%\n   of  your  screen.   Not  that  you  should put a lot of nonsense in your\n   comment  door,  no,  but  KiLLER  Comment puts an extra line between the\n   names  of  the  (co-)sysops  if  that''s possible with the user''s maximum\n   numbers  of  lines  he  can  handle  on his screen.  It''s these kinds of\n   things I miss in those 13-in-a-dozen [C]omment doors.  That''s why I made\n   KiLLER Comment.\n\n   And  oh, just like I first introduced cursor control in ConfScan, KiLLER\n   comment also uses cursor control for selecting a user you can write to.\n\n                             »» END OF EXCERPT ««\n\n   To get hold of this tool, simply @{"contact" link Contact} me.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Update "Update Information - USR Rules Again!"\n                        -//-  Update Information -//-\n\n   Today  (June  1st,  1995)  US  Robotics  surprised  the  scene with an\n   unexpected  present:   USR modems with a flash rom can update to v.34+\n   allowing  speeds  upto  33.600  baud  instead of the 28.800 baud other\n   modems have as a maximum.  Since I own one myself I love it :)\n\n   This  ofcourse  asked  for  a quick update of KiLLER Baud, because you\n   want  those 33.600 connects showing in your bulletin!  I thought I had\n   outsmarted  USR  by  already  recognizing  31.200 baud in the previous\n   version,  but  I had not expected 33k6.  But I''m not complaining here,\n   hell no, I love 4000CPS on LHA files :)\n\n   So all this update does extra is recognize 33k6 connects.\n\n   Because  of  the  new data format involved, I''ve included a very small\n   util  to update your KiLLER_Baud.Data.  All you have to do is start it\n   in  the same directory as where your data file resides.  A backup file\n   will be created by it!\n\n   AFTER  YOU  HAVE  DONE THAT, you can start using the new version.  All\n   you  have  to  do  is copy the new executable over the old version and\n   you''re done.\n\n   That''s  all about this update, if you have any suggestions, questions,\n   bug-reports, feel free to @{"contact" link Contact} me.\n\n                                                      -KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC-\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),'{"TYPE":"XIM"}','auto-added',54902,5,0,NULL,NULL,'klr_kbau','scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('klr_kcom','KLR-KCOM.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KLR-KCOM.LHA','KC-Config','XIM','KC-Config',NULL,'KLR','KLR','kILLER-cOMMENT eXEC & sOURCE bY KLR -----------------------------------',replace('-----------------------------------\nkILLER-cOMMENT eXEC & sOURCE bY KLR\n-----------------------------------\n','\n',char(10)),'KiLLER-Comment.Guide',replace('@DATABASE "KiLLER-Comment.GUiDE"\n@REMARK	.-------------------------------------------------------.\n@REMARK  | This document should be read with AmigaGuide (OS 2.x) |\n@REMARK  | or MultiView (OS 3.x) or any other guide reader. Tnx. |\n@REMARK  `-------------------------------------------------------''\n@REMARK	$VER: KiLLER Comment 1.4.2\n@WIDTH 80\n@NODE MAIN "KiLLER Comment - MYSTiC 1994/1995 - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            Guide to KiLLER Comment\n                     for Ami-Express Bulletin Board Systems\n\n                             Program version: 1.4.2\n                Compilation Date/Time: Mon Dec 25 22:10:20 1995\n\n                         © 1994/1995 by @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n\n                   ... another fine MYSTiC /X production ...\n\n                       @{"       UPDATE iNFORMATiON        " link WhatsNew}\n\n      @{" First words... " link WhatIs}    What/Why is KiLLER Comment?\n      @{" Features       " link Features}    What can it do?\n      @{" Examples       " link Examples}    What does it look like?\n      @{" Installation   " link Install}    How to install KiLLER Comment on your BBS\n      @{" Usage /X       " link AmiUsage}    How things go with Ami-Express\n      @{" Usage Config   " link Config}    How to use the Config GUI\n      @{" History        " link History}    History of KiLLER Comment\n      @{" History Config " link HistoryGUI}    History of KiLLER Config\n      @{" Greetings      " link Greetings}    Greetings, messages and contact info\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatsNew "What is new in this version - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                          .----------------------.\n                          |  New in version 1.4  |\n                          `----------------------''\n\n   The  Commentistics  have changed quite a bit.  On a lot of boards that\n   use  KiLLER-Comment  it  appeared that the sysop had received the most\n   messages, seems only logical.  As a result the #-bars that graphically\n   showed you the number of messages written to a certain person compared\n   to the total messages always looked like this setup:\n\n   Sysop          ##############\n   Person 1       ###\n   Person 2       #\n   Person 3       \n   Person 4       ##\n   Person 5       #\n\n   In  words:   the sysop''s bar would always be the largest and the other\n   people  in  the list were lucky to have a bar with one ''#'' or two ''#''s\n   in it.  Not very entertaining to look at and -more importantly- it had\n   lost it functionality.  Time for a change.\n\n   The  sysop  has  been  placed  on  an ivory tower, so to speak.  He no\n   longer  participates  in  the  calculation  routine  that makes up the\n   length  of  the  bars from other people.  As a base for their bars the\n   total  number  of messages will be taken minus the ones written to the\n   sysop. A far clearer and more interesting image can be seen.\n\n   Does  this  mean the sysop''s bar will always be 100%?  No, the sysop''s\n   bar  will  now  show the amount of messages written to him compared to\n   the  total  amount of messages written.  So if there were 500 messages\n   written  in  total  and 250 of those were written specifically to him,\n   his bar would show 50%.\n\n                             *^* BUG FIXES *^*\n\n   A  rather dumb bug has been fixed.  It has been there since day 1, but\n   nobody  noticed or didn''t care to report it.  Until last week.  KiLLER\n   Comment  would simply not add up the number of messages written to the\n   people  in  the KC-list.  The total amount of messages always equalled\n   the number of messages written to the sysop.\n\n   Some  parts  of  KC  needed to be rewritten in order to get it to work\n   correctly  with people who use a header in front of their KC-list.  It\n   now should work 100%.  Please report any bugs if you find them.\n\n   That''s  all,  if you wish to @{"contact" link contact} me for any reason, please do ''coz\n   it''s always nice to know that someone has read this guide 8^).\n\n                          .----------------------.\n                          |  New in version 1.3  |\n                          `----------------------''\n\n   I''ve  built  in  an  option  to have KiLLER-Comment show a header file\n   before it will display the list of users that can be commented to.\n\n   To do so, add the following tooltype to your C.info:\n\n        HEADER=<file>\n   e.g. HEADER=DOORS:K-Comment/Header.ANSI\n\n   When a header is chosen,  the copyright message of KiLLER-Comment will\n   be surpressed so you have more room for your header.\n\n   I''ve included a default header which was made my Zeus/MST/Pro^Arts!\n\n                             *^* BUG FIXES *^*\n\n   And  a  bugfix  in  the  Config Editor, it refused to put <3 character\n   users  in  the  user-list  when  you  wanted  to  add  a user.  Pretty\n   annoying.    Nobody  seemed  to  have  any  trouble  with  it,  except\n   Phantom/OVL who reported it. \n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatIs "What/Why is KiLLER Comment? - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                         .---------------------------.\n                         |  What is KiLLER Comment?  |\n                         `---------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Comment  is  number 1001 in the row of a [C]omment doors.  But\n   this  is not just *a* comment door, otherwise I wouldn''t have made it.\n   I have some profound reasons.\n\n   Despite  the  fact  that there must be over a 1000 different [C]omment\n   doors, there is not one comment door that suits my needs.  I have seen\n   plenty  of  them but to me they all look ugly, have static interfaces,\n   are as flexible as a log of wood and generally suck :-).\n\n   With  this  mind I''ve tried to make KiLLER Comment as smart and pretty\n   as  I  can  and  I feel I have succeeded.  I have never seen a comment\n   door  that works as simple as this one with a fair amount of so-called\n   ''intelligence'' and comes with a nice GUI.\n\n   What  is this ''intelligence'' I speak about you say?  Well, all comment\n   doors  sofar  weren''t able to see that if I typed "c kill" I wanted to\n   comment  to  "KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"  nor  where they able to see that if I\n   typed  "c  amiga"  I  wanted to comment to the Amiga co-sysop.  KiLLER\n   Comment is able to detect this!\n\n   Another  thing  is  security.   Every  new  user was able to see which\n   cosysops  you  had, but by giving every cosysop a security level, they\n   will  not  see  their  names/jobs.  And if there is only one (co)sysop\n   they  are allowed to see, they will not even see it is done via KiLLER\n   Comment.\n\n   Also,  per  user  in the [C]omment list there is a check wether he/she\n   has got access to the conference that KiLLER [C]omment is called from,\n   so  it  is  impossible  to  write  a  person  to  someone in the wrong\n   conference.\n\n   What  I  personally also dislike are comment doors that only fill like\n   25% of your screen.  Not that you should put a lot of nonsense in your\n   comment  door,  no,  but KiLLER Comment puts an extra line between the\n   names  of  the  (co-)sysops if that''s possible with the user''s maximum\n   numbers  of  lines  he  can handle on his screen.  It''s these kinds of\n   things  I  miss  in those 13-in-a-dozen [C]omment doors.  That''s why I\n   made KiLLER Comment.\n\n   And  oh,  just  like  I  first  introduced cursor control in ConfScan,\n   KiLLER  comment  also uses cursor control for selecting a user you can\n   write to.\n\n   Anyway,  I  hope  you will enjoy KiLLER Comment as much as I will.  Go\n   check out the features if you want to know more.\n\n                                                        -KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Features "Features of KiLLER Comment - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                        .------------------------------.\n                        |  Features of KiLLER Comment  |\n                        `------------------------------''\n\n   Although  the  list of features isn''t big, it''s enough for a [C]omment\n   door.  Remember, it''s only a [C]omment door.\n\n   » GUI Config\n\n   A small program comes with it to set it up the way you want it as easy\n   as  possible.   It''s just click and save, you probably won''t even need\n   the keyboard but to fill in some personal info.\n\n   » Security\n\n   Users  with low access will not see all cosysops in the [C]omment list\n   (esspecially useful with new users).\n\n   » Known as\n\n   Very  often  your  cosysops  will have names that contain some sort of\n   group  name  in  it  (like KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC), but it''s not always that\n   nice  or  handy to have that group name in the comment door (be it for\n   cosmetic  reasons,  be  it for some other reason).  That''s why you can\n   enter  an  alias for each of your cosysops (and for yourself ofcourse)\n   which will be used to display in the comment list.\n\n   » Info\n\n   For  every  (co)sysop  you can enter one line of information regarding\n   why  he  is  in  the  comment list.  This can be something like "Amiga\n   Co-Sysop" or "Takes care of users / files / lamers".\n\n   » Comments\n\n   Most  comment  doors  offer  you  the possibility to display a message\n   after  the  user  has selected someone to write to.  This is generally\n   something  like  "<name> says:  bla bla".  With KiLLER Comment you can\n   make  lines  like this:  "And there goes another message to <name>" or\n   "<name> is cheering of joy!".\n   \n   An example file with fortune cookies has been supplied.\n\n   » Cursor Control / Numeric entries / Default sysop\n\n   Selecting  a  user can be done with the cursor keys or by entering the\n   corresponding number.  A default sysop is also available (space).\n\n   » Comment Statistics\n\n   KiLLER Comment keeps record of the number of messages written to every\n   user and also knows when every user received his last message plus the\n   user  who wrote it to him.  So with this information it is possible to\n   create  some  nice looking statistics.  These statistics can be viewed\n   within /X or with the Config program.\n\n   » Optionable header file\n\n   If  you  like,  you  can  have  KiLLER Comment show a header before it\n   displays the list of receptors.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Install "How to install KiLLER Comment - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                      .----------------------------------.\n                      |  Installation of KiLLER Comment  |\n                      `----------------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Comment  can  be installed like any /X 3.x command, but please\n   take notice of these tooltypes:\n\n   "KC-Config.info" - The icon of the GUI config program\n   \n   · ACP_LOCATION=<path>      - Location of your ACP.info file.\n                                (eg. ACP_LOCATION=SYS:WBStartUp)\n\n   · KC_LOCATION=<path>       - Location of the /X version\n                                (eg. KC_LOCATION=DOORS:K-Comment)\n                                ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE **\n                                 THIS SHOULD BE THE PATH TO THE DIRECTORY\n                                 AND SHOULD _NOT_ INCLUDE THE NAME OF THE\n                                 EXECUTABLE! DON''T FORGET THIS! (HI JAKE)\n                                ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE **\n\n   · KC_VIEWER=<full name>    - External viewer for the Commentistics\n                                (eg. KC_VIEWER=C:PPMORE)\n\n   · PUB_SCREEN=<name>        - Optional(!) name of public screen that the\n                                config program will open on instead of the\n                                Workbench.\n                                (eg. PUB_SCREEN=ECS_PUB)\n\n   "C.info"          - The icon in your BBS command directory\n\n   · ACS_STATS=<level>        - The minimum level required to view the\n                                statiscal mumbo jumbo that KiLLER Comment\n                                is able to create for you (with ''C ?'').\n                                (eg. ACS_STATS=50)\n\n   · BBS_LOCATION=<path>      - This should be the path to your BBS:, or\n                                if you keep your USER.DATA located some-\n                                where else, the path to your USER.DATA.\n\n   · HEADER=<file>            - An optional header can be shown before the\n                                list of users will be build up.\n\n   · SEPARATOR_LINES          - An empty line is by default put  between\n                                two sysops if possible, but if you don''t\n                                like  that,  you can also have a line of\n                                ''-''s put between them.  To do so, simply\n                                this tooltype.\n\n   I  have  found  it  very  useful  to  put the Config program in my ACP\n   command list.  Maybe this can be useful for you too.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE AmiUsage "Usage in Ami-Express - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                          .------------------------.\n                          |  Usage in Ami-Express  |\n                          `------------------------''\n\n                          ^-^  Selecting a user  ^-^\n\n   Once  installed, the usage of the [C]omment door is (and should be) real\n   easy.  You can simply start it with ''C'' and you will be presented with a\n   menu from which you can select a user to send some mail to.\n\n   However,  like I said in the first words, KiLLER comment is a bit smart.\n   If  you enter a parameter after the ''C'', you can skip the menu and still\n   select  the  person you want to write to.  For example, suppose you want\n   to write a message to "Muad''Dib".  We all know that this is a very lousy\n   name  to spell, so we can suffice by entering "C Muad" and Muad''Dib will\n   be selected.\n\n   However,  if  you''ve  got  another  cosysop  in  the  list that is named\n   Muad''Dab,  KiLLER  Comment  will  take  the first name that has the part\n   "muad"  in it.  This is just the way it goes, so if you want to write to\n   Muad''Dab  instead  of  Muad''Dib  you  have  to enter another string that\n   uniquely identifies Muad''Dab, for instance:  "C Dab" :)\n\n   Another  way to select a user is entering his or her appropriate number.\n   If  you  know  that Muad''Dib is number 2 in the list, you can suffice by\n   entering  "C 2".   And  if  you  know that Muad''Dab is number 5, you can\n   enter  "C 5"  to  write  to Muad''Dab.  Real easy.  If you enter a number\n   that does not exist, you will be presented with the menu again.\n\n   But  that''s not all, let''s say you got separate sysops (or cosysops) for\n   different  conferences;  Amiga,  SNES  and  PC.  And suppose you want to\n   write  to  the Amiga cosysop, you can simply enter "C Amiga" or "C Ami".\n   Make  sure  the  length  of  the  string  after  the  "C"  is at least 3\n   characters,  otherwise  it will be marked as ambigious and the menu will\n   be  prompted.   And  yes,  that means that "C PC" will NOT work (haha!).\n   But  hey,  there  are  3  other  ways of selecting that sysop.  So don''t\n   despair (or enter "C p PC" if the text is "Cosysop PC").\n\n                           ^-^  Commentistics  ^-^\n\n   The  commentistics  can be viewed by entering a question mark behind the\n   "C".  Couldn''t be more simple.  Just enter "C ?" and you will be able to\n   view  all  sorts  of  statistic mumbo jumbo on your screen.  Once you''ve\n   seen the first screen, you can go to the next screen by pressing any key\n   BUT Q, ESC can CTRL-C.  Than the screen will be cleared in a fashionable\n   style and the last page will be shown, containing the times and names of\n   users who wrote last to the users in the Comment list.\n\n                           ^-^  Syntax Summary  ^-^\n\n   For all you folks that didn''t bother to read the upper texts, here''s the\n   brief list of all commands:\n\n   C        - A menu will be shown from which you can select a user\n\n   C <text> - The  <text> will be compared to the names of the users in the\n              list  and  if  that fails, it will be compared to the info of\n              each user in the list. This comparing is case insensitive.\n\n   C <#>    - The corresponding name with the number will be selected.\n\n   C ?      - The commentistics will be shown.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Config "The Config Editor - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             .-----------------.\n                             |  Config Editor  |\n                             `-----------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Comment  comes  with  a  full C= compliant Guided User Interface\n   (commonly  known  as  GUI).  With this Configuration Editor you are able\n   to setup KiLLER Comment the way YOU want.\n\n   When  you  start  the  Config  Editor for the first time, the sysop will\n   automatically  added as the first entry in the list of users that can be\n   commented  to.   There  should  at  least  be  ONE  entry in the list or\n   otherwise the user can''t comment to anybody :-)\n\n   Okay,  I  think it is best if I just tell you what every button does and\n   how to do things.  Here we go...\n\n   » Known As\n\n   You  can  enter an alias for every user that is in the list.  By default\n   the  alias  is  the  same  as  their real name in /X but you are free to\n   change  it.  KiLLER Comment will still know their real name, don''t worry\n   about that.  This function can be handy if you got a cosysop with a name\n   like [-Bad Boy^Lamers-R-Us^I-Suck-] but you don''t want to have that damn\n   ugly handle in your comment door.  Fill in whatever you like.\n\n   » Info\n\n   As  usual  you are able to enter one line of text that somehow describes\n   why  this  user  is put in the list of ''commenteers'' :) You can put info\n   like "Amiga Cosysop" in here or "Nuking is my nick-name!".  You are free\n   to  use  any colours in it, just enter them in the gadget like you would\n   do  in  a  text  file.   If pressing the ESC removes the cursor from the\n   gadget,  you  need to TURN OF THE TEXT-FILTER OPTION IN YOUR PREFERENCES\n   FROM YOUR AMIGA (Prefs/IControl)!!\n\n   Although  you  can only have like 34 chars on screen for info about that\n   user,  the  Config  Editor  takes 80 chars.  You are responsible for the\n   length  of  the  text.   If you put 80 chars of text in it, it will look\n   like a mess on screen in Ami-Express.\n\n   » Comments\n\n   A  comment is a line of text that is shown to the user who just selected\n   somebody.   This  can  be  a  message  like  "Muad''Dib says:  Thanks for\n   nothing!"  or  "You can hear Muad''Dib shout with joy.".  Or you can show\n   him fortune cookie (max.  1 line!).  An example file has been included.\n\n   This gadget wants a full path plus filename to a file that contains such\n   messages.   This can be a different file for every user or the same file\n   for all users.  The lines in the text file can contain anything you like\n   where you can use ''%s'' to represent the name of the choosen user.\n\n   Examples:   %s says: Thanks for your mail bud.\n               You can hear %s scream with joy.\n               %s is overwhelmed with tears by your gesture.\n               Who is General Failure and why is he reading my hard drive?\n\n   Be creative!\n\n   With  the  [S]  button  you  can select a file via a file-requester than\n   rather typing in its full name.\n\n   » Req. Acs\n\n   For  security  reasons,  you  are able to set a minimum access level for\n   every  user in the comment list.  This way you are able to have multiple\n   cosysops and not let all the users know what they all do.\n\n   Imagine  you''ve got a cosysop for the Amiga section, SNES section and PC\n   section.   You''ve  got  a lot of Amiga users, but you don''t want them to\n   know  that  you  got  a  PC  section.   If  they would use the ''C'' door,\n   normally  they  would  see  that  you  got a cosysop for the PC section.\n   "What  PC  section?"  they will think.  Or even worse, new users who use\n   the door can easily spot your conferences.  But not with KiLLER Comment,\n   just  make  sure that the Amiga users have a lower access level than the\n   PC users.\n\n   It''s  not  perfect yet, but it''s a start.  I am thinking of a better way\n   to secure this (it''s possible, I just have to work it out a bit more).\n\n   By  the  way,  cosysops  with  "C"  access  will  also NOT see the other\n   cosysop(s) (not even in the Commentistics).\n\n   » Commentistics\n\n   Not  only in Ami-Express, but also in the Config Editor, you are able to\n   look  up  the statistics that KiLLER Comment can create.  The statistics\n   are  put  into  a  file  and that file is shown to you with the external\n   viewer  you specified.  For your information, that file is always called\n   T:KC_Commentistics,  does  NOT  contain  any  ANSI  codes and is removed\n   afterwards.\n\n   » Add / Del\n\n   Adding  a  user  to  the  list is quite easy, select Add and you will be\n   presented  with  a  list  of all your users in your USER.DATA.  This may\n   take  a  while,  depending on your type (read:  speed) of computer, hard\n   drive  and number of users in your USER.DATA.  A new user will always be\n   inserted at the END of the list.\n\n   To  remove  a user from the list, make it the active user (its name will\n   be  shown  below the listview) and press Del.  Easy as peanuts.  The new\n   current user will be the last user in the list.\n\n   You can not remove all users from the list.  A protection has been built\n   in  so please try to do so :) It''s impossible to remove them all because\n   to who should the users in your system comment to?\n\n   » Up / Down\n\n   With  those  gadgets  you  are  able to determine the order in which the\n   users  will  appear a) in the list and b) on screen in Ami-Express.  The\n   most upper user will be the default sysop!\n\n   To  move  a user, make it the current one by selecting it and then press\n   either up or down (repeatively if neccessary).\n\n   » Save\n\n   Guess what?\n\n   » &\n   \n   Sorry? What did you say? :)\n\n   » Quit\n\n   You  guessed  it.   By  the  way,  there  is  NO  warning if you changed\n   something and did not save it before quitting.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Greetings "Greetings go to... - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            .-------------------.\n                            |  Greetings to...  |\n                            `-------------------''\n\n                           In no specific order:\n\n          Muad''Dib ^ Motive Power ^ QuartZ ^ Jabbah ^ Jam & Spoon\n\n                 Exotic ^ UFOk ^ Easy Rider ^ I-GO ^ Galaxy\n\n         bObO ^ Famous''O''Hara ^ Zed ^ Green Beret ^ Arcade ^ Diablo\n\n                   XTD ^ Idea ^ XCS ^ Skol ^ Jake ^ aLLAH\n\n                 Zeus ^ nUp! ^ aXe ^ cASSeoPaYa ^ Psychlops\n\n             Rhino/Team Hoi ^ The Undertaker ^ The Phantom/OVL\n\n                     ... and to all my other friends ...\n\n\n   If you want to contact me, read @{"this!" link Contact}\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Contact "How to get in touch with me - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            .--------------------.\n                            |  Contacting me...  |\n                            `--------------------'' \n\n            It is prefered you contact me via my e-mail address:\n\n                               [email removed]\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                 I can be reached on the following boards:\n\n             The Undiscovered       +31-229-541-442 (USR 33k6)\n                                    +31-229-544-442 (USR 33k6)\n                                    +31-229-544-331 (V34 28k8)\n\n             Last Outpost           +31-72-520-1182 (USR 33k6)\n                                    +31-72-515-7682 (USR 33k6)\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                   Or try to find me at #amigascne (IRC).\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE History "History of KiLLER Comment - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n   1.4.2   21-Dec-95    Altered the "Overall" remarks and added a text\n                        for receivers with less than 5 messages.  They\n                        are a bunch of losers :-).\n\n   1.4.1   17-Dec-95    Rewrote a lot of KC so the HEADER option works\n                        without problems.\n\n   1.4.0   16-Dec-95    Made sure the total number of messages written\n                        was actually something cumulative ;-).\n                        REPORTED BY: [·tYRELL & sNAKE·] & QuartZ/MST\n\n   1.3.0   04-Jul-95    Built in the HEADER option to show an external\n                        header before building the actual user list.\n                        Done in 10 minutes, thanks to modular coding.\n\n   1.2.0   03-Feb-95    Fixed the version numbers in the executable ;)\n                        REPORTED BY: MOTiVE POWER\n                        \n                        You can now enter an asterisk behind the name of\n                        the person you want to write to (ie. C MUAD*).\n                        REQUESTED BY: MUAD''DiB\n                        \n                        Made the percentage bar in the commentistics a\n                        relative one, like in KiLLER-Baud. The user with\n                        the most messages will always have 100% and the\n                        rest will be a relative representation to that\n                        user.\n\n   1.1.0    26-12-94    KiLLER [C]omment now checks wether the persons in\n                        the [C]omment list have access to the current conf\n                        that KC is called from. If not, that person will\n                        not be put in the list.\n                        REQUESTED BY: QuartZ\n\n   1.0.5    02-11-94    You can now have lines used as a separator on screen\n                        instead of a blank line.\n                        New tooltype: SEPARATOR_LINES\n                        REQUESTED BY: JAKE\n\n   1.0.4    20-10-94    Added an access level check. If a user does not have\n                        the minimal required access to actually see a cosysop\n                        in the Comment list, than that user will not see\n                        that cosysop. See also @{"History Gui" link HistoryGUI}.\n                        REQUESTED BY: TYCOON/MYSTiC & MUAD''DiB\n\n            20-10-94    The program now attempts to write messages with the\n                        external "E" door (if any). This makes it more\n                        flexible for sysop, allowing them to have external\n                        "E" doors installed, like the one from 5D and the\n                        external one from Tycoon/MYSTiC (/X-Fido).\n                        REQUESTED BY: TYCOON/MYSTiC\n\n   1.0.3    15-10-94    A bug in the "Commentistics" routine caused a guru\n                        when there were users with no messages. A division\n                        by zero was the result -> guru :( Fixed that.\n\n   1.0.0    14-10-94    First initial release to friends.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE HistoryGUI "History of KiLLER Config Editor - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n   1.0.3    14-02-95    Fixed a bug that caused all users with a name\n                        shorter than 4 characters to be left out of the\n                        user-list when you wanted to add a user.\n                        REPORTED BY: PHANTOM/OVERLORDS\n\n   1.0.2    20-10-94    Restyled the GUI bit. I removed the "Show Comments"\n                        gadget because I found it not useful. Removed the\n                        code also (ofcoz, but you never know who reads\n                        this :) ).\n\n            20-10-94    New option: [ Req. Acs ] (Required Access)\n                        You can set a minimum access level per user in the\n                        list. Users with an access level lower than that\n                        level will NOT see him in the [C]omment door.\n                        This enables you to let even new users use this\n                        door and not reveal to them that you (for instance)\n                        have an Amiga cosysop and/or SNES cosysop and/or\n                        PC cosysop.\n                        REQUESTED BY: TYCOON/MYSTIC & MUAD''DIB/DLT\n\n            20-10-94    New option: [ Clear Statistical Data ]\n                        With this option you can clear all statistical data\n                        that is kept for every user. This means that the\n                        total # of msg-s, last msg date and user will be set\n                        to 0 (or None).\n\n            20-10-94    Another restyle: [ Save ][ & ][ Quit ]\n                        Save -> Saves the (new) prefs\n                        &    -> Save and Quit\n                        Quit -> Quit the program\n\n            20-10-94    Another restyle: I changed the placed of the [ Add ]\n                        [ Del ] [ Up ] [ Down ] a bit, to make it a bit more\n                        logical.\n\n   1.0.1    14-10-94    First bug found in 3 secs by Muad''Dib :) If you\n                        selected "Commentistics" from the GUI Config and\n                        there were no messages written yet, a crash was\n                        the result (division by zero). Fixed it.\n\n   1.0.0    14-10-94    First initial release to friends.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Examples "Example output - From Undiscovered BBS"\n\n   These  examples  were  taken from The Undiscovered BBS, sysop Muad''Dib\n   presiding.   Admire  his  statistics  and  behold  his staff :-).  All\n   screens  are copied without an extra blank line between the members in\n   the list, it looks better this way in a guide.\n\n                            - Selection screen -\n\n               KiLLER COMMENT v1.4 ^ (c) 1995 by KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC\n\n.--------------------------.---------------------------------------.---------.\n: Sun Dec 17 16:46:31 1995 : Conf: oNLY 4 tHE mODULES              : MYSTiC! :\n`--------------------------^---------------------------------------^---------''\n.--------------------------.-------------------------------------------------.\n|  1. mUAD''diB             | aLWAYS oN tHE uNDiSCOVERED!                     |\n|  2. LiGHtNiNG!           | uNDiSCOVERED dESiGN fOReVER!                    |\n|  3. mOTiVE pOWER         | How can I love you if you won''t lie down?       |\n|  4. pYXiS/COCAiNE!       | uNDiSCOVERED mAiNTANANCE                        |\n|  5. sCHWABENpOWER!       | mAJOR aMiGA&PC tRADER                           |\n|  6. aUTOPSY!             | hOODLUM lEADER                                  |\n|  7. cASSEOPAYA           | AH ALl Ya RaVeRs!!! LeaVe mE yAr MaiL!          |\n|  8. rAYON                | liGHTWAVE pROBLEMS? aSK mE!                     |\n|  9. hARDbYTE             | fOR aLL yOUR lIGHTwAVE qUESTIONS!               |\n| 10. KiLLraVeN            | MYST¡C WøRLD LEADER ^ MDMA LøVER ^ MST dOORS!   |\n`--------------------------^-------------------------------------------------''\n.-------------------------------------------------------------.--------------.\n: CURSOR KEYS / 1 - 10 / [SPACE] FOR DEFAULT SYSOP / [Q] QUIT : SELECTED:  9 :\n`-------------------------------------------------------------^--------------''\n\n                        - Commentistical Screen 1 -\n\n.----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER COMMENT v1.4  ^  <X>   COMMENTiSTiCS   <X>  ^  (c) KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC |\n`----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------------.----------------------.-------.-------.--------------.\n| User Name            | 0%--------------100% | Total | Perc% | Overall      |\n:----------------------:----------------------:-------:-------:--------------:\n| mUAD''diB             | #################    |  2434 |   86% | Ruler!       |\n| LiGHtNiNG!           |                      |     6 |    1% | Starting out |\n| mOTiVE pOWER         | ###                  |    64 |   16% | Not bad      |\n| pYXiS/COCAiNE!       | ##                   |    54 |   14% | Not bad      |\n| sCHWABENpOWER!       | #                    |    29 |    7% | Starting out |\n| aUTOPSY!             |                      |     6 |    1% | Starting out |\n| cASSEOPAYA           | ###                  |    66 |   17% | Not bad      |\n| rAYON                |                      |     2 |    0% | Starting out |\n| hARDbYTE             |                      |     8 |    2% | Starting out |\n| KiLLraVeN            | #######              |   150 |   38% | Buzzing...   |\n:----------------------^----------------------^-------^-------^--------------:\n| Total messages:   2819 ^ Average:    281 msgs per user ^ [Q]uit / Any key  |\n`----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n                        - Commentistical Screen 2 -\n\n.----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER COMMENT v1.4  ^  <X>   COMMENTiSTiCS   <X>  ^  (c) KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC |\n`----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.------------------------.--------------------------.------------------------.\n| User Name              | Date/Time of last msg    | Last msg came from     |\n:------------------------:--------------------------:------------------------:\n| mUAD''diB               | Sun Dec 17 20:45:59 1995 | Brooker                |\n| LiGHtNiNG!             | Tue Dec 12 19:55:26 1995 | Muad''Dib               |\n| mOTiVE pOWER           | Sat Dec 02 23:40:02 1995 | Muad''Dib               |\n| pYXiS/COCAiNE!         | Fri Nov 24 20:47:11 1995 | mOTiVE pOWER           |\n| sCHWABENpOWER!         | Mon Nov 27 22:28:38 1995 | FLoppy Robby           |\n| aUTOPSY!               | Sun Dec 10 18:12:48 1995 | Quiaos                 |\n| cASSEOPAYA             | Thu Dec 14 22:18:50 1995 | Sir IQ                 |\n| rAYON                  | Sat Dec 02 20:07:25 1995 | Muad''Dib               |\n| hARDbYTE               | Sun Dec 17 16:46:54 1995 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC       |\n| KiLLraVeN              | Mon Dec 04 21:09:35 1995 | EDGE/SAINTS            |\n`------------------------^--------------------------^------------------------''\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),'{"LOCATION":"<path>"}','auto-added',92748,5,0,NULL,NULL,'klr_kcom','scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('klrbau14','KLRBAU14.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KLRBAU14.LHA','KiLLER_Baud','XIM','KiLLER_Baud',NULL,NULL,NULL,'.-\_  \/  _/   __/______\/___/-------------. | /   \/   \______  \_  \/  \_   mYSTIC!   | :/     \____/   _____/___    / /X dIVISION .',replace('  __/\  __________ _ ___  /\__              \n.-\_  \/  _/   __/______\/___/-------------.\n| /   \/   \______  \_  \/  \_   mYSTIC!   |\n:/     \____/   _____/___    / /X dIVISION .\n/_______\-\_____/--sTZ--l___/--------------:\n| KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC BRiNGS: KiLLER-BAUD 1.4 |\n| *Creates bulls about users'' baud-rates!* :\n: MAJOR BUGFiX AND CORRECTED SUPPLiED iCON !\n| o Exclude baud-rates  o Nice lay-out     ¡\n: o Two pages of info!  o Future proof!    |\n`------------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),'KiLLER_Baud.Guide',replace('@DATABASE "KiLLER-Comment.GUiDE"\n@REMARK	.-------------------------------------------------------.\n@REMARK  | This document should be read with AmigaGuide (OS 2.x) |\n@REMARK  | or MultiView (OS 3.x) or any other guide reader. Tnx. |\n@REMARK  `-------------------------------------------------------''\n@REMARK	$VER: KiLLER Baud 1.4.0\n@WIDTH 80\n@NODE MAIN "KiLLER Comment - MYSTiC 1994 - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             Guide to KiLLER Baud\n                 for Ami-Express 3.38+ Bulletin Board Systems\n\n                             Program version: 1.4.0\n                Compilation Date/Time: Thu Feb  2 21:30:48 1995\n\n                         © 1994/1995 by @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n\n                   ... another fine MYSTiC /X production ...\n\n      @{" First words... " link WhatIs}    What is KiLLER Baud\n      @{" Features       " link Features}    What can it do?\n      @{" Installation   " link Install}    How to install KiLLER Baud on your system\n      @{" Example 1      " link BaudUsage}    Example output of bulletin 1\n      @{" Example 2      " link LastBauds}    Example output on bulletin 2\n      @{" History        " link History}    History of KiLLER Baud\n      @{" Greetings      " link Greetings}    Big hellos and messages\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Contact "How to contact KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC - © Me me me! :)"\n                            .--------------------.\n                            |  Contacting me...  |\n                            `--------------------''\n\n       For FAST response (preferred) please contact me on Internet:\n\n                               [email removed]\n\n                    ( [email removed] -- backup )\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                 I can be reached on the following boards:\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                The Undiscovered       +31-2294-1442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-4442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-tRY! (USR 28k8)\n\n                Last Outpost           +31-72-201182 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-72-157682 (USR 28k8)\n\n         I know, you have to call The Netherlands for it, but it''s\n            the only boards I call on a frequent basis, sorry.\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n        You can also find me on IRC once in a while on the channels\n                   #amigascne, #amiga, #rave and #megawatt\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatIs "What is KiLLER Baud - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                        .------------------------.\n                        |  What is KiLLER Baud?  |\n                        `------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Baud is (another) statistical mumbo jumbo door.  It is able to\n   create  some  nice  statistics  about  the baud rate your users use to\n   logon your system.  It is styled in the same way as @{"KiLLER Comment" link KiLLERComment}, so\n   if  you  are familiar with that door, you will know that the output of\n   it will be fair enough :-)\n\n   I  made  this  door  as a simple diversion of some other projects I am\n   working  on.   This  door  was created in two days and I don''t feel it\n   should  be  released  to  the  public  because  it is too simple to be\n   released.   The  idea  is not my own, I saw another ''accumulative baud\n   report''  door  and  I  am  still  recovering from the output that door\n   creates.   I  don''t  know where some people get their inspiration from\n   but that output reminded me of kindergarten ;-)\n\n   And  because  of  the  nice  respons  I  got from some sysops about my\n   previous  releases,  I  thought  it  would  be  nice  to  make another\n   statistical  door,  sysops can''t get enough of BBS statistics.\n\n   Anyway,  I hope you like it and if you do, please give me a yell.\n\n                                                         @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Features "Features of KiLLER Baud - © Me me me and nobody else! :)"\n                           .--------------------------.\n                           |  Features of KiLLER Baud |\n                           `--------------------------''\n\n   ­ Create two pages of statistical data in one bulletin\n     (With a ''Space to Resume'' between them ofcourse)\n\n   ­ Create two pages of statistical data divided over 2 bulletins\n     (So two separate files)\n     \n   ­ Create either one of the two possible bulletins.\n     (You can choose between @{"Baud Usage" link BaudUsage} and @{"Last Bauds" link LastBauds})\n\n   ­ Only show the output on screen, don''t create the bulletins.\n     (Two pages, not selectable).\n\n   Other program features are:\n\n   · Fast baud detecting, virtually no delay for users.\n   · Very small data file to create bulletins\n   · You can remove baud rates from the bulletin(s).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Install "How to Install KiLLER Baud - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC (that''s me)"\n                      .------------------------------.\n                      |  How to install KiLLER Baud  |\n                      `------------------------------''\n\n   You are free to call the programs whatever you want and you can locate\n   the  program  wherever  you want.  I hate programs that have hardcoded\n   paths in their source.\n\n   The KiLLER_Baud executable has got a double function:  update the data\n   file  and create the bulletin(s).  Depending on available tooltypes in\n   the command icon, KiLLER Baud will know what to do.\n\n   ­­ Baud Detection ­­\n\n   First  of  all,  make  sure  that  KiLLER_Baud is run at least once to\n   update the data file.  I suggest you install it as an MCI command in a\n   bulletin  that  is  shown  to  every  user  that logs in (or logs out,\n   whatever fits you best).\n\n   · Example ·\n\n   ~CC_KB-DETECT\n   \n   · End ·\n\n   To  have  KiLLER_Baud  only  update the data file, create an icon that\n   holds  the tooltype "DETECT_BAUD".  If this tooltype is present in the\n   icon with what KiLLER_Baud was started, the data file will be updated.\n   This data file will reside in the same directory as the program itself\n   and will  be called "KiLLER_Baud.Data" and is 484 bytes in size.  Note\n   that  the  bulletin(s)  will not be created!  An example icon has been\n   provided called "KB-DETECT.info".\n   \n   Important tooltypes for that command are:\n   \n   DETECT_BAUD             ­ This tooltype MUST be present if you want KB\n                             to update the data file.\n                             \n   LOCAL_NODE=<number>     ­ KiLLER_Baud  will  not add your local logins\n                             to its data file.  Normally,  the local node\n                             would be node 0, but certain boards that run\n                             /X-Fido  for example have their last node as\n                             their local node.  So with this tooltype you\n                             can specify which node is the local node.\n                             \n                             You  can  also specify a node which does not\n                             exist,  and  your local logins will be added\n                             to the data file.\n                             \n                             Default value is node 0.\n\n   ­­ Bulletin Creation ­­\n\n   Unless  you  want  to install KiLLER_Baud as a regular /X command that\n   can  be  started  from  the  main  prompt,  you  also  need to install\n   KiLLER_Baud  as  another  MCI command, but this time to have it create\n   the bulletin(s).\n\n   You  can  specify the name(s) of the bulletin(s) with tooltypes.  Here\n   are the important tooltypes for this command:\n\n   BULL=<filename>         ­ When specified, one bulletin will be created\n                             containing 2 pages of statistics,  separated\n                             by a "Space to Resume".\n\n   BULL.1=<filename>       ­ This  will  result in one bulletin that only\n                             contains the @{"Baud Usage" link BaudUsage} information.\n\n   BULL.2=<filename>       ­ This  induces KiLLER_Baud to create only the\n                             @{"Last Bauds" link LastBauds} bulletin and nothing more.\n\n   NOTE¹ : The `BULL'' tooltype overrides both BULL.1 and BULL.2 !!\n\n   NOTE² : If you ommit all of these tooltypes, KiLLER_Baud will show the\n           bulletins on screen and will not create any bulletin.\n           \n   If  you  wish,  you  can  have  certain  baud  rates  removed from the\n   bulletins.   Normally,  the  bulletin would contain baud rates ranging\n   from  SLOW  to FAST, whereas SLOW means all baud rates below 9600 baud\n   and  FAST all baudrates above the highest speed available. Please note\n   that both the SLOW and FAST baud rates will not be put in the bulletin\n   until they are actually used.\n\n   For  example,  there  is  a BBS somewhere in Finland that has no 12000\n   baud users.  As strange as it is, this was true.  But the bulletin did\n   contain  the  12000  baud  line.  It was much disliked by the sysop of\n   that  board.   It even got to a point where he get himself drunk, felt\n   bad (a la Michael Jackson) and called The Undiscovered BBS.\n\n   There  he  wrote me a message saying that he hated the 12000 baud line\n   and  that  it  should  be removed.  I made him clear that although his\n   board  did  not  have 12000 baud users, the Undiscovered has got them!\n   He felt like an ass and left crying.  I felt so bad about this, that I\n   made it possible to leave out any baudrate you like.  However, this is\n   purely  COSMETIC.  The 12000 connects are still being counted and they\n   are  also used for the total number of connects.  But it was the least\n   I could do for such a lonely guy (snif).\n\n   So  without telling you the name of the sysop (QUARTZ QUARTZ QUARTZ!),\n   this is how to disable baudrates:\n\n   NO_SLOW     - Disable  SLOW baudrate\n   NO_9600     - Disable  9600 baudrate\n   NO_12000    - Disable 12000 baudrate (see! :) )\n   NO_14400    - Disable 14400 baudrate\n   ...\n   NO_FAST     - Disable FAST baudrate\n   \n   These  tooltypes  can be put in the K-Baud tooltype and remember, they\n   are  purely  cosmetic,  if  you  disable all baudrates and look at the\n   bulletin,  there  will  be NO baudrates mentioned, but there will be a\n   total number of connects and a favourite baudrate.\n\n   ­­ Regular /X Command ­­\n\n   If  you want it to install like a regular command, install it like any\n   other  command but make sure you remove the BULL#?  tooltypes from the\n   icon or it will create bulletins instead of showing them.\n\n   ­­ Overview ­­\n\n   ONCE AGAIN (but in short):\n   \n      · K-Baud can either create bulletins or display them pronto\n      · To make it create bulletins, either put the BULL tooltype\n        or BULL.x tooltypes in the icon (NOT BOTH!).\n      · To disable baudrates use tooltype: NO_<baudrate>\n      · If  you  remove all BULL* tooltypes from the K-Baud icon,\n        K-Baud will show the results on screen right away.\n\n   ­­ Reset the counting ­­\n\n   If  you  want  to reset the counting, simply remove the data file.  It\n   will  detect itself that the file is gone and create a new one (with a\n   new starting date).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE LastBauds "Example Output: Last Baud (Bull.2) - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n.-----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER BAUD v1.4  ^  <> Baud Rate Statistical View <>  ^  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC! |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------.--------------------------.---------------------------------.\n| Connect String | Date/Time last Connect   | Last Connect made by            |\n:----------------:--------------------------:---------------------------------:\n|   9.600 baud   | Fri Dec 16 12:14:45 1994 | ArCaDe/MYSTiC                   |\n|  12.000 baud   | Never                    | Nobody                          |\n|  14.400 baud   | Sun Dec 18 19:35:31 1994 | Kerstman                        |\n|  16.800 baud   | Sat Dec 17 09:03:12 1994 | Sal-One                         |\n|  19.200 baud   | Sun Dec 18 15:39:33 1994 | Incognito/MST                   |\n|  21.600 baud   | Sun Dec 18 11:32:50 1994 | WiSHBRiNGER                     |\n|  24.000 baud   | Fri Dec 16 23:11:22 1994 | skate BOB                       |\n|  26.400 baud   | Sun Dec 18 19:59:58 1994 | Motive Power/MST                |\n|  28.800 baud   | Sun Dec 18 02:13:26 1994 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC                |\n:----------------^--------------------------^---------------------------------:\n| Total Connects :  64041 ^ Favourite speed: 21.600 baud ^ Since: 16-Dec-1994 |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n                     This is bulletin 2 (BULL.2 tooltype).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE BaudUsage "Example Output: Baud Usage (Bull.1) - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n.-----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER BAUD v1.4  ^  <> Baud Rate Statistical View <>  ^  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC! |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------.----------------------.-------.-------.---------------------.\n| Connect String | 0%--------------100% | Total | Perc% | Overall             |\n:----------------:----------------------:-------:-------:---------------------:\n|   9.600 baud   | ####                 |    52 |    5% | Doing fine          |\n|  12.000 baud   | ######               |    86 |    9% | Popular rate        |\n|  14.400 baud   | #########            |   120 |   13% | Steady size.        |\n|  16.800 baud   | ############         |   154 |   17% | Substantial         |\n|  19.200 baud   | #                    |    17 |    1% | Among others        |\n|  21.600 baud   | #####                |    66 |    7% | Steady basis        |\n|  24.000 baud   | ####                 |    59 |    6% | Steady basis        |\n|  26.400 baud   | #################### |   255 |   29% | THE baud rate!      |\n|  28.800 baud   | ##                   |    34 |    3% | Doing fine          |\n:----------------^----------------------^-------^-------^---------------------:\n| Total Connects :    867 ^ Favourite speed: 26.400 baud ^ Since: 16-Dec-1994 |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n   The  graphical percentages shown are a RELATIVE comparison between the\n   most used baud rates and the rest.\n\n   Both  the  `Slower''  and  `Faster'' baudrate are ONLY shown when it has\n   been actually used by someone.\n\n                   (And yes, this output is a fraud :) )\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE History "History of KiLLER Baud - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                          .--------------------------.\n                          |  History of KiLLER Baud  |\n                          `--------------------------''\n\n   \n   1.4.0    02-Feb-95   Fixed       After spending two hours with on the\n                                    with Muad''Dib, we finally discovered\n                                    that I forgot to put the tooltype\n                                    TYPE=XIM in the icon that I delivered\n                                    in the package. This caused quite\n                                    some problems on some boards, but not\n                                    on mine ;) But we found it, and I\n                                    fixed it so it works again.\n                                    *Reported by Muad''Dib*\n\n   1.3.1    02-Feb-95   Fixed       I misplaced the code from the KiLLER\n                                    detect program causing two errors ;(\n                                    This has been fixed, sorry dudes.\n                                    *Detected by NightShade & Motive Power*\n\n   1.3.0    05-Jan-95   Improved    Put the code to update the data file\n                                    into the main KiLLER_Baud program to\n                                    save space. This saved about 14KB.\n\n   1.2.2    04-Jan-95   NEW         You can now have baud rates removed\n                                    from the bulletin with the tooltypes:\n                                    NO_<baudrate>.\n                                    *Cried for by Quartz/MST*\n\n   1.2.1    25-Dec-94   Improved    Added two more checks that prevent a\n                                    division by zero (guru #80000005).\n\n   1.2.0    21-Dec-94   Improved    The baud rates "Slower" and "Faster" are\n                                    now only shown when they are actually in\n                                    use. It''s a cosmetic change.\n                                    *Requested by Muad''Dib*\n\n   1.1.0    20-Dec-94   Improved    The graphical percentage bar is now made\n                                    relative to the most used baud rate for\n                                    better representation.\n\n   1.0.0    18-Dec-94   Release     Muad''Dib got the first version to test.\n\n   1.0.0    18-Dec-94   NEW         Created KiLLER Baud.\n\n                                    -*-\n\n                                    OBSOLETE! Put the code into the\n                                    main executable (saving about 14KB).\n\n   1.2.1    25-Dec-94   Improved    When the data file is first created,\n                                    all variables will be cleared now on\n                                    a more firm basis.\n\n   1.2.0    21-Dec-94   Improved    Made it work with the new structure and\n                                    revised it to 1.2.0 for compatibility\n                                    reasons.\n\n   1.0.1    18-Dec-94   Fixed       KiLLER Detect now detects the line speed\n                                    and not the speed between modem and\n                                    computer (sigh).\n                                    Reported by: Muad''Dib/DLT\n\n   1.0.0    16-Dec-94   NEW         Created KiLLER Detect.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Greetings "Aloha! - By KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                                .-------------.\n                                |  Greetings  |\n                                `-------------''\n\n   Muad''Dib/DLT/iFT     Let''s crash your machine! Moehaha! :)\n   \n   Quartz/MYSTiC        You cry baby! :)\n\n   Motive Power/MST     28k8 beats 26k4 bud... ;)\n   \n   I-GO/Perseus         To bad you weren''t at the O22VW\n   \n   Zed                  I hope to contact you soon!\n   \n   Jake/Prestige        One day I will come and leech, don''t worry :)\n   \n   Tycoon/MYSTiC        It was fun, but to all good things come an end\n\n   Shuyuki Tanaka       Try to contact me at my Internet address! Japan\n                        is very very very cool!\n\n                      ...and to all my other good friends!\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE KiLLERComment "KiLLER-WHAT? - © 1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n         «« THIS IS AN EXCERPT OUT OF THE GUIDE OF KILLER COMMENT ««\n\n                         .---------------------------.\n                         |  What is KiLLER Comment?  |\n                         `---------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER Comment is number 1001 in the row of a [C]omment doors.  But this\n   is not just *a* comment door, otherwise I wouldn''t have made it.  I have\n   some profound reasons.\n\n   Despite  the  fact  that  there  must be over a 1000 different [C]omment\n   doors,  there  is not one comment door that suits my needs.  I have seen\n   plenty of them but to me they all look ugly, have static interfaces, are\n   as flexible as a log of wood and generally suck :-).\n\n   With  this mind I''ve tried to make KiLLER Comment as smart and pretty as\n   I  can  and  I  feel I have succeeded.  I have never seen a comment door\n   that  works  as  simple  as  this  one  with  a fair amount of so-called\n   ''intelligence'' and comes with a nice GUI.\n\n   What  is  this  ''intelligence'' I speak about you say?  Well, all comment\n   doors  sofar  weren''t  able  to see that if I typed "c kill" I wanted to\n   comment to "KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" nor where they able to see that if I typed\n   "c  amiga" I wanted to comment to the Amiga co-sysop.  KiLLER Comment is\n   able to detect this!\n\n   Another  thing  is  security.   Every  new  user  was  able to see which\n   cosysops  you  had,  but  by giving every cosysop a security level, they\n   will  not see their names/jobs.  And if there is only one (co)sysop they\n   are  allowed  to  see,  they  will  not  even  see it is done via KiLLER\n   Comment.\n\n   Also,  per user in the [C]omment list there is a check wether he/she has\n   got access to the conference that KiLLER [C]omment is called from, so it\n   is  impossible  to write a message to someone who doesn''t have access to\n   that conference.\n\n   What I personally also dislike are comment doors that only fill like 25%\n   of  your  screen.   Not  that  you  should put a lot of nonsense in your\n   comment  door,  no,  but  KiLLER  Comment puts an extra line between the\n   names  of  the  (co-)sysops  if  that''s possible with the user''s maximum\n   numbers  of  lines  he  can  handle  on his screen.  It''s these kinds of\n   things I miss in those 13-in-a-dozen [C]omment doors.  That''s why I made\n   KiLLER Comment.\n\n   And  oh, just like I first introduced cursor control in ConfScan, KiLLER\n   comment also uses cursor control for selecting a user you can write to.\n\n                             »» END OF EXCERPT ««\n\n   To get hold of this tool, simply @{"contact" link Contact} me.\n\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),'{"TYPE":"XIM"}','auto-added',34913,1,0,NULL,NULL,'klrbau14','scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('klrbauds','KLRBAUDS.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KLRBAUDS.LHA','Baud','XIM','Baud',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,replace('Killer Baud Source Code for Amiexpress\n','\n',char(10)),'KiLLER_Baud.Guide',replace('@DATABASE "KiLLER-Comment.GUiDE"\n@REMARK	.-------------------------------------------------------.\n@REMARK  | This document should be read with AmigaGuide (OS 2.x) |\n@REMARK  | or MultiView (OS 3.x) or any other guide reader. Tnx. |\n@REMARK  `-------------------------------------------------------''\n@REMARK	$VER: KiLLER Baud 1.5.0\n@WIDTH 80\n@NODE MAIN "KiLLER Comment - MYSTiC 1994 - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             Guide to KiLLER Baud\n                 for Ami-Express 3.38+ Bulletin Board Systems\n\n                             Program version: 1.5.0\n                Compilation Date/Time: Thu Jun  1 15:45:51 1995\n\n                         © 1994/1995 by @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n\n                   ... another fine MYSTiC /X production ...\n\n                        @{"     UPDATE INFORMATION     " link Update}\n\n      @{" First words... " link WhatIs}    What is KiLLER Baud\n      @{" Features       " link Features}    What can it do?\n      @{" Installation   " link Install}    How to install KiLLER Baud on your system\n      @{" Example 1      " link BaudUsage}    Example output of bulletin 1\n      @{" Example 2      " link LastBauds}    Example output on bulletin 2\n      @{" History        " link History}    History of KiLLER Baud\n      @{" Greetings      " link Greetings}    Big hellos and messages\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Contact "How to contact KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC - © Me me me! :)"\n                            .--------------------.\n                            |  Contacting me...  |\n                            `--------------------''\n\n       For FAST response (preferred) please contact me on Internet:\n\n                               [email removed]\n\n                    ( [email removed] -- backup )\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                 I can be reached on the following boards:\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                The Undiscovered       +31-2294-1442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-4442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-tRY! (USR 28k8)\n\n                Last Outpost           +31-72-201182 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-72-157682 (USR 28k8)\n\n         I know, you have to call The Netherlands for it, but it''s\n            the only boards I call on a frequent basis, sorry.\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n        You can also find me on IRC once in a while on the channels\n                            #amigascne and #rave\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatIs "What is KiLLER Baud - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                        .------------------------.\n                        |  What is KiLLER Baud?  |\n                        `------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Baud is (another) statistical mumbo jumbo door.  It is able to\n   create  some  nice  statistics  about  the baud rate your users use to\n   logon your system.  It is styled in the same way as @{"KiLLER Comment" link KiLLERComment}, so\n   if  you  are familiar with that door, you will know that the output of\n   it will be fair enough :-)\n\n   I  made  this  door  as a simple diversion of some other projects I am\n   working  on.   This  door  was created in two days and I don''t feel it\n   should  be  released  to  the  public  because  it is too simple to be\n   released.   The  idea  is not my own, I saw another ''accumulative baud\n   report''  door  and  I  am  still  recovering from the output that door\n   creates.   I  don''t  know where some people get their inspiration from\n   but that output reminded me of kindergarten ;-)\n\n   And  because  of  the  nice  respons  I  got from some sysops about my\n   previous  releases,  I  thought  it  would  be  nice  to  make another\n   statistical  door,  sysops can''t get enough of BBS statistics.\n\n   Anyway,  I hope you like it and if you do, please give me a yell.\n\n                                                         @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Features "Features of KiLLER Baud - © Me me me and nobody else! :)"\n                           .--------------------------.\n                           |  Features of KiLLER Baud |\n                           `--------------------------''\n\n   ­ Create two pages of statistical data in one bulletin\n     (With a ''Space to Resume'' between them ofcourse)\n\n   ­ Create two pages of statistical data divided over 2 bulletins\n     (So two separate files)\n     \n   ­ Create either one of the two possible bulletins.\n     (You can choose between @{"Baud Usage" link BaudUsage} and @{"Last Bauds" link LastBauds})\n\n   ­ Only show the output on screen, don''t create the bulletins.\n     (Two pages, not selectable).\n\n   Other program features are:\n\n   · Fast baud detecting, virtually no delay for users.\n   · Very small data file to create bulletins\n   · You can remove baud rates from the bulletin(s).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Install "How to Install KiLLER Baud - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC (that''s me)"\n                      .------------------------------.\n                      |  How to install KiLLER Baud  |\n                      `------------------------------''\n\n   You are free to call the programs whatever you want and you can locate\n   the  program  wherever  you want.  I hate programs that have hardcoded\n   paths in their source.\n\n   The KiLLER_Baud executable has got a double function:  update the data\n   file  and create the bulletin(s).  Depending on available tooltypes in\n   the command icon, KiLLER Baud will know what to do.\n\n   ­­ Baud Detection ­­\n\n   First  of  all,  make  sure  that  KiLLER_Baud is run at least once to\n   update the data file.  I suggest you install it as an MCI command in a\n   bulletin  that  is  shown  to  every  user  that logs in (or logs out,\n   whatever fits you best).\n\n   · Example ·\n\n   ~CC_KB-DETECT\n   \n   · End ·\n\n   To  have  KiLLER_Baud  only  update the data file, create an icon that\n   holds  the tooltype "DETECT_BAUD".  If this tooltype is present in the\n   icon with what KiLLER_Baud was started, the data file will be updated.\n   This data file will reside in the same directory as the program itself\n   and will  be called "KiLLER_Baud.Data" and is 484 bytes in size.  Note\n   that  the  bulletin(s)  will not be created!  An example icon has been\n   provided called "KB-DETECT.info".\n   \n   Important tooltypes for that command are:\n   \n   DETECT_BAUD             ­ This tooltype MUST be present if you want KB\n                             to update the data file.\n                             \n   LOCAL_NODE=<number>     ­ KiLLER_Baud  will  not add your local logins\n                             to its data file.  Normally,  the local node\n                             would be node 0, but certain boards that run\n                             /X-Fido  for example have their last node as\n                             their local node.  So with this tooltype you\n                             can specify which node is the local node.\n                             \n                             You  can  also specify a node which does not\n                             exist,  and  your local logins will be added\n                             to the data file.\n                             \n                             Default value is node 0.\n\n   ­­ Bulletin Creation ­­\n\n   Unless  you  want  to install KiLLER_Baud as a regular /X command that\n   can  be  started  from  the  main  prompt,  you  also  need to install\n   KiLLER_Baud  as  another  MCI command, but this time to have it create\n   the bulletin(s).\n\n   You  can  specify the name(s) of the bulletin(s) with tooltypes.  Here\n   are the important tooltypes for this command:\n\n   BULL=<filename>         ­ When specified, one bulletin will be created\n                             containing 2 pages of statistics,  separated\n                             by a "Space to Resume".\n\n   BULL.1=<filename>       ­ This  will  result in one bulletin that only\n                             contains the @{"Baud Usage" link BaudUsage} information.\n\n   BULL.2=<filename>       ­ This  induces KiLLER_Baud to create only the\n                             @{"Last Bauds" link LastBauds} bulletin and nothing more.\n\n   NOTE¹ : The `BULL'' tooltype overrides both BULL.1 and BULL.2 !!\n\n   NOTE² : If you ommit all of these tooltypes, KiLLER_Baud will show the\n           bulletins on screen and will not create any bulletin.\n           \n   If  you  wish,  you  can  have  certain  baud  rates  removed from the\n   bulletins.   Normally,  the  bulletin would contain baud rates ranging\n   from  SLOW  to FAST, whereas SLOW means all baud rates below 9600 baud\n   and  FAST all baudrates above the highest speed available. Please note\n   that both the SLOW and FAST baud rates will not be put in the bulletin\n   until they are actually used.\n\n   For  example,  there  is  a BBS somewhere in Finland that has no 12000\n   baud users.  As strange as it is, this was true.  But the bulletin did\n   contain  the  12000  baud  line.  It was much disliked by the sysop of\n   that  board.   It even got to a point where he get himself drunk, felt\n   bad (a la Michael Jackson) and called The Undiscovered BBS.\n\n   There  he  wrote me a message saying that he hated the 12000 baud line\n   and  that  it  should  be removed.  I made him clear that although his\n   board  did  not  have 12000 baud users, the Undiscovered has got them!\n   He felt like an ass and left crying.  I felt so bad about this, that I\n   made it possible to leave out any baudrate you like.  However, this is\n   purely  COSMETIC.  The 12000 connects are still being counted and they\n   are  also used for the total number of connects.  But it was the least\n   I could do for such a lonely guy (snif).\n\n   So  without telling you the name of the sysop (QUARTZ QUARTZ QUARTZ!),\n   this is how to disable baudrates:\n\n   NO_SLOW     - Disable  SLOW baudrate\n   NO_9600     - Disable  9600 baudrate\n   NO_12000    - Disable 12000 baudrate (see! :) )\n   NO_14400    - Disable 14400 baudrate\n   ...\n   NO_FAST     - Disable FAST baudrate\n   \n   These  tooltypes  can be put in the K-Baud tooltype and remember, they\n   are  purely  cosmetic,  if  you  disable all baudrates and look at the\n   bulletin,  there  will  be NO baudrates mentioned, but there will be a\n   total number of connects and a favourite baudrate.\n\n   ­­ Regular /X Command ­­\n\n   If  you want it to install like a regular command, install it like any\n   other  command but make sure you remove the BULL#?  tooltypes from the\n   icon or it will create bulletins instead of showing them.\n\n   ­­ Overview ­­\n\n   ONCE AGAIN (but in short):\n   \n      · K-Baud can either create bulletins or display them pronto\n      · To make it create bulletins, either put the BULL tooltype\n        or BULL.x tooltypes in the icon (NOT BOTH!).\n      · To disable baudrates use tooltype: NO_<baudrate>\n      · If  you  remove all BULL* tooltypes from the K-Baud icon,\n        K-Baud will show the results on screen right away.\n\n   ­­ Reset the counting ­­\n\n   If  you  want  to reset the counting, simply remove the data file.  It\n   will  detect itself that the file is gone and create a new one (with a\n   new starting date).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE LastBauds "Example Output: Last Baud (Bull.2) - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n.-----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER BAUD v1.5  ^  <> Baud Rate Statistical View <>  ^  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC! |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------.--------------------------.---------------------------------.\n| Connect String | Date/Time last Connect   | Last Connect made by            |\n:----------------:--------------------------:---------------------------------:\n|  Slower baud   | Sat May 27 10:56:37 1995 | celtic                          |\n|   9.600 baud   | Thu Jun 01 21:49:11 1995 | Alf                             |\n|  12.000 baud   | Thu Jun 01 17:51:58 1995 | wHIRLWIND                       |\n|  14.400 baud   | Thu Jun 01 18:00:15 1995 | yhatware                        |\n|  16.800 baud   | Thu Jun 01 16:45:12 1995 | UFOk                            |\n|  19.200 baud   | Thu Jun 01 20:46:07 1995 | D`sTyLE                         |\n|  21.600 baud   | Fri Jun 02 16:02:02 1995 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC!               |\n|  24.000 baud   | Thu Jun 01 01:32:36 1995 | I-GO                            |\n|  26.400 baud   | Thu Jun 01 21:26:36 1995 | Arlette                         |\n|  28.800 baud   | Thu Jun 01 17:39:38 1995 | [MEGA^FORTRESS]                 |\n|  31.200 baud   | Thu Jun 01 17:56:02 1995 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC                |\n|  33.600 baud   | Fri Jun  2 16:03:29 1995 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC                |\n|  Faster baud   | Thu Jun 01 18:00:36 1995 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC                |\n:----------------^--------------------------^---------------------------------:\n| Total Connects :  14352 ^ Favourite speed: 14.400 baud ^ Since: 25-Dec-1994 |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n                     This is bulletin 2 (BULL.2 tooltype).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE BaudUsage "Example Output: Baud Usage (Bull.1) - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n.-----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER BAUD v1.5  ^  <> Baud Rate Statistical View <>  ^  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC! |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------.----------------------.-------.-------.---------------------.\n| Connect String | 0%--------------100% | Total | Perc% | Overall             |\n:----------------:----------------------:-------:-------:---------------------:\n|  Slower baud   |                      |    75 |    0% | Private             |\n|   9.600 baud   |                      |   127 |    0% | Private             |\n|  12.000 baud   |                      |    50 |    0% | Hardly used         |\n|  14.400 baud   | #################### |  5724 |   39% | The Ruler!          |\n|  16.800 baud   | ###                  |   869 |    6% | Used by few         |\n|  19.200 baud   | ##                   |   728 |    5% | Used by few         |\n|  21.600 baud   | ####                 |  1424 |    9% | Not too bad         |\n|  24.000 baud   | ##                   |   656 |    4% | Used by few         |\n|  26.400 baud   | ######               |  1975 |   13% | Popular rate        |\n|  28.800 baud   | #########            |  2704 |   18% | Much liked          |\n|  31.200 baud   |                      |     2 |    0% | Hardly used         |\n|  33.600 baud   |                      |     5 |    0% | Hardly used         |\n|  Faster baud   |                      |    13 |    0% | Hardly used         |\n:----------------^----------------------^-------^-------^---------------------:\n| Total Connects :  14352 ^ Favourite speed: 14.400 baud ^ Since: 25-Dec-1994 |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n   The  graphical percentages shown are a RELATIVE comparison between the\n   most used baud rates and the rest.\n\n   Both  the  `Slower''  and  `Faster'' baudrate are ONLY shown when it has\n   been actually used by someone.\n\n                   (And yes, this output is a fraud :) )\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE History "History of KiLLER Baud - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                      -//- History of KiLLER Baud -//-\n   \n   1.5.0    01-Jun-95   Improved    33.600 baud is recognized.\n\n            01-Jun-95   Improved    Changed the texts in KiLLER Baud.\n\n   1.4.0    02-Feb-95   Fixed       After spending two hours with on the\n                                    with Muad''Dib, we finally discovered\n                                    that I forgot to put the tooltype\n                                    TYPE=XIM in the icon that I delivered\n                                    in the package. This caused quite\n                                    some problems on some boards, but not\n                                    on mine ;) But we found it, and I\n                                    fixed it so it works again.\n                                    *Reported by Muad''Dib*\n\n   1.3.1    02-Feb-95   Fixed       I misplaced the code from the KiLLER\n                                    detect program causing two errors ;(\n                                    This has been fixed, sorry dudes.\n                                    *Detected by NightShade & Motive Power*\n\n   1.3.0    05-Jan-95   Improved    Put the code to update the data file\n                                    into the main KiLLER_Baud program to\n                                    save space. This saved about 14KB.\n\n   1.2.2    04-Jan-95   NEW         You can now have baud rates removed\n                                    from the bulletin with the tooltypes:\n                                    NO_<baudrate>.\n                                    *Cried for by Quartz/MST*\n\n   1.2.1    25-Dec-94   Improved    Added two more checks that prevent a\n                                    division by zero (guru #80000005).\n\n   1.2.0    21-Dec-94   Improved    The baud rates "Slower" and "Faster" are\n                                    now only shown when they are actually in\n                                    use. It''s a cosmetic change.\n                                    *Requested by Muad''Dib*\n\n   1.1.0    20-Dec-94   Improved    The graphical percentage bar is now made\n                                    relative to the most used baud rate for\n                                    better representation.\n\n   1.0.0    18-Dec-94   Release     Muad''Dib got the first version to test.\n\n   1.0.0    18-Dec-94   NEW         Created KiLLER Baud.\n\n                                    -*-\n\n                                    OBSOLETE! Put the code into the\n                                    main executable (saving about 14KB).\n\n   1.2.1    25-Dec-94   Improved    When the data file is first created,\n                                    all variables will be cleared now on\n                                    a more firm basis.\n\n   1.2.0    21-Dec-94   Improved    Made it work with the new structure and\n                                    revised it to 1.2.0 for compatibility\n                                    reasons.\n\n   1.0.1    18-Dec-94   Fixed       KiLLER Detect now detects the line speed\n                                    and not the speed between modem and\n                                    computer (sigh).\n                                    Reported by: Muad''Dib/DLT\n\n   1.0.0    16-Dec-94   NEW         Created KiLLER Detect.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Greetings "Aloha! - By KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                                .-------------.\n                                |  Greetings  |\n                                `-------------''\n\n   Muad''Dib/MST/DLT     USR sure as hell rulez!\n\n   UFOk/MST^LSD^PSG     Trying to break the world-record? ;)\n\n   QuartZ/MYSTiC        Let''s get it going! Intro''s!\n\n   Motive Power/MST     33k6 beats 26k4 bud... ;)\n   \n   I-GO^MDMA            0.25 is not enough for energy.\n   \n   Zed                  I hope to contact you soon!\n   \n                      ...and to all my other good friends!\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE KiLLERComment "KiLLER-WHAT? - © 1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n         «« THIS IS AN EXCERPT OUT OF THE GUIDE OF KILLER COMMENT ««\n\n                     -//- What is KiLLER Comment? -//-\n\n   KiLLER Comment is number 1001 in the row of a [C]omment doors.  But this\n   is not just *a* comment door, otherwise I wouldn''t have made it.  I have\n   some profound reasons.\n\n   Despite  the  fact  that  there  must be over a 1000 different [C]omment\n   doors,  there  is not one comment door that suits my needs.  I have seen\n   plenty of them but to me they all look ugly, have static interfaces, are\n   as flexible as a log of wood and generally suck :-).\n\n   With  this mind I''ve tried to make KiLLER Comment as smart and pretty as\n   I  can  and  I  feel I have succeeded.  I have never seen a comment door\n   that  works  as  simple  as  this  one  with  a fair amount of so-called\n   ''intelligence'' and comes with a nice GUI.\n\n   What  is  this  ''intelligence'' I speak about you say?  Well, all comment\n   doors  sofar  weren''t  able  to see that if I typed "c kill" I wanted to\n   comment to "KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" nor where they able to see that if I typed\n   "c  amiga" I wanted to comment to the Amiga co-sysop.  KiLLER Comment is\n   able to detect this!\n\n   Another  thing  is  security.   Every  new  user  was  able to see which\n   cosysops  you  had,  but  by giving every cosysop a security level, they\n   will  not see their names/jobs.  And if there is only one (co)sysop they\n   are  allowed  to  see,  they  will  not  even  see it is done via KiLLER\n   Comment.\n\n   Also,  per user in the [C]omment list there is a check wether he/she has\n   got access to the conference that KiLLER [C]omment is called from, so it\n   is  impossible  to write a message to someone who doesn''t have access to\n   that conference.\n\n   What I personally also dislike are comment doors that only fill like 25%\n   of  your  screen.   Not  that  you  should put a lot of nonsense in your\n   comment  door,  no,  but  KiLLER  Comment puts an extra line between the\n   names  of  the  (co-)sysops  if  that''s possible with the user''s maximum\n   numbers  of  lines  he  can  handle  on his screen.  It''s these kinds of\n   things I miss in those 13-in-a-dozen [C]omment doors.  That''s why I made\n   KiLLER Comment.\n\n   And  oh, just like I first introduced cursor control in ConfScan, KiLLER\n   comment also uses cursor control for selecting a user you can write to.\n\n                             »» END OF EXCERPT ««\n\n   To get hold of this tool, simply @{"contact" link Contact} me.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Update "Update Information - USR Rules Again!"\n                        -//-  Update Information -//-\n\n   Today  (June  1st,  1995)  US  Robotics  surprised  the  scene with an\n   unexpected  present:   USR modems with a flash rom can update to v.34+\n   allowing  speeds  upto  33.600  baud  instead of the 28.800 baud other\n   modems have as a maximum.  Since I own one myself I love it :)\n\n   This  ofcourse  asked  for  a quick update of KiLLER Baud, because you\n   want  those 33.600 connects showing in your bulletin!  I thought I had\n   outsmarted  USR  by  already  recognizing  31.200 baud in the previous\n   version,  but  I had not expected 33k6.  But I''m not complaining here,\n   hell no, I love 4000CPS on LHA files :)\n\n   So all this update does extra is recognize 33k6 connects.\n\n   Because  of  the  new data format involved, I''ve included a very small\n   util  to update your KiLLER_Baud.Data.  All you have to do is start it\n   in  the same directory as where your data file resides.  A backup file\n   will be created by it!\n\n   AFTER  YOU  HAVE  DONE THAT, you can start using the new version.  All\n   you  have  to  do  is copy the new executable over the old version and\n   you''re done.\n\n   That''s  all about this update, if you have any suggestions, questions,\n   bug-reports, feel free to @{"contact" link Contact} me.\n\n                                                      -KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC-\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),'{"TYPE":"XIM"}','auto-added',48850,1,0,NULL,NULL,'klrbauds','scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('klrchats','KLRCHATS.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KLRCHATS.LHA','Chat-O-Meter','XIM','Chat-O-Meter',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,replace('Killer Chat Source Code for Amiexpress\n','\n',char(10)),'Chat-O-Meter.GUIDE',replace('@DATABASE "Chat-O-Meter.GUiDE"\n@REMARK  .-------------------------------------------------------.\n@REMARK  | This document should be read with AmigaGuide (OS 2.x) |\n@REMARK  | or MultiView (OS 3.x) or any other guide reader. Tnx. |\n@REMARK  `-------------------------------------------------------''\n@REMARK  $VER: Chat-O-Meter 2.2.3\n@WIDTH 80\n@NODE MAIN "Chat-O-Meter - MYSTiC ''94/''95 - © ''94/''95 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            Guide to Chat-O-Meter\n                 for Ami-Express 3.38+ Bulletin Board Systems\n\n                            Program version: 2.2.3\n               Compilation Date/Time: Mon Jun 26 21:50:06 1995\n\n                        © 1994/1995 by @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n\n                   ... another fine MYSTiC /X production ...\n\n                       @{"       UPDATE iNFORMATiON        " link WhatsNew}\n\n      @{" Welcome Sysop! " link WhatIs}    What is Chat-O-Meter?\n      @{" Features       " link Features}    What can it do?\n      @{" Installation   " link Install}    How to install Chat-O-Meter\n      @{" Conversion     " link Conversion}    How to convert your old 1.x bulletin to 2.x\n\n      @{" Example 1      " link BullExample}    Example output the bulletin of Chat-O-Meter\n      @{" Example 2      " link ExitChatExample}    Example output when you exit a chat\n\n      @{" History        " link History}    History of Chat-O-Meter\n      @{" Greetings      " link Greetings}    Greetings, messages and contact info\n      @{" Troubles?      " link Troubles}    Trouble-shooting\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatIs "What is Chat-O-Meter - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                          - -----------------------.\n                         |  What Is Chat-O-Meter?  |\n                         `----------------------- -\n\n   It  started out as a tool to keep track of the time you spent chatting\n   with  your users and to be able to make a nice looking @{"bulletin" link BullExample} out of\n   that  information.   But  things  have  changed, not only does it keep\n   track  of  the  time  the sysop spends chatting, it also registers the\n   time every user ever spent chatting with you, the sysop, in a separate\n   data  file.   So  for every user there is information available on how\n   long  s/he  chatted with you and how many times s/he chatted with you.\n\n   Needless to say that it''s also possible to create a full ANSI bulletin\n   based upon the users'' data file.\n\n   At  the  end  of  every  chat  you  will  get  to see a banner holding\n   information  about  your  latest  chat  and  accumulative  info  about\n   previous  chats.   At  your  request,  it  can  also  show  the users''\n   accumulated information.\n\n   Together  with  Chat-O-Top  you  are  able  to  create a wide range of\n   bulletins  from  the  data  gathered while you chat.  Examples of such\n   bulletins are:\n\n   · Top 10 of the longest chats ever.\n   · Top xx of the users that have chatted the longest/sportest with you.\n   · Top xx of the users that have chatted the most/least with you.\n\n   How  will  this  affect  your  chats  you say?  Well, I think you will\n   hardly  notice  a difference since the creation and/or updating of the\n   data  files  is  done  in  a  most  efficient way with as less fuzz as\n   possible.   Creation  of  the  Top Ten Longest Chats only takes upto 1\n   second.\n\n                                ** NOTICE **\n\n   Chat-O-Meter  is  giftware,  this means that you can use it as much as\n   you  like,  where  you like, dressed as you like and how you like, but\n   YOU MAY NOT SELL IT IN ANY WAY!  Permission is granted for distribtion\n   on AmiNet.\n\n                                ** NOTICE **\n\n                                   Enjoy!\n\n                                                        @{"-KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatsNew "What''s New - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                           - --------------------.\n                          |  New in version 2.2  |\n                          `-------------------- -\n\n   Again,  I  have  optimized the executable and I am sure it can be even\n   more optimized, maybe in the future (and without startup-code).\n\n   I  also rewrote the routine that would keep track of your daily chats,\n   as a result it is much faster and more reliable.  Please note that the\n   first  time  you  use  this  version  and  a  new bulletin needs to be\n   generated  by  Chat-O-Meter (because of a new record) there might be a\n   record of 0 seconds and 0 chats.  This will only last for a day.\n\n   The  total  time  that  you''ve  used  chatting with your users used to\n   displayed  in  hours,  minutes and seconds (such as:  2254:33:15), but\n   because  it  lacked clearity on the long run, I''ve altered it a bit so\n   it is now displayed in days, hours and minutes (i.e. 93d 11h 23m).\n\n   And  also  new  in  the  version  is the arrival of Chat-O-Top, a neat\n   little  thingy  that  is  able  to  create  several  very  interesting\n   bulletins, among others:\n\n   * Bulletin of total chatting times with the users\n   * Bulletin of the number of chats with the users\n   * Bulletin of the average time per chat with the users\n\n   These  bulletins  can be sorted either from high to low or vice versa.\n   It  is able to handle custom designs and has some other handy options.\n\n   Please check out the guide to Chat-O-Top for more information.\n\n                           - --------------------.\n                          |  New in version 2.1  |\n                          `-------------------- -\n\n   Added  a  routine  to  keep  track of your daily chats.  At the end of\n   every day (that is, when you start to chat for the first time on a new\n   day)  it  will  calculate  your  total chatting time and if it''s a new\n   record it will be added at the bottom of the Chat-O-Meter bulletin.\n   See supplied @{ "example." link BullExample }\n\n   Also,  at the end of every chat, you will get to see the current score\n   of today''s chatting time.\n   See supplied @{ "example." link ExitChatExample }\n\n   And due to some optimising the executable has become smaller.\n\n                           - --------------------.\n                          |  New in version 2.0  |\n                          `-------------------- -\n\n   The  previous  version ( 1.2 ) was released over one year ago (October\n   1993)  and  to tell you the truth, the coding was lousy and it was not\n   that  sysop  friendly.   But  do  not  despair,  this version has been\n   totally rewritten (from scratch!) and is much smarter and has got some\n   nice new options.  Better forget all about the previous version (1.2).\n\n   It  has  to  be  said  that I would have never created this version if\n   Quartz  hadn''t  asked  for  it  AND if Stezothetic hadn''t made such an\n   awesome logo.  It seems people use my tools after all :-)\n\n   First  of  all,  the best improvement is that it is now able to detect\n   wether you started or ended a chat.  So if you were a previous user of\n   Chat-O-Meter,  the  times  of  deleting  the  old data file before you\n   started another chat are over :) Lost carriers are no problem anymore.\n\n   Another  *BIG* improvement is the @{"new layout" link BullExample}, done by Stezo (Stz).  It\n   now  has  got a really great logo and nice colours.  You''ll love it, I\n   do!  Many thanks to Stezothetic for creating the logo!\n\n   But  that''s not all, another exciting new feature is the creation of a\n   data  file  that  contains  data about chats and times from every user\n   individually  (but  stored  in one single file).  I will also create a\n   door/tool  that creates a nice bulletin out of that one.  If you think\n   this  is nonsense, you are able to turn it off so no data file will be\n   created.\n\n   The  previous  version also did not set the ENV:  variable of AMi-X to\n   the right value, so users that performed a ''who'' saw that the user you\n   were  chatting  with was ''using a door''.  This has been fixed.  But if\n   you  liked  that ''feature'' of Chat-O-Meter, it is still able to hide a\n   chat. Privacy above all (nope, there is no PGP function :) ).\n\n   That''s it.  I hope you''ll like it, it''s one of those nice little doors\n   that  makes  just  the  difference  between your board and your follow\n   sysops'' boards.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Features "Features of Chat-O-Meter - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                      - ------------------------------.\n                     |  Features of Chat-O-Meter 2.0  |\n                     `------------------------------ -\n\n   · Super  Location(tm),  place  the  executable wherever you want (ENV:\n     will do just fine ;) ).\n\n   · Incredible  Creation(tm),  the  Chat-O-Meter bulletin can be created\n     anywhere  you  like it, there is no discrimination of directories at\n     all!  A first in Ami-X land!  :)\n\n   · Ultra  Cool  Layout(tm), thanks to the Great Stezothetic a fantastic\n     layout accompanies this package.\n\n   · Top Security(tm), prevent users from detecting you are chatting with\n     someone (optionable, but comes free with the package as a bonus).\n\n   · Speed  Creation(tm), you have never seen such fast creation/updating\n     of the data files and the bulletin.\n\n   · Space  Saver(tm),  miniscule  files to store the data.  Now you have\n     more room for your public domain software :)\n   \n   · Big  Brother(tm), not only YOUR chats are recorded, a record is kept\n     per  user  for  ultimate  security and reference.  Now you can proof\n     with  whom  you  have  chatted.   A  wide  variety of reports can be\n     generated about this subject (Chat-O-Top).\n\n   · Banner  Baffler(tm), comes in the famous Ultra Cool Layout(tm) style\n     and  tells  you  all  about  your  latest  chats  together  with the\n     accumulated info from the user you''ve chatted with (so you can check\n     if  you''ve  chatted too much with him/here this month, remember your\n     quota!  :) ).\n     \n   · Artificial  Intelligence,  Chat-O-Meter is able to detect wether you\n     want  to  have  yourself asked the question "Show Bulletin [Y/n]" or\n     not when a user hits the Top Ten.\n\n   · Spelling Checker, optionable removal of plural ''s''s (no, this is not\n     a typo) from words like "mINS", "hOURS" and "sECS".\n\n   · Once installed, you will never have to do any maintenance!\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE ExitChatExample "Exit Chat Example - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n   Every time you exit a chat, you will get to see this small bulletin.\n\n                                 FULL SIZE\n :                                                                         :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :  /X MYSTiC       Chat-O-Meter v2.2  by  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     /X MYSTiC  :\n ¡                                                                         .\n :  tIME: 00:02:24 ^ cHAT #: 12404 ^ tODAY: 00:02:26 ^ oVERALL: 1199:04:34 ¡\n .            pRIVATE sCORE: 0000:07:42 ^ pRIVATE chAT #: 22               :\n .                                                                         |\n :   sORRY dUDE, nO Chat-O-Meter tOP tIME tODAY, tRY aGAIN sOON, oKAY?!    :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :                                                                         :\n\n                                 NO_USERFILE\n\n :                                                                         :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :  /X MYSTiC       Chat-O-Meter v2.2  by  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     /X MYSTiC  :\n ¡                                                                         .\n :  tIME: 00:02:24 ^ cHAT #: 12404 ^ tODAY: 00:02:26 ^ oVERALL: 1199:04:34 ¡\n .                                                                         |\n :   sORRY dUDE, nO Chat-O-Meter tOP tIME tODAY, tRY aGAIN sOON, oKAY?!    :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :                                                                         :\n\n                                NO_DAY_RECORD\n\n :                                                                         :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :  /X MYSTiC       Chat-O-Meter v2.2  by  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     /X MYSTiC  :\n ¡                                                                         .\n :    tHIS cHAT:  0:00:51 ^ oVERALL tIME: 1199:04:35 ^ cHAT #: 12406       ¡\n .            pRIVATE sCORE: 0000:07:42 ^ pRIVATE chAT #: 22               :\n .                                                                         |\n :   sORRY dUDE, nO Chat-O-Meter tOP tIME tODAY, tRY aGAIN sOON, oKAY?!    :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :                                                                         :\n\n                         NO_USERFILE & NO_DAY_RECORD\n\n :                                                                         :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :  /X MYSTiC       Chat-O-Meter v2.2  by  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     /X MYSTiC  :\n ¡                                                                         .\n :    tHIS cHAT:  0:00:51 ^ oVERALL tIME: 1199:04:35 ^ cHAT #: 12406       ¡\n .                                                                         |\n :   sORRY dUDE, nO Chat-O-Meter tOP tIME tODAY, tRY aGAIN sOON, oKAY?!    :\n-O-------------------------------------------------------------------------O-\n :                                                                         :\n\n                                   QUIET\n\n                                    :-)\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Install "Installation of Chat-O-Meter - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                      - -----------------------------.\n                     |  How to install Chat-O-Meter  |\n                     `----------------------------- -\n\n   For the illiterate sysops among us:  smack the executable anywhere you\n   like  but  it''s  better you put it in DOORS:Chat-O-Meter/ because then\n   you  will not have to mess with those nasty icons!  Drag those vicious\n   icons  to  your  SYSCmd  directory  as  quick as you can.  After that,\n   modify  your  STARTCHAT.TXT  and  ENDCHAT.TXT  so  that  CHAT_START is\n   started  when you start a chat and END_CHAT is started when your END a\n   chat.  Now, that wasn''t too hard was it?  :-)\n\n   And now, for those of you who like to read huge guides, here comes the\n   verbose version of the above alinea.\n\n   Step 1 - Place  the  executable any place on this earth.  The supplied\n            icons  have  been setup to find it in DOORS:Chat-O-Meter, but\n            if  you  don''t  mind editing the icons, place it anywhere you\n            like.   Please  be  aware of the fact that the Chat-O-Meter''s\n            data  files  will  be created in the same directory you place\n            it.\n\n            Note  to  other  programmers:  Haven''t you guys ever heard of\n            the  PROGDIR:   assign?   Come  on, hard coded paths in doors\n            suck big time, it lacks knowledge of the system.\n\n   Step 2 - Copy   the   two   supplied   icons,   "Chat_Start.info"  and\n            "Chat_End.info" to your SYSCmd directory.\n\n   Step 3 - For  Chat-O-Meter  to do its job, you will have to alter your\n            STARTCHAT.TXT  and  ENDCHAT.TXT  file(s).  These files can be\n            found  in  your  BBS:NODEx/  directory(-ies), as you probably\n            know (well, did ya?).\n            \n            Make  sure  you  call the "Chat_Start" command when you start\n            chatting and call the "End_Chat" command when exiting a chat.\n            You  can do this with MCI commands.  Consult your Ami-Express\n            manual on how to do this (nah nah :) ).\n\n   Step 4 - We''re  done  with  the  steps. For ultimate performance, make\n            sure  to  check  out  the tooltypes described below, they can\n            really make your day.\n            \n   Step 5 - Oops, sorry.\n\n   Chat_Start.info\n   ---------------\n   STACK=10000          ; Should be enough.\n\n   There  are  no more tooltypes for this icon.  Sorry, I promise to work\n   on it :)\n   \n   Chat_End.info\n   -------------\n   CHAT_END             : This  is how Chat-O-Meter recognizes the end of\n                          a chat. Make sure it is in the icon.\n\n   BULLETIN=            : Use  this  tooltype  to set the name (including\n                          path)  of  the  bulletin that Chat-O-Meter will\n                          create.\n                          (i.e. BULLETIN=BBS:Bulletins/Chat-O-Meter.TXT)\n\n   DONT_ASK             : By  default, whenever a user enters the Top Ten\n                          of  longest  chats  s/he  will be asked if s/he\n                          wants  to see the updated Top Ten.  If you have\n                          something  against  that, you can override this\n                          question  with  this  tooltype,  it will NOT be\n                          shown.\n\n   DONT_TRUNC           : By  default  all plural ''s''-es will be removed.\n                          So  for  example:  1 hOURS  becomes 1 hOUR  and\n                          1 sECS  becomes  1 sEC.  However,  if you think\n                          that  ruins  the  bulletin (cosmetically),  you\n                          can  override  it  by specifying this tooltype.\n\n   QUIET                : If you are one of those quiet types, and do NOT\n                          wish  to see that ugly banner after every chat,\n                          quickly  use this tooltype to make sure it will\n                          never happen (god forbid!) :-).\n\n   NO_USERFILE          : As from version 2.0.3 a file is created holding\n                          all  information  about your users'' chats.  But\n                          if  you  think  that  the  8 bytes per user are\n                          taking  up  way  to much of your precious drive\n                          space,  you  can stop the creation of this file\n                          with  this  tooltype. \n\n   NO_DAY_RECORD        : And  if  it  isn''t enough already, Chat-O-Meter\n                          creates  another  file  of  20 bytes (ack) that\n                          holds info about the time you''ve spent chatting\n                          today  with  users  and  it  also harbours your\n                          tODAY  rECORD.   Again,  for  you poor guys out\n                          there  with  too  little  drive  space, you can\n                          prevent the creation of this file by using this\n                          tooltype.  Goodluck!  :)\n\n   That are all tooltypes for this moment.  When you are done setting the\n   tooltypes  you  are ready with the installation of Chat-O-Meter.  Have\n   fun and chat your head off!  :-)\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE BullExample "Bulletin Example Output - Design by Stezothetic!"\n\n   If  your  viewer  is  just as lousy as mine (AmigaGuide) then you will\n   agree  with  me  that the bulletin looks just plain ugly!  But believe\n   me, in Ami-Express or a good viewer/editor it looks way better.\n\n   And  yes,  it  is  possible for one user to be in the Top Ten multiple\n   times.   Why?   Well obviously I don''t check for it :) But why don''t I\n   check  for  it?   Easy,  how would you feel when you''ve just chatted 4\n   hours  with  the  sysop and it''s not put in the Top Ten because you''ve\n   chatted for 5 hours a previous time?  Not happy, let me tell you that.\n\n   What?  You would like to decide that for me?  Oh.  Well, tell it to me\n   in  my  face  and  maybe, just maybe I will fix it.  Pfwah, I''m amazed\n   that  you are even reading this, 99% of sysops don''t read guides, lazy\n   bastards.\n\n .                          _  __           /        __  _      ·          .\n-O-_____   ___.___  ___________ //\___   / ·   ______\  .        \ _      -O-\n :/  __/__/   |  ¬\/   _ ¬\____\/____/  · /   /  __ ¬\ -:---------\/-----. :\n_/   |   ¬\_  _    \_  _   \_  \/  ¬\_ / / /_/   \/   \_| >cHAT·O·mETER< | :\n\_____     /__|     /__|    /___     //_/ / \_____     /`----------------'' ¦\n-:---l____/---l____/---l___//\-l____/-\_\/-------l____/------ ---  --   -  ¡_\n ¦                       __/  \  ___  ___________  /\_________    _______  \/\n | .------------------.  \_    \/  ¬\/  __ ¬\____\/____/  __ ¬\___\____ ¬\_¦\n ¡ :   ·/X mYSTIC!·   :  /     \/    \_ \____/__ \/  ¬\_  \____/__   \/  _/:\n :_`---------------- - _/       \_____/___     /__     /____     /___/    \_\n \/ -  -- --- ---------\_________/--Stz--\____/--l____/----\____/----\_____/-\n :                                                                         |\n !   o1. Veteran              03 hOURS, 42 mINS aND 42 sECS  (06-jAN-95)   :\n |   o2. Veteran              03 hOURS, 28 mINS aND 53 sECS  (29-dEC-93)   :\n O   o3. skate BOB            03 hOURS, 08 mINS aND 03 sECS  (25-sEP-94)   ¡\n |   o4. Monte Q              03 hOURS, 07 mINS aND 03 sECS  (11-dEC-93)   O\n ¦   o5. SKATE BOB            03 hOURS, 03 mINS aND 40 sECS  (22-dEC-93)   |\n :   o6. KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC     02 hOURS, 52 mINS aND 01 sEC   (28-aPR-94)   ¦\n ¦   o7. skate BOB            02 hOURS, 50 mINS aND 55 sECS  (22-oCT-94)   :\n ¡   o8. Sir IQ               02 hOURS, 48 mINS aND 52 sECS  (28-oCT-93)   ·\n_:   o9. bULLOCK/jETSET       01 hOUR , 08 mINS aND 15 sECS  (05-jUN-95)   :_\n\:   1o. [MEGA^FORTRESS]      01 hOUR , 08 mINS aND 09 sECS  (20-jUN-95)   :/\n \                                                                         /\n :\        tOTAL # oF cHATS: 22866       tOTAL cHAT tIME: 2190:19:18      /:\n-O-\---------------------------------------------------------------------/-O-\n :  ·          dAY rECORD: 04:15:10 oN 23-jUN-95 ( 44 cHATS)            ·  :\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE History "History of Chat-O-Meter - Amazing... :)"\n                    - ---------------------------------.\n                   |  History: Something irreversible  |\n                   `-------------------------------- -\n\n                        Chat-O-Meter Ami-Express Door\n                             (more history below)\n\n   26-Jun-95   2.2.3    Chat-O-Meter  can  now also be used in the year\n                        2000 and up, inserted a simple check.\n\n   26-Jun-95   2.2.2    The  total  chatting  time  is now displayed in\n                        units of days, hours and minutes to enhance the\n                        clearity  of  the  time  spent chatting (entire\n                        days are wasted chatting :) ).\n\n                        Combined  some fprintf()''s so the executable is\n                        a few bytes smaller.\n\n   24-Jun-95   2.2.1    Finally  found  that  damn  bug  in  the  today\n                        routine.   How  the hell was I supposed to know\n                        that  time()  works from 1970 and not from 1978\n                        as  demanded  by Date2Amiga()?  Sigh.  But it''s\n                        fixed now, no more passing days at 18:00 hours.\n\n   22-Jun-95   2.2.0    Rewrote the ''today'' routine and replaced some\n                        of the fopen()-fread()-fwrite()''s with AmigaDOS\n                        function calls (Open, Read, Write) for speed.\n\n                        Rewrote  some  parts of the guide, my English\n                        has improved and I think it should show :)\n                        (Hi Radavi, better this way?)\n\n   07-Feb-95   2.1.1    Fixed the ''today''-routine. It could totally ruin\n                        your Chat-O-Meter.Data if things went wrong. The\n                        day-record is now stored in the .today file and\n                        no longer in the Chat-O-Meter.Data file (as it\n                        did in version 2.1.0).\n\n   06-Feb-95   2.1.0    Chat-O-Meter also keeps track of the total chat\n                        time per day. This can be turned off with:\n                        New tooltype: NO_DAY_RECORD\n\n                        Optimized /X link code. Smaller executable.\n\n   25-Dec-94   2.0.9    Chat-O-Meter can also run in silent mode now.\n                        New tooltype: QUIET\n\n   25-Dec-94   2.0.8    Merry X-Mas :) Made some internal changes, removed\n                        the stack-check (smaller executable).\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.7    Found the bug that caused the strange resets.\n                        I adjusted the wrong variable... sigh... it\n                        should be fixed now.\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.6    Allocated memory for some structures by myself\n                        instead of allocating them on the stack. Hope\n                        to have fixed the strange guru''s.\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.5ß   Special version for Muad''Dib with DEBUG info.\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.5    Tried to fix a bug that caused strange resets on\n                        Muad''Dibs machine (040) and weird crashes on my\n                        own system (total freeze and I had to turn my\n                        computer off for at least one minute!).\n\n   24-Dec-94   2.0.4    Fixed a bug that caused the bulletin only the be\n                        updated in the event of a new entry in the Top\n                        Ten. The Total Chat Time was never updated.\n                        *Reported by Quartz*\n\n   23-Dec-94   2.0.3    Built in the User file, now a record is kept of\n                        every user. A new bulletin perhaps.\n                        New tooltype: NO_USERFILE\n                        \n                        Altered the output after the chat again, now also\n                        contains info about the user him/herself.\n\n   22-Dec-94   2.0.2    Altered the output a bit and made some minor\n                        internal changes that caused a smaller executable.\n\n   21-Dec-94   2.0.1    Altered the output after a chat.\n\n   21-Dec-94   2.0.0    Totally rewrote it from scratch:\n                        - END_CHAT tooltype fixes the problem with lost\n                          carriers and then starting a chat again causing\n                          the start of the chat being the end of a chat.\n                          (Never mind if you don''t get it, it''s fixed!)\n                        *Requested by Quartz*\n\n                        New layout from Stezothetic implemented.\n                        *Thanks to Stezothetic*\n\n   26-Oct-93   1.2      Output file is now sysop-configurable. Now you can\n                        have it saved anywhere you like!\n                        Released: 30-Oct-93\n\n   23-Oct-93   1.1      Implemented safety-procedure so when user drops\n                        carrier in chat the Chat-O-Top doesn''t get mixed up.\n                        Released at: 27-Oct-93\n\n   17-Oct-93   1.0      First release to friends.\n\n                          Chat-O-Meter Convert Util\n\n   22-Jan-95   1.0.1    Fixed CoM-Convert; it now reads the name of the\n                        user from the correct place.\n\n   04-Jan-95   1.0.0    Completed CoM-Convert and restyled it.\n\n   03-Jan-95   1.0.0    Created CoM-Convert.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Greetings "Greetings and thanks - © 1994/1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            - --------------------.\n                           |  Greetings & Thanks  |\n                           `-------------------- -\n\n   A *BIG* THANKS TO STEZOTHETIC (Stz) FOR CREATING THE DESiGN, THANKS MATE!\n\n                                      :\n                                     -O-\n                                      :\n\n   Muad''Dib       -  Gheez,  using  custom  code to use linked lists is a\n                     bit unneccessary, but it sure shows that your skills\n                     are  improving.   Before  you know it you can finish\n                     KiLLER all by yourself :)\n\n   Wishbringer    -  Congratulations with passing your examinations.  Now\n                     it''s  time  for  the  REAL work at school or are you\n                     going  to  do  labour,  maybe working as a gardener?\n                     :-)\n   \n   Motive Power   -  Lost  your data line?  Last your data line?  How can\n                     you  be  a  good MYSTiC board then?  Naughty boy for\n                     not  telling me, I had to find out via xxxxx, afraid\n                     I  would kick you?  Well, you are right (not)!  It''s\n                     the  friendship that counts (together with megabytes\n                     :) ).\n   \n   I-GO           -  He  man,  let''s  puke  all  over this place!  Puking\n                     rules!   Let''s  inhale the odor and maybe we can get\n                     high  on  that,  woooow, sounds like the trip of our\n                     life times (and the last...  barf!).\n         \n   Exotic         -  My dear, dear friend. How is life treating you?\n   \n   Zed            -  Let  me  get this straight, you''ve made X-Caller for\n                     both XLK and AMi-X?  Why?\n   \n   Nocturn        -  Earth  calling  Nocturn,  Earth calling Nocturn.  If\n                     you  are  reading  this,  please  contact me.  Since\n                     you''ve  moved,  you seemed to have vanished from the\n                     Earth (did the gays get you in Philly?  :) ).\n                     \n   Empire         -  Why  work  for  the  money?   We  work for pleasure,\n                     pleasure  is  what  we  want out of life, so we work\n                     with  pleasure  (not).   Here''s  a  piece of advice:\n                     work to live, don''t live to work.\n   \n   bObO/MST       -  Ofcourse  you  send me 1000+ messages, they all just\n                     happened  to  be  re-routed to NIL:  ?  Oh well, I''m\n                     just  happy  your e-mail address works again.  Where\n                     the hell is File-ID 2.0 with colour support? :)\n   \n   Stezothetic    -  You''re  in the army now, wo oh oh, he''s in the army,\n                     nohow...  snief!\n   \n   UFOk           -  You  are the fastest trader I''ve ever seen!  And you\n                     don''t  trade  little  as well, almost 1 gigabyte per\n                     month.  Wouzers...  you rule for sure!\n\n   Radavi         -  You  don''t  give up easily, do you now?  You have to\n                     find EVERY tiny error I''ve made in my guide(s).  But\n                     now  I  am  convinced  that this file is free of any\n                     mistakes,  I''ve even corrected some that you did not\n                     find  (moeha!).   But  it''s all for the best, even a\n                     perfectionist  like  myself can make mistakes (did I\n                     tell you about my finest quality?  Modesty :) ).\n\n   QuartZ         -  You  thought  I  had  forgotten all about you didn''t\n                     you?   You  are thinking "why am I mentioned last in\n                     the  list?"  Getting paranoid?  No friends?  :) Nah,\n                     just teasing you my friend.\n   \n                      and to all my friends on Internet!\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Contact "How to get in touch with me - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             - ------------------.\n                            |  Contacting me...  |\n                            `------------------ - \n\n       For FAST response (preferred) please contact me on Internet:\n\n                               [email removed]\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                 I can be reached on the following boards:\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                The Undiscovered       +31-2294-1442 (USR 33k6)\n                                       +31-2294-4442 (USR 33k6)\n                                       +31-2294-tRY! (USR 33k6)\n\n                Last Outpost           +31-72-201182 (USR 33k6)\n                                       +31-72-157682 (USR 33k6)\n\n         I know, you have to call The Netherlands for it, but it''s\n            the only boards I call on a frequent basis, sorry.\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n        You can also find me on IRC once in a while on the channels\n                   #amigascne, #amiga, #rave and #megawatt\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Conversion "How to convert your old bulletin - © 1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                           - ---------------------.\n                          |  Bulletin Conversion  |\n                          `--------------------- -\n\n   For  sysops  that  used  Chat-O-Meter  1.x, there is a special program\n   called  "CoM-Convert"  that allows you to convert your old bulletin to\n   the  new  data  file  that  Chat-O-Meter  2.0  uses.  If you are not a\n   previous user of Chat-O-Meter, you can skip this part.\n\n   To  convert  it,  simply  start  the  program  "CoM-Convert"  from the\n   directory  where  you  have Chat-O-Meter 2.0 installed.  The data file\n   will be created in the directory where you started "CoM-Convert".\n\n   Once  you  have  started the program, a file requester will pop up and\n   you  must select the old bulletin.  Press `OK'' and you will get to see\n   the names (plus additional info) from that bulletin.\n\n   After that, the program will exit (`Ready.'') and you can find the data\n   file  called  `Chat-O-Meter.Data''  in that same directory.  Move it if\n   you  have  to.  The new bulletin will not be created yet!  To do this,\n   you  have  to  start Chat-O-Meter 2.0 from /X (start and end a chat is\n   sufficient).\n\n   That''s it, easy a as a fiddle.\n\n   NOTE : To use this tool, you must have req-tools installed.\n\n   NOTE¹: There  is *NO* check whether the file you feed to "CoM-Convert"\n          really  is an old bulletin from 1.x.  You can even cheat if you\n          want to ;-)\n\n   NOTE²: To  all beta-testers, this conversion util takes in account any\n          old  `Chat-O-Meter.Data'' file.  That is, it doesn''t recalculate\n          a new Top-Ten (it simply converts the old one), but it does ADD\n          the  old  `Total Chat Time''  to the new time and that also goes\n          for the total number of chats.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Troubles "Troubles with Chat-O-Meter? - © 1995 by KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            - ------------------.\n                           |  Trouble-Shooting  |\n                           `------------------ -\n\n   Q: Chat-O-Meter does not generate the bulletin, nor does it show\n      it on screen. What should I do?\n\n   A: Remove the "CHAT_END" tooltype from your CHAT_END icon.\n\n                               - -- ---- -- -\n\n   Q: Chat-O-Meter says it has got a daily record of 0 seconds? How\n      is that possible?\n\n   A: This can only happen the first day when a new record from a user\n      has been set, but there has not been a daily record yet. This\n      will be fixed by tomorrow, be patient :)\n\n                               - -- ---- -- -\n\n   Q: Why doesn''t Chat-O-Top show the total number of chats?\n   \n   A: Because there was no room, but if you contact me I might\n      consider doing a new version.\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),'{"STACK":"10000"}','auto-added',114695,2,0,NULL,NULL,'klrchats','scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('klrcom11','KLRCOM11.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KLRCOM11.LHA','KC-Config','XIM','KC-Config',NULL,NULL,NULL,'.-\_  \/  _/   __/______\/___/-------------. | /   \/   \______  \_  \/  \_   mYSTIC!   | :/     \____/   _____/___    / /X dIVISION .',replace('  __/\  __________ _ ___  /\__              \n.-\_  \/  _/   __/______\/___/-------------.\n| /   \/   \______  \_  \/  \_   mYSTIC!   |\n:/     \____/   _____/___    / /X dIVISION .\n/_______\-\_____/--sTZ--l___/--------------:\n| KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC BRiNGS: KiLLER-COMMENT! |\n| *WiTHOUT A DOUBT THE BEST COMMENT DOOR!* :\n: o GUI Config program o Top Safe Security |\n| o Nice lay-out!      o Statistical info! :\n: o Intelligent user selection (see guide) |\n`------------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),'KiLLER-Comment.Guide',replace('@DATABASE "KiLLER-Comment.GUiDE"\n@REMARK	.-------------------------------------------------------.\n@REMARK  | This document should be read with AmigaGuide (OS 2.x) |\n@REMARK  | or MultiView (OS 3.x) or any other guide reader. Tnx. |\n@REMARK  `-------------------------------------------------------''\n@REMARK	$VER: KiLLER Comment 1.1.0\n@WIDTH 80\n@NODE MAIN "KiLLER Comment - MYSTiC 1994 - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            Guide to KiLLER Comment\n                     for Ami-Express Bulletin Board Systems\n\n                             Program version: 1.1.0\n                Compilation Date/Time: Mon Dec 26 01:10:53 1994\n\n                           © 1994 by @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n\n                   ... another fine MYSTiC /X production ...\n\n      @{" First words... " link WhatIs}    What/Why is KiLLER Comment?\n      @{" Features       " link Features}    What can it do?\n      @{" Installation   " link Install}    How to install KiLLER Comment on your BBS\n      @{" Usage /X       " link AmiUsage}    How things go with Ami-Express\n      @{" Usage Config   " link Config}    How to use the Config GUI\n      @{" History        " link History}    History of KiLLER Comment\n      @{" History Config " link HistoryGUI}    History of KiLLER Config\n      @{" Greetings      " link Greetings}    Greetings, messages and contact info\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatIs "What/Why is KiLLER Comment? - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                         .---------------------------.\n                         |  What is KiLLER Comment?  |\n                         `---------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER Comment is number 1001 in the row of a [C]omment doors.  But this\n   is not just *a* comment door, otherwise I wouldn''t have made it.  I have\n   some profound reasons.\n\n   Despite  the  fact  that  there  must be over a 1000 different [C]omment\n   doors,  there  is not one comment door that suits my needs.  I have seen\n   plenty of them but to me they all look ugly, have static interfaces, are\n   as flexible as a log of wood and generally suck :-).\n\n   With  this mind I''ve tried to make KiLLER Comment as smart and pretty as\n   I  can  and  I  feel I have succeeded.  I have never seen a comment door\n   that  works  as  simple  as  this  one  with  a fair amount of so-called\n   ''intelligence'' and comes with a nice GUI.\n\n   What  is  this  ''intelligence'' I speak about you say?  Well, all comment\n   doors  sofar  weren''t  able  to see that if I typed "c kill" I wanted to\n   comment to "KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" nor where they able to see that if I typed\n   "c  amiga" I wanted to comment to the Amiga co-sysop.  KiLLER Comment is\n   able to detect this!\n\n   Another  thing  is  security.   Every  new  user  was  able to see which\n   cosysops  you  had,  but  by giving every cosysop a security level, they\n   will  not see their names/jobs.  And if there is only one (co)sysop they\n   are  allowed  to  see,  they  will  not  even  see it is done via KiLLER\n   Comment.\n\n   Also,  per user in the [C]omment list there is a check wether he/she has\n   got access to the conference that KiLLER [C]omment is called from, so it\n   is impossible to write a person to someone in the wrong conference.\n\n   What I personally also dislike are comment doors that only fill like 25%\n   of  your  screen.   Not  that  you  should put a lot of nonsense in your\n   comment  door,  no,  but  KiLLER  Comment puts an extra line between the\n   names  of  the  (co-)sysops  if  that''s possible with the user''s maximum\n   numbers  of  lines  he  can  handle  on his screen.  It''s these kinds of\n   things I miss in those 13-in-a-dozen [C]omment doors.  That''s why I made\n   KiLLER Comment.\n\n   And  oh, just like I first introduced cursor control in ConfScan, KiLLER\n   comment also uses cursor control for selecting a user you can write to.\n\n   Anyway,  I  hope  you  will  enjoy KiLLER Comment as much as I will.  Go\n   check out the features if you want to know more.\n\n                                                          -KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Features "Features of KiLLER Comment - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                        .------------------------------.\n                        |  Features of KiLLER Comment  |\n                        `------------------------------''\n\n   Although  the  list  of  features isn''t big, it''s enough for a [C]omment\n   door.  Remember, it''s only a [C]omment door.\n\n   » GUI Config\n\n   A small program comes with it to set it up the way you want it as easy\n   as possible.  It''s just click and save, you probably won''t even need the\n   keyboard but to fill in some personal info.\n\n   » Security\n\n   Users  with  low  access will not see all cosysops in the [C]omment list\n   (esspecially useful with new users).\n\n   » Known as\n\n   Very often your cosysops will have names that contain some sort of group\n   name  in  it  (like  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC), but it''s not always that nice or\n   handy  to  have  that group name in the comment door (be it for cosmetic\n   reasons,  be  it  for  some  other reason).  That''s why you can enter an\n   alias  for  each of your cosysops (and for yourself ofcourse) which will\n   be used to display in the comment list.\n\n   » Info\n\n   For  every (co)sysop you can enter one line of information regarding why\n   he  is in the comment list.  This can be something like "Amiga Co-Sysop"\n   or "Takes care of users / files / lamers".\n\n   » Comments\n\n   Most  comment doors offer you the possibility to display a message after\n   the  user has selected someone to write to.  This is generally something\n   like  "<name>  says:   bla bla".  With KiLLER Comment you can make lines\n   like  this:   "And  there  goes another message to <name>" or "<name> is\n   cheering of joy!".\n   \n   An example file with fortune cookies has been supplied (Comments.ASCII).\n\n   » Cursor Control / Numeric entries / Default sysop\n\n   Selecting  a  user  can  be done with the cursor keys or by entering the\n   corresponding number.  A default sysop is also available (space).\n\n   » Comment Statistics\n\n   KiLLER  Comment  keeps record of the number of messages written to every\n   user  and  also knows when every user received his last message plus the\n   user  who  wrote  it to him.  So with this information it is possible to\n   create  some  nice  looking  statistics.  These statistics can be viewed\n   within /X or with the Config program.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Install "How to install KiLLER Comment - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                      .----------------------------------.\n                      |  Installation of KiLLER Comment  |\n                      `----------------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Comment  can  be installed like any /X 3.x command, but please\n   take notice of these tooltypes:\n\n   "KC-Config.info" - The icon of the GUI config program\n   \n   · ACP_LOCATION=<path>      - Location of your ACP.info file.\n                                (eg. ACP_LOCATION=SYS:WBStartUp)\n\n   · KC_LOCATION=<path>       - Location of the /X version\n                                (eg. KC_LOCATION=DOORS:K-Comment)\n                                ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE **\n                                 THIS SHOULD BE THE PATH TO THE DIRECTORY\n                                 AND SHOULD _NOT_ INCLUDE THE NAME OF THE\n                                 EXECUTABLE! DON''T FORGET THIS! (HI JAKE)\n                                ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE ** NOTE **\n\n   · KC_VIEWER=<full name>    - External viewer for the Commentistics\n                                (eg. KC_VIEWER=C:PPMORE)\n\n   · PUB_SCREEN=<name>        - Optional(!) name of public screen that the\n                                config program will open on instead of the\n                                Workbench.\n                                (eg. PUB_SCREEN=ECS_PUB)\n\n   "C.info"          - The icon in your BBS command directory\n\n   · ACS_STATS=<level>        - The minimum level required to view the\n                                statiscal mumbo jumbo that KiLLER Comment\n                                is able to create for you (with ''C ?'').\n                                (eg. ACS_STATS=50)\n\n   · BBS_LOCATION=<path>      - This should be the path to your BBS:, or\n                                if you keep your USER.DATA located some-\n                                where else, the path to your USER.DATA.\n\n   · SEPARATOR_LINES          - An empty line is by default put  between\n                                two sysops if possible, but if you don''t\n                                like  that,  you can also have a line of\n                                ''-''s put between them.  To do so, simply\n                                this tooltype.\n\n   I  have  found  it  very  useful  to  put the Config program in my ACP\n   command list.  Maybe this can be useful for you too.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE AmiUsage "Usage in Ami-Express - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                          .------------------------.\n                          |  Usage in Ami-Express  |\n                          `------------------------''\n\n                          ^-^  Selecting a user  ^-^\n\n   Once  installed, the usage of the [C]omment door is (and should be) real\n   easy.  You can simply start it with ''C'' and you will be presented with a\n   menu from which you can select a user to send some mail to.\n\n   However,  like I said in the first words, KiLLER comment is a bit smart.\n   If  you enter a parameter after the ''C'', you can skip the menu and still\n   select  the  person you want to write to.  For example, suppose you want\n   to write a message to "Muad''Dib".  We all know that this is a very lousy\n   name  to spell, so we can suffice by entering "C Muad" and Muad''Dib will\n   be selected.\n\n   However,  if  you''ve  got  another  cosysop  in  the  list that is named\n   Muad''Dab,  KiLLER  Comment  will  take  the first name that has the part\n   "muad"  in it.  This is just the way it goes, so if you want to write to\n   Muad''Dab  instead  of  Muad''Dib  you  have  to enter another string that\n   uniquely identifies Muad''Dab, for instance:  "C Dab" :)\n\n   Another  way to select a user is entering his or her appropriate number.\n   If  you  know  that Muad''Dib is number 2 in the list, you can suffice by\n   entering  "C 2".   And  if  you  know that Muad''Dab is number 5, you can\n   enter  "C 5"  to  write  to Muad''Dab.  Real easy.  If you enter a number\n   that does not exist, you will be presented with the menu again.\n\n   But  that''s not all, let''s say you got separate sysops (or cosysops) for\n   different  conferences;  Amiga,  SNES  and  PC.  And suppose you want to\n   write  to  the Amiga cosysop, you can simply enter "C Amiga" or "C Ami".\n   Make  sure  the  length  of  the  string  after  the  "C"  is at least 3\n   characters,  otherwise  it will be marked as ambigious and the menu will\n   be  prompted.   And  yes,  that means that "C PC" will NOT work (haha!).\n   But  hey,  there  are  3  other  ways of selecting that sysop.  So don''t\n   despair (or enter "C p PC" if the text is "Cosysop PC").\n\n                           ^-^  Commentistics  ^-^\n\n   The  commentistics  can be viewed by entering a question mark behind the\n   "C".  Couldn''t be more simple.  Just enter "C ?" and you will be able to\n   view  all  sorts  of  statistic mumbo jumbo on your screen.  Once you''ve\n   seen the first screen, you can go to the next screen by pressing any key\n   BUT Q, ESC can CTRL-C.  Than the screen will be cleared in a fashionable\n   style and the last page will be shown, containing the times and names of\n   users who wrote last to the users in the Comment list.\n\n                           ^-^  Syntax Summary  ^-^\n\n   For all you folks that didn''t bother to read the upper texts, here''s the\n   brief list of all commands:\n\n   C        - A menu will be shown from which you can select a user\n\n   C <text> - The  <text> will be compared to the names of the users in the\n              list  and  if  that fails, it will be compared to the info of\n              each user in the list. This comparing is case insensitive.\n\n   C <#>    - The corresponding name with the number will be selected.\n\n   C ?      - The commentistics will be shown.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Config "The Config Editor - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             .-----------------.\n                             |  Config Editor  |\n                             `-----------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Comment  comes  with  a  full C= compliant Guided User Interface\n   (commonly  known  as  GUI).  With this Configuration Editor you are able\n   to setup KiLLER Comment the way YOU want.\n\n   When  you  start  the  Config  Editor for the first time, the sysop will\n   automatically  added as the first entry in the list of users that can be\n   commented  to.   There  should  at  least  be  ONE  entry in the list or\n   otherwise the user can''t comment to anybody :-)\n\n   Okay,  I  think it is best if I just tell you what every button does and\n   how to do things.  Here we go...\n\n   » Known As\n\n   You  can  enter an alias for every user that is in the list.  By default\n   the  alias  is  the  same  as  their real name in /X but you are free to\n   change  it.  KiLLER Comment will still know their real name, don''t worry\n   about that.  This function can be handy if you got a cosysop with a name\n   like [-Bad Boy^Lamers-R-Us^I-Suck-] but you don''t want to have that damn\n   ugly handle in your comment door.  Fill in whatever you like.\n\n   » Info\n\n   As  usual  you are able to enter one line of text that somehow describes\n   why  this  user  is put in the list of ''commenteers'' :) You can put info\n   like "Amiga Cosysop" in here or "Nuking is my nick-name!".  You are free\n   to  use  any colours in it, just enter them in the gadget like you would\n   do  in  a  text  file.   If pressing the ESC removes the cursor from the\n   gadget,  you  need to TURN OF THE TEXT-FILTER OPTION IN YOUR PREFERENCES\n   FROM YOUR AMIGA (Prefs/IControl)!!\n\n   Although  you  can only have like 34 chars on screen for info about that\n   user,  the  Config  Editor  takes 80 chars.  You are responsible for the\n   length  of  the  text.   If you put 80 chars of text in it, it will look\n   like a mess on screen in Ami-Express.\n\n   » Comments\n\n   A  comment is a line of text that is shown to the user who just selected\n   somebody.   This  can  be  a  message  like  "Muad''Dib says:  Thanks for\n   nothing!"  or  "You can hear Muad''Dib shout with joy.".  Or you can show\n   him fortune cookie (max.  1 line!).  An example file has been included.\n\n   This gadget wants a full path plus filename to a file that contains such\n   messages.   This can be a different file for every user or the same file\n   for all users.  The lines in the text file can contain anything you like\n   where you can use ''%s'' to represent the name of the choosen user.\n\n   Examples:   %s says: Thanks for your mail bud.\n               You can hear %s scream with joy.\n               %s is overwhelmed with tears by your gesture.\n               Who is General Failure and why is he reading my hard drive?\n\n   Be creative!\n\n   With  the  [S]  button  you  can select a file via a file-requester than\n   rather typing in its full name.\n\n   » Req. Acs\n\n   For  security  reasons,  you  are able to set a minimum access level for\n   every  user in the comment list.  This way you are able to have multiple\n   cosysops and not let all the users know what they all do.\n\n   Imagine  you''ve got a cosysop for the Amiga section, SNES section and PC\n   section.   You''ve  got  a lot of Amiga users, but you don''t want them to\n   know  that  you  got  a  PC  section.   If  they would use the ''C'' door,\n   normally  they  would  see  that  you  got a cosysop for the PC section.\n   "What  PC  section?"  they will think.  Or even worse, new users who use\n   the door can easily spot your conferences.  But not with KiLLER Comment,\n   just  make  sure that the Amiga users have a lower access level than the\n   PC users.\n\n   It''s  not  perfect yet, but it''s a start.  I am thinking of a better way\n   to secure this (it''s possible, I just have to work it out a bit more).\n\n   By  the  way,  cosysops  with  "C"  access  will  also NOT see the other\n   cosysop(s) (not even in the Commentistics).\n\n   » Commentistics\n\n   Not  only in Ami-Express, but also in the Config Editor, you are able to\n   look  up  the statistics that KiLLER Comment can create.  The statistics\n   are  put  into  a  file  and that file is shown to you with the external\n   viewer  you specified.  For your information, that file is always called\n   T:KC_Commentistics,  does  NOT  contain  any  ANSI  codes and is removed\n   afterwards.\n\n   » Add / Del\n\n   Adding  a  user  to  the  list is quite easy, select Add and you will be\n   presented  with  a  list  of all your users in your USER.DATA.  This may\n   take  a  while,  depending on your type (read:  speed) of computer, hard\n   drive  and number of users in your USER.DATA.  A new user will always be\n   inserted at the END of the list.\n\n   To  remove  a user from the list, make it the active user (its name will\n   be  shown  below the listview) and press Del.  Easy as peanuts.  The new\n   current user will be the last user in the list.\n\n   You can not remove all users from the list.  A protection has been built\n   in  so please try to do so :) It''s impossible to remove them all because\n   to who should the users in your system comment to?\n\n   » Up / Down\n\n   With  those  gadgets  you  are  able to determine the order in which the\n   users  will  appear a) in the list and b) on screen in Ami-Express.  The\n   most upper user will be the default sysop!\n\n   To  move  a user, make it the current one by selecting it and then press\n   either up or down (repeatively if neccessary).\n\n   » Save\n\n   Guess what?\n\n   » &\n   \n   Sorry? What did you say? :)\n\n   » Quit\n\n   You  guessed  it.   By  the  way,  there  is  NO  warning if you changed\n   something and did not save it before quitting.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Greetings "Greetings go to... - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                            .-------------------.\n                            |  Greetings to...  |\n                            `-------------------''\n\n                  ^-^   ALL MYSTiC MEMBERS WORLD-WIDE!   ^-^\n\n                            YOU ARE THE GREATEST!\n\n                  ^-^   ALL MYSTiC MEMBERS WORLD-WIDE!   ^-^\n\n\n   Muad''Dib       Happy New Year! Come to the Mazzo!\n\n   Quartz         And you made me make another update... :)\n   \n            To all my other friends I say: Happy New Year!\n\n   If you want to contact me, read @{"this!" link Contact}\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Contact "How to get in touch with me - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             - ------------------.\n                            |  Contacting me...  |\n                            `------------------ - \n\n                 I can be reached on the following boards:\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                The Undiscovered       +31-2294-1442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-4442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-tRY! (USR 28k8)\n\n                Last Outpost           +31-72-201182 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-72-157682 (USR 28k8)\n\n         I know, you have to call The Netherlands for it, but it''s\n            the only boards I call on a frequent basis, sorry.\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n       For FAST response (preferred) please contact me on Internet:\n\n                               [email removed]\n\n                    ( [email removed] -- backup )\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n        You can also find me on IRC once in a while on the channels\n                     #amigascne, #amiga and #megawatt\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE History "History of KiLLER Comment - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n   1.1.0    26-12-94    KiLLER [C]omment now checks wether the persons in\n                        the [C]omment list have access to the current conf\n                        that KC is called from. If not, that person will\n                        not be put in the list.\n                        REQUESTED BY: QuartZ\n\n   1.0.5    02-11-94    You can now have lines used as a separator on screen\n                        instead of a blank line.\n                        New tooltype: SEPARATOR_LINES\n                        REQUESTED BY: JAKE\n\n   1.0.4    20-10-94    Added an access level check. If a user does not have\n                        the minimal required access to actually see a cosysop\n                        in the Comment list, than that user will not see\n                        that cosysop. See also @{"History Gui" link HistoryGUI}.\n                        REQUESTED BY: TYCOON/MYSTIC & MUAD''DIB\n\n            20-10-94    The program now attempts to write messages with the\n                        external "E" door (if any). This makes it more\n                        flexible for sysop, allowing them to have external\n                        "E" doors installed, like the one from 5D and the\n                        external one from Tycoon/MYSTiC (/X-Fido).\n                        REQUESTED BY: TYCOON/MYSTiC\n\n   1.0.3    15-10-94    A bug in the "Commentistics" routine caused a guru\n                        when there were users with no messages. A division\n                        by zero was the result -> guru :( Fixed that.\n\n   1.0.0    14-10-94    First initial release to friends.\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE HistoryGUI "History of KiLLER Config Editor - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n   1.0.2    20-10-94    Restyled the GUI bit. I removed the "Show Comments"\n                        gadget because I found it not useful. Removed the\n                        code also (ofcoz, but you never know who reads\n                        this :) ).\n\n            20-10-94    New option: [ Req. Acs ] (Required Access)\n                        You can set a minimum access level per user in the\n                        list. Users with an access level lower than that\n                        level will NOT see him in the [C]omment door.\n                        This enables you to let even new users use this\n                        door and not reveal to them that you (for instance)\n                        have an Amiga cosysop and/or SNES cosysop and/or\n                        PC cosysop.\n                        REQUESTED BY: TYCOON/MYSTIC & MUAD''DIB/DLT\n\n            20-10-94    New option: [ Clear Statistical Data ]\n                        With this option you can clear all statistical data\n                        that is kept for every user. This means that the\n                        total # of msg-s, last msg date and user will be set\n                        to 0 (or None).\n\n            20-10-94    Another restyle: [ Save ][ & ][ Quit ]\n                        Save -> Saves the (new) prefs\n                        &    -> Save and Quit\n                        Quit -> Quit the program\n\n            20-10-94    Another restyle: I changed the placed of the [ Add ]\n                        [ Del ] [ Up ] [ Down ] a bit, to make it a bit more\n                        logical.\n\n   1.0.1    14-10-94    First bug found in 3 secs by Muad''Dib :) If you\n                        selected "Commentistics" from the GUI Config and\n                        there were no messages written yet, a crash was\n                        the result (division by zero). Fixed it.\n\n   1.0.0    14-10-94    First initial release to friends.\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),'{"LOCATION":"<path>"}','auto-added',61016,5,0,NULL,NULL,'klrcom11','scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('klr_bps','KLR_BPS.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KLR_BPS.LHA','KiLLER_Baud','XIM','KiLLER_Baud',NULL,NULL,'KLR','.-\_  \/  _/   __/______\/___/-------------. | /   \/   \______  \_  \/  \_   mYSTIC!   | :/     \____/   _____/___    / /X dIVISION .',replace('  __/\  __________ _ ___  /\__              \n.-\_  \/  _/   __/______\/___/-------------.\n| /   \/   \______  \_  \/  \_   mYSTIC!   |\n:/     \____/   _____/___    / /X dIVISION .\n/_______\-\_____/--sTZ--l___/--------------:\n| KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC BRiNGS: KiLLER-BAUD 1.4 |\n| *Creates bulls about users'' baud-rates!* :\n: MAJOR BUGFiX AND CORRECTED SUPPLiED iCON !\n| o Exclude baud-rates  o Nice lay-out     ¡\n: o Two pages of info!  o Future proof!    |\n`------------------------------------------''\n[iSDN][-÷ lEEChEd fRoM gOd''s hArEm ÷-][16.8]\n','\n',char(10)),'KiLLER_Baud.Guide',replace('@DATABASE "KiLLER-Comment.GUiDE"\n@REMARK	.-------------------------------------------------------.\n@REMARK  | This document should be read with AmigaGuide (OS 2.x) |\n@REMARK  | or MultiView (OS 3.x) or any other guide reader. Tnx. |\n@REMARK  `-------------------------------------------------------''\n@REMARK	$VER: KiLLER Baud 1.4.0\n@WIDTH 80\n@NODE MAIN "KiLLER Comment - MYSTiC 1994 - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             Guide to KiLLER Baud\n                 for Ami-Express 3.38+ Bulletin Board Systems\n\n                             Program version: 1.4.0\n                Compilation Date/Time: Thu Feb  2 21:30:48 1995\n\n                         © 1994/1995 by @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n\n                   ... another fine MYSTiC /X production ...\n\n      @{" First words... " link WhatIs}    What is KiLLER Baud\n      @{" Features       " link Features}    What can it do?\n      @{" Installation   " link Install}    How to install KiLLER Baud on your system\n      @{" Example 1      " link BaudUsage}    Example output of bulletin 1\n      @{" Example 2      " link LastBauds}    Example output on bulletin 2\n      @{" History        " link History}    History of KiLLER Baud\n      @{" Greetings      " link Greetings}    Big hellos and messages\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Contact "How to contact KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC - © Me me me! :)"\n                            .--------------------.\n                            |  Contacting me...  |\n                            `--------------------''\n\n       For FAST response (preferred) please contact me on Internet:\n\n                               [email removed]\n\n                    ( [email removed] -- backup )\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                 I can be reached on the following boards:\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                The Undiscovered       +31-2294-1442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-4442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-tRY! (USR 28k8)\n\n                Last Outpost           +31-72-201182 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-72-157682 (USR 28k8)\n\n         I know, you have to call The Netherlands for it, but it''s\n            the only boards I call on a frequent basis, sorry.\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n        You can also find me on IRC once in a while on the channels\n                   #amigascne, #amiga, #rave and #megawatt\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatIs "What is KiLLER Baud - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                        .------------------------.\n                        |  What is KiLLER Baud?  |\n                        `------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Baud is (another) statistical mumbo jumbo door.  It is able to\n   create  some  nice  statistics  about  the baud rate your users use to\n   logon your system.  It is styled in the same way as @{"KiLLER Comment" link KiLLERComment}, so\n   if  you  are familiar with that door, you will know that the output of\n   it will be fair enough :-)\n\n   I  made  this  door  as a simple diversion of some other projects I am\n   working  on.   This  door  was created in two days and I don''t feel it\n   should  be  released  to  the  public  because  it is too simple to be\n   released.   The  idea  is not my own, I saw another ''accumulative baud\n   report''  door  and  I  am  still  recovering from the output that door\n   creates.   I  don''t  know where some people get their inspiration from\n   but that output reminded me of kindergarten ;-)\n\n   And  because  of  the  nice  respons  I  got from some sysops about my\n   previous  releases,  I  thought  it  would  be  nice  to  make another\n   statistical  door,  sysops can''t get enough of BBS statistics.\n\n   Anyway,  I hope you like it and if you do, please give me a yell.\n\n                                                         @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Features "Features of KiLLER Baud - © Me me me and nobody else! :)"\n                           .--------------------------.\n                           |  Features of KiLLER Baud |\n                           `--------------------------''\n\n   ­ Create two pages of statistical data in one bulletin\n     (With a ''Space to Resume'' between them ofcourse)\n\n   ­ Create two pages of statistical data divided over 2 bulletins\n     (So two separate files)\n     \n   ­ Create either one of the two possible bulletins.\n     (You can choose between @{"Baud Usage" link BaudUsage} and @{"Last Bauds" link LastBauds})\n\n   ­ Only show the output on screen, don''t create the bulletins.\n     (Two pages, not selectable).\n\n   Other program features are:\n\n   · Fast baud detecting, virtually no delay for users.\n   · Very small data file to create bulletins\n   · You can remove baud rates from the bulletin(s).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Install "How to Install KiLLER Baud - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC (that''s me)"\n                      .------------------------------.\n                      |  How to install KiLLER Baud  |\n                      `------------------------------''\n\n   You are free to call the programs whatever you want and you can locate\n   the  program  wherever  you want.  I hate programs that have hardcoded\n   paths in their source.\n\n   The KiLLER_Baud executable has got a double function:  update the data\n   file  and create the bulletin(s).  Depending on available tooltypes in\n   the command icon, KiLLER Baud will know what to do.\n\n   ­­ Baud Detection ­­\n\n   First  of  all,  make  sure  that  KiLLER_Baud is run at least once to\n   update the data file.  I suggest you install it as an MCI command in a\n   bulletin  that  is  shown  to  every  user  that logs in (or logs out,\n   whatever fits you best).\n\n   · Example ·\n\n   ~CC_KB-DETECT\n   \n   · End ·\n\n   To  have  KiLLER_Baud  only  update the data file, create an icon that\n   holds  the tooltype "DETECT_BAUD".  If this tooltype is present in the\n   icon with what KiLLER_Baud was started, the data file will be updated.\n   This data file will reside in the same directory as the program itself\n   and will  be called "KiLLER_Baud.Data" and is 484 bytes in size.  Note\n   that  the  bulletin(s)  will not be created!  An example icon has been\n   provided called "KB-DETECT.info".\n   \n   Important tooltypes for that command are:\n   \n   DETECT_BAUD             ­ This tooltype MUST be present if you want KB\n                             to update the data file.\n                             \n   LOCAL_NODE=<number>     ­ KiLLER_Baud  will  not add your local logins\n                             to its data file.  Normally,  the local node\n                             would be node 0, but certain boards that run\n                             /X-Fido  for example have their last node as\n                             their local node.  So with this tooltype you\n                             can specify which node is the local node.\n                             \n                             You  can  also specify a node which does not\n                             exist,  and  your local logins will be added\n                             to the data file.\n                             \n                             Default value is node 0.\n\n   ­­ Bulletin Creation ­­\n\n   Unless  you  want  to install KiLLER_Baud as a regular /X command that\n   can  be  started  from  the  main  prompt,  you  also  need to install\n   KiLLER_Baud  as  another  MCI command, but this time to have it create\n   the bulletin(s).\n\n   You  can  specify the name(s) of the bulletin(s) with tooltypes.  Here\n   are the important tooltypes for this command:\n\n   BULL=<filename>         ­ When specified, one bulletin will be created\n                             containing 2 pages of statistics,  separated\n                             by a "Space to Resume".\n\n   BULL.1=<filename>       ­ This  will  result in one bulletin that only\n                             contains the @{"Baud Usage" link BaudUsage} information.\n\n   BULL.2=<filename>       ­ This  induces KiLLER_Baud to create only the\n                             @{"Last Bauds" link LastBauds} bulletin and nothing more.\n\n   NOTE¹ : The `BULL'' tooltype overrides both BULL.1 and BULL.2 !!\n\n   NOTE² : If you ommit all of these tooltypes, KiLLER_Baud will show the\n           bulletins on screen and will not create any bulletin.\n           \n   If  you  wish,  you  can  have  certain  baud  rates  removed from the\n   bulletins.   Normally,  the  bulletin would contain baud rates ranging\n   from  SLOW  to FAST, whereas SLOW means all baud rates below 9600 baud\n   and  FAST all baudrates above the highest speed available. Please note\n   that both the SLOW and FAST baud rates will not be put in the bulletin\n   until they are actually used.\n\n   For  example,  there  is  a BBS somewhere in Finland that has no 12000\n   baud users.  As strange as it is, this was true.  But the bulletin did\n   contain  the  12000  baud  line.  It was much disliked by the sysop of\n   that  board.   It even got to a point where he get himself drunk, felt\n   bad (a la Michael Jackson) and called The Undiscovered BBS.\n\n   There  he  wrote me a message saying that he hated the 12000 baud line\n   and  that  it  should  be removed.  I made him clear that although his\n   board  did  not  have 12000 baud users, the Undiscovered has got them!\n   He felt like an ass and left crying.  I felt so bad about this, that I\n   made it possible to leave out any baudrate you like.  However, this is\n   purely  COSMETIC.  The 12000 connects are still being counted and they\n   are  also used for the total number of connects.  But it was the least\n   I could do for such a lonely guy (snif).\n\n   So  without telling you the name of the sysop (QUARTZ QUARTZ QUARTZ!),\n   this is how to disable baudrates:\n\n   NO_SLOW     - Disable  SLOW baudrate\n   NO_9600     - Disable  9600 baudrate\n   NO_12000    - Disable 12000 baudrate (see! :) )\n   NO_14400    - Disable 14400 baudrate\n   ...\n   NO_FAST     - Disable FAST baudrate\n   \n   These  tooltypes  can be put in the K-Baud tooltype and remember, they\n   are  purely  cosmetic,  if  you  disable all baudrates and look at the\n   bulletin,  there  will  be NO baudrates mentioned, but there will be a\n   total number of connects and a favourite baudrate.\n\n   ­­ Regular /X Command ­­\n\n   If  you want it to install like a regular command, install it like any\n   other  command but make sure you remove the BULL#?  tooltypes from the\n   icon or it will create bulletins instead of showing them.\n\n   ­­ Overview ­­\n\n   ONCE AGAIN (but in short):\n   \n      · K-Baud can either create bulletins or display them pronto\n      · To make it create bulletins, either put the BULL tooltype\n        or BULL.x tooltypes in the icon (NOT BOTH!).\n      · To disable baudrates use tooltype: NO_<baudrate>\n      · If  you  remove all BULL* tooltypes from the K-Baud icon,\n        K-Baud will show the results on screen right away.\n\n   ­­ Reset the counting ­­\n\n   If  you  want  to reset the counting, simply remove the data file.  It\n   will  detect itself that the file is gone and create a new one (with a\n   new starting date).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE LastBauds "Example Output: Last Baud (Bull.2) - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n.-----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER BAUD v1.4  ^  <> Baud Rate Statistical View <>  ^  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC! |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------.--------------------------.---------------------------------.\n| Connect String | Date/Time last Connect   | Last Connect made by            |\n:----------------:--------------------------:---------------------------------:\n|   9.600 baud   | Fri Dec 16 12:14:45 1994 | ArCaDe/MYSTiC                   |\n|  12.000 baud   | Never                    | Nobody                          |\n|  14.400 baud   | Sun Dec 18 19:35:31 1994 | Kerstman                        |\n|  16.800 baud   | Sat Dec 17 09:03:12 1994 | Sal-One                         |\n|  19.200 baud   | Sun Dec 18 15:39:33 1994 | Incognito/MST                   |\n|  21.600 baud   | Sun Dec 18 11:32:50 1994 | WiSHBRiNGER                     |\n|  24.000 baud   | Fri Dec 16 23:11:22 1994 | skate BOB                       |\n|  26.400 baud   | Sun Dec 18 19:59:58 1994 | Motive Power/MST                |\n|  28.800 baud   | Sun Dec 18 02:13:26 1994 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC                |\n:----------------^--------------------------^---------------------------------:\n| Total Connects :  64041 ^ Favourite speed: 21.600 baud ^ Since: 16-Dec-1994 |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n                     This is bulletin 2 (BULL.2 tooltype).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE BaudUsage "Example Output: Baud Usage (Bull.1) - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n.-----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER BAUD v1.4  ^  <> Baud Rate Statistical View <>  ^  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC! |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------.----------------------.-------.-------.---------------------.\n| Connect String | 0%--------------100% | Total | Perc% | Overall             |\n:----------------:----------------------:-------:-------:---------------------:\n|   9.600 baud   | ####                 |    52 |    5% | Doing fine          |\n|  12.000 baud   | ######               |    86 |    9% | Popular rate        |\n|  14.400 baud   | #########            |   120 |   13% | Steady size.        |\n|  16.800 baud   | ############         |   154 |   17% | Substantial         |\n|  19.200 baud   | #                    |    17 |    1% | Among others        |\n|  21.600 baud   | #####                |    66 |    7% | Steady basis        |\n|  24.000 baud   | ####                 |    59 |    6% | Steady basis        |\n|  26.400 baud   | #################### |   255 |   29% | THE baud rate!      |\n|  28.800 baud   | ##                   |    34 |    3% | Doing fine          |\n:----------------^----------------------^-------^-------^---------------------:\n| Total Connects :    867 ^ Favourite speed: 26.400 baud ^ Since: 16-Dec-1994 |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n   The  graphical percentages shown are a RELATIVE comparison between the\n   most used baud rates and the rest.\n\n   Both  the  `Slower''  and  `Faster'' baudrate are ONLY shown when it has\n   been actually used by someone.\n\n                   (And yes, this output is a fraud :) )\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE History "History of KiLLER Baud - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                          .--------------------------.\n                          |  History of KiLLER Baud  |\n                          `--------------------------''\n\n   \n   1.4.0    02-Feb-95   Fixed       After spending two hours with on the\n                                    with Muad''Dib, we finally discovered\n                                    that I forgot to put the tooltype\n                                    TYPE=XIM in the icon that I delivered\n                                    in the package. This caused quite\n                                    some problems on some boards, but not\n                                    on mine ;) But we found it, and I\n                                    fixed it so it works again.\n                                    *Reported by Muad''Dib*\n\n   1.3.1    02-Feb-95   Fixed       I misplaced the code from the KiLLER\n                                    detect program causing two errors ;(\n                                    This has been fixed, sorry dudes.\n                                    *Detected by NightShade & Motive Power*\n\n   1.3.0    05-Jan-95   Improved    Put the code to update the data file\n                                    into the main KiLLER_Baud program to\n                                    save space. This saved about 14KB.\n\n   1.2.2    04-Jan-95   NEW         You can now have baud rates removed\n                                    from the bulletin with the tooltypes:\n                                    NO_<baudrate>.\n                                    *Cried for by Quartz/MST*\n\n   1.2.1    25-Dec-94   Improved    Added two more checks that prevent a\n                                    division by zero (guru #80000005).\n\n   1.2.0    21-Dec-94   Improved    The baud rates "Slower" and "Faster" are\n                                    now only shown when they are actually in\n                                    use. It''s a cosmetic change.\n                                    *Requested by Muad''Dib*\n\n   1.1.0    20-Dec-94   Improved    The graphical percentage bar is now made\n                                    relative to the most used baud rate for\n                                    better representation.\n\n   1.0.0    18-Dec-94   Release     Muad''Dib got the first version to test.\n\n   1.0.0    18-Dec-94   NEW         Created KiLLER Baud.\n\n                                    -*-\n\n                                    OBSOLETE! Put the code into the\n                                    main executable (saving about 14KB).\n\n   1.2.1    25-Dec-94   Improved    When the data file is first created,\n                                    all variables will be cleared now on\n                                    a more firm basis.\n\n   1.2.0    21-Dec-94   Improved    Made it work with the new structure and\n                                    revised it to 1.2.0 for compatibility\n                                    reasons.\n\n   1.0.1    18-Dec-94   Fixed       KiLLER Detect now detects the line speed\n                                    and not the speed between modem and\n                                    computer (sigh).\n                                    Reported by: Muad''Dib/DLT\n\n   1.0.0    16-Dec-94   NEW         Created KiLLER Detect.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Greetings "Aloha! - By KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                                .-------------.\n                                |  Greetings  |\n                                `-------------''\n\n   Muad''Dib/DLT/iFT     Let''s crash your machine! Moehaha! :)\n   \n   Quartz/MYSTiC        You cry baby! :)\n\n   Motive Power/MST     28k8 beats 26k4 bud... ;)\n   \n   I-GO/Perseus         To bad you weren''t at the O22VW\n   \n   Zed                  I hope to contact you soon!\n   \n   Jake/Prestige        One day I will come and leech, don''t worry :)\n   \n   Tycoon/MYSTiC        It was fun, but to all good things come an end\n\n   Shuyuki Tanaka       Try to contact me at my Internet address! Japan\n                        is very very very cool!\n\n                      ...and to all my other good friends!\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE KiLLERComment "KiLLER-WHAT? - © 1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n         «« THIS IS AN EXCERPT OUT OF THE GUIDE OF KILLER COMMENT ««\n\n                         .---------------------------.\n                         |  What is KiLLER Comment?  |\n                         `---------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER Comment is number 1001 in the row of a [C]omment doors.  But this\n   is not just *a* comment door, otherwise I wouldn''t have made it.  I have\n   some profound reasons.\n\n   Despite  the  fact  that  there  must be over a 1000 different [C]omment\n   doors,  there  is not one comment door that suits my needs.  I have seen\n   plenty of them but to me they all look ugly, have static interfaces, are\n   as flexible as a log of wood and generally suck :-).\n\n   With  this mind I''ve tried to make KiLLER Comment as smart and pretty as\n   I  can  and  I  feel I have succeeded.  I have never seen a comment door\n   that  works  as  simple  as  this  one  with  a fair amount of so-called\n   ''intelligence'' and comes with a nice GUI.\n\n   What  is  this  ''intelligence'' I speak about you say?  Well, all comment\n   doors  sofar  weren''t  able  to see that if I typed "c kill" I wanted to\n   comment to "KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" nor where they able to see that if I typed\n   "c  amiga" I wanted to comment to the Amiga co-sysop.  KiLLER Comment is\n   able to detect this!\n\n   Another  thing  is  security.   Every  new  user  was  able to see which\n   cosysops  you  had,  but  by giving every cosysop a security level, they\n   will  not see their names/jobs.  And if there is only one (co)sysop they\n   are  allowed  to  see,  they  will  not  even  see it is done via KiLLER\n   Comment.\n\n   Also,  per user in the [C]omment list there is a check wether he/she has\n   got access to the conference that KiLLER [C]omment is called from, so it\n   is  impossible  to write a message to someone who doesn''t have access to\n   that conference.\n\n   What I personally also dislike are comment doors that only fill like 25%\n   of  your  screen.   Not  that  you  should put a lot of nonsense in your\n   comment  door,  no,  but  KiLLER  Comment puts an extra line between the\n   names  of  the  (co-)sysops  if  that''s possible with the user''s maximum\n   numbers  of  lines  he  can  handle  on his screen.  It''s these kinds of\n   things I miss in those 13-in-a-dozen [C]omment doors.  That''s why I made\n   KiLLER Comment.\n\n   And  oh, just like I first introduced cursor control in ConfScan, KiLLER\n   comment also uses cursor control for selecting a user you can write to.\n\n                             »» END OF EXCERPT ««\n\n   To get hold of this tool, simply @{"contact" link Contact} me.\n\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),'{"TYPE":"XIM"}','auto-added',41290,5,0,NULL,NULL,'klr_bps','scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('klr_db14','KLR_DB14.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KLR_DB14.LHA','KiLLER_Baud','XIM','KiLLER_Baud',NULL,NULL,'KLR','.-\_  \/  _/   __/______\/___/-------------. | /   \/   \______  \_  \/  \_   mYSTIC!   | :/     \____/   _____/___    / /X dIVISION .',replace('  __/\  __________ _ ___  /\__              \n.-\_  \/  _/   __/______\/___/-------------.\n| /   \/   \______  \_  \/  \_   mYSTIC!   |\n:/     \____/   _____/___    / /X dIVISION .\n/_______\-\_____/--sTZ--l___/--------------:\n| KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC BRiNGS: KiLLER-BAUD 1.4 |\n| *Creates bulls about users'' baud-rates!* :\n: MAJOR BUGFiX AND CORRECTED SUPPLiED iCON !\n| o Exclude baud-rates  o Nice lay-out     ¡\n: o Two pages of info!  o Future proof!    |\n`------------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),'KiLLER_Baud.Guide',replace('@DATABASE "KiLLER-Comment.GUiDE"\n@REMARK	.-------------------------------------------------------.\n@REMARK  | This document should be read with AmigaGuide (OS 2.x) |\n@REMARK  | or MultiView (OS 3.x) or any other guide reader. Tnx. |\n@REMARK  `-------------------------------------------------------''\n@REMARK	$VER: KiLLER Baud 1.4.0\n@WIDTH 80\n@NODE MAIN "KiLLER Comment - MYSTiC 1994 - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                             Guide to KiLLER Baud\n                 for Ami-Express 3.38+ Bulletin Board Systems\n\n                             Program version: 1.4.0\n                Compilation Date/Time: Thu Feb  2 21:30:48 1995\n\n                         © 1994/1995 by @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n\n                   ... another fine MYSTiC /X production ...\n\n      @{" First words... " link WhatIs}    What is KiLLER Baud\n      @{" Features       " link Features}    What can it do?\n      @{" Installation   " link Install}    How to install KiLLER Baud on your system\n      @{" Example 1      " link BaudUsage}    Example output of bulletin 1\n      @{" Example 2      " link LastBauds}    Example output on bulletin 2\n      @{" History        " link History}    History of KiLLER Baud\n      @{" Greetings      " link Greetings}    Big hellos and messages\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Contact "How to contact KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC - © Me me me! :)"\n                            .--------------------.\n                            |  Contacting me...  |\n                            `--------------------''\n\n       For FAST response (preferred) please contact me on Internet:\n\n                               [email removed]\n\n                    ( [email removed] -- backup )\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                 I can be reached on the following boards:\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n                The Undiscovered       +31-2294-1442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-4442 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-2294-tRY! (USR 28k8)\n\n                Last Outpost           +31-72-201182 (USR 28k8)\n                                       +31-72-157682 (USR 28k8)\n\n         I know, you have to call The Netherlands for it, but it''s\n            the only boards I call on a frequent basis, sorry.\n\n                       - - -- --- ------- --- -- - -\n\n        You can also find me on IRC once in a while on the channels\n                   #amigascne, #amiga, #rave and #megawatt\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE WhatIs "What is KiLLER Baud - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                        .------------------------.\n                        |  What is KiLLER Baud?  |\n                        `------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER  Baud is (another) statistical mumbo jumbo door.  It is able to\n   create  some  nice  statistics  about  the baud rate your users use to\n   logon your system.  It is styled in the same way as @{"KiLLER Comment" link KiLLERComment}, so\n   if  you  are familiar with that door, you will know that the output of\n   it will be fair enough :-)\n\n   I  made  this  door  as a simple diversion of some other projects I am\n   working  on.   This  door  was created in two days and I don''t feel it\n   should  be  released  to  the  public  because  it is too simple to be\n   released.   The  idea  is not my own, I saw another ''accumulative baud\n   report''  door  and  I  am  still  recovering from the output that door\n   creates.   I  don''t  know where some people get their inspiration from\n   but that output reminded me of kindergarten ;-)\n\n   And  because  of  the  nice  respons  I  got from some sysops about my\n   previous  releases,  I  thought  it  would  be  nice  to  make another\n   statistical  door,  sysops can''t get enough of BBS statistics.\n\n   Anyway,  I hope you like it and if you do, please give me a yell.\n\n                                                         @{"KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" link Contact}\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Features "Features of KiLLER Baud - © Me me me and nobody else! :)"\n                           .--------------------------.\n                           |  Features of KiLLER Baud |\n                           `--------------------------''\n\n   ­ Create two pages of statistical data in one bulletin\n     (With a ''Space to Resume'' between them ofcourse)\n\n   ­ Create two pages of statistical data divided over 2 bulletins\n     (So two separate files)\n     \n   ­ Create either one of the two possible bulletins.\n     (You can choose between @{"Baud Usage" link BaudUsage} and @{"Last Bauds" link LastBauds})\n\n   ­ Only show the output on screen, don''t create the bulletins.\n     (Two pages, not selectable).\n\n   Other program features are:\n\n   · Fast baud detecting, virtually no delay for users.\n   · Very small data file to create bulletins\n   · You can remove baud rates from the bulletin(s).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Install "How to Install KiLLER Baud - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC (that''s me)"\n                      .------------------------------.\n                      |  How to install KiLLER Baud  |\n                      `------------------------------''\n\n   You are free to call the programs whatever you want and you can locate\n   the  program  wherever  you want.  I hate programs that have hardcoded\n   paths in their source.\n\n   The KiLLER_Baud executable has got a double function:  update the data\n   file  and create the bulletin(s).  Depending on available tooltypes in\n   the command icon, KiLLER Baud will know what to do.\n\n   ­­ Baud Detection ­­\n\n   First  of  all,  make  sure  that  KiLLER_Baud is run at least once to\n   update the data file.  I suggest you install it as an MCI command in a\n   bulletin  that  is  shown  to  every  user  that logs in (or logs out,\n   whatever fits you best).\n\n   · Example ·\n\n   ~CC_KB-DETECT\n   \n   · End ·\n\n   To  have  KiLLER_Baud  only  update the data file, create an icon that\n   holds  the tooltype "DETECT_BAUD".  If this tooltype is present in the\n   icon with what KiLLER_Baud was started, the data file will be updated.\n   This data file will reside in the same directory as the program itself\n   and will  be called "KiLLER_Baud.Data" and is 484 bytes in size.  Note\n   that  the  bulletin(s)  will not be created!  An example icon has been\n   provided called "KB-DETECT.info".\n   \n   Important tooltypes for that command are:\n   \n   DETECT_BAUD             ­ This tooltype MUST be present if you want KB\n                             to update the data file.\n                             \n   LOCAL_NODE=<number>     ­ KiLLER_Baud  will  not add your local logins\n                             to its data file.  Normally,  the local node\n                             would be node 0, but certain boards that run\n                             /X-Fido  for example have their last node as\n                             their local node.  So with this tooltype you\n                             can specify which node is the local node.\n                             \n                             You  can  also specify a node which does not\n                             exist,  and  your local logins will be added\n                             to the data file.\n                             \n                             Default value is node 0.\n\n   ­­ Bulletin Creation ­­\n\n   Unless  you  want  to install KiLLER_Baud as a regular /X command that\n   can  be  started  from  the  main  prompt,  you  also  need to install\n   KiLLER_Baud  as  another  MCI command, but this time to have it create\n   the bulletin(s).\n\n   You  can  specify the name(s) of the bulletin(s) with tooltypes.  Here\n   are the important tooltypes for this command:\n\n   BULL=<filename>         ­ When specified, one bulletin will be created\n                             containing 2 pages of statistics,  separated\n                             by a "Space to Resume".\n\n   BULL.1=<filename>       ­ This  will  result in one bulletin that only\n                             contains the @{"Baud Usage" link BaudUsage} information.\n\n   BULL.2=<filename>       ­ This  induces KiLLER_Baud to create only the\n                             @{"Last Bauds" link LastBauds} bulletin and nothing more.\n\n   NOTE¹ : The `BULL'' tooltype overrides both BULL.1 and BULL.2 !!\n\n   NOTE² : If you ommit all of these tooltypes, KiLLER_Baud will show the\n           bulletins on screen and will not create any bulletin.\n           \n   If  you  wish,  you  can  have  certain  baud  rates  removed from the\n   bulletins.   Normally,  the  bulletin would contain baud rates ranging\n   from  SLOW  to FAST, whereas SLOW means all baud rates below 9600 baud\n   and  FAST all baudrates above the highest speed available. Please note\n   that both the SLOW and FAST baud rates will not be put in the bulletin\n   until they are actually used.\n\n   For  example,  there  is  a BBS somewhere in Finland that has no 12000\n   baud users.  As strange as it is, this was true.  But the bulletin did\n   contain  the  12000  baud  line.  It was much disliked by the sysop of\n   that  board.   It even got to a point where he get himself drunk, felt\n   bad (a la Michael Jackson) and called The Undiscovered BBS.\n\n   There  he  wrote me a message saying that he hated the 12000 baud line\n   and  that  it  should  be removed.  I made him clear that although his\n   board  did  not  have 12000 baud users, the Undiscovered has got them!\n   He felt like an ass and left crying.  I felt so bad about this, that I\n   made it possible to leave out any baudrate you like.  However, this is\n   purely  COSMETIC.  The 12000 connects are still being counted and they\n   are  also used for the total number of connects.  But it was the least\n   I could do for such a lonely guy (snif).\n\n   So  without telling you the name of the sysop (QUARTZ QUARTZ QUARTZ!),\n   this is how to disable baudrates:\n\n   NO_SLOW     - Disable  SLOW baudrate\n   NO_9600     - Disable  9600 baudrate\n   NO_12000    - Disable 12000 baudrate (see! :) )\n   NO_14400    - Disable 14400 baudrate\n   ...\n   NO_FAST     - Disable FAST baudrate\n   \n   These  tooltypes  can be put in the K-Baud tooltype and remember, they\n   are  purely  cosmetic,  if  you  disable all baudrates and look at the\n   bulletin,  there  will  be NO baudrates mentioned, but there will be a\n   total number of connects and a favourite baudrate.\n\n   ­­ Regular /X Command ­­\n\n   If  you want it to install like a regular command, install it like any\n   other  command but make sure you remove the BULL#?  tooltypes from the\n   icon or it will create bulletins instead of showing them.\n\n   ­­ Overview ­­\n\n   ONCE AGAIN (but in short):\n   \n      · K-Baud can either create bulletins or display them pronto\n      · To make it create bulletins, either put the BULL tooltype\n        or BULL.x tooltypes in the icon (NOT BOTH!).\n      · To disable baudrates use tooltype: NO_<baudrate>\n      · If  you  remove all BULL* tooltypes from the K-Baud icon,\n        K-Baud will show the results on screen right away.\n\n   ­­ Reset the counting ­­\n\n   If  you  want  to reset the counting, simply remove the data file.  It\n   will  detect itself that the file is gone and create a new one (with a\n   new starting date).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE LastBauds "Example Output: Last Baud (Bull.2) - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n.-----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER BAUD v1.4  ^  <> Baud Rate Statistical View <>  ^  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC! |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------.--------------------------.---------------------------------.\n| Connect String | Date/Time last Connect   | Last Connect made by            |\n:----------------:--------------------------:---------------------------------:\n|   9.600 baud   | Fri Dec 16 12:14:45 1994 | ArCaDe/MYSTiC                   |\n|  12.000 baud   | Never                    | Nobody                          |\n|  14.400 baud   | Sun Dec 18 19:35:31 1994 | Kerstman                        |\n|  16.800 baud   | Sat Dec 17 09:03:12 1994 | Sal-One                         |\n|  19.200 baud   | Sun Dec 18 15:39:33 1994 | Incognito/MST                   |\n|  21.600 baud   | Sun Dec 18 11:32:50 1994 | WiSHBRiNGER                     |\n|  24.000 baud   | Fri Dec 16 23:11:22 1994 | skate BOB                       |\n|  26.400 baud   | Sun Dec 18 19:59:58 1994 | Motive Power/MST                |\n|  28.800 baud   | Sun Dec 18 02:13:26 1994 | KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC                |\n:----------------^--------------------------^---------------------------------:\n| Total Connects :  64041 ^ Favourite speed: 21.600 baud ^ Since: 16-Dec-1994 |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n                     This is bulletin 2 (BULL.2 tooltype).\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE BaudUsage "Example Output: Baud Usage (Bull.1) - © KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n.-----------------------------------------------------------------------------.\n| KiLLER BAUD v1.4  ^  <> Baud Rate Statistical View <>  ^  KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC! |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n.----------------.----------------------.-------.-------.---------------------.\n| Connect String | 0%--------------100% | Total | Perc% | Overall             |\n:----------------:----------------------:-------:-------:---------------------:\n|   9.600 baud   | ####                 |    52 |    5% | Doing fine          |\n|  12.000 baud   | ######               |    86 |    9% | Popular rate        |\n|  14.400 baud   | #########            |   120 |   13% | Steady size.        |\n|  16.800 baud   | ############         |   154 |   17% | Substantial         |\n|  19.200 baud   | #                    |    17 |    1% | Among others        |\n|  21.600 baud   | #####                |    66 |    7% | Steady basis        |\n|  24.000 baud   | ####                 |    59 |    6% | Steady basis        |\n|  26.400 baud   | #################### |   255 |   29% | THE baud rate!      |\n|  28.800 baud   | ##                   |    34 |    3% | Doing fine          |\n:----------------^----------------------^-------^-------^---------------------:\n| Total Connects :    867 ^ Favourite speed: 26.400 baud ^ Since: 16-Dec-1994 |\n`-----------------------------------------------------------------------------''\n\n   The  graphical percentages shown are a RELATIVE comparison between the\n   most used baud rates and the rest.\n\n   Both  the  `Slower''  and  `Faster'' baudrate are ONLY shown when it has\n   been actually used by someone.\n\n                   (And yes, this output is a fraud :) )\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE History "History of KiLLER Baud - © 1994 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                          .--------------------------.\n                          |  History of KiLLER Baud  |\n                          `--------------------------''\n\n   \n   1.4.0    02-Feb-95   Fixed       After spending two hours with on the\n                                    with Muad''Dib, we finally discovered\n                                    that I forgot to put the tooltype\n                                    TYPE=XIM in the icon that I delivered\n                                    in the package. This caused quite\n                                    some problems on some boards, but not\n                                    on mine ;) But we found it, and I\n                                    fixed it so it works again.\n                                    *Reported by Muad''Dib*\n\n   1.3.1    02-Feb-95   Fixed       I misplaced the code from the KiLLER\n                                    detect program causing two errors ;(\n                                    This has been fixed, sorry dudes.\n                                    *Detected by NightShade & Motive Power*\n\n   1.3.0    05-Jan-95   Improved    Put the code to update the data file\n                                    into the main KiLLER_Baud program to\n                                    save space. This saved about 14KB.\n\n   1.2.2    04-Jan-95   NEW         You can now have baud rates removed\n                                    from the bulletin with the tooltypes:\n                                    NO_<baudrate>.\n                                    *Cried for by Quartz/MST*\n\n   1.2.1    25-Dec-94   Improved    Added two more checks that prevent a\n                                    division by zero (guru #80000005).\n\n   1.2.0    21-Dec-94   Improved    The baud rates "Slower" and "Faster" are\n                                    now only shown when they are actually in\n                                    use. It''s a cosmetic change.\n                                    *Requested by Muad''Dib*\n\n   1.1.0    20-Dec-94   Improved    The graphical percentage bar is now made\n                                    relative to the most used baud rate for\n                                    better representation.\n\n   1.0.0    18-Dec-94   Release     Muad''Dib got the first version to test.\n\n   1.0.0    18-Dec-94   NEW         Created KiLLER Baud.\n\n                                    -*-\n\n                                    OBSOLETE! Put the code into the\n                                    main executable (saving about 14KB).\n\n   1.2.1    25-Dec-94   Improved    When the data file is first created,\n                                    all variables will be cleared now on\n                                    a more firm basis.\n\n   1.2.0    21-Dec-94   Improved    Made it work with the new structure and\n                                    revised it to 1.2.0 for compatibility\n                                    reasons.\n\n   1.0.1    18-Dec-94   Fixed       KiLLER Detect now detects the line speed\n                                    and not the speed between modem and\n                                    computer (sigh).\n                                    Reported by: Muad''Dib/DLT\n\n   1.0.0    16-Dec-94   NEW         Created KiLLER Detect.\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Greetings "Aloha! - By KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n                                .-------------.\n                                |  Greetings  |\n                                `-------------''\n\n   Muad''Dib/DLT/iFT     Let''s crash your machine! Moehaha! :)\n   \n   Quartz/MYSTiC        You cry baby! :)\n\n   Motive Power/MST     28k8 beats 26k4 bud... ;)\n   \n   I-GO/Perseus         To bad you weren''t at the O22VW\n   \n   Zed                  I hope to contact you soon!\n   \n   Jake/Prestige        One day I will come and leech, don''t worry :)\n   \n   Tycoon/MYSTiC        It was fun, but to all good things come an end\n\n   Shuyuki Tanaka       Try to contact me at my Internet address! Japan\n                        is very very very cool!\n\n                      ...and to all my other good friends!\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE KiLLERComment "KiLLER-WHAT? - © 1995 KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC"\n\n         «« THIS IS AN EXCERPT OUT OF THE GUIDE OF KILLER COMMENT ««\n\n                         .---------------------------.\n                         |  What is KiLLER Comment?  |\n                         `---------------------------''\n\n   KiLLER Comment is number 1001 in the row of a [C]omment doors.  But this\n   is not just *a* comment door, otherwise I wouldn''t have made it.  I have\n   some profound reasons.\n\n   Despite  the  fact  that  there  must be over a 1000 different [C]omment\n   doors,  there  is not one comment door that suits my needs.  I have seen\n   plenty of them but to me they all look ugly, have static interfaces, are\n   as flexible as a log of wood and generally suck :-).\n\n   With  this mind I''ve tried to make KiLLER Comment as smart and pretty as\n   I  can  and  I  feel I have succeeded.  I have never seen a comment door\n   that  works  as  simple  as  this  one  with  a fair amount of so-called\n   ''intelligence'' and comes with a nice GUI.\n\n   What  is  this  ''intelligence'' I speak about you say?  Well, all comment\n   doors  sofar  weren''t  able  to see that if I typed "c kill" I wanted to\n   comment to "KiLLraVeN/MYSTiC" nor where they able to see that if I typed\n   "c  amiga" I wanted to comment to the Amiga co-sysop.  KiLLER Comment is\n   able to detect this!\n\n   Another  thing  is  security.   Every  new  user  was  able to see which\n   cosysops  you  had,  but  by giving every cosysop a security level, they\n   will  not see their names/jobs.  And if there is only one (co)sysop they\n   are  allowed  to  see,  they  will  not  even  see it is done via KiLLER\n   Comment.\n\n   Also,  per user in the [C]omment list there is a check wether he/she has\n   got access to the conference that KiLLER [C]omment is called from, so it\n   is  impossible  to write a message to someone who doesn''t have access to\n   that conference.\n\n   What I personally also dislike are comment doors that only fill like 25%\n   of  your  screen.   Not  that  you  should put a lot of nonsense in your\n   comment  door,  no,  but  KiLLER  Comment puts an extra line between the\n   names  of  the  (co-)sysops  if  that''s possible with the user''s maximum\n   numbers  of  lines  he  can  handle  on his screen.  It''s these kinds of\n   things I miss in those 13-in-a-dozen [C]omment doors.  That''s why I made\n   KiLLER Comment.\n\n   And  oh, just like I first introduced cursor control in ConfScan, KiLLER\n   comment also uses cursor control for selecting a user you can write to.\n\n                             »» END OF EXCERPT ««\n\n   To get hold of this tool, simply @{"contact" link Contact} me.\n\n@ENDNODE\n','\n',char(10)),'{"TYPE":"XIM"}','auto-added',34973,1,0,NULL,NULL,'klr_db14','scan',1780048560);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('kur_lo','KUR-LO.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/KUR-LO.LHA','KuR-Logoff','XIM','.----------------------------------------.',NULL,NULL,'KUR','|    KuRGaN of aFL/aBS!/CRC Presents!    |-. | [====================================] | | `----[ Logoff v1.0 ]--[ /X 4.x Door ]----'' |',replace('.----------------------------------------.\n|    KuRGaN of aFL/aBS!/CRC Presents!    |-.\n| [====================================] | |\n`----[ Logoff v1.0 ]--[ /X 4.x Door ]----'' |\n `-----------------------------------------''\n\n','\n',char(10)),'KuR-Logoff.guide',replace('@DataBase KuR-Logoff Documentation\n@Author KuRGaN(aFL/aBS!/CRC)\n@Index Documentation\n\nCopyright © 1994 KuRGaN of aFL/aBS!/CRC Australia\n\n@Remark $VER: KuR-Logoff v1.0 by KuRGaN (aFL/aBS!/CRC)\n@Remark AmigaGuide DataBase\n\n@node MAIN "KuR-Logoff Documentation © 1994 KuRGaN of aFL/aBS!/CRC Australia"\n  _____  ______________________________  ___________________  ________\n |     |/     /     |     \_______     \/    ______)_____   \|        \\n |     |     /|     |      \     |     /     |     |    |    \    |    \ \n |          /\|     |      /          /\     |     |          \   |     \\n |_____\      \___________/|_____\      \_____     |____|     /___|     /\n        \______\             [adt]\______\   |_____|    |____/    |____/ \n\n                            ---[ Presents ]---\n\n                 -----------[ KuR-Logoff v1.0 ]-----------\n\n                 Coded by KuRGaN of aFL/aBS!/CRC Australia\n\n                              @{" Description  "link description}\n                              @{" Installation "link installation}\n                              @{" Greets       "link greetings}\n@endnode\n\n@node Description "KuR-Logoff Description"\nKuR-Logoff has been coded in 100% E! by KuRGaN of aFL/aBS!/CRC Australia\n\nKuR-Logoff is a replacement for the normal Goodbye Command\nof AmiExpress ''G'' but with many more options. They include:\n\n                          Y - Yes, logoff (Same as <CR>)\n                          N - No, go back to BBS\n                          R - Relogon\n                          C - Comment to sysop\n                          ? - Help Screen\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Installation "KuR-Logoff Installation"\nCopy all files which are in the ''BBS'' directory of the archive\ninto your bbs directory.\n\nContents of archive:\n[======================]\n BBS/ Commands/ BBSCmd/      G.info\n      Doors/    KuRGaN/      KuR-Logoff\n                             KuR-Logoff.Guide\n[======================]\n\nJust copy the directory KuRGaN to your DOORS: directory\nand copy the G.info into your BBS:Commands/BBSCMD directory.\n\n                    Then Hit G at the MainLine prompt!\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Greetings "KuR-Logoff Greetings"\nGreetings go to:\n\nRaNCiD (aFL/aBS!/CRC) - When are you going to finish your door!!?\n                      - Thanks for doing this guide as well ;)\n                      - Thanks for helping me to learn AmigaE!\nBowen/SR              - How''s the BBS going dude?\nFelon/DD              - Will you EVER have your BBS running? ;)\nsTc/aFL               - What did ya think of the BBS?\n                      - Thanks for the source! this door would never\n                      - be alive :)\nDartagnan             - Get a REAL computer!! hahaha\nKRaNG                 - Finally back in the scene! Nice to see.\nX-Cell/CRC            - How''s life over there dude! ;)\nLord/aBS!             - Nice BBS dude, talk to ya voice again soon!\nFester/aBS!           - Working hard at SledgeHammer!!?\nADT/aBS!              - Will have to talk to you voice sometime!\n\n         ALL other aFL/aBS!/CRC Members + people I have forgotten\n\n@endnode\n\n/*\n** Yep, RaNCiD here, this tool is fucking better than the utter\n** crap you''re using right now!\n** Wanna know why? COZ IT''S A FUCKING...\n** Alpha Flight - aBSOLUTE! - cODE rED cOURIER Production\n**\n** p.s. Many GREEEEEEEETS! to SvEN ThE CrEATOR/aFL (Hi Buddy!)\n*/\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,NULL,9615,3,0,NULL,NULL,'kur_lo_kur_logoff','scan',1780048560);
