_:___________                                            |\n|     \   ____/___   ¦   _    __/______                                      |\n|    /    /___   _   |   /____\ ____  /_       nODE#0: +49-aSK_aKIRO!        | \n|   /________/_______|   ___/   _____/ /       nODE#1: +49-rINGDOWN!         | \n`--/-------------.aC!¦____|/__________/                                      | \n                 `---:-------------.    a2ooo/o3o^620 mB hARDKEKS^9 mB rAM   |\n                                   |                                         |\n                                   `-----------------------------------------''\n\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE Description "dESCRiPTiON"\nyO fOLKS...\n\ndIZ iZ aNUTHA pHUCCIN` sTATUS-dOOR 4 /X ......\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n@NODE greetings\nGreetz fly 2:\n\naKIRO&dR.pOOP / sCX-cO-lEADER 4 sUGESSTIONZ\ncRAZY / sCX-lEADER 4 aNSI`n sO\ndiSC-DEViL / sCX 4 bEING a cOOL oNE\ndiABOLO / sCX 4 tRADiNG lIKE hELL\nsTEAM 4 dA /X-eNGINE \n\naLL oTHER sCX-mEMBAZ aND aLL cOOL gUYZ iN dA sCENE !\n\n\n@ENDNODE\n\n\n                                           (»·CrEEpIn-DeaTh·/·sCX!·«)\n\n\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,NULL,30659,5,0,NULL,NULL,'sta_scx_status','scan',1780048749);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('stake','STAKE.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/STAKE.LHA','SmallTalk','XIM','IconEdit',NULL,NULL,NULL,'|   -|-   /   l_  \\    __)  *    Presents | | *      /         \\   _)__+  +   Today:  | -======-/    ___    \\      )-=============-',replace('.----------___ _/\ /\_____-----------------.\n|   -|-   /   l_  \\    __)  *    Presents |\n| *      /         \\   _)__+  +   Today:  |\n-======-/    ___    \\      )-=============-\n|  +  _/      |      \\____/nErGy  *  [Ns] |\n`-----`-------l_______)iDDeN---------------''\n \n sTAKE yOUR cREDITS v 1.0  iS a vERY lITTLE\n        dOOR fOR /X wRITTEN iN aREXX\n    iT aLLOWS tHE uSER sTAKE hIS cREDITS','\n',char(10)),'sTAKE.dOC',replace('                   sTAKE yOUR cREDITS v1.0 by dR fAUSTUS\n                   \n                               -- Install --\n\n                         Copy the dir on your bbs:\n\n                              -- What is?? --\n\n       This little door for /X allows the user to stake his credits.\n\n      If the user has disabled ratio the program gives him 3:1 ratio.\n\n                        -- Tradeware & Funware! --\n\n This program was made for fun. But i''ll be very happy if you call my board\n        not only for support but also to say me that you like this!\n\n                  Dr Faustus ( iNVASiON / HiDDeN EnErGy )\n                                      \n                                      ','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',11804,3,0,NULL,NULL,'stake','scan',1780048749);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('status','STATUS.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/STATUS.LHA','Status','XIM','Status',NULL,'Faktor/Independent',NULL,'## Replacement for during logon statics   ## ## -------------------------------------- ## ##   For AmiExpress or compatibilties     ##',replace('############################################\n## Replacement for during logon statics   ##\n## -------------------------------------- ##\n##   For AmiExpress or compatibilties     ##\n##                                        ##\n##  Copyright (c) by Faktor/Independent   ##\n############################################\n','\n',char(10)),'lOSeNDOS.guide',replace('                   \n                     __  _ __  /\__ _  _ __/\  __ _  __\n                    _\/___   \/              \/   ___\/_\n              ......\/\  /........................\  /\/......\n             :         \/                          \/         :\n             :      .___             .___         .___        :\n             :   _ _|   \      ______|   \  ______|   \       :\n             :   \\\__   7___  \______   /  \__   ____/       :\n             :      /.   |   \__/.  l/   \___/_____   \__ _   :\n             :     /::.  |    _/::.       _/::.  l/    _///   :\n             :     \___________\___________\____________\     :\n             :___        .___        .___        .___         :___\n     _ ______|   \ ______|   \ ______|   \ ______|   \  ______|   \\n     \\\__   ____/ \______   / \______   / \______   /  \__   ____/\n        /.   ___|___/.   |   \__/.  _|   \__/.  l/   \___/_____   \__ _\n       /::.  l/   _/::.  |    _/::. \___  _/::.       _/::.  l/    _///\n       \_ _________\_____|_____\___________\___________\___________ \\n         Y   :                                                :[nM$]Y\n      .------''                                                `------.\n      :eXON/rIOT^jORMAS · gHANDY/gODS · rAHIEM/eSSENCE · cELIC/rEKTUM: \n      `------.                                                .------''\n             `------------------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',10018,5,0,NULL,NULL,'tms_us16','scan',1780048749);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('stcps106','STCPS106.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/STCPS106.LHA',NULL,'XIM','sonnencreme.exe','1.06','P0T-NOoDLE',NULL,NULL,replace('Super-TopCPS v1.06 by P0T-NOoDLE\n','\n',char(10)),'STOPCPS_V1.06.DOC',replace('Compared to the wholescale  violence  of  capital and government,  political\nacts of violence are but a  drop in the  ocean...  High strung like a violin\nstring, [the anarchists] weep and moan for life, so relentless, so cruel, so\nterribly inhuman. In a desperate moment the string breaks. Emma Goldman,1910\n\nSuper-TopCPS - Top CPS Recorder for /X - Docs, Code, Output ©1993 P0T-NOoDLE\n============================================================================\nFeatures:	Does NOT lose CPS Records when you cut your CallersLog!\n		MultiNode Compatable (And of course single node too).\n		FAST & SMALL 680x0 Assembler Coding! (AND I MEAN FAST!)\n		Beautiful & Informative Output! Look at Example output.\n		Upto a Top-20 with UserName,BaudRate,Filename,CPS-Speed...\n		Pure, re-entrant code (resident-able).\n		Gunna format your harddrive (NOT).\n\nMini-Index\n==========------------------------------------------------------------------\n	1	Command Line\n	2	Command: D-ocs\n	3	Command: C-Lear\n	4	Command: U-pdate\n	5	Command: O-utput\n	6	Command Stacking\n	7	Note: CPS Rates in Storage File\n	8	Thanx & Mini-Greetings\n	9	Slagging off for this util\n	10	BBS Numbers for Contact/BugReports/Etc\n	11	Program Release History\n	12	BOLLOX\n\n\n\nCOMMAND LINE\n============----------------------------------------------------------------\n	Super-TopCPS  has a very  flexible command line.  You may have it do\nmany operations in just one run by using command stacking (See below).  Each\ncommand is always preceeded with a "-", eg "-C" is command "C".  Most of the\ncommands also have extra options and requirements.\n	Output filenames may NOT contain either "-" or "ÿ".\n	Output filenames are converted to uppercase, not that it matters.\n	Entering an Output filename of "*" will output to your CLI window.\n\n	While reading the  docs on  each command,  I advise that you also be\nlooking at the CommandLine help text for the Command templates. (Now there''s\nsomething you can''t do on a fucking PC (or a toy(games console!))).\n\n\n\nCommand D-ocs\n=============---------------------------------------------------------------\n	This command creates this doc file by uncrunching it from the Super-\nTopCPS code and outputing it to the specified filename.  The  example  would\noutput the docs to `RAM:STC.DOC''\n\nSTopCPS -D"RAM:STC.DOC"\n\n	By the way,  this is done so that  the util is never spread  without\nit''s docs,  and so there is never the age old question of  HOW THE FUCK DOES\nTHIS WORK  when  you''re  setting up  a board  from  someone''s  HD-Backup  or\nwhatever.  It does not  slow down  the program  at all,  and as the docs are\ncrunched,  memory loss is hella-small  (You''d lose  three times as much just\nby using a shitty C program instead of STC!).\n\n\n\nCommand C-lear\n==============--------------------------------------------------------------\n	This will create a new and blank storage file  if it does not exist,\nor blank the already  existing  storage file.  A storage file  must  be made\nbefore Commands U and O will work.\n	The storage file is called `S:STopCPS.Store''  and is where STC keeps\nall its information about the fastest 20 uploads and downloads.\n\nExample: STopCPS -C\n\n\n\nCommand U-pdate\n===============-------------------------------------------------------------\n	This will check the  last caller  to your board for any  uploads  or\ndownloads  which were  fast enough to get them into your Top-20.  If any are\nfound, they will be added into the Storage file.\n	You must run this Command everytime someone logs off a node.\n	U-pdate  requires  the node number  which you wish  to update (0-9).\nExample1  would be used  after a user  has just  logged off Node0.  Example2\nwould be used after a user has just logged off Node1.\n\nExample1: STopCPS -U"0"\nExample2: STopCPS -U"1"\n\n	Note that  you can  use one logoff  script for all of your  nodes by\nputting  `.k node''  at the start of your logoff script, and having  `<NODE>''\nin place of the node number. See the included example logoff script.\n\n	NOTE: Due to the way that  STC  looks for the Baud Rates you have to\nhave your HST set to output FULL connect strings,  like any non-idiot would.\n(eg "CONNECT 9600/ARQ/HST/HST/V42BIS" NOT "CONNECT 9600")\n\n\n\nCommand O-utput\n===============-------------------------------------------------------------\n	This command outputs a list of all time fastest transfers, using the\ninfo in the storage file.  You must give it an output path and the number of\nusers you wish to be displayed (0-20).\n	Example1 will output the top 10 users to `RAM:TEST_BULL''.\n	Example2 will output the top 20 users to `BBS:NODE0/BULL100.TXT.GR''\n\nExample1: STopCPS -O"RAM:TEST_BULL"10\nExample2: STopCPS -O"BBS:NODE0/BULL100.TXT.GR"15\n\n                                             O-utput - Header & Tailer texts\n---------------------------------------------===============================\n	If  `S:STopCPS.Header.txt(.gr)''  exists, it will be added to the top\nof the output, and if  `S:STopCPS.Tailer.txt(.gr)''  exists, it will be added\nto the end of the output.  The ".gr" is dependant on  Option N being On/Off.\n\n                                                  O-utput - Option N: NoANSI\n--------------------------------------------------==========================\n	This option removes all ANSI codes from the text and slightly alters\nthe output so that it will look better without the ANSI. It will replace the\n"|"''s in the header line with "-"''s etc.\n	This option also effects what Header/Tailer text is used (See above)\n	The Example  will output  the top 20 speeds to  `RAM:BULL'' ,  with a\nNoANSI output, and add `S:STopCPS.Header.TXT'' &/or `S:STopCPS.Tailer.TXT'' if\neither/both exist.\n\nExample: STopCPS -ON"RAM:BULL"20\n\n                                     O-utput - Option U: UpperCase Filenames\n-------------------------------------=======================================\n	This option converts all the filename texts in the output into upper\ncase.  In my opinion it looks  better this way,  but some people may wish to\nhave the filenames left as they were originally.\n	The Example will output the top 5 speeds to  `T:FISHFINGER''  with an\nANSI output,  and filenames in UpperCase, adding `S:STopCPS.Header.TXT'' &/or\n`S:STopCPS.Tailer.TXT'' if either/both exist.\n\nExample: STopCPS -OU"T:FISHFINGER"5\n\n                                           O-utput - Option T: Text Dissable\n-------------------------------------------=================================\n	This option simply ignores and does not use the Header/Tailer texts\nin S:. Simple.\n	The Example will output the top 3 speeds to `RAM:ERP'' without ANSI,\nand with Uppercase filenames, and will NOT add any Head or Tail texts, even\nif they do exist.\n\nExample: STopCPS -ONUT"RAM:ERP"3\n\n\n\nCommand Stacking\n================------------------------------------------------------------\n	A user has just logged off node 1. You his/her transfers checked and\nadded to the store file if needed,  and you also want to update the ANSI and\nnon-ANSI TopCPS lists in the Amiga conf bulletins directory. No problem:\n\nSTopCPS -U"1" -O"BBS:Ami/Bulls/Bull1.TXT.GR" -ON"BBS:Ami/Bulls/Bull1.TXT"\n\n	Should be pretty clear to the non-stupid among you.\n\n\n\nNote\n====------------------------------------------------------------------------\n	The  CPS  rates in the  Storage  file are  checked  for tempering by\ncheating sysops. So don''t change them, and also, don''t touch my name!\n\n\n\nTHANX\n=====-----------------------------------------------------------------------\n	They all know who they are and why there are here,  but for the rest\nof you, I must thank the following people, without whom STopCPS would not be\nwhat it is.\n\nSquiz                 - Who wrote a TopCPS  Util in Basic for me a long time\n                      - ago (obviously before he left the scene!) from which\n                      - I''ve borrowed ideas,some of which were mine to start\n                      - with.\nThrash   of   Anthrox - Technical Assistance and Disk Drives!\nFay of the real world - <Insert musshy B.S. which has all been said before!>\nBluelight             - Quick Bug Reporting & Sending Logs/Outputs with it!\n\nMy other friends & group members in the scene.\nMy other friends & family outside of the scene.\n\nAnd, In No Duck-Fucking-Order... Oranges,Bananas,Fish Fingers,Beef Curtains,\nYour Mum,Pepsi,Pizza,Metallica,Megadeth-?,Anthrax,Slayer,C.O.C,Ugly Kid Joe,\nFaith No More,Nine-Inch Nails,D.R.I,Exodus,MortalSin,OverKill,Paradox,Toxik,\nFlotsam and Jetsam,Lawnmower Deth,Testament,Xentrix,Venom,Re-Animator,S.O.D,\nNuclear Assault,Guns''n''Roses,Possessed,Skid Row,Onslaught,MorotHead,Pantera,\nand people who talk like this: "swark swark swark swark" That''s you that is.\n\nFUCK THE REST OF THE WORLD\n\n\n\nP0T''S NOTE FOR THIS UTIL:\n=========================---------------------------------------------------\n	Aimed at people who make and use Archive-BBSAd-Adders that strip the\nother adds.  You idiots  are so fucking hypocritical.  If you dissagree with\nBBS Ads that''s fine, strip them all. BUT, if you strip and then add your own\nBBS Ad, you are  STUPID!  Think about this. If we all did this, when someone\ndownloads a file from a BBS, they simply get the add from that BBS.  I don''t\nthink that anyone''s users need to be asked to call a board that they already\ncall??  Or, do you think that you''re Board is so special that it should have\nit''s ad and  noone else''s in the file!?  Or is your Board so  shit  that you\nwanna remove other adds so that it looks like it got the file first??\n	Whatever the reason,  if you strip AND add,  you''re a stupid,  lame,\nno-thinking,  idiotic person,  and you will probably end up raping your  own\nchildren after your sex-partner gets a pair of glasses.\n\n	Also,  stupid  assholes  who strip the  front text from DMS files or\n"*.displayme" from LHAs are extra-extra-STUPID! These texts may (or may not)\ncontain important information about the archive they are in.  Nearly all the\nutilities  indiscriminately  delete these files,  whether they are important\ninfo or just another BBS-Ad.\n	While I''m on the subject, DMS-Boot-Block adds are a nice idea, pitty\nthat the util-makers didn''t fucking think. What if it''s a fix or part b of a\ndisk or whatever? Not all,but some of the Boot-Block adders don''t check that\nthe DMS file will actually write the  correct bootblock  after the Add-Block\nhas been written. EG: If it''s a fix that  doesn''t change  the bootblock, the\nfix is written and the  propper bootblock  of the disk has a  BBS-Ad written\nall over it,  thus totally screwing up the disk and getting someone a little\nbit pissed off! IT''S STUPID! DON''T DO IT!\n\n                     To the person who did that little "Ultimate Util" Joke:\n---------------------=======================================================\nHAHAHA You make me laugh man! I don''t know whether to be mad at you or what?\nWell, I take it as a joke, a fucking wierd one! Nice try, YOU MUTHERFUCKER!!\n\n\n\nContact\n=======---------------------------------------------------------------------\n	If you can call England, drop me a message on...\n<./\.|    (Anthrox UKHQ) +44-813466842 Sysop:P0T-NOoDLE\n	Or if you can''t, call...\nMartyrium (Anthrox USHQ) +713-460-8217 Sysop:Wintermute (5 nodes-Ringdown)\n\n	Contact me if you have any bug reports or ideas on any of our utils,\nor if you have a cool idea for a new util that you want written.\n\n\n\nHistory\n=======---------------------------------------------------------------------\nVersion - Release Number -------------------------------------- Release Date\nv1.00 ===-[01]-================================================ 2nd Jan 1993\n	Initial Release.\nv1.05 ===-[02]-=============================================== 16th Jan 1993\n	BUG FIX: If someone leeched and got a TopCPS, and the next caller\n	         did not upload at all, he would get the same speed/filename\n	         or he would get a wierd username. (I''m Hella-Stupid!)\n	O-utput and C-lear routines almost completely re-written.\n	Information output to CLI about operations performed.\n	New O-utput Oution: T - Dissables header/tailer texts.\nv1.06 ===-[03]-=============================================== 18th Jan 1993\n	BUG FIX: Root-Dir Download paths do not mess up filenames anymore.\n============================================================================\n	About this release, in detail:\nWhen working out the name of downloaded  files from the callerslog, the util\npreviously searched for "/" and would take the word after it,  assuming that\nall files would be  downloaded  from  a  directory  and not a root-dir or an\nassign (such as "Dh1:" or "FILES:". This was stupid, I know! Now it searches\nfor a "/" OR a ":" and should work fine!\n	Thanx (or no thanx!)  to  Bluelight  for once again finding this bug\nin record time (how long was it,  3 hours since release?) and sending me the\ncallerslog so that I could discover the obvious and stupid mistake I''d made!\n============================================================================\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',21357,5,0,NULL,NULL,'stcps106','scan',1780048749);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('stdcall','STDCALL.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/STDCALL.LHA',NULL,'XIM','LE-win5-96.exe',NULL,NULL,NULL,'|NEW    ____________   ____________  BRINGS| |ORDER  \    \      \  \           \    YOU| |       /     \     /\ /    \      /\      |',replace(replace('.------------------------------------------.\r\n|NEW    ____________   ____________  BRINGS|\r\n|ORDER  \    \      \  \           \    YOU|\r\n|       /     \     /\ /    \      /\      |\r\n- - ---/    \      /--/     /     /-/ -- ---\r\n - - -/______\____/--/___________/-/- - - --\r\n|     \_______\___\/ \___________\/<md>    |\r\n|------------------------------------------|\r\n| LAST24HRS UPLOADS BOTH AMI/X & SYSTEM/X  |\r\n`------------------------------------------''\r\n','\r',char(13)),'\n',char(10)),'lOSeNDOS.guide',replace('                   \n                     __  _ __  /\__ _  _ __/\  __ _  __\n                    _\/___   \/              \/   ___\/_\n              ......\/\  /........................\  /\/......\n             :         \/                          \/         :\n             :      .___             .___         .___        :\n             :   _ _|   \      ______|   \  ______|   \       :\n             :   \\\__   7___  \______   /  \__   ____/       :\n             :      /.   |   \__/.  l/   \___/_____   \__ _   :\n             :     /::.  |    _/::.       _/::.  l/    _///   :\n             :     \___________\___________\____________\     :\n             :___        .___        .___        .___         :___\n     _ ______|   \ ______|   \ ______|   \ ______|   \  ______|   \\n     \\\__   ____/ \______   / \______   / \______   /  \__   ____/\n        /.   ___|___/.   |   \__/.  _|   \__/.  l/   \___/_____   \__ _\n       /::.  l/   _/::.  |    _/::. \___  _/::.       _/::.  l/    _///\n       \_ _________\_____|_____\___________\___________\___________ \\n         Y   :                                                :[nM$]Y\n      .------''                                                `------.\n      :eXON/rIOT^jORMAS · gHANDY/gODS · rAHIEM/eSSENCE · cELIC/rEKTUM: \n      `------.                                                .------''\n             `------------------------------------------------''\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',62420,5,0,NULL,NULL,'stdcall','scan',1780048759);
INSERT INTO door_catalog VALUES('stega','STEGA.LHA','~/Code/amiexpress_doors/Archives/AmiExpress/STEGA.LHA','Stega','XIM','Stega',NULL,NULL,NULL,'NAME  : stega.lha SHORT : Enables you to hide messages inside pictures, mods or samples.',replace('############################################\n\nNAME  : stega.lha\nSHORT : Enables you to hide messages inside\n        pictures, mods or samples.\n\n############################################\n#     sHAiTAN / uLTiMA ^ dATA dIVIsION     #\n#             iNTERNET fREAK!              #\n############################################\n ·A·T· *30/03/96*  ·L·A·N· 00:14 ·T·|·C· \n','\n',char(10)),'Stega.guide',replace('@database Stega.guide\n@author "Lyle Zapato"\n@(c) "Copyright © 1995-96 Lyle Zapato"\n@$VER: Stega.guide 1.03 (03.15.95)\n@node Main "Stega Help"\n\n                       Stega v1.03\n                LSB Steganographic Utility\n\n\n             Copyright © 1995-96, Lyle Zapato\n                   All rights reserved\n\n                Release date 15 March 1996\n\n\n                       User Manual\n\n      @{" Introduction     " link Introduction} What is Stega?\n\n      @{" Usage            " link Use} Stega usage syntax\n         @{" Hiding        " link Hide} Hiding mesages\n         @{" Extracting    " link Ext} Removing messages\n         @{" Viewing       " link View} Viewing mesages\n         @{" Carriers      " link Car} Supported carriers\n         @{" Messages      " link Mess} Supported messages\n         @{" Hints & Tips  " link Hint} Things to do with Stega\n\n      @{" Future           " link Future} To be implemented\n      @{" Signatures       " link PGP} PGP verification\n      @{" Bugs/Limitations " link Bugs} Things you should know\n      @{" Lyle Zapato      " link About} About the author\n      @{" Legalities       " link Leg} Annoying Mumbo-Jumbo\n      @{" History          " link History} New in this version\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Introduction "Stega: About Steganography and Stega"\n\n- Steganography\n\nSteganography is the process of hiding one thing inside of another.\nThe purpose of this is to transmit information without people knowing\nyou are doing so by placing the important/secret information inside\nsomething innocuous.\n\nSteganography isn''t the same as cryptography, as the security of a\nsteganographicaly transmited message lies in people''s ignorance of its\nexistance.  However, the two can and should be used in tandem.\nEncrypting a message prior to hiding it will make sure that if someone\ndoes find it, they won''t be able to read it.  But why bother to hide it\nif you have strong encryption?  Well, sometimes the people in power, be\nthey the government or an employer, don''t like it when those under them\ncommunicate in a way that they can''t easily pry into.  Even if the \ninformation being sent is harmless to them, or just plain none of their\nbusiness, they will still place a burden of guilt on the sender for\nacting covertly.  If those same people couldn''t care less about someone\nsending pictures of Cindy Crawford, samples from Monty Python, or other\ntypes of ubiquitous net-noise, then by hiding the transmission inside\nthose things a person could conduct their business without being\nbothered by someone else''s power trip.\n\n- How Stega Works\n\nStega uses the Least Significant Bits (LSB) in sound samples and true\ncolor images to hide the bits of a message.\n\nThe LSB is the binary equivalent of the ones place.  Just as the ones\nplace is less significant to the overall value of a decimal number than\nthe tens or hundreds place, so too is the LSB not as significant as the\nother bits in a binary number.  Changing the value of the LSB will only\nchange the overall value of the number by 1.  In an 8-bit number, which\ncan have a value from 0-255, changing the LSB will change the overall\nvalue by only 1/256th.  For most things we preceive, a difference of\n1/256th is just too small to notice.\n\nLets say we have 3 pixels from a 24-bit picture:\n\n     RED    GREEN     BLUE       PIXEL COLOR\n  --------------------------------------------\n  11111111 00000000 00000000  -  Red\n  11111111 11111111 11111111  -  White\n  00000000 00000000 11111111  -  Blue\n\nNotice that the pixels are made up of three 8-bit numbers, one each for\nthe red, green, and blue color values.  The LSB is the right-most bit.\nNow, we want to hide the letter ''A'' inside those pixels.  To do that we\ntake each bit of the character (ASCII characters are made up of 8-bits)\nand place them, one-by-one, inside the LSBs of the color registers for\neach pixel:\n\n  A = 65 = 01000001\n\n  11111110 00000001 00000000  -  Redish\n  11111110 11111110 11111110  -  Whiteish\n  00000000 00000001 11111111  -  Blueish\n\nWe end up with pixels who''s colors are very close to the original, so\nclose your eye wouldn''t notice the difference in most situations.\n\n[Note that this will only work with 24-bit color and 8-bit grey scale\nimages since enough gradations of color/greyness are needed in order\nfor the change to be unnoticable.]\n\nThis works the same for sound samples (it''s just easier to illustrate\nwith images.)  In an 8-bit sample, each sample is made up of 8 bits\n(duh) and has a LSB.  The value of a sample represents the volume\nof the sound at a given point in time and a 1/256th difference in\nvolume is hard for the human ear to hear.\n\n- Technical Notes\n\nThe size of the message is hidden with the message for use when\nextracting.  It is converted into three bytes and hidden inside the\ncarrier just as the message bytes are.\n\nTo get the size of the message, take the first 3 extracted bytes and use\nthe following formula:\n\n   size (in bytes) = byte1 + (byte2 * 256) + (byte3 * 65536)\n\n[Note this allows for a message size up to 16.7 million bytes.]\n\n@endnode\n\n@node About "Stega: About the author"\n\nThe author is Lyle Zapato.\n\nThat is all you need to know.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Car "Stega: Carriers"\n\nFor lack of a better name, the thing in which you hide a message is\ncalled the carrier.  Stega currently is known to support only the\nfollowing types of carrier:\n\n · Sound samples\n   - IFF-8SVX 8-bit\n   - RIFF-WAVE 8/16-bit (Windows .WAV files)\n · Images\n   - uncompressed 24-bit IFF-ILBM\n   - IFF-DEEP\n\nStega might work with some other types of IFF file formats, but it\ndoesn''t yet support compression.  Stega looks for a BODY chunk in\nthe IFF file and starts doing it''s thing there, so if a format\ndoesn''t have a BODY chunk it won''t work.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Hint "Stega: Hints & Tips"\n\nCONVERTING CARRIER:\n\n You can convert the carrier to and from different file formats\n (8SVX to WAV, WAV to .au, DEEP to ILBM, etc.) and the message will\n remain intact.\n\n NOTE: this is only true for LOSSLESS file formats.  JPEG and MPEG\n and other LOSSY (is that a word?) formats will remove subtlety\n (ie your message) and as such are unsuitable.\n\n To extract the message from the converted carrier, you will need\n to convert it to a supported file format if it isn''t so already.\n example:\n\n 8SVX + message = 8SVX(message) -> WAV(message) = WAV + message\n\nHIDING ENCRYPTED MESSAGES:\n\n Since the output of encryption programs such as PGP usually have\n headers and other information attached that let the program know\n that it is an encrypted file, detecting encrypted messages hiden\n in carriers is only a matter of searching for that header info,\n something that a script could do automatically (this is also\n generally true for any situation where someone has a bit of the\n unhidden message.)  To make your encrypted messages easier to\n hide, use some method that will remove the headers.\n\n One program that will do this for PGP output is called Stealth\n by Henry Hastur, which is available on Unix, PCs and Amigas\n (maybe more... the version I have, v1.0, is from 1994.)  Good\n luck trying to find it, since the author didn''t include any\n address to reach him (hmmmm...  sounds familiar :)\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Mess "Stega: Messages"\n\nA message is whatever you want to hide.  It may be text, data, an\nexecutable program, or anything else made up of bits (this means\nthings such as elephants and soap aren''t legal messages, sorry.)\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Use "Stega: Usage"\n\nUSAGE: Stega [-<options>] <command> <carrier> [<message>] [destination]\n\n COMMANDS:\n\n   H - @{" Hide " link Hide}\n   X - @{" Extract " link Ext}\n   V - @{" View " link View}\n\n OPTIONS:\n\n   q - Quiet: don''t print any disgnostic information.\n\n CARRIER:\n\n   Complete path of @{" Carrier " link Car}.\n\n MESSAGE:\n\n   Complete path of @{" Message " link Mess}.\n\n DESTINATION:\n\n   Default destination is "RAM:encrypt" when hiding and "RAM:decrypt"\n   when extracting.\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Hide "Stega: Hiding"\n\nThe Hide command is used to hide a message inside a carrier.\n\n Example usage:\n\n   Stega h Sounds:Bevis Work:text/MySecretPlan RAM:Bevis\n\n   - Hides the file "Work:text/MySecretPlan" inside "Sounds:Bevis"\n     and saves to "RAM:Bevis".\n\n   Stega h Sounds:Butthead c:MyProgram\n\n   - Hides the program "c:MyProgram" inside "Sounds:Butthead" and\n     saves to the default destination "RAM:encrypt".\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Ext "Stega: Extraction"\n\nThe Extract command is used to read a message from a carrier and save\nit to a file.\n\n Example usage:\n\n   Stega x Sounds:Butthead c:MyProgram\n\n   - extracts the message from "Sounds:Butthead" and saves it to the\n     file "c:MyProgram"\n\n   Stega x RAM:Bevis\n\n   - extracts the message from "RAM:Bevis" and saves it to the default\n     destination "RAM:decrypt".\n\n@endnode\n\n@node View "Stega: Viewing"\n\nThe View command prints the hiden message to the shell from which Stega\nwas run.  It is also possible to use a pipe to redirect the output to\na printer.\n\nView should only be used for text messages.  Using it with binary\nmesages will result in garbage being printed and probably a system beep\nor two.  Although harmless, this is annoying and should be avoided.\n\n Example usage:\n\n   Stega v RAM:Screams\n\n   - Reads hidden text in file "RAM:Screams" and displays in the shell.\n\n   Stega -q v Work:GenericMPython > PRT:\n\n   - Reads hidden text in file "Work:GenericMPython" and sends it to\n     the printer without any diagnostic information (-q).\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Future "Stega: Future"\n\n- Support for compressed formats\n- support for DEMs (Digital Elevation Maps) if possible\n\n@endnode\n\n@node Bugs "Stega: Bugs & Limitations"\n\nBUGS:\n - none yet known\n\nLIMITS:\n - no support for compression\n\n@endnode\n\n@node History "Stega: History"\n1.0b - initial release\n1.01 - added RIFF-WAVE compatability\n     - recognizes weather or not an ILBM is compressed\n       (still won''t use compressed ones, though)\n     - general cleaning up of the IFF/RIFF parsing code\n1.02 - fixed problem with the encoding of the message size\n       Wouldn''t work properly with sizes 512 <> 256\n     - Another problem with message size fixed... It is now\n       not quite compatable with earlier releases. DANG!  Oh\n       well... it works better now.\n     - changed command names (encrypt=hide, decrypt=extract,\n       read=view.) so that it makes more sense.  Old commands\n       still work (EDR).\n     - created AmigaGuide documentation\n1.03 - added IFF-DEEP compatability\n@endnode\n\n@node Leg "Stega: Legal Stuff"\n\nStega copyright © 1995-96 Lyle Zapato, all rights reserved\n\nStega is copyrighted freeware.\n\nIt may be freely distributed as long as this document (Stega.guide)\nis kept with it.  Stega may not be used for military or comercial\npurposes.  Nor may it be used for temporary relief of minor back\naches, but that''s another story.\n\nThere is no guarantee, expressed or implyed, as to the ability or\nsuitability of Stega.  The author, Lyle Zapato, takes no\nresponsibility for the use or misuse of Stega.  Nor does he take\nany responsibility for the rise in the price of paper, but that''s\nanother story.\n\nNOTE: The exportation of cryptography from the USA is governed by\nthe same trade sanctions as sidewinder missiles and germ-warfare\nagents.  As far as I know, steganography is not included with\ncryptography in these matters, but I may be wrong.  In any case,\nLyle Zapato shall not be held accountable for the egregious\ntransgression of any exportation regulations by miscreants who\nfailed to heed the above warning and either didn''t check to see\nif what they were doing was legal or, knowing full well the\nillegality of such actions, went ahead and did them anyway. Lyle\nZapato denounces such actions as counter to the greater good and\njoins with the regulatory body pertinent in such matters in\nagreeing that the perpetrators deserve whatever punishment the\nlaw decrees.  ALL HAIL THE NSA!!  VIVE EL HERMANO GRANDE!!\n(there, that should cover my ass.)\n\n@endnode\n\n@node PGP "Stega: PGP Signatures"\n\nStega uses PGP signature files for authenticity verification.\nYou will need PGP (Pretty Good Privacy), the public-key\ncryptographic program, in order to use these.\n\nBelow is my (Lyle Zapato) signed public key.  Add it to your\nkeyring by typing:\n\n   pgp Stega.guide\n\nTo check if a signature (xxx.sig) matches its associated file,\ntype:\n\n   pgp <filename>.sig <filename>\n\nPGP will warn you if the signature doesn''t belong to the file\nor if it is not from me.\n\nIf you have any trouble understanding or using the above\ninstructions, please consult the PGP documentation.\n\n-----BEGIN PGP MESSAGE-----\nVersion: 2.6.2\n\nowHrZJjKzMpgMEm1a9XS+Beze/atZGQs/sn83zfqvKxi+9XuU9a8Pl9/v1/93X/2\n4nDuA0onErjYQ1VcSyJPiKyda7SturjM8bFXV2+7rs/L3kfvXBJeHpzQEGn/cL+r\n5/6DpWFB4TX3nIqUeq+kqTl1H7z/9eLEZ5cOb5xz91NZ56dJWcEed1preFdu8Fyd\nUnPm6r7Q9e3P9CMq3sqJHF1bs5bRJYmjJLW4RC+xOJkBCHRBwMnV3dNPIcA9QCEg\n1MnH01nB2zVSwcnH39kbLM0VllpUnJmfZ6VgpGemZ8TFlRvo7OdY5Rzs6eTp6Ojo\n6uoYkFFRYBiRnlNpFFIY5hLkVBUcFOlh4JwSpJ+U4mee5G6YHJmYl6Ht72uQbGHo\n5sflbhJq6WsQFhXu4uXt51nh6xPpqu/n5V+V61IeUq7vlVxoGeVempIXHpxtbGpg\nnulk7O5rUBaZ6JQe7OEUmZjtwxVskOFsnmluaOgTVeihHeRsXOxs7pTun+1s6J2U\nFJJv4luUXeTuU1ISlZdhUGIZkeFlVpgToZ2ZlOVjVuDoGBrEVeJY4psaXpHj6VaQ\nkezuZpBUbmvLZVsU7OrPBfazq58LngABAA==\n=W+gj\n-----END PGP MESSAGE-----\n\n@endnode\n','\n',char(10)),NULL,'auto-added',51805,4,0,NULL,NULL,'stega','scan',1780048759);
